{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest","id":1001132,"title":"Oathsworn [Quest]","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Exalted"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-11-09T18:57:22-08:00","id":1001132,"title":"Oathsworn [Quest]","content":"<div align=\"center\">20th Decending Wood, western Hundred Kingdoms</div> <br /> <br /> The rain, as usual, hisses down like arrows fired from a great bow from heaven.<br /> <br /> The overhanging elm and bamboo foliage provides some respite from the rain, but out on the trail overlooking the river's edge, you often receive the full force of the weather whenever the wind shifts. Your straw raincoat and hat provide only scant protection, and even then, cold drops from the hat leak through your wool jacket and stream down the line of your back, chilling you to the bone.<br /> <br /> &quot;Forget the Season of Wood, this should be the first Season of Water&quot;, you glumly mutter to yourself. Xuansu, your horse, picks up the sound and snorts in response, flicking his ears and spraying drops. You reach down to reassure him, scratching underneath the jawline.<br /> <br /> &quot;Relax Xuansu, we should be out of this weather soon, as soon as we come across a village or something&quot;, you say, more for your own benefit than his. To be honest, it has been 9 days since the last farmhouse, over which you have slept on the ground, eaten little other than tea and oatmeal, and been pounded on by rain and mountain wind. Your clothing is soaked, and you most definitely need a bath. Xuansu needs to be brushed, your armor likely needs to be dried, and to top it off, despite being trained as a cavalryman, your ass and back are sore from non-stop riding over rugged terrain.<br /> <br /> To put it short, you are looking forward to a hot meal and a dry place to sleep, and are even willing to part with some of your hard-earned copper coins to get it. If only there was a village!<br /> <br /> Which, to your pleasant surprise, there is, after you come around a bend in the river some time later. Clustered around a stream descending from the mountainside, surrounded by terraces churned into mud by the rain, is a small farming village. The peasant's houses, made from daub, wood and thatch, are rude, but look well-kept compared to the forests you have been sleeping in for the last week. Already, you can imagine hot bread and a warm fire, and you eagerly push Xuansu on.<br /> <br /> A couple of minutes later, you pass by the village outbuildings, and you almost instantly know something is off. Even with the almost-incessant rain, there should still be people about, either farmers working on some project or another, or simply children playing under the eaves. So far, nothing.<br /> <br /> Suddenly, there is movement in the corner of your eye! On reflex, you throw back your raincoat and free the chappe of your sword from its sheath, allowing it be drawn quickly. The movement reveals itself to be little more than a child peeking around a doorframe, a child that is quickly snatched up by a woman and the door slammed shut. Almost as soon as it happened, the village is silent again.<br /> <br /> It doesn't take a genius to realize that something has got the peasants scared. And, contrary to the popular opinion held by many of your social class, it usually takes a <u>lot</u> to set these hard-worn rural folk on edge like this. Over your travels throughout these parts, you have come to realize that the people are often as rugged as the land they work, often (sadly, you realized a while ago) as a result of defending themselves from bandits and raiding parties.<br /> <br /> Whatever happened here, it must have been <u>bad</u>. It doesn't look like the village has been raided recently, but there could have been some disease or something. But, you didn't see any signs of recent burials when you approached. Something else? Something ..... worse?<br /> <br /> Perturbed, you pull the collar of your jacket up around your nose and mouth as a precaution, and head further into the village. You are already here, so there is no use avoiding possible calamity. Underneath your coat, you keep your blade ready to be drawn, hidden from view.<br /> <br /> At the relative center of the village, there is an open spot forming a rough plaza of sorts, upon which sits the largest building in the settlement. The &quot;common house&quot; isn't usually used as a dwelling, but as a meeting-house, a drinking establishment, generic shrine and as a store-house for goods to be paid to the local lord for taxes. On top of all that, there is usually a warm corner or something that compassionate farmers would let a weary traveler sleep in to get out of the weather. Unfortunately, it looks like that won't be happening.<br /> <br /> On the veranda of the common-house, a couple of louts lounge about, drinking from clay jugs, throwing dice ,and avoiding the rain as best they can. As soon as you enter the &quot;plaza&quot;, they perk up. Just from looking at them, from their unshaved appearances, to their rough-and-mixmatched weaponry, you can immediately tell what they are.<br /> <br /> Bandits.<br /> <br /> There is about 5 of them (unless there are more inside the common-house), and they are all in various stages of drunkenness. Two of them have clubs studded with metal spikes, one of them is kicked back in a stool next to a spear leaned up on the wall of the building, there is a crossbow (mercifully unloaded) in the lap of the drunkest, and the bandit that walks out to confront you has a heavy, broad-bladed chopping sword suspended from his belt. Thankfully, none of the bandits are actually brandishing the weapons, merely watching you approach with bleary eyes.<br /> <br /> All of this you take in from under the edge of your straw hat, as you ride up to the veranda. They are paying more attention to your horse and to your weapons strapped to the saddle than to you, greed apparent in their gaze. The bandit with the sword (whom you take to be their leader) calls out to you as you dismount.<br /> <br /> &quot; Ho, there! A poor day to be travelling, especially with this weather. Come up and share a drink or two, and tell us some stories of the road&quot;! The leers from his companions suggest the offer isn't being made in actual honesty.<br /> <br /> You don't reply, instead focusing as you dismount Xuansu, removing the bit and lashing the halter to a railing.<br /> <br /> &quot;Hey, didn't you hear me! I'm talking to you, horse-boy!&quot; <u>Now</u> the bandit's hand is on his sword, but he hasn't quite drawn it yet. Neither have his friends moved yet, content to watch.<br /> <br /> <b><u>YOU:</u></b><br /> <br /> 1) Draw your sword. A little less than a meter of fine, castle-forged Vaneha steel, double-edged and needle-pointed, your sword is already partly drawn and ready to go.<br /> 2) Draw your spear. Lashed to the right side of your saddle, your spear is 6 feet of solid Mountain Ash, with another foot of razor-sharp spearhead. The reach provided could give you the edge in this fight.<br /> 3) Draw your bow. Lashed to the back of your saddle, in a lacquered wood case, your bow is 3 feet long, made of laminated wood, bamboo and rawhide, and powerful enough to take down a deer at 60 meters. There is still 30+ feet of mud between you and the villains, enough space to get off at least one arrow, maybe more<br /> 4) Try to talk the bandits down. You have always been told you were good with your tongue (especially by the castle maids /snerk), and doesn't your book on philosophy state that &quot;to win without fighting is best&quot;? Regardless, maybe nobody has to bleed today.<br /> 5) Something else? -write insert-<br /> <br /> ** Both the sword and spear can be used solo, or with a shield that is hanging off the pommel of the saddle**<br /> ++ Keep in mind, the weapon you choose is going to be the weapon our character here is going to prioritize with, specialize with, in a fashion. We will almost certainly use all of them eventually, however, but the weapon you choose with be his &quot;favorite&quot;++<br /> <br /> <u>OOC</u>:<br /> <br /> Hello everyone!<br /> <br /> After hanging around this forum for quite some time, I have finally decided to work out the quest that I have had rattling around in my head.<br /> <br /> Feel free to ask questions, about the setting or about the characters or whatever, and I will gladly answer.<br /> <br /> So keep in mind that this is my first actual Quest thread, so be gentle. Constructive criticism is gladly welcome.<br /> <br /> First things first, the title &quot;Oathsworn&quot; refers to the protaganists social class, which is basically the combination of European feudal knights, Japanese Samurai, and Chinese Youxia. They are the military caste of the feudal Hundred KIngdoms, which themselves are a combination of Warring-States Era China, Sengoku Jidai Era Japan, and Feudal Europe. So ..... landlords, peasants, castles and stuff. They train as mounted and dismounted archers, spearmen and swordsmen (and women. So long as one can fulfill the obligations of the position, both men and women can be Oathsworn, and Lords for that matter), and fight for Lords in order to be granted lands (and therefore, food and money). The title comes from the Oaths they make when being invested, basically Chivalry + Bushido.<br /> <br /> Of course, not every Oathsworn starts out with an Oath of Fealty made, and therefore, they have to wander around the Hundred Kingdoms, fighting under different lords and making a name for themselves, until they attract the right sort of attention. This ..... doesn't always happen, and some, if not many, Oathsworn end up forsaking their vows to protect people, and end up as glorified bandits and mercenaries.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11892,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","user":"Boston123","date":". 11-09-2016, 10:14 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-09T19:10:19-08:00","id":1001138,"title":"","content":"Outnumbered by drunken louts? The sword would be too good for them. Draw the spear. With the exception of that crossbow, none of their weapons can threaten that kind of reach.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Share your wonders in <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/823884-3e-the-artifact-and-evocation-workshop#post823884\" target=\"_blank\">The Artifact and Evocation Workshop</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1315-ferryman","name":"Ferryman","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1315_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"960"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-10T05:20:10-08:00","id":1001318,"title":"","content":"Spear... Although, with the benefit of hindsight, maybe it would have been better to stay mounted if we were attacking with that, jinete-style.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>On the frontier of the Wild South, there's only one woman with the grit to take on its most dangerous outlaws and bring them <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/984977-back-alive-or-maybe-dead-quest\" target=\"_blank\">Back Alive, or Maybe Dead.</a><br /> <br /> Avatar by <a href=\"http://puzzlinghappenstance.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">K.S. Brenowitz</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/908-semicasual","name":"semicasual","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar908_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2499"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-10T05:27:20-08:00","id":1001322,"title":"","content":"Use your silver tongue to draw the leader into a false sense of security. Kill him with your sword and give the others a chance to surrender.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I write things.","edited":{"userid":3950,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3950-wonderandawe","user":"wonderandawe","date":". 11-10-2016, 08:31 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3950-wonderandawe","name":"wonderandawe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3950_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5410"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-10T06:04:10-08:00","id":1001329,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>semicasual</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1001318#post1001318\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Spear... Although, with the benefit of hindsight, maybe it would have been better to stay mounted if we were attacking with that, jinete-style.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> The majority (4 / 5) of the bandits are on the veranda of the common-house. Not saying we couldn't reach them with the spear, but they could duck behind the railing, and we would only have about the length of the spearhead reaching them.<br /> <br /> Plus, riding a horse across sticky mud at anything faster than a walk tends to be a bad idea.<br /> <br /> OOC:<br /> <br /> So far, we have 2 for spear and one for sweet-talk + sword. I'll leave it open to later tomorrow, and then we will roll with whatever the numbers say.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11892,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","user":"Boston123","date":". 11-10-2016, 09:57 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-11T06:20:01-08:00","id":1001732,"title":"","content":"Silver tongue, they are unworthy oh our blade.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11567-jairain","name":"Jairain","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11567_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"345"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-11T07:33:38-08:00","id":1001750,"title":"","content":"So, <br /> - 2 for spear<br /> -1 for sweet-talk + sword<br /> -1 for sweet-talk<br /> <br /> I'll leave it open till about noon","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-11T09:43:42-08:00","id":1001814,"title":"","content":"I will change to sweet talk + sword.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11567-jairain","name":"Jairain","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11567_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"345"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-11T09:58:00-08:00","id":1001820,"title":"","content":"I'm gonna vote for sweet-talk then swording.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1311230-badassoverlord-s-assorted-homebrew-folder\" target=\"_blank\">Are you in the market for some Martial Arts? Perhaps some custom Artifacts for your campaign?</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10131-badassoverlord","name":"BadassOverlord","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar10131_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"322"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-11T12:14:07-08:00","id":1001870,"title":"","content":"Spear it is! To be honest, the humble spear has got to be my all-time favorite weapon.<br /> <br /> In response, you reach around the saddle and grab the shaft of your spear, and with a quick twist of the hips, draw the blade from it's sheath. Holding in an a neutral mid-guard, you advance to the center of the plaza, mindful to keep the bandit-chief between you and the crossbow.<br /> <br /> The bandit grins through stained teeth, than draws his sword into a high guard. Held in a two-handed grip over the head, this stance can deliver brutal chopping blows, especially with that backsword of his.<br /> <br /> You are fairly confident that you are both better-trained and in a better mindset (aka not drunk as hell) than the bandit -scum in front of you. Individually, each wouldn't be much of a threat, but you are, indeed, outnumbered here, unarmored, and there is that crossbow to think about. Better hope they are too drunk to attack you all at once, since as your copy of the <i>Thousand Correct Actions</i> states, to divide and conquer is best. <br /> <br /> The bandit roars and jumps forward, blade raised to split you from neck to navel. In response, you flick the speartip at his face. He jerks back, clumsily parrying the thrust at his midsection. He raises his blade again, in effort to get inside the reach of your spear, only for you to sidestep the swing and swing the backend of your spear into the back of his knee. Already off-balance, it only takes a slight amount of pressure to force him facefirst into the mud. You control the sword with the blade of your spear, then draw back and drive the point through his throat. He shudders, then lies still, blood mixing with the cold mud.<br /> <br /> You straighten to see the two club-wielders charging you, one on either side. Despite being drunk, they are experienced enough to realize that dividing your attention is they way to win. Catching a two-handed downswing on the bottom third of your spear, you use the momentum from the block to pivot the spearblade up to the ruffians throat. Pulling the blade in a draw-cut, you open his throat up, while simultaneously shattering the other bandit's knee with the spiked bronze ferrule on the base of the shaft. He howls, falling to his knees, only to get finished off by the same ferrule to the gut.<br /> <br /> Sudden pain! The spear-wielder used the distraction of his mates to thrust at your back from the side. Only the slippery mud and the tightly-wound strip of hemp cloth wound around your midsection prevent you from getting spitted like a deer. Regardless, the cut over the ribs burns, and the bandit grins at the sight of your blood on his blade. He drops into another full-out thrust, in effort to finish you off while you are distracted. Indeed, you are barely able to knock the spearhead aside with your own, and your sliding of the blade down his spearshaft was more accidental than intentional. The bandit cries out, dropping the spear and grabbing the remains of his now-half-severed hand. Gathering your wits in spite of the cold fire burning down your side, you take the momentary opportunity to drive your spearpoint into his chest. He falls back, and <b>takes your spear with him</b>, yanking the shaft out of your suddenly-weak hands! The blade is stuck on something inside.<br /> <br /> Crossbow! Where is the crossbow! You need to deal with the crossbow! Now, of all times, is not good to be unarmed! You draw your blade and stumble-run to the veranda, where the drunken lout is <u>still</u> attempting to load his weapon. By the time you make it to the stair, he has the string drawn back, and he is clumsily fishing a bolt from the box by his side. <br /> <br /> There is no way you are going to make it to sword-range in time. <br /> <br /> In near-panic, you grab the closest thing you have at hand; your straw rain-hat. Ripping the hat from your head, you send it spinning into his face, where the rough straw scratches his eyes. With a cry, he buries the crossbow bolt into the floor of the veranda by your feet and grabs his face with both hands. Stepping forward, you whip your sword-tip up from hip to breastbone, disemboweling him with a twist of your hips.<br /> <br /> That entire encounter was over in the space of about a minute and a half, a couple of score of heartbeats that are now pounding in your ears, a counterpoint to the rain on the roof. You are suddenly very cold and weak, barely able to grip your sword-hilt. You sink to your knees as the adrenaline leaves your system, replacing the cold fire in your veins with aching throbbing. The cut on your side throbs, and after pressing your hand over the wound, you are unsure if the wetness you feel is blood or rainwater.<br /> <br /> Regardless, you are still alive for now, and for all you know, there might be more bandits skulking around. Using your sword to help you to your feet, you stumble out into the rain to retrieve your spear. You find the bandit you spiked in the gut earlier to be still alive, dragging himself through the mud and gasping up red froth. Your armsmaster would have beaten you stupid for leaving a living enemy behind you like that, but she isn't here to watch you almost get your ass kicked by common thugs, now is she? As an afterthought, you drive the bronze spike through his temple.<br /> <br /> The rain continues to pound down on the earth and on the roofs, so you didn't hear the person walking up behind you until they lay a hand on your shoulder. In a panic, you grab the dagger thrust sideways through your sash, but another hand firmly grasps your wrist and turns you around.<br /> <br /> In front of you stands an older man, tall and strong, in a peasants smock. He chuckles, then throws an arm around your shoulders and starts to lead you to the common-house.<br /> <br /> &quot;Relax there, son. My name is Ten-Sheaves, and I am the headman of this little village. You just did us a hell of a favor. Why don't we head on inside, where it is warm, and we can get that wound looked at and some food to eat?. Don't worry about your horse, I'll have one of the boys put him up&quot;.<br /> <br /> You can't really argue with some hot food and medical attention, and so you let the headman lead you back to the common-house (leaning on him a bit more than you would like). Inside, you strip off your water-and-blood-stained outer clothing before a bevy of older women, who cluck and chitter in disapproval at the long cut over your ribs. From what you can see, the cut is long, but shallow, and already the cut is bleeding slowly, seeping rather than pulsing. They bathe the wound in boiled tea, then stitch up the sides, with you wincing all the while. After, with you all bundled up in a quilted blanket, you sit down with the headman for a hot meal courtesy of the village.<br /> <br /> A succotash of corn and beans and pork, served with applesauce. Hot corn-pone bread, served with buttermilk, baked onions and potatoes, and hot pine-needle tea and hard apple cider. All standard peasant-fare and .........suspiciously generous for this time of year, in the weeks before the planting. They must have dipped deeply into their stores in order to put this on for you out of gratitude. You partake happily, only to stop when Ten-Sheaves clears his throat. <br /> <br /> Setting aside your spoon and bowl, you look up to see him bowing formally at the head of the table. <br /> <br /> &quot;Our humble village thanks you, your Lordship, for his benevolent assistance in this matter. For this we are eterna..&quot;<br /> <br /> You dismiss the bowing and scraping with a wave of your hand &quot;It was nothing, Headman. Well, <i>almost</i> nothing,&quot;, you say with a wry smile, gesturing to your bandaged side. &quot; Regardless, helping the defenseless is one of my Oaths, and one that I take seriously. I am glad to be of service&quot;.<br /> <br /> &quot;And we are indeed grateful&quot;, he said, earnestly this time. &quot;May I sit? And, without being presumptuous, ask your Lordship his name?<br /> <br /> You gesture to the table, pushing over the jug of cider. &quot; My name is Kasai.&quot; If your lack of a clan name is surprising, he doesn't show it &quot;I take it that this feast, <i><span style=\"font-size:10px\">which is wonderful by the way </span></i><span style=\"font-size:14px\">, was not entirely out of gratitude?&quot;.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\">Ten-Sheaves hesitates, then looks you straight in the eyes. To his credit, he doesn't look away from your yellow wolf-eyes or wolf-pelt hair, like most superstitious peasants, and your opinion of him improves immensely. </span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\">&quot;To be honest, Lord, this isn't the only problem we have with banditry&quot;. No surprise there, bandits and thuggery are like a chronic case of syphilis in the Hundred Kingdoms.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\">&quot;If I may be honest, where is your Lord in all this? If you are having such a problem with bandits, why did they not deal with it?</span><span style=\"font-size:14px\">&quot;<br /> </span><br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\">Ten-Sheaves shifts uncomfortably, &quot; They have been interfering with us for quote some time now, since last Season of Water. We sent a runner to our Lord before they got entrenched. He ....&quot; The man looks immensely uncomfortable.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\">You sigh, bringing a cup of cider to your lips, predicting what the answer will be.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\">&quot;He .... well ..... he beat our messenger, told him to get back to our village, and told him to deal with the bandits on our own, that he didn't have time for this, and that we had better have the annual taxes on time!&quot; Ten-Sheaves explodes, gesturing animatedly. </span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\">You rub your eyes to disperse a sudden headache. The whole &quot;point&quot; of the feudal order is that it is a system based on &quot;give and take&quot;, peasants work the land in return for defense. Too many lords in your opinion have all-but forsaken these vows, and instead abuse those lower to better focus on their games of politics and war. Despicable. Your father always told you your word, your Oaths, was your life, and to fail to fulfill those promises was worse than death, a lesson you held close to your heart.</span><br /> <br /> &quot;Regardless, I assuredly just killed off a major part of their band, so they won't be able to give you too much trouble from now on.&quot; You rely, drinking deeply, only to choke on your tea when Ten-Sheaves replies.<br /> <br /> &quot;Ah, not quite, Lord. This bandit band is ...... uh...... quite large. The first time they showed up, they had about 40 people. 35, now.&quot; acknowledging your recent actions.<br /> <br /> Your head is pounding, and you rest your head on your palms. &quot; 40 bandits....... Gods, of all the prices I have to pay in return for some food and a dry place to sleep!&quot; You rely, half-jokingly. &quot;And, let me guess, you have no weapons here to defend yourselves with?&quot;.<br /> <br /> &quot;Officially, no&quot;, states the headman.&quot; Our Lord prefers us to only have access to weaponry at his leisure and only at his estate&quot;. The silence at the end of the statement is profound.<br /> <br /> &quot;Unofficially?&quot;, you sigh.<br /> <br /> &quot; We do, in fact, have weapons hidden in stashes in the mountains, as well as food. Which was helpful, when the bandits first showed up, took all the weapons we had in the village as well as most of our stores of food. We might be <u>peasants</u>, but we aren't <u>stupid</u>&quot;. replies Ten-Sheaves hotly. <br /> <br /> &quot;What have you got? Are there people trained in their use, or are they just wall-hangers?&quot;, from your position, deep in thought, head resting on the table.<br /> <br /> &quot; Mostly spears, a couple of crossbows and self-bows, some helmets. Mostly ....uh..... looted from battlefields&quot;. He sounds embarrassed, although you don't agree. Weapons are expensive and to let them rot on a battleground is a waste. Looting them is entirely practical. &quot;And, we are capable of defending ourselves. We have fought off bandits before&quot;.<br /> <br /> You nod,&quot; Just not against 40.&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;And, not against a group so well-led&quot;<br /> <br /> Confused, you ask,&quot; What do you mean&quot;?<br /> <br /> &quot;Most bandits are rather disorganized, only in it for the loot or blood. These ones are led by ........um&quot;. Again, he looks and sounds uncomfortable. &quot; An ....... ah........<span style=\"font-size:10px\">Oathsworn</span><span style=\"font-size:12px\">, </span><span style=\"font-size:14px\">like your Lordship, ex. except you're not, you know ...... a </span><u>bandit scumbag&quot;</u><span style=\"font-size:14px\"> he trails off.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\">You sigh, and consider the consequences of what you just walked into. On one hand, if you want to uphold your Oath (and you do), you can't just leave these peasants here at the not-so-tender mercies of the bandits, especially after you kill several of their men, something they won't be too happy to learn. The peasants would be lucky to escape with their lives, much less their livelihoods. On the other hand ..........</span><u><b><i>40 BANDITS</i></b></u><span style=\"font-size:14px\"> (well, 35 now, but the difference between 40 and 35 stab-wounds is trivial.) You might not make it our of that scrum with all your limbs or life intact.</span><br /> <br /> <b><u><i>YOU</i></u></b><br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\">1) Agree to face the (angry) bandits, like something out of a story. BLOOD AND GLORY</span><br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\">2) Challenge the Oathsworn leader to a duel to the death. Play with whatever honor they might have left.</span><br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\">3) Train the villagers to fight. They already have some experience, they just need something to &quot;stiffen their backbone&quot;, as it were. </span><br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\">4) Make small talk until everyone goes to sleep, then slip out and ride away under the cover of night. 'Discretion is the better part of valor&quot; and all that, and only you will know of your shame. You can make it up eventually ........right?</span><br /> <br /> <u>OOC</u><span style=\"font-size:14px\">:</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\">Holy crap, that took, like, 2 hours to write out. </span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\">Some background info:</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\">1- Yes, our character is a Wyld mutant, or, more accurately, the descendant of one. In my Hundred Kingdoms campaign, there were two major ethnic groups; Lowlanders (who live in the river valleys), and Hill-Tribes, who live in the mountains. The Lowlanders are similar to the Yamato, and the Hill-Tribes are similar to the Ainu, except with various Wyld Mutations (mostly derived from various animal traits, like wolf fur and eyes, etc. Nothing too squicky). They are discriminated against by the Lowlanders, and are largely marginalized.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\">Kasai is the descendant of a minor Lowland (who has an estate in the highlands) noble and the daughter of a Hill-tribe chieftain, and despite the connotations of that relationship, had a happy childhood. His parents loved each other deeply, and after his mother died in childbirth (ah, the most classic of origin stories!) his father raised him, and in fact wanted to legitimize Kasai when he came of age. &quot;Polite&quot; society, and his noble-born stepmother, would not accept that, and Kasai left in order to make his own fate.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\">2- Kasai is a mortal, as evidenced by his lack of anima and his bleeding everywhere. I am a fan of mortal stories in general, and I am unsure if Kasai will Exalt or not. If he is, it will be later in the story.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\">Anyways, how has it been so far? Too wordy, not enough dialogue, etc?</span><br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-11T12:16:11-08:00","id":1001871,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>BadassOverlord</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1001820#post1001820\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm gonna vote for sweet-talk then swording.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Woops.<br /> <br /> Sorry, saw your post after I wrote out the gigantic wall-o-text involving spears. If it is any consolation, Kasai did use his sword, and I can have him use said sword more often if you would like.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-11T13:10:36-08:00","id":1001898,"title":"","content":"Let's challenge the Oathsworn lord. We can't take out 35 bandits on our own. Maybe we can reawaken his honor and together we can take out the bandits.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I write things.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3950-wonderandawe","name":"wonderandawe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3950_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5410"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-11T13:13:05-08:00","id":1001901,"title":"","content":"Yeah, I probably spend about an hour a page on my own quest. Most posts are between two to four pages. <br /> <br /> My only comment is to make sure the text formatting is all the same. The changes in font makes it difficult to read. Also, i always run my posts through <a href=\"http://www.hemingwayapp.com/\" target=\"_blank\">Hemingway App</a>. I don't fix everything it flags, but it helps with the wonky sentences.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I write things.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3950-wonderandawe","name":"wonderandawe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3950_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5410"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-11T13:26:34-08:00","id":1001910,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Boston123</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1001871#post1001871\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> Woops.<br /> <br /> Sorry, saw your post after I wrote out the gigantic wall-o-text involving spears. If it is any consolation, Kasai did use his sword, and I can have him use said sword more often if you would like.</div> </div> </div> </div>No worries! It's my fault for turning up late anyway.<br /> <br /> Now it's time to d-d-d-duel!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1311230-badassoverlord-s-assorted-homebrew-folder\" target=\"_blank\">Are you in the market for some Martial Arts? Perhaps some custom Artifacts for your campaign?</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10131-badassoverlord","name":"BadassOverlord","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar10131_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"322"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-11T15:11:15-08:00","id":1001943,"title":"","content":"&gt;2. Challenge to a duel!<br /> <br /> I think your writing so far is fine, but I would recommend being careful about how you highlight bits of text. You should probably use <i>italics </i>instead of <u>underlining</u>for emphasis, and I don't recommend shrinking text unless someone has suddenly started speaking in a whisper or trailing off.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>On the frontier of the Wild South, there's only one woman with the grit to take on its most dangerous outlaws and bring them <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/984977-back-alive-or-maybe-dead-quest\" target=\"_blank\">Back Alive, or Maybe Dead.</a><br /> <br /> Avatar by <a href=\"http://puzzlinghappenstance.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">K.S. Brenowitz</a>","edited":{"userid":908,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/908-semicasual","user":"semicasual","date":". 11-11-2016, 07:46 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/908-semicasual","name":"semicasual","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar908_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2499"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-11T16:49:01-08:00","id":1001976,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>semicasual</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1001943#post1001943\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">&gt;2. Challenge to a duel!<br /> <br /> I think your writing so far is fine, but I would recommend being careful about how you highlight bits of text. You should probably use <i>italics </i>instead of <u>underlining</u>for emphasis, and I don't recommend shrinking text unless someone has suddenly started speaking in a whisper or trailing off.</div> </div> </div> </div>Thanks!<br /> <br /> Amusingly, that was what I was attempting to go for, using smaller font.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-12T08:34:29-08:00","id":1002227,"title":"","content":"Train the villagers.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Like my Avatar? Courtesy of<span style=\"color:#0000FF\"> Jen</span>! : <span style=\"font-size:12px\">Anybody want their characters to be experimented on ? </span>post 98<br /> <i>An Exalt is never unarmed.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4018-l-het-esh","name":"L'het'esh","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4018_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"521"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-12T23:44:07-08:00","id":1002469,"title":"","content":"Duel it is!<br /> <br /> After a quiet moment of deliberation, you decide that challenging the bandit-leader to a duel would probably result in the least amount of bloodshed. Charging into the bandit camp, sword raised to Heaven, was all well-and-good in the stories, but was <i>stupid</i> in practice; the bandits would probably fill you full of arrows before you got to the camp. And leading the peasants into battle was risky, as is <i>all</i> battles. You doubt there would be 40 peasants willing or able to fight, and going up against experienced killers meant you either wanted a skilled band (not happening), or force-of-numbers. No, fighting the Oathsworn-bandit face-to-face was the best possible choice, given the circumstances.<br /> <br /> You sigh, &quot; Ten-Sheaves, if I challenge the leader to a duel, would the bravest of the villagers have my back? It is traditional for a duelist to be surrounded by their seconds and retainers, as insurance against interference.&quot;<br /> <br /> The headman scratches his chin.&quot; There are some of the more rowdy boys who were always willing to fight, and your victory out front might have started a fire in their bellies. I'll put the word out, but what does this involve, exactly?&quot;<br /> <br /> Pushing back from the table, you pace back and forth, gathering your thoughts.&quot; Usually, both fighters meet at a predetermined place and time, usually somewhere where neither party will have an advantage in terrain or elevation. They are surrounded by a circle of their seconds and retainers, both to mark out the boundaries of the dueling ground, and to ensure either one of us won't catch an arrow in the throat from the treeline. Generally, most duels go to either the first blood, or the third blood, but in this case I am going to challenge the leader to a duel to the death&quot;. You smile grimly.<br /> <br /> Ten-Sheaves stares for a moment.&quot; Isn't that ...... a little drastic?&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;No. The situation is dramatic enough that it warrants it, it is rather ...... dishonorable to refuse a duel to the death, without at least showing up, as it implies cowardice on their part, and besides.&quot; Your lips pull back from your teeth, gleaming in the firelight, making your look remarkably similar to your namesake. &quot; Their refusal to heed their Oath offends me, on a particularly deep level. Their denial of their vows sullies the honor of the Oathsworn as a whole, disgusts me <i>personally</i>, and needs to be amended as soon as possible&quot;. <br /> <br /> You stop pacing, and walk over to the door of the common-house. From here, you can look out over the village plaza, and to the river. On the other side of the river, there is a small bit of earth, between the shore and the scarp, that from here looks relatively flat. Without turning your head, you address Ten-Sheaves.<br /> <br /> &quot;Headman, send out your notice, and if you will, please bring me paper and ink, or some other writing material. I have a challenge to deliver.&quot;<br /> <br /> Ten-Sheaves bows formally, forehead touching the floor of the house, then scurries past you to address the crowd of gathered peasants. Ignoring them, you walk down to the riverbank, then, grimly ignoring the ice-cold springmelt (gah! And you just got dry and warm, too <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/frown.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Frown\" smilieid=\"11\" class=\"inlineimg\" />), stride across the gravel riverbed to the other shore. <br /> <br /> From there, you can clearly see that the flat land you saw from the village is perfect for dueling. Flat, large enough to fit a band of people, relatively free of rocks and large hummocks, and within bowshot range of both the village and the treeline, so no side could claim unfair advantage. Deciding on the spot, you splash (Damn!) back to the village in order to compose a letter of challenge.<br /> <br /> The peasants have no paper, but have ample supplies of paperlike birchbark, which is almost as good, and ink made from soot and pine resin. After scraping the birchbark smooth, you sit down and compose a letter using the graceful characters of Dragontongue, whose brushstrokes are something all &quot;proper&quot; Oathsworn learn to write, alongside the sword, bow and spear. Your armsmaster actually started you on calligraphy before you ever held a blade, only deeming you capable of swordplay when you could make brushstrokes with a brush 3 feet long and 10lbs in weight.<br /> <br /> In your letter, you &quot;call out&quot; the Oathsworn bandit, calling them dishonoured and dishonorable, their behavior with the bandits a shameful display of character, and that they were not even fit to care for pigs. In the closing statements of your letter, you formally challenge the Oathsworn to a duel, held tomorrow at noon, on the field below the village. After the ink dries, you curl up the birchbark and bind it tightly to one of your arrows. Upon leaving the common-house to go check on Xuansu, you get buttonholed by Ten-Sheaves, who informs you that the aforementioned rowdy peasants would be more than happy to back you up, and that the bandit camp was at the top of the falls, about 3 miles downstream. With that in mind, and partially due to the fact that the rain has actually let up for once, you saddle up Xuansu and proceed up the riverbank.<br /> <br /> Like most peasant trails, the going is rough, with the trail between the hillside and the river more suited for foot-traffic than a mount and rider, so it takes you a couple of hours to make the distance between the village and the falls. There, a stream tumbles down a rock face, joining the river with a roaring crash and spray of mist. At the top of the falls, about 30 feet above the surface of the water, a bandit leans up against a tree, watching the trail both up and down-stream. You know he sees you, because he ceasing leaning back and takes a bow in hand, watching you to gauge your intentions. <br /> <br /> Upon seeing you raise your own bow and draw back an arrow, he ducks behind a trees in a sudden panic. Amused, you loose the arrow so it <i>thunks</i> into one of the tree-trunks, sadly not the one the ruffian hid behind. With that, you turn around and start to ride back to the village to prepare, not heeding the angry yells of the embarrassed bandit.<br /> <br /> Once at the village, you are informed that a meal has yet again been prepared for you, but you are unable to enjoy the food to the extent it deserves. The tenets of your Oaths, as well as your personal honor, demand that you be unflinching in the face of death in the acts required to uphold said Oaths, and you are....... technically, in the sense that you have weighed the choices and accepted the consequences, but that <i>doesn't mean you are happy to die</i>. You still, for a lack of a better term, fear death, at least the painful part that usually precedes death. Dying as a result of your Oaths has never actually been this close in the forefront of your mind before. You don't even understand why you are so nervous: it isn't like you haven't been in a fight before, or even killed. Your first battle was at age twelve! Regardless, your stomach is in knots, and you push away the food and drink brought before you. You need to find something to occupy your mind.<br /> <br /> Reaching over to your saddlebags, you pull out the bits and pieces of your armor, checking them for damaged laces and rusting metal. Now, your father definitely cared for you, and tried to provide the best equipment he could, but his lands were high in the mountains and far from a large town, making him relatively poor in a land where most wealth is caught up in agricultural production. Your weapons, your horse, and your armor are literally the only things you own, in all of the world. <br /> <br /> Making lamellar-style armor is more of a Lowlander thing, with access to large deposits of &quot;iron sand&quot;. Up in the hills, the people have to make due with smaller deposits, meaning their main weapons are spears and bows, and metal armor (when they even have it. Hill-tribespeople mainly protect themselves with shields, and armor made of leather and bronze), is instead made up of hundreds and thousands of small rings, linked in a fine mesh and backed by a quilted cloth padding. In your case, the mesh is sewn to the padding, in a 6-in-1 pattern, meaning the mesh is very fine and resists slashes and stabs just as well as lamellar armor, and there are no laces to cut or absorb water or blood. It has served you perfectly well so far, and is actually relatively comfortable to wear for long periods of time. Composed of a jacket, a coif/hood, sleeves, thigh protectors, and greaves, all bound together with loops and ties and buttons, the armor is light enough for you to put on on your own, always a plus when you lack retainers and pages.<br /> <br /> Now preoccupied, you go over your armor, piece by piece, checking each lacing, each ring, each piece of padding, to make sure it is functional and not compromised. With that finished, you inspect your weapons, examining your sword and spear for nicks, then whetting them on a fine piece of slate until they are capable of shaving hair. <br /> <br /> Thus preoccupied, you are startled by the sudden opening of the door. On the other side is a peasant girl, who is <i>very</i> surprised (and none too happy), to open the door only to get a dagger held up to her face. She jumps back with a yelp, falling to the floor in a mess of skirts and ribbons. Amused and partly embarrassed, you help the girl up, apologizing for startling her. She quickly offers a bow, stammering pleasantries.<br /> <br /> &quot;O..Our humble village would like to thank our Lord for aiding us, and I offer your Lordship suc...succ..... <i>assistance</i>, if he so desires&quot;<br /> <br /> What the hell? It isn't likely that she would know how to care for, or put on, arms and armor, and you have already stacked the dishes the farmers brought for you, so she can't really be referring to food. And, why is she so dressed up? Ribbons in her hair, powdered chalk on the face, fine (for rural peasants) clothing .....<br /> <br /> Oh. <b><i>OH</i></b>.<br /> <br /> The girl looks up from her bow at the sound of your face meeting both palms. Your are, to be entirely honest, mortified. Based on her tone, she was probably put up to this by Ten-Sheaves, the bastard, out of some misplaced sense that you would appreciate the gesture. She is obviously embarrassed, color showing through the chalk on her face, though for your continued lack of approval or for this farce as a whole, you are unsure.<br /> <br /> &quot;Listen, girl, come over here...... <i>quickly</i>&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;A ... at once, my Lord&quot; She scurries over, reaching to the back of her sash and beginning to untie it. You cut her off hurriedly<br /> <br /> &quot; No, <i>NO</i>, not like that!&quot; Almost immediately, you regret it, as tears of shame start to well up.<br /> <br /> &quot;Am .... am I not <i>pretty enough</i>? I ... I could get one of the other girls ....&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;No, <b><i>listen</i></b><i><u>,</u></i> you are fine, plenty pretty enough. I just....... what I want right now, is a <b><i> bath</i></b>. A nice, <i>hot</i>, <i><b>bath</b></i>. With soap. And something to drink. And more blankets. And maybe, after all that is over and done with, a nice, pretty girl like yourself to talk to. But not ...... that.&quot;, you say, waving your hand as if warding off flies.<br /> <br /> She stares, shocked, then smiles, relief apparent in her eyes. &quot;I'll see what I can do&quot;, wiping the tears and chalk from her face. Without it, she looks much better, not so doll-like.<br /> <br /> She goes to leave, only to turn around at your question,&quot; If you are going to be helping me, I would like to know your name.&quot;<br /> <br /> She smiles,&quot; My name is Saiko, my Lord&quot;. She pauses, then impetuously , &quot;And if I am going to be helping <i>you</i>, then I would like to know your name!&quot;. She gasps, then covers her mouth and bows. To be so forward with one of the nobility is to presume to be close to them, which is <i>extremely</i> rude if one isn't actually close to the noble, and a good way to lose your tongue with less-benevolent blue-bloods. Instead, you chuckle, and reply,&quot; My name, miss Saiko, is Kasai, of Clan Harutane.&quot; With that, she grins, bows again, and leaves.<br /> <br /> In the end, you get your bath, with pine resin-scented soap, a jug of apple cider, plenty of quilted blankets, and Saiko, who returns wearing much more comfortable clothing. The two of you stay up late into the night, talking about your respective backgrounds ( She is fascinated by your stories of growing up in a noble Manor, with horses and hunts and things. You, on the other hand, agree with her that watching potatoes grow sounds, quite frankly, boring as hell) By the time the fire dies down, the cider is gone, Saiko is out cold on the pallet set out for you, and you have to wrap yourself up in a blanket in front of the hearth. So much for a good nights sleep. At least, you were distracted from the looming possibility of your own pending demise, at least for a few hours.<br /> <br /> ---<br /> <br /> The next day dawns bright and early, much to your displeasure, while Saiko (who drank most of the cider, if you remember correctly) mumbles something incoherent and pulls the blanket over her head in a pile. Leaving the common-house, you hold your hand to the sun, taking note that you have a few hours until noon. Almost immediately, you get blindsided by Ten-Sheaves.<br /> <br /> &quot;Was the girl to my Lord's satisfaction?&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;Yes, for what it is worth.&quot; (You decide to not lay into the man for his moral shortcomings this early in the morning).<br /> <br /> &quot;Good, good!&quot;, rubbing his hands together. &quot;It is good that Saiko can actually be of us..&quot;<br /> <br /> You cut him off with a wave of your hand, not wanting to discuss this any farther. &quot; I want to see the fighters that will be supporting me, Headman&quot;. <br /> <br /> &quot;Of course, my Lord&quot;, he replies, leading you out to the terraced fields, where the population of the village is hard at work planting various crops. He calls out several names as he walks.<br /> <br /> Several of the peasants put up their tools and make their way over to you. All young men, they all appear to be sturdy, strong from working in the fields and at crafts. They line up at the direction of Ten-Sheaves.<br /> <br /> You walk down the line, looking each of the men in the eyes as you pass. To their credit, none of them look down or away, indicating strength of will and purpose.<br /> <br /> &quot;Now, as I am going to be dueling the bandit leader, there will not be much fighting on your part. <i>However</i>&quot;, you state, holding up a finger. &quot; We are dealing with bandits here, so they might be a little ..... less <i>honorable</i> , and not uphold their end of the bargain, if you understand. Are each of you willing to fight, quite possibly to the death, if that happens?&quot;<br /> <br /> One of the men stirs, then states,&quot; To be honest, m' Lord, we've been wanting to fight back for a long time. Letting them walk all over us was a bad idea from the start. Makes us look weak, makes us <i>feel</i> weak. Not nice, if you get me.&quot;<br /> <br /> You nod in understanding. <i>Exactly</i> what you wanted to hear. &quot; Sounds good. There is a few hours left before noon, so go get your weapons, relax, hang around until I call for you.&quot; With that, they disperse back to their houses, and you head back to the common-house ..... only to be met by Saiko, who has your armor and weapons spread out across the room.<br /> <br /> &quot;<i><b>Please</b></i> be careful with all that, girl. The sword alone is probably worth more than your whole village&quot;, you say, half-jokingly.<br /> <br /> &quot;Oh, I was, Lord Kasai, I just ...... wanted &quot; she replies, flustered.<br /> <br /> You chuckle at her embarrassment,&quot; Do you even know how to put this stuff on&quot;?<br /> <br /> &quot;No, but I can try, it doesn't look too hard&quot;, chin jutting out.<br /> <br /> You sigh, then hold out your arms and spread your legs,&quot; Might as well get started, this is probably going to take a while&quot;.<br /> <br /> First, you strip down to the waist (to Saiko's immense embarassment), then pull on a jacket up undyed linen. Over that, around the midsection from the waist to the upper ribs, is wound a length of tough hemp cloth, to reinforce the stomach. Then, greaves, bound to the calves. Then, the thigh guards around the waist. Then, the armored jacket, which buttons up the side and is bound on the inside to the thigh guards. Then, the sleeves, bound to the jacket, and finally, the coif, which buttons on the side as well. Over that, another sash of hemp, through which is thrust your fighting-dagger and from which your sword sheath is suspended. Once everything is in place, you wriggle and jump in place until everything is comfortable and &quot;where it is supposed to be&quot;.<br /> <br /> You grunt in mild appreciation,&quot; You aren't half bad at girding, Saiko. That took much less time than it would on my own, you wouldn't be looking for a job as a page, would you?&quot;, you joke.<br /> <br /> She flushes, beaming, &quot; It's just like putting on regular clothes, my Lord, only .... heavier, and ....... made of metal...&quot; she trails off, lamely.<br /> <br /> You grin, and leave her on the veranda of the common-house, to head down to the field to prepare it for the duel. Mostly, said preparations involve kicking flat any clumps of grass and dirt, and kicking out any large-ish rocks. Most of this is to keep your mind occupied, as the &quot;knot&quot; is growing again in your stomach, and you are rather desperately attempting to stay busy.<br /> <br /> Eventually, there is nothing left for you to do. You go over any possible strategies (there are none, you don't even know what weapons this bandit uses, you <i>really</i> went into this one blind), you go through various weapon-forms and techniques to keep yourself limber, panic some, pray some, and, finally, fall into the last refuge you can think of: meditation. Your armsmaster, a devout Immaculate, attempted to teach you meditative techniques, but you never really took to them. All they did was make you sleepy and bored. Now, in your last moments (HAH) of possible peace, you sink to your knees in the damp grass, close your eyes, and attempt to empty your mind. At first, it is rather difficult, what with the undercurrent of nervous tension running through your body, like a strung bowstring gently plucked. Eventually though, your breathing steadies, the sound of the river rushing over the gravel bar fills your ears, and the smell of the wind rushing down the slopes of the hills fills your nostrils, bringing with it the smell of wet, green growing things. You focus on these, and your mind empties. <br /> <br /> ---<br /> <br /> &quot;You know, I thought it would have been someone older, or taller at least. This guy is just a kid!&quot; Brash laughter shatters your empty mind, and your eyes snap open.<br /> <br /> In front of you, in a circle, stands a motley collection of assorted bandits, thugs and ruffians, armed with an equally-mixed collection of weapons; spears, clubs, axes, a sword. There are about 12 of them, spread out around the outside of the ring you flattened out earlier.<br /> <br /> Closer, in the foreground, about 10 feet away, stands the bandit-lord, the Oathsworn. She stands rather nonchalantly, glaive thrown over both shoulders, sheath still over the blade. She is dressed in a lamellar vest, lacquered black against rusting, with a single lamellar thigh plate and shoulder pauldron, both of different colors and styles (indicating they were looted from different corpses) on the left arm and leg. On her head, she wears not a helmet, but a conical hardned -leather cap, like what a peasant Man-at-arms, would wear. At her waist is a long, single edged slashing saber, not the straight sword of an Oathsworn. From her face and figure, she could be anywhere between 20 and 50, but she definitely carries herself as someone older, rather beaten down and weatherworn. She smirks coldly at her joke.<br /> <br /> You rise to your feet easily, with no cramps. Surprising, since you have been kneeling in the damp grass for over an hour. The knot in your stomach is gone.<br /> <br /> Sounds of running come up from behind you, and the peasants from before fill in the rest of the circle. The outspoken one steps forward and taps you on the shoulder,<br /> <br /> &quot; Sorry, my Lord, they just came in so fast, we didn't see them until they got here.....&quot;<br /> <br /> You dismiss the man with a shrug, and he steps back into the circle. The peasants come equipped with mainly spears and shields made of planks, and two have crossbows. Ten-Sheaves joins the circle, armed with the chopping sword from the bandit you killed yesterday. At your raised eyebrow, he replies ,&quot; I am the Headman of this village, and I would think less of myself if I couldn't bring myself to defend it&quot;. You nod your agreement.<br /> <br /> With that, the circle is complete, and you are technically ready to duel. You turn to the Oathsworn, still smirking like this is a party of sorts.<br /> <br /> You reply to her snipe,&quot; You know, I am actually rather surprised that you had enough shregs of honor to actually show up. Here I was thinking you were going to back out, and that I was going to have to hunt you down like some kind of rabid animal.&quot;<br /> <br /> She snorts. &quot; Speaking of <i>animals.</i>&quot; She gestures at your hair and eyes. &quot; What, Daddy or Mommy got snagged by the &quot;forbidden fruit of the forest&quot;, and thought to raise some pups? HAH&quot;. She shakes her head. &quot; Regardless, you show your age and lack of experience, kid, by bringing up honor. <b><i>Honor</i></b>. Honor is just a pretty word, one that is as empty as the words we say. Can you <i>eat</i> honor, burn pride to keep you warm at night?&quot; She sneers. &quot;No. No, no you can't. I've <b><i>tried</i></b>. Believe me, kid, &quot;honor&quot; won't keep you alive when you get abandoned by your lord in the middle of winter. Want to know what will?&quot;<br /> <br /> She flicks the sheath off the blade of her glaive, the curved blade gleaming in the noonday sun.<br /> <br /> &quot;<b><u><i>THIS</i></u></b> will. Trust me, all those pretty Oaths and vows and promises we make, kid, all they are are ribbons, ribbons wrapped around a sword. Sure, they look nice and make the sword beautiful, something you can show off to the rest of the fops in court, but they really just make the sword <i><b> hard to use</b></i>. That is what we really are ..... swords. The second you realize that, and stop trying to uphold some ultimately meaningless words said to someone who <i>really</i> doesn't care about you, the happier you will be. A sword is just a tool, and tools are made to be used. Discard all that useless shit that is holding you down, and realize what you can <i>truly</i> be.&quot;<br /> <br /> She raises an eyebrow,&quot; That is, of course, <i><b>if</b></i> you survive this, which I doubt. I've been doing this for almost as long as you've been alive. This ain't my first swordfight, kid&quot;. <br /> <br /> You ponder her words for a moment.<br /> <br /> &quot;.....You are right&quot;.<br /> <br /> Her other eyebrow shoots up. &quot;Really?&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;I <b><i>AM</i></b> a sword, I will not deny that. But ....... &quot; You look her in the eyes, staring her down &quot; I am a sword raised in the defense of the weak, those that cannot defend themselves. I am a sword, aimed at destroying those that oppose the righteous. Yes, a sword is &quot;just&quot; a tool. A tool made for killing and blood. But, what matters is <i><b> how you use it</b></i>. I am the ribbons wrapped around the blade. <b><i><u> I am Oathsworn</u></i></b>, and that is why I am here today, here right now, and that is why one of us is going to get laid out on the ground in this circle, and <i>it certainly isn't going to be me</i>. Without my Oaths, I am <i><b>nothing</b></i> ........ but that is what you already are, isn't it? A twisted little ball of hate, filling the void with bloodshed?&quot; You shrug<br /> <br /> &quot;Go ahead, preach to me some more, about how my Oaths are meaningless and how we are so alike. By all means, fill the day with talk, but ...... <i>I am going to have to &quot;interrupt&quot; you in a minute or so!&quot;</i><br /> <br /> She snarls, whipping the shaft and blade of the glaive up and around. You snap your shield up, by your face in a high guard, stepping forward to catch the shaft of the glaive in early-swing. You slide your shield down the shaft, directing the blade of the glaive out of the way, and stab your spear in an overhand grip aimed at her face. She jerks her head back, and your speartip snags in the underside of her pauldron, With a push, you cut into the flesh of her upper arm, drawing a gout of blood and a cry. She drops the glaive with a curse, and makes to draw her saber. You, in turn, drop both the spear and the shield and step in close, close enough to be face to face, Grabbing ahold of her wrist and upper arm, you prevent her from drawing her blade, and try to wrestle her to the ground, only to receive a knee to the gut for the effort.<br /> <br /> The wind is blown out of you in a rush, and you stumble back all-but-helpless. In your stumble back, your foot catches on a tuft of grass that you somehow missed (although you swear you went over this ground like, 30 times). It ended up being a blessing in disguise (and you darkly promise to offer sacrifice to the God of Grass later on), as your tumble to the ground caused a two-handed swing at your face to miss. <br /> <br /> She steps forwards, raising her blade with a cry of victory, and drives the blade towards your face in a glittering arc. You manage to grasp the edge of your shield, and bring it up in time to stop the blade punching through your teeth. The tip of the blade skates off the chamfered wood, and she snarls in frustration, bringing the blade hacking down in great slices that knock chips from the wood only inches from your face. On the third strike, she hits the edge of the shield, rather than the face, and the tip of the blade punches through, splitting the shield down to the reinforced ridgeline and scoring a line across your mail coif. <br /> <br /> You wrench the shield back to your side, jerking the blade in her hands to the side and throwing her off balance. There is an opening, if only for a moment! You scrabble for the dagger tucked into your sash, then, almost in slow motion it seems, pull it back and drive it up into her midsection, up under the edge of the lamellar vest. <br /> <br /> She coughs, releasing her blade and grabbing hold of the dagger. You lift up and give the pommel of the dagger a good kick, to drive it deeper in and to drive her back, then roll over and jump to your feet, drawing your sword as you go. <br /> <br /> She remains standing, but doubled over the dagger embedded in her ribcage. She coughs again, then falls to her knees, blood running over her chin. She straightens out, armored cap falling off as she does so, and you stride forward to give the finishing blow. <br /> <br /> She holds up a hand, and you stop. She raises her head and <i>smiles</i>, looking you in the eyes. She whispers something through blood-stained lips, and you lean forwards (not <i>too close</i>, mind you), to attempt to hear it.<br /> <br /> &quot;<i>Let me ..... do it right...&quot;</i><br /> <br /> She looks you in the eyes again, and you come to an understanding. Almost imperceptibly, so only she can see, you nod, and stand beside her, sword raised in a two-handed grip. She closes her eyes, draws in a long, shuddering breath, then pulls the dagger upwards, cutting up into the stomach. At the same moment, you drop the blade, drawing it back with a twist of the hips. <br /> <br /> You don't completely sever the head from the body, but you <i>do</i> cut into the spine and the jugular, killing her almost instantly. Her body topples forwards, blood seeping into the grass, and you bow deeply, respecting the Oathsworn she was again in her last moments. Every Oathsworn makes the oath to destroy those who oppose the weak, and, in a way, that was what she did. <br /> <br /> In the middle of your bow, you hear the beat of feet rushing up behind you, and you snap up straight, your sword into a high guard, to see a bandit rushing you with axe held high. Evidently, you were right, and the bandits wouldn't uphold the agreement you made. You sidestep the downswing, then open his throat with a drawcut that sends him tumbling to earth. <br /> <br /> It is 11 vs 11, and the odds are <i><b>not</b></i> in favor of the bandits. None of them have ranged weapons, for starters, and two of them go down almost immediately with crossbow bolts buried in their chests. 11 vs 9. You stand up tall and roar,&quot; ON ME!&quot;.<br /> <br /> The peasants with spears and shields rush forwards, forming a rough shield wall with their plank shields. With that, they are able to ward off and harry the bandits with spear thrusts, all while staying safe behind their shields. Meanwhile, the crossbow-users reload, but it is an unwieldy process at the best of times. The remaining bandits turn to run and flee.<br /> <br /> You, however, have other ideas. Picking up the slain Oathsworn's glaive, you twirl it around, then charge home into the mass of confused ruffians. Separated and confused as they are, there is plenty of space in between them to swing the glaive in mighty arcs, opening up chests and slicing into limbs. 4 go down before your onslaught, and 1 more takes a crossbow bolt in the throat.<br /> <br /> The remaining four bandits panic and break, some fleeing down the riverbank and some trying to scale the escarpment. You direct the spear -wielders to chase down the river-runners, and the crossbow-wielders to take potshots at the villains trying to scale through the scrub and gravel, while you gather up your equipment and head back to the village.<br /> <br /> Pressing a hand to your side, you feel wetness, but on further inspection, the mail and padding on your side is un-compromised. You assume that you just popped your stitches again. Regardless, you would have the villagers take a look. <br /> <br /> When you get back to the village, the whole settlement is in an uproar, with cheering, yelling, clapping and dancing. Evidently, everyone in the village watched the fight from the banks of the river, and now <u>everyone</u> wants to slap you on the back, shake your hand, or some odd combination of the two. The celebration goes on late into the night, with singing, dancing, booze and food, brought out from the farmers hidden storehouses.<br /> <br /> Damn, it feels good to be the hero.<br /> <br /> Early the next morning, when everyone else is still sleeping off the libations, you bundle up the armor and weapons of the slain Oathsworn in a burlap sack, to either be brought to market or to be worked into something you can use. That, along with a bulging pouch of food (dried oats and corn, a rasher of smoked pork, a jug of cider, dried apples, aka enough food to ensure you could eat well until the next castle-town, and then some) that is your &quot;payment&quot;, gets lashed to Xuansu's saddle. You start walking him through the quiet streets of the village, only to be met with Saiko, dressed in travelling clothes and equipment bundled up in a blanket, and with a determined expression on her face.<br /> <br /> You stop, and raise an eyebrow, without saying a word.<br /> <br /> &quot;I'm coming with you&quot;. No flustered words, no bowing; just determination.<br /> <br /> &quot;......Ok.&quot;, as you lead Xuansu around her and down the trail.<br /> <br /> &quot;...Wait, <i>what</i>?. You aren't even going to argue?&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;No, because it isn't going to matter. I can say no, and you are still going to follow me, and probably get lost in the woods and eaten by wolves. Or, I could say yes, and avoid all that, and the argument in general. I just want to know <i>why</i>?&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;Way to suck all the fun out of it&quot; She muttered. &quot;It is ...... it's just that ...... I have <b><i>no future</i></b> here! Everyone thinks I am useless. I can't spin, I can't weave, the only thing I can do is plant crops and feed pigs! I want to do something, <i>anything</i>, worthwhile with my life!&quot;<br /> <br /> You stop, and stare at her,&quot; Girl, how old are you, 15?&quot;<br /> &quot;<b><u><i> 16. And a half</i></u></b>&quot;<br /> You roll your eyes, &quot; Regardless, what do you think my life is? I roam around and kill people for a living. Is that the kind of life you want to lead? Before I came to your village, I slept on the ground for almost 2 weeks straight!. In the woods! Surrounded by animals!. My life <u>isn't fun</u>.&quot;<br /> <br /> She doesn't say anything, just juts her chin out defiantly.<br /> <br /> You sigh.You can respect her determination, and can understand her desire. It reminds you somewhat of yourself, and the discrimination you face as a scion of a Hill-tribe .&quot; Fine, whatever, let's go. But, I don't want to hear any complaining! Expect weapons training <u>every day</u>. Oh, and you are in charge of cooking my meals, washing my clothes, cleaning and brushing and feeding Xuansu, as well as <i>whatever else I tell you to do</i>. Got it?<br /> <br /> Her response is a hug, full-force about the shoulders. You sigh, &quot; None of that, at least not in public. In public, I expect, to be addressed as &quot;my Lord&quot;, at the very least. No first names, either! Too familiar.&quot;<br /> <br /> She pulls away and bows. &quot;Yes.....my Lord&quot;.<br /> <br /> &quot;Great. We can start by working on strength training&quot; You unsling the back of food from the saddle and toss it to her, along with the glaive. &quot; That is yours. I'll show you how to take care of it&quot;. She grins in response, and the two of you start down the trail.<br /> <br /> &quot;Shouldn't you tell your parents&quot;?<br /> She snorts ,&quot; You mean my uncle? He is <i>glad</i> I am gone. He always thought I was useless.&quot;<br /> <br /> <br /> And so they walked in silence, until they came up to the bend in the river, around which the little village would pass out of sight. She stops, turns and looks back.<br /> &quot;You can still go back, you know&quot;, you say, in a quiet voice.<br /> &quot;No ... No. I just .... need a minute. I'll catch up.<br /> <br /> After a moment, she turns, and follows you down the trail.<br /> <br /> - End Chapter 1<br /> <br /> Holy fucksticks, that literally took 6 hours or so to type out.<br /> <br /> -ahem- (works claws back into fingers again)<br /> <br /> 1) How does everyone feel about Saiko. I am ...... unsure about how I portrayed her so far.<br /> <br /> Her character was ultimately part of one of my PC's NPC retainers when I ran my Hundred Kingdoms campaign and that ...... was pretty much it. I chose to include her here to see if I can flesh her out more effectively than &quot;peasant girl that joins for adventure, washes clothes and cooks meals, yadda yadda&quot;.<br /> <br /> 2) Fighting in armor.<br /> <br /> This is something many movies and suchlike fail to portray. Armor, when compared to weapons from a similar &quot;era&quot; is largely invulnerable to said weapons, meaning combats between two heavily-armored opponents usually doesn't involve swinging/stabbing away at armor, but instead devolves into &quot;who can sneak a blow into a space <u>not</u> protected by armor&quot;?.<br /> <br /> When this happens, you could do one of two things:<br /> <br /> - Use a heavy weapon to just bust your way through the armor.<br /> <br /> With a weapon like a mace or a hammer, armor becomes largely irrelevant due to concussive force. Even using a large slashing weapon on armor, like the Oathsworns &quot;glaive&quot; aka a Naginanta, would be reasonably effective due to the force impacted. Kasai would still be getting whapped in the head with a heavy weight on the end of a long stick, which would probably kill him outright, mail or no.<br /> <br /> - Go through the unarmored spots.<br /> <br /> There are almost always unarmored spots on a warrior, even ones in Full-plate. When facing a heavily armored warrior, one of the more effective ways to take them out is either wrestling them to the ground then slotting a knife through their eye, or stabbing them under the armpit or in the inside of the thigh. This was what Kasai was attempting to do, first with his spear-to-the-face, and then when he tried to manhandle the Oathsworn to the ground, to slot his knife through her eye. In the end, he <u><i>still</i></u> went around her armor, just up-and-under as opposed to a place that was unarmored to start with.<br /> <br /> Another thing, and a personal pet-peeve of mine: <u>shields</u><br /> <br /> You know how in the movies, when you see warriors &quot;block&quot; blows with shields and take the full force of the blow perpendicular to the face of the shield?<br /> <br /> Yeah, no, that is how you break your arm and the shield, unless your shield is domed/lenticular, which <u>most</u> personal shields ended up becoming. Kasai's shield is lenticular. Rounded shields deflect blows along the surface, as opposed to taking the force directly.<br /> <br /> And, even with lenticular shields, you still kinda want to use them to &quot;parry&quot; blows, not merely &quot;block&quot; them, as it tends to be more effective.<br /> <br /> As always, criticism and comments are more than welcome.<br /> <br /> I must let everyone know, this week I am actually going <u>back</u> to work ( I was on vacation for these last few posts), so I will be slowing down posts to maybe once/twice a week. Probably on Mondays.<br /> <br /> Thanks, everyone.!<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-13T16:54:43-08:00","id":1002699,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Boston123</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1002469#post1002469\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Holy fucksticks, that literally took 6 hours or so to type out.<br /> <br /> ....<br /> 1) How does everyone feel about Saiko. I am ...... unsure about how I portrayed her so far.<br /> ...<br /> <br /> 2) Fighting in armor.<br /> ...</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes, writing is a time-consuming task. Moreso if you want to do it properly. 6 hours isn't bad for... what looks like 5-6 pages of text? More?<br /> <br /> 1. Saiko: Too early for me to say, really. I'm not offended by my first impression, but then again I usually find youngsters in search of adventure to be tiresome. We'll see how she develops through dialogue and behavior.<br /> <br /> 2. Generally, I think your fight choreography is good. Nothing you wrote stood out to me as especially unrealistic. <br /> <br /> On another note... I think your dialogue needs some work. The words are fine, but your punctuation is all over the place. Also, I would advise against using italics, bold lettering, and so forth to indicate a person's tone of voice. Adjectives are much clearer.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>On the frontier of the Wild South, there's only one woman with the grit to take on its most dangerous outlaws and bring them <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/984977-back-alive-or-maybe-dead-quest\" target=\"_blank\">Back Alive, or Maybe Dead.</a><br /> <br /> Avatar by <a href=\"http://puzzlinghappenstance.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">K.S. Brenowitz</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/908-semicasual","name":"semicasual","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar908_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2499"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-13T18:52:33-08:00","id":1002738,"title":"","content":"I'm disappointed we didn't get another decision point or two before the end of the chapter. I try to keep my posts between two and four pages. If I go over, I go back and figure out if I missed any potential decision points. <br /> <br /> For example, how to react to having a random village girl offering her body to you would have been a great character defining decision point. You could have had a mentor student, brother sister, father daughter, or something else. <br /> <br /> Remember, in writing a quest you are giving up part of the control of your story. You can be surprised at what unexpected ideas you could farm from your players. My quest character ended up much different than I imagined him when I started. Yet he ended up being a more fleshed out character due to my players comments and decisions.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I write things.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3950-wonderandawe","name":"wonderandawe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3950_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5410"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-14T03:20:27-08:00","id":1002813,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>wonderandawe</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1002738#post1002738\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm disappointed we didn't get another decision point or two before the end of the chapter. I try to keep my posts between two and four pages. If I go over, I go back and figure out if I missed any potential decision points.<br /> <br /> For example, how to react to having a random village girl offering her body to you would have been a great character defining decision point. You could have had a mentor student, brother sister, father daughter, or something else.<br /> <br /> Remember, in writing a quest you are giving up part of the control of your story. You can be surprised at what unexpected ideas you could farm from your players. My quest character ended up much different than I imagined him when I started. Yet he ended up being a more fleshed out character due to my players comments and decisions.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Good point, I'll try to keep that in mind for the future.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-14T21:16:30-08:00","id":1003235,"title":"","content":"I'm enjoying the read. Thanks for sharing!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11567-jairain","name":"Jairain","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11567_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"345"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-15T09:19:53-08:00","id":1003434,"title":"","content":"Wait wait wait, why did we leave already? There are still 23 bandits! And we never trained the villagers to defend themselves! They're cooked.<br /> <br /> Yeah, like WonderandAwe I would have wanted more decision points, especially at the ending there when we abandoned the village for no good reason, and when Saiko was sent to sex us. And more socializing : describe the partying and let us talk to NPCs! It's a great way to flesh out the PC and the world he lives in.<br /> <br /> I'd actually like a retconned-in decision point whether to leave the village, train the villagers, or go kill the other 23 bandits.<br /> <br /> I am bad at writing compliments but I liked it overall. Looking forward to the next post. Please keep writing!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">She/Her. I am literal-minded and write literally. If I don't say something explicitly, please never assume I implied it. The only exception is if I try to make a joke.</span></span></span></div> <span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">My point of view may be different from yours but is equally valid.</span></span></span><br /> <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1x1ycoxJF0G9niMt5D3Pp7owZ7tw8AJvL?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">Exalted-cWoD-ArM url mega-library</a>. Exalted <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/663334-random-name-generator\" target=\"_blank\">name-generators</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/896-erinys","name":"Erinys","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar896_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4235"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-15T10:50:40-08:00","id":1003482,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Erinys</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1003434#post1003434\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Wait wait wait, why did we leave already? There are still 23 bandits! And we never trained the villagers to defend themselves! They're cooked.<br /> <br /> Yeah, like WonderandAwe I would have wanted more decision points, especially at the ending there when we abandoned the village for no good reason, and when Saiko was sent to sex us. And more socializing : describe the partying and let us talk to NPCs! It's a great way to flesh out the PC and the world he lives in.<br /> <br /> I'd actually like a retconned-in decision point whether to leave the village, train the villagers, or go kill the other 23 bandits.<br /> <br /> I am bad at writing compliments but I liked it overall. Looking forward to the next post. Please keep writing!</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> The peasants actually can fight. They have weapons (even more now, off the bandits that were killed), and the will to use them in the defense of their livelihoods. They just weren't capable of fighting off 40 bandits, led by a trained and skilled warrior. By &quot;train&quot;, I probably should have instead used &quot;lead&quot;, or &quot;organize&quot;. You can see the second in a small part after the duel, when Kasai hastily organizes the peasants into a rough formation. Without that, they are largely a mob of individuals, and that wouldn't have been effective in the slightest vs the Oathsworn-led bandits.<br /> <br /> For the bandits: we killed their leader, and a significant portion of their fighters. While they still have a (slight. When push comes to shove, there would be about 25-35 peasants capable, and more importantly, willing, to fight for themselves) numbers advantage, they are now disorganized and disheartened, and fighting a foe that is armed and willing to face them.<br /> <br /> You have to remember the bandits MO: they are out for &quot;loot&quot; (usually in the form of food and other supplies. They aren't raiding and extorting villages only for the fun of it), full-stop, and while they are willing to fight for it if need be, if they can avoid bleeding for their bread, they will do do. Hence, extorting villages and holding up isolated caravans, not attacking them. Most bandit groups don't have either the size nor the leadership of this one, and they usually hit their targets and move on to the next one, to avoid reprisals from both the peasants or the feudal authority.<br /> <br /> TL;DR, the remaining bandits are not all that willing to actually go fight the peasants now, as they lack force-of-numbers and effective leadership. Also, the peasants can fight (they have driven off smaller, less-organized groups of bandits before), they just lacked the necessary &quot;backbone&quot; to stand up in this case.<br /> <br /> As for the lack of decision points: I do apologize, and I do realize that I almost certainly could have thrown another one in there, probably with Saiko, but I wrote that whole bit at, like, 3 am.<br /> <br /> As for Kasai leaving ...... as far as he is concerned, his &quot;job&quot; there is done. The bandits are broken, the peasants grew a spine, and he has bigger fish to fry, as well as something to do, further downriver. While sticking around and sucking up the goodwill of the peasants would be pleasant, he has got stuff to do, plus it would tick off the lord of these lands, who we have been told is not very nice. He is looking to avoid causing trouble.<br /> <br /> In the next chapter, I will explore the society, culture and what-have-you of the Hundred Kingdoms a wee bit more, and with that, go into some greater depth with our characters. How does on decision-point per post sound?<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11892,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","user":"Boston123","date":". 11-15-2016, 01:54 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-15T11:17:35-08:00","id":1003495,"title":"","content":"There is a topic about quests on the forum - <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/874109-how-the-sausage-is-made-the-quest-game-discussion-thread\">http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/m...cussion-thread</a><br /> <br /> I was thinking back to my own quest and considered all the decision points I use. I am all about moral/character defining decision points.<br /> <br /> 1. Climax of a chapter should always the most frequent decision points. I always have one climax decision point in mind when I am writing a chapter. In Chapter five, it was whether the quest character would keep his agreement with the Fair Folk. In Chapter Six, it was what the quest character should sacrifice for their sorcery trials.<br /> <br /> 2. Combat decision points. Violence in Hero is not taken lightly. I let my players decide if they want to join combat or, if there is a surprise attack, I let them decide how to react to it. Later, when Svante is older, they will have to decide whether to spare a combatant or kill them. I also give them decision points over the goals of combat.<br /> <br /> 3. Info Dump decision points. After a long explanation, I usually have some &quot;not really important&quot; decision point to give the players a break and to see if they understood the info dump. This also give my players a chance to catch me in any plot holes.<br /> <br /> 4. Character Introduction decision points. I try to have a decision point after I introduce a new and important character so the players can define their relationship to that character. I just introduced a Neomah and am trying to gauge how the players are reacting to her.<br /> <br /> Though for some characters, I heavily influence the PC's relationship with the NPC. A lot of the players have strong opinions about Chejop Kejak due to OOC knowledge. Thus I declared the relationship as a tense mentor student one. As the game goes on, I am letting up on the control and letting the players decide how to proceed with Kejak.<br /> <br /> <br /> There are also things that should NOT be decision points.<br /> <br /> The Decision point to avoid plot. Early on, I made the mistake of giving the players decision points that basically avoided plot. Chapter three was suppose to be the &quot;Svante runs wild around Yu-Shan&quot; plot. I seeded that plot among the other decision points....and the players choose to do something else. I felt stupid afterwards for even offering that choice.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I write things.","edited":{"userid":3950,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3950-wonderandawe","user":"wonderandawe","date":". 11-15-2016, 02:24 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3950-wonderandawe","name":"wonderandawe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3950_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5410"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-16T16:40:07-08:00","id":1004159,"title":"","content":"<div align=\"center\">Chapter 2<br /> <br /> <br /> 28th Descending Wood, western Hundred Kingdoms<br /> \u200b</div> <br /> Only a few days after leaving the nameless village, you and your new page start heading down out of the hills and into the broad valley of the Red River, the breadbasket of the Hundred Kingdoms. The smaller river, which before was fast-rushing and swollen by rains, slows and widens, until it reaches over 100 feet wide as it passes through fields and forests bursting with fresh growth. The peasants trail also widens and smoothens, mimicking the river, and you start to pass signs of human habitation. Lone farms at first, then larger villages, dwarfing the one you earlier left, always clustered on the banks of the river.<br /> <br /> For Saiko, who never truly left her home village before, these little farming hamlets are bustling with people and activity, and even for you, used to the solitude and quiet of the highlands, are a bit overwhelmed with the noise and rush of the new years planting, although you try to hide it from Saiko, to avoid appearing provincial. <br /> <br /> Provisioned as you are, you are able to avoid staying in these villages for too long, and make good time, even if you have to train Saiko in the use and care of arms as you travel. Physical training, endurance-building, not to mention sparring with glaive and dagger, there are very few nights that the girl doesn't fall asleep covered in bruises. By far your favorite training regimen (and her most hated), is &quot;those stupid buckets&quot;, as she calls them, where, after obtaining two 5-gallon buckets from a farmer in return for some of your provisions, Saiko gets to fill each bucket up with river water, then suspend them from each end of the glaive-shaft, then run ahead down the trail, in order to build up coordination, balance and strength. She had yet to complain about any of this, however, which is a sign of her character and reasonably impressive. You remember, with a not-insignificant twinge of embarrassment, that when you started as a page to your father, you complained about seemingly every task. Granted, you were <i>seven</i>, but .......... you would rather her not discover this, it might go to her head!<br /> <br /> Still, you follow the river (which, you learn to be named the Tumblestone) downstream, through fields of rapidly-growing wheat, corn, potatoes and other produce, and by pastures of green spring grass where sheep, cattle, and occasionally, one of the famed warhorses that are the pride of the Oathsworn nobility, graze. The land here is incredibly rich, like much of the East, but troubled as well, as the occasional burned out farm, in overgrown ruins off the road, or the fortified manors of the landed noble-clans, frowning from the hillsides, bears witness. You pass as quickly as possible through the lowland forests of bamboo and oak, elm and willow, as even this close to the population centers, you can still see the telltale signs of bandits and other desperate men.<br /> <br /> Finally, you crest over a ridge and catch sight of the great castle of Teriake, home to the ruler of the domain of Xia, Lord Iysen, head of the Shiba clan. Even from this distance, the high walls and towers of stone are impressive, and they rise over the castle-town pinched between the knob the castle is built on and the river. The roofs of said castle-town shine gold in the late-afternoon sun, and you have to remind yourself that the gates close at sundown, and if you want to sleep in a bed tonight, you had better get a move on. You start down the road, only to stop and look back at Saiko, who is staring at the expanse, mouth agape.<br /> <br /> &quot;Come along, girl. The town gates close at sundown, and we are cutting it close as it is. I don't know about you, but I definitely want to sleep in a real bed tonight.&quot; You say, half-jokingly, startling her out of her reverie.<br /> <br /> She shakes her head, clearing away the daydream, &quot; It's.... so big, even bigger than the mountains back home. Did you live in a castle like that, Lord Kasai?&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;No, my family lived in a manor high in the mountains, not much bigger than your village&quot;, you admit. &quot; We were rather poor, by the standards of the nobility, and my father's land grew mostly rocks. It was one of the reasons I left to pursue my own fortunes, and my father almost bankrupted himself purchasing my equipment as it was&quot;.<br /> <br /> She frowns, &quot; Well, this is all very impressive, but, if I may ask, what exactly are we going to do here? There can't be too many bandits under the nose of the lord&quot;.<br /> <br /> &quot;You would be surprised&quot;, you chuckle, starting again down the road. &quot; Besides, do you see that open field before the gates of the town, the one with all the tents?&quot;<br /> <br /> She has to hold her hand up to her brow against the sun, another reminder that you have to pick up the pace. &quot;Yeah?&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot; Well, two days from now, Lord Iysen will be hosting a tournament, held in honor of the marriage of her eldest daughter to the son of the Lord of Yue. At said tournament, there will be plenty of contests of arms, and at riding, and other things. The winner of the whole tournament gets offered a position as a part of Lord Iysen's household, as a retainer.&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;Do... do you expect to win?&quot; Saiko asks, thoughtful, only to be surprised by you laughing.<br /> <br /> &quot; <i>Gods, no</i>. Anyone who is anyone is going to be competing in that tournament. The most I am hoping for is to win, or at least score high, in one of the secondary contests, earn some cash and get my name out there. Anything else is just wishful thinking on my part&quot;.<br /> <br /> &quot; Well, that certainly isn't the right outlook to have! You've got to reach higher, Lord Kasai, or you are going to wander around, helping us dirty peasants and sleeping on the ground for the rest of your life!&quot; she shouts, impetuously.<br /> <br /> You chuckle again, in spite of her impertinence, &quot; Your optimism and goodwill are certainly welcome, Saiko, but I am trying to be a realist,here. Besides, are you sure you aren't just saying all that because you get to share in my success?&quot;.<br /> <br /> She looks thoughtful at that last comment, only to be startled by your hand thrust in front of her face. &quot; Come along, I wasn't joking about the gates closing before nightfall, and at the rate you walk, we aren't going to make it.&quot;<br /> <br /> She looks uncomprehendingly at your outstretched hand, and you sigh and lift her up into the saddle in front of you, ignoring her startled squawk. With her sitting sidesaddle (and holding on to you for dear life), you are now able to pick up the pace considerably, nudging Xuansu into a mild trot, and you are able to make it down the ridge and to the gate as the sun starts touching the horizon.<br /> <br /> This close to the gate, multiple roads and trails converge into one, funneling masses of humanity towards the gate, and you are soon surrounded on all sides by pressed bodies. Xuansu snorts and stamps at the press, and you direct him forwards, parting the seas in order to avoid someone getting too close for his comfort and getting kicked in the head for their trouble. After passing through the gate and into the castle-town, the press spreads out and proceeds to their own destinations, and you lift Saiko down from the saddle. She stumbles, cramping.<br /> <br /> &quot; I <i>really</i> don't know how you can ride that horse all day.&quot;<br /> <br /> You shrug,&quot; Used to it, I guess. Have you never ridden a horse, before, or a donkey or a mule&quot;?<br /> <br /> &quot; I haven't ridden <i>anything</i> before, so warn me next time, so I don't freak out and fall off!&quot; She groused, rubbing at the kinks<br /> <br /> You smirk, then nudge Xuansu on. &quot; Come along, let us find a hostel for the next few days&quot;.<br /> <br /> You ride on through the town, Saiko hot on your heels, passing by streetfront homes, shops, workshops and plazas. All the &quot;streets&quot; are set at odd angles to each other, zig-zagging back and forth as opposed to a grid pattern, supposedly to aid in defense, but to you it seems more annoying than anything else. Along the way, you pass by citizens getting their work done for the day, and can't help but hear their whispered remarks and feel their stares, aimed at your hair and eyes. The sheathed sword at your side, however, ensures that the whispers and stares remain quiet, and they avert their gaze then you look at them. Saiko tugs at your sleeve.<br /> <br /> &quot; Can't you hear them, Lord Kasai&quot;?<br /> <br /> You sigh, &quot; I can, but nothing is going to come of their whispers, and calling them out on it will just reinforce whatever negative opinions they hold. They are just small-minded small folk, Saiko, nothing more. 'Water over a duck's back', and all that. Let them talk, it doesn't bother me, not any more&quot;.<br /> <br /> And so the two of you pass onwards, you in silence and Saiko shooting glares at the whispers, until you come up to one of the smaller plazas that sit at intersecting streets. There, you hear a commotion and see a gathered crowd, shouting and shoving at each other, gawking at whatever is occurring in the center of the group.<br /> <br /> Frowning, you dismount Xuansu, passing the reins to Saiko, and approach the encircled group. As you push and elbow your way to the center, you finally catch a glimpse of what is causing all the ruckus.<br /> <br /> A woman, looking rather poor and bedraggled, is lying curled up on the ground, hands arms wrapped around her head in vain effort to protect herself from a fusillade of kicks and staff-blows directed at her by a figure wearing the robes of an Immaculate Monk. The monk is screaming a tirade of Immaculate Dogma at the woman, screeching of &quot;HERESY!' and &quot; ICONOGRAPHY!, and, as you watch, he leans back and drives a hard foot into the pit of her stomach. Scattered around is a series of cloth puppets, a bowlful of scattered coins, several weeping, frightened children, and the tipped-over remains of a crude stand. It is rather obvious what has happened here.<br /> <br /> Several of the closer members of the crowd are muttering darkly, although in support of the monk or the woman, you aren't quite sure. Regardless, the monk is really laying into the woman, who starts sobbing under the onslaught of kicks and curses.<br /> <br /> <b><i><u>YOU</u></i></b>:<br /> 1) Meet force with force. He is <u>really</u> laying into the poor woman, if you don't do something right now, she could get seriously hurt!<br /> 2) Meet force with words. Maybe you can calm the situation down, give the puppeteer a chance to get out of kick-range.<br /> 3) Call the militia! Keeping the peace is, after all, their job<br /> 4) Nothing. You are technically an Immaculate, after all, being baptized into the faith, and from what you can see, the monk .... isn't wrong. The woman is technically guilty of iconography, a heresy, Though this level of reaction is ........ rather unwarranted. Besides, laying hands on a monk is socially unacceptable.<br /> 5) Something else?<br /> <br /> <u>OOC</u><br /> <br /> I really don't know what happened to the formatting at the start of each paragraph, here, and it is driving me insane.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-16T19:36:48-08:00","id":1004208,"title":"","content":"4) Nothing. You are technically an Immaculate, after all, being baptized into the faith, and from what you can see, the monk .... isn't wrong. The woman is technically guilty of iconography, a heresy, Though this level of reaction is ........ rather unwarranted. Besides, laying hands on a monk is socially unacceptable.<br /> 5) <b>Something else</b>? Whenever the monk finally stops, disperse the crowd and help the woman to get moving. She has children to feed, apparently.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Like my Avatar? Courtesy of<span style=\"color:#0000FF\"> Jen</span>! : <span style=\"font-size:12px\">Anybody want their characters to be experimented on ? </span>post 98<br /> <i>An Exalt is never unarmed.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4018-l-het-esh","name":"L'het'esh","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4018_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"521"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-16T20:25:21-08:00","id":1004219,"title":"","content":"2. Meet force with words. <br /> <br /> This is conduct unbecoming of a monk. Beating people to death in the streets won't bring them to the faith.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>On the frontier of the Wild South, there's only one woman with the grit to take on its most dangerous outlaws and bring them <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/984977-back-alive-or-maybe-dead-quest\" target=\"_blank\">Back Alive, or Maybe Dead.</a><br /> <br /> Avatar by <a href=\"http://puzzlinghappenstance.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">K.S. Brenowitz</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/908-semicasual","name":"semicasual","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar908_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2499"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-17T04:02:39-08:00","id":1004325,"title":"","content":"A couple of quick things:<br /> <br /> 1) The kids aren't hers.<br /> <br /> They are the children of the citizens who were finishing up their shopping, and stopped to watch the nice woman put on a puppet show. They are crying because they just saw a scary man start screaming, tip over the woman's stand, and start beating on her in public.<br /> <br /> 2) A bit about religion.<br /> <br /> Generally, in my Hundred Kingdoms games, if someone has a generally-East Asian name, they should be considered to be Immaculate followers, if only nominally ( Having a name in what I generally consider High/Low Realm / Dragontongue, means they were baptized as Immaculates, which in my games is not! Christianity meets not! Buddhism/ Confucianism). Likewise, if someone has a descriptive name (Ten-Sheaves, for example), they should be considered followers of the native gods (not! Shinto, basically), or other folk religions (not! tribal animism).<br /> <br /> However, just because someone was baptized into the Immaculate Faith doesn't mean they are particularly devout. Even the Oathsworn, who all-but have to be Immaculates (there are certain rites and rituals that involve the Immaculate Philosophy, much like Knighthood involved Christian rituals) , can range from die-hard believers to ,&quot; Meh&quot;. I personally view Kasai as the latter. He views his Oaths as being more important.<br /> <br /> Though, as always, we will run with whatever gets chosen. If you guys want Kasai to be a die-hard Immaculate, then he will be a die-hard Immaculate.<br /> <br /> 3) &quot;Monk&quot;.<br /> <br /> He isn't so much a &quot;regular&quot; Immaculate monk, as he is an ascetic. One of those fundamentalists that go and seclude themselves on the top of mountains, or, in modern terms, a fire-and-brimstone &quot;we are all going to hell&quot; knight-templar preachers. I was going to touch on this more in the next post, but I want to &quot;clear the air&quot; if it makes any decision easier.<br /> <br /> For example, he has the pentacle of the Immaculate Faith <u>burned</u> into his forehead, something he did himself and for which he feels quite a bit of pride, when he can feel anything other than pure, burning fanaticism and not-quite hatred for anything reminding him of his own mortality.<br /> <br /> <b>He is not a nice guy</b>. The local abbot, when he hears about this, is likely going to facepalm so hard he pops his own eyeballs out.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-17T04:49:31-08:00","id":1004334,"title":"","content":"All well and good, but I'm sticking to &quot;Meet force with words.&quot;<br /> <br /> If nothing else, we may distract the raging firebrand.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>On the frontier of the Wild South, there's only one woman with the grit to take on its most dangerous outlaws and bring them <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/984977-back-alive-or-maybe-dead-quest\" target=\"_blank\">Back Alive, or Maybe Dead.</a><br /> <br /> Avatar by <a href=\"http://puzzlinghappenstance.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">K.S. Brenowitz</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/908-semicasual","name":"semicasual","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar908_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2499"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-17T05:01:07-08:00","id":1004338,"title":"","content":"Meet force with words. <br /> <br /> I'm going to stick with the silver tongue option.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I write things.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3950-wonderandawe","name":"wonderandawe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3950_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5410"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-17T13:45:54-08:00","id":1004605,"title":"","content":"Attacking a monk will not do us well, and he is unlikely to listen to words. Call the militia!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1311230-badassoverlord-s-assorted-homebrew-folder\" target=\"_blank\">Are you in the market for some Martial Arts? Perhaps some custom Artifacts for your campaign?</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10131-badassoverlord","name":"BadassOverlord","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar10131_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"322"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-17T21:40:11-08:00","id":1004806,"title":"","content":"Silver tongue approach I think.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11567-jairain","name":"Jairain","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11567_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"345"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-18T11:15:40-08:00","id":1005036,"title":"","content":"This is conduct unbecoming of a monk.<br /> <br /> Start with words. If that doesn't work immediately, call the militia.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">She/Her. I am literal-minded and write literally. If I don't say something explicitly, please never assume I implied it. The only exception is if I try to make a joke.</span></span></span></div> <span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">My point of view may be different from yours but is equally valid.</span></span></span><br /> <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1x1ycoxJF0G9niMt5D3Pp7owZ7tw8AJvL?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">Exalted-cWoD-ArM url mega-library</a>. Exalted <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/663334-random-name-generator\" target=\"_blank\">name-generators</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/896-erinys","name":"Erinys","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar896_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4235"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-21T17:44:02-08:00","id":1006484,"title":"","content":"Sorry about the wait, guys!<br /> <br /> I was camping all weekend, and I won't be <i>back-back</i> until later on tonight.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-24T00:48:29-08:00","id":1007608,"title":"","content":"IT'S ALLLIIIIIIIIIIIIVEEEEEEEEEEEEEE<br /> <br /> -ahem-<br /> <br /> <br /> So, &quot;force vs words&quot; it is, with a slight hint of &quot;call the militia&quot;, seasoned to taste.<br /> <br /> .....<br /> <br /> Frowning at the display, you look to Saiko, who is watching the spectacle with a grimace.<br /> <br /> &quot;Quickly, girl, mount Xuansu and go find a militia patrol. We passed one back at the last plaza. Tell them to come here with all haste.&quot;<br /> <br /> She looks at you, wide-eyed, &quot;Wh- what are you going to do, Lord&quot;?<br /> <br /> You stride forward,&quot; I am going to make sure the priest doesn't kill the woman, for a start.. The militia will break up any quarrelsome townsfolk. I need them here in case this gets violent.&quot;<br /> <br /> She hesitates, &quot; Don't you want me here, in that case?&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;No, having the militia here would make the odds more even, as opposed to two versus fifty. Quickly, Saiko!&quot;. With that, she hauls herself awkwardly up into the saddle and sets off back down the road. You continue towards the crowd, cursing under your breath.<br /> <br /> She headed off none too soon. The crowd, agitated to a fever pitch by the tirade of the priest and the cries of the puppeteer, are milling about anxiously, with some of the angrier townsfolk picking up chunks of firewood and pushing towards the two figures in the center. You elbow your way through the gathered bodies, some people starting to complain, but silenced by your wrathful expression and the sword at your waist (more likely the latter). The Immaculate Monk has worked himself into a full-blown fury, all-but frothing at the mouth, bending down to scream at the woman, inches away from her face. She sobs in response, until the monk rears back and drives the heel of his foot full into her face, breaking her nose with a <i>crack</i> that can be heard even over the angry shouts of the crowd. Stunned, she drops back into the dust, blood pulsing from her face, and the monk turns to address the crowd, which had been stunned into silence by the sudden brutality of the blow. You take the opportunity to push into the open center of the crowd which closes in behind you, sealing off all retreat, and raise yourself up to your full height.<br /> <br /> &quot;Tell me, preacher, is this how we bring new followers to the faith? By beating them bloody in the marketplace for minor transgressions? Now, I might not be a monk, or even truly a devout follower of the Immaculate faith, but to me it seems like there must be a <i>much </i> better way&quot;, you say, in a voice that is calm and level, with only the set of your jaw and the fire in your eyes revealing your inner feelings.<br /> <br /> &quot;Pah, merely educating this <i>wretch</i> in the proper displays of faith! She had the <i>nerve</i> to display the figure of one of the Most Enlighted Dragonblooded and the Anathema in public, <i>for money</i>!&quot; The Monk rants, lifting his foot to kick again. Quickly, you place yourself in between the monk and the woman, preventing his kick from connecting.<br /> <br /> &quot;It was a godsdamned <i>kids show, </i>you crazy bastard!' replies the woman, voice hoarse with pain, who has sat up and is attempting (and failing) to stem the tide of blood from her nose.<br /> <br /> You spread out your hands, indicating the crowd and the mess caused by the monks &quot;education&quot;. &quot; Surely, Enlightened Father, this mild transgression did not warrant quite this level of a response? A discussion of the tenets of the faith, or even a penance? Not a <i>beating</i>. This is not the conduct of a monk. Are you not supposed to be a man of peace, of progressive enlightenment and meditation?&quot;<br /> <br /> The firebrand snorts, &quot; The Immaculate Faith is beset by foes on all sides. Anathema, the Hundred Gods Heresy, the degradation of moral and spiritual values.&quot; He turns to address the crowd ,&quot; Because of the focus on meditation, peaceful and quiet enlightenment, that the faith has been allowed to fall for so long! We are as mice, and we must be as <i>LIONS</i>! We must root out any threat to the faith, wherever it may hide, with fire and sword! FIRE AND SWORD!<i>&quot; </i>He turns to address you,<br /> <br /> &quot; So, no, Oathsworn, I am not a man of peace, not when my faith, the one true one, needs, no, <i>demands<u>,</u></i>, my righteous anger! For too long has this filth, this <i>HERESY</i>, been tolerated! Words and chants can only be so effective, only for so long, before we mortals seek to subvert any messages the faith tries to purport. Look! Look at what this woman has done! She has reduced the figure, the very purpose, of our Dragon-Blooded saviors to a public mockery, for children and prostitutes to <i>gawk</i> at and throw coins! Such ignorance, such disrespect, <i>cannot</i> go uncorrected!&quot;<br /> <br /> He breathes, for seemingly the first time of his rant, and gestures at the sword at your waist ,&quot; Besides, complaining to me of 'peace&quot; is awfully ignorant of <i>your</i> purpose, Oathsworn. Were you not pledged to be a defender of the faith, to uphold the tenets of the Immaculate faith and to live your life by its creed? By those standards, by the Oath you swore, you <i>are</i> a sword, you <i>are</i> of violence, and you <i>should</i> be up here assisting me in administering righteous punishment for this disgusting act of profligacy!&quot;,<br /> <br /> You frown,&quot; Do <i>not</i> presume, monk, to speak to me of my Oaths. I swore them, not you, and I understand their demands with <i>much</i> greater clarity than you do.&quot; You bend down, pulling a swatch of cloth from your belt and passing it to the woman, an impromptu handkerchief. &quot; Yes, I swore to defend and uphold the Immaculate faith ...... but I <i>also</i> swore to defend the weak, help the helpless, and uphold the small. I am doing that now. Some Oaths take precedence. And, <i>yet again</i>, this woman was just trying to earn a few coins for her daily bread. Such a response is, yet again and as always, <i>completely </i><i>unnecessary&quot;.</i><br /> <br /> At the mention of money, the monk sneers. &quot; Ah, yes. <i>Money</i>. The root of all evil! Every mortal is in pursuit of wealth of some kind, be it gold, food or pleasure! This desire, this <i>LUST</i>, distracts us from realizing the <i>true purpose</i> of our existence! We MORTALS, BLINDED and WRITHING in the DUNG OF OUR OWN MORTALITY, cannot see that life is pain, is suffering, and it is only through realizing the fragility of our existence that we become Enlightened. If we had no .....&quot; He trails off and stares at you, as if noticing you for the first time. He raises his hand, fingers trembling, to point at your face, the clear indicators of your ancestry. With a shaking voice, he shouts to the crowd ;<br /> <br /> &quot; <i>Anathema</i>!&quot;<br /> <br /> .........<br /> <br /> &quot;...... what.&quot;<br /> <br /> The bemused statement is all you can muster. You have heard some insults in your time amidst the Lowlanders, but nobody has had the stones to directly compare you to a <i>demon</i> before. At least, not to your face, or in public. <br /> <br /> The firebrand continues to point, distracted entirely from his earlier point of hatred. He backs away, head twisting to address the crowd, who have backed away in fear, confusion, or both.<br /> <br /> &quot; You carry the mark of the Anathema, the ones that come from beyond the boundaries of the world! The Corruption, the Twister, that which turns the mind and body away from the Dragons and to the purposes of evil! &quot; He makes the sign against the evil eye, twisting his finger beside his temple in that age-old symbol. &quot; You cannot be trusted! Your words drip venom, poison, and you seek to turn me against the Faith, against the Dragons! You try to draw me to the bosom of the Anathema! I reject you! I reject you and your <i>lies!&quot; </i> He spits on the ground.<br /> <br /> This is ...... almost funny, in some deeply ironic fashion. You hook your hands under your sash, exhale in a loud sigh, then start to talk to the monk,&quot; Listen, preacher, this is all well and good, and you can speak to the crowd of the futility of existence at your leisure, but I will not let you beat on this woman any lon...&quot;<br /> <br /> He spits in your face. <br /> <br /> Mind blank, you blink once, twice, three times.<br /> <br /> It feels like the world has come to a stop, then sudden fire burns through your body, and you rip your sword from its sheath and sweep it aloft, all form and composure lost in sudden, blinding fury. The surrounding crowd scrambles back, throwing themselves out of range of the blow. Some people scream at the impending bloodshed.<br /> <br /> &quot;<b><i>What the everloving FUCK is going on here!</i></b>&quot;<br /> <br /> The shout, resounding off the surrounding buildings, cuts through the rage clouding your mind, and is the only thing that prevents you from sweeping your blade down and wiping that smug look off the monks face. Your entire body trembles, withholding the force and desire to cut. The monk smirks, either unmindful or unaware of his close brush with reincarnation.<br /> <br /> &quot;Look, see how the Anathema-spawns <i>true</i> nature shows itself, prevented from cutting down a holy man of the faith by the command of the terrestrial authority!. It is only barely in control of its bestial desires!&quot; He turns to address the crowd. &quot; This is why, people, we must be willing to combat evil, whatever it's form! This spawn of evil is allowed to walk among us true, honest folk, and subvert us with it's lies!&quot; He turns, as the crowd is broken up by burly men with spears and clubs, who shove and push aside the gathered onlookers. The sergeant in command of the patrol strides forward, along with Saiko leading Xuansu.<br /> <br /> The sergeant bawls again, &quot;<i>Somebody better start talking, or by the gods I'll haul everyone here to gaol!&quot; </i> Saiko steps forwards and takes ahold of your arms, whispering in your ear.<br /> <br /> &quot;Lord Kasai, <i>please</i> lower the sword and talk to the nice militia officer.&quot; You snarl in response, lips pulled back from the teeth and eyes fixated on the monk. If you could start a fire with your glare, the monk would be on fire, hot enough to crack the flagstones of the street.<br /> <br /> &quot;<i>Kasai&quot;, </i> she pleads, finally pulling your eyes away from the monk. After a moment of eye contact, you suddenly relax, dropping your sword to your side, drained of all energy. All of your muscles are suddenly stiff and sore, and you realize you have been tense like a drawn bow for the last few seconds. You pant, barely able to remain standing, much less answer the sergeant. <br /> <br /> &quot; I was putting on a show, when this <i>monk</i> attacked me for &quot;heresy&quot;. He started beating the crap outta me, and only the intervention of the Oathsworn here prevented me from getting seriously injured.&quot; the woman interjects. &quot; Well, <i>more</i> seriously injured.&quot; She says, muffled by the kerchief. &quot; Everything was going as smooth as silk up until the monk spit in his face. Regardless, if it weren't for his Lordship, here.&quot; She bows painfully. &quot; I probably would have been dead.&quot;<br /> <br /> The sergeant huffs,&quot; You got a permit for that stand?&quot;<br /> <br /> She stares at him flatly, &quot;:....... nope.&quot;<br /> <br /> The sergeant sighs and rolls his eyes,&quot; Normally, I'd collar you, but in this case I think you learned enough.&quot; She acquiesces, starting to pick up the remnants of her overturned and broken stand.. With that, the sergeant turns to the monk, still standing there with a look of smug self-satisfaction on his face. Said look is suddenly wiped away by the blunt end of a baton being driven into his stomach.<br /> <br /> &quot;<i>That</i> is for assaulting two different people, as well as almost causing a riot&quot;.<br /> <br /> &quot;I .....-wheeze- I was preaching!&quot; complains the monk from the ground.<br /> <br /> &quot; Preaching is acceptable. Causing a public disturbance, interrupting the flow of traffic, and almost causing a riot is not.&quot; He jerks his chin at two of his men. &quot; Boys, bring this rabble-rouser down to the Temple, tell the abbot what happened. If he wants a written report with my signature, he can pick it up tomorrow morning.&quot; <br /> <br /> The sergeant turns to the still-sizable crowd. &quot; What the hell are you are all standing around for?! Show's over, go back to your own damn business!&quot; With that, he turns back to you, where Saiko is busy wiping the spittle from your face. Much more respectfully, &quot; My lord, do you mind coming down to the gaol, in order to give a statement?&quot;<br /> <br /> You shrug. You just want this over and done with, so you can find a hostel and a bath, some food, and a bed. Suddenly bone-tired, weary beyond all measure, you allow Saiko to lead you, mounted on Xuansu, down to the gaol. Giving a description of the events and getting them transcribed take a couple of hours, and it is well-past sundown when you finally make it to the hostel (well-recommended by the sergeant, and you are pretty sure you got a reduced rate as a result, by far the only good thing to happen today.) There, after Xuansu is stabled, food is brought and a bath is drawn, you are relaxing in your rented room when there is a knock at the door. Saiko answers it, to see one of the hostel staff. They explain that they have a visitor asking for you downstairs, and if you would like for them to be sent up. You agree, and a few moments later, the woman from earlier, looking rather worse for wear, comes up. <br /> <br /> She enters the room, then slowly and painfully, bows formally, forehead to the floorboards, waiting for your permission to rise. At your gesture, she kneels, then ,<br /> <br /> &quot; I just came by to thank your lordship for helping me. If it weren't for you, I would be injured far worse, or even dead&quot;. You acknowledge this with a nod.<br /> <br /> &quot;....... and, if I may, without being too forward, I would like to ask your lordship for a boon.&quot;<br /> <br /> You sigh. &quot; What is it?&quot;<br /> <br /> She hesitates, then, &quot; I wish to follow you, serve as your retainer.&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;....Why?&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot; Well, you are the first figure of authority I've met that has actually tried to do right by me. From my sergeants and commanders in the army, to the militia here in town, everyone so far has been out for themselves first. Follow the 'letter of the law&quot; first, and the actual people second. Meeting someone that actually sticks to their word is ...... impressive, if that makes sense. Much less someone that sticks to their word, even then they are inconvenienced by it.&quot; Again, she hesitates. &quot; And ..... to be honest, I don't exactly have anything else to do or anywhere to go. After I got discharged from the army, I got stuck here and I've been slowly but surely getting hungrier and poorer ever since. Getting my face fixed up used up all the money I had left, so I will be left to begging if I stay in town.<br /> <br /> &quot;You were in the army?&quot;<br /> <br /> She nods,&quot; I was a crossbowman, under Lord Iysen. With her daughter getting married to another noble, she suddenly didn't need so many soldiers. I got mustered out with nothing more than 3 months of levy-pay and the clothes I had on my back. That money disappeared pretty quickly, and I have been reduced to odd-jobs and street-work.&quot;<br /> <br /> She sighs, then, &quot; I can fight, not that your follower here can't, but I actually saw battle, &quot;in the shit&quot; as we called it, and I can use the backsword and the crossbow equally well. Actually won the company marksman contest 3 weeks running, and take a rabbit on the run at 50 paces. I can march for days with a load on my back, set up and tear down camp, as well as care for horses and armor. And.&quot; She admits,&quot; Not to make it seem like I am trying to bribe you, your lordship, but if you take me, I've got a little nugget of information I found out a couple of weeks ago that you might find interesting, help you climb the social ladder as it were. If you take me, I'll tell you.&quot;<br /> <br /> You ponder the decision, resting your cheek on your fist. On the one hand, building up a retinue of loyal followers and retainers is part of an Oathsworn's success. From men-at-arms, to grooms and valet's, to pages and such, having a retinue would mean you would have to spend less time on <i>&quot;preparing&quot;</i> to do something, (caring for Xuansu, maintaining arms and armor, writing letters, buying provisions, carrying things, etc), and more time actually &quot;doing&quot; things, such as performing great deeds and getting your name known. On the other hand ........ keeping a retinue is <u>expensive</u>. In just food cost alone, your expenses have doubled since picking up Saiko. Now, if you were to take this woman as your retainer, she would need arms and armor, equipment of her own. The <u>only</u> reason you could afford to take Saiko along with you is she could kinda-sorta fit/use some of the Oathsworn-bandit's gear. And you don't really know this woman from a person in the street. <br /> <br /> On the third hand, between the gear either you or Saiko couldn't use, and the possibility of winning <i>something</i> in the tournament in a couple of days, you could be set for a while, money-wise. Enough to start building up a reputation <u>and</u> a retinue, which is the separation between being a &quot;swallow-sworn&quot; and a &quot;proper&quot; Oathsworn warrior.<br /> <br /> You straighten,&quot; Before I come to a decision, I need to know your name. Calling Saiko &quot;girl&quot; is starting to wear thin, and I don't think I could go through that again&quot; Saiko snickered in response.<br /> <br /> She nods,&quot; My name is Blue Pines, your lordship&quot;.<br /> <br /> You nod, then:<br /> <br /> 1) Accept her oath of service. Why not? Besides, her information could be useful.<br /> 2) Deny her. You don't &quot;need&quot; another retainer, not now<br /> 3) Tell her to come talk to you after the tournament. This is a big decision to make on such a short notice.<br /> 4) Something else?<br /> <br /> OOC:<br /> <br /> Technically, if Kasai felt he was sufficiently disrespected or offended (and he was, by the end), he could have turned to any of his hecklers and cut them down. He wouldn't have gotten in any particular trouble for it, legally or socially, (more of a wag of the finger), as long as he was insulted <u>before</u> getting all stabby. Technically, if the two parties are alike in social rank, there is supposed to be a dueling process that takes place, with formal challenges, meeting places, and conditions, but if some peasant in the street got uppity, he could respond via sword to the tender bits. But, as we have seen, Kasai tends to ignore most insults, so long as they aren't blatant. Getting spat on in the face was <u>very</u> blatant, and the monk could get in some pretty severe trouble for it.<br /> <br /> The crowd is complicated. On one hand, they were <u>definitely</u> not in favor of the monk beating the ever-loving shit out of Blue Pines, even the Immaculate-fearing townsfolk. On the other hand, they were not too fond of Kasai, being a Wyld mutant-descendant and all. On the other-other hand, Kasai made a good impression on many of the rubberneckers, stepping forwards to help Ten-Pines like that. Oathsworn that actually uphold their Oaths to defend the smallfolk are ...... rare. Think &quot;Dunk and Egg&quot; by GRRM, if anybody is familiar with that. <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-24T10:35:49-08:00","id":1007759,"title":"","content":"4. Tell her upfront that you cannot equip or pay her and will not be able to do either until you make your fortune. If she's willing to work for free, then she can come on board. <br /> <br /> Also ask if there's any money in puppeteering.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>On the frontier of the Wild South, there's only one woman with the grit to take on its most dangerous outlaws and bring them <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/984977-back-alive-or-maybe-dead-quest\" target=\"_blank\">Back Alive, or Maybe Dead.</a><br /> <br /> Avatar by <a href=\"http://puzzlinghappenstance.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">K.S. Brenowitz</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/908-semicasual","name":"semicasual","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar908_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2499"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-24T10:48:11-08:00","id":1007763,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>semicasual</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1007759#post1007759\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">4. Tell her upfront that you cannot equip or pay her and will not be able to do either until you make your fortune. If she's willing to work for free, then she can come on board. <br /> <br /> Also ask if there's any money in puppeteering.</div> </div> </div> </div>I second this.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I write things.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3950-wonderandawe","name":"wonderandawe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3950_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5410"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-24T19:31:25-08:00","id":1007909,"title":"","content":"...is this the start of a harem?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1311230-badassoverlord-s-assorted-homebrew-folder\" target=\"_blank\">Are you in the market for some Martial Arts? Perhaps some custom Artifacts for your campaign?</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10131-badassoverlord","name":"BadassOverlord","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar10131_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"322"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-24T20:40:49-08:00","id":1007926,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>BadassOverlord</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1007909#post1007909\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">...is this the start of a harem?</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Considering how Blue Pines is, like, mid-to-late 30s, probably not. Not ..... not that there is anything <i>wrong</i> with that kind of thing, preferring older women, that is.<br /> <br /> Just trying to aim for a decent mix of male and female characters. My Hundred Kingdoms characters had several women as part of the main PC's retinue, including several actual combatants. Most of the retinue Kasai could possibly recruit are based off those NPCs. Building new NPC's is a PITA, and I already have the notes, soooooooooo.<br /> <br /> I will admit it, the thought did cross my mind after writing this chapter. Not to <u>give</u> Kasai a harem, that it <u>looked</u> like I was giving Kasai a harem. I'll see who I have next on the roster, and try to make it a little less &quot;Gaddaffi's Amazon Guard&quot;.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-24T21:08:35-08:00","id":1007934,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>semicasual</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1007759#post1007759\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">4. Tell her upfront that you cannot equip or pay her and will not be able to do either until you make your fortune. If she's willing to work for free, then she can come on board.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, you'll see about food and shelter as best as you can.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Like my Avatar? Courtesy of<span style=\"color:#0000FF\"> Jen</span>! : <span style=\"font-size:12px\">Anybody want their characters to be experimented on ? </span>post 98<br /> <i>An Exalt is never unarmed.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4018-l-het-esh","name":"L'het'esh","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4018_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"521"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-24T22:07:18-08:00","id":1007947,"title":"","content":"Accept her, sounds like she can hunt for food\ud83d\ude00","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11567-jairain","name":"Jairain","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11567_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"345"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-25T04:48:25-08:00","id":1008027,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Boston123</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1007926#post1007926\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Considering how Blue Pines is, like, mid-to-late 30s, probably not.</div> </div> </div> </div>I dunno - as far as I'm aware, what distinguishes a harem from any other group of [insert desired demographic here] is a certain variety. There needs to be a range of people with differing characteristics, whose only common quality is an attachment (not necessarily romantic) to the main character. Each one needs to have a readily identifiable archetype, something that makes them uniquely appealing in comparison to their group peers.<br /> <br /> So, now we've got a Teenage Ingenue and a Mature Opportunist. That's a good start. What we need now is a hotheaded rival (red hair optional) who is close to Kasai in age and rank, an empathic wallflower who is older than Saiko but younger then Kasai, and someone kind of weird - like a talking cat who can turn into a woman or a figment of Kasai's imagination given physical form, or something. <br /> <br /> Then again, the stereotype of people between 26 and 45 in dating sims is that they're generally very attractive and yet for some reason don't have a route. So maybe I'm talking out of my hat. :P","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>On the frontier of the Wild South, there's only one woman with the grit to take on its most dangerous outlaws and bring them <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/984977-back-alive-or-maybe-dead-quest\" target=\"_blank\">Back Alive, or Maybe Dead.</a><br /> <br /> Avatar by <a href=\"http://puzzlinghappenstance.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">K.S. Brenowitz</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/908-semicasual","name":"semicasual","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar908_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2499"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-25T11:02:13-08:00","id":1008200,"title":"","content":"I'm impressed my throwaway joke sparked such thoughtful discussion. I just found the idea of it funny xD<br /> <br /> btw my vote is on bringing her aboard","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1311230-badassoverlord-s-assorted-homebrew-folder\" target=\"_blank\">Are you in the market for some Martial Arts? Perhaps some custom Artifacts for your campaign?</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10131-badassoverlord","name":"BadassOverlord","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar10131_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"322"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-30T17:12:25-08:00","id":1010412,"title":"","content":"((We don't even know if Kasai is interested in women. Or lowlanders, though he probably is that.))<br /> <br /> Admit up front that we can't afford to pay, equip, or feed a second follower right now, but that may change if we manage to win anything at a tournament side-event. She should come back to talk to us after the tourney finishes.<br /> <br /> About insults... the racism has long ago gotten real old, but if we called out or punished every racist lowlander that would be nearly every peasant we meet. High-rank people and spitting are totally different, though. There is only so much a noble can be expected to put up with from peasants.<br /> <br /> <br /> ((I appreciate that his ancestry isn't just cosmetic window-dressing, it actually influences his behavior somewhat, with the teeth-snarling like a wolf.))","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">She/Her. I am literal-minded and write literally. If I don't say something explicitly, please never assume I implied it. The only exception is if I try to make a joke.</span></span></span></div> <span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">My point of view may be different from yours but is equally valid.</span></span></span><br /> <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1x1ycoxJF0G9niMt5D3Pp7owZ7tw8AJvL?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">Exalted-cWoD-ArM url mega-library</a>. Exalted <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/663334-random-name-generator\" target=\"_blank\">name-generators</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/896-erinys","name":"Erinys","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar896_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4235"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-30T17:53:04-08:00","id":1010430,"title":"","content":"Just a couple of quick notes:<br /> <br /> 1) Racism<br /> <br /> I am attempting to quasi-accurately portray at least some of the real-life racism and discrimination that most real-world groups felt for each other. Like, picture the English and the Welsh/Irish/Scottish. They <u>hated</u> each other, considered each other downright barbaric and less-than-human at times ...... and, really often, intermarried and interbred with each other more often than not. Such as it is/was in my Hundred Kingdoms campaign. On a macro level, the Lowlanders and the Hill Tribes didn't really like each other, but on a micro level they mostly got on alright.<br /> <br /> Coincidentally, I am basing the Hill Tribes off of not only various Asian tribal groups (mainly the Montagnards/Degar and the Ainu) but also various Celtic groups, mostly the Welsh.<br /> <br /> Also, it goes without mention that many of the Lowlander clans and villages detest each other as well. &quot;Don't trust those shifty bastards from that village over there, they are all thieves, the lot of them!&quot;<br /> <br /> However, if you guys feel like I am stepping on the racism-pedal a wee bit too hard, then I could certainly tone it down. I thought of two main methods through which Kasai could interact with the general population; how he interacts due to racism, and how he interacts due to class differences. I, personally, thought &quot;knight-expie vs peasants&quot; was a little bland.<br /> <br /> 2) Kasai and gettin' some lovin'.<br /> <br /> Kasai is interested in women, but it is ..... a wee bit complicated. On one hand, he is Oathsworn, a member of the nobility, and any woman of common birth he is with will be treated little different than a concubine. If he weds a commoner, he should expect to hear a lot of tittering from various nobles, disparaging of her virtues, and if he were to feel enough for the woman to wed her, he wouldn't want to put her through that. Not to mention a complete <u>ass- ton</u> of duels to defend her honor. Of course, if he felt strongly enough about the issue, he could also not really give a shit about gossip, much like he already does.<br /> <br /> On the other hand, as a Hill Tribesman-descendant, he is unlikely to actually receive any marriage-offers from Lowlander nobility. Ancestry nonwithstanding, his family is poor and not well known, both strikes against him. It would take a lot (say: becoming the retainer of a powerful Lord, or becoming known as a hero of various sorts, etc) for him to receive some offers.<br /> <br /> His best chances of being offered an actual &quot;noble&quot; marriage would be from one of the Hill Tribes, which wouldn't do much for his social standing among the Lowlanders. They have their own &quot;nobility&quot; (think ulchelwyr <a href=\"http://homepages.rootsweb.ancestry.com/~engwales/tribes.html\" target=\"_blank\">http://homepages.rootsweb.ancestry.c...es/tribes.html</a>) that Kasai could marry into if he catches someone's eye.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-02T16:53:27-08:00","id":1011278,"title":"","content":"I bet the hill tribes don't trust each other all the time, either. Don't trust those shifty snakefolk, they're treacherous. Don't trust those wolffolk, they're violent and smell funny. Those catfolk are lazy good-for-nothin's. Etc.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">She/Her. I am literal-minded and write literally. If I don't say something explicitly, please never assume I implied it. The only exception is if I try to make a joke.</span></span></span></div> <span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">My point of view may be different from yours but is equally valid.</span></span></span><br /> <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1x1ycoxJF0G9niMt5D3Pp7owZ7tw8AJvL?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">Exalted-cWoD-ArM url mega-library</a>. Exalted <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/663334-random-name-generator\" target=\"_blank\">name-generators</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/896-erinys","name":"Erinys","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar896_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4235"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-09T19:48:52-08:00","id":1015111,"title":"","content":"Bring her aboard it is, with forewarning about lack of pay and equipment.<br /> <br /> I would, however, like to mention that Kasai isn't <u>poor</u>, or, really, dirt poor. He has <i>some</i> money, although nothing worth noting using the Resources system, and nothing approaching the levels needed to support a number of followers, nor equip them with shiny new arms and armor. For example: Saiko got her glaive from the dead Oathsworn bandit, not via Kasai buying it for her. <br /> <br /> In my Hundred Kingdoms campaign, I ruled there was a monetary system &quot;underneath&quot; the Guild-backed silver system, using copper and bronze coinage. 1 silver Dinar can be broken into 8 bits, and each bit was worth something like 4 or so bronze coins, and each bronze coin was worth around 10 or so copper coins. Nobility would use silver, and townsfolk would use bronze/copper. Peasants would use the turnip : P. One Dinar was worth the approximate agricultural/industrial output of a single household for a month (although many peasant familes have holdings worth far less), for reference. Not that the peasants would see the actual money, of course. Instead, they would pay their taxes to the Lord in food or goods (cloth, pottery, raw materials, etc), who would then take (or have some lackey move, more likely), the produce to towns where it could be exchanged for actual money.<br /> <br /> So, right now Kasai has a relative bunch of copper and bronze coins a-jangling in his purse, which is enough to support him and Xuansu under moderate means, so long as they stay to the cheap side of town and eat the Creation-equivalent of &quot;street-meat&quot; or fast food. Adding Saiko to the mix strained his ability to stretch funds.<br /> <br /> Arms and armor are <u>expensive</u>. Kasai's armor and weaponry are worth more than Saiko's entire village several times over, and while his father held rather poor lands, noble .families bankrupting themselves to equip warrior children (or provide dowries) isn't uncommon. Which is the major reason said children (and Kasai) leave to find their own fortunes, and try to become retainers to Lords. <br /> <br /> It is also <u>expensive</u> for said Lords to keep said retainers. They laze about holdings all day, eating your food and drinking your good wine, and they need equipment and suchlike on top of that....... which is why most Lords will give most retainers the effective &quot;boot&quot; and subinfeudate their own lands. If a retainer owns land, <u>you</u> don't have to pay for their upkeep.<br /> <br /> Hence, feudalism. The need for more and more land is what initially caused the Lowland peoples to drive the Hill-Tribes from the valleys, and is what causes the constant back-and-forth wars between the domains of the Hundred Kingdoms. <br /> <br /> Anyways, back to it.<br /> <br /> -----<br /> <br /> Resting your cheek on your fist again, you purse your lips and look at Blue Pines flatly, before stating, &quot;While I certainly can appreciate both your compliments towards my moral fiber, and your need for money, I am sure you must be aware that I am not particularly well-off? As you undoubtedly experienced in your time enlisted in Lord Iysen's men-at-arms, keeping and holding troops under arms is <b>expensive. </b>I am unable to properly equip Saiko as it is, and in fact, I had hoped to earn enough money to do so competing in Lord Iysen's tournament. Even if I were to win, equipping a second armsman up to standard would be difficult.&quot;<br /> <br /> You pause, and and let your meaning sink in.<br /> <br /> &quot;If you are willing to labor for me , up to and including sleeping in common or in the wilderness, fighting with poor wargear, and servicing my arms, armor and warhorse, all without a promise of recompense in the immediate future, then by all means, welcome. If that does not sound desirable, then come and talk to me after the tournament, and we will see if we can work something out. Other than that, I cannot make any promises.&quot; you finish with a shrug.<br /> <br /> Blue Pines ponders this for a long moment, then nods. &quot; To be honest, my Lord, almost anything beats begging for scraps and pennies. And, following you will allow me to hold my head high, even if it is hard. It isn't like I don't already do the sort of work you talk about anyways.&quot; She bows formally, head to the floorboards, until you indicate for her to rise. <br /> <br /> Saiko claps once, excited, then adds, &quot; So, Kasai, what is the plan now?&quot;<br /> <br /> You shrug, &quot; Same as it was last. Tomorrow, we will head to the market to see what we can get for the bandits arms and armor, which undoubtedly won't be much, considering the shape it is in, and the day after that is registration.&quot;<br /> <br /> She frowns, &quot; I still think you should compete in every event. 'If you cast your hook into many ponds, you can catch more fish, I've always heard&quot;.<br /> <br /> Amused at her exuberance, &quot; You are not the one that has to compete, Saiko! Tournaments can be dangerous. Not the individual events, but the horseback competitions, the melees, people have broken bones, and even died. \u2026..Now, you are not wrong, the more events I compete in, the more chances I have to win and earn rewards, but on the other hand, I would rather not shatter a limb and be bedridden for months&quot;. You get up from the bed and stride over to the narrow window, contemplating the possible choices. \u201c I want to enroll in the spear contests, for sure. Mounted and dismounted, I have always been skilled with the spear. Probably at least one archery contest. Sword\u2026\u2026 maybe dismounted, although I am unsure. Duels can be dangerous, even with tourney weapons, and I like my teeth where they are. As for the melee\u2026\u2026, \u201c you wince. \u201c The melees are where the money and the glory is, but those are the most dangerous events of all, and victory is less-likely than in other events. But, as the saying goes, \u2018no pain, no gain\u2019, I guess.\u201d<br /> <br /> Blue Pines interjects with , \u201c What does each event involve? Why are they so dangerous?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cMost events use the bow, the spear, or the sword, in demonstrations of skill. The bow is almost certainly going to be a competition for points in a bracketed contest, while the spear and sword are duels. The duels take place using mock weapons of bamboo or wood, but bones get broken and teeth get shattered regardless. The melees are free-for-alls, where everyone charges at each other, and the last one standing wins the event. Most competitors end up unconscious as a result, and there have been several fatalities over the years.\u201d You shrug.<br /> <br /> \u201cI am going to be in armor, at least for the duels, but a wooden sword to the face is still a wooden sword to the face. Decidedly unpleasant, to say the least.\u201d With that, you lay back on the quilted mattress. \u201c I don\u2019t know about you, but I am going to get some sleep. Blue Pines, you can join us for the night if you wish, or you can meet us at the marketplace tomorrow at 9-Bells. There is some food left on the platter, if you want it.\u201d.<br /> <br /> \u201cI\u2019ll meet up with you tomorrow, my Lord. I have a few things to clean out in my garret, so I guess this is good night. We can talk more tomorrow.\u201d, and she leaves with a bow. Saiko, before curling up on the floormat at the foot of the bed, blows out the tallow taper. It is quiet for a long while, then Saiko speaks suddenly, jerking you out of your doze.<br /> <br /> \u201c Was taking Blue Pines on a good idea, Kasai?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cI\u2019m not sure\u201d, you admit. \u201cOn one hand, more hands make for light work, and I could use all the hands I can get. On the other, feeding and equipping followers is expensive, and I can barely afford you as it is.\u201d You continue, half-joking. \u201cWe\u2019ll have to see what happens with the tournament. If I can pull through with a win, then this whole argument is moot. I\u2019m confident in my ability to do so, but the Gods often foil man\u2019s best laid plans\u2026..\u201d You shrug into the darkness. \u201cDon\u2019t worry about it Saiko. It is my job to think about this aspect of things.\u201d She doesn\u2019t answer, and soon both of you drift off.<br /> <br /> `````<br /> Per usual, you rise with the dawn, accompanied by the tolling of the temple-bell. After washing your face and hands with a ewer of water, you rouse Saiko (who, as usual, sprawled out all over the floor and is complaining of stiff muscles) and go to check out. After picking up Xuansu, your little group heads to breakfast at a streetcorner stand; bread and onions fried in drippings, and several copious servings of \u201cbuilder\u2019s tea\u201d, a tea boiled with milk and honey, strong enough to stand up a spoon in. After, you proceed down to the market, which despite (or because of) the early hour, is already bustling with activity, shouting and haggling and the clatter of stands being set up and product being moved around.<br /> <br /> In Teriake Castle-town, there are three \u201cmain\u201d markets; the \u201cfarmers\u201d market both inside and outside the gate, where local farmers sell their wares and various agriculture-related businesses (seed stores, liveries and livestock pens, etc) can be found, the \u201cmain\u201d market, located dockside, which is almost always a hive of activity, even in the middle of the night, and the \u201cindustrial\u201d district, downwind of the rest of the town, where the forges, tanneries, leatherworkers, carpenters and other craftsmen are, along with the main Guildhouses of the city.<br /> <br /> This last market is where you are headed, and you are only waiting for a few minutes after the tolling of the 9<sup>th</sup> Bells before Blue Pines come strolling up. In the light of day, her bruises and broken nose are particularly lurid, and you find yourself glad to have stepped in when you did, before her injuries were made even worse. She acknowledges your presence with a quick nod of the head, and asks,<br /> <br /> \u201cYou mentioned something about selling some equipment, my Lord? What kinds of equipment?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cMostly cast-off arms and armor, from bandits. I am interested to see what we can get for it, although I am not expecting very much.\u201d<br /> <br /> She scratches the back of her neck, apparently in thought. \u201c I used to work as a go-fer for a weaponsmith down here, I might be able to get a decent price for the swag, depending on what it is. Let\u2019s take a walk down there, we aren\u2019t that far.\u201d With that, she strides off down the road, you and Saiko in hot pursuit.<br /> <br /> After a few moments of weaving around stands and shoppers (many of whom appear to be Oathsworn, here for the tournament), you stand in front of a long, low building, built of brick and roofed in slate. Inside, a multitude of laborers work at a large forge, with apprentices working the bellows and hauling fuel and journeymen working on smaller projects. At the center of the chaos, overseeing everything at once and jumping into projects, is the master smith. Contrary to what you expected, the man is small and wiry, with small watery eyes. As soon as he sees Blue Pines, his eyes widen and he bellows over the clatter of hammers on anvils.<br /> <br /> \u201cGood <b>Gods</b>, woman, what the hell happened to you?!\u201d<br /> <br /> Blue Pines grins, \u201c Good morning to you too, you old bastard!.\u201d She shrugs,\u201d Got mugged by some crazy preacher-man who got all butthurt over a puppet show. Got my ass kicked.\u201d<br /> <br /> The smith laughs, \u201cWell, at least he didn\u2019t make you any uglier, Blue. That must have been some hell of a puppet show, kinda makes me want to see it. \u201c He claps her on the shoulder. \u201c Speakin\u2019 of that, you lookin\u2019 to do some work? With the tournament, I\u2019ve had so many orders and need another runner to deliver \u2018em.\u201d She shakes her head.<br /> <br /> \u201cNope, here to sell, hopefully.\u201d She indicates you and Saiko, still standing in the street, and you step forwards, offering your hand regardless of the soot and grime.<br /> <br /> He grins, \u201cLook at you, Blue, moving up in the world\u201d. Quickly wiping his hand on his apron, he almost crushes your fingers in a clasp. \u201c My name is Red Eagle, Master Smith of the Honorable Guild of Weaponcrafters. What can I do for you, Lord?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c I have with me some arms and armor I wish to sell. If you could take a look at it, I would be grateful\u201d You reply, trying to mentally work some feeling back into your fingers.<br /> <br /> \u201cOf course. If you would just follow me into the shop, we can get to it right away.\u201d, and the smith turns about and heads through a door at the far end of the building. Saiko and Blue Pines unties the bag of gear from Xuansu and heads into the mercantile section of the forge.<br /> <br /> Inside, away from the noise of the forge, you open the bag and spread out the various pieces of arms and armor, looted from the bandits in Saiko\u2019s village, on the counters. The lamellar chest piece, thigh plate and arm-guards. The padded underarmor. Various helmets, of leather and quilted cloth and metal. Some leather gauntlets and greaves. Axeheads and spearheads. All are far from pristine, rust-pitted and dented. You aren\u2019t expecting much as a result.<br /> <br /> Red Eagle goes over each piece, flipping each over and examining them with a trained eye, looking for things you aren\u2019t even sure of. Some, he smells and rubs with a piece of soft stone, examining the colors of the streaks. Others, he scrapes with a file. After a long while, he sets down the last piece and sighs, lifting his head and looking at you.<br /> <br /> \u201cWell, in all honesty, most of this stuff isn\u2019t very good. A lot of it is shit steel, or is in bad shape from rust. The bits that are good, I can either refurbish or reuse for something else, but they are far and in-between.\u201d He indicates the lamellar pieces, \u201cThese are the best by far, but the lacings have gone to rot, and they will have to be redone. Nothing wrong with the metal, though. Pretty good steel.\u201d<br /> <br /> He clasps his hands, eyeing the equipment and lost in thought, quiet for a moment.<br /> <br /> \u201cReally, the best offer I could make is \u2026. Hmm\u2026. Probably 60 or so dinars, for the lot. And that is only because you know Blue here. Anyone else would be getting 40, 50 at best\u201d.<br /> You nod, expecting much less and pleased with the new amount. \u201cAcceptable\u201d.<br /> <br /> He holds up a finger. \u201cThere is one hold-up, however. I\u2019ve only got about 25 dinars in hard cash in the shop. The rest is in an account at my Guildhouse. I could send you with a note, and they would give you the rest, or you could take credit or kind, so long as it isn\u2019t something already commissioned.\u201d.<br /> <br /> You ponder this for a moment. \u201c I think I will take 25 in cash, then some pieces in kind, and whatever left over I can take from the Guildhouse coffers, if that is acceptable\u201d. He nods, and then pulls out an absolute plethora of keys and chains, all to unlock a trapdoor under the counter, then a chest bolted to the floor, <u>then</u> a strongbox inside the chest. From inside, he pulls out a paper envelope of silver coins, which clink pleasantly as he passes it over. This envelope gets tucked away inside your shirt, safe from prying eyes and snatching fingers.<br /> <br /> After a walkthrough of the shop, examining the various weapons on display on the walls and racks and counters, you pull out a broad-bladed spear, two daggers, and a truncheon studded with bronze knobs.<br /> <br /> \u201cHow much\u201d?<br /> <br /> He glances at the weaponry, \u201cAbout 7 dinars worth, right there\u201d. Nodding your assent, you then accept a paper note from the smith, signed and stamped by Red Eagle, along with directions to his Guildhall.<br /> <br /> After you leave the shop, you notice Saiko has a pale, almost sick look on her face. \u201cIs there something wrong, Saiko? Are you sick?\u201d<br /> <br /> She closes her eyes and shakes her head. \u201cI don\u2019t know how you can so easily just accept 25 Dinars, Lord Kasai. <b>25 Dinars</b>! That is more money than I have ever seen in my life! I thought you said you weren\u2019t expecting much!\u2019\u201d<br /> <br /> You blink, confused. \u201cI wasn\u2019t, and, really, this isn\u2019t all that much, relatively speaking. My armor and weapons, as well as Xuanu\u2019s equipment, cost upwards of 500 dinars. Not to mention Xuansu himself.\u201d You grin suddenly, and then almost-impulsively pull out one of the dinars. Grabbing each side, you crack the coin in half down the middle, resulting in four \u201cpieces\u201d in each hand. Passing each half to Saiko and Blue Pines, you smugly proclaim, \u201cDo not doubt my generosity! Let it never be said that I did not reward you for loyal service!\u201d<br /> <br /> Saiko gapes at the gift, looking particularly like a landed fish, while Blue Pines more pragmatically promptly stuffs the piece into a neck-pouch.<br /> <br /> \u201cWhile I\u2019m not about to look a gift horse in the mouth, why the sudden generosity, Lord?\u201d<br /> <br /> You shrug, \u201cI am in a good mood after getting that much cash, and am feeling particularly foolish as a result.\u201d Glancing towards the sun, which is still quite a ways away from noon, you make a snap decision. \u201cI am going to take a hot bath and visit a barber, and then go shopping. If you want to join me, you are welcome, but as far as I am concerned, you two can have the rest of the day off. Meet me in the main market at 6-Bells for dinner.\u201d You divvy out the weapons you just bought, a dagger for Saiko and the rest for Blue Pines, and head out.<br /> <br /> Both of your followers decide to follow you to the Guildhouse, where after presenting Red Eagles promissory note to a clerk, you find yourself relaxing in a nice chair, sipping from a cup of wine, while the rest of your cash is retrieved from a strongbox. After only a few minutes, you leave the Guildhouse quite a few coins wealthier, whistling a jaunty tune and jingling with every step.<br /> After a visit to a bathhouse and a barber (and removing about a weeks\u2019 worth of grime and road-dust), Blue Pines heads off to do her own thing, while Saiko, apparently uncomfortable with the idea of being in a strange city all alone, sticks with you. The two of you mosey on down to the main marketplace by the docks, stopping at stores and stalls as they take your fancy.<br /> <br /> With your newfound cash, you are now able to afford new supplies and equipment, stuff that you simply made do without back when it was just you and Xuansu wandering the northern hills. Now that you have followers, the stuff isn\u2019t merely a luxury, but a necessity. A cast-iron pot, with legs and a cover and a ladle. Sleeping mats made from reeds, with blankets and tie-straps. A tarpaulin of canvas, a length of rope, a woodcutters axe. Actual dishware; cups and bowls and trenchers, made of wood and tin. Soap (plenty of it!) A bucket and a shovel. Oilcloth for protecting weapons and armor from moisture. Armor for Saiko and Blue Pines, in the form of quilted canvas-and-wool jackets. (Saiko is adamant in putting it on immediately, and learns to regret it later when the sun moves overhead and the insulative properties of 25+ layers of wool and canvas become apparent)<br /> <br /> Now, the stuff you are buying isn\u2019t <i>all</i> bare necessities. While browsing, you admire several pieces of clothing, accessories and jewelry. While you aren\u2019t a dandy, you were partially raised by your grandfather in the Dumoniapoccasset confederation, and if a Tribesman appreciates anything, it is fine clothing. The Hill-Tribes are some of the best weavers and gold/silver/coppersmiths in the Hundred Kingdoms, and many Tribespeople carry their wealth in the form of their clothing and jewelry; clothing woven in intricate patterns and beautifully dyed, and bracelets, armlets, necklaces, brooches and torcs worked in bronze, copper, silver and gold.<br /> <br /> And so, now that you have the chance, you buy up as much new clothing and accoutrements you can safely afford, partially out of need (your trousers are as thin as an Immaculate prayer-strip!) and partially out of plain desire. New trousers, socks, a vest made of tooled sheepskin fleece, a copper torc and penannular brooch, a hood and wood-and-hemp shoes for Saiko. A comb carved from elk antler. Finally, an absolutely wonderful wool cloak, dyed green and red and trimmed in rabbit fur. While the oncoming summer would limit its practicality, you cut a particularly dashing figure in it, as evidenced by Saiko blushing furiously when you throw it over your shoulders.<br /> <br /> Finally, to throw it all together (and to carry it all), you stop by a livery and purchase a mule and a wooden cart. With all the new equipment, as well as your newfound entourage, you start to feel more like an actual Oathsworn warrior, and not simply a particularly well-equipped sellsword.<br /> <br /> It\u2019s about goddamn time, in your opinion. Feels good.<br /> <br /> While wandering, you stop for a quick bite and can\u2019t help but notice a worn man, naked to the waist, climbing the stairs in front of the Immaculate Temple on his knees. As soon as he gets to the top, he proceeds down again and repeats the process. When you get closer, you notice that it is the firebrand preacher you almost killed yesterday, and while it might be petty and below your station, you can\u2019t help but feel some sense of satisfaction at the penance levied out, and wave with a shit-eating grin wide on your face. It looks like the abbot has a sense of compassion, at least.<br /> <br /> After a full day of shopping, you meet up with Blue Pines for dinner at a cornerhouse down by the river. There, while gorging on dumplings stuffed with pork and onions, fried fish and good beer (you feel like splurging a bit), you ask Blue Pines about her \u201cuseful information\u201d she mentioned last night.<br /> <br /> \u201cOh, that\u201d, she mutters around a mouthful of meat bun. \u201cA couple of weeks ago, when I was working down at the docks as a longshoreman, I was coming off a night shift when I saw something \u2026. \u2018weird\u2019, I guess?\u201d She puts down her spoon to scratch at her head, trying to remember the details.<br /> <br /> \u201cI got off the shift in the early morning, and I was leaving the docks when I noticed a small group of people gathered down by the north end of the docks. After sunset, non-dockworkers aren\u2019t allowed on the grounds, so I walked down there to kick them out. Before I could get up to them, I noticed them, what they were wearing, and I jumped back into the shadows all stealthy-like.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cWhat do you mean, \u2018what they were wearing\u201d?<br /> <br /> \u201c A couple of the guys were dressed in all dark clothes, and had cloth covering their faces, like they didn\u2019t want their faces to be seen, and one guy was dressed real nice. Not dockworker clothing, or even merchant or townsman. <i>Noble</i> stuff, if you catch my drift. He had a sword and everything, like yours.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cAnyways, the dandyman passed off a package to the sketchy guys, who got into a small rowboat and headed down the river, without a lantern, mind you. Who the hell does that in the dark? Someone that doesn\u2019t want others to know they are leaving, I tell you. So, as soon as the boat passed out of sight, the dandyman turned around and started to head out of the dockyards. He passed a couple of feet away from me, so close I could smell the cologne he was wearing.\u201d She closes her eyes, as if it will help bring back the smell.<br /> <br /> \u201c Lavender. Lavender and something else, some flower. But the lavender was <i>strong</i>.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cWhat happened next?\u201d you interrupt.<br /> <br /> She shrugs, \u201cI followed him. It wasn\u2019t that hard, even if the streets were empty. I just kept to the shadows behind him, not like he looked behind him at all.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cWhere did he go\u201d?, you ask, already guessing the answer.<br /> <br /> She rolls her eyes, \u201cThe castle. Not the main gate, a side door, on one of the towers. He had a key apparently, because nobody opened the door for him.\u201d<br /> <br /> Ignoring her impertinence, you rest your chin in your hand and furrow your brow, deep in thought. On the sword alone, the figure was an Oathsworn, someone important and trusted enough by Lord Iysen (or one of her retainers) to be granted a key to her castle, but what were they doing passing suspicious packages off to dubious couriers in the dead of night? They could be a spy, or an assassin\u2026..<br /> <br /> You are roused from your thoughts by Saiko shaking your shoulder urgently. Looking up, you can see her looking up at the castle rising above you, seated on a stone knob overlooking the river. The sun is setting over the river, lighting up the cliff face, and you start to praise the beauty of the scene before you, only for Saiko to grab ahold of your face with one hand and pointing with the other at the rocks. Squinting, you can just barely make out what she is indicating.<br /> <br /> \u201cLook, Lord Kasai, someone is climbing down the cliff, right there!\u201d<br /> <br /> And, to your surprise, someone is scaling the face. As the sun started to set, they were illuminated by its rays, catching the light and coming into full-view for but a split second. Even as you watch, they suddenly press themselves to the face, as if they knew they were spotted, and vanish into the shadow of the crags. It is only because you know where to look that you are capable of seeing them work themselves around the edge of the knob, to the face overlooking the river and out of sight.<br /> Standing up, you:<ol class=\"decimal\"><li>Grab Saiko and Blue Pines to go chase after the individual on foot. You want answers!</li> <li>Leave Blue Pines here, and take Siako on Xuansu to chase them on horseback. Someone has to watch the cart, and pay, after all.</li> <li>Tell the nearest militia patrol. This is really none of your concern, or business really. Do you really want to get caught up in this</li> <li>Something else?</li> </ol>OOC:<br /> <br /> On Money:<br /> <br /> I don\u2019t particularly like how the EX3 handbook describes money and Resources, and so I basically just use the D&amp;D system in a fashion for actual prices, modified according to real-world examples, with the Silver Dinar replacing the gp.<br /> <br /> So, a dagger (not a utility knife, but a two-edged fighting knife made of differentially hardened and tempered steel. There is a significant difference between the two, in cost and manufacture at least) would cost around 2 Dinars. Same with a Spear. A straight sword would be between 75 dinars (for a shitty sword) and 150 dinars (for a nice one). A chopping backsword, like a <i>dao</i> or falchion, would be about 50 dinars. Kasai\u2019s mail cost about 150 dinars, and his bow cost 100 dinars. Xuansu cost 250 dinars.<br /> <br /> Now, for some comparisons: you could trade (at wholesale) a bushel (56 lbs) of dried corn for about \u00bc of a dinar, or a bushel (60 lbs) of wheat for about \u00be of a dinar. A sheep or goat would go for a single dinar, while a pig and a cow would go for about 3 dinars and 10 dinars, respectively. A pound of workable iron ingots would go for about \u00bd a dinar, with most ingots being 5 lbs. A bolt of wool cloth measuring 4 square yards would go for 2 dinars a bolt, linen for about 4 dinars, hemp about 1, and silk and velvet between 20 and 100.<br /> <br /> Most peasant farmers and unskilled urban laborers will make, on average, about 1 dinar a month on their labor and products. Most of that, in turn, will go towards rent, food costs, taxes, and other necessities, with the peasant/laborer making only around a dinar a year after that. Skilled urban laborers (like the journeymen seen in the forge), as well as more well-off farmers, the class that Men-At-Arms are usually drawn from, make on average around 2 Dinars a year, after expenses. Landed Oathsworn usually make about 5 dinar a year, off their peasants labor and production.. Lord Iysen\u2019s domain of Xia makes about 10,000,000 + Dinar a year, on agriculture, skimming off trade, taxes, etc. She, personally, only holds lands and manors with an estimated value of about 5,000 dinar a year. The other value of her domain either goes towards upkeep (road-building, bridge repair, dredging the river, etc), or given as fiefs to retainers, her sworn noble warlords and associated Oathsworn.<br /> <br /> So, yeah, shit isn\u2019t cheap. This is something that many modern people don\u2019t understand, with industrialized everything. Without that, all food is more expensive, tools and weapons even more so, clothing, houses, <i><u>everything</u></i><u>, </u> because it has to be made by hand by skilled artisans, or wrested from the ground with blood, sweat, and tears.<br /> <br /> Relatedly, Kasai made <u>bank</u> by trading in the old equipment, but when you examine the costs associated with being a permanent mounted warrior, with no real source of income, you realize that he was right, and that he didn\u2019t really make all that much. Most of that went into arms and armor and supplies, and, again, <u>that isn\u2019t cheap</u>. He also wasn\u2019t expecting to make nearly that much, around 20 dinars or so, and because he made 200% (300%) on top of what he expected, he could then go and actually buy Blue Pines equipment, after he said he couldn\u2019t.<br /> <br /> With the above costs, keep in mind that you can \u201cget around them\u201d by not purchasing the items from a store, but making them yourself. If a peasant wants a sling, they don\u2019t go to the nearest store (which is likely several miles away) and plonks down some coins. No, they twist the cordage and make it themselves. Same with metal tools, farm animals, crops, houses, etc. They are going to team together and help each other out, like buying and owning an ox in common so they can get the plowing done, for example.<br /> <br /> As for Kasai giving Saiko and Blue Pines half a dinar each, that was <u>amazingly</u> generous, and if he wasn\u2019t practically giddy in excitement, he probably wouldn\u2019t have done it. A dinar is a months wages, so half a dinar is about 2 weeks wages. In modern terms, at minimum wage, that would be about $800. For shits and giggles.<br /> <br /> Of course, being generous with your followers is pretty much how you keep them, and attract new ones.<br /> <br /> On Language:<br /> <br /> So far, I have been trying to play Kasai as using more formal language when talking to strangers, as he is educated and urbane compared to the commoners, and less formal and more familiar (more contractions, for example) with friends and in private. You can see this most with Saiko.<br /> <br /> I am unsure how well it has come off so far, and if it seems too forced, I will lay off it a bit.<br /> <br /> Posts:<br /> <br /> So, I recently (as in, for the last two weeks) got hired at my first \u201cadult\u201d job (working for my home city as a Water Treatment Operator), meaning I have steady hours finally. Yay! However, I also now work from 730 am to 4pm, and have to commune 45 minutes to an hour, each way, and basically have to go to bed around 9pm every night. This leaves me very little time for much of anything, much less brainstorming and writing Oathsworn. I do, however, have the weekends off, so it looks like I will be all-but-forced to do my writing then, which is a good thing in all seriousness, as the irregular hours of my old job quasi-caused me to put the thing off for days, then weeks, etc.<br /> <br /> \u2018Eh, I\u2019ll get to it tomorrow\u2026. \u2013cue next week- \u2026\u2026 fuck\u201d<br /> <br /> You get the deal.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-10T13:24:49-08:00","id":1015351,"title":"","content":"Leave Blue Pines here, and take Siako on Xuansu to chase them on horseback. Someone has to watch the cart, and pay, after all.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Like my Avatar? Courtesy of<span style=\"color:#0000FF\"> Jen</span>! : <span style=\"font-size:12px\">Anybody want their characters to be experimented on ? </span>post 98<br /> <i>An Exalt is never unarmed.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4018-l-het-esh","name":"L'het'esh","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4018_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"521"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-10T18:10:07-08:00","id":1015435,"title":"","content":"Number 2 please!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1311230-badassoverlord-s-assorted-homebrew-folder\" target=\"_blank\">Are you in the market for some Martial Arts? Perhaps some custom Artifacts for your campaign?</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10131-badassoverlord","name":"BadassOverlord","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar10131_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"322"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-10T18:32:53-08:00","id":1015450,"title":"","content":"Choice number 2","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I write things.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3950-wonderandawe","name":"wonderandawe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3950_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5410"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-10T20:29:09-08:00","id":1015510,"title":"","content":"#2, but leave Saiko to guard the cart instead of Blue Pines. Unless Xuansu can't carry her weight?<br /> <br /> (Your interpretation of the dinar is way inflated beyond what I usually see. I expect that to be a source of ongoing confusion. Maybe if you set the Dinar equivalent to the Platinum Piece?)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>On the frontier of the Wild South, there's only one woman with the grit to take on its most dangerous outlaws and bring them <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/984977-back-alive-or-maybe-dead-quest\" target=\"_blank\">Back Alive, or Maybe Dead.</a><br /> <br /> Avatar by <a href=\"http://puzzlinghappenstance.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">K.S. Brenowitz</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/908-semicasual","name":"semicasual","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar908_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2499"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-10T20:58:27-08:00","id":1015519,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>semicasual</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1015510#post1015510\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">#2, but leave Saiko to guard the cart instead of Blue Pines. Unless Xuansu can't carry her weight?<br /> <br /> (Your interpretation of the dinar is way inflated beyond what I usually see. I expect that to be a source of ongoing confusion. Maybe if you set the Dinar equivalent to the Platinum Piece?)</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Xuansu can carry Kasai and <u>Saiko</u>. Not Kasai and Blue Pines. She is just a wee bit heftier than Saiko, who is rather on the slender side. And we are lucky that Kasai isn't in armor, as if he was, Xuansu wouldn't be carrying anything other than Kasai. Xuansu could best be described as a courser or somesuch, not a destrier, a light warhorse as opposed to a heavy warhorse.<br /> <br /> As for the value of the Dinar, I just pretty much took it out of the EX3 corebook, and the D&amp;D 3.5 DM Guide. In the EX3 corebook, it is stated that a silver Dinar is the approximate monthly income of a peasant household with an active cottage industry (or other source of income) [ page 77]<br /> <br /> In the DM's guide, it is stated that the average laborer makes around 1 sp a day (in many places through the book), so about 3 gp a month. Take out taxes, necessities and other expenses in value of around 2-3 gp a month (using the Upkeep rule, page 130). With the Exalted and D&amp;D examples in mind, and doing some basic simplification, and keeping in mind that &quot;real world&quot; prices for things in a preindustrial economy are going to be more expensive, often vastly so, than one might think, one Dinar is approximately equal in value to one gp. Everything else just followed suit after that, using the prices listed in the D&amp;D PHB as a guide.<br /> <br /> This is actually in-line with many real world prices and incomes. For example, in Anglo-Saxon and Medieval England, a &quot;hide&quot; of land was the amount of land that would produce about 1 pound (the coin, not the weight) and was considered the basic minimum of land and income needed to support a family for a year, after they pay taxes and such. Ergo, the basic peasant family would hold land valued at 1 pound (or Dinar) per year, after expenses (they are really making 12, but factoring in taxes and other stuff, leaves about a Dinar)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-19T20:42:01-08:00","id":1019590,"title":"","content":"Choice #2 (follow on horseback with Saiko in-tow) it is!<br /> <br /> Turning quickly, you stride towards Xuansu, tied up at a hitch by the front of the restaurant. Having left the saddle on, with the straps loosened, it only takes you a moment to re-tighten the buckles and ties around the beast\u2019s girth, but in that timeframe the unknown climber could\u2019ve hid in any number of places, and it is with a sense of almost-panic that you pull Saiko up into the saddle behind you.<br /> <br /> Blue Pines, in the midst of shrugging on her quilted jacket, asks, \u201cWhat about me, Lord? There is no way I am going to fit on that horse, and I\u2019ll just slow you down\u201d.<br /> \u201cStay here, and watch the cart. It is just one person, this shouldn\u2019t take that long\u201d, and she nods and leans against the cart, spear in hand. With that, you wheel Xuansu and head towards the stone knob at a solid trot.<br /> <br /> Working your way off the flagstone quay of the docks and onto the scree-strewn path at the base of the cliff, you soon realize that this was an exceptionally poor idea. In addition to the jostling up and down of Xuansu, as a result of the pace, the rough terrain itself is all-but throwing poor Saiko over the horse\u2019s rump. She has avoided this so far by clinging to you for dear life, but it is obvious that she can\u2019t hold on for much longer. After a few minutes, you bring Xuansu to a walk, and Saiko slides down (with quite a bit of help from you) to the ground gratefully.<br /> <br /> So, now, she isn\u2019t going to fall from a horse and break her neck, but you are also limited to walking speed. In addition, there is no evidence of the climber on the cliff above, even with the setting sun shining full on the face, illuminating fully all the cracks and crevices in which anyone could hide.<br /> <br /> A sense of irritation fills you as you follow the path besides the river, Saiko bringing up the rear. This is almost certainly a waste of time, something you bring up to Saiko. She stays silent, shrugging, and you are about ready to turn around and head back to the cornerhouse with its exceptional beer, when there is a sudden scream from down the trail, a scream that is suddenly cut off.<br /> <br /> With a sudden meeting of the eyes, Saiko presses herself to a boulder on the slope, and you hurriedly dismount from Xuansu, pulling his head down to limit his silhouette. Grabbing a length of cord tied to the front of his breastplate, you pass it around a sturdy root and preventing him from running off. Rising to a half-crouch, you go to pull off your panoply from the saddle, only to realize in sudden horror that almost all of it is back in the cart. Mail, spear, shield. The only thing you have is your bow, lashed to the saddle, your sword at your waist, and the clothes on your back.<br /> <br /> You whirl to Saiko, hissing, \u201cThis<i> was an exceptionally dumb idea!</i><br /> <br /> She bites her lip, glaive held close and face pale at implications of the cut-off scream, and nods in silent agreement. Grumbling, with stomach in knots, you pull the bow and quiver from its case, attaching the latter to your sash and stringing the former with a quick twist of the hips. Nocking an arrow to the string, you set off down the trail, ducking from boulder to boulder to remain hidden.<br /> <br /> A few tens of feet down the trail, the river pulls away from the cliff a bit, opening up the shoreline into a kind of forest, made of scrub trees and bushes. A good ambush point or a hide-a-way to avoid being spotted. Keeping that mind, you lead Saiko down to the shoreline, where the silt-heavy river laps at your feet, skirting the trees, heavy with hostile warriors in your imagination.<br /> <br /> After a few minutes of <i>slowly</i> picking your way along the shoreline, mindful of loose stones, puddles and tree roots, you suddenly come up on a \u201chidden\u201d cove in the forest, a bight where, unless one was right up next to it, overhanging scrub and bushes would conceal the shallow beach from casual observation. On said beach, there is a small rowboat, dragged up on the cobbles, around which are clustered several individuals. About 2 of them are preparing the rowboat for travel, another is digging hurriedly with a shovel (you are assuming it to be a grave, and can guess the fate of the climber already) back away from the trail, and two more are watching up and down the trail. From this distance, less than 30 feet at most, you can tell their body language is concerned, almost panicked, and you would guess the ruffians were not expecting their prey to fight so much, nor make so much noise, and are attempting to beat a quick retreat. None of the sneaks are wearing much in the way of armor, nor are there many weapons you can see, mainly knives.<br /> <br /> Saiko sidles up next to you, close enough for you to feel her breath on your ear, whispering, \u201c What\u2019s the plan?\u201d<br /> <br /> You point down towards the shore, away from the bushes and brambles overhead. \u201cCover me. Stay down for as long as possible, but if I step out, follow\u201d, and she nods.<br /> <br /> Settling down on your haunches, you stop for a second, to collect your breath and steady yourself. Then, you slowly raise the bow, pushing forward with your left arm and drawing back with your right, loading all of the 100+ pounds of pressure onto the muscles of your back. As you do so, the lessons of your tribal grandfather, who first taught you to use the bow, 12 years ago, echoes in your mind.<br /> <br /> \u201cBreathe. Draw the bow. Loose.\u201d<br /> <br /> You do so, drawing the string back to your jaw, right below the ear, and sighting down the shaft. As you coolly line up the broadhead with the torso of the sneak watching the far trail, back turned to you, you almost absently realize that this is going to be the first time you have killed a man in cold blood, not in defense. Putting the thought from your mind, and just as the muscles in your back start to tremble, you twitch your fingers and release the arrow. The string, wrapped with poufs of raccoon fur, twangs almost as silently as a twig snapping underfoot, and the arrow, fletched expertly, makes no noise as it blurs through the air.<br /> <br /> The sneak, pierced through with an arrow, collapses to the damp forest floor with a gurgling cry, and the other bandits panic, not knowing where the assault is coming from. With that distraction, you are able to put down another, arrow buried in his stomach, and nock another, before they realize where the arrows are originating from.<br /> Saiko leaps forwards, swinging her glaive in an arc around her, sweeping back the bandits. Armed only as they are with knives, she has the definite advantage in reach, and takes the opportunity to slash the closest on the knee before stabbing him through the chest as he falls.<br /> <br /> The glaive stays stuck in the dying bandits ribcage, and Saiko gets dragged off-balance as the man pitches backwards. The other bandit rushes forwards, and Saiko releases the glaive-shaft and desperately grabs at the dagger sheathed at her waist. More importantly, she is in between you and the bandit; you don\u2019t have a clear shot!<br /> <br /> <br /> The bandit leaps forwards, knife of his own in hand, and overpowers Saiko, grabbing her wrist and forcing it down and away. His knife-hand pivots up, almost in slow-motion, into her chest, and she doubles over the knife with a cry, falling to the ground and laying there, terrifyingly still.<br /> <br /> You <u>roar</u>, in anger and in horror, and jump from the bushes, drawing back the arrow as you do. The bandit\u2019s head snaps up, and you can see the sudden realization writ full in his eyes. Almost mechanically, you put an arrow between his eyes, and he falls to the earth right beside Saiko.<br /> <br /> Pounding footsteps sound in your ears, and you whirl around, teeth bared. The last bandit is trying to leg it, sprinting down the trail full-tilt, eyes back towards you and not even looking where he is running. With a snarl, you drop to one knee and put an arrow through his calf. He faceplants into the turf, screaming, and you all-but throw your bow down and run to Saiko.<br /> <br /> She is lying on the ground, doubled-up around the knife buried in her chest, and for a sudden cold moment, you think she might be dead. You sink to your knees beside her and softly touch her shoulder, and her eyes open with a start. Almost instantly, she starts crying, from pain or fright you aren\u2019t sure.<br /> <br /> \u201c\u2026\u2026Saiko?.......\u201d<br /> <br /> She gasps, then slowly, painfully, tries to sit up, only to cry out again and sink back down.<br /> <br /> \u201c\u2026Let me see.\u201d<br /> <br /> Whimpering, she pulls her hands away from the knife, and it falls to the ground with a clatter. Undoing the buttons on her jacket, you pull it open to reveal her hemp shirt, stained with blood around a puncture. Tearing the hole wider, you catch a glimpse of a small wound, more a prick than a stab, and oozing blood rather than flowing freely. So far, so (relatively) good. Examining the wound further, you are capable of seeing the white of bone, and realize that her quilted gambeson actually worked as intended, and took most of the blow, turning what would have been a knife through the lung into a poke. Regardless, it still hurt like hell, and after gingerly feeling around the puncture, almost certainly cracked the rib at least.<br /> Pulling yet another swatch of cloth from your sash, you gently (she still blanches in pain) press it to her side, to stop the bleeding, then wind another around the first, creating an impromptu dressing. Setting her up against a tree, you wipe the tears from her eyes.<br /> <br /> \u201cNow\u2026 I don\u2019t mean to be heartless, but can you walk? These guys might have friends\u201d.<br /> <br /> She nods, then after a second, struggles to her feet, arm wrapped about her chest for support. Meanwhile, you go to each bandit and pluck the arrows from their corpses.<br /> You can\u2019t find the last one. Following a large scrape-mark into the brush, you come across the bandit trying to make his way to the river. He is dragging himself along using one arm and leg, and with the other, he clutches a small package to his chest. Upon seeing you, he clumsily tries to throw the package into the water, but from his awkward position on the ground, can\u2019t hurl the thing more than a few feet away.<br /> <br /> Laughing coldly, you stride forward and pick up the package. Tucking it into your sash, you turn to the bandit, white-faced in pain and terror.<br /> <br /> \u201cNow, I know you aren\u2019t going to tell me, but I have to ask; who are you working for?\u201d<br /> <br /> He spits blood-colored sputum at you, but is otherwise silent. You shrug in response, then draw your dagger and slit his throat, leaving him for the scavengers<br /> <br /> As you make your way back to the cove, the first thing you see is Saiko standing stock-still, staring wide-eyed at the bandit she killed. She doesn\u2019t even acknowledge you when you walk up to her and touch her on the elbow. Shaking her a little causes her to shake her head, as if waking from a deep sleep, and say in a small voice.<br /> <br /> \u201cI\u2026.. I killed him, didn\u2019t I?\u201d It wasn\u2019t a question.<br /> <br /> You meet her eyes, hold her gaze. \u201cYes. Yes you did.\u201d<br /> <br /> She stands still for a moment, and then shakes her head slightly in affirmative. \u201c\u2026Okay.\u201d<br /> <br /> You raise an eyebrow, \u201cYou are taking this rather well. Better than I did.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cNot really. I feel like I am about to throw up, but I don\u2019t know if it is \u2026\u2026 that, or my chest hurting.\u201d She takes a deep breath, wincing. \u201c I guess it helps that he was going to kill me if I didn\u2019t get him first.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cThat usually makes it easier\u201d. You shrug. \u201cIt helps that I have been doing this, or things similar, since I was young. You didn\u2019t have that luxury. Up until 3 weeks ago, you were digging turnips\u201d<br /> You chuckle, then look away \u201c. I want to say that it gets \u201ceasier\u201d, for a lack of a better term, but I would be lying. I recognize that the people I have slain were still, you know, <b>people</b>, and I regret every time I kill a person\u201d.<br /> <br /> She interjects, \u201c But I have seen you kill plenty of people before. The bandits in my village, the guys here\u2026\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cAnd? Still people. You yourself got to see what it looks like when someone\u2019s soul leaves their body.\u201d<br /> <br /> She winces at that, and you are silent for quite some time.<br /> <br /> \u201c I guess the one thing that keeps me going, the one difference between me and a bandit, is the Oaths I swore when I was invested. Yeah, sure, I kill people, but I don\u2019t kill people for money, or for fun. I kill to defend other people.\u201d<br /> <br /> You shrug again, \u201cMakes the difference, at least for me. You\u2019ll have to find something that works for you.\u201d. With that, you offer out your arm, for support. The two of you limp-walk back to Xuansu, where you gingerly pick Saiko up and put her into the saddle. She grips the saddle-horn tightly as you lead Xuansu down the trail, now barely visible in the gloom of late twilight.<br /> <br /> By the time you make it back to town, it is already past dusk, and lamplighters are walking the streets, leading their patrons back to their houses. The cornerhouse, however, is still brightly lit; the evenings drinking has just gotten started, and drunken sailors and dockworkers amble around, singing hilariously off-key and spilling most of their booze. They do, however, keep a wide berth around Blue Pines and your cart. When she sees you walking up, she moves to meet you, only to stop short when she sees Saiko is wounded.<br /> <br /> Glancing in your direction, she snarks a comment at you before turning back to the cart, moving articles around and clearing a space for Saiko to recline.<br /> <br /> \u201cSo much for it \u2018not taking that long\u2019, Lord. What happened?\u201d<br /> <br /> Lifting Saiko from the saddle again, \u201cThere were \u2026. Complications. There was more than one person, and they apparently killed the one who was climbing down the cliff in the first place. Saiko got stabbed when we tried to intervene.\u201d<br /> <br /> Blue Pines bends down, to look more closely at Saiko\u2019s wound. \u201cShe was wearing her arming jacket, right? Looks like it.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cYes\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cAnyways, she needs to see a doctor. That bone needs to be set.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cOf course. Do you know anybody?\u201d<br /> <br /> She shakes her head, \u201cNope, I was never that lucky. Guy I went to fix my face cleared me out, so we don\u2019t want to go to him\u201d. She looks up, at the carousing longshoremen. \u201cOy! Three Eels, know any doctors? You get into bar-brawls a lot.\u201d<br /> <br /> One of the dockworkers, slightly less drunk than the rest, meandered over. \u201cThere\u2019s that one down by the fisherman\u2019s quarter. That\u2019s who I usually go to anyways. Set my broken bones for cheap, and doesn\u2019t start pulling until the poppies kick in. Always a plus.\u201d<br /> <br /> You interject, \u201cCan you lead us there? We are in a hurry.\u201d<br /> <br /> Three Eels hesitates, up until you pull a bronze coin, enough for a night of boozing, from your pouch and flick it at him. He snaps it up, then heads off, following the dockyards and the course of the river. The houses and businesses get smaller and seedier, and reeks ever more of garbage and fish, until you find yourself in front of a small, one-story shack. The windows are small and dark, and you don\u2019t feel particularly comforted by the implications.<br /> <br /> \u201cIs \u2026. Is he open?\u201d<br /> <br /> Three Eels snorts, \u201cShe is a doctor, always open.\u201d, and knocks on the door. After a few moments, the door opens a crack, illuminated by a candle, and a thin face peers out.<br /> \u201cEel? Is that you? What the hell did you do this time?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cNot me, Mino. Them.\u201d He jerks his head back at you. \u201cStabbing, looks like.\u201d<br /> <br /> The woman sighs, and throws open the door, \u201cBring her inside.\u201d With a little effort, you and Blue Pines work Saiko out of the cart and arm-chair-carry her into the building.<br /> <br /> Inside, the walls are covered from wall to wall with shelves, themselves filled with bits of paper, rolled-up scrolls, a few books, and jars filled with liquids and other unidentifiable things. Strong-smelling herbs hang from the rafters, and the entire building reeks of strong, undiluted alcohol. There is a curtain blocking off the other half of the building, and after Mino throws open back the curtain, you can see a futon and a few chairs and tables.<br /> <br /> \u201cSet the girl on the futon, and let me see the wound.\u201d<br /> <br /> After opening Saiko\u2019s jacket and shirt again, exposing the wound, Mino lights an alcohol lantern and sets it in a hemisphere of burnished copper, reflecting a bright light onto Saiko\u2019s torso. After dunking her hands and some copper tools into a bowl of yet more alcohol, she pokes and prods at the small puncture in Saiko\u2019s side, who puts on a brave face and bites her lip instead of crying out.<br /> <br /> She stands up and sets the tools aside into the bowl. \u201cWell, the actual stab-wound in and of itself isn\u2019t severe. That should heal itself in a couple of days. The broken rib is far more serious. I\u2019ll have to set it, and that will hurt.\u201d She turns to you, \u201cAlmost as bad, it will be expensive. Bandages and plaster, disinfectant and painkillers, drugs to fight infections. All aren\u2019t cheap. This isn\u2019t a nose, or an arm, that I can slap a splint and a sling onto and let it be.\u201d<br /> <br /> You shrug, \u201cWhatever it takes\u201d. She nods, and turns to several cupboards, pulling out supplies and medicines.<br /> <br /> In the meantime, you pull the package out from inside your sash and study it. Quite a small thing, a bundle not much bigger than a fist or a deck of cards, wrapped in oilcloth and twine. Cutting the twine, you unwrap the cloth to find a tightly-folded section of paper. The paper itself is quite high-quality, smooth to the touch and bleached white. Unfolding the paper takes but a moment, and you are soon studying the message within.<br /> <br /> The first thing you notice is that the entire message is written not in the \u201cstandard\u201d Riverspeak, nor cultured Dragon-tongue, but instead the complex, multi-branching symbols of Forest-tongue, the language of tribals and primitive groups throughout the East. Luckily, your Hill-Tribe \u201cwrote\u201d in Forest-tongue, so it only takes you a few minutes to pick out words and meanings behind the scribbles.<br /> <br /> However, as you do so, you just as rapidly realize that what you are reading is gibberish. Complete and utter jibberish.<br /> <br /> The few words and phrases there are to pick out are nonsensical, either not connected to each other in any way, or merely associated with each other only due to placement and read-order rules. Other ideograms make no sense whatsoever, and the entire message is a confused mass of grammatical errors and word-salad. There is <u>nothing</u> to be gained from this.<br /> <br /> Reading and re-reading the nonsensical message, your ire rises until your temper flares and you leap to your feet with a snarl, crumpling the paper in your fist and throwing it to the floor. The occupants of the small room stare at you in shock, until Blue Pines chimes in,<br /> <br /> \u201cThat bad, huh?\u201d<br /> <br /> You snarl,\u201d This entire thing was a waste of time. No, <u>more</u> than a waste of time, because we actually got hurt by it!\u201d<br /> <br /> Blue Pines picks up the paper, smooths it out, and glances at the figures.<br /> <br /> \u201cWhat are these?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cForest-tongue.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cWhat does it mean\u201d?<br /> <br /> \u201c<b><u>Nothing</u></b>. It\u2019s just jibberish!\u201d<br /> <br /> She flips the paper around, then looks at it through the light of the lantern.<br /> <br /> \u201cLord, I don\u2019t want to call you stupid, here, but this is definitely more than it seems\u201d.<br /> <br /> After taking a few breaths to calm down, you ask, \u201cWhat do you mean?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cWell, obviously, the guy who carried this message didn\u2019t want to be seen. That is why he climbed down the castle-cliff, as opposed to waltzing out the front door. Secondly, he passed it off to some <i>very</i>-sketchy guys, who, might I add, killed him rather than let him go free and talk about it.\u201d<br /> <br /> She sits down, cheek on fist. \u201cIn fact, I would be willing to bet this is connected to the guy I saw passing stuff off late at night a while back. Remember, down by the docks? It all lines up.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cSo, with that in mind, what do you think?\u201d<br /> <br /> She looks back at the paper. \u201c I\u2019m going to go out on a limb and say that this letter has stuff that the writer doesn\u2019t want most people to know about. You said this was Forest-tongue, right? Who reads that?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cAround here? Just the Hill-Tribes.\u201d<br /> <br /> She raises an eyebrow. \u201cAre the nobles in their fancy castles likely to write fancy letters to the Hill-Tribes, in their own language?\u201d<br /> <br /> Scratching your chin, \u201c \u2026. No. No, no they aren\u2019t.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c So, then that means this letter has info the writer doesn\u2019t want the common folk, <i>or</i> the nobles to know about. With that and the sneaky message-passing, we can put two and two together\u2026\u201d She shrugs.<br /> <br /> She has a point. Now, all that remains is what you are going to do about this.<ol class=\"decimal\"><li>Nothing. To get involved in the affairs on inter-domain politics, which is almost overwhelmingly likely what this is, is a good way to end up dead. At least, without powerful protectors.</li> <li>Bring this to the attention of the Captain of the Castle Guard. They should know that their integrity is compromised. But \u2026\u2026 what if the guard is in on it?</li> <li>Go talk to the Captain of the Town Guard. They might have heard something, reports of suspicious activity or some such, and they are less likely to throw you out on your ass out of hand.</li> <li>Bring this straight to the top. Lord Iysen Shiba should be warned of a possible plot, against her person or against her domain. From what you have heard, she isn\u2019t likely to dismiss you out of hand, not with your level of evidence.</li> <li>Something else?</li> </ol>OOC:<br /> On archery, and on warfare in the Hundred Kingdoms<br /> .<br /> So, in this post, we saw a bit of Kasai\u2019s archery skillz. Now, Kasai-as-archer has a different skillset than Blue Pines-as-archer, and I will attempt to go over it below<br /> Warfare.;<br /> <br /> Since the Hundred Kingdoms is essentially, a feudal society, the feudal order to a large extent dictates how warfare is fought.<br /> <br /> The heart of most forces will be the Lord, their sworn bannerlords, and their associated retainers/Oathsworn. Said fighters will almost always be in heavy armor, equipped with relatively heavy weapons (mainly the sword, the spear, and the bow), and form the heavy shock infantry and, where terrain and strategy allows, both heavy melee cavalry and ranged skirmish cavalry.<br /> Now, the Oathsworn are <u>good</u>, skilled, disciplined and motivated, but they tend to be small in number, even in large, wealthy domains. So, Lords pad their forces with both semi-professional Men-at-arms and peasant levies.<br /> <br /> Now, in order to differentiate between the two, one must first understand the difference between a \u201cpeasant\u201d and a \u201cserf\u201d. The major difference between them is land ownership. Peasants own their own land, usually given to them by a Lord, while Serfs rent land from their overlord. While both are \u201ccommoners\u201d, Peasants tend to be wealthier (due to generally owning more and better farmland), better fed (so, bigger and stronger), more respected (peasants usually are offered positions of management and responsibility by Lords), and sometimes have more free time (since they are wealthier and own more land, they can generally afford a farmhand/servant or two, leading to them needing to do less work personally), leading to more time available for practicing at arms. And, since they tend to have more disposable income, they can actually afford arms and armor.<br /> <br /> Most Men-at-Arms will be equipped with a spear and shield, a halberd, or a longbow, plus a backup weapon and a quilted jacket and helmet for protection. The spear/shield and halberd is self-explanatory, but the longbow, itself a holdover from the Hill-Tribes (for whom it was, and still is, a favorite weapon), has long been a favorite of both Men-at-Arms and their Lords, to the point where the longbow is seen as a symbol for the Peasantry, much like the sword is the symbol of the Oathsworn.<br /> <br /> Much of the land in the Hundred Kingdoms is forested, hills, or forested hills. That means hunting is a common pastime, both for nobility and the peasants that own land. This leads to skill with the bow, but some Lords have taken it even farther, mandating that farmers that own specific values of land have to be proficient with certain weapons. Below a certain amount; Spear and shield. Above a certain amount; Spear, shield and longbow. Even the serfs are not exempt from this, and they have to be proficient with spear and crossbow, in case of invasion.<br /> <br /> Serfs, on the other hand, usually don\u2019t have the money to buy weapons, and their Lords usually wouldn\u2019t want them owning arms anyways. But, in order to comply with the letter of the law, they have to make sure their serfs are at least familiar with arms, in order to defend themselves and their Lords lands. Hence, they usually stockpile spears and, whenever economically possible, crossbows, in their manors, to rapidly equip serfs in case of invasion.<br /> <br /> So, to summarize the three \u201cclasses\u201d of warrior in the Hundred Kingdoms.<br /> <br /> -Oathsworn; Heavy Infantry or Cavalry (The \u201chammer\u201d), archers focusing on accuracy and speed.<br /> <br /> -Men-at-Arms: Medium Infantry (The \u201canvil\u201d) or light cavalry (think Hobilars), archers focusing on massed fire and range.<br /> <br /> - Serf Levies: Light Infantry (screening, etc), massed direct fire<br /> <br /> We haven\u2019t seen any peasants yet, for reference. Both Saiko and Blue-Pines were serfs.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> ,","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-19T21:16:26-08:00","id":1019600,"title":"","content":"<i>Something else</i><br /> Kasai should keep his eye on the ball. Kasai came to participate in the tourney, so stick to the plan. Other action can be taken later. or not!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Like my Avatar? Courtesy of<span style=\"color:#0000FF\"> Jen</span>! : <span style=\"font-size:12px\">Anybody want their characters to be experimented on ? </span>post 98<br /> <i>An Exalt is never unarmed.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4018-l-het-esh","name":"L'het'esh","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4018_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"521"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-20T05:13:58-08:00","id":1019723,"title":"","content":"Rambling thought processes condensed:<br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"><br /> Ooh, this is a tricky one. I feel we should tell <i>someone</i>, mostly because up to this point we haven't been playing Kasai as a discrete sort.<br /> <br /> We <i>could</i> go to the lord and tell her, &quot;Hey, I found a guy covertly leaving your castle carrying what I assume is an encrypted message. He was killed by bandits.&quot; But we don't know how she would react or if she would see us at all. After all, Kasai isn't exactly visiting royalty.<br /> <br /> We know nothing about the Castle Guard, other than that there's a gap in their security.<br /> <br /> The captain of the city guard knows us already. He'd want to know about those bodies in the woods, if nothing else. But this will be the second time Kasai has found himself in an altercation, and he could earn himself a reputation as a troublemaker if these &quot;interventions&quot; become habit.<br /> <br /> Something else to consider is what each of these people could do for us. Any of them might be a good ally (assuming they respond positively to our message), but only Lord Shiba would be able to reward Kasai in a meaningful way. She might even be our ticket to stable, long-term employment as an Oathsworn. </div></div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> <b>I vote for going directly to the top - see Lord Iysen Shiba.</b> If we can't get to her immediately, the tourney might provide a plausible excuse for later.<br /> <br /> As a sidenote - clearly, Kasai isn't versed in espionage. After reading that the message was unreadable, literally the first thing I thought was &quot;Oh, it's a cipher.&quot; But then, I suppose those would be rare in an time and place where most people can't read anyway. Ah, for the days when a Caesarian substitution was all you needed to confound everyone...","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>On the frontier of the Wild South, there's only one woman with the grit to take on its most dangerous outlaws and bring them <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/984977-back-alive-or-maybe-dead-quest\" target=\"_blank\">Back Alive, or Maybe Dead.</a><br /> <br /> Avatar by <a href=\"http://puzzlinghappenstance.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">K.S. Brenowitz</a>","edited":{"userid":908,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/908-semicasual","user":"semicasual","date":". 12-20-2016, 08:43 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/908-semicasual","name":"semicasual","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar908_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2499"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-20T14:56:28-08:00","id":1019988,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>semicasual</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1019723#post1019723\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Rambling thought processes condensed:<br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"><br /> Ooh, this is a tricky one. I feel we should tell <i>someone</i>, mostly because up to this point we haven't been playing Kasai as a discrete sort.<br /> <br /> We <i>could</i> go to the lord and tell her, &quot;Hey, I found a guy covertly leaving your castle carrying what I assume is an encrypted message. He was killed by bandits.&quot; But we don't know how she would react or if she would see us at all. After all, Kasai isn't exactly visiting royalty.<br /> <br /> We know nothing about the Castle Guard, other than that there's a gap in their security.<br /> <br /> The captain of the city guard knows us already. He'd want to know about those bodies in the woods, if nothing else. But this will be the second time Kasai has found himself in an altercation, and he could earn himself a reputation as a troublemaker if these &quot;interventions&quot; become habit.<br /> <br /> Something else to consider is what each of these people could do for us. Any of them might be a good ally (assuming they respond positively to our message), but only Lord Shiba would be able to reward Kasai in a meaningful way. She might even be our ticket to stable, long-term employment as an Oathsworn. </div></div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> <b>I vote for going directly to the top - see Lord Iysen Shiba.</b> If we can't get to her immediately, the tourney might provide a plausible excuse for later.<br /> <br /> As a sidenote - clearly, Kasai isn't versed in espionage. After reading that the message was unreadable, literally the first thing I thought was &quot;Oh, it's a cipher.&quot; But then, I suppose those would be rare in an time and place where most people can't read anyway. Ah, for the days when a Caesarian substitution was all you needed to confound everyone...</div> </div> </div> </div>-On Kasai:<br /> Kasai is reasonably intelligent, and, for the time and place, rather well educated. He is fluent in Riverspeak, conversational in Dragon-tongue (enough to be able to recite and write poetry, really), and quasi-fluent in *some* dialects of Forest-tongue. He can read and write in all three.<br /> <br /> In my games, Low Realm is (kinda like. More like cultural &quot;stand-ins&quot;) Vulgar Latin + Standard/Simplified Chinese (and pretty well branched out into proto-psuedo-Romance languages at this point), High Realm is Classical Latin/ Traditional Chinese, the official language of the Immaculate Faith.<br /> Dragon-tongue is the super-fancified language of court nobility and calligraphy, aka Ye Old Shakespearean Butchered English.<br /> Sky-tongue is made up of various Germano-Celtic languages, using a runic script,<br /> Flame-tongue is Spanish meets Arabic.<br /> Sea-tongue is Hawaii'an plus other Oceanic tongues.<br /> Forest-tongue is Algonquin meets Japanese.<br /> <br /> What he isn't, however, is very good at mind-games, puzzles, and riddles. Much like myself, I hate the damned things. Since I can't figure them out to save my life, that basically means he can't. For most of his life, he has been able to pretty much bull his way through most problems, either with force (or implied force), or sheer charm. Discrete isn't really in his nature. While he is capable of being polite, if he doesn't like you, or your idea, you will find it out pretty quickly.<br /> <br /> Kasai is also relatively young (In Europe, &quot;knighthood&quot; was usually bestowed around the age of 21), and comparatively &quot;innocent&quot;, for a lack of a better term. Meaning, he almost certainly thinks that most Oathsworn will behave the way he tries to; that is, as upstanding, moral, and, well .... driven by the meaning of their oaths. Up until the Oathsworn bandit in Saiko's village, he hasn't really seen much to disprove that notion, but over the last couple of days, some cracks have formed in the shell of his relative idealism. He also hasn't really participated much in feudal politics, and hasn't truly witnessed the level of bloodthirsty single-mindedness some Lords will stoop to for power, for riches, or because they really don't care about the people beneath them.<br /> <br /> On Lord Shiba Iysen:<br /> Her &quot;official&quot; title is &quot;Lord Xia, Iysen Shiba&quot;, but she is very much the rough equivalent of high nobility, and while there are several domains and lords in the Hundred Kingdoms that are more powerful than she is .... she isn't on the bottom of the totem pole, either. Far from it. <br /> <br /> She isn't the only noble Lord in Xia. Discounting her retainers (landed and un-landed Oathsworn), she has sworn to her several smaller Lords, who themselves are wealthier, more powerful and more influential than Kasai's family. To them, he is pretty much just an up-jumped and well-lettered peasant.<br /> <br /> So, while her title might not be &quot;royal&quot;, Lord Iysen is very much the equivalent of such.<br /> <br /> However, keeping that in mind, Lord Iysen, while relatively dour (serious, gruff, severe, etc) , is also known to be fair and accepting. She will, at the very least, listen to what he has to say, <u>especially</u> with the proof he is capable of presenting. But, if he is going to talk to her, he has to do so <u>now</u>, as in, tonight, and soon. Tomorrow is the start of the tournament, and between that and the wedding right after, Lord Iysen will have very little time to listen to talk of conspiracies. Not to mention not be in the mood.<br /> <br /> -Other:<br /> <br /> The Captain of the City Guard is known (he and Kasai never actually met) to be a cigar-chomping, whisky-swilling hardass, but in a <b>good</b> way. He wants, and almost always gets, results, (which is why Lord Iysen promoted him to the position from the militia 15 years ago) and takes the complaints and needs of the common city-folk seriously. While the castle is technically outside his responsibility, he would send someone to check it out, and ensure Kasai's info and proof made it up to the proper channels. However, that means the effective end of Kasai's involvement.<br /> <br /> The Captain of the Castle Guard is a dick. He is likely to take Kasai's info, throw him out on his ass without as much as a &quot;thanks for the heads up&quot;, and pass it along to Lord Iysen as his own findings. Again, the info would get to the proper people, but it would be the end of Kasai's involvement in the matter.<br /> <br /> In a way, however that might be preferable. This whole thing is almost certainly going to end in blood.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-20T15:39:33-08:00","id":1020012,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Boston123</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1019988#post1019988\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">...</div> </div> </div> </div>None of this makes me inclined to change my answer.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>On the frontier of the Wild South, there's only one woman with the grit to take on its most dangerous outlaws and bring them <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/984977-back-alive-or-maybe-dead-quest\" target=\"_blank\">Back Alive, or Maybe Dead.</a><br /> <br /> Avatar by <a href=\"http://puzzlinghappenstance.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">K.S. Brenowitz</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/908-semicasual","name":"semicasual","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar908_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2499"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-20T15:43:25-08:00","id":1020017,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>semicasual</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1020012#post1020012\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> None of this makes me inclined to change my answer.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Not trying to.<br /> <br /> You see, I tend to write these all in one go, then post it before I get the chance to read.re-read, read again then delete the entire thread in self-conscious disgust and shame. So, when people bring up ideas, I go back, realize I actually left gaps, and try to elucidate things that were clear to me, or things that I forgot to elaborate on because I had them in my head and thought I put them in the text.<br /> <br /> I really, <u>really</u> struggle with choosing between putting in more (probably too much) detail; descriptions, exposition, more characters, and leaving my posts as they currently are. Part of writing my first Quest thread, I suppose","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11892,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","user":"Boston123","date":". 12-20-2016, 06:49 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-20T18:26:52-08:00","id":1020097,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Boston123</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1020017#post1020017\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> <br /> I really, <u>really</u> struggle with choosing between putting in more (probably too much) detail; descriptions, exposition, more characters, and leaving my posts as they currently are. Part of writing my first Quest thread, I suppose</div> </div> </div> </div>I had a similar issue with my quest at first. Except I put too little detail in my posts. I was trying not to lead the players to a decision. But what happened was the players were confused and asked a bunch of questions. Useful questions - I was able to learn to focus on the details that help the players make decisions.<br /> <br /> <br /> Also, I vote we go straight to the top. <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I write things.","edited":{"userid":3950,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3950-wonderandawe","user":"wonderandawe","date":". 12-20-2016, 10:03 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3950-wonderandawe","name":"wonderandawe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3950_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5410"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-20T21:12:02-08:00","id":1020167,"title":"","content":"I guess lord","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11567-jairain","name":"Jairain","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11567_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"345"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-22T21:22:09-08:00","id":1021136,"title":"","content":"Being clueless about espionage, would Kasai figure out that Lord Iysen might herself be involved in this plot and if so, could get angry/murderous about it being foiled?<br /> <br /> And does he know the Captain of the Castle Guard's reputation? Probably not.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">She/Her. I am literal-minded and write literally. If I don't say something explicitly, please never assume I implied it. The only exception is if I try to make a joke.</span></span></span></div> <span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">My point of view may be different from yours but is equally valid.</span></span></span><br /> <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1x1ycoxJF0G9niMt5D3Pp7owZ7tw8AJvL?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">Exalted-cWoD-ArM url mega-library</a>. Exalted <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/663334-random-name-generator\" target=\"_blank\">name-generators</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/896-erinys","name":"Erinys","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar896_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4235"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-23T03:33:01-08:00","id":1021212,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Erinys</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1021136#post1021136\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Being clueless about espionage, would Kasai figure out that Lord Iysen might herself be involved in this plot and if so, could get angry/murderous about it being foiled?<br /> <br /> And does he know the Captain of the Castle Guard's reputation? Probably not.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> 1) It's ........possible. Although Lord Iysen is unlikely to respond &quot;publically&quot; about being found out. She would just thank him civilly about finding the info, send him away, burn the evidence, and if he continued to pry, have Kasai assassinated.<br /> <br /> But, on the other hand, Lord Iysen is the sort who would make sure nobody caught on in the first place. Walking from the docks at midnight, in clearly-tracable clothing and identifiable equipment (weapons and cologne)? Sending a messenger who wasn't smart enough to wait until after the sun went down to climb down an exposed rock face? A group of scruffians who left said messenger buried in a shallow grave nearby?<br /> <br /> Not likely to be the ruler of the domain. Too obvious.<br /> <br /> And, I originally wrote a section where Kasai agonized over this distinct possibility, but cut it for length and for the fact that, no matter how I wrote it, it came off as narmy/angsty.<br /> <br /> 2) Reputation. People talk. People angry about getting blown off and mistreated by the guy talk <u>a lot</u>.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-23T07:49:15-08:00","id":1021274,"title":"","content":"So let's go to the top personally! Kasai is a direct guy.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">She/Her. I am literal-minded and write literally. If I don't say something explicitly, please never assume I implied it. The only exception is if I try to make a joke.</span></span></span></div> <span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">My point of view may be different from yours but is equally valid.</span></span></span><br /> <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1x1ycoxJF0G9niMt5D3Pp7owZ7tw8AJvL?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">Exalted-cWoD-ArM url mega-library</a>. Exalted <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/663334-random-name-generator\" target=\"_blank\">name-generators</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/896-erinys","name":"Erinys","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar896_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4235"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-27T19:36:04-08:00","id":1023074,"title":"","content":"I've got the next post <i>allmoosstttt</i> done, just trying to work out the final kinks. <br /> <br /> Tomorrow, for sure.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-28T18:22:06-08:00","id":1023523,"title":"","content":"Choice # 4 aka \u201cBring it all the way to the top\u201d it is!<br /> <br /> You stop and think for a moment, pondering your choices and the possible outcomes of each. Sure, you could technically bring this info to any of the authority-figures in the city, and something would get done as a result. But \u2026.. what would that do for <u>you</u>? In all likelihood, the info would just get passed on, levels of security would increase, and the plot would progress without any more involvement on your part. You would likely be \u201csafe\u201d from any repercussions, so long as you stay quiet about your part.<br /> <br /> On the other hand, doing so would completely eliminate any possible rewards or renown for foiling the plot, especially from a lord grateful for a spoiled assassination/coup attempt. On the other-other hand, even if an assassination or coup was foiled as a result of your actions, you<i> could</i> be viewed as an accomplice, or even just as a scapegoat for blame. <i>Those</i> kinds of things tend to end badly, even if you avoided outright execution or assassination. Your character in the region would be shot as long as you lived.<br /> <br /> You sigh. What the hell have you stumbled into?<br /> <br /> Glowering, you stare at the wall for quite some time, as the doctor works on Saiko and Blue Pines looks curiously at the various oddities lining the walls and shelves. All three of which start in surprise as you suddenly rise from your chair and make to put on your cloak.<br /> <br /> \u201cWhere are you going, Boss?\u201d asks Blue Pines<br /> <br /> \u201cI am going to bring this straight to Lord Iysen, as it almost overwhelmingly likely concerns her safety. It seems like the method most suited to actually seeing results, and Lord Iysen is known to be fair. I am not <i>that</i> concerned about repercussions, but if you don\u2019t hear from me in three days, flee the city. Go somewhere, <i>anywhere</i>, and lay low for a while. The money I\u2019ve earned should last you quite some time. With that in mind, I am going to ask you to repeat your story again, so I can be sure of the details.\u201d<br /> <br /> Perturbed, Blue Pines repeats her tale of following the mysterious Oathsworn from the docks to the castle, the passing-off of another package, and as many details as she can remember. The cut of the cloak the Oathsworn was wearing, how they walked, the odors of the cologne they were wearing (Lavender and some other flower), etc. Once you are certain of the details, you turn to leave without another word, before your followers can see the anxiousness on your face.<br /> <br /> No need to give them another thing to worry about.<br /> <br /> Mounting Xuansu, you make your way through the town to Teriake Castle, passing through the market so you can examine your person by the light of the lanterns. Being presentable would make it more likely you got an audience at all, much less being believed or even humored.<br /> <br /> Overall, your clothing is decent, if commonly-cut and a little dust-stained at the cuffs. You brush this off and run your fingers through your hair, trying to make some semblance of order in the mass of grey, tan, white and black strands. Eventually, you decide on your usual style (in a tail on the back of your head) and run your licked-palm over any flyaway strands in effort to make them lie down. It suddenly occurs to you that you are nervous. Very, very nervous. Asides from the obvious (that you are carrying proof of a plot against Lord Iysen on your person), this would be the first time you meet the equivalent of royalty. Back on your father\u2019s estate, you mainly interacted with your father\u2019s peers, themselves as equally poor and rural as your family, little more than up-jumped and lettered peasants compared to the likes of Lord Iysen.<br /> <br /> What if she doesn\u2019t want to see you at all? What if she takes offense at your impertinence of assuming you could have an audience? The doubts gnaw a hole in your heart, and they make the trip to the castle-gates seemingly take no time at all. <br /> <br /> Before you know it, the walls and gates loom over you, illuminated by the lanterns of patrolling guards, who look down on you with interest. After a moment, a small panel in the day-door of the gate slides open, revealing the eyes of one of the guards within.<br /> <br /> \u201cCan I help you?\u201d<br /> <br /> Dismounting from Xuansu and drawing yourself up to your full height, you walk over to the port and whisper at the guard.<br /> <br /> \u201cI have on my person something that would be of immense interest to the Lord Xia. It is of great importance that I present it to her, and to her alone.\u201d<br /> <br /> The mans eyebrow rises, \u201cInteresting, but I can\u2019t just let you in. I don\u2019t have the key, first of all, and this sounds like it\u2019ll be above my pay grade. I\u2019m sorry, but I\u2019m gonna have to go get my captain. He\u2019ll be able to help you once you talk to him\u201d.<br /> <br /> You frown, but it won\u2019t accomplish very much to argue. \u201c\u2026.very well.\u201d<br /> <br /> With that, the panel slams shut, and you are left in the dark. You become intimately aware that there is no cover from here to the town, well-within range for a crossbow, and you firmly grasp the handle of your sword to prevent your hands from trembling. Maybe this was a mistake.<br /> <br /> After a few minutes, the lock on the day-door rattles, and the foot-thick door swings open to reveal a small chamber, illuminated by a paper lantern and filled with armed men. The majority of them are guards, wearing helmets and armed with spears, but in front is the Captain of the Castle Guard.<br /> <br /> He can\u2019t as well be anything else. Dressed in mail and armed with a sword, the man stands with the figure of one used to giving orders, and having those orders followed without question. He is well-groomed, with a neatly-trimmed goatee and a top-knot. However, by far the most prominent aspect of his character is the aroma lingering about his person, evident even a couple of feet away. His cologne. The man stinks of lavender and roses.<br /> <br /> \u2026. Shit. This could be awkward.<br /> <br /> The Captain taps his foot impatiently, and you realize you were just staring at the man. He clears his throat and states,<br /> <br /> \u201cI am of the understanding that you are in possession of \u2018sensitive information\u201d concerning the Lord Xia?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c\u2026 Yes. But it is for he..\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c I am the Captain of her Guard, and partly responsible for her safety and person.\u201d He holds out his hand. \u201c I can, and will, be taking whatever information you have. I will present it to her Lordship when it is expedient. If you please, pass whatever you have over to me. Nobody else will see it, I assure you\u201d. His tone makes it certain that he isn\u2019t asking.<br /> <br /> \u201c\u2026 No. I mean no offense, but it is imperative I show it to her\u201d.<br /> <br /> The man sneers. \u201cYou think a ruffian such as yourself is worthy of speaking to the great Lord Xia in person?!. \u201c His hand falls down to the sword at his side. \u201cMen, arrest him, at once!. I\u2019ll see what you have on you, and you can spend a \u2018pleasant\u201d couple of evenings in the cells, until I figure out what best to do with you!\u201d.<br /> <br /> The guards surge forwards and you leap back, hand falling to your sword instinctively. The men circle around you, spears at the ready, and your heart jumps into your throat. This was the worst possible outcome, what were you thinking? The captain strides forwards, smirking fully now, and takes hold of your sword-arm, jerking you forwards towards the doorway<br /> <br /> \u201cWhat is all this shouting? What is going on?<br /> <br /> Almost instantly, the guards snap to attention, spears to their sides and bent at the waist in a bow. Even the Captain releases his grip on your wrist and bends his head in respect. From inside the doorway, descending the stairs from the top of the wall-walk, strides a woman, middle-aged and dressed in plain, but finely-cut, robes, and at her heels closely follows a warrior in full armor, hand on sword and partially-drawn.<br /> <br /> The Lord of Xia and head of the Shiba family Iysen Shiba, and her personal bodyguard. You are either saved or doomed.<br /> <br /> The Captain clears his throat, clearly surprised at her sudden interference. \u201cMy Lord, forgive me. I was not expecting to meet you so late at night.\u201d<br /> <br /> She raises an eyebrow. \u201cI was not aware that I was prohibited from walking along my own walls in my own home after dinner, Captain. Besides, that does not answer my question.\u201d<br /> <br /> He stumbles, \u201cForgive me, Lord. I was just apprehending this scoundrel, who has some nonsensical claim that he had information for you. Please forgive this disturbance.\u201d<br /> <br /> The other eyebrow raises, \u201cFor all you know, Captain Ito, he just might. Either way, manhandling him is not the way to get the information.\u201d She turns to the guards and directs them to stand down.<br /> <br /> The bodyguard rumbles from within his helm, \u201cLord, may I suggest we continue this exchange from within the safety of your own walls, as opposed to out here in the open?\u201d<br /> <br /> She shrugs, \u201cQuite. It would be warmer, anyways.\u201d With that, she turns and walks back through the door. The Captain stands and glares at you with a look that would have set tinder to smoldering, and then follows the Lord. Flanked (more like guided, shoulder-to-shoulder) by two of the guards, you bring up the rear. After Xuansu is brought in, the door is shut and locked with a boom, sealing you inside the castle.<br /> You are escorted to a small chamber, within one of the watchtowers along the outer wall of the castle. Inside, a brazier with some coals, a candle and a table and chairs are the sole furnishings. Lord Iysen sits on one side of the table, chin resting on a palm, and the Captain and her bodyguard flank both sides of the door.<br /> There is no possibility of escape.<br /> <br /> <br /> Once inside the room and free of your armed escort, you quickly follow protocol, falling to your knees and pressing your forehead to the floor in the most formal of formal bows. There, you wait until she indicates you to rise (with a slightly amused inflection to her command), whereupon you turn to the side and remove your sword from your sash, offering the sheathed blade to the imposing (seriously, the crest on his helmet almost scrapes the ceiling) bodyguard, who takes it without a word.<br /> <br /> Lord Iysen gestures for you to sit, then continues, \u201cNow, I understand you have something for me?\u201d<br /> <br /> You hesitate for a moment, \u201cYes, great Lord. I have on me certain evidence that suggests there is a plot in motion, either against you personally or against Xia\u201d. Contrary to your nervously-beating heart, your voice is strong and without stutter. Good.<br /> <br /> She raises an eyebrow and sits up in her chair. \u201c \u2026.. Interesting. If I may ask, could I see this proof, Oathsworn\u2026.?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c Kasai. Kasai Harutane. Son of Mino Harutane, himself of the domain of Tsen, sworn to Que.\u201d<br /> <br /> She stops for a moment, \u201cKasai. I know that name.\u201d Frowning, then \u201cDid you beat up a priest yesterday, in the market? My Captain of the Militia sent me up a report concerning that very topic, and I remember the name \u2018Kasai\u201d clearly\u201d<br /> <br /> You wince. \u201c \u2026.. Yes, Lord, I was the one mentioned in the report. I did <u>not</u> actually assault the priest, however. More of an accostation.\u201d<br /> <br /> She smirks, resting her chin on both palms. \u201cAnd, if I may ask, what caused you to accost this priest?<br /> <br /> \u201cI took offense with how he administered \u201cinstruction\u201d to a puppeteer, who was performing in the marketplace. He beat her, badly, breaking several bones and destroying her stand. All for the minor infraction of idolatry. If I did not intervene when I did, the woman could have been killed. As of now, she now serves as one of my armsmen.\u201d<br /> <br /> She sits back, raising an eyebrow again (this must be a personal affectation), \u201cSo, you hold the Oath of Compassion in high regard. Admirable, and rare.\u201d<br /> <br /> The Captain interjects, \u201cMy Lord, I really must protest. We are here to determine if the intelligence this man carries is noteworthy, not to conduct a performance review!\u201d<br /> <br /> She frowns, \u201cQuite.\u201d Turning back to you, \u201cIf I may, Kasai Harutane, might we see this information?\u201d<br /> <br /> You nod, \u201cGreat Lord, this information is not only physical, but also recollective, in the sense that events were witnessed, both by myself and by my retainers.\u201d<br /> <br /> She tilts her head, \u201cExplain, please.\u201d<br /> <br /> You take a deep breath.\u201d Several weeks ago, my current armsmen, the one whom I rescued from the turbulent priest, was working down by the riverside docks as a longshoreman. Late at night, at the end of one of her shifts, she took notice of a group of individuals down at an unused section of the wharf. She proceeded down to the group, to admonish their presence, only to hide when she saw what was occurring.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cShe told me that she saw several men, dressed for stealth and to prevent their faces from being witnessed, accept a package from another individual. This individual was well-dressed, bearing clothing of a cut not common to dockworkers or wealthy townsfolk. Upon receiving this package, the group headed downriver in a rowboat, with no lantern, and the individual proceeded to make their way back up to this very castle. My armsman tells me that she saw that the person was armed, with the straight sword indicative of an Oathsworn, much like myself. The individual also entered the castle not by the main gate, but by a sally-port on one of the towers, indicating they had access to a door-key.\u201d<br /> <br /> Lord Iysen purses her lips, and then raises her gaze to her Captain, who shifts slightly under her gaze. \u201cHow many keys are there to the castle-gates, Captain Ito?\u201d It wasn\u2019t really a question.<br /> <br /> \u201cThree, my Lord,\u201d comes the instant reply. One on my person, one on yours, and a third in the vault. My key never leaves my side, Lord. I sleep and bathe with it around my neck!\u201d<br /> <br /> Her lips deepen into a frown. \u201cWe shall have to see if that last key is in the vault, then.\u201d Turning back to you, \u201cPlease continue\u201d.<br /> <br /> \u201cThen, just today, my retainers and I were eating dinner, in a restaurant right at the foot of the mount this castle rests on, when my page spotted someone climbing down the cliff-face. Following them, my page and I came across a group of bandits hurriedly burying the climber in a shallow grave, while making ready to flee the area. After engaging the scoundrels, I found one of the bandits attempting to throw a package in the river. They were unsuccessful, and I picked up the package, as it was obviously important.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cAre the bodies still at the scene?\u201d<br /> <br /> You shrug. \u201cThey should be. This only happened right before dusk, right at sunset. A couple of hours ago, at most.\u201d<br /> <br /> Lord Iysen stands, \u201cCaptain Ito, take two of your men, whoever can be trusted to remain quiet about this, and go down and investigate this site. Where did you say it was?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cRight at the base of the cliff, on the river side, in a small copse of trees, Lord\u201d<br /> <br /> With that, she nods to Captain Ito, who bows and leaves the room. Lord Iysen remains standing, now pacing the small chamber, hands clasped behind her back.<br /> <br /> \u201cNow, I understand there was actual physical evidence as well?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cYes, Lord.\u201d, you say, reaching within your sash to pull out the letter. \u201cBut\u2026. If I may, and I do not want to imply anything, nor accuse anyone, but I left out a key detail of one of the stories.\u201d<br /> <br /> She stops pacing, turns to look at you, \u201cSince the good Captain is no longer present, I am assuming it has to do with him?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c\u2026\u2026 Possibly. When my armsman was stalking the well-heeled stranger from the docks, she took careful note of a very particular identifier of the man.\u201d<br /> <br /> She stares at you levelly. \u201cGo on\u201d<br /> <br /> At this, you sigh internally, all-but consigning yourself to a possible death<br /> .<br /> \u201cShe told me that the man passed quite close to her, close enough where his cologne was cloying. That said cologne reeked of lavender, and some other flower. But that the smell of lavender was particularly strong.\u201d<br /> <br /> Lord Iysen sits down, face in hands, slowly massaging her temples.<br /> <br /> \u201cAre you implying that the Captain of my castle guard is a part of this plot, Kasai Harutane?\u201d<br /> <br /> You are treading on <i>very</i> shaky ground, here. \u201cLike I stated earlier, Lord, I do not want to accuse anyone, not with the scant proof I have to offer. All I know is that the essence of lavender is expensive, out-of-reach for all but the wealthiest of merchants and nobility. Most of the Oathsworn here for the tournament are as poor as I am, and I am far from being able to afford cologne in general, much less essential essence of <i>anything.</i> And, when I first , <i>ah</i>, met your Captain Ito, the first thing I noticed about him was the scent of lavender and rosewater, itself rather expensive.\u201d<br /> <br /> Lord Iysen\u2019s bodyguard chimes in, \u201cCaptain Ito has been wearing heavy cologne as of late, my Lord, as well as fine clothing and other frivolities. This only started a few scant months ago.\u201d<br /> <br /> She sighs, nodding her head. \u201cI have noticed as well, Nori.\u201d Standing, \u201cThe only remaining option is to inspect my vault, to see if the one other key is there. If it is, then Captain Ito is all-but guilty of treason, or, at least, lending out the key to my fortress. Basically the same thing.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cHow so, Lord?\u201d<br /> <br /> She smiles grimly,\u201d Because I have the only key to the vault, Kasai Harutane. I am the only one with access. \u201cShe makes to leave. \u201c Come; let us get this over with, before the good Captain returns.\u201d<br /> <br /> With that, the three of you make your way deep into the bowels of the fortress, passing through hallways and down stairwells, until the rock walls of the cliff press tight overhead. Ahead of you, at the end of a long passageway, sits a rather depressingly-nondescript door. Striding forward, Lord Iysen pulls a small bronze key from underneath her robes and inserts it into the keyhole. From the other side of the door comes the rattle and clank of many chains, and it is a few moments before the door is able to be opened.<br /> <br /> Inside, as Lord Iysen\u2019s bodyguard raises the lantern, your jaw drops as you take in the sheer level of riches on display. Bars of silver, iron and copper, bolts of wool and silk <b><i>(silk</i></b>!), charters for damming rights, logging rights, guild-charters. Racks of spearheads, helmets on stands, enough to equip an army (which is probably its purpose). The wealth is boggling, and you stare, mouth open, until Lord Iysen reaches up and closes it with a <i>klik</i>.<br /> <br /> \u201cDo try not to stare, Kasai. It is making me self-conscious.\u201d She quips, amusedly.<br /> <br /> You shake your head embarrassedly. Is this how Saiko felt? You feel like such a yokel\u2026.<br /> <br /> Lord Iysen and her bodyguard are already at the far end of the room. Walking up to them, you take note of what they are looking at; a bronze plaque, adorned with hooks. On these hooks hang sets of keys, in iron and bronze. All of the hooks are full, and the keys are heavy with dust.<br /> <br /> \u201c\u2026. What does this mean, Lord Iysen?\u201d<br /> <br /> She sighs. \u201cIt means I have been betrayed, Kasai Harutane.\u201d Turning to her bodyguard, \u201cNori?\u201d<br /> <br /> The man bows, hand on his sword. \u201cYes, my Lord. Shall I bar the gate, or allow him inside?\u201d<br /> <br /> She ponders this for a moment.\u201d Allow him inside. If we can capture him, he will tell us what he knows.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cUnderstood.\u201d<br /> <br /> With this, your group leaves the vault, locking the door behind you, and head back up to the tower, where Lord Iysen reclines back in her chair, staring up at the ceiling. Nori buttonholes several of the guards, who hide in various places around the tower, ready to rush in at a moment\u2019s call.<br /> <br /> After a few silently-awkward moments of waiting, Captain Ito returns, striding in through the door and bowing at the waist.<br /> <br /> \u201cIt is as he says, my Lord. The bodies, the grave, all are present.\u201d<br /> <br /> Lord Isyen remains silent, still staring at the ceiling.<br /> <br /> \u201c.M..My Lord?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c\u2026 Your gate-key, Captain Ito. I wish to see it.\u201d<br /> <br /> The Captain blanches. \u201cMy gate-key, Lord?\u201d<br /> <br /> She sits up, staring directly at the Captain. \u201c Yes. The gate-key.\u201d<br /> <br /> He bites his lip, face pale. \u201cAre\u2026 are you <i>accusing</i> me of losing the key!?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c\u2026..Yes. Are you confessing?\u201d<br /> <br /> The Captain wheels about, glaring at you. \u201c <i>You!</i> You accuse me of wrong-doing, you peasant!. How dare you! You have no proof! Lord, you <u>must</u> believe me, I did not do anything!\u201d<br /> <br /> Her eyes are as hard as stone. <i>\u201cYour key</i>, Ito Aori.\u201d<br /> <br /> His gaze jumps from you, to Lord Iysen, to the massive man now standing behind him, hand on sword. With a curse, he jumps up and makes for the door, only to get clotheslined by the steel bracer of Nori. He drops to the floor, choking, and Nori gives him a solid kick to the ribs to ensure he stays down.<br /> <br /> Lord Iysen stands. \u201cStrip him, Nori.\u201d<br /> <br /> Reaching down, Nori yanks off the former captain\u2019s armor and clothing, popping the buttons on his mail and tearing cloth. In a minute, Ito Aori lies naked before you. There is no key around his neck.<br /> <br /> Exhaling slowly, Lord Iysen rubs at the bridge of her nose. With a quick command, she orders the former captain brought to a cell, and for Nori to personally ransack his former quarters to see if the gate-key can actually be found. Standing, she gestures for you to follow her.<br /> <br /> \u201cIf I am going to be doing anything else tonight, it is going to be with a drink in hand, in some place more comfortable.\u201d<br /> <br /> And so you head up into the central keep, followed at a respectful distance by two guards. Higher and higher you go, up to the upper levels, until you walk through a door into the Lords personal Solar. Thick carpets cover the floor, wall-hangings and curtains adorn the walls, and there are comfortable tables and chairs throughout. After a servant kindles a fire in the fireplace and brings a lantern and a carafe of hot coffee, the two of you are left alone.<br /> <br /> Pulling out the letter, the two of you bend over it, and almost instantly Lord Iysen bursts out,<br /> <br /> \u201cWhat is this?!\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cForest-tongue, Lord. The glyphic language of the Hill-tribes.\u201d<br /> <br /> She frowns, glaring at the mass of branching glyphs. \u201cYou can read this?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cYes, Lord. My grandfather taught me to both read and speak it. I must tell, you, however\u2026.<br /> <br /> She waves her hand. \u201cKasai, I know this is a breach of proper protocol, but you are already inside my private quarters, drinking my coffee and telling me about a conspiracy. You don\u2019t have to be so <i>stiff</i>, at the least.\u201d<br /> <br /> You stiffen, surprised, then laugh. \u201cIf that is what you want, Lord.\u201d<br /> <br /> She shrugs,\u201d If you say everything so formally, something might get lost in the doublespeak. For now, at least, you may speak plainly.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c\u2026.sure.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cSo, you were saying this is, what, Forest-tongue?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cYes. It isn\u2019t really used as a \u201creading\u201d alphabet, not really. More of a symbolic and ritualistic one. Each character can be read vertically and diagonally, and word-breaks and punctuation is indicated by specific \u201cbranches\u201d. You start at the middle-bottom and work upwards, like a tree\u2026..\u201d<br /> <br /> You work your way through the document, writing out what each \u201csentence\u201d means, only to be interrupted by Nori, who informs the Lord Iysen that there was no sign of the gate-key in any of the former captain\u2019s quarters, nor the barracks. Lord Iysen acknowledges this with a nod, and Nori takes a seat by the door.<br /> <br /> You finish translating the Forest-tongue into Riverspeak, and it makes just as much sense as it did before (read: none). Lord Iysen stares at the words for a few minutes, then asks,<br /> <br /> \u201cKasai, have you ever heard of a cipher?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c\u2026. A what?\u201d You never heard of that, living out in the hinterland.<br /> <br /> \u201cA cipher. It is a means to disguise letters as something else, to hide the meaning of a document. I use one all the time for personal correspondence, and I am willing to bet this is in a cipher as well.\u201d She stands and walks over to a desk. Pulling out another key, she unlocks a drawer and takes out a small brass disc. On one \u201cside\u201d of the disk are the 36 characters of Riverspeak, and on the other is a matching disk. Both sides are able to spin, letting each alphabet match up with other letters.<br /> <br /> \u201cThis is called a substitution cipher. It basically replaces a letter of Riverspeak with another. It is probably the most common cipher in existence, and I am almost certain the particular cipher will be on here.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cWhy?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cNot many people actually speak Forest-tongue, as you said. Therefore, all the \u2018difficulty\u2019 of disguising the contents was done by translating to Forest-tongue. A complicated cipher would be superfluous.\u201d<br /> <br /> She sits down, and with a pad of paper between you, you start copying down each possible substitution of Riverspeak that is possible using the disk. Even with the disk, each substitution still takes some time to do. The lantern has burned down and the coffee is long-cold before Lord Iysen sits up, startling you from your doze, and blurts out,<br /> <br /> \u201cI think I\u2019ve got it.\u201d<br /> <br /> You bend over the paper, and even with your fatigue-blurred eyes, the message is still clearly visible in Lord Iysen\u2019s neat hand.<br /> <br /> <i>S.</i><br /> <i>The preparations are all in place. The footpads all know their places, the armaments are secured, and I have even got help from several greedy persons inside the castle. It is amazing what a little silver passed around can do! We only wait for day of the wedding, after the tournament, and everything our ancestors have yearned for will come to pass! Be patient, I play my part well.</i><br /> <i>Yours, H.</i><br /> <br /> Lord Iysen sits quiet for some time, absorbing the meaning of the letter.<br /> <br /> \u201cSo, Lord, what does this mean?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cI am \u2026. Not sure. It suggests either an assassination, or a coup-attempt, but the letter itself doesn\u2019t really imply either\u2026\u2026.\u201d<br /> <br /> She looks up from the letter, out the windows and across the Red River. The eastern horizon is stained red with the coming of the sun, and birdsong is starting to filter its way up to where you are sitting.<br /> <br /> \u201cEither way, I have three days to figure out what it does mean.\u201d She frowns, shakes her head. \u201cWonderful, just what I needed, on top of planning a wedding and officiating at a tournament.\u201d<br /> <br /> You cough delicately, \u201cI was actually planning on competing in the tournament, Lord\u2026\u2026 If you want, I can ask around if anyone has seen anything suspicious\u2026\u201d<br /> <br /> She shakes her head, \u201cNo, that would draw too much attention. I don\u2019t want to spring a trap too early and scare off the conspirators.\u201d<br /> <br /> She turns and looks out the window again. \u201cEither way, I need rest. The day was already going to be busy, and now it will be even worse.\u201d Walking over to Nori (who is still wide awake. Is the man even human?) and whispering something in his ear, she turns to leave, but not before looking to you,<br /> <br /> \u201cKasai Harutane, we thank you for your assistance in this matter. Without your forewarning, we would have no idea about this plot concerning our possible safely. We owe you our thanks, and in return, offer you a place to rest for the night\u201d. With this, she nods her head (about as much of a bow as you are probably ever going to get) and closes the door softly.<br /> <br /> Groggily following the bodyguard, you are led to a small chamber on the lower levels of the keep. Inside, there is little more than a futon, some blankets, and a rack holding your sword, all of which you quickly take in before falling to the futon, asleep in moments with your clothing still on.<br /> <br /> It only feels like a few moments have passed before you are woken up by a soft knock on the door. Outside, a serving-girl offers you a bowl of heated water and some fresh clothing, both of which are welcome. After you perform your morning ablutions and put on the fresh clothing, the girl informs you that the Lord Xia is waiting for you up in her personal dining room.<br /> Confused (and still quite fatigued, which almost certainly is contributing to the confusion), you allow yourself to be led up the stairs again; back to the room you were in a few scant hours ago. Inside, Lord Iysen sits at a long trestle table, set up for breakfast.<br /> <br /> Seeing you standing awkwardly, not sure of what to do, she calls out, \u201cSit. I kept you here all night, I might as well give you something to eat. The tournament can\u2019t start until I get there anyways, so might as well do something useful with the time.\u201d, and you gratefully sink down at one of the corners. A servant offers you a cup of coffee, which you accept happily (you might be developing a taste for the stuff. Not good, as it is expensive, imported from the Southeast.). Only a moment later, the door opens again and a group of people move through, talking far too animatedly for your taste this early in the morning.<br /> <br /> Lord Iysen embraces each as they come in, and introduces you.<br /> <br /> \u201cChildren, this is Kasai Harutane, an Oathsworn who has been of immense assistance to me of an important matter, yet in development. He will be joining us for breakfast today.\u201d<br /> <br /> She gestures at the oldest, a girl about your age.<br /> \u201cKasai, this is Mino, my eldest daughter and heir. She is betrothed to Haichi Matsu, son of the Lord Yue.\u201d<br /> <br /> The middle, a girl of about Saiko\u2019s age.<br /> \u201cMy middle child, Airi.\u201d<br /> <br /> And the last, a young boy around 10.<br /> \u201cAnd, last but certainly not least, my son Aketchi, who has just started arms training\u201d.<br /> <br /> The boy bounces closer, eyes excited, \u201c So, Mr. Kasai, why is your hair so <i>weird</i>?\u201d<br /> <br /> The sudden silence is only broken by the sound of Airi dope-slapping her forehead, and Lord Iysen sighs.<br /> <br /> \u201cIt is way too early in the morning for you to be so <i>forward</i>, Aketchi. Please apologize to the man.\u201d<br /> <br /> You hold up your hand in protest, \u201cIn all honesty, Lord, his questions are far more innocent than most.\u201d Turning to the lad, \u201cMy hair and eyes look like this, Master Aketchi, because I was born amongst the Hill-tribes, in the mountains to the north. We all have some form of indication of our heritage as Wyld-touched.\u201d<br /> <br /> Lord Iysen sips her coffee. \u201cYou said that you were taught Forest-tongue by your grandfather, yet you are an Oathsworn warrior. Were you raised along the Tribes?<br /> <br /> Nodding, \u201cPartially. After my mother died, I lived with my father for some time. After a few years, my grandfather told me it was time for me to experience the other half of my heritage, and fostered me at Dun Paunuxet for several years. In addition to Forest-tongue, he also taught me archery and woods-craft.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cDun Paunuxet? So you are a member of the \u2026.. ah\u2026.. Nemasket tribe?\u201d<br /> <br /> You are surprised. Not too many Lowlanders know about the different tribal groups and Confederations.<br /> <br /> \u201cYes. He was the Chief back then.\u201d<br /> <br /> Eyebrow raised, Lord Isyen sets down her coffee. \u201c\u2026 You are telling me that you are the relative of a tribal Chief?\u201d<br /> \u201cNo. He is no longer the Chief of the Nemasket. He hasn\u2019t been so for many years.\u201d<br /> <br /> Confused, \u201cWhat exactly do you mean?\u201d<br /> <br /> Ruing revealing this much information already, \u201cA Chief can only be removed from station through death, a vote of no-confidence by the tribal council, or through ascension to another post.\u201d<br /> \u201cSo, he is dead then? I am sorry\u201d.<br /> <br /> Slowly, anticipating the upcoming reaction and not looking forwards to it. \u201c No.\u201d<br /> <br /> Her eyes narrow. \u201c No?. Out with it, man.\u201d<br /> <br /> Taking a deep breath, \u201cMy grandfather is the Warchief of the Dumoniapoccasset Confederation, Lord.\u201d<br /> <br /> Lord Iysen sets her hands down on the table, silent for some time, as her children look confusedly at her, then at you.<br /> <br /> \u201cMother, what does that mean?<br /> <br /> She takes a deep breath, \u201cIt means we are basically breaking bread with the equivalent of Hill-tribe royalty.\u201d<br /> <br /> Surprised at the revelation you,<ol class=\"decimal\"><li>Drive home your relation to your Grandfather. Damn straight you are important!</li> <li>Downplay said relation. Humility is a virtue.</li> <li>Describe in detail how Hill-Tribe society works.</li> <li>Something else?</li> </ol>OOC: (<u>you actually should probably read this)</u><br /> Firstly, I would like to describe some of the funny-looking words I am throwing around.<br /> <br /> I live in New England, and a couple hundred years ago, the place was populated with Native tribal groups. The one native to where I live was called the Wampanoag, a subset ( the reality is far more complicated than this, but bear with me) of the larger Algonquin cultural group, which in turn spread across most of the Northeast US and eastern Canada.<br /> <br /> I tend to use examples of Native American culture in tribal groups across my version of Creation, and Wampanoag/Algonquian is especially prevalent in the East and Northeast. I find it adds a bit of flair to my otherwise boring whitebread northern European cultures that I usually play with. For example, in Wampanoag, the suffix \u201c\u2013et\u201d usually indicates a place-name, and you will see it in many town, river and landform names around southern New England (Nemasket, Poccasset, Mattapoisett, Acushnet, etc) My home state of Massachusetts comes from the <i>Massachuset</i> tribe, which means \u201cpeople of the great hills\u201d, referencing the Blue Hills (<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue_Hills_Reservation\" target=\"_blank\">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue_Hills_Reservation</a>) in the Boston area<br /> <br /> One of the most common aspects of Native American life is use is agricultural techniques and crops. I am a big fan of the Colombian Exchange, and therefore, much of Creation is going to be growing a mix of Old World and New World crops. Most commonly, I am going to have most farmers grow the \u201cThree Sisters\u201d, an interplanting of corn, beans and pumpkins (itself arguably the most effective pre-Industrial form of agriculture, space and efficiency-wise), along with potatoes and sunflowers.<br /> <br /> Also, most groups are going to have some cultural aspect of Algonquin origin thrown in. Most of the time, this is going to be governmental, with tribal/familial \u201celders\u201d voting on matters of state, and the chief/king being a diplomat and warleader (much like the modern US President) as opposed to decision-maker. I am doing this in my up-and-coming Byrtenwalda campaign, which meshes well with the real-world Germanic \u201cassembly\u201d system.<br /> <br /> Secondly, Lord Iysen is <u>seriously</u> misrepresenting the relationship between Kasai and his grandfather here. The Tribes don\u2019t exactly practice inheritance of position like the Lowlanders, and Kasai can\u2019t really get much of anything &quot;concrete&quot; from his grandfather, asides from being the relative of the most powerful Tribal warleader in the northwestern Hundred Kingdoms.<br /> <br /> Kasai\u2019s grandfather got his position by getting elected by the Chiefs of the 12 tribes of the Dumoniapoccasset Confederation, and can only get removed from the position by dying, failing a vote of confidence, or being challenged for the role and losing (not likely). When he dies (or is removed), Kasai will not \u201cinherit\u201d the position, it isn\u2019t like a Lordship of a domain.<br /> <br /> On the other hand, Kasai is the much-beloved grandson of the figurative equivalent of Metacomet and Verceingetorix. While Kasai\u2019s grandfather isn\u2019t actually capable of making decisions-of-state on his own (he is merely the \u201cface\u201d of the Confederation, representing it in peace and war. The Elder council of the 12 Tribes makes the actual decisions) , he is still <u>insanely</u> influential, with a large number of warriors in his warband, and his word carries heavy weight. If Kasai could persuade him to act in a certain way, a decent number of Hill-Tribes would follow suit, or at least be amenable.<br /> <br /> On the other hand, the Dumoniapoccasset Confederation is basically at war with most of the domains in the Hundred Kingdoms. There isn\u2019t <u>official</u> war, but warbands of young, glory-hungry warriors regularly raid trade caravans, outlying farms and suchlike. For more information, look up \u201cMourning Wars\u201d to get an idea.<br /> <br /> Make of this what you will.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-28T21:16:07-08:00","id":1023575,"title":"","content":"<i>Downplay said relation. Humility is a virtue.</i><br /> Kasai may be a grandson, but there are sons and other grandsons. Tell the Lord this, and liken our position to a poor relation. It is actually true. We could get a message through, but otherwise have no real influence or standing. It is why we are a wanderer...<br /> <br /> forgot to say sons and daughters, other grandsons and granddaughters...","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Like my Avatar? Courtesy of<span style=\"color:#0000FF\"> Jen</span>! : <span style=\"font-size:12px\">Anybody want their characters to be experimented on ? </span>post 98<br /> <i>An Exalt is never unarmed.</i>","edited":{"userid":4018,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4018-l-het-esh","user":"L'het'esh","date":".  forgot 01-01-2017, 04:17 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4018-l-het-esh","name":"L'het'esh","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4018_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"521"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-29T12:21:16-08:00","id":1023886,"title":"","content":"Describe in detail how Hill-Tribe society works.<br /> <br /> I assume this will inevitably involve some form of the phrase &quot;Well, technically yes, but I don't actually get any privileges or authority by blood.&quot;","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>On the frontier of the Wild South, there's only one woman with the grit to take on its most dangerous outlaws and bring them <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/984977-back-alive-or-maybe-dead-quest\" target=\"_blank\">Back Alive, or Maybe Dead.</a><br /> <br /> Avatar by <a href=\"http://puzzlinghappenstance.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">K.S. Brenowitz</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/908-semicasual","name":"semicasual","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar908_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2499"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-30T18:19:59-08:00","id":1024595,"title":"","content":"Humility, educating her is good but not in font of her children, that could be taken as correcting her.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11567-jairain","name":"Jairain","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11567_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"345"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-01T09:59:09-08:00","id":1025092,"title":"","content":"I'd go with humility. Your wandering life style and relative poverty are probably best explained by downplaying your relation.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8897-joshopotamus","name":"joshopotamus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8897_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"97"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-01T10:45:49-08:00","id":1025110,"title":"","content":"Describe in detail on how Hill-Tribe Society works.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I write things.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3950-wonderandawe","name":"wonderandawe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3950_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5410"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-08T05:03:34-08:00","id":1028506,"title":"","content":"Sorry guys, I meant to write a post yesterday, but got called in to plow out my city after a blizzard..<br /> <br /> On one hand, 19 hours in a smelly truck making small piles of snow into larger piles is the very antithesis of enjoyable, but on the other, 19 hours of time-and-a-half is quite nice.<br /> <br /> I'll see when I can actually write the damn thing, I have it all done out in my head.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-08T17:48:12-08:00","id":1028801,"title":"","content":"Family and work have priority, Happy New Year!<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Like my Avatar? Courtesy of<span style=\"color:#0000FF\"> Jen</span>! : <span style=\"font-size:12px\">Anybody want their characters to be experimented on ? </span>post 98<br /> <i>An Exalt is never unarmed.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4018-l-het-esh","name":"L'het'esh","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4018_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"521"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-09T17:12:14-08:00","id":1029351,"title":"","content":"Thanks for plowing! Comming from somewhere super snowy we all appreciate it!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11567-jairain","name":"Jairain","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11567_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"345"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-17T20:03:31-08:00","id":1033849,"title":"","content":"Ugh.<br /> <br /> This next post is increasingly difficult to write. I've come to learn that dialogue outside my head isn't my strong suit, and I have scrapped the damn thing about twice now, as it sounds increasingly wooden.<br /> <br /> Regardless, progress is progress, yet I am quite embarrassed to see that my last actual post-post was three weeks ago tomorrow. Any other Quest-writers have quasi-writer's block happen, when you have a scene planned out in your head, yet can't get it down &quot;on paper&quot; satisfactorily?<br /> <br /> I've got that <b><u><i>bad</i></u></b>.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-18T06:06:42-08:00","id":1033973,"title":"","content":"At times like those, I just sit myself down in front of the computer and refused to leave until the page is done.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>On the frontier of the Wild South, there's only one woman with the grit to take on its most dangerous outlaws and bring them <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/984977-back-alive-or-maybe-dead-quest\" target=\"_blank\">Back Alive, or Maybe Dead.</a><br /> <br /> Avatar by <a href=\"http://puzzlinghappenstance.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">K.S. Brenowitz</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/908-semicasual","name":"semicasual","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar908_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2499"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-18T06:34:06-08:00","id":1033981,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Boston123</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1033849#post1033849\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Any other Quest-writers have quasi-writer's block happen, when you have a scene planned out in your head, yet can't get it down &quot;on paper&quot; satisfactorily?<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>I've had that before. This is what I do:<br /> <br /> 1. I get something down on paper. Anything. It may be crap, but I find it's easier to figure out what is wrong with the post when it is out of my head. <br /> 2. I'll delay the post a bit. Most of the time, the post isn't coming together because I got other things on my mind, like work. <br /> 3. I'll give up, post the damn thing, and move on. No post is perfect and frankly, a writer is their own worst critic. This is why I have a schedule. To tell me when it is time to stop messing around with a scene and move on to the next scene. <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I write things.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3950-wonderandawe","name":"wonderandawe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3950_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5410"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-19T03:50:10-08:00","id":1034513,"title":"","content":"If there is still time, maybe we could <b>humbly explain it isn't quite like that.</b>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Thoughts ripple out, birthing others","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1624-falleneco","name":"FallenEco","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1624_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1927"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-19T18:35:30-08:00","id":1034985,"title":"","content":"Thanks for the words of advice, Semicasual and wonderandawe. I took a step back, looked at how things were working out, and decided to change up my writing methods a bit. Now, instead of writing a whole gigantic post all in one go, I have been working on it steadily over the last couple of days. Since Monday, I have written 12 pages or so, with much less stress.<br /> <br /> As for FallenEco ..... there is plenty of time. I have a tendency to &quot;blend together&quot; different choices, depending on how popular they are, so if there is a &quot;runner up&quot;, I usually incorporate some aspects of it in the next post.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-21T20:51:14-08:00","id":1035936,"title":"","content":"Downplay your relation to your Grandfather/ describe how Hill-Tribe society works<br /> <br /> You blink, rather startled by the semi-positive reception such a revelation has brought about. Usually, when you bring this up, most people respond <u>extremely</u> negatively, usually with cries of \u201c savage heathen\u201d and \u201c marauding bastard\u201d being the \u201cnicest\u201d, leading you to outright refuse to acknowledge the issue at all. The flak you catch from your obvious ancestry is enough.<br /> <br /> \u201cLord it\u2026. the situation is <u>far</u> more complicated than what you make it to be, if I may say so without giving offense.\u201d<br /> <br /> Lord Isyen leans forward, cheek resting on her hand, \u201cIs it now? It does not seem so. I may not be as well-versed in Hill-Tribe culture as you are, but I know enough to recognize that your grandfather has more warriors under his command than I do, and has influence spread across the entirety of the western Tribes. If he were to attack Xia outright, as opposed to the cattle-raids and border-skirmishes he limits himself to, there is not much I could do to hold him back, if I may be frank. And you are telling me that you are a direct relative of the man. What am I misunderstanding, exactly?\u201d<br /> <br /> Swallowing, you continue, \u201cIf I may address my relation to my grandfather, first?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cOf course. I find the implications <i>greatly</i> interesting.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cFirstly, the Tribes do not practice fealty and oaths of servitude like the Hundred Kingdoms do. That means that they do not have the same ideas of inheritance. Back when my grandfather was the Chief of the Nemasket, his daughter was, socially, \u201cjust\u201d another tribeswoman, albeit the daughter of the chieftan. Myself, as his grandson, do not stand to receive any honor or glory from his position, the exact opposite as a matter of fact. If he were to die, or be removed from his post, I would not inherit the position of Warchief. The position is elected by the other Chiefs of the Confederation, and I would not be welcome to it, to put it lightly. \u201c<br /> <br /> Spreading your hands, \u201cI mean, there is a reason I am a wandering sword, not dressed in furs and gold.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cSecondly, I haven\u2019t seen, much less talked with, my grandfather in years. I started Oathsworn training when I was nine years of age, and during that period I lived with my father on his estate. I am 21 now, so I haven\u2019t been face-to-face with him for 12 or so years. The last time I heard from him was about 4 years ago, when he wrote me a letter.\u201d<br /> <br /> She sits up, raising an eyebrow, \u201cYour grandfather speaks Riverspeak?\u201d<br /> <br /> Nodding, \u201cYes, amongst several other languages. He reads it as well.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cInteresting. My sworn lords always portrayed him as an unlettered savage, to be honest. To learn otherwise makes me reconsider several preconceptions I had about him. Please continue.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cThirdly, my grandfather\u2019s position is quite different from what it appears you view it as. While he is the Warchief of the confederation, his powers as Warchief are actually pretty limited.\u201d<br /> <br /> Now Lord Iysen looks confused. \u201cWhat exactly do you mean?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cAs Warchief, his authority is only to lead the confederations warriors in battle, and to represent the confederation to outsiders. He doesn\u2019t actually \u201cmake\u201d any decisions, like matters of state or declarations of war. The decision-making power of the Tribes is instead invested in Tribal Elders, who make day-to-day decisions for the tribes and the confederation as a whole when they actually gather.\u201d<br /> <br /> You shrug, \u201cTo be honest, I have no idea where the yearly elder council meets, or what they actually \u201cdo\u201d; \u201cnormal\u201d tribespeople weren\u2019t supposed to attend, and the whole thing is steeped in ritualism and mysticism. In order to do something like declare war or make peace, my grandfather would have to petition either the Nemasket elder council, for matters pertaining to my tribe, or the Confederation council, for the confederation as a whole. And, just because he is the Warchief doesn\u2019t mean he always gets what he wants, either. I can remember plenty of times when he would come home upset with some decision the Elder council made.<br /> <br /> \u201cBut the position must carry <i>some</i> degree of influence, correct\u201d?<br /> <br /> \u201cOf course. He <i>is</i> the head Warchief of the Confederation, elected by the 11 other Chiefs of the tribes. By my understanding, his word carries great weight on the Council, just not enough to <i>always</i> get what he wants. He still has to wheedle, compromise and coerce the Elders into making favorable decisions.\u201d<br /> <br /> She laughs, \u201cPolitics, as always! It is a pleasant thought to think that all men are the same.\u201d Sitting back, she picks up her coffee again. \u201cSo, what does this all have to do with the nigh-constant raids and skirmishes that my marcher-lords always complain about?\u201d<br /> <br /> Again, you shrug, \u201cPardon me, Lord, but I really do not know. When I left, the Dumoniapoccasset were not at war with Xia.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cBut these raids have been occurring since my father was Lord, how are we not at war?\u201d<br /> <br /> You think for a moment, \u201cAs far as my understanding goes, there is a difference between \u201cwar\u201d and \u201craids\u201d among the Hill-tribes. If you were at war \u2026\u2026 trust me, you would know. There would be fewer cattle-raids and more burnt villages, if you understand the implication.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cA common statement amongst the Tribes is \u201cdeeds, not blood\u201d. Chieftans and elders are not appointed based on who they are descended from, but elected by their supporters. A warrior gains fame by engaging and overcoming enemies, and warleaders gain warriors by hosting feasts, giving gifts, and through success in battle, leading though charisma and reputation as opposed to loyalty.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cWith that in mind, many young men seek to gain fame and status through raiding on their own initiative, without the leadership and \u2026. moderation of a warchief. They band together and attack others almost indiscriminately, other Tribes included. My grandfather spent more time warding off intertribal squabbles than he did fighting your lords.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cTo the chiefs, these bands are little more than excited boys, burning off energy in a way that prevents them from causing problems in their own villages. More to the point, because they are not under the command of a chieftain, their actions are not considered \u201cwar\u201d, and effectively, the chieftains have very little, if any, control over their actions.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cSo \u2026.. if I were to lead a retributive strike against these raiders, how would the tribes react?\u201d<br /> <br /> You cringe, \u201cAlmost certainly overwhelmingly negative. As far as they are concerned, such an attack would be entirely unwarranted, and the Tribes would band together to retaliate. Entire sections of your country would be laid to waste. I \u2026. Cannot recommend such an action, it would be effective suicide.\u201d<br /> <br /> She nods, considering the implications. \u201cGood thing that is what I have been telling my marcher-lords, for all the good it has done. Some of them are positively chomping at the bit to charge into the mountains.\u201d<br /> <br /> Lifting her head to look at you directly,\u201d Finally, are you on good terms with your grandfather?\u201d<br /> <br /> You are taken aback, never truly having considered the issue before.<br /> <br /> \u201cI \u2026 I think so. I remember he was disappointed when I left to live with my father, but I never understood why. Thinking back, I get the feeling that he wanted me to stay with the Tribes and learn more about the other side of my family, but I had made up my mind at an early age that I wanted to be an Oathsworn, like my father.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cIf I remember correctly, I am the only current member of his family still alive. He had only one wife, and one daughter, and my mother left to live with my father at an early age, younger than I am now. He had grey hair when I was living with him, so he must be pretty old by now\u2026\u2026\u201d<br /> <br /> The room is silent for a minute, as you realize the implications of that thought. You are startled from your introspection by Lord Isyen idly tapping her fingers on the table, herself deep in thought.<br /> <br /> \u201cHm. If what you are telling me is true, then hypothetically, if I were to send you with a message, how would it..\u201d<br /> <br /> She gets interrupted by the wood-and-paper door suddenly sliding open, startling the diners seated around the able, who were apparently leant forward listening to your story. In strides a young man, about your age, dressed in fine silks in spite of the early-morning humidity, a fancy sword (seemingly too light in the blade, yet handle-heavy with ornamentation, a show-piece effectively) thrust through his sash.<br /> <br /> He stops, offers a perfunctory bow, little more than a nod of the head, to the Lord seated at the head of the table.<br /> <br /> \u201cPlease, pardon my tardiness. I had business to attend to early this morning, down at the tournament-grounds. It took longer than expected.\u201d Realizing you are present, and looking down his nose at you, \u201cLord Iysen, who is this?\u201d<br /> <br /> Lord Isyen raises her hand to introduce you. \u201cThis is Kasai Harutane, an Oathsworn who has been assisting me with a very important task, of which I am incredibly grateful. Kasai, this is Haichi Matsu, the eldest son of the Lord Yue and betrothed to Mino Shiba.\u201d<br /> <br /> You rise to your feet, bowing at the waist. Even if you are lower in status, at least you were raised with <i>manners</i>.<br /> <br /> Essentially ignoring your greeting, Matsu grabs a piece of bread, smearing it with jam and pours himself a cup of coffee. \u201cIf I may ask, Lord, what is he helping you with? If he is so proficient, perhaps he can help me as well.\u201d<br /> <br /> You shoot a quick glance to the Lord, who acknowledges it with an almost-imperceptible nod.<br /> <br /> \u201cI am assisting the Lord Xia with matters concerning security of the tournament, Master Matsu.\u201d<br /> <br /> Matsu stops, bread halfway to his mouth, \u201cReally?\u201d He turns to the Lord, \u201cI thought Captain Ito and Captain Reeds were collaborating on that. Did they find anything that would affect the wedding?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cIto Aori is no longer in my employ, Haichi.\u201d<br /> <br /> He starts, surprised, \u201cWhy not?\u201d<br /> <br /> The Lords face hardens. \u201cHis performance was no longer satisfactory, that is all you need to know. Why is this so concerning? Both the tournament and the wedding will proceed as planned\u201d.<br /> <br /> He sits for a minute, then bows, embarrassed. \u201cForgive me. The man owes me money, and to suddenly learn that I will not get it back is \u2026\u2026 disheartening. I forgot my place\u201d.<br /> The Lord forgives him with a wave of her hand, and her eldest daughter Mino leans up next to her betrothed.<br /> <br /> \u201cWas it the dog-fighting again, Haichi?\u201d<br /> <br /> He laughs, \u201cNo, the ponce wanted some coin to impress some tart, downby the market. I leant it to him, supporting his professions of love, which in hindsight was a bad idea. May the bastard catch some pox, that is retribution enough!\u201d<br /> <br /> The boy Aketchi giggles and Mino facepalms again, and Lord Shiba chides the man for his inappropriate language. After a moment, the awkwardness of the sudden interruption fades out, and Airi leans forward.<br /> <br /> \u201cPlease, sir Harutane, if you would tell us some stories from your childhood, growing up as you did\u201d.<br /> <br /> At another nod from Lord Iysen, you begin to regale the group with tales of your childhood, some of your more memorable experiences, some of the legends and fables your grandfather used to tell you. Aketchi Shiba was most interested in the stories of hunting and weapons, while Mino and Airi are enthralled by the stories of harvest festivals, when entire tribes would come together and celebrate the ending of the year, encapsulated with the Feast of the Moon. Throughout the conversation, Haichi interjects often with various comments, usually disparaging, and you find yourself becoming more and more irritated, far more so than from the usual peasants superstitious banter. His jibes and snarking, while less outwardly offensive than most, cut more deeply from being aimed at your culture\u2019s traditions and history as opposed to yourself.<br /> <br /> \u201cSo, your people are heretics, worshipping the Anathema? Makes sense, considering their savagery\u201d.<br /> <br /> You bite your tongue, mindful of the possible repercussions of insulting such a social superior.<br /> <br /> \u201cNo, not at all, Master Matsu. The Tribes do not worship the Anathema, instead they worship the Moon itself, in the aspects of the Hunter and the Mother. Hunting is an important food source to the Tribes, as there is little land suitable for agriculture in the mountains, and the Mother, as without new births, both man and animal would disappear. Without the guidance of the moon, the Tribes would not survive. \u201c<br /> <br /> \u201cOf course, the Tribes also worship other spirits, animal-totems, the very plants they grow to harvest, etc. Most aspects of life are devotional in some metaphorical fashion, and many aspects of life can be expressed as a craft: marriage as basketry, war and struggle as smithing, and many Tribespeople sing songs of praise as they work, to thank the gods for giving them the opportunity to show their skills.\u201d<br /> <br /> You shrug, \u201cNot that it matters in my case. I am Oathsworn, and stood my vigil overnight in an Immaculate shrine.\u201d<br /> <br /> With that, you sit back, perhaps a bit more forcefully than politeness allowed.. \u201cMy Lord, I thank you for the meal, you do me a great honor by inviting me to your table. I now ask your leave, I must go prepare myself and my equipment for the tournament.\u201d<br /> <br /> A quick burst of irritation crosses her face, but after a moment, Lord Iysen dismisses you with a nod.<br /> <br /> As you walk back to your sleeping chamber, it suddenly occurs to you that you are actually pretty angry, angrier than you had ever gotten before with regards to your heritage. Perhaps it is because you have thought about your grandfather for the first time in many years, or maybe the sneering disdain of Haichi Matsu set off a nerve that the whispers of peasants didn\u2019t. Or, more likely, you are just short-tempered from lack of sleep. Either way, you stride down the halls and stairs of Teriake Castle with purpose, and the few servants to cross your path back away from your scowl.<br /> <br /> After a few minutes, and after getting turned around in the twisting internal passages of the keep (to your increasing annoyance), you make it back to the chamber where you spent the night. Upon opening the door, you find your previous clothing, neatly folded. Upon picking them up, you realize that they have been washed and ironed, tightly-creased and pleasant-smelling. At least one thing is going your way this morning.<br /> <br /> Once changed, you pick up Xuansu by the stables, who has also been brushed and fed, and you are almost through the castle gate when you are stopped by a manservant, who apparently ran all the way down the 6 stories of the keep. He passes over a folded piece of paper, which when unraveled reveals a family crest and quickly-scrawled message in Riverspeak.<br /> <br /> <i>Kasai,</i><br /> <i>We have much to talk about still. If you will, return back here tonight, after 9-bells. Show my mark and give the password \u201cnorthern pines\u201d to the guards at the gate.</i><br /> <i>I</i><br /> <br /> Crumpling the note, you jam it into your jacket and thank the man for bringing it to you so promptly.<br /> <br /> Turning Xuansu down the cliffside path back to town, the potential ramifications of this entire mess bounce through your thoughts. On one hand, going straight to Lord Iysen with your findings went amazingly well, far better than you had hoped (and feared). On the other hand, it now looked like she, in turn, wanted to actually get you <i>involved</i>, something which you hadn\u2019t really foreseen.<br /> <br /> You exhale in a huff. Politics <i>really</i> aren\u2019t your strong suit, all efforts of your father to the contrary. You really lack the ability to parse the double-speak and hidden meanings of social interactions, yet another reason why you almost preferred your time in solitude in the mountains.<br /> <br /> Plus, politics is <i>confusing</i>, especially compared to combat, or upholding your Oaths. In a fight, you generally knew which side you were on, and understanding the messages of your Oaths was practically easy: Defend the weak, serve as an example, treat others with honesty and fairness, be mindful of honor and dignity, so on and so forth.<br /> <br /> Not so with politics! In any situation, an ally could just as easily become an enemy, if certain seemingly-small criteria were not met. Constant \u201cfavors\u201d being asked and given, to sway a decision, lies being told, emotions flaring, and all that just in managing a village and settling the petty squabbles of peasants! Now, you are almost certainly getting dragged into the affairs of kingdoms\u2026<br /> <br /> Morose and lost in your thoughts, you retrace your path back to the run-down doctors shack, where Saiko and Blue-Pines were waiting. When you bang on the door, it takes a few minutes for the woman to make her way over and answer. When she opens the door, it is readily apparent that she got about as much sleep as you did.<br /> <br /> \u201cCan I help you? You don\u2019t look hurt\u2026 don\u2019t tell me you\u2019ve got the clap! It\u2019s too early in the morning to deal with that\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c\u2026 What? No, listen, I was here last night, with a girl with a stab-wound and a woman. The woman had a broken nose and a face covered in bruises. I need to know where they went.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cOh, yeah, I remember. The woman said if you came back, you could find them at the hotel down by the docks, marked with the sign of a dog.\u201d<br /> <br /> With this, the doctor closes the door, leaving you standing on the curb. Various ne\u2019er do wells are watching you with some degrees of interest, and you quickly beat feet on Xuansu. Getting mugged is the last thing you need to have happen to you.<br /> <br /> It takes you about an hour to find this dog-marked hotel (stupid cross-streets), and you are not all that impressed by the exterior. The fa\u00e7ade is crumbling at the corners, roof-tiles are cracked, and the place just exudes tackiness. You are confused, you left Blue-Pines with enough coin to rent a room at a better place than this!<br /> <br /> Upon tying Xuansu to a handy pole and opening the door, you are greeted by the surly, suspicious glares of several daydrinkers, already deep into their cups at only midmorning. Ignoring their leers at your comparatively-fine clothing, you make your way over to the publican. He tells you Blue-Pines and Saiko rented a room early this morning, before the sun even came up, and gives you the room number.<br /> <br /> The stairs creak as you climb them, and you half-heartedly hope they don\u2019t collapse under you as you make your way up to the third floor, to the garret-room Blue-Pines rented. Once at the marked room, you knock on the door, only for Blue-Pines to crack open the door and peer out into the hallway. She seems (pleasantly) surprised to see you.<br /> <br /> \u201cOh, Lord. You aren\u2019t dead! Or in jail. I was kinda concerned.\u201d<br /> <br /> When you enter the room, you notice that Blue-Pines had her dagger drawn and ready. Slightly impressed (and amused), \u201cWhat were you planning on doing with that, exactly?\u201d.<br /> <br /> \u201cHey, in my defense, you tromped up the stairs loud enough to make me think you were the Guard.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cIf I were the Guard, woman, I would kick the door in, not knock, but good instincts nonetheless. Anyway, how is Saiko doing, and <i>what the hell</i> are you doing in this shithole? I gave you more than enough coin to rent a room in a fancy hotel, much less one that doesn\u2019t have lice in the sheets!\u201d<br /> <br /> She shrugs, \u201cIt\u2019s kinda hard to rent a room in the middle of the night, when you look like two mugging victims. This was pretty much the best I could do on short notice. As for the girl, she is asleep; the doctor doped her up on opium before kicking us out.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cFair enough, but we can\u2019t stay here. Saiko needs a decent place to recuperate, preferably not with beds reeking of piss. Come on, pack your gear, I\u2019ll take Saiko\u201d.<br /> <br /> After a few moments, you descend the stairs with Saiko carried bridal-style(who is sleeping rather uncharacteristically quietly. Must be the drugs.), Blue-Pines following close on your heels. Once outside, you wake up Saiko, as much as you are able, and lift her up into the saddle, making sure she is seated securely, before leading both Xuansu and Blue-Pines down the street.<br /> <br /> You make your way closer to the mainmarket, where you pay for <i>much </i>more adequate accommodations, where the rooms don\u2019t reek of spilled booze and vomit, the hallways are swept, and, most importantly, the locks on the doors actually work. There, you leave Saiko in bed, with order for food to be brought up (and for her to be told where you went), along with the majority of your gear.<br /> <br /> Blue-Pines chimes in with, \u201cSo, what is the plan, boss?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cWell, first of all, I am going to get dressed in my full armor, as I have to present myself to the Master of Ceremonies for the tournament, and sign up for the events I want to participate in. After that, we are going to hang around the tournament-grounds for a while, to kill some time and to scope out the opposition.\u201d you reply, grinning sheepishly at the last comment.<br /> <br /> While Blue-Pines is helping you put on your armor, she reveals that she also purchased a quantity of medical supplies from the doctor: bandages, ointments, various medications in jars, etc. It was rather expensive, but considering how you had pretty much none of that before, you consider it money well-spent. After all, you would rather have it and not need it, than need it and not have it.<br /> <br /> After locking Saiko into the room, you head out, with you cutting a fine figure mounted on Xuansu. The mid-afternoon crowds part around you, keeping a healthy distance from your warhorse, and you and Blue-Pines make good time, passing through the main gate in only a few minutes. Outside, a distance away from the city walls, sits the wooden structure of the tournament grounds, constructed only a scant few days ago by laborers contracted by Lord Iysen. However, in between you and the outbuildings is a veritable sea of tents, stalls and fenced-off paddocks.<br /> <br /> The living quarters of the majority of not only the Oathsworn competitors in the tournament, but also their hangers-on, vendors, entertainers, sword-sharpeners, smiths, thieves, murderers, and <i>plenty</i> of prostitutes, this press of barely-ordered construction is filled with a press of humanity, all looking to make some coin, score some tail, and generally cause a ruckus. The Town Militia is hard-pressed to keep even a semblance of law-and-order, in spite of heavily-increased patrols, and in the few days since the tent-city sprang into existence, there has already been a rash of stabbings, fire outbreaks, and even a rumored outbreak of smallpox, despite no evidence being found. The gaily-colored pavilions of the richer Oathsworn warriors stands in stark contrast to the literal rivers of shit flowing between then, both horse and human, and the smells of roasting meat and sweets mingles horridly with the stench of rotting garbage and unwashed humanity. Worse still, the terrain prevented a breeze from clearing out the reek, and the miasma often <i>condensed</i> into a semi-permanent cloud over the \u201csettlement\u201d. The general consensus from the townsfolk concluded that the tournament was hopefully over as soon as possible.<br /> <br /> On top of that, the completely-unorganized placement of the tents and horse-paddocks meant whatever travel took place was forced into a \u201cmain thoroughfare\u201d of sorts, running between the gates of the town and the walls of the tournament arena. This \u201croad\u201d, little more than a wider path between the chaos, was choked with humanity and vendor stalls, pressed shoulder-to-shoulder.<br /> Xuansu, testy at the best of times, is downright pissed from being jostled and rubbed against, and snaps out at someone that passes by too close, and Blue-Pines presses close to your saddle in order to avoid being separated by the crowd. She turns to you, gagging, and says,<br /> <br /> \u201cI always meant to ask you why you preferred spending money instead of just setting up a tent, but now I get why you wanted to stay in town.\u201d<br /> <br /> Keeping your mouth firmly closed, you nod your assent. If anything could give a glimpse of hell, this was it.<br /> <br /> It takes almost an hour to press through the teeming mass of humanity, and you are almost late to the signing-in. Once there, the MC, harried to the point of frenzy, presses a pad of paper into your hands and hurries off. On the paper, there is a list of events you can participate in, with space for your name or mark next to each.<br /> <br /> Keeping in mind your earlier plan, you jot down your name next to both spear duels, the mounted archery competition, the dismounted sword duel, and, almost on a whim, the mounted melee bout.<br /> <br /> As Saiko so exuberantly said; the more competitions, the better the chance of winning. You don\u2019t hold the same level of enthusiasm (after all, you, not her, could possibly lose some teeth), but she has a point regardless.<br /> <br /> Just as you finish penning the final characters, a selection of horns sound, and the MC gestures wildly for you to follow him, out of the antechamber and up onto the arena floor. Leaving Blue-Pines behind to watch over Xuansu, you quickly fall in with the rest of the Oathsworn making their way through the staging-room doors, out onto the sands beneath the crowds.<br /> <br /> The sudden noise is almost deafening. Asides from the horns, wildly blowing to announce the arrival of Lord Iysen and her entourage, the entire stadium is awash with screaming, shouting, and just general noise. Hawkers walk up and down the stands, selling bread, booze and other foods, audience-members bang and stomp on the stands in patterns, from excitement or to show support for a favored competitor, and tourney-staff gesture and shout in order to move you and your fellow Oathsworn into proper position, operating according to some unknown plan.<br /> <br /> Once you are all in position, on a sudden signal the Lord Iysen raises her hands, asking for quiet, and the conversation and noise peters off. On another signal, you and your fellow Oathsworn raise your weapons in salute to the Lord, then drop to one knee in just about the only form of bow that a heavily-armored figure can adopt. After a few seconds of silence, the Lord speaks, thanking the community and the competitors for coming together to celebrate the wedding of her daughter, and she brings up an Immaculate Monk to recite a benediction for the competing warriors, to ask the Dragons to look on them with favor, and may the best combatant win, yadda yadda yadda. The noonday-sun is <i>very</i> hot, standing as you are out on the sand with no shade, and the sun beating down on you means your armor underpadding is soon soaked in sweat. You, to be honest, tone out the various speeches and benedictions, shifting from foot to foot irritated, only belatedly reacting to the various whispered orders from the tourney-staff.<br /> <br /> It feels like forever, but you find yourself eventually being shepherded off the field, to the sound of a cheering crowd. Once back inside the cover of a building, you gratefully open up the buttons of your mail-hood and armored jacket, allowing heat to escape in a blessed rush. You make your way to Blue-Pines, and the two of you work your way out to a stairwell and up to the stands.<br /> Initially, the press of the crowd prevents you from getting much of a view, but a few moments of elbowing and the sight of your sword and armor quickly clears a path for you to sit and watch the first few events. After buying some snacks from a passing vendor, you settle down to watch the common archery bout.<br /> <br /> Taking the form of a simple elimination bracket, the contest was pretty straightforward: 10 arrows from a longbow, and the lowest scorer each round was eliminated. There were around 80 competitors at the start, so working down to a final champion took quite some time. The winner, a young peasant from the northern hills, is awarded with a fat purse of silver and a great double-convexed bow, made from ironwood from Halta.<br /> <br /> In between the various bouts, while targets were moved and arrows were retrieved, performers where brought onto the stage; puppeteers, tumblers, fire-breathers (who interest you greatly. How do they avoid burning their throats?), sword-eaters and snake-charmers, all to entertain the crowd.<br /> <br /> By comparison, the second \u201ccommon\u201d competition, the melee bout, is over in only a few minutes. About 60 challengers, armed with staves and clubs, rush each other <i>en masse</i>, some fighting as individuals while others band together to fight off other groups, only to turn on each other as the numbers of targets dwindle.<br /> <br /> In seemingly no time at all, the bout is reduced to a single combatant, who raises his bloodstained club in victory and accepts his prize (another sum of silver and a quality backsword). All around the arena floor, there are great bloodstains, unconscious or dazed challengers, knocked-out teeth, and other assorted detritus from the scrum.<br /> <br /> Leaning over to Blue-Pines, who watches the bloodshed with fascination, you quip, \u201cNow you can tell why I was leery about competing in a melee!\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c<i>Damn</i>, you weren\u2019t kidding when you said it was dangerous! I think one guy down there lost an eye!\u201d<br /> <br /> The rest of the \u201cnoble\u201d competitions will start tomorrow, so you and Blue-Pines go to make your way back to Xuansu, stabled underneath the stands with the rest of the Oathsworn horses. Getting caught up with the press of humanity slowly making its way back towards the city gates, the two of you stick together as best you can. With all the people leaving the tournament grounds, the overcrowding is even worse than it was earlier, if that is possible, even if members of the city militia are present and directing traffic, and you can tell that Xuansu is starting to get stressed again.<br /> In order to avoid any possible bites or kicks, you direct Xuansu off to the side of the road, in between two tents. Once you are back away from the path aways, you dismount Xuansu and try to calm him down some, loosening the straps of his saddle and bridle and stroking his mane. After a few minutes, the horse relaxes, and you are about to start tightening the straps up again and get underway when you hear a sound that sends a sudden chill down your spine.<br /> <br /> The sound of a blade being removed from its sheath.<br /> <br /> The whisper of the steel being pulled from the wooden scabbard is faint, barely noticeable over the murmur of the crowd only a few dozen feet away, but to your practiced ears it sounds as loud as a thunderclap. You whirl about, half-drawing your sword, to Blue-Pines confusion, to see at least 10 or so assailants coming your way, armed with various blades and clubs. None of them are wearing armor, but they all are wearing tied lengths of cloth wrapped about their faces, covering all but their eyes. Silently, they move to surround you and Blue-Pines, who by now has recognized the situation and has readied her spear.<br /> <br /> In spite of their poor equipment, the combatants move as professionals, covering each other\u2019s flanks (as much as ones flank could be exposed tight in between two tents), and advancing in unison. In but a few moments, they will be upon your group.<br /> <br /> You:<ol class=\"decimal\"><li>Fight! You, and Blue-Pines, are wearing armor (you in <u>mail</u>, even!), and the tents arguably limit the directions you can get attacked from, meaning the effects of their numbers are limited.</li> <li>Run! 10 assassins are 10 assassins, combat ability or not\u2026\u2026</li> <li>Call the Militia! If you can get some militiamen over here, the odds will be more in your favor.</li> <li>Something else?</li> </ol>OOC:<ol class=\"decimal\"><li>Tournament</li> </ol>I read a book a while back, about large gatherings of people in the European Middle Ages, specifically about how a French army was mustered, and how the protagonist had to camp literally 20 or so miles away from the town the muster was taking place in in order to avoid the <u>stench</u> of human/horse shit, from all the men and animals being gathered.<br /> <br /> <u>20. Goddamn. Miles</u>. Imagine what it was like for the poor townsfolk?<br /> <br /> 2) Cleanliness<br /> <br /> I have always imagined Creation as an intrinsically \u201cpost-apocalyptic\u201d society, one that was previously broken by disease, and I almost always portray disease as an ever-present and almost-all-encapsulating fear of the surviving societies. Rumors of disease are taken very seriously in my games.<br /> <br /> This is because, of course, disease was taken pretty seriously in real life. Contrary to popular opinion, the European \u201cmiddle ages\u201d were actually not nearly as dirty as people make it out to be. In many places, Roman bathing culture remained intact for several centuries, and the decline of bathing and public health wasn\u2019t so much a decline in the desire for cleanliness, but for economic and \u201cpracticality\u201d reasons.<br /> <br /> In many cases, Roman public baths were heated with wood. After the (long and slow) collapse of Rome, urban populations migrated to the countryside in order to take up farming so they, well, didn\u2019t starve. In order to grow enough food, they needed to fell forests, removing the supply of wood available for heating bathwater. Plus, pre-industrial agriculture is <u>hard</u>. At the end of a long-ass day of digging, plowing, raking and planting, are you really gonna want to haul around a couple heavy buckets of water, build a fire, wait for the water to heat up, ,and so on and so forth?<br /> I don\u2019t think so.<br /> <br /> However, that doesn\u2019t mean people didn\u2019t bathe, or otherwise clean themselves. Anglo-Saxons, for example, tried to bathe at least once a week, and wash their clothing regularly (with soap, even!). The Norse did so even more frequently, having more access to ample wood supplies, as well as geothermally-heated water.<br /> <br /> There was a documentary I watched a while back, that stated that soap was plentiful enough, and easy enough to make, that even peasants could wash their clothes pretty cheaply. I can\u2019t find it, but I\u2019ll look for it.<br /> <br /> So, while I am basing the Hundred Kingdoms largely off feudal Japan, \u201cmost\u201d people in \u201cmy\u201d Creation are not going to be stumbling, dirt-covered ham-and-cheese-smelling morons, even \u201ctribal peoples\u201d away from centers of civilization. If not because they just like being clean, then because of a social terror of disease.<br /> <br /> Now, keep in mind that my education is in Biology, not history, and I learned this largely through internet-research and personal interest. I can, as always, be wrong.<ol class=\"decimal\"><li>Japanese sword/weapon making</li> </ol>Can I just bring up the topic that, as someone largely immersed in Migration-Era weapons armor, and the means of production thereof, that Japanese weaponmaking is \u2026. Well, kind of weird?<br /> <br /> I mean \u2026\u2026 using tanged weapons just seem like a Bronze-Age thing to me. Sockets, in my opinion, are much more \u201creliable\u201d as you don\u2019t have to compromise the integrity of the handle in order to fix the blade. With a tang, you have to drill out the center of the handle, then some holes for the pins. All of that weakens the structure of the handle and makes it more likely for it to break.<br /> <br /> I mean, <u>obviously</u> it worked, but why did the Japanese go from using sockets (Hoko Yari) to using tangs (Su Yari), or didn\u2019t use pommels on swords or knives? Most of their older weaponry was pretty much straight copies of the stuff from China, which used pommels and sockets. Was it in effort to use less metal?<br /> <br /> On a slightly-related note:<br /> Kasai\u2019s sword and dagger are pretty much just a mix between <i>Jian</i> and <i>Tsurugi/Ken</i> style swords and daggers<br /> His spear is a <i>Su Yari</i><br /> His bow is, uh, like a traditional Chinese composite bow, being equal in both limbs<br /> Saiko\u2019s glaive is a <i>Naginata</i>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-21T22:25:49-08:00","id":1035960,"title":"","content":"Run, shouldn't be crazy hard to escape them and 2v10 is never a possible thing without essence powers. We already had a taste of gritty combat, it wouldn't do to put pines in that much danger.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11567-jairain","name":"Jairain","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11567_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"345"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-22T08:08:27-08:00","id":1036073,"title":"","content":"I vote for a bit of all three. If we just run, they'll try to corner us somewhere even less advantageous for fighting.<br /> <br /> &gt; Perform a fighting retreat to an area where you know other Oathsworn or militia will be, then call for help.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>On the frontier of the Wild South, there's only one woman with the grit to take on its most dangerous outlaws and bring them <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/984977-back-alive-or-maybe-dead-quest\" target=\"_blank\">Back Alive, or Maybe Dead.</a><br /> <br /> Avatar by <a href=\"http://puzzlinghappenstance.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">K.S. Brenowitz</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/908-semicasual","name":"semicasual","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar908_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2499"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-22T16:51:58-08:00","id":1036337,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>semicasual</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1036073#post1036073\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I vote for a bit of all three. If we just run, they'll try to corner us somewhere even less advantageous for fighting.<br /> <br /> &gt; Perform a fighting retreat to an area where you know other Oathsworn or militia will be, then call for help.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes. Let's do a strategic retreat. <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I write things.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3950-wonderandawe","name":"wonderandawe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3950_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5410"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-22T16:55:14-08:00","id":1036339,"title":"","content":"Calls for help won't get far in the din, and running is impossible in the press. (If possible, mount up and use Xaunsu in the)<b> fight</b>.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Like my Avatar? Courtesy of<span style=\"color:#0000FF\"> Jen</span>! : <span style=\"font-size:12px\">Anybody want their characters to be experimented on ? </span>post 98<br /> <i>An Exalt is never unarmed.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4018-l-het-esh","name":"L'het'esh","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4018_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"521"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-23T06:56:12-08:00","id":1036589,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>L'het'esh</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1036339#post1036339\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Calls for help won't get far in the din, and running is impossible in the press. (If possible, mount up and use Xaunsu in the)<b> fight</b>.</div> </div> </div> </div>We just loosened all the straps and ties holding the saddle and bridle to Xuansu. If we were to try to mount, chances are we, and the saddle, would just kinda slide off his side, or over his rump.<br /> <br /> Now, it would only take a few minutes to retighten everything, but, well...... we don't have a few minutes.<br /> <br /> However, don't forget Xuansu is a <u>warhorse</u>, not just a horse we can ride into battle. He can kick and bite, things which are arguably as lethal as our weaponry.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11892,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","user":"Boston123","date":". 01-23-2017, 09:58 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-23T09:09:19-08:00","id":1036670,"title":"","content":"Well, fight in some fashion. if we can Xuansu to work out some of his frustration and irritation on the assailants, so much the better.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Like my Avatar? Courtesy of<span style=\"color:#0000FF\"> Jen</span>! : <span style=\"font-size:12px\">Anybody want their characters to be experimented on ? </span>post 98<br /> <i>An Exalt is never unarmed.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4018-l-het-esh","name":"L'het'esh","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4018_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"521"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-26T03:45:39-08:00","id":1038322,"title":"","content":"yeah...fighting retreat is our best option to get out of this. We can't run and just calling for help wouldn't work.<br /> <br /> By the way, anyone notice that the only &quot;H&quot; we know in this part of the world found out we were assisting with matters concerning security of the tournament, this morning?<br /> We may not be well versed in political intrigue but this bares remembering.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Thoughts ripple out, birthing others","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1624-falleneco","name":"FallenEco","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1624_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1927"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-26T07:21:41-08:00","id":1038408,"title":"","content":"I've got a couple ideas:<br /> <br /> 1. If there are any sorts of things we can push in the way between us and the assassins then we should do so, we should then try and find a natural choke point near the most impassable barriers possible where they have to come at us 1 at a time if possible. If we can force them into a bottle neck then fight and blue pines should use the spear to hold them back from a long distance while Kisai can stab back. All while shouting for help<br /> <br /> 2. Try and find the largest group of toughs or traders or people in the din we can and engage them in some sort of trade pursuit. Basically make ourselves a big pubic target that shows the assassins that someone else is paying attention to us and hope that that dissuades them from all the stabbing so we can stall and come up with a bigger plan such as getting the guards?<br /> <br /> 3. Try and torch a tent if possible causing a massive commotion we might be able to escape in?<br /> <br /> 4. Run, Fight, Shout all at the same time.<br /> <br /> Haven't decided what i like<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8897-joshopotamus","name":"joshopotamus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8897_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"97"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-29T15:09:00-08:00","id":1040235,"title":"","content":"Sorry guys, my jobs has been kicking my ass as of late.<br /> <br /> My &quot;usual&quot; schedule is 730 am to 4pm, with the weekends off. This means I can do my writing on the weekends, no problem.<br /> <br /> For the last three days, I have been working from 7 pm to 7 am, a 12 hour shift. I cam home today, meaning to write the next post, only to fall asleep.<br /> <br /> This is going to be my schedule for the next two weeks, so it looks like I am going to have to go to a Wednesday release schedule.<br /> <br /> Keeping to a regular schedule is easy when the rest of your life does the same : (","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-29T20:26:25-08:00","id":1040388,"title":"","content":"It is okay, make sure to get some rest. We aren't going anywhere","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Thoughts ripple out, birthing others","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1624-falleneco","name":"FallenEco","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1624_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1927"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-30T10:33:53-08:00","id":1040751,"title":"","content":"While regular updates are appreciated, we all understand that sometimes that life happens. I've loved the thought and care you've given into each post. I love that you've preferred to let us explore your setting with a mortal character (or at least that he isn't explicitly an exalt). I've also really enjoyed your attention to detail and to keeping the setting gritty but not oppressive. So far its been one of the reasons i keep coming back to the exalted forums (that and updates for anathema reincarnated!). I've been using your stories as inspiration and motivation for the games i'm currently running. Rest, recover, and keep coming back but please know your sanity and safety are more important than the creative energy you give me. I dont mind a lack of updates, just glad to know that its still on your mind and that you still want to keep going with it. Thank you for this story.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8897-joshopotamus","name":"joshopotamus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8897_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"97"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-31T18:20:50-08:00","id":1041609,"title":"","content":"So, the general consensus is \u201crun + fight + call for help\u201d, with some added flair<br /> <br /> Quickly, before the two groups can box you in tight, you slap a mailed fist down on Xuansu\u2019s rump, causing him to rear and snort in surprised anger. He surges forwards, out towards the main pathway and the crowd.<br /> <br /> The assailants, advancing shoulder-to-shoulder between two canvas walls, couldn\u2019t have dodged even if they had time to, and get bowled over by about 1200 pounds of horseflesh. A couple of the men go flying, into the canvas walls and out into the pathway, and one gets trodden underneath Xuansu\u2019s hooves. This close, you can clearly hear bones <i>snap</i>, and the man so stepped on do not get up.<br /> <br /> Before the enemy has a chance to recover from this sudden reversal of fortunes, you push Blue-Pines out towards Xuansu, out to the open where you can work together to \u201cblock off\u201d the narrow alleyway and prevent your assailants from surrounding you. One of the men thrown into the tents tries to grab her by the leg as she passes by, in order to drag her down to earth.<br /> Without missing a step, she drives the spike on the butt-end of her spear through his forearm, breaking the bones with a crack, and as the man screams, she stomps on his head, driving him face-first into the mud (er, part of it is mud, at least). With this, she whirls about, spear at the ready, giving you the breathing room necessary to rush up to Xuansu and pull your spear from where it is lashed to the tack.<br /> <br /> As soon as you step out from between the tents, you go to call for the militia, or anyone available, and are immediately hit with a wall of sound. The thunder of hundreds or thousands of stampeding feet, the crash of stalls as they get turned over and trampled, and screams, from the throat of the man at your feet, crushed underfoot, and a rising crescendo of cries of fright from the rushing crowd. Above all else, however, is the building roar of fire, racing from tent to stall and back again, a backdrop above the spreading morass that is the tournament-grounds. The sight of the flames climbing into the sky shock you into dumbstruck silence, if only for a moment.<br /> <br /> Someone, somewhere, set something on fire, and now not only do you have to fight off the assassins in front of you, but now you must also beat the flames, or risk dying from smoke-inhalation or burning. Already, the smoke is building, swirling around your feet in thick black eddies, and it is rapidly becoming hard to see and breathe.<br /> <br /> Grimly, you turn back to Blue-Pines, who is apparently having great success in fighting off the assailants all by herself, fending off the group via the reach of her spear, and you take this opportunity to silence the assassins that were thrown to the ground, still struggling for purchase in the muck. After a few quick stabs with the blade of your spear, they are brought down, and you step forwards to assist your armswoman with the remainder.<br /> <br /> Evidently, the roughnecks weren\u2019t expecting such aggressive/competent resistance, and disheartened by the rapid loss of half their group, the remainder quickly turns and flee. They disappear into the maze of tents and alleys, and it would be suicidal to follow them, even if there wasn\u2019t the fire to contend with.<br /> <br /> Coughing, you reach into your waist-sash and pull out some scraps of cloth, passing one to Blue-Pines and tying the other over your nose and mouth. It helps but little, and your eyes are starting to blur and water from the flying smoke and embers.<br /> <br /> As soon as Blue-Pines catches a glimpse of the growing flames, by this point rising up to the level of the walls of Teriake Castle, she curses , eyes wide,\u201d <i>ever-shifting hells</i>! What happened?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cNo idea. Regardless, we have to get out of here, or the fire will kill us just as well as sure as those assassins. \u201c<br /> <br /> After but a few minutes of stumbling through the smoke, you come to realize that the central-pathway through the tournament grounds practically no longer exists. The stampeding crowd knocked over and crushed the market stalls and carts, and wagons and pallets of supplies were left sitting in the middle of the path when the porters fled.<br /> <br /> You <i>might</i> be able to clamber through the twisted wreckage on foot, but you would certainly have to leave Xuansu behind to his fate, and after examining just how much <i>stuff</i> there is to climb over, there is no way you would be able to make it through before the fire reached you, not armored as you are.<br /> <br /> You turn to Blue-Pines, who in spite of only being arms-length away, is barely visible.<br /> <br /> \u201cCome on, we have to go back!\u201d<br /> <br /> She stares at you, \u201cThrough the fire? Are you crazy?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cWe aren\u2019t going to get through here! Do you have a better idea?\u201d<br /> <br /> She thinks for a moment,\u201d How far away is the stadium? There was some clear space around it that should stop the fire from getting to us!\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cWe are going to have to hurry, then! Can you run?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cNot in this smoke, I can barely see, much less run!\u201d<br /> <br /> You curse. Already, you think you can feel the heat of the flames reflecting off your armor, and a line of cold sweat is trickling down your spine. After a long moment of thought, an idea enters your head and you grab Blue-Pines hand.<br /> <br /> \u201cHold on to this, Xuansu will be able to pick a path!\u201d, and you guide her hand to one of the girth-straps of Xuansu\u2019s saddle.<br /> <br /> With each of you holding the saddle in a death-grip, Xuansu finds his way through the wreckage and smoke, keeping as clear from the actual flames as best as he can. To help him out, you and Blue-Pines probe ahead with the butts of your spears, to find obstructions through the smoke.<br /> <br /> Even with your spear thrust ahead of you, you stumble over something left lying in the path, and Xuansu balks away from it. Bending forwards, you start when the body of man comes into view, huddled on the ground. From a cursory inspection, there is no way to tell what killed him, but the corpse is badly burned, and you suddenly realize that the smoke smells not only of burning wood and canvas, but also roasting meat. Sudden nausea rises in your throat, and you retch up your breakfast.<br /> <br /> Standing, throat burning with smoke and bile, you jerk on Xuansu\u2019s saddle, pulling him forwards. After a few yards, you find more bodies, singly at first, then in clumps and groups, and Blue-Pines cries out when she finds out what she was stepping on. All of the bodies are covered in burns, and some were crushed, either by collapsing structures or by the feet of their fleeing compatriots. Men, women, children, no one was spared.<br /> <br /> There is no time to reflect on this, and you get brought back to earth by the sudden crash of a nearby tent, collapsing into an inferno with a roar of flame.<br /> <br /> All ahead of you, tents and stalls are consumed in fire, a roaring dragon of smoke and embers that dances and swirls overhead.<br /> <br /> You start to feel the cold buzz of panic. In all this smoke, you can\u2019t tell where the hell you are, much less the way to the open plaza around the stadium. You are lost in the flames, just waiting to suffocate or worse, get consumed by the conflagration. In a sudden fit of desperation, you plead to any deity that will listen, asking for some way through the hellscape.<br /> <br /> As if by a miracle, a sudden gust of wind rushes by you, cutting through the smoke and clearing the sky for but a moment. Ahead of you, under the light of the afternoon sun, lies revealed the stadium, still rising above the tent-city. Heartened by your sudden good fortune, you all-but drag Xuansu and Blue-Pines towards the possible salvation.<br /> <br /> After a few minutes, you reach the plaza surrounding the stadium. To your chagrin, you are not the only one to have this idea, and the open space is filled to capacity with refugees from the fire, to the point where people have to shy away from the fires but a few feet away, cringing from the heat and flames. The air is filled with the cries of the frightened and the injured, to the point where human noise drowns out the crackle and roar of flames.<br /> <br /> Pulling the bunched cloth away from your mouth,\u201d This is a disaster just waiting to get worse. Where is the militia, the fire-brigade?\u201d<br /> <br /> Blue-Pines shakes her head. \u201c There \u2026 there isn\u2019t much that can be done against this, Boss. All the water in the river wouldn\u2019t stop it now. They are probably waiting behind the town walls for all this to burn out.\u201d<br /> <br /> You snarl, angry at this cowardice. \u201cThere are <i>hundreds</i> of people here! They can\u2019t just leave them here to die!\u201d<br /> <br /> She shrugs weakly, exhausted. \u201cYou tell me, Lord. There is fuck all I can do.\u201d<br /> <br /> You glare, at the flames and at the people standing around, effectively waiting to die. There has to be <i>something</i> that can be done, some way to salvage something from this catastrophe.<br /> <br /> You:<ol class=\"decimal\"><li>Try to organize the crowd into doing something. Build a firebreak, fight the flames, <i>something</i></li> <li>Push you way through, to the stadium, and try to get inside. The space inside should be a refuge from the smoke, if just for a little while, and which is, despite the obvious flames, the most dangerous aspect of the fire.</li> <li>Head back into the tent-city. There might be parts that have already burnt down, and you can escape the fire that way.</li> <li>Try to help the wounded. Even at this distance, it looks like many people got crushed by debris, suffer from burns, or are just sitting listlessly, deep in shock. While there isn\u2019t much you can do, anything is better than just sitting here.</li> <li>Something else?</li> </ol>OOC: Just a little snippet, before we get to the end of the chapter.<br /> <br /> Now we get to the meat of the matter. Shit has hit the fan, and it is being sprayed around the room at high velocity.<br /> <br /> I originally meant to make this longer, and end the chapter on this post, but I realized it would be a good 15-20 pages without much in the way of a decision point, with the exception of the very end, which is obviously a pretty major, arguably <u>the</u> major, decision of our early career as an Oathsworn. Not liking that very much, and thinking ahead to my (fucked up) work schedule, I decided I could fit at least one more meaningful decision in. I'll probably have one more after this, dependent, of course, on what you choose as our course of action.<br /> <br /> Our actions here, along with how we have behaved thus far in Teriake, will determine how the <u>common</u> people will view us in the future.<br /> <br /> On combat:<br /> <br /> I am <i>kinda</i> amused at how seriously you lot took the &quot;assassination&quot; (which is, of course, is what it was, albeit a very unprofessional and quickly-planned one).<br /> <br /> I mean, mortal or not, Kasai and Blue Pines are in armor, and comparatively well-armed, and Kasai fought off 5 bandits unarmored in a 5v1 earlier in the Quest, giving a good indication of his skill level (I've got him pegged at Melee 3, with a Specialty in Spears, for those interested). The &quot;real&quot; danger here wasn't from the actual assassins per se, but because we were surrounded, and through the implication that we pissed someone off enough to warrant an assassination in the first place.<br /> <br /> However, if you guys decided to actually stand and fight, things would probably have been much more hairy, but we would have come through in the end, albeit in quite a bit worse shape.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11892,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","user":"Boston123","date":". 01-31-2017, 09:31 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-31T20:39:04-08:00","id":1041668,"title":"","content":"1 and 2. Organize the people into the stadium.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Like my Avatar? Courtesy of<span style=\"color:#0000FF\"> Jen</span>! : <span style=\"font-size:12px\">Anybody want their characters to be experimented on ? </span>post 98<br /> <i>An Exalt is never unarmed.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4018-l-het-esh","name":"L'het'esh","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4018_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"521"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-31T21:31:30-08:00","id":1041679,"title":"","content":"See I am leaning to 1 and 4. That said I do also firmly believe 3 can work but not with all the civilians. Given we were just attacked by assassins, I am leery of headed into an enclosed space, especially since the fire is a tad convenient. I had considered shouting &quot;Fire&quot; in order to get the guard's attention but came to the conclusion that it would cause too big a panic. With this thought in mind and the threat against the event...yeah, the stadium feels like a trap. Haichi is on my 'bad list'.<br /> <br /> So let's start organizing that firebreak, and anyone who isn't able to help in that regard should tending the wounded. Hopefully we can save lives.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Thoughts ripple out, birthing others","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1624-falleneco","name":"FallenEco","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1624_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1927"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-01T04:44:17-08:00","id":1041763,"title":"","content":"1 first, then 4 once the civilians are mobilized. <br /> <br /> A firebreak would be great right now.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>On the frontier of the Wild South, there's only one woman with the grit to take on its most dangerous outlaws and bring them <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/984977-back-alive-or-maybe-dead-quest\" target=\"_blank\">Back Alive, or Maybe Dead.</a><br /> <br /> Avatar by <a href=\"http://puzzlinghappenstance.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">K.S. Brenowitz</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/908-semicasual","name":"semicasual","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar908_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2499"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-01T09:17:22-08:00","id":1041867,"title":"","content":"I'd say 1. is a definite must. Number 3 isn't a great idea in any sense and quite frankly while i sympathize with number 4 we're all on a sinking ship that will die together so i think its more important that we find a solution first and then tend to the others. If there is a way we can use implements to push the walls outward while it burns so the crumbling stadium falls away from us, that would be my vote. <br /> <br /> Also let me echo george clooney in o brother where art thou<br /> <br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pK3zSdpACp0\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/google-material/image_placeholder.svg\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pK3zSdpACp0\" target=\"_blank\"> </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pK3zSdpACp0\" target=\"_blank\"> https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pK3zSdpACp0 </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> </div> </div> </div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":8897,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8897-joshopotamus","user":"joshopotamus","date":". 02-01-2017, 12:20 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8897-joshopotamus","name":"joshopotamus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8897_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"97"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-01T09:44:05-08:00","id":1041880,"title":"","content":"Let's go with one to try and mitigate the damage. And make sure 4 is taken care of at the same time.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I write things.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3950-wonderandawe","name":"wonderandawe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3950_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5410"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-01T14:41:18-08:00","id":1042039,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>joshopotamus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1041867#post1041867\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'd say 1. is a definite must. Number 3 isn't a great idea in any sense and quite frankly while i sympathize with number 4 we're all on a sinking ship that will die together so i think its more important that we find a solution first and then tend to the others. If there is a way we can use implements to push the walls outward while it burns so the crumbling stadium falls away from us, that would be my vote.<br /> <br /> Also let me echo george clooney in o brother where art thou<br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pK3zSdpACp0\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pK3zSdpACp0</a></div> </div> </div> </div><br /> The stadium is fine, actually. It is pretty much the only thing in this place that isn't on fire, and so long as the flames don't get too high, too close, or get windblown, it should stay that way for some time.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-01T18:01:18-08:00","id":1042141,"title":"","content":"All good thoughts, 1 then 4","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11567-jairain","name":"Jairain","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11567_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"345"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-03T06:59:16-08:00","id":1042911,"title":"","content":"yea 1 then 4","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8897-joshopotamus","name":"joshopotamus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8897_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"97"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-12T20:34:29-08:00","id":1048052,"title":"","content":"It appears that the general desire is ,\u201c organize the crowd into doing something\u201d, and \u201cTry to help the wounded\u201d, along with a couple \u201cget into the stadium\u201d and, \u201chead out into the already-burnt part of the tent city\u201d.<br /> <br /> On another note: these last two weeks have been rough, with 12-hour overnight shifts, along with two overnight snow plowings. After this chapter, I\u2019ll have to seriously evaluate how much I am actually able to do in the future. Not saying \u201cI bit off more than I could chew\u201d by undertaking this Quest-thread, I merely didn\u2019t anticipate the level of work required. I am, of course, planning on finishing this. Amusingly, I actually have ideas for several other Quests brewing, but ..... let us finish one thing at a time.<br /> <br /> During the overnight shifts, I actually did a whole line-diagram \u2013type deal with how several possible chapters could go out, so I have quite a bit of stuff down. It is finding the time to get it on paper (out of my mind and on the computer, in actuality) that I am having problems with.<br /> <br /> ------<br /> <br /> You curse, well aware that if something doesn\u2019t get done soon, a whole lot of people are going to die painfully, you included. More importantly, it is rapidly becoming apparent that you are going to have to organize the something. As irritating as it is, being the only one willing to step forwards, you realize with a burst of wry humor that this is what being an Oathsworn is all about, and while the situation is a bit \u2026. ah, <i>extreme</i>, you are supposed to serve as an inspiration, leader in both peace and war, through the following of your sworn Oaths. Here, they are directly applicable.<br /> <br /> Hell, if anything, this situation is the <i>easiest by far</i> situation to interpret through your Oaths. Either you act, and people (might) be saved, or you don\u2019t, and they die. Through your actions, others might be inspired to act, where before they might stay silent. The right course of action is now clear, and with that in mind, any tension and trepidation fade away, replaced with a calm sense of purpose. Sure, things look bad, but at least you can do something!<br /> <br /> Blue Pines starts at your sudden laugh, filled with relief as you are.<br /> <br /> \u201cIs something \u2026.. funny, Lord? Because I don\u2019t see it.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cNo, just finally realizing something I should have long ago.\u201d. She looks at you like you just suddenly grew another head, and you wave off the building question. You have work to do.<br /> <br /> Nearby, there is the wreck of a wagon or cart laying in the middle of the plaza, with refugees clustered around it, and you clamber up to the top in order to get a better view. Up above the heads of the crowd, amidst the swirling smoke and sparks, you suddenly spot the conical helmet of one of the Teriake Militia!<br /> <br /> \u201cHey! HEY! Miltiaman!\u201d. Of course, over the crowd and crackle of the flames, they probably can\u2019t hear you, and you jump off the wagon and push over to the man. Grabbing him by the shoulder, you wheel him about, only to come face-to-face with a young man, barely out of adolescence, all gangly limbs and bones, with a face filled with terror. Face pale, lips drawn tight, and you can even feel his limbs shaking through his overlarge jacket.<br /> <br /> \u201cHey, do you know what is going on? \u2026.. I mean, do you know what happened?\u201d<br /> <br /> The kid stares at you for a second, then mutely shakes his head.<br /> <br /> \u201cAre there any other militiamen around?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cN.. Not that I have seen! I was only hired a week ago, for the tournament, I don\u2019t know <i>anything</i>! I ran here after the fire started. A\u2026 are we gonna <i>die</i>?\u201d The poor kid is practically in tears, and you clap him on the shoulder, as much a means to keep his attention as to give comfort.<br /> <br /> \u201cNot if I have any say in the matter. Do you have a trumpet, or a horn or anything?\u201d<br /> <br /> He brightens a bit, \u201c yeah, I\u2019ve got a whistle, but what are you gonna do?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cFirst, I am going to get the attention of everyone that I can, then I am going to try to organize this mob into something that could save us all. Stick by me, son (son? He is only a few years younger than you, it looks like), I could use some help. Do you have a name?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cH.. Haru, my Lord\u201d, as he fumbles through his padded coat for his whistle.<br /> <br /> \u201cGood, Haru, follow me and we will get through this. When I get on top of that wagon over there, I want you to blow your whistle as long as you can, until you get <i>somebody\u2019s</i> attention. Got it?\u201d<br /> <br /> He nods, and even with such a meager task to accomplish, he looks much more focused, still pale and frightened, but determined.<br /> <br /> When you get back to the wagon wreckage, Blue Pines is leaning against it, staring out glumly at the huddled crowd and the encroaching flames. So enthralled, she doesn\u2019t notice when you get up next to her, and you have to grab and shake her shoulder in order to drag her attention away from the scene.<br /> <br /> \u201cAre you ready to jump into action?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cWhat?\u201d She looks incredibly confused.<br /> <br /> \u201cJust follow along\u201d, as you climb again to the top of the wagon. Even in the few minutes it has been since you\u2019ve got here, the air is filling with smoke, and even a few feet above the ground, it is increasingly difficult to make out individual people in the crowd below, not to mention hard to breathe. You are going to have to get to work <i>fast.</i><br /> <br /> \u201cAlright, Haru, blow your whistle for all you are worth! Blue Pines, go and get the attention of the closest people who are willing to listen!\u201d<br /> <br /> Even with the press of the crowd, it takes a few minutes for the piercing whistle and Blue Pines shakings to rouse the people from their shock, and a few moments longer for them to gather around you in a decently-sized group. All the while, the smoke and flames creep closer.<br /> <br /> One large, roughish-looking man, bolder or less-shocked than the rest, calls out ,\u201d What\u2019re you calling us over here for, boss? Things are a little fucked, can\u2019t you tell?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cThat is what I plan on doing something about, my good man. If we don\u2019t, everyone here is going to die, either from burns, or more likely, from breathing in the smoke. Now, I don\u2019t know about you, but that doesn\u2019t seem like my cup of tea.\u201d You grin at your attempt at humor, and it even draws a few chuckles from the crowd, lame as it is.<br /> <br /> The man shakes his head, \u201c So, what\u2019ve you got in mind?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cWell, fire needs fuel, right? What happens if it doesn\u2019t have fuel?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cIt\u2026.. goes out?\u201d, he replies, confused at such an obvious question.<br /> <br /> \u201cRight! Now, with the fire here, it is fueling itself on the tents, shacks and other stuff on the ground. If we push the stuff that isn\u2019t on fire yet <i>back</i>, into the flames, there won\u2019t be any fuel left for it to burn. Plus, that will limit the space it has to advance. How does that sound?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cIt\u2026.. sounds a hell of a lot better than just sitting here and waiting to die, honest.\u201d, to the murmured affirmation of the crowd. \u201cHow do we go about that?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cTake hold of some poles or staves, and knock down tents and such, or push the wreckage that is in the streets into the flames, to open a path.\u201d You meet the man\u2019s eyes and hold his gaze. \u201cCan I leave you in charge of that?\u201d<br /> <br /> He holds your gaze, nodding assent. \u201cSure\u201d.<br /> <br /> Your shoulders sag in relief, something you are barely able to keep the crowd from seeing. \u201cGood, good. Rouse up some sturdy boys and get to it. Don\u2019t forget to send some runners to the other parts of the plaza, so they can get to work on their side.\u201d With that, the man walks away, grabbing several other people and dragging them with him. Some of them grab poles and chunks of timber, while others race away around the plaza.<br /> <br /> You turn to Blue Pines, \u201cWe need to get away from the sparks and smoke, and in order to do that, we need to get inside the stadium. Between the walls and the plaza, we should be safe from the fires for some time, enough time for it to burn itself out, hopefully. I need you to take charge of that.\u201d<br /> <br /> She nods, \u201cAny ideas?\u201d<br /> <br /> You shrug, \u201cTo be honest, so long as we can get the wounded inside in one piece, I don\u2019t really care. Same thing: grab some people and make it happen. Once you get inside, start moving people inside, but make sure there isn\u2019t a stampede.\u201d<br /> <br /> You clamber down from the wagon, only to get buttonholed by Haru, \u201c Is there anything you want me to do, Lord?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cNot just yet. Stay with me; I am going to do the same thing all around the plaza, and I need your whistle to get people\u2019s attention.\u201d<br /> <br /> And so you make your way through the crowd, stopping every time there is a crowd to repeat your plan and give orders. In many cases, runners have already beaten you to the punch, and the people are already up and animated, needing only a slight push to get working, and by the time you complete a circuit around the stadium, the \u201caura\u201d of the crowd has changed drastically, being given purpose and hope, even with the actual situation becoming worse. The smoke has thickened (probably as a result of more fuel being added to the flames), blocking out the sun and dropping the world into an artificial dusk. You can only see by the light of the very flames you are trying to extinguish, an irony that would be highly amusing in any other situation.<br /> <br /> Slowly, you notice the crowd start to move in a single direction, and you make your way over to where you left Blue Pines to her work. There, you see her, along with several others, shepherding the crowd into the open gates of the stadium through a series of queues, cunningly built of piled lumber and other wreckage. In addition, the most heavily-wounded are being carried directly into the stadium, on stretchers made of tent-canvas.<br /> <br /> Now that things are actually proceeding in an organized fashion, you feel yourself relaxing, even if only slightly. Delegating tasks out to others took much of the work off your shoulders, letting you keep an eye on the big picture, while ensuring the necessary things got taken care of at the same time.<br /> <br /> At this point, you no longer <i>really</i> need Haru with you, and you send him inside the stadium, to supervise the people milling around inside on the field. As you do so, you hear the people around you whispering to their fellows, and several of the bolder ones come forwards, grasping your arms and thanking you for saving them. This actually makes you rather uncomfortable, unused as you are to praise from the public eye, and you brush it off awkwardly, explaining that your Oaths required it of you, that it was nothing, etc. You try to make your way to Blue Pines, only to get held back by more ingratiatingly-thankful townsfolk, and by the time you get up to the gate, you are a little irritated by the attention.<br /> <br /> Blue Pines looks even more exhausted, if possible, than she did an hour ago.<br /> <br /> \u201cHow goes the relocation effort?\u201d<br /> <br /> She shakes her head in annoyance. \u201cIt\u2019s like herding <i>cats</i>, except cats are probably smarter.\u201d<br /> <br /> Chuckling, \u201cGood work, anyways. How are you organizing them?\u201d<br /> <br /> Gesturing to the barriers, \u201cwell, the guys you have knocking down stuff brought this back, and the people who ca\u2026\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cLORD, come quick!\u201d Haru comes sprinting up, helmet bouncing around loosely on his head.<br /> <br /> \u201cWhat? What is it?\u201d you reply, hand going to your sword hilt.<br /> <br /> \u201cThere are <i>dead people</i> inside!\u201d<br /> <br /> You shoot a quick look at Blue Pines, then stride off behind Haru, who is running up the stairs and benches of the stands, up to the box where Lord Iysen was sitting during the competition. Your stomach drops, and you hurry up into the box.<br /> <br /> Inside the wooden-walled box is an abbatoir. The chairs inside are turned over and smashed, several crossbow bolts are buried into the walls, and strewn over the floor in pools of black, congealed blood are several bodies. One of them, you notice in sudden disquiet, is the armored figure of Lord Iysen\u2019s bodyguard (Nori?).<br /> <br /> You quickly step over to him, turning him over as gently and respectfully as you can, so you can better see what killed him. Almost instantly, as he rolls over, his throat yawns open in a second mouth, and you hear Haru retching outside the box. The cut to the neck is but one wound on his body. A crossbow bolt in the thigh, another buried under his arm, a cut to the elbow that left the couter dangling by a half-severed strap. In spite of these wounds, he obviously survived long enough to take out those who attacked him, and when you pick up his sword, you can see where the blade struck bone<br /> <br /> Despite all this (and not meaning any disrespect to Nori), you don\u2019t see what you came to look for: any sign of Lord Iysen, or her children.<br /> <br /> Unnerved, you go to check the bodies of the possible assassins. They are, to a man, comparatively unremarkable. Clothing of middling cuts and colors, no jewelery, insignia or identifiers that could point out who they were or who they worked for. Even their weapons are unremarkable; munitions-quality crossbows and short backswords, something any well-off peasant could buy.<br /> <br /> \u201cL\u2026 Lord, what are we gonna do about this?\u201d<br /> <br /> You turn your head, to look at the young man over your shoulder, \u201cWe? We are going to do nothing about this. There is nothing that can be done, not while this fire is still burning, but I have my suspicions about that.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cWhat do you mean?\u201d<br /> <br /> You shake your head, \u201cNothing. You never saw any of this.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cI\u2026 I don\u2019t understand.\u201d<br /> <br /> Spreading your arms, you indicate the bloody scene before you. \u201cThis. You never came up here, you never saw this. There are no bodies; no indication there was an assassination attempt on Lord Iysen.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cOh\u2026.. I\u2026. I understand.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cDid anybody else see this with you?\u201d<br /> <br /> He nods a negative, \u201cNo, I came up here alone.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cNobody knows there are bodies up here?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cAsides from the people who heard when I told you, no.\u201d<br /> <br /> You grunt, \u201cGood, I guess. Help me block this off, so people don\u2019t find out by accident.\u201d. He steps forwards to help, and in a few minutes, you have piled up enough furniture and pried-up boards to block off access into the box.<br /> <br /> With that taken care of, the two of you return to the stadium floor, which is now filled with people. As soon as they come in, they start prying up boards to build impromptu lean-tos, covering them with scavenged canvas and other materials, including what looks like silk. Others meander out into the preparation chambers and stables, coming back with bales of hay and reports of cisterns and containers. Once hearing these, you organize groups of sturdy men to go and fill these containers and bring them out for people to use. Once there is an ample supply of water available, many of the people who are comparatively unwounded go around to those worse off, offering water that, while tepid, must seem like Heaven to smoke-ravaged throats and eyes.<br /> <br /> While all of this is going on, you take a moment to sit down and think about what has happened. So far, you have what looks like an assassination attempt (or a successful one, you have to admit, uncomfortable with the implications), a fire that is raging out of control, and a still-panicked populace that, if the news of the first gets out, or the second gets worse, could boil over into a riotous mob. Pondering on all of this brings you into a morose funk, and lost in your thoughts, you don\u2019t notice that you have people standing before you until one of them loudly clears their throat.<br /> Lifting your head, you see several men grouped together, many with strips of cloth bound about their upper arms in a crude uniform of sorts. Leading them is the man from earlier, the one who headed up the debris-moving crew, and he looks right-<i>pissed</i>. Several of the men are all-but dragging others, in various stages of disarray and bloodiness.<br /> <br /> \u201cWhat is going on, gentlemen?\u201d, you ask, confused.<br /> <br /> \u201cRight, Lord, we were working on dropping tents and moving wreckage, and we came across several guys who were, well, <i>not behaving too well</i>, to put it politely.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cWhat do you mean?\u201d standing up.<br /> <br /> He snarls, \u201cSome people apparently didn\u2019t think the line was moving quite fast enough for them, and they decided to push ahead in line. A fight broke out, and several people got stabbed or coshed. Some of the people who got hurt <i>died</i>.\u201d<br /> <br /> Your lips pull back from your teeth, almost of their own volition, and the group pulls back almost as a man. Struggling, you control your sudden anger, and settle on a glare that causes the restrained men to flinch away.<br /> <br /> \u201c<b><i>Really</i></b>? You didn\u2019t think there has already been enough death and pain today, you felt the need to add to it?\u201d<br /> <br /> The headman continues hotly, \u201cThat\u2019s not all, we were pulling down a tent, in order to slow the fire, when some guy in armor comes running out, yelling at us to stop. We \u2026.. well, we ignored him, after telling him what we were doing, and he <i>drew</i> on us! He cut up Tachi!\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cWhere is this man?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cRight here, I followed this mummer\u2019s dance to see this farce for myself.\u201d, an Oathsworn says as he elbows his way through the group.<br /> <br /> You shift your glare to the man, who meets it with a smirk.<br /> <br /> \u201c I responded as is my right-of-status. The peasant didn\u2019t listen to me, so I had to get their attention <i>somehow</i>. I am sure you understand.<br /> <br /> The headman explodes, \u201c <i>You<b> killed</b> him</i>, you bastard!\u201d, and makes to grab for the Oathsworns arm. The Oathsworn jerks away, spitting, \u201cUnhand me, peasant, unless you want me to cut you like I did your friend!?\u201d<br /> <br /> <b><i><u>\u201cEnough</u></i></b>\u201d, you shout, attracting the attention of seemingly half the stadium. Many of the closest people move to surround the situation, muttering darkly when they hear about what happened, and Blue Pines rushes over, spear at the ready. You stop her with a gesture.<br /> <br /> You sigh, mastering your temper for the second time in as many minutes, and jerking your chin at the headman,<br /> <br /> \u201c<i>Why</i>, exactly, did you bring these men to me? What do you want me to do with them?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c\u2026 To dispense justice, Lord. We don\u2019t have the authority.\u201d<br /> <br /> Massaging your temples, you consider just what has happened here.<br /> <br /> You:<ol class=\"decimal\"><li>Let <u>all</u> of them go. You don\u2019t have time for this, and you can understand how tempers can flare in this situation</li> <li>Execute <u>all</u> of them. Fuck \u2018em, they killed people.</li> <li>Listen to what each other them actually did, then \u201cpass sentence\u201d based on that. Not <u>all</u> of them actually killed people</li> <li>Restrain all of them, and let the actual Magistrate deal with it. While you are touched that the people are actually assigning you authority, you legally don\u2019t have the ability to pass sentence.</li> <li>Restrain the commoners, and let the Oathsworn go. Technically, he was within his right-of-status.</li> <li>Restrain the commoners, and execute the Oathsworn. You are supposed to be <i>better</i> than this, asshole!</li> <li>Something else? (write in)</li> </ol>OOC:<br /> Technically, the Oathsworn is correct here, and the situation is legally the same as if some commoner pissed Kasai off sufficiently, and Kasai killed them in retaliation. Or, if Kasai actually killed the monk who spat on him.<br /> <br /> On the other hand, Kasai is <u>officially </u><i>done with that shit</i>. He has had, to this point, a pretty goddamn bad day, and is in a pretty poor mood.<br /> <br /> Roll with it.<br /> <br /> One more decision, mates!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11892,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","user":"Boston123","date":".  Copy-pasting from Word to a forum is headache-inducing 02-12-2017, 11:41 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-13T02:44:35-08:00","id":1048139,"title":"","content":"As much as would like to do 7, we do have to respect the Magistrate and the Lord of the Land. So I vote <b>4. Restrain all of them, and let the actual Magistrate deal with it. While you are touched that the people are actually assigning you authority, you legally don\u2019t have the ability to pass sentence.</b> Mind you, I would add it to the list of things to talk about with the authorities to see the 'Oathsworn' is punished; the commoners were acting under our orders to save lives. Killing Tachi could be compared to killing Blue Pines. Not well, but...<br /> <br /> That said, if any actually resist being restrained or stripped of weapons, gut the offender. You don't have time for this and you aren't going to let them threaten you or anyone else. This goes double for the 'noble'. Remind him that his rank came with expectations that he has failed if he makes a fuss and if he threatens anyone, draw our blade and remind him that he does not have the right. These aren't his 'peasants' and even then it would be a shameful violation of the Oaths. Plus we can't be sure mr. fancy there isn't in on the plot (he did try to stop the people risking their<br /> lives to stop the fire,) so we can point out that the chances for more trouble outweighed the continued mercy of not punishing him for murder.<br /> More than happy to gut the swine.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Thoughts ripple out, birthing others","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1624-falleneco","name":"FallenEco","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1624_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1927"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-13T05:01:33-08:00","id":1048183,"title":"","content":"My preliminary answer has two parts:<br /> <br /> For the commoners who fought or killed people,<b> restrain them and save them for the magistrate.</b> Use force <i>only</i> to ensure compliance. <br /> <br /> For the Oathsworn who probably wouldn't face any punishment otherwise, <b>c</b><b>hallenge him to a duel</b>. Assuming you cannot legally pass judgement (read: execute) this man, this allows you to circumvent normal conventions and demonstrate to the watching crowd that you will, in fact, fight for them. As long as you are within the law, this does not have to be a &quot;fair&quot; duel - you may keep your armor and sword while allowing him the minimum armament necessary to participate. After all, it's not like he gave the peasant any chance to defend himself. <br /> <br /> That said, my answer might change depending on the answers to some questions:<br /> * What rules govern when one oathsworn is allowed to kill another one?<br /> * Does this society have laws about restitution, i.e. when a murderer pays recompense to the murdered party's family?<br /> * Are Oathsworn normally allowed to take the law into their hands? <br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>On the frontier of the Wild South, there's only one woman with the grit to take on its most dangerous outlaws and bring them <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/984977-back-alive-or-maybe-dead-quest\" target=\"_blank\">Back Alive, or Maybe Dead.</a><br /> <br /> Avatar by <a href=\"http://puzzlinghappenstance.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">K.S. Brenowitz</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/908-semicasual","name":"semicasual","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar908_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2499"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-13T06:06:44-08:00","id":1048204,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>semicasual</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1048183#post1048183\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">My preliminary answer has two parts:<br /> <br /> For the commoners who fought or killed people,<b> restrain them and save them for the magistrate.</b> Use force <i>only</i> to ensure compliance.<br /> <br /> For the Oathsworn who probably wouldn't face any punishment otherwise, <b>c</b><b>hallenge him to a duel</b>. Assuming you cannot legally pass judgement (read: execute) this man, this allows you to circumvent normal conventions and demonstrate to the watching crowd that you will, in fact, fight for them. As long as you are within the law, this does not have to be a &quot;fair&quot; duel - you may keep your armor and sword while allowing him the minimum armament necessary to participate. After all, it's not like he gave the peasant any chance to defend himself.<br /> <br /> That said, my answer might change depending on the answers to some questions:<br /> * What rules govern when one oathsworn is allowed to kill another one?<br /> * Does this society have laws about restitution, i.e. when a murderer pays recompense to the murdered party's family?<br /> * Are Oathsworn normally allowed to take the law into their hands?<br /></div> </div> </div> </div><br /> 1) He is in armor, or, really, parts of armor. Lamellar breastplate, splinted greaves and bracers, the only thing he doesn't have on is a helmet. While his full panoply is heavier than Kasai's mail, in his current state the two are about equal.<br /> <br /> 2) Oathsworn can kill each other either on the field of battle, or in an official (aka, announced, with challenges recieved, and with a number of witnesses and seconds observing) duel. Any other time is murder. So long as you get caught, that is. Nobility (and Oathsworn) assassinate each other all the time, relatively speaking. In many cases, a quiet assassination is preferred to a duel, feud or war.<br /> <br /> 3) If Kasai just up and killed anyone, he would be imprisoned until a Magistrate (almost always a fellow Oathsworn) could hear the case. At that point, Kasai and the injured party (if he killed someone, the deceased family members or dependents) would be allowed to present their side of the story. After hearing both sides, and any other witness recounts, the Magistrate would pass sentence based on precedent, the laws of each domain, and the circumstances of the crime. In the case of murder, there would almost always be money changing hands (kind of like <i>weregeld</i>, but not codified in law), with actual punishment being doled out based on social status. <br /> <br /> Kasai is technically a noble, so if he killed a commoner, he would basically get away punishment-free, asides from the death-fee (and any social/reputation damage). If he killed a noble, then things could get dicey. Depending on the social status of the deceased noble, he could be imprisoned for an amount of time (with accompanying hard labor), stripped of rank, made a slave to the deceased' house, or executed (privately)<br /> <br /> If Kasai was a commoner, he would just get imprisoned, or likely executed (publicly)<br /> <br /> Of course, official duels between nobles are outside and above the law, so they don't count as murder.<br /> <br /> For an &quot;official&quot; duel to be official, there has to be a recognized injured party, where their honor, dignity or somesuch has been offended, challenges have to be officially exchanged in the presence of witnesses, and the combatants have to have seconds and 5 witnesses apiece. Most duels are to first blood, third blood in the case of particularly grievous slights, or death when nothing else will suffice. Duels to the death are rare, and frowned upon, but not legally prohibited, as one's honor is considered &quot;above the law&quot; in the eyes of most nobles. Finally, both parties must be &quot;equal&quot; in equipment; challenging someone to a duel in plate and longspear, when all they have is mail and sword is considered highly dishonorable and contemptable.<br /> <br /> As long as those conditions are met, the two parties can pretty much go to town.<br /> <br /> Duels between nobles and commoners are rare, as it is considered exceptionally poor form to challenge someone not of your social class to a duel of honor. On the other hand, commoners are supposed to be deferential in most respects to nobility, and nobility are &quot;allowed&quot; to strike, beat or kill commoners that fail to show proper deference. It is considered rare to <u>actually</u> do so (most nobles will respond to improper deference or outright impropriety with a roll of the eyes and having the rude peasant removed from their presence, and most commoners aren't going to go out of their way to be assholes to people technically allowed to kill you), as it is considered low-class to be so ruled by your emotions, but the &quot;right-of-class&quot; is still present. <br /> <br /> For instance, this is why people weren't giving Kasai much shit about being a Hill-Tribesman, at least not to his face. If he was sufficiently insulted, he could strike them or even kill them. If he actually went through with killing the asshole monk that spat on him, he would receive no official punishment, at least from the secular authority. The abbot would probably ask him to do some sort of penance, however.<br /> <br /> For other crimes, there are fees and punishments, but generally nobles (and Kasai) are going to receive lesser versions, unless they piss off someone above them enough.<br /> <br /> 4) No, Oathsworn are not officially allowed to take the law into their hands. One has to be a Lord (or a Lord's duly-appointed representative, such as a Magistrate) in order to deliberate on the law, and pass judgement and punishment on cases. <br /> <br /> For example Kasai couldn't pass judgement on the peasants on his father's estate, but his father could. He was Oathsworn <u>and</u> the Lord of the estate (although a poor and rural one). One can hold land and be Oathsworn, and <u>not</u> be the Lord (in which case they have to bring the case to their Lord), or one could hold land and be Lord, but not an Oathsworn.<br /> <br /> Generally, depending on the domain, around 1/2 of the warriors that &quot;fight as&quot; Oathsworn (aka, heavy infantry + cavalry) aren't actually Oathsworn, just nobility. Being an Oathsworn at least nominally requires the swearing of Oaths (hence the name) that many nobles find onerous or impossible to uphold (not that many Oathsworn are all that great about upholding them).<br /> <br /> But, as above, the circumstances of a case influence the judgement, and if Kasai were to kill people in order to prevent a mob or riot, then his punishment would likely be highly lessened.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-13T06:45:23-08:00","id":1048214,"title":"","content":"OK then. Slightly revising my answer: <br /> <br /> For the commoners who fought or killed people,<b> restrain them and save them for the magistrate.</b> Use force <i>only</i> to ensure compliance. <br /> <br /> For the Oathsworn who probably wouldn't face any punishment otherwise, <b>c</b><b>hallenge him to a duel, </b>on the grounds that he killed someone working on your behalf and violated his Oaths. Have him fight as he is now. <br /> <br /> It's risky, but our alternatives seem to be:<br /> * Do nothing - look weak or heartless to the populace. <br /> * Kill him outright - probably provoke legal consequences or revenge killing from his family<br /> * Let him go for now and work to make him suffer later - look weak or heartless to the populace, <i>and</i> risk angering him when he's in a stronger position, <i>and </i>probably violate our Oaths. <br /> <br /> ...none of which promise a better outcome. Also, I think it would be a good character-building opportunity for Kasai to become the kind of person who tries to use duels to solve everything.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>On the frontier of the Wild South, there's only one woman with the grit to take on its most dangerous outlaws and bring them <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/984977-back-alive-or-maybe-dead-quest\" target=\"_blank\">Back Alive, or Maybe Dead.</a><br /> <br /> Avatar by <a href=\"http://puzzlinghappenstance.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">K.S. Brenowitz</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/908-semicasual","name":"semicasual","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar908_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2499"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-13T06:57:56-08:00","id":1048217,"title":"","content":"Here is the thing about writing ideas. They always pop up. Always. I got ideas for three different quests brewing, two different letter writing games, and three different real life games. That doesn't include short story/novel ideas.<br /> <br /> Ideas are easy. Finishing what you started is hard. You simply do not have enough time/energy to create every idea you have. Focus on the current project, write down the other ideas and save them for later.<br /> <br /> /soapbox<br /> <br /> Restrain commoners and save them for the magistrate. They are his problem. If you can't restrain them, let them go and let the Magistrate's people hunt them down.<br /> <br /> But... the Oathsworn who violated his Oaths? Challenge him to a Duel. A smear on one Oathsworn's honor is a smear on all Oathsworn's honor.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I write things.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3950-wonderandawe","name":"wonderandawe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3950_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5410"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-13T09:36:06-08:00","id":1048282,"title":"","content":"I was going to say 5 then 3 then 7, but reading the addendum, 5 then 7.<br /> Restrain the commoners, sort out who the witnesses are for later, then challenge the Oathsworn for doing nothing and letting people die.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Like my Avatar? Courtesy of<span style=\"color:#0000FF\"> Jen</span>! : <span style=\"font-size:12px\">Anybody want their characters to be experimented on ? </span>post 98<br /> <i>An Exalt is never unarmed.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4018-l-het-esh","name":"L'het'esh","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4018_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"521"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-13T13:08:36-08:00","id":1048401,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>semicasual</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1048214#post1048214\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">OK then. Slightly revising my answer:<br /> <br /> For the commoners who fought or killed people,<b> restrain them and save them for the magistrate.</b> Use force <i>only</i> to ensure compliance.<br /> <br /> For the Oathsworn who probably wouldn't face any punishment otherwise, <b>c</b><b>hallenge him to a duel, </b>on the grounds that he killed someone working on your behalf and violated his Oaths. Have him fight as he is now.<br /> <br /> It's risky, but our alternatives seem to be:<br /> * Do nothing - look weak or heartless to the populace.<br /> * Kill him outright - probably provoke legal consequences or revenge killing from his family<br /> * Let him go for now and work to make him suffer later - look weak or heartless to the populace, <i>and</i> risk angering him when he's in a stronger position, <i>and </i>probably violate our Oaths.<br /> <br /> ...none of which promise a better outcome. Also, I think it would be a good character-building opportunity for Kasai to become the kind of person who tries to use duels to solve everything.</div> </div> </div> </div>Seconded. He shouldn't get away with murder.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Thoughts ripple out, birthing others","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1624-falleneco","name":"FallenEco","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1624_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1927"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-13T22:45:22-08:00","id":1048593,"title":"","content":"Hmmmm. Restrain them and I guess the duel. It should be to fitst blood though. Or just restrain the lot including the oathsworn","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11567-jairain","name":"Jairain","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11567_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"345"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-18T19:14:16-08:00","id":1051295,"title":"","content":"I guess the general consensus is to duel the Oathsworn, but, as Jairain brought up above, there are more than one possible outcomes to a duel.<br /> <br /> We could wound him, to assuage our offended sensibilities, or we could swordmurder (or spearmurder/bowmurder. Technically, he gets first choice in armament) him. Both would send the message that we won't stand for such behavior, and uphold our own Oath of Compassion, but the two situations are <u>very</u> different, in tone and in how the two characters interact.<br /> <br /> So, before I write out the next post, I'm gonna have to ask what &quot;kind&quot; of duel we are challenging the guy to.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11892,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","user":"Boston123","date":". 02-19-2017, 12:06 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-19T07:21:09-08:00","id":1051510,"title":"","content":"To first blood, to show we are displeased. He was technically correct, the best kind of correct....<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Like my Avatar? Courtesy of<span style=\"color:#0000FF\"> Jen</span>! : <span style=\"font-size:12px\">Anybody want their characters to be experimented on ? </span>post 98<br /> <i>An Exalt is never unarmed.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4018-l-het-esh","name":"L'het'esh","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4018_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"521"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-19T13:12:39-08:00","id":1051631,"title":"","content":"Hmmmm..... how about something like cashiering?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cashiering\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/52/Degradation_alfred_dreyfus.jpg/1280px-Degradation_alfred_dreyfus.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=index&amp;utm_content=thumbnail\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cashiering\" target=\"_blank\"> Cashiering - Wikipedia </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cashiering\" target=\"_blank\"> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cashiering </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> Where, if we win a (nonlethal) duel, we strip them of their Oathsworn rank?<br /> <br /> I'll be honest; the idea occurred to me while I was browsing TV Tropes and came across the &quot;Insignia Rip-Off Ritual Page&quot; (<a href=\"http://tvtropes.org/pmwiki/pmwiki.php/Main/InsigniaRipOffRitual\" target=\"_blank\">http://tvtropes.org/pmwiki/pmwiki.ph...iaRipOffRitual</a>), and the idea resonated with me. It would serve as a much more potent punishment than &quot;merely&quot; humiliating him in combat.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-19T17:17:49-08:00","id":1051762,"title":"","content":"I was gonna vote for &quot;to the death&quot; but <b>c</b><b>ashiering</b> sounds better. <br /> <br /> Perhaps as a bonus afterwards we can turn him over to the magistrate for murder, since he will no longer be Oathsworn.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>On the frontier of the Wild South, there's only one woman with the grit to take on its most dangerous outlaws and bring them <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/984977-back-alive-or-maybe-dead-quest\" target=\"_blank\">Back Alive, or Maybe Dead.</a><br /> <br /> Avatar by <a href=\"http://puzzlinghappenstance.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">K.S. Brenowitz</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/908-semicasual","name":"semicasual","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar908_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2499"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-19T17:41:06-08:00","id":1051777,"title":"","content":"You two are counting on Kasai winning. It would be a quick end to the thread otherwise, but that's metagaming. What would Kasai do if he lost? What would nameless do? So why would he agree instead of elevating to death?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Like my Avatar? Courtesy of<span style=\"color:#0000FF\"> Jen</span>! : <span style=\"font-size:12px\">Anybody want their characters to be experimented on ? </span>post 98<br /> <i>An Exalt is never unarmed.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4018-l-het-esh","name":"L'het'esh","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4018_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"521"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-19T17:50:43-08:00","id":1051780,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>L'het'esh</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1051777#post1051777\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You two are counting on Kasai winning. It would be a quick end to the thread otherwise, but that's metagaming. What would Kasai do if he lost? What would nameless do? So why would he agree instead of elevating to death?</div> </div> </div> </div>Because Kasai's not thinking of himself.<br /> <br /> Ever been so angry that you entirely stopped thinking about what the consequences of your actions might be? So full of self-righteous indignation that you've convinced yourself that killing is <i>good</i> act? Or maybe just so desperate for a resolution that you stop thinking about the risk and just take whatever comes?<br /> <br /> The whole duel idea is reckless. Even fighting to first blood would seem to be a dangerous route to what would feel like a hollow victory. So if we're going to duel at all, might as well go for broke.<br /> <br /> As for why the opposing oathsworn might agree - he might just be arrogant as hell. If he truly has no regard for the lives of others, the opportunity to kill an upstart who had the audacity to challenge his right to do as he pleased might be a pleasant diversion from the nihilistic void of his life.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>On the frontier of the Wild South, there's only one woman with the grit to take on its most dangerous outlaws and bring them <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/984977-back-alive-or-maybe-dead-quest\" target=\"_blank\">Back Alive, or Maybe Dead.</a><br /> <br /> Avatar by <a href=\"http://puzzlinghappenstance.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">K.S. Brenowitz</a>","edited":{"userid":908,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/908-semicasual","user":"semicasual","date":". 02-19-2017, 08:55 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/908-semicasual","name":"semicasual","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar908_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2499"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-19T18:49:09-08:00","id":1051801,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>L'het'esh</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1051777#post1051777\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You two are counting on Kasai winning. It would be a quick end to the thread otherwise, but that's metagaming. What would Kasai do if he lost? What would nameless do? So why would he agree instead of elevating to death?</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> It all depends on what sort of duel we challenge him to.<br /> <br /> If it is a lethal duel, then the outcome is obvious. Of course, if Kasai gets killed, Blue Pines would certainly not just let him walk away, and neither would the peasants. If he won, he would almost-certainly get lynched, and he <i>knows this</i>. This, coupled with the fact that he doesn't view the killing of a peasant, something which was technically within his rights to do so, as something &quot;worth&quot; fighting a duel to the death over, meaning he wouldn't want to fight to the death, and likely try to refuse a challenge.<br /> <br /> Of course, refusing a duel outright would mean he would now get viewed as a coward and a bully, and his reputation in the region would get shot. He doesn't want that, so in that case, he would prefer to duel.<br /> <br /> If it were a non-lethal duel;<br /> <br /> If Kasai won, the Oathsworn would be honor-bound to concede the point to Kasai, and in this case, submit.<br /> If the Oathsworn won, Kasai would be honor-bound to let him go, as much as he and the commoners wouldn't like it.<br /> <br /> The Oathsworn would almost certainly prefer to duel to one or three-wounds, while Kasai (in what I think of him, at least) would prefer to duel to the death. However, cashiering/stripping a defeated Oathsworn of his status would be almost as acceptable, and in some ways <i>more</i> acceptable (it would certainly send a stronger message) , than killing him outright.<br /> <br /> As far as possibility of injuries:<br /> <br /> In a duel to the death, anything goes. Even victorious duelists can come away with broken bones, severe blood loss, grievous cuts anywhere armor doesn't cover, etc.<br /> <br /> In a non-lethal duel, both parties are honor-bound to <i>do their best</i> to prevent actually-lethal wounds, although accidents (and &quot;accidents&quot;) are always possible. Since both parties are in armor, they would actually have to put some effort into killing each other, and most injuries in a non-lethal duel are going to be relatively superficial. (Basically, if the Oathsworn killed Kasai, or vice-versa, polite society would have a &quot;what the fuck, man?!&quot; type-reaction, and lead to mild social shunning)<br /> <br /> As for the Oathsworn:<br /> <br /> He isn't nearly <u>that</u> nihilistic. He is relatively polite, charming and such, it is just that he, like most Lowlander high-nobility (although this guy isn't <i>that</i> rich, but is more affecting the behaviors of the upper class as a front), think of themselves as superior-by-birth to the peasants, and is consequently snotty. Most Lowlander nobility are going to be relatively isolated from the commoners (having Peasants direct Serfs, etc), and that, coupled with the Immaculate Faith preaching &quot;superiority through reincarnation&quot;, leads to most nobility treating peasants with mild, if coolly polite, disdain.<br /> <br /> Kasai, on the other hand, like many poor Lowlander noble families (especially those living on the fringes of Lowlander society, like in the hinterlands and such), interacted with the peasantry much more, to the point where he and his father helped out at planting and harvesting. They didn't have the money to hire many extra workers, and since their effectively-entire income came from the work of the peasants, they actually got down into the dirt and gave a hand. Even if they didn't have to, Kasai's father likely would have made him, if just to promote the idea that &quot;peasants are people too.&quot;<br /> <br /> Kasai gets the overwhelming majority of his moral upbringing, and therefore most of his Oathsworn sensibilities, from his father. His father is the most moral man that he knows (something that actively held his father back from promotion, although Kasai doesn't know that), and he tries to emulate him as best he can. <br /> <br /> This means that the Oathsworn flat-out doesn't understand why Kasai is being so potentially-pissy about this. Of course, he knows about the Oath of Compassion (he swore it, like many/most Oathsworn), but in his mind his noble status comes first, and he expects Kasai to believe the same.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-19T19:17:35-08:00","id":1051809,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Boston123</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1051801#post1051801\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This means that the Oathsworn flat-out doesn't understand why Kasai is being so potentially-pissy about this. Of course, he knows about the Oath of Compassion (he swore it, like many/most Oathsworn), but in his mind his noble status comes first, and he expects Kasai to believe the same.</div> </div> </div> </div>Learning new moral lessons late in life is hard. Sometimes a sudden shock is needed to get a body to re-evaluate things.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>On the frontier of the Wild South, there's only one woman with the grit to take on its most dangerous outlaws and bring them <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/984977-back-alive-or-maybe-dead-quest\" target=\"_blank\">Back Alive, or Maybe Dead.</a><br /> <br /> Avatar by <a href=\"http://puzzlinghappenstance.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">K.S. Brenowitz</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/908-semicasual","name":"semicasual","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar908_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2499"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-21T21:19:19-08:00","id":1053028,"title":"","content":"So, it appears everyone wants to duel this Oathsworn, with two for \u201csingle strike\u201d and 3 for \u201cgeneric\u201d duel, which I am going to take as \u201cthree strikes\u201d. To be fair, I am going to metaphorically flip a coin:<br /> <br /> Heads is \u201csingle strike\u201d<br /> <br /> Tails is \u201cthree strikes\u201d<br /> <br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"https://www.random.org/coins/?num=1&amp;cur=60-usd.0025c-md\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://www.random.org/faces/60-usd/0025c-md/obverse.jpg\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://www.random.org/coins/?num=1&amp;cur=60-usd.0025c-md\" target=\"_blank\"> RANDOM.ORG - Coin Flipper </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://www.random.org/coins/?num=1&amp;cur=60-usd.0025c-md\" target=\"_blank\"> https://www.random.org/coins/?num=1&amp;cur=60-usd.0025c-md </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> This form allows you to flip virtual coins based on true randomness, which for many purposes is better than the pseudo-random number algorithms typically used in computer programs. </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> Tails it is!<br /> ------<br /> <br /> You sigh, rubbing at the bridge of your nose to ward off the oncoming headache.<br /> <br /> \u201cBlue Pines, find some way to restrain these men, until we can find a way to bring them before the city magistrate.\u201d Turning to the gathered vigilantes, \u201cWhile I appreciate the idea that you hold me as an authority figure, I <i>really</i> can\u2019t pass judgement, not legally. As such, the culprits will be restrained, and turned over to the rightful authorities. Is that acceptable?\u201d<br /> <br /> The leader flushes, and then nods his assent, not very happy with the outcome, but accepting your decision nonetheless.<br /> <br /> Hooking his hands under his sash, the Oathsworn rocks back on his heels, \u201cSo, does this mean I am free to go?\u201d His half-smile indicates just how seriously he is taking all of this.<br /> <br /> He is surprised to see you frown, stepping forward in front of him as you do so. \u201c\u2026.. No.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c..What?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cYour behavior, even in light of current events, is <i>despicable</i>. Do you not know of the Oath of Compassion?\u201d<br /> <br /> Annoyed, \u201cOf course I do! What the hell are you going on about?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cThen your actions are to be <i>doubly damned</i>! Instead of killing people, you should have been here, helping organize the rescue effort, not <i>cowering</i> in your tent away from the danger. What do you have to say in defense of your behavior?!\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cI\u2026. is this about the peasant? Listen, I swore the Oath of Compassion, but that doesn\u2019t mean the common rabble can just mouth off whenever they want! He had to be put in his place, made an example of! There are rules about that sort of thing, and I..\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cYes, <b>there are</b>, and you <b>broke them</b><i>. </i>How dare you. <b><i><u>How dare you</u></i></b>.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c\u2026. I don\u2019t really understand why you are making such a big deal about this. In a way, this is a \u201c<i>good</i> thing\u201d, as I am now here, and can help out, therefore fulfilling my Oaths. Is that not correct?\u201d<br /> <br /> In response, you pop the hanguard of your sword from the sheath with your thumb, ringing bronze against brass, a soft sound that nonetheless carries across the sand with all the impact of a temple bell. Even the low muttering of the crowd silences, and the Oathsworn jerks back as if struck, face pale and eyes wide.<br /> <br /> \u201cYou\u2019ve\u2026\u2026 you\u2019re <b>joking</b>. You have got to be kidding me. This, of all things, is worth fighting over?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cYes. This, of all things, is most definitely worth fighting over! We are supposed todefend these people, serve as examples and role-models, not cow them with fist and blade. Our status is but a means toward that end, not the end itself.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cYour behavior and demeanor disgusts me, and your flagrant flouting of your Oaths dishonors not only me, but anyone else who swears the same Oaths, holds themselves to the same standards. On top of that, your lack of remorse for your deed is appalling, and shows your true colors. In my eyes, <i>you have no honor</i>, and pretending to do so dishonors everyone around you. There is but one action possible in the face of such an insult, and I mean to take it.\u201d<br /> <br /> You arch an eyebrow, \u201cUnless, of course, you wish to admit to your failings, and submit for proper sentencing. That would but reinforce your cowardice, however.\u201d<br /> <br /> He stares, and then responds animatedly, \u201cYou know you can\u2019t just \u201chave\u201d a duel, you need seconds, and witnesses, and challenges! A bout with honor and dignity, not just some roughs tangling in the street!\u201d<br /> <br /> The other eyebrow raises, \u201cWell, as far as I can see it, we have witnesses, and seconds; my armswoman and the militiaman, who is by comparison a neutral party. You can consider this my official challenge, so all that remains is your answer.\u201d<br /> <br /> Raising your head, to glare full into his face, \u201cWell? If you do not accept, I will have no choice but to consider your denial as submission, and you will be restrained with the rest of the lawbreakers.\u201d Smirking, \u201cI am sure we can find some nice chains to make sure you won\u2019t cause any more trouble.\u201d<br /> <br /> He glances around, defeated, at the angry faces of the surrounding crowd, at you, full of wrath. \u201cIs this going to be a duel to the death?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cNo. Unlike you, I am not a <i>murderer</i>. \u201c<br /> <br /> He snarls, angry, \u201c <i>Don\u2019t call me that</i>!\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cI am only speaking the truth. Win the duel, and I will call you whatever you want. What weapon do you choose?\u201d<br /> <br /> After a moment, he smirks coldly, \u201cThe sword, of course. I will have you know that I attended the Seigen school of swordcraft, and knelt on the dojo floor in front of Sifu Seigen himself.\u201d<br /> You shrug, \u201cI have no idea what that means, nor do I care.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cIt means you are going to have a <i>bad time</i>.\u201d<br /> <br /> You shrug again, if only to irritate the guy a little more. \u201cBlue Pines, stand over\u2026\u2026 here. Haru, you go stand opposite her, about 30 feet away. Everyone else, stand outside of them, and don\u2019t get in the way, or interfere.\u201d<br /> <br /> It takes a few moments for the crowd to shuffle away from the imaginary line drawn in the sand, and soon you are left alone with the Oathsworn, glaring at each other across the empty expanse.<br /> <br /> The air is thick with tension, and on the dropping of a length of timber to the sand, you explode into action. Sprinting toward the man, drawing your blade as you do so, you are forced to suddenly backpedal in the face of several blinding slashes aimed at your face. He is <i>fast</i>: you didn\u2019t even see him draw, and you are soon put on the back foot from his rapid, looping slashes and thrusts.<br /> <br /> Some form of consternation must have shown itself in your face, because the Oathsworn suddenly laughs, drawing back as he does so into a high-guard, blade lifted over his head. In response, you shift into a low-guard, handle held at waist-height with the blade pointed at his knee, in effort to draw him into overexposing himself.<br /> <br /> He doesn\u2019t fall for the bait, instead rapidly stepping forward, stomping hard on the sand and bringing his sword down in a mighty blow. You twist to the side, in an effort to dodge, and try to knock his blade to the side to set up a strike of your own, to no avail. His blade snakes around your own, and your elbow burns with cold fire as he scores a cut on the underside of your arm with the weak edge of his sword.<br /> <br /> He exults, crowing, \u201cThat\u2019s one!\u201d<br /> <br /> You give no response as you head back to your starting position; best not to show weakness, and being stone-faced could intimidate the bastard as much as pain could provoke him.<br /> Back in your spot, while waiting for the start signal to impact the earth, you consider your options. This guy is <i>fast</i>, surprisingly so, and he apparently uses that alongside an aggressive style of footwork and strikes to quickly overwhelm and wound the opponent. You, on the other hand, prefer a more defensive style, probably inherited from your skills with the spear, and lack the speed to really compete with him on that level.<br /> <br /> However, you are fairly certain you\u2019ve got a few inches at least on him, and that means you are probably stronger, with a higher degree of leverage. If you can get him in a bind, or throw some grappling moves into the mix, you could very well defeat him on that front. Sounds like a plan or, at least, something better than just feeding yourself into his strikes.<br /> <br /> This time, when the timber strikes the ground, you don\u2019t rush out of your position, instead stepping carefully forward, blade held in a middle-guard, handle again at waist-height, blade pointed at his stomach. He, in turn, takes up the same stance as before.<br /> <br /> The two of you slowly orbit around each other, neither one stepping forward to test what the other hand planned. This, apparently, psyches out the Oathsworn, who proceeds to taunt and degrade you from across the circle, calling you a coward and other choice insults. You, all part of your plan, don\u2019t even dignify him with a response, instead continuing your slow circling, still in a defensive position.<br /> <br /> After but a few moments, he has apparently had enough waiting, and with a wild yell, charges forward, blade still raised high. In response, you <i>step into </i>his strike, getting inside the reach of his blade, so that the weakest part of the strike lands on your armored shoulder. Your mail crunches, and the impact causes your knees to buckle and you to grunt in pain, but your plan, so far, works flawlessly; his blade is relatively neutralized, and you are up close where you can use your size and leverage to your advantage.<br /> <br /> To that end, you grab hold of your own sword by the tip, and bring the whole thing up into his elbows, in effort to push his arms (and blade) off the centerline and allow you to attack. This takes him by surprise, apparently, and he staggers off to the side, only to shout in pain as you draw your sword across his cheek.<br /> <br /> \u201cOne for me\u201d, you say calmly, already turning around to head back to your position. He stands still for a moment; hand over his bleeding cheek, before spitting some blood onto the sand in your general direction and returning to his spot.<br /> <br /> In spite of yourself, you can\u2019t help but feel a sense of satisfaction (?) at how the duel is going so far. Sure, sure, you are both evenly wounded, but your wound is comparatively minor, while he has in return taken a wound across the face, which is bleeding heavily now. The pain from the wound would evaporate any sense of cockiness he might have, and he could be intimidated or even cowed as a result. Both far preferable to his aggressiveness and blinding speed from earlier.<br /> <br /> Unfortunately, it would seem the cut had the opposite effect, incensing him instead. Straight off the bat, he charges in at a dead sprint, chopping down from left to right in great, glittering arcs. This time, you step to the side of the strike, interspersing your own blade at the nadir of his strike. With a push to the side, you divert his blade again, flowing the movement into an elbow strike that impacts with his nose <b>most satisfactorily</b>. With a strangled cry, the Oathsworn staggers, and drops to a knee, only to get cut again, this time on the opposite cheek.<br /> <br /> \u201cNow, does that count for one strike, or for two?\u201d You quip, almost amused, as you turn to head back to your side of the circle.<br /> <br /> \u201c<b>Fuck you!</b>\u201d, through a broken lip and mouthful of blood. \u201cI\u2019ll kill you!\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cBased on your current score, I don\u2019t find that too likely. What will I bre.\u201d<br /> <br /> With a sudden, furious yell, he lunges across the sand, blade in hand. Before you can turn around, he drives his sword deep into your upper thigh, up under the mail of your thigh-guards. White fire lances up and down the limb and it collapses beneath you, dropping you to the ground with a gasp. It hurts so much you can\u2019t even scream, just curl up into a ball on the sand.<br /> <br /> The Oathsworn draws back in sudden shock, stammering, \u201c Oh <i>shit!</i>\u2026. I\u2026.. I didn\u2019t mean to\u2026\u201d<br /> <br /> Mastering yourself through the pain, you manage to grasp the blade projecting out the back of your thigh with enough leverage to <i>slowly</i> pull the steel out. If possible, it hurts even worse than the initial stabbing, and tears come unbidden, streaming down your face.<br /> <br /> Painfully, you manage to stand up into a sort of half- crouch, enough to look out at the circle of onlookers. Most of the peasants are staring, in various shades of shock and horror, while Blue Pines looks pissed enough to spit the bastard like a pig at a barbecue. None of them, however, move to intervene, whether from fear of reprisal or shock.<br /> <br /> By bracing a hand on your knee, and locking the joint, you are able to stand up to close to full height, shuffling as you do so in order to face the Oathsworn challenger, who flinches in fear and shame, diverting his eyes.<br /> <br /> \u201c\u2026 Way to prove your cowardice, striking from behind.\u201d<br /> <br /> He cringes, expecting a blow. Instead, you toss him his sword, where it bounces in the sand at his feet.<br /> <br /> \u201cLet\u2019s finish this.\u201d<br /> <br /> Once you manage to actually put weight on the limb, it actually stops hurting to a significant degree, throbbing instead of burning, and you find yourself actually not too bothered by the blow. Instead, a cold kind of calm spreads through your body, and you shake your head to clear it, the better to focus on the man in front of you.<br /> <br /> You are focusing so intently that you don\u2019t even hear the starting signal (if someone even remembered to set it off), and react to your opponents movements after the fact. He is already half way over to you before you start lifting your blade, and it takes all the effort you can muster to deflect the falling blow from your face. You can\u2019t exactly dodge, so instead you drive your shoulder into his, driving him back and scoring a line across his lamellar as he stumbles. Since you are already moving with him, you continue the check, stumbling forward and carving another sloppy gouge over his breastplate. Again, you follow him back, too close now to actually strike. You actually fall into him, confused, and he drops down to a knee from your sudden dead-weight. Grabbing on to him, as much to hold yourself upright as to hold him in place, you clumsily (your fingers don\u2019t want to work. Odd.) pull your dagger from its sheath and hold it to his throat.<br /> <br /> \u201c\u2026 Do you yield?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c\u2026. I do! Stop fighting!\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cG\u2026 Good. Give me your sword.\u201d<br /> <br /> Taking the blade, you turn to address the crowd, \u201cThrough my victory, I prove my superiority, and uphold the intent of my Oaths. Men, take this miscreant and\u2026an.\u201d The fog in your mind is suddenly overwhelming, and none of your extremities quite feel like working properly all of a sudden. The blade drops to the sand with a distant muffled clang, your vision fades to almost-black, and the last thing you see before passing out is the dim sight of a shape rushing at you in the fog.<br /> <br /> ---------<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> It feels like millennia before you are able to draw yourself fully from your sleep, down on the edge of Oblivion, and even now you are \u201cawake\u201d, you can\u2019t quite see anything. An inky blackness covers your eyes, and you panic, reaching up to your face to make sure your eyes are still there, that the spirits of the dead didn\u2019t steal them when you tarried too close to their realm.<br /> <br /> To your surprise (and immense relief), you find there is a strip of cloth wound about your face, around your forehead and eyesockets. Puzzled, you feel around the winding bandage, searching for the knot, only to hear the sudden rasp of a door sliding back. You jerk your head towards the unseen motion, only to be struck by a sudden, overwhelming sense of nausea and weakness. Choking back bile, you reach out towards the sound, calling out but only managing a croaking rasp. You are rewarded by the sound of shattering ceramic and the patter of running feet. Cursing, you grasp at the floorboards, trying to pull yourself towards the sound, but stricken with weakness as you are, you just manage to effectively pull yourself off your futon and into the middle of the room, gasping and drenched in sweat. After a few minutes (maybe longer. You might have passed out, to be honest), the sound of footfalls return, multiple ones, and you are greeted by the voice of Lord Iysen.<br /> <br /> \u201cWell, <i>about time</i>. You\u2019ve been lying in bed for long enough!\u201d<br /> <br /> Despite the choice of words, there is no chiding in her voice, only relief (?), and she walks over to where you are laying. Kneeling down, she gently places a cool hand on the side of your face, helping you sit up in order to accept a cup full of water. After a few sips, the desert that was your mouth and tongue is capable of speech once more, and you croak out,<br /> <br /> \u201c<i>What happened? Why are my eyes bandaged?\u201d</i><br /> <br /> Lord Isyen laughs, more of a huff than a chuckle, \u201cOh, believe me Kasai, <i>plenty</i> has happened. In a way, I envy you. You got to sleep through it.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cFirstly, your eyes are bandaged because they were damaged by smoke and heat. My physician thought it best if they were protected from light until they recovered. Do you want the wrapping off?\u201d<br /> <br /> At a nod, she fumbles around by the back of your head, tugging at the linen until; finally, the wrappings come away from your eyes. Almost instantly, even with the low level of light in the room, your eyes tear up and pain, and it takes a few moments for them to clear. Blinking away tears, you gradually make out the figure of Lord Iysen, kneeling beside you, and Airi (you think), her youngest daughter, standing in the doorframe.<br /> <br /> \u201cSo\u2026.. I take it that there was an assassination attempt?\u201d<br /> <br /> At this, Lord Iysen turns and looks to her daughter, who closes the door and leaves.<br /> <br /> \u201cYou would be right. In hindsight, it was so <i>obvious</i>, to the point that I am embarrassed to admit that I didn\u2019t see it happening earlier.\u201d<br /> <br /> She sits back; closing her eyes, and looks every bit of her forty-or-so years.<br /> <br /> \u201cMy former son-in-law was masterminding a plot to have me assassinated. As you discovered, in that letter of yours.\u201d<br /> <br /> You stare, considering the implications.<br /> <br /> \u201c\u2026. I don\u2019t want to sound stupid, here, but what would he get out of that? Isn\u2019t your daughter your heir, so she would be Lord Xia after your death?\u201d<br /> <br /> Her lips work,\u201d True, but Mino was absolutely <u>enthralled</u> with Haichi, and would likely have gone along with whatever he wanted. His effect on her was pretty much the only reason I allowed the betrothal to go through, and she was so happy, so comparatively well-behaved after the fact, that I\u2026\u2026 well, I must have ignored several obvious signs.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cSo, there will be war then? The Lord Yue would certainly not take too kindly to his son and heir being killed.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c\u2026. Haichi is still alive, Kasai.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cWait, what?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cAfter the assassination attempt, I was in no condition to go chase down a fleeing man,\u201d At this, she holds up her hands, which have dual half-healed slices deep in the palms. \u201cPlus, my bodyguards were dead, and I had to look out for my children. On top of that, the fire he and his cronies set prevented any real sort of effective response. You saw that yourself first-hand.\u201d<br /> \u201c\u2026.I see. I\u2026. I am sorry about Nori. I found him in the audience-box.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cThank you, that means quite a lot. We were\u2026\u2026 close.\u201d<br /> <br /> There are a few moments of silence, rather awkward on your part, until;<br /> <br /> \u201cSo,\u2026 what else happened. How are your children holding up?\u201d<br /> <br /> She stands, walking over to the window. \u201cTo say that Mino is taking it rather hard is an understatement. She has locked herself in her room for the last two weeks, an..\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c<i>Two weeks</i>! It has been two weeks since the attack?!\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cYes, Kasai. You have been unconscious for the last two weeks. A little longer, actually. Not surprising, considering how badly-wounded you were. Between the blood-loss, the infection, the lack of sleep, and the dehydration, you almost died several times. I, to be crude, have had to spend a lot of silver, sweat, tears and blood to keep you alive.\u201d<br /> <br /> She grins, \u201cBlood is literal, by the way. You received several blood transfusions, from me and from your page. We both were compatible, and you took a <i>lot</i> out of us. And my Airi, who has all-but taken over as your caregiver while you recovered. Do you realize how much you weigh, and how difficult it is to move you as dead-weight?\u201d<br /> <br /> You stare, confused. \u201cLord\u2026.. if I may ask; why did you go through so much effort to keep me alive? Not that I don\u2019t <i>appreciate</i> it, but\u2026. I am a nobody. Not important in the least.\u201d<br /> <br /> She kneels down close, \u201cYou <b>are</b> important, Kasai. Firstly, because of what you did during the fire, and what you have been doing ever since you got here. Saiko told me what you did at her village, and Blue Pines recapped what happened in the square. The people that you saved during the fire have been all-but singing your praises for the last two weeks. I am fairly certain that if you walked into any public-house in the city, you could drink for free, and if I just left you to die, I could have had a riot on my hands.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cMore importantly, what you did was <i>rare</i>. Most Oathsworn don\u2019t pay nearly as much attention to the intent of their Oaths as you do. Hell, <i>I</i> didn\u2019t, and don\u2019t. Such is the reality of ruling a country, unfortunately. Such determination is worthy of respect, if not outright admiration, and I would seek to cultivate that, if I could. For that reason alone, I would seek to gain your loyalty. I am going to need every skilled warrior I can find, after all.\u201d<br /> <br /> She inhales, then, \u201cOn top of that, I owe you a life-debt. Without your interference, I most-certainly would have died in the assassination plot, probably after Mino and Haichi were already married. You, almost single-handedly, saved my life, saved the lives of my children, and prevented my domain from falling into the hands of the Lord Yue. I owe you everything I have at this moment.\u201d<br /> With this, she bends at the waist and presses her forehead to the floor, in the most formal of formal bows.<br /> <br /> You stare silently, in shock at the sudden reversal of protocol, and after a moment, she raises back into her kneeling position.<br /> <br /> \u201cWith that in mind\u2026.. What do you want, Kasai?\u201d<br /> <br /> ------------<br /> <br /> Yeah, Kasai got fucked up. Life sucks when you are a mortal, then you die!<br /> <br /> Blood loss is a bitch. On top of actual blood loss, Kasai had to deal with the shock of the wound, dehydration, lack of effective sleep, and stress. The only reason he lived is because Blue Pines basically jammed a bunch of cloth inside the wound\u2026\u2026 hence the infection.<br /> <br /> After this last decision, there will be a kind of epilogue-type-deal, then a timeskip, as Kasai needs to recuperate before he gets into more trouble.<br /> <br /> For those who guess Haichi was the culprit \u2026.. surprise, even though it should have been <i>really</i> obvious, both in-story and out-story. Lord Iysen did, in fact, notice Haichi behaving suspiciously, but she kinda ignored it because he made Mino happy and not so much of a problem child. More below.<br /> <br /> Mino, the oldest girl, has been a quintessential living terror since her father died about 11 years ago. Acting out, getting in arguments with her mother, refusing any and all possible suitors, etc. On top of that, she was always a very \u2026. Willful and emotional child, doing and saying thing by impulse instead of actually thinking things through. Very intelligent, just stubborn and the like. Not able to compromise. Lord Iysen despairs the day when Mino becomes Lord, but she also doesn\u2019t really want to disinherit her, because that would just finish the rift that is already started.<br /> She actually calmed down quite a bit when she got betrothed then engaged to Haichi Matsu, the reasons being 1) they were actually alike in age, as opposed to marrying some guy in his late 30s, and 2) they actually got along very well. She took his attempted assassination attempt (of her mother. He didn\u2019t want her to be involved at all) as a very personal betrayal, and wondered if everything was a lie, yadda yadda yadda.<br /> <br /> The assassination attempt was actually \u201csupposed\u201d to go off after the wedding, but Kasai entered the picture and cocked it all up. In desperation, since he thought he got found out and the noose was closing, Haichi started the plot off early, trying to murder Lord Isyen and scamper off home. Obviously, she is made of sterner stuff, and he started the fires in effort to cover his own escape.<br /> Oh, and he sent assassins after Kasai, primarily because Kasai took out the former Captain of the Guard. Ironically, if Kasai didn\u2019t do that, Haichi would have had enough assassins to kill Lord Iysen so\u2026\u2026 win?<br /> <br /> As for the Oathsworn, we dueled\u2026 he was a Martial Artist, aka the arrogant kung-fu guy that gets his ass kicked and hopefully learns something as a result. He is pretty young, and has never fought anyone outside of a tournament or regulated duel, meaning he has never killed anyone. He was a minor beginning antagonist in my Hundred Kingdoms campaign, and my players beat his ass, scarred him, then recruited him as a retainer. He might show up again.<br /> <br /> What Kasai did with his sword at the start of the duel was the cultural-equivalent of cocking the hammer on a revolver, or pumping a shotgun. Shorthand for &quot;shit's about to go down.&quot;<br /> <br /> On a final note: Lord Iysen is basically giving us a metaphorical \u201cblank check\u201d. Shoot out ideas, guys!<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11892,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","user":"Boston123","date":". 02-22-2017, 08:44 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-22T00:23:16-08:00","id":1053083,"title":"","content":"...can i ask for a deadline extension? Because I want to really think about this. Kasai has been pushing rather hard since we met him and an offer like this is a big deal...At the very least we could join her house, (meaning we work for Lord Iysen) but that might compromise the rocky relationship with our grandfather that I hoped to rekindle. And given our father's status...though we could...<br /> <br /> this is why i need time to think.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Thoughts ripple out, birthing others","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1624-falleneco","name":"FallenEco","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1624_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1927"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-22T05:41:36-08:00","id":1053148,"title":"","content":"My first request is for you to put a horizontal line or some other divider between your story and your OOC commentary.<br /> <br /> My second request, in character, is to ask to became one of Lord Ilysen's sworn retainers. Steady employment with someone who respects Kasai in a community full of people who think Kasai is some kind of hero... sounds like exactly what we've been shooting for since the beginning. Alternately, we could just ask for money and equipment to carry on with our knight-errantry elsewhere, but I feel that would be a waste. <br /> <br /> Also, we should ask what became of the Oathsworn that Kasai defeated. Kasai passed out before he could give a judgement, and that was two weeks ago.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>On the frontier of the Wild South, there's only one woman with the grit to take on its most dangerous outlaws and bring them <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/984977-back-alive-or-maybe-dead-quest\" target=\"_blank\">Back Alive, or Maybe Dead.</a><br /> <br /> Avatar by <a href=\"http://puzzlinghappenstance.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">K.S. Brenowitz</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/908-semicasual","name":"semicasual","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar908_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2499"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-22T05:54:07-08:00","id":1053151,"title":"","content":"I originally did insert a dividing line. It must have gotten sent off to Elsewhere when I reformatted the text after copypasting it from Word.<br /> <br /> You guys are also being <u>awfully</u> vanilla and humble with requests. Just throwing it out there, and I don't want to influence anything. Humility is a virtue, yes, but at the same time...... you gotta get ahead. One of the more powerful leaders in the Hundred Kingdoms <u>owes us</u> a favor, and is asking us what we want, as opposed to telling us what they will give us. Pretty significant.<br /> <br /> Kasai isn't going to be able to walk for at least a month, or fight/travel for quite some time after that, so his actual capability to..... well, do much of anything meaningful for the next half-year or so is pretty much nil.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-22T07:10:18-08:00","id":1053175,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>FallenEco</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1053083#post1053083\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">...can i ask for a deadline extension? Because I want to really think about this. Kasai has been pushing rather hard since we met him and an offer like this is a big deal...At the very least we could join her house, (meaning we work for Lord Iysen) but that might compromise the rocky relationship with our grandfather that I hoped to rekindle. And given our father's status...though we could...<br /> <br /> this is why i need time to think.</div> </div> </div> </div>The relationship between Kasai and his grandfather isn't &quot;rocky&quot;, just currently nonexistent. Before Kasai left, they got along very well, and Kasai's leaving wasn't because his Grandfather didn't want him there, but because Kasai needed to go home to receive Oathsworn training. Granted, his grandfather would have preferred for him to stay, but he wasn't about to force him to.<br /> <br /> His grandfather also isn't really going to care all that much about what Kasai does, and in fact, if/when (far more likely. This debacle is <u>big</u> news) he hears about it, he is more likely to be impressed with Kasai's behavior than anything else.<br /> <br /> As for Kasai's father: remember..... if we want to be entirely technical, Kasai really isn't a member of the Harutane family, not legally. By the standards and customs of the Lowlanders, he is a bastard child, born out of wedlock. Kasai uses his father's name because not doing so would be sociocultural suicide. Not that his father would mind all that much, he wanted to legitmize Kasai, but his stepmother totally would. On the other hand, they are across the Red River, in another domain entirely, so what his stepmother thinks about him is largely irrelevant.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-22T07:18:37-08:00","id":1053182,"title":"","content":"Now, I don't particularly want to influence any possible decisions, but here are several of the things I've come up with plans for.'<br /> <br /> - Become one of Lord Iysen's retainers. Vanilla<br /> -Ask for quite a large sum of money. Helpful, to say the least.<br /> - Become Lord Iysen's Captain of the Guard. We took out the last one through our snooping, and have already indicated we are capable of finding out plots.<br /> <br /> The above could be implemented without too large of an effect, socially.<br /> <br /> - Take on Aketchi Shiba as a page. In Lord Iysen's eyes, we have already demonstrated our worth as a paragon, and therefore, who better to train her son to be a &quot;proper&quot; Oathsworn?<br /> - Ask for Airi Shiba's hand in marriage. She <u>volunteered</u> to take care of us in our invalid state, that has to stand for something.<br /> - Ask for Mino Shiba's hand in marriage. I too could serve as a moderating influence, and I promise not to stab you in the back!<br /> - Ask to be adopted into the Shiba family. This one that I have ruminated on the least, but would potentially be the most interesting (and hilarious) choice.<br /> <br /> The above would twist the silken panties of the nobility to various degrees, what with us being an unknown. However, Lord Iysen owes us a life-debt, so......","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-22T07:31:48-08:00","id":1053192,"title":"","content":"What's wrong with vanilla? I like vanilla. Vanilla mixes well with all kinds of other flavors <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/frown.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Frown\" smilieid=\"11\" class=\"inlineimg\" />. Kasai, up to this point, hasn't shown any goals other than &quot;Uphold the Oaths&quot;, &quot;Make Money&quot;, and &quot;Build a Reputation.&quot; \u200bWe're pretty much set on the first and third, so I figure now's a good time to focus on the second.<br /> <br /> OK, how about this - can Lord Ilysen give us a title of our own? Maybe a small estate? I'm not sure how you're using nobility rankings, but IRL lords had the right to assign fiefs to vassals.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> - Take on Aketchi Shiba as a page. In Lord Iysen's eyes, we have already demonstrated our worth as a paragon, and therefore, who better to train her son to be a &quot;proper&quot; Oathsworn?<br /> - Ask for Airi Shiba's hand in marriage. She <u>volunteered</u> to take care of us in our invalid state, that has to stand for something.<br /> - Ask for Mino Shiba's hand in marriage. I too could serve as a moderating influence, and I promise not to stab you in the back!<br /> - Ask to be adopted into the Shiba family. This one that I have ruminated on the least, but would potentially be the most interesting (and hilarious) choice. </div> </div> </div>I have no strong feelings about getting another page - we could certainly still do that as a vassal/retainer.<br /> <br /> Jumping into a marriage with someone we've only met once (being unconscious doesn't count) seems... premature, even for a determined gold digging sex fiend. Let's wait on that one for a while.<br /> <br /> What are the social implications of adoption?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>On the frontier of the Wild South, there's only one woman with the grit to take on its most dangerous outlaws and bring them <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/984977-back-alive-or-maybe-dead-quest\" target=\"_blank\">Back Alive, or Maybe Dead.</a><br /> <br /> Avatar by <a href=\"http://puzzlinghappenstance.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">K.S. Brenowitz</a>","edited":{"userid":908,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/908-semicasual","user":"semicasual","date":". 02-22-2017, 10:40 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/908-semicasual","name":"semicasual","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar908_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2499"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-22T08:43:48-08:00","id":1053240,"title":"","content":"The point of going to the tournament was to make a little money and, if lucky, find a position. Ask to be made a retainer, and modestly let Lord Iysen choose the rest.<br /> (It would be greedy and grasping to ask for a village to rule, or to replace Nori or the late, unlamented captain.)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Like my Avatar? Courtesy of<span style=\"color:#0000FF\"> Jen</span>! : <span style=\"font-size:12px\">Anybody want their characters to be experimented on ? </span>post 98<br /> <i>An Exalt is never unarmed.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4018-l-het-esh","name":"L'het'esh","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4018_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"521"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-22T17:50:56-08:00","id":1053541,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>semicasual</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1053192#post1053192\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What's wrong with vanilla? I like vanilla. Vanilla mixes well with all kinds of other flavors <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/frown.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Frown\" smilieid=\"11\" class=\"inlineimg\" />. Kasai, up to this point, hasn't shown any goals other than &quot;Uphold the Oaths&quot;, &quot;Make Money&quot;, and &quot;Build a Reputation.&quot; \u200bWe're pretty much set on the first and third, so I figure now's a good time to focus on the second.<br /> <br /> OK, how about this - can Lord Ilysen give us a title of our own? Maybe a small estate? I'm not sure how you're using nobility rankings, but IRL lords had the right to assign fiefs to vassals.<br /> <br /> <br /> I have no strong feelings about getting another page - we could certainly still do that as a vassal/retainer.<br /> <br /> Jumping into a marriage with someone we've only met once (being unconscious doesn't count) seems... premature, even for a determined gold digging sex fiend. Let's wait on that one for a while.<br /> <br /> What are the social implications of adoption?</div> </div> </div> </div>If it is any consolation, vanilla is by far my favorite ice cream flavor.<br /> <br /> She can most certainly enfeoff an estate, and in many ways, it would be preferable to having Kasai be a live-in retainer.<br /> <br /> With live-in retainers, their services are always available, and therefore keeping a solid body of troops for offense or defense is fast and easy. On the other hand, keeping retainers on-hand on your own dime is <u>expensive as hell</u>. Figure fodder for the horses (most Oathsworn/retainers own more than one. Kasai is just poor as hell), food, wine, clothing, linens, etc. Costs rack up fast, especially when the retainers are noble-born, and therefore would complain about being given the same standards-of-living as a common footsoldier.<br /> <br /> On the other hand, having retainers hold estates and manors removes them from immediate use, since in the case of military action;<br /> <br /> 1) the message has to get out to them<br /> 2) they have to get ready, which could take some time if they have a retinue of any appreciable size, including peasant Men-at-arms<br /> 3) they have to make their way (which, again, could take some time) to the central mustering point<br /> <br /> However, it also essentially eliminates any cost to your wallet, as owning a fief allows the retainer to support themselves off the labor of the commoners, while keeping them happy (it is nice to be recognized for your service, after all), and ensures that the hinterlands of your domain will at least have token protection. Individually, landed retainers have banded together and fought off threats ranging from bandit infestations all the way to minor invasions, since they are individual and capable of moving quickly, compared to an infantry regiment.<br /> <br /> Generally, the social ranking system in my Hundred Kingdoms goes:<br /> **NOBLES**<br /> -(Great) Lord; Iysen Shiba, the Lord Yue (Haichi's father)<br /> -Lord: Aiken Eisho, Lord Que, Kasai's father's overlord. There are several different &quot;grades&quot; to this rank, depending on amount of land held, reputation, etc. For example: Kasai's father is a Lord, but he is very much the social inferior to his overlord, even though they are both Lords.<br /> - Retainers/Oathsworn<br /> <br /> **COMMONERS**<br /> -Peasants<br /> -Serfs<br /> -Slaves<br /> <br /> BY right of rulership, Lord Iysen can, quite literally, do whatever the hell she wants with any and all lands and titles within the domain of Xia. She can raise anyone to Lordship, of any rank, and inversely, strip anyone of land and titles. She doesn't really even need a reason, technically...... if it weren't for the fact that her sworn lords have just about as much military might as she does, and that doesn't all disappear if she removes titles and land-rights. She has to play a careful balancing game between her sworn lords, keeping them happy while also ensuring they don't get too powerful, since they might decide to try to become independent Lords in their own right, or (probably more likely), swear oaths of loyalty to other rival Great Lords.<br /> <br /> ...... you know, thinking about it, if she raised Kasai to Lordship, she would have someone who is basically loved by the commoners, and would be immensely personally loyal to Lord Iysen alone..... hmm. I like the implications!<br /> <br /> On the other hand, Lord Isyen is likely to view giving Kasai an estate a ....... waste, for a lack of a better term, of his talents and such. Not that she wouldn't, if that is what he (and we) want, but she would prefer something else, something that would keep him ....... a little closer, where he can be of use.<br /> <br /> As for marrying Mino or Airi ...... if it is any consolation (to them), we have already interacted with both of them to a greater extent than most noble-marriages do. Overwhelmingly most noble marriages are arranged years ahead of time (usually when one of the two are children), and the two partners meet for the first (and usually only, and even then in a heavily formalized fashion and setting) time when they get betrothed, (itself only a year or so before the actual wedding).<br /> <br /> A significant portion of noble girls get married to someone several years, if not a decade or more, years their senior, and the two partners actually liking each other isn't very high on the list of priorities. &quot;Getting along&quot;, yes, actual romantic feelings or even friendliness, no.<br /> <br /> Nobles don't marry for love...... which is why poetry about courtly and romantic love are so popular amongst the upper classes. Relatedly, Mino was considered to be uncommonly fortunate to have a fiancee that was so close in age, <u>and</u> one that reciprocated her feelings.<br /> <br /> As for adoption, technically, all it would mean is that we would now be an officially recognized member of the Shiba family, specifically as Lord Iysen's son. We would be her oldest child, but since we were adopted, we would also be the last in line to inherit anything..... unless she decided otherwise.<br /> <br /> Adoption into noble families isn't even that rare, being a semi-common method of rewarding and ensuring the loyalty of particularly-useful retainers or even commoners (although commoners are likely to be rewarded with retainer-status before getting adopted. It would have to be something <b><u><i>BIG</i></u></b> for a commoner to get adopted straightaway into a noble family.). Many ancient noble dynasties have entire cadet branches and minor lines made up of the descendants of adopted sons (and daughters). What would be &quot;weird&quot; about Lord Iysen adopting us isn't the actual adoption, but more the fact that it would come out of relative nowhere for the other noble families. Remember; nobody asides from Kasai and Lord Iysen know what <u>actually</u> happened, or what Kasai did for her. Most adoptions come about as a result of heroics on the battlefield, or some other noteworthy event.<br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11892,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","user":"Boston123","date":". 02-22-2017, 10:40 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-22T18:25:15-08:00","id":1053559,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/908-semicasual\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar908_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"908\" data-vbnamecard=\"908\">semicasual</a> <br /> <br /> With regards to the Oathsworn we dueled, he is currently occupying a nice cell in the city Gaol. Ordinarily, the killing of a peasant for being uppity wouldn't result in much punishment at all, but Blue Pines told Lord Iysen what happened (namely, that the guy attacked Kasai from behind with intent to kill, during a nonlethal duel) and what Kasai and the Oathsworn were arguing/dueling over.<br /> <br /> With this in mind, Lord Iysen leaned on her magistrate a bit, telling him to give the guy a &quot;nice&quot; cell, and to let him stew in his own sweat for a while. Since the two were fighting a nonlethal duel, attacking Kasai from behind could (and likely would) be considered assault, or even attempted murder, the penalty for which could be quite severe. Likely, the guy will just get off with a heavy fine and some social shunning from most social classes in Xia. The fine would likely be enough to force him to sell his weapons and armor, the sum of which would go to Kasai (after the levying of legal fees, one of Lord Isyen's many sources of income) as the injured party.<br /> <br /> With regards to pages: Most Oathsworn worth their salt (money-wise) will have several pages, and wealthy Oathsworn will have entire teams of them. The less work the Oathsworn has to do with regards to maintaining their own equipment, the better. Kasai, for example, still maintains his weapons and armor, and takes care of Xuansu when they aren't at a stable. Saiko is progressing, but she isn't quite ready yet to have those responsibilities.<br /> <br /> Most (wealthy) Oathsworn will have at least one page (preferably more), a designated groom for their horse(s), several armsmen, a secretary/steward, sometimes a cook, etc. Hence, the big tents and pavilions at the tournament. <br /> <br /> Yes, having such a retinue is expensive, but then again, most retainers/Oathsworn are going to come from comparatively-wealthy families. The actual cost of such a retinue is often defrayed by the sheer use one can find for the members: armsmen prevent attack on the roads and your stuff from being stolen, pages maintain arms, armor and equipment, secretaries/stewards arrange appointments, buy supplies and the like, etc etc etc.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-22T18:33:48-08:00","id":1053563,"title":"","content":"I say adoption with the noble elevation seems like a good request. It would put us in a position to take care of our servants while still working towards the public good. Also the other Oathsworn we've met have kinda been Oathsucks and a new role model in town could be a good thing, especially with the increadpsed influence that comes with the new family. I'm not at all against marriage and it would not be an unreasonable request from the lord as a way to make our request happen without ruffling too many feathers. My favorite characters have been attached to families, so much lovely drama and so much to lose <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11567-jairain","name":"Jairain","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11567_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"345"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-23T05:44:54-08:00","id":1053771,"title":"","content":"If our goal is to settle down in a stable situation, we should ask for adoption with noble elevation. I was going to go with Marriage and Job, but we want status too.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I write things.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3950-wonderandawe","name":"wonderandawe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3950_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5410"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-23T07:06:59-08:00","id":1053798,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Boston123</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1053541#post1053541\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A significant portion of noble girls get married to someone several years, if not a decade or more, years their senior, and the two partners actually liking each other isn't very high on the list of priorities. &quot;Getting along&quot;, yes, actual romantic feelings or even friendliness, no.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's what I was saying. Frankly, we haven't interacted with any of the Shiba girls to guess if we'd develop a tolerant respect or a deep mutual hatred during the first year of marriage. Gotta watch out for that. <br /> <br /> I'm sticking to my guns with &quot;Get a title, maybe some land&quot;, but I'm curious - if we <i>were</i> adopted into the family, would that preclude a marriage? <br /> <br /> Also, can homosexual marriages happen in this society?<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>On the frontier of the Wild South, there's only one woman with the grit to take on its most dangerous outlaws and bring them <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/984977-back-alive-or-maybe-dead-quest\" target=\"_blank\">Back Alive, or Maybe Dead.</a><br /> <br /> Avatar by <a href=\"http://puzzlinghappenstance.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">K.S. Brenowitz</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/908-semicasual","name":"semicasual","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar908_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2499"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-23T14:28:34-08:00","id":1054010,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>semicasual</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1053798#post1053798\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That's what I was saying. Frankly, we haven't interacted with any of the Shiba girls to guess if we'd develop a tolerant respect or a deep mutual hatred during the first year of marriage. Gotta watch out for that.<br /> <br /> I'm sticking to my guns with &quot;Get a title, maybe some land&quot;, but I'm curious - if we <i>were</i> adopted into the family, would that preclude a marriage?<br /> <br /> Also, can homosexual marriages happen in this society?</div> </div> </div> </div>Kasai's case is ..... different, because any marriage he got involved in won't have been arranged for years.<br /> <br /> If we were adopted into the Shiba family, <u>obviously</u> it would preclude a marriage to either of the two girls (legally, Kasai would be their brother), but in all likelihood it would, in turn, set up opportunities for marriages with other noble girls. Kasai would actually be a pretty hot ticket; he is the &quot;right age&quot; to be married ( with regards to nobility, most peasants get married in their mid-to-late 20s.), as well as having a good reputation and known skill at arms.<br /> <br /> Since he would be a member of the &quot;main&quot; (there are other lines of the Shiba clan, just 'lesser&quot;; cousins, cadet-houses and minor families) Shiba royal family, he could technically get engaged to any other noble girl of a family equal in status (around Xia, there is Yue {which is obviously a no-go], Qian, Yang, and Ji, all of which are Great Lords), or of lesser status ( Lord Iysen's three vassals, as well as lesser Lords in other domains), all of which have several fine young ladies of marrying age.<br /> <br /> Kasai won't have much influence over whom he weds, although Lord Iysen will take his feelings into account when choosing (much like she did with Mino and Haichi, which also wasn't arranged ahead of time, but quickly worked out in order to calm relations, to prevent a war........ <i><b>awkward</b></i>). The main driver of noble marriages is political benefits, followed by economic ones. Of course, it is always <u>preferable</u> if the bride and groom get along/like each other, but that is ultimately rather low on the list of priorities. The main line of thought is that the partners will gradually learn to live with each other, even if things start out rough or awkward. To counter that, the idea of concubines, mistresses and lovers-on-the-side are accepted among the nobility, as long as children are born on the &quot;right side of the bed&quot;, as it were (or, preferably, bastard children aren't born at all. Contraceptive drugs are known of), and the side-people aren't just paraded around in the public eye.<br /> <br /> Of course, marriages won't just pop out of the woodwork. First there is the courting process, where he basically goes trawling around for potential brides, getting wined and dined at various castles and manors, usually meeting multiple times. If he finds someone he likes / someone Lord Iysen can use politically, Lord Iysen will approach her guardian and discuss the possibility of betrothal. If that goes through, the two are considered engaged, and marriage usually follows within a year or so. The same thing would happen if Kasai were to court Mino or Airi. They would have time to actually communicate, and see if they get along with each other. The plus to Kasai marrying Mino/Airi is that Lord Iysen wouldn't have to pay a dowry, which is a <u>gigantic</u> plus to her mind. Mino's dowry was about 25,000 dinars, and Airi's is 15,000, for reference.<br /> <br /> With that in mind; <u>anything</u> and <u>everything</u> we choose as a reward for Kasai will offer Lord Iysen some benefit. Now, yes, she wants to reward Kasai for saving her life, but she is also highly pragmatic.<br /> <br /> If she were to adopt Kasai, she would gain the loyalty of a skilled and renowned Oathsworn warrior, loved and respected by the common-folk...... but she would also gain, in essence, a &quot;bargaining chip&quot; she could use to broker an alliance with other domains and families. Something which she rather badly both wants and needs at this point in time. He could (and probably should) expect to at least get betrothed within a couple months after he regains the ability to travel.<br /> <br /> As for homosexual marriages and relationships, they are accepted without complaint or even discussion, but among noble families it is all-but expected that you get married and continue the family line first before fooling around. For example, in one of the domains bordering Xia, a noble lady is in a open lesbian relationship with a commoner, and the main gossip about it is that it is a noble / commoner relationship, not a homosexual one. Of course, the noble was married to a man (who is dead), has had several children who are grown, so as far as noble society is concerned, her &quot;duty&quot; to her family is done.<br /> <br /> Commoners do what they want.<br /> <br /> If Kasai just became a retainer of Lord Iysen, the process would be similar (courting, betrothal, marry,), except he would be getting married to someone of lower rank (a landed retainer's daughter), and he would have to bargain and pay bride-price himself.<br /> <br /> Generally, the lower the social rank, the more personal feelings get involved in marriage, but politics and economics are at play at every social level, even commoners.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11892,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","user":"Boston123","date":". 02-23-2017, 05:49 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-23T16:36:26-08:00","id":1054080,"title":"","content":"If we were to take/accept the position of Captain of the Castle Guard, we would get a relatively decent salary (My version of Resources 2, equal to the yearly output of a Peasants farmstead), with room and board included (some comparatively nice quarters would be ours, with separate bedroom, office and &quot;main&quot; {part dining room, part general hall) room, with carpeting and sliding wood-and-paper doors, on the top floor of the &quot;Guard Barracks Tower&quot;). The position is also relatively prestigious, and comes with an armsman unaffiliated with the Guard, to serve as a bodyguard.<br /> <br /> In return, we would be in charge of the 25 or so permanent armsmen stationed at Teriake Castle ( organized in 3 squads of 6, each led by a Sergeant, with 4 extra crossbowmen. Each squad would take an 8-hour shift. 8 hours on, 8 hours &quot;on call&quot; (aka awake and around the barracks, probably training, but not walking a beat), and 8 hours off), schedule their shifts, manage their training, ensure proper coverage of angles-of-approach to the Castle, as well as maintain the integrity of the castle wall. We would get a key to all the locks (which have already been changed), and be responsible for making sure the gates, doors, battlements (parapet, crenelations, embrasures, murder-holes, etc) are all functional, and if they are not, we have the authority to contract out for repairs. We would also be responsible for stocking, maintaining and testing the equipment within the armories of the castle; not just the guards armory (which is part of the barracks), but the &quot;general&quot; armory, used for arming the inhabitants when the castle falls under siege. In these combined locations, we have about 700 spears, 500 helmets, 300 crossbows, several thousand-pounds-worth of fist-and-head-sized stones, several thousand crossbow bolts, as well as stores of worked stone, iron nails and timber for emergency repairs to the walls and gates. Anywhere from 1/3 to 1/2 of the weapons and armor really should be replaced, or not depended on in an actual fight.<br /> <br /> For this, and for any other purposes we deem fit, we would get a relatively substantial budget of about 3000 dinar a year. (Which isn't as much as it looks. The armsmen get 2 dinar a month, sergeants 3, for a total of about 612 dinar a year, just in personnel costs. Weapons and armor are expensive, and even preventative maintenance on locks and chains can go for a couple hundred dinar. )<br /> <br /> We would also be responsible for &quot;honor guards&quot; when various worthies come to visit, as well as being in charge of defense in case the castle itself comes under siege (technically, Lord Iysen is in charge, but the Captain of the Castle Guard would be right on her heels in any case). If that were to occur, we would collaborate with the Captain of the City Milita (who is supposed to retreat to the castle if the city cannot be held ....... which is likely. The walls are more to contain and control traffic, and allow for the collection of fees and tariffs, than to actually prevent an attacking force from entering the city. It is &quot;just&quot; a cut-stone single-thickness curtain wall, with timber walkabouts and bracing.) for defense. Teriake Castle has almost-infinite water from pressure-fed wells and cisterns inside the walls, and enough preserved food (dried grains and beans, pretty much. Not tasty, but you will live) for several hundred people for about a year, which pretty goddamn long in the Hundred Kingdoms.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11892,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","user":"Boston123","date":". 02-23-2017, 07:40 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-24T02:03:37-08:00","id":1054233,"title":"","content":"yeah...i'm going to need more time to figure this out. At least one additional read-through to confirm character goals and priorities.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Thoughts ripple out, birthing others","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1624-falleneco","name":"FallenEco","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1624_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1927"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-24T19:01:30-08:00","id":1054579,"title":"","content":"Would it be <b>presumptuous</b> to ask for adoption or marriage? The difference in social standing between Shiba and Harutane is large, plus the part where Kasai is a wolfman.<br /> <br /> Being taken as a retainer, vassal, or adopted son would be the best outcomes for Kasai, and his own retainers. A steady and honorable job, a home, a respected position with a lord and commoners who respect him, and actual prospects of eventual (or hasty) marriage, all great. And especially, a Lord who respects the ideals of the Oaths as Kasai sees them. Lord Iysen is the sort of Lord Kasai definitely would want to serve.<br /> <br /> Because Kasai wanted to get noticed by a Lord, and puts so much emphasis on his Oaths, serving or joining the Shiba house in some capacity is the way to go.<br /> <br /> Being Captain of the Guard is not really what Kasai seems to have in mind to do as an Oathsworn.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">She/Her. I am literal-minded and write literally. If I don't say something explicitly, please never assume I implied it. The only exception is if I try to make a joke.</span></span></span></div> <span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">My point of view may be different from yours but is equally valid.</span></span></span><br /> <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1x1ycoxJF0G9niMt5D3Pp7owZ7tw8AJvL?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">Exalted-cWoD-ArM url mega-library</a>. Exalted <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/663334-random-name-generator\" target=\"_blank\">name-generators</a>.","edited":{"userid":896,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/896-erinys","user":"Erinys","date":". 02-24-2017, 10:14 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/896-erinys","name":"Erinys","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar896_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4235"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-24T19:59:31-08:00","id":1054607,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Erinys</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1054579#post1054579\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Would it be <b>presumptuous</b> to ask for adoption or marriage? The difference in social standing between Shiba and Harutane is large, plus the part where Kasai is a wolfman.<br /> <br /> Being taken as a retainer, vassal, or adopted son would be the best outcomes for Kasai, and his own retainers. A steady and honorable job, a home, a respected position with a lord and commoners who respect him, and actual prospects of eventual (or hasty) marriage, all great. And especially, a Lord who respects the ideals of the Oaths as Kasai sees them. Lord Iysen is the sort of Lord Kasai definitely would want to serve.<br /> <br /> Because Kasai wanted to get noticed by a Lord, and puts so much emphasis on his Oaths, serving or joining the Shiba house in some capacity is the way to go.<br /> <br /> Being Captain of the Guard is not really what Kasai seems to have in mind to do as an Oathsworn.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> 1) Kasai is <u>entirely</u> human in appearance, asides from wolf-fur hair and eyes (as well as slightly darker skin tone compared to most nobility, but that is a general Eastern thing). He isn't a Beastman, and neither are Hill-Tribespeople. Sure, they have animal traits, but they are humans-with-animal-traits, not animals-that-look-like-people.<br /> <br /> 2) In most cases, it would be <u>hilariously</u> presumptuous to ask for either adoption or marriage. Kasai is the lowest of low nobility, while Lord Iysen is about as about as much of high nobility as one could get in the Hundred Kingdoms.<br /> <br /> In <u>this</u> case, however, it wouldn't be. Saving a Lords life is one of the actions one could reasonably expect to be adopted for. It would cause some social friction with Lord Iysen's vassals, but that is more because Kasai is a nobody than anything else. <br /> <br /> 3) If we get adopted, it doesn't mean we are going to get money. Sure, as one of Lord Iysen's children, we aren't going to worry about money..... but that is because &quot;Mom&quot; is rich as hell, not because we have silver falling out of our pockets. In almost all cases, if we wanted something, we would have to go ask her...... and with the war coming, she isn't necessarily going to say yes. In fact, unless it is absolutely necessary, she will probably say no. She has troops to levy, mercenaries to raise, and other logistical nightmares to take care of.<br /> <br /> 4) If we become a retainer, we will get money..... eventually. The number of harvests varies throughout the Hundred Kingdoms, dependent on local fertility (which, as it is the East, tends to be astronomically high, on par with the most fertile arable lands in reality), weather (which is severe as hell. Torrential downpours in spring, incredibly hot summers, more torrential downpours in fall, and snowstorms out the ass in winter. Most of this weather is due to the Continental climate, as well as the moisture that gets dropped onto the Hundred Kingdoms from the Thunder Plains to the north), as well as availability of arable land (most of the land in the Hundred Kingdoms is either hills, forest, or forested hills, with only the land right by rivers being quasi-flat.). Most farms in the Hundred Kingdoms average about 3 harvests a year, with some reaching 4, and yields tend to be pretty large. However, once we return to health, the growing/harvest season would be over, and we would have another 3-4 months to wait until the next one.<br /> <br /> 5) If we became Captain of the Guard, we would receive a steady income, and not have to worry about food or boarding costs, not just for us, but for Saiko, Blue Pines and Xuansu. The paycheck wouldn't be large, but it would be 100% profit.<br /> <br /> 6) Plus, we have to consider what kind of role we want Kasai to take in the upcoming war.<br /> <br /> a- If we are adopted into the family, we will almost certainly be playing a command role, and a significant one at that, since Kasai is the only one of Lord Iysen's children that has ever actually been in &quot;real&quot; combat. Sure, Kasai has read his copy of &quot;The Thousand Correct Actions of the Upright Soldier&quot;, and has lead the peasants of his father's village in drills, but ........ this would be his first &quot;mass&quot; battle, at least one where he was actually <u>in charge</u> of things. Could we be responsible for the lives of potentially hundreds of men?<br /> <br /> b- If we are accepted as a retainer, we will play a role, but not in command. Instead, we would serve as a cavalryman as part of Lord Iysen's retinue. So, no, the burden of command would not be on our shoulders, but on the other hand, could Kasai kill people he has no enmity for? Up to this point, Kasai has fought and killed people in self-defense, and to uphold his Oaths. Would he be comfortable charging down a peasant who, in all likelihood, doesn't even want to be there?<br /> <br /> c- If we take the position of Captain of the Guard, we would, in effect, get to sit the whole war out, as we would be in command of Teriake Castle during Lord Iysen's absence. That does mean we are in command if, <i>somehow</i> , Teriake Castle falls under siege. Of course, sieges are much less hectic than a pitched battle. Less opportunity to get hurt that way. Almost like a vacation, in fact.<br /> <br /> 7) Which segues into my next point: Basically, Kasai isn't going to be capable of doing much of <u>anything</u> until next spring. It is going to take him a couple months to be able to walk again, much less fight, and quite some time after that to be able to run, jump, ride a horse, etc.<br /> <br /> If Kasai was any older, or in less top-physical-shape, this would have been a career-ending injury. All the muscle in his outer thigh, from below his ass to above his knee, got cut clean-through, as well as getting a chipped femur,plus severe blood loss, <u>ON TOP</u> of having an almost-lethal infection develop. Luckily, Lord Iysen keeps a <u>very</u> skilled chirurgeon on staff, and they managed to save his life. Even still, he required several large blood transfusions. In game terms, he was (and still is, and will be for some time) Incapacitated. This injury will plague him for the rest of his life, and if it acts up in combat......<br /> <br /> So, when you choose a reward, keep Kasai's &quot;capabilities&quot; in mind.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-24T20:08:49-08:00","id":1054610,"title":"","content":"Kasai is not qualified to a command position, and he knows it.<br /> <br /> In the case of being a retainer, I thought his Lord would ensure he has things like food and shelter and clothes, in return for his services.<br /> <br /> <br /> I know Kasai looks almost entirely human, but I'm pretty sure there are humanoid &quot;beastfolk&quot; in addition to the ones who look like anthro animals. He looks different, people treat him different, and he even acts a tiny bit different. I think of the word in a broad sense, I guess.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">She/Her. I am literal-minded and write literally. If I don't say something explicitly, please never assume I implied it. The only exception is if I try to make a joke.</span></span></span></div> <span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">My point of view may be different from yours but is equally valid.</span></span></span><br /> <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1x1ycoxJF0G9niMt5D3Pp7owZ7tw8AJvL?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">Exalted-cWoD-ArM url mega-library</a>. Exalted <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/663334-random-name-generator\" target=\"_blank\">name-generators</a>.","edited":{"userid":896,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/896-erinys","user":"Erinys","date":". 02-25-2017, 12:16 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/896-erinys","name":"Erinys","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar896_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4235"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-24T20:35:06-08:00","id":1054614,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Erinys</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1054610#post1054610\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I thought being adopted included being like a retainer, in a sense. In both cases I thought his Lord would ensure he has things like food and shelter and clothes, in return for his services.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Nope. They are alike, but not the same<br /> <br /> Being a retainer is an entirely-military thing. Either he would be housed in Teriake Castle (which, to be honest, is the more likely thing to happen, as Lord Iysen wants to keep him around), or he would be enfeoffed an estate (again, honestly, comparatively unlikely, as 1) Even Lord Iysen doesn't have estates just laying around, she would have to give him land in the maginal hinterlands of her domain, where he could build his own manor, and 2) again, she would prefer if he stuck around.) [ Finally, accepting an estate would, in a way, essentially remove his Oaths from the &quot;equation&quot;, as it were, even more so than becoming Captain of the Guard]. If he gets housed in Teriake Castle, he would get room and board, but if he gets enfeoffed an estate, he is basically on his own.<br /> <br /> As a retainer, he could (and likely would) be asked (read: told) to lead Lord Iysens troops as a minor subofficer, and/or serving in her retinue. When not at war, he likely would be 1) training her household troops, 2) leading said troops against bandits, rebels, or anything else not requiring the whole army, or, 3) doing other tasks that couldn't be trusted to a lesser functionary, like the collection of taxes or the passing of messages.<br /> <br /> On the other hand, getting adopted would basically eliminate his free will in a sense, as Lord Iysen is going to have a great deal of control over his life from that point on. He wouldn't be able to just walk around and talk to people (at least, not without an armed guard), out of fear of assassins, <u>especially</u> after what just happened. He isn't <i>really</i> going to be able to choose whom, when or why he marries. When she goes to war, so does he, and he will almost - overwhelmingly-likely be in a command position.<br /> <br /> Once he gets married and has a kid (or several), Lord Iysen will relax, but until then , Kasai is going to be on a <b><i>very </i></b>tight leash. Rank has it's privileges, and rank has it's problems. A large &quot;part&quot; of Mino Shiba's problems with her mother is that she (Mino) essentially feels like she is living in a cage. A very nice gilded cage, but a cage nontheless.<br /> <br /> Meanwhile, becoming Captain of the Guard would arguably be the best of both worlds: he would be considered a retainer of the Lord, a high-ranking member of her inner-circle (at least when she is at home), while remaining <u>relatively</u> independent, and able to interact with the populace and influence social policy relatively easily. Marriage potential, on the other hand, is &quot;diminished&quot; (in the sense that Kasai wouldn't be considered &quot;worthy&quot; of marrying the cultural equivalent of royalty, but he could wed a retainer's daughter/sister/cousin, or even a wealthy/influential commoner if he wanted to defy social convention...... which he has already been doing for 15 or so years at this point), but he would be able to marry for love instead of duty. Kasai is young, and that appeals to him.<br /> <br /> Amusingly, I didn't even consider adoption in my first or even second flowchart of possibilities.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-25T11:44:31-08:00","id":1054895,"title":"","content":"I'd argue that a command position is actually ideal for Kasai.<br /> <br /> The one thing I keep noticing about this guy is that, badass warrior though he may be...he is blessed with real charisma. Bandits attacking a village? He motivates the villagers to take up arms and then tries to come up with a tactic to win the battle with the fewest possible losses. And recruits a new minion! Immaculate zealot abusing an innocent? He solves the problem with an inspiring and moralizing speech! And recruits a new minion. Gets sucked into politics? He makes a new powerful friend through his honesty and forthright integrity. Everything is on fire? He whips a mob into an effective firefighting squadron...and would have immediately gotten a new minion out of Whistle Boy if not for his injuries in the resulting duel. Just by being himself he's become a bit of a popular hero-of-the-people.<br /> <br /> Kasai is an intensely charismatic leader, and he genuinely seems to care for the people under his command. Sure, he may not be confident in his tactical experience...but it seems to me that he has the gift. Captain of the Guard would be a waste of his talents-it's a dead end. Kasai should go for a position where it's his job to recruit, train, and command men in the field. The retainer position seems like the best way to do that. Let's ask Lord Iysen if we can become one of her retainers housed here in Tenake Castle (why leave the place where you've already made your name to go have to make it anew?) and be granted a command. While Kasai recovers he can oversee the drilling and training of his forces. It gives him some freedom, it gives him ample chances to prove himself over and over again in the field (instead of being stuck in a defensive position as Guard Captain) and earn more favors from a Lord who has proven herself generous to those who get results...and it gives Kasai a chance to share his success by making his current minions into minor officers under his command. Also, if and when Lord Iysen wins her war or the political situation becomes stable, Kasai retains the opportunity to get enfeoffed in an estate later and build and rule and grow his own fief...but working as a retainer will help him develop a retinue of personally loyal badasses who will likely follow him there.<br /> <br /> That being said...as a wise old matchmaker I once knew used to say, it's just as easy to fall in love with someone rich as someone poor. Getting adopted lets you do all of the above with more clout...and a lack of freedom isn't necessarily a huge issue. Lord Iysen seems to be fair and honorable, I doubt serving her would be a huge pain for Kasai, and I suspect his forthrightness and integrity will earn him fast friends in the land of court politics. Everybody has a vested interest in keeping the honest predictable guy around; he's predictable.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>So I'm making <a href=\"http://godkickingboot.smackjeeves.com/\" target=\"_blank\">God-Kicking Boot</a>, an Exalted webcomic, now. Updates on Sundays. Full-color, mediocre but slowly improving art. It's a thing.<br /> <br /> The absence of a monument can, in its own way, be something of a monument also.</i> -Roger Zelazny","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/512-wise-old-guru","name":"Wise Old Guru","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar512_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1097"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-25T14:17:03-08:00","id":1054936,"title":"","content":"Not exactly good at politics. Could be an issue, if we were to be adopted or elevated to a higher station.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Thoughts ripple out, birthing others","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1624-falleneco","name":"FallenEco","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1624_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1927"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-25T15:59:55-08:00","id":1054973,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>FallenEco</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1054936#post1054936\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Not exactly good at politics. Could be an issue, if we were to be adopted or elevated to a higher station.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think he's better at politics than he thinks he is.<br /> <br /> Politics does not necessitate dishonesty or dishonorable behavior. In fact, much of politics is about maintaining the appearance of honesty and integrity. If I was Lord Iysen, having Kasai in my pocket as a political tool would be AMAZING! His mere presence shows that I'm above board...and he's such a bull-in-a-China-shop easy target that everyone is going to think he's either an amazing schemer and waste energy trying to figure him out or going to think he's a fool and discount him.<br /> <br /> Never underestimate the power of brute, blunt force. Even in politics. Perhaps especially in politics. People like an honorable strong man.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>So I'm making <a href=\"http://godkickingboot.smackjeeves.com/\" target=\"_blank\">God-Kicking Boot</a>, an Exalted webcomic, now. Updates on Sundays. Full-color, mediocre but slowly improving art. It's a thing.<br /> <br /> The absence of a monument can, in its own way, be something of a monument also.</i> -Roger Zelazny","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/512-wise-old-guru","name":"Wise Old Guru","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar512_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1097"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-25T17:39:39-08:00","id":1055011,"title":"","content":"So, while Kasai is recuperating, and while you lot of fine worthies debate his future, I am going to discuss several pertinent topics, namely those involved with war, politics, and the like<br /> <br /> 1) War in the Hundred Kingdoms<br /> <br /> The Hundred Kingdoms is a region beset by war and bloodshed ..... but often not in a fashion that many recognize.<br /> <br /> Of course, the Lords wage overt war, with banners snapping in the breeze, spearpoints glinting in the rising sun, and warhorses stamping in the chill morning air..... but many, if not most, Lords will actually seek to a<u>vert</u> such warfare. Waging war in such an overt fashion might be the stuff of stories, the origin of glory, and a chance to prove one's worth (at least for the nobility), but it is also <u>expensive as hell</u>. <br /> <br /> Overt war is expensive not in just silver, but in blood and sweat, the goodwill of the people, and in prestige. The armies of the Hundred Kingdoms are not professionals, instead being drawn from the agrarian classes. If said soldiers are kept away from their fields for too long (or do not return at all), then the harvest will be impacted, at the very least. Many a Lord has returned triumphant from the campaign, only to be beset by crops rotting in the field, famines and plagues, and a <u>very</u> unhappy, if not outright rebellious, populace. Generally, most field armies will only be kept in the field for the months of summer (aka Ascending, Resplendent and Descending Fire, for those using Shogunate terms), and even then, most Lords allow for several weeks before and after planting/harvesting season, to allow for their feudal levies to both plant and harvest the crops, and allow for troops to move and muster before moving out. With that in mind, &quot;campaign season&quot; generally only lasts about 60 or so days, with many troops outright deserting the army around that timeframe, in order to return home and tend to their farms so their families won't starve. Technically, it is within the Lords rights to punish the deserters, but most will not, understanding that the agriculture of the peasants is, by proxy, the fuel that feeds the machine of their domains, and most of these Lords will actively muster out their forces before the desertion takes place. And <u>nobody</u>, not even the Hill-Tribes, fights in the winter. <br /> <br /> Secondly, when feudal levies are away at war, the farms and villages of the Lords domain are therefore comparatively undefended, making them vulnerable to attack from hostile soldiers, bandits, or the ever-marauding Hill Tribes. Many a Lord has led their army to war, only to get crippled as another party reduces their countryside to waste through raiding. Therefore, long times in the field have to be planned and executed <u>very carefully</u>, and limited in scope, so that the villages are undefended for as little time as possible. Or, alternatively, a Lord will only muster a small section of their domain, leaving a number of levies behind to protect their assets. This, in turn, decreases the amount of available troops for field campaigning.<br /> <br /> Finally, a Lords prestige, and therefor, authority over their subjects, is highly dependent on how they &quot;handle&quot; war. Almost all Lords will personally lead their troops into battle (there are several Lords that appoint a &quot;Field General&quot; in their stead, but doing so will decrease their personal prestige immensely) , so therefore the outcome of every skirmish, battle and campaign directly affects how they are viewed, both by other nobles and by the commoners.<br /> <br /> Lead the troops in battle, and suffer a defeat? Good luck getting anyone to listen to you again. Lead the troops in battle, and neglect logistics, leading to not-adequate amounts of food, medicine and other supplies? The troops will be unhappy, and you will be known as a harsh taskmaster, regardless of what led to poor logistics. Levy troops for too long, leading to harvest-failures and famine at home? Almost certainly face sedition, probably from both commoners and nobility both.<br /> <br /> So, if outright war is avoided as much as possible, how then is war fought?<br /> <br /> As opposed to outright massed warfare, instead many (if not most) Lords will resort to raiding enemy villages (both for food, and to attack the morale of the enemy), assassinations and various methods of rabble-rousing, and &quot;trade warfare&quot;; heavy tariffs, stiff fees, and outright embargoes on products produced in other domains, as well as raiding enemy trade routes and shipping (most domains of any worth are located on the banks of the Red, Black and Blue Rivers, leading to heavy shipping traffic).<br /> <br /> In some cases, bandits (or &quot;bandits&quot;, to bring up plausible deniability) will be bribed or coerced into attacking or robbing outlying/weak villages in a rival domain...... and in many cases, as soon as war actually is declared, bandit activity will skyrocket, due to decreased levels of security and roving bands of deserted/ &quot;deserted&quot; troops flooding the countryside. Bandits are the fleas of the Hundred Kingdoms: everywhere, annoying, and impossible to get rid of. <br /> <br /> Lords might order their retainers/Oathsworn to lead rapid cavalry raids on villages, caravans or any other target of value, both to essentially steal whatever materiel the site is producing (and therefore, deny it to the enemy), kill any resistance (most Men-at-Arms are drawn from rural peasantry, and will fight to defend their homes), and attack the morale and fighting-will of the defender. This is the most common form &quot;actual warfare&quot; takes; a series of back-and-forth raids, until one or both sides can't take any more.<br /> <br /> If a rebellion, or even discontented mutterings, can be organized amongst the rural peasantry or the urban townsfolk, then the Lord holding the territory either 1- won't be able to bring as many troops to bear in the field, having to leave a number behind to pacify the populace, or 2- be unable to fight at all, because they either can't levy troops [they are the ones rebelling], or is busy fighting the rebellion, which contrary to &quot;popular belief&quot;, is probably going to be pretty badass, being partially made up of the same group Men-at-Arms are from.<br /> <br /> Assassinations could/would cripple the ability of a domain to fight off an invader, either killing or incapacitating the potential leader of the defending army. A less-capable leader would be forced into their place, diminishing the effectiveness of the force as a whole.<br /> <br /> 2) Strategy and tactics<br /> <br /> (copied partially from an earlier post)<br /> <br /> Since the Hundred Kingdoms is essentially, a feudal society, the feudal order to a large extent dictates how warfare is fought.<br /> <br /> The heart of most forces will be the Lord, their sworn bannerlords, and their associated retainers/Oathsworn. Said fighters will almost always be in heavy armor, equipped with relatively heavy weapons (mainly the sword, the spear, and the bow), and form the heavy shock infantry and, where terrain and strategy allows, both heavy melee cavalry and ranged skirmish cavalry.<br /> Now, the Oathsworn are <u>good</u>, skilled, disciplined and motivated, but they tend to be small in number, even in large, wealthy domains. So, Lords pad their forces with both semi-professional Men-at-arms and peasant levies.<br /> <br /> Now, in order to differentiate between the two, one must first understand the difference between a \u201cpeasant\u201d and a \u201cserf\u201d. The major difference between them is land ownership. Peasants own their own land, usually given to them by a Lord, while Serfs rent land from their overlord. While both are \u201ccommoners\u201d, Peasants tend to be wealthier (due to generally owning more and better farmland), better fed (so, bigger and stronger), more respected (peasants usually are offered positions of management and responsibility by Lords), and sometimes have more free time (since they are wealthier and own more land, they can generally afford a farmhand/servant or two, leading to them needing to do less work personally), leading to more time available for practicing at arms. And, since they tend to have more disposable income, they can actually afford arms and armor.<br /> <br /> Most Men-at-Arms will be equipped with a spear and shield, a halberd, or a longbow, plus a backup weapon and a quilted jacket and helmet for protection. The spear/shield and halberd is self-explanatory, but the longbow, itself a holdover from the Hill-Tribes (for whom it was, and still is, a favorite weapon), has long been a favorite of both Men-at-Arms and their Lords, to the point where the longbow is seen as a symbol for the Peasantry, much like the sword is the symbol of the Oathsworn.<br /> <br /> Much of the land in the Hundred Kingdoms is forested, hills, or forested hills. That means hunting is a common pastime, both for nobility and the peasants that own land. This leads to skill with the bow, but some Lords have taken it even farther, mandating that farmers that own specific values of land have to be proficient with certain weapons. Below a certain amount; Spear and shield. Above a certain amount; Spear, shield and longbow. Even the serfs are not exempt from this, and they have to be proficient with spear and crossbow, in case of invasion.<br /> <br /> Serfs, on the other hand, usually don\u2019t have the money to buy weapons, and their Lords usually wouldn\u2019t want them owning arms anyways. But, in order to comply with the letter of the law, they have to make sure their serfs are at least familiar with arms, in order to defend themselves and their Lords lands. Hence, they usually stockpile spears and, whenever economically possible, crossbows, in their manors, to rapidly equip serfs in case of invasion.<br /> <br /> When the call to war comes (usually given by a mounted retainer riding through every village, screaming their head off ) every <u>land-owner</u> will grab their weapons and armor, as well as however much food and various supplies (blankets, cooking utensils, etc) they can carry, and report to the local manor/castle (more on castles later). From that mustering point, the various groups will proceed to move to a more central point, for organization into regiments. <br /> <br /> A &quot;regiment&quot; isn't a permanent unit like we readers would think of today, instead being an ad-hoc thing formed out of a conglomeration of merged local musterings, for a single campaign or even battle. As smaller groups come together, they get clumped into larger units based on location first, and function second.<br /> <br /> For example, let us say that an average village supplies 3 spearmen, 1 longbowman and 1 halberdier. At the regional muster, the spearmen will be grouped together with other spearmen, to form spear-regiments. Same thing with the longbowman and the halberdier. Almost all regiments will be 100% one &quot;soldier type&quot;, meaning depending on what weapon you have could mean you get put into a different regiment from your comrades. However..... most regiments would be made up of fighters that largely know each other, either from previous musterings or from their daily life. This leads to increased morale and unit-cohesiveness..... until your friends and family start dying, that is.<br /> <br /> Each regiment will be under the command of either a retainer of the Lord of the region, or a chosen representative of the same. Sergeants and other subofficers will usually be elected by the soldiers, or rise by evidence of ability. However, the existence of sergeants and subofficers doesn't mean the regiment will be split up into subunits on the actual battlefield; most regiments fight as massed units of dozens of men. Instead, sergeants will take responsibility for subdivisions of soldiers when encamped, or (usually &quot;and&quot;) be responsible for discipline, the ordering of camp, distribution and implementation of work-details (the classical &quot;latrine duty&quot;, but also picket details as well as cooking and such) as well as the passing-along of battlefield orders (In a group of 60+ spearmen organized in rectilinear blocks, having the commander issue orders by voice alone would be ..... hard. Ergo, orders will be passed along via flags, trumpet-calls, or whistle-blasts, and the sergeants interpret those signals).<br /> <br /> Multiple regiments will be grouped together in battle-groups, and this is where combined arms comes into play. Overwhelmingly most battle-groups will be made up of all the regiments from a particular region, and be made up of Spear-regiments, Halberd-regiments, Longbow-regiments, Crossbow-regiments (levied serfs armed with crossbows, usually only used in the case of invasion, and skittish at the best of times), or Light Cavalry or Oathsworn/Retainer groups. Commanders of battle-groups (usually the Lord of a region, or the representative of the Lord of the domain) will usually be also in command of a reserve-force, itself usually the close Retainers of the Lord, for use when the situation calls for heavy shock cavalry. Battle-groups can be linked with other battle-groups, for use as a larger army, or be tasked with independent missions and objectives.<br /> <br /> As for how battles play out, that depends on terrain and the disposition of the battle-group/ overall army commander. Generally, the BG commander will sit back away from the actual fighting, observing how things are playing out. Orders are passed from the BG commander to regimental officers through runners, mounted messengers, or through flags.<br /> <br /> Actual tactics tend to be pretty simple: Men-at-arms are only semi-professional, and can't really be trusted to be able to understand or implement complex maneuvers. Most battles occur in the following fashion;<br /> <br /> 1) scouts and outriders of the armies come across each other. Generally, some skirmishing will occur, but most scouts will try to escape and get word back. Both sides will attempt to do this, while at the same time prevent the enemy from doing so.<br /> 2) Suitable ground for a massed battle will be chosen. The side that has the initiative will, <u>of goddamn course</u>, try to choose terrain that will give them as great an advantage as possible; on top of a slope, or with a flank covered by a swamp or stream, etc etc. Many battles actually get won without fighting, as many commanders will withdraw as opposed to attacking in such detrimental conditions. <br /> 3) Missile troops start long-range bombardment, while melee infantry get set up.<br /> 4) Melee infantry advance, as missile troops stop firing (unless the commander is an <u>asshole</u>), and start maneuvering behind/to the side of the melee regiments<br /> 5) Melee units clash, while missile troops and cavalry groups try to flank the two lines<br /> 6) Fighting continues until one side breaks, then everything is over in minutes.<br /> <br /> Generally, unless one has <u>at least</u> a degree of terrain neutrality (or one could force the opponent from their positive terrain, or take advantage of terrain yourself), most commanders will withdraw instead of fighting. This isn't a Total War videogame, and replenishing troops in the middle of a campaign is excruciatingly difficult at best of times. You, as a commander, will want to &quot;spend your currency&quot; were it is best used. Most campaigns revolve around maneuvering armies around each other, jockeying for position, and an army/battlegroup/regiment that gets outmaneuvered so will usually withdraw or surrender instead of fight. Only when an army/battlegroup/regiment is incapable of maneuvering (like, say, a battlegroup is defending a bridge that the attackers need to cross, or a battlegroup withdraws up to the margins of a river/swamp/impassible terrain, and can't escape), will <u>actual</u> battle take place. Skirmishes, scouting-in-force, yes, but actual set-piece battles are rare.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-25T18:17:54-08:00","id":1055021,"title":"","content":"3) Castles and fortifications.<br /> <br /> Despite the Hundred Kingdoms being famous for its examples of &quot;Vanehan style&quot; (basically.... look up Japanese Castles for ideas,), actual castles are pretty rare, being mainly owned by the Lords of domains (and only wealthy or powerful domains at that), or by special trusted vassals of said Lords. Owning a castle means you have a significant degree of social and military power, and an almost-impossible-to-take base of power, and most Lords 1) want that for themselves, and 2) Don't want their vassals to get big heads, or ideas of grandeur.<br /> <br /> For example: the domain of Xia is about the size of the state of Massachusetts ( ~ 30,000 square miles), and there are about 5 castles in the entire domain. Teriake, the castles held by her vassals, and a small one in the northern hills that was her wedding gift from her late father.<br /> <br /> Most vassal-Lords (and by proxy, landed retainers and Oathsworn) will instead built/inherit a fortified manor, aka a large house, almost always walled, on defensive terrain overlooking their village(s). Against the attack of angry peasants, or marauding bandits, the walls of a fortified manor-house are more than sufficient, while still being comfortable and some degree of opulent. Against <u>actual</u> military assault..... they don't really stand a chance. They don't have enough supplies stored, nor strong-enough defenses, to withstand an <u>actual</u> siege.<br /> <br /> Most fortified manors are going to be built in the siheyuan-style (<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Siheyuan\" target=\"_blank\">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Siheyuan</a>), of drystone, wood and bamboo, paper and rugs, reed mats and tile. The &quot;outside&quot; walls of the manor will be built of stone, with the interior walls being made of wood and bamboo and paper. The design is very versatile; being used as not only rural manor-houses, but as townhouses for wealthy commoners, hunting lodges, and stations/inns on postroads.<br /> <br /> Actual castles, on the other hand, are going to almost always be built on a hilltop, preferably overlooking a river-valley, mountain-pass, river-ford or some other terrain feature that restricts movement. Almost all castles are going to be built in a motte-and-bailey style <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Motte-and-bailey_castle\" target=\"_blank\">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Motte-and-bailey_castle</a>), with a central fortified keep surrounded by a number of baileys, or enclosed courtyards. Very simple castles will have a drystone-and-wood tower, with a single bailey surrounded by a wooden palisade and ditch, while larger castles will have multiple baileys (Teriake Castle has two), surrounded by fitted-stone curtain walls and supplanted by wooden watchtowers and hoardings, all watched over by the keep.<br /> <br /> A common saying amongst nobility is that &quot;high walls and towers impress the lowly&quot;, and castles serve as much as for intimidation as they do for actual defense. Many a peasant has quieted their grumbling at the sight of the frowning towers of their Lords keep looming overhead. To that end, relatively-simple and cheap castles (sometimes just a manor-house with a tower added) are sometimes built in areas recently conquered by a Lord, to better cow the populace and remind them who their Lord is.<br /> <br /> As above, many Lords retain the right to build castles, but there are sometimes trusted vassals to which this privilege is given. So-called &quot;Marcher Lords&quot;, named after the Hundred-Kingdoms term for a border region, are often given the &quot;right to crenellate&quot; by their liege, in order to deter or at least slow down an invasion. Marcher Lords also have other rights other vassals do not, such as the right to declare market-days and market-towns, the right to implement their own laws, grant charters and so on and so forth. Many Marcher Lords have as much, if not more, power as their liege-lords. Lord Isyen's three vassals are Marcher Lords, and she has to try to keep them all happy, lest they decide to declare themselves Lords of new domains (and kicking them out would be hard, as they have castles), or declare loyalty to her rival Great Lords.<br /> <br /> Since the Hundred Kingdoms have been around for a relatively long time in Creation (at least since the collapse of the Shogunate), there are plenty of ruins of manor-houses and even castles scattered around. Either destroyed in war, of because of neglect, these ruins are often used as bandit hideouts. Many of them are rumored to be haunted by the spirits of the dead. Some of them actually are.<br /> <br /> They provide shelter to travellers in the case of inclement weather, they provide havens for bandits (and increase the work required by Oathsworn retainers to burn them out), and, if taken up by an enterprising individual, could serve as a stable and effective power base once rebuilt...........","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11892,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","user":"Boston123","date":". 02-26-2017, 12:03 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-26T12:50:35-08:00","id":1055351,"title":"","content":"I actually like the idea of retainer as well. Allows us to play a number of roles. I assume we'll be probably used as an envoy at some point to the hill tribes and being the head of the castle guard would make it difficult for us to get away. We could essentially be used as Iysen's man for odd wetwork which would lead to a lot of growth for Kasai and for his retainers. It seems retainer is the most flexible story wise and doesn't limit us and allows us to keep growing Kasai's skills and rank. I say retainer personally.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8897-joshopotamus","name":"joshopotamus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8897_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"97"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-26T15:17:10-08:00","id":1055408,"title":"","content":"So far (by my count, and I could always be reading into something I shouldn't), it looks to be 4-2 in favor of Retainer. I will leave it open until tomorrow or so, to give people who still wanted some time to think about it.<br /> <br /> For the rest...... a lot could hinge on how we interact with the coming war. If we play our cards right, and do good things in service to Lord Iysen, being enfeoffed an estate, getting entitled, or even getting extended an offer of adoption could all be possible.<br /> <br /> For those in favor of retainership, would you be in favor of being given command of Lord Iysens already-existing levies, or attempting to raise one (or several) of our own from the inhabitants of Teriake Castle-Town?<br /> <br /> If we take command of an already-existing regiment, basically all the &quot;work&quot; (training, logistics, subofficers, etc) will already be done, and we will effectively just jump in command and go.<br /> <br /> If we try to raise our own forces, we will literally have to do most of the work in staffing, equipping, drilling and maintaining whatever forces we do raise. Overwhelmingly most townsfolk lack weapons or military drill, except for those few with militia-experience, who already have a cushy job and won't want to go play soldier, unless the pay is <u>really</u> good. Yep, if we raise regiments of townsfolk, we are going to have to pay them, as they are <i>legally</i> serfs, the majority of them renting apartments as opposed to owning land, and as such are exempt from feudal military obligation. <br /> <br /> On the other hand, if we use our rapport with the townsfolk right, and play to both our strengths (known to be fair and have their best interests at heart) and to their emotions (many, if not most, of the town are going to be pretty pissed at getting attacked by the agents of Yue), we could very well come up with multiple regiments of eager-to-serve recruits, that while lacking in skill, have the heart to persevere. Oh, and here is the kicker: since the regiments you raise won't be farmers, they won't <u>necessarily</u> have to leave once the campaign season is over, allowing Xia to have at least a token force in the field.<br /> <br /> In order to get enough money to raise, equip and maintain regiments, we will quite possibly have to needle Lord Isyen for money. This is something we could do with our boon.<br /> <br /> In a crunch-sense, both actions: using our rapport with the townsfolk and asking for money would be an example of &quot;burning&quot; dots in the Influence Merit. Becoming a retainer of Lord Iysen would, in turn, represent the &quot;Ally/Mentor&quot; merit.<br /> <br /> In my games, in order to have dots of Command (I use a modified 2E system), one either has to have n+2 dots in either Resources (to represent you paying for the force from your own income), or in Influence (to represent you using, well, personal influence to either get people to join, or to have someone else pay for the force).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-26T15:52:37-08:00","id":1055424,"title":"","content":"I still say adoption but it all looks fun.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11567-jairain","name":"Jairain","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11567_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"345"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-03-01T16:26:01-08:00","id":1056836,"title":"","content":"Since nothing has been posted since the 26th, I am in favor of Retainer. Though I think Kasai won't be comfortable leading raids on villages.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">She/Her. I am literal-minded and write literally. If I don't say something explicitly, please never assume I implied it. The only exception is if I try to make a joke.</span></span></span></div> <span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">My point of view may be different from yours but is equally valid.</span></span></span><br /> <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1x1ycoxJF0G9niMt5D3Pp7owZ7tw8AJvL?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">Exalted-cWoD-ArM url mega-library</a>. Exalted <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/663334-random-name-generator\" target=\"_blank\">name-generators</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/896-erinys","name":"Erinys","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar896_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4235"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-03-02T14:13:50-08:00","id":1057246,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/896-erinys\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar896_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"896\" data-vbnamecard=\"896\">Erinys</a> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8897-joshopotamus\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar8897_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"8897\" data-vbnamecard=\"8897\">joshopotamus</a> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3950-wonderandawe\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar3950_2.gif');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"3950\" data-vbnamecard=\"3950\">wonderandawe</a> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1624-falleneco\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar1624_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"1624\" data-vbnamecard=\"1624\">FallenEco</a><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11567-jairain\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar11567_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"11567\" data-vbnamecard=\"11567\">Jairain</a> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/512-wise-old-guru\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar512_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"512\" data-vbnamecard=\"512\">Wise Old Guru</a> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4018-l-het-esh\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar4018_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"4018\" data-vbnamecard=\"4018\">L'het'esh</a> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/908-semicasual\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar908_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"908\" data-vbnamecard=\"908\">semicasual</a><br /> So, it appears your three options are as follows:<br /> <br /> 1) Become a personal retainer of Lord Iysen.<br /> 2) Request a command position, in one of Lord Iysens already formed regiments<br /> 3) Request leave to recruit your own forces from the population of Teriake Castle-town.<br /> <br /> As you go up in number, the level of personal responsibility increases.<br /> <br /> If Kasai becomes a personal retainer of lord Iysen, he will just pretty much live in her castle, undertake certain small-scale tasks (wipe out bandits, pass along messages to her vassals, etc), and in times of war, he would serve as a member of her Oathsworn reserve, a heavy calvary force. He <u>might</u> be asked (read: ordered, but politely) to do things he might find distasteful, like raiding villages and caravans, procuring food for the army and suchlike, but Lord Iysen is aware of his feelings on the matter, and if there is something else he could do, or someone else she could send, she would <u>try</u> to avoid having him do such things. It all depends on the situation.<br /> <br /> If Kasai gets thrown into a command position, then things get ...... well, not automatically complicated, but &quot;different&quot;. If it is an already-formed regiment, he would just have to listen to Lord Iysens orders on the battlefield and implement them. He would have to sit in on command meetings, help plan strategy, so on and so forth. All the rest of the logistics of running the regiment would largely already be handled, by the sergeants of the regiment and by Lord Isyens quarter, drill and camp-masters.<br /> <br /> If he <u>recruited</u> his own forces, then he would effectively be thrown into the deep end, so to speak. He would have to recruit the soldiers, train them, equip them, promote sergeants (veterans from prior battles are good for this) and officers (probably train them as well), provide food and supplies, not to mention come up with overall campaign strategies and battle tactics.<br /> <br /> On the other hand, as you go up, the possible awards for good and loyal service increases as well.<br /> <br /> If we remain as a personal vassal of Lord Iysen, we will pretty much get room and board for free (pretty decent quarters and food, for what it's worth), as well as a pick of any spoils of war we come across. If we do something <u>really special</u> in combat, like say, capture an enemy Lord, or defend Lord Iysen's life (again) in the thick of battle, we could be rewarded with valuable gear (horses, fine weapons and armor, etc), or entitlements / positions of responsibility (Magistrate, Captains of X, etc), or land<br /> <br /> If we lead a regiment in combat, a common and traditional reward for <u>successful</u> officers is to be given lands, usually part of which they conquer. Usually after the war is over and done.<br /> <br /> If we lead <u>our own</u> forces, independently of any command, the sky is the effective limit..... based on what we do, of course. If we were to, say, take and hold one of the castles of a Yue vassal, we could almost be guarenteed to be enfeoffed into that position ourselves. If we were to (and this is a stretch, mind you), <i>besiege and capture</i> Miyakawa Castle (the personal holdings of Lord Yue), and therefore end the war in one fell swoop, well..... we could effectively declare ourselves Lord of Yue if we so felt.<br /> <br /> Not that that is likely, or even plausible, but ..... hell, this is Exalted. Mortals can do some pretty awesome shit too. For example: in my Hundred Kingdoms campaign, one of my players basically went all Qin Shi Huang and united most of the Hundred Kingdoms, declaring themselves Emperor and planning on taking out Vaneha before logistics got in the way.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11892,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","user":"Boston123","date":". 03-02-2017, 05:37 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-03-02T16:20:23-08:00","id":1057315,"title":"","content":"Choice 1 makes the most sense for Lord Iysen, she has somebody popular to deal with townfolk and could serve as messenger to the hill tribes.<br /> Choice 3 would be the hardest for somebody with no army experience. I expect this to win the vote, as it presents the most chances for moving around and having adventures.<br /> <br /> I vote (1) personal retainer, mostly on the hope of seeing ambassador Kasai.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Like my Avatar? Courtesy of<span style=\"color:#0000FF\"> Jen</span>! : <span style=\"font-size:12px\">Anybody want their characters to be experimented on ? </span>post 98<br /> <i>An Exalt is never unarmed.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4018-l-het-esh","name":"L'het'esh","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4018_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"521"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-03-02T16:40:43-08:00","id":1057332,"title":"","content":"Eh, personal retainer.<br /> <br /> ...does that come with a title? Something equivalent to &quot;Baron&quot; maybe?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>On the frontier of the Wild South, there's only one woman with the grit to take on its most dangerous outlaws and bring them <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/984977-back-alive-or-maybe-dead-quest\" target=\"_blank\">Back Alive, or Maybe Dead.</a><br /> <br /> Avatar by <a href=\"http://puzzlinghappenstance.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">K.S. Brenowitz</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/908-semicasual","name":"semicasual","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar908_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2499"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-03-02T16:58:02-08:00","id":1057339,"title":"","content":"One so we can play politics.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I write things.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3950-wonderandawe","name":"wonderandawe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3950_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5410"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-03-02T17:43:53-08:00","id":1057360,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>semicasual</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1057332#post1057332\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Eh, personal retainer.<br /> <br /> ...does that come with a title? Something equivalent to &quot;Baron&quot; maybe?</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> No. We are the rough equivalent of unlanded knights or samurai, not yet titled nobility. A baron, count, duke would all fall under the title of &quot;Lord&quot;, which is why there is different graduations of the rank. I originally planned on using said titles, but I felt that it would be too confusing. In hindsight, that might have been incorrect.<br /> <br /> Lord Iysen is the equivalent of a King/Queen. She is the monarch of a major domain<br /> Her 3 Marcher-Lord vassals are the equivalent of Dukes. Powerful lords, ranking right below the monarch.<br /> Any landed nobles beneath them (or, alternatively, sworn to Lord Iysen), would be the equivalent of Barons/Counts. Kasai's father is one of these, but an absurdly poor and marginalized one.<br /> At the bottom of the totem pole, landed retainers with a single village, are the equivalent of landed knights or ji-samurai. We, as retainers, are equivalent in social rank, but we don't yet have any land.<br /> <br /> The system is several different levels of mixed-up. It doesn't go: Monarch &gt; Duke &gt; Baron &gt; Retainer. Lord Iysen has three &quot;Dukes&quot; in her service, but she also has &quot;Barons&quot; and &quot;landed retainers&quot;.<br /> <br /> Functionally, we would be one of the mooks that make up the bulk of this unit.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://www.honga.net/totalwar/shogun2/unit.php?l=en&amp;v=shogun2&amp;f=oda&amp;u=Gen_Taisho\" target=\"_blank\">http://www.honga.net/totalwar/shogun...a&amp;u=Gen_Taisho</a><br /> <br /> Or,<br /> <br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"https://www.quora.com/In-the-samurai-culture-what-exactly-does-the-term-retainer-refer-to\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/google-material/image_placeholder.svg\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://www.quora.com/In-the-samurai-culture-what-exactly-does-the-term-retainer-refer-to\" target=\"_blank\"> </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://www.quora.com/In-the-samurai-culture-what-exactly-does-the-term-retainer-refer-to\" target=\"_blank\"> https://www.quora.com/In-the-samurai-culture-what-exactly-does-the-term-retainer-refer-to </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> </div> </div> </div><br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Retainer\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/static/images/icons/enwiki-25.svg\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Retainer\" target=\"_blank\"> Retainer - Wikipedia </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Retainer\" target=\"_blank\"> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Retainer </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> Many, if not most, noble families don't have either enough money or enough land to divide it equally between children, and their children's children, and so on and so forth. In response, many second and third (and later) noble children either:<br /> <br /> 1) Go become a monk at a monastery/abbey (which are pretty much the same thing). Noble children, who can almost-always read and write, have plenty of chances to rise far (as far as a mortal can rise, pretty much) in the Immaculate Faith, and many abbots/abbesses are influential, powerful, wealthy, etc. <br /> 2) Suck it up, and learn a trade.<br /> 3) Compete in the tournament circuit<br /> 4) Try and become a retainer to a landed noble.<br /> <br /> After a war is won, a Lord will often repossess the estates and manors held by the vassals of the former lord, and give them out as gifts to their retainers and followers for their (hopefully continued) loyalty, and to put the costs of maintaining a fully-armed heavy cavalryman on the land as opposed to their wallet. Or, alternatively, they will grant these retainers land in the hinterlands of their domains, in order to prompt the settlement/taming of underutilized land, or to defend against the Hill-Tribes and bandits. Marcher-Lords are but the largest and most obvious of this last group.<br /> <br /> This can, and often does, backfire. Most Liege-Lords aren't going to be paying <u>that</u> much attention to what one of their retainers is doing in the backwoods, and a crafty landed retainer can (and they often do) effectively hold more land that what they were officially enfeoffed, by attracting the enterprising young sons and daughters of commoners, who themselves might lack for inheritance opportunities in the <i>hilariously</i>-crowded lowland valleys. The commoners settle in the lands around the retainers estate, giving them more money than what they should &quot;officially&quot; have, in turn giving the retainer more power, who can then attract more commoners, and build more infrastructure, again and again, until the retainer (or, more likely, the retainers children, grandchildren or what-have-you), are effectively Liege-Lords in their own right. Some, if not many, of these not! Lords eventually end up building castles, and attracting retainers and vassals of their own. And, really, there is very little the original Liege-Lord can do about it, asides from either:<br /> <br /> 1) Accepting them as a peer, or<br /> 2) Besieging their castle, in hostile terrain<br /> <br /> ..... Most Liege-Lords do the first. If not because the second is expensive as hell, then because by that point they might very well be intermarried to the now-high nobility family, and actually view them as a peer.<br /> <br /> In fact, several of the (smaller) domains in the Hundred Kingdoms are the cultural offspring of these gifted estates waxing in power as the original liege wanes. <br /> <br /> Plus, Lord Iysen can make any retainer any &quot;degree&quot; of vassal she wants. They don't have to start out as a &quot;mere&quot; landed retainer. If she values their service, she could technically bump them all the way up to &quot;Duke&quot; status. Kinda like how Oda Nobunaga promoted Totoyomi Hideyoshi.<br /> <br /> Finally, overwhelmingly most of the land held by Liege-Lords is going to be relatively undersettled, when compared to the lands right by the Red, Black and Blue Rivers. The lands by the rivers are the flattest, and the most agriculturally productive (therefore, the wealthiest), and most of the population is going to be clustered there. The hinterlands Xia are almost empty by comparison, as they abut the territory of the Dumonipocasset Confederation, and therefore the Hill-Tribes <i>de facto</i> own it. There are still villages there, but they are few and far between.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-03-02T22:22:00-08:00","id":1057462,"title":"","content":"2 has potential. But I think 3 is better, for who were are and for story.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Thoughts ripple out, birthing others","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1624-falleneco","name":"FallenEco","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1624_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1927"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-03-03T16:20:04-08:00","id":1057814,"title":"","content":"So, based on the votes I have seen so far, things seem pretty slanted towards Kasai accepting a position as one of Lord Iysen's personal retainers. I will wait until tomorrow, to see if several other people decide to chime in.<br /> <br /> A few people brought up the idea of Kasai becoming an envoy/ambassador of sorts. I like it, and if &quot;personal retainer&quot; is chosen, that is probably what Kasai will be doing. Admittedly, that is what Lord Iysen wanted, and arguably the second reason she wanted to keep Kasai around. His relationship with his grandfather, aka <u>the</u> bugaboo for most of the western Hundred Kingdoms, and his potential use as an ambassador to the Hill-Tribes is what originally piqued her interest in him, for lack of a better term.<br /> <br /> Originally, his potential as ambassador was idle musings, but now with war with Yue looming on the horizon, it is all-but <u>necessary</u>. With regards to what I wrote concerning war above, once open warfare starts in earnest, and armsmen leave their farms for the field of battle, the heartlands of Xia will be basically wide-open, something the Hill-Tribes are going to take notice of. Lord Isyen cannot afford, both in manpower and in materiel, to wage a war on two fronts, and if the Hill-Tribes can be appeased, or even potentially allied with, that is one less enemy she has to worry about.<br /> <br /> Of course, the Hill-Tribes aren't just going to back off for just <i>anything, </i>even if Kasai leverages his influence with his grandfather as much as possible. Even the comparatively-friendly Hill-Tribes (Aka the Nemasket, the tribe Kasai and his grandfather are members of, and the lesser affiliated tribes, the Assawompsset and the Nasketucket) are going to want to at least &quot;break even&quot; with regard to lucre/loot/tribute, and the already-mostly-hostile Tribes (the Neponset, the Assonet, and the Mattepoisett) are going to need some <u>heavy </u> bribing and ego-stroking. Concessions will have to be made, and any decision could possibly throw the Lowlander/ Hill-Tribe relationship into an upheaval.<br /> <br /> Plus, serving as an envoy would actually give us a reason to use all that camping equipment Kasai bought a couple days back","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11892,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","user":"Boston123","date":". 03-03-2017, 07:25 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-03-04T00:24:16-08:00","id":1057962,"title":"","content":"Well if we let slip that the fields of Yue are going to be undefended and that Lord Isyen has no problem with them conducting raids there (and might be willing to aid/overlook passage), we might be able to get away with less generous bribes. Maybe.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Thoughts ripple out, birthing others","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1624-falleneco","name":"FallenEco","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1624_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1927"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-03-04T18:31:02-08:00","id":1058234,"title":"","content":"It appears that the majority of you chose \u201cpersonal retainer\u201d, and several brought up the idea of playing politics or serving as an envoy to the Hill-Tribes.<br /> <br /> --------<br /> <br /> <br /> You stare in blank shock; not even in your wildest dreams have you ever thought this would occur. One of the most powerful rulers in the Hundred Kingdoms, <u>asking</u> you what you wanted out of life! Preposterous! Yet\u2026. Here you were.<br /> <br /> A subdued cough startles you from your reverie. \u201cIf you do not want to decide now, and want some time to think, I could always come back later.\u201d<br /> <br /> Shaking your head, \u201cNo\u2026. my decision was made long ago. My whole reason for traveling to Teriake was to compete in your tournament, with the hope that I would win a position in your entourage. In doing so, I could both serve you and fulfill the intent of my Oaths at the same time, and there is nothing I would want more.\u201d<br /> <br /> An eyebrow shoots up, \u201cNothing? Be careful, I might yet hold you to that remark.\u201d She then relaxes, smiling softly, \u201cKasai, it would be my honor, and my privilege, to accept you into my household. To be honest, I was considering doing so even before you saved my life; to have such a well-regarded retainer attached to my family could only increase my personal and familial honor and reputation. In light of recent events, it would be amiss if I didn\u2019t offer you such a reward.\u201d<br /> <br /> She pauses, taking a deep breath,\u201cAnd, now that you are a servant of mine, I can use that reputation of yours more than ever. I was planning on asking you to serve as an envoy to the Hill-Tribes, to put the relationship between you and your grandfather to good use\u2026 \u201c, she holds up a hand, to stymie your interruption, \u201c Yes, I am aware of how your culture works, but listen to me first.\u201d<br /> \u201cXia cannot afford to fight a war on two fronts, and with war with Yue coming, and the accompanying muster of my forces, that is <u>exactly</u> what will end up happening. The Hill-Tribes will see the heartland of Xia undefended, and there will be nothing I can do to prevent them from raiding and burning the center of my domain. Who better could I send to broker a deal to prevent that; one of my other retainers, full of pomp and sneering contempt, or you, one of their blood, one who respects their culture, who speaks their language, and, most of all, is known as a man of integrity and righteousness?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cServe me in this fashion, secure at least a temporary peace with the Hill-Tribes, and you will have a place of honor by my side. With war looming on the horizon, and your Oaths in mind, you could very well rise far in my service.\u201d She shrugs, \u201cFor all I know, if things go off as planned, I might need some new vassals to govern territory, or seek to adopt a worthy and valiant retainer worthy of recognition.\u201d<br /> <br /> Stirring, \u201c I\u2026 understand the implications, My Lord. What is it you desire of me in the present?<br /> <br /> She stands, laughing, \u201c Kasai, you <u>can\u2019t even walk</u>, on top of almost-literally just waking up from a coma. For right now, you need to heal. Eat, sleep, and regain your strength. My chirugeon will almost-certainly be waiting outside to look you over. When you are capable of functioning again, come talk to me. These will be your quarters; when you are ready, talk to a servant and they will bring in your gear and more suitable furnishings.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cYes, my Lord. Thank you for the help, and the opportunity to serve you.\u201d<br /> <br /> Lord Iysen nods her head in acceptance, \u201cNo, Kasai Harutane, <i>thank you</i>. You are crucial to my future plans, so take the time you need to recover.\u201d<br /> <br /> As she leaves, in sweeps Saiko, Blue Pines, and a thin, bespectacled man stinking of unguents and medications. The second he sees you sitting up, he clucks his tongue in disapproval.<br /> \u201c 10 minutes after waking up from an infection-induced coma, you are up and about?! No, none of that! I didn\u2019t put 21 hours straight worth of work into keeping you alive, only for you to try and tough it out! Lie back down and throw the blanket back, let me take a look at you\u2026\u201d<br /> <br /> With only your underlinens left to preserve your modesty, the doctor peels back the wrapped bandages in order to expose the wound on your leg. In spite of knowing it is \u201cthere\u201d, you suck in a gasp when the edges of the cut get exposed. The wound stings upon contact with air, and it appears at first glance that your outer thigh is a mass of fine-stitches and foul-smelling pine tar. To your surprise, the man grunts in approval.<br /> <br /> \u201cGood! At least you didn\u2019t tear any stitches out when you decided to go crawling across the floor. And no lingering traces of blood infection, excellent.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cYou consider that <u>good</u>?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cCompared to what it could be, yes. You will walk again\u2026. Eventually. If you were any older, or the wound any more severe, I probably would have had to take the leg off. Even still, there will be quite a lot of recuperating in your future! I will prescribe you a regimen of tonics, a specialized diet, and eventually a series of exercises to stretch out the muscles, and I do not want to hear of any shirking, no matter how mundane it gets. Firstly, we will start with the\u2026\u201d<br /> <br /> As the doctor drones on and on about powders and poultices, Saiko kneels down next to you, grasping at the futon with white knuckles. Leaning close,<br /> <br /> \u201cKasai\u2026. I\u2019m glad you are alive, but you are an <u>idiot</u>! \u201d<br /> <br /> You groan good-humoredly, \u201cYes, thank you for reminding me. Remind me to never turn my back on an opponent in live combat again.\u201d<br /> <br /> She seethes, \u201cDo you know how long I waited for you at the gate?! I didn\u2019t know if you two were alive or dead, just that you headed to the tournament without me! No idea what is going on, no idea what time you planned on being back, just that you were in the area that looked like Hell on earth! And, on top of that, what do I see of you the first chance I get? You passed out and covered in blood, thrown in the back of a milita wagon like the town drunk sleeping it off!\u201d<br /> <br /> Bemused, you shift your attention to your armswoman, who shrugs, \u201cI had to get you out of there <i>somehow</i>, and I certainly wasn\u2019t going to carry you out. Once the fire burned out, the militia swept in to evacuate us, and I got you in the first ride. Oh, and I knocked some of the bastard\u2019s teeth out, if that makes you feel any better.\u201d<br /> <br /> You laugh, \u201cAt least I got out of there in one piece. That is all that matters.\u201d A pause, then, \u201c I suppose you might as well start bringing our equipment and such up into this room, we are going to be here for a while.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c\u2026.. How long, Kasai? We heard you talking to the Lord, what happened?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cPermanently. I have been accepted into her service.\u201d<br /> <br /> Such a revelation is enough to make Saiko sit back, and for the notably stone-faced Blue Pines to react with a smile.<br /> <br /> \u201cReally? What does that involve?\u201d, all anger forgotten.<br /> <br /> \u201c\u2026 I will largely do what you are already doing, Saiko, just on a larger scale. Support her in battle, serve her in peace, maintain her holdings, that sort of thing. Although, the Lord is almost certainly going to ask me, and therefore you, to go to the Hill-Tribes as an envoy. Just as a forewarning.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cUh\u2026 when?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cProbably not for many months. Our friend the doctor here has final say on that.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cDamn straight I do! You, young man, are not going to be getting up from that mattress for at least a month. No riding, no climbing, and <u>especially</u> no fighting, not until the winter at least. You have to let the muscle heal.\u201d<br /> <br /> You roll your eyes, \u201c Of course. In the meantime, how about some food? What exactly have I been living on for the last 2 weeks, water?<br /> <br /> \u201cNo, broth and tea. Both easily digestible.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cThat must be why I feel like a newly-born puppy. Saiko, can you send a servant to go and get something a little more substantial? Something with actual body? Have them bring some up for you two as well.\u201d<br /> <br /> With that, Saiko sticks her head out the door, in order to buttonhole a servant, Blue Pines heads out in order to supervise the moving of equipment and furniture, leaving the doctor to poke and prod at your wound with a copper rod, until he too takes his leave, parting with a packet of powder and some words of advice.<br /> <br /> There, once again, you are alone with your thoughts, and can actually sit back and think over what has transpired over the last few days (er, weeks). So far, you have made a not-insubtantial amount of money, gained a follower, cemented your reputation through the upholdings of your Oaths, prevented an assassination and coup, and, last but certainly not least, have successfully become a personal retainer to one of the more powerful Lords in the Hundred Kingdoms.<br /> <br /> In return, all you have to do is try and broker peace between two peoples that have largely hated each others guts for the last 500+ years. Should be easy, right?<br /> <br /> \u2026 Great<br /> <br /> -end of Chapter 2<br /> <br /> \u2026.ugh. I definitely had some sort of ending-fatigue in this one. Thank God it was only a little end-of-chapter dialogue and exposition, not actual plot.<br /> However, I must say that I am excited to have reached this part of the story. During my actual gameplay campaign, this is when the story started taking off.<br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-03-04T21:22:01-08:00","id":1058288,"title":"","content":"The Hill-Tribes of the Hundred Kingdoms<br /> <br /> The Hill-Tribes. Boogeymen of backwoods tales meant to frighten children into good behavior. Blamed whenever a tool or animal goes missing, or a daughter runs off. Faraway watchers on hilltop bluffs, leaders of savage raids and robberies, great craftsmen, in wool and silver and steel. The great noble families of the Red, Black and Blue river-valleys would tell you the Hill-Tribes are barbarians in every sense of the word, lacking true culture, great works of art, with only the desire to pillage and plunder left to keep them alive in their cold and forlorn mountain hovels. Cursed by the gods with animalistic features, to better suit their souls, the Hill-Tribes dwell on the fringes of settled society, looking on their social and cultural betters with hate in their hearts and murder in their eyes. To be captured by them is a fate worse than death.<br /> <br /> The truth is very different.<br /> <br /> While the Hill-Tribes are indeed a warlike and roughshod people, they are also a noble one, if not in the style that the Vaneha-aping Lowlanders would understand or accept. Such differing views, largely based on the circumstances of their mountain homes, is a large part of why the Hill-Tribes and the Lowland peoples do not get along.<br /> <br /> -History:<br /> <br /> The ancestors of the modern Hill-Tribes were a conglomeration of refugees, cast adrift on the turbulent sea that was the collapsing Shogunate in the face of the Great Contagion, the incroaching Wyld, and the never-ceasing civil wars. Already marginalized due to the high level of mutations brought about by the exposure to Wyld-energies, the nascent Hill-tribes pushed east, into the largely depopulated River Province. Once there (and not to marginalize their travels, that is a saga worth telling in and of itself), they found the valley of the Black River and its two tributaries, the Red and Blue Rivers, good farmland even in the worst of times. So, they settled it, taming the Wyld-contaminated fields and forests, driving out tainted beasts and building new farms, then villages. Life, so far, was pretty good, and continued as such for several generations.<br /> <br /> Until the Vanehani attacked.<br /> <br /> The first Sword-Prince of Vaneha, Kendorei, after crushing the various clans that made up his formerly-fractious empire, looked north with a gimlet eye, hungry for more lands to conquer (and, more pragmatically, give his massive pool of warriors something to do). The peoples of the Black River Valley were weak, and despised by all around, and the lands they held were rich and bountiful. Why not turn to them with fire and sword? And so, Kendorei ordered a fleet be built, so his warriors could be ferried across the Yellow River to glory.<br /> To their surprise, they got their asses handed to them.<br /> <br /> After years of fighting off Wyld-tainted beasts, and amongst themselves, the proto-Hill-Tribes were <u>very</u> skilled in combat, and after searching so long for a home, were not about to just roll over and give it up. And so, they resisted, using the woods and hills of their home to lead hit and run attacks on the slower Vanehani armies. More than one army, thousands of sword-bearers in the finest Vaneha tradition, vanished into the northern hills, never to be seen again.<br /> <br /> This enraged Kendorei, unused as he was to actually being beaten, and in his fury, he led the next army personally, taking with him even the dregs of the Vanehani countrysides, peasants unfit to look upon a blade, much less hold it. This time, the fleet so covered the Yellow River, it looked like the water had suddenly transformed into land, with trees below and clouds above. Sword-Prince Kendorei led his forces into battle, marching straight into the hills, regardless of how many losses he took, trapping the Hill-Tribesmen where they could no longer run.<br /> <br /> In response, the Tribes united under a charismatic warleader, Tsenamatoric, and marched as a unified army into the field. Under the banner of the Red Boar, the spears gleamed, the carnyxes moaned, and the ground shook.<br /> <br /> Upon meeting Kendorei in open battle, they were crushed utterly. Kendorei stomped all those that resisted, until the only way ragged remnants of the culture could survive was to flee to the northern mountains. The ones left behind were made slaves of the invaders.<br /> <br /> Kendorei, satisfied with <u>finally</u> crushing the barbarians, made to return home, only to be told that while he was away, a civil war broke out in Vaneha, bringing an end to his dynasty. In grief and rage, the Sword-Prince took to the sea with but a handful of chosen warriors, leaving the rest of his army behind to safeguard his newly-conquered land. He was never seen again, not in Vaneha at least.<br /> <br /> The Vanehani left behind basically looked at each other, shrugged their shoulders, and settled the land they conquered, carving out domains as soon as they could. The officers and clanleaders left behind by Kendorei became, in time, the great Lords of the Hundred Kingdoms.<br /> <br /> Meanwhile, the remnants of the Tribes scattered by Kendorei regrouped in the northern mountains, a bleak and inhospitable land when compared to what they left behind. There, they persevere to this very day, clinging to the mountainside and looking down on the fertile lowlands with envy and bitterness.<br /> <br /> -Mutations and physical characteristics<br /> <br /> Every single Hill-Tribesperson has some sort of animalistic Wyld-mutation, usually in the eyes, face, hair or teeth. They do not usually interfere with day-to-day life, but they also do not generally confer much in the way of an advantage, either. Traits can be passed down and inherited, and many Tribepeople exhibit traits from several different animals as a result of intermarriage.<br /> <br /> The more Hill-Tribe blood one has, the stronger the mutations are. For example, Kasai \u201cjust\u201d has wolf-fur-hair and wolf-colored (read: yellow) eyes, and otherwise is indistinguishable from a \u201cnormal\u201d human, while a full-blooded Hill-Tribesman (his Grandfather, for example) has the same wolf-fur-hair and wolf-eyes, but also has hawk-feathers interspersed in his hair, elongated canines, and mottling around his eyes and across his cheeks, like that of a deer.<br /> <br /> Asides from the mutations, Hill-Tribespeople largely look rather similar to other Eastern tribal groups; skin tones in various shades of brown, tan or dark beige, with long, straight hair in black, brown or red. Any \u201cforeign\u201d phenotypes they picked up on their ancestral migration to the Hundred Kingdoms has long since been extinguished.<br /> <br /> -Settlements<br /> <br /> The settlements of the Hill-Tribes are far-flung, separated from each other by many miles of cliffs, crags and mountain valleys. This essentially means that each settlement must be as self-sufficient as possible, with smiths and farmers, weavers and doctors skilled enough to not require help. This also means that, instead of leading to bonds of brotherhood, each settlement will largely view other settlements as competition; for farmland, for pastureland, or for hunting grounds.<br /> <br /> Each settlement is comprised of a low stone-and-log palisade surrounding a collection of roundhouses, themselves built from drystone, wattle-and-daub and bark, all in turn built around a central longhouse, where the chief lives and community meetings take place.<br /> <br /> These settlements, in turn, are surrounded by densely-packed farming terraces, pastures for sheep and goats, and the bleak, wind-swept and crag-faced mountains and hills<br /> <br /> -Social Organization.<br /> <br /> The Hill-Tribes, unsurprisingly, are grouped into many dozens of tribes, themselves each made up of clans and connected families.<br /> <br /> Each clan is made up of inter-related tribespeople, and may be spread across several villages through a tribes territory. Men marry into a womans clan, and any children born are considered to be a member of the mother\u2019s clan. Marriage is arranged by clan elders, but is almost always for love, the partners bringing up the idea of marriage first.<br /> <br /> Each clan sends its oldest man and woman to tribal conclaves, where these representatives debate matters pertaining to the tribe as a whole. If the tribe is allied to others, like in the Dumoniapoccasset Confederation, each tribe will send two representatives to that conclave.<br /> <br /> Each clan also has a chief, \u201celected\u201d in a fashion by the warriors of the clan, to lead them in war and to interact with outsiders. Each chief is \u201celected\u201d not through voting, but through sheer fiat of warrior support and loyalty. If a warrior is known to be brave in battle, generous with spoils, and an effective warleader, then will attract the service of lesser warriors. Get enough followers, and you can declare yourself chief. Or, the elders of a tribe will appoint a noteworthy warrior. Same thing.<br /> <br /> -Arts and Crafts<br /> <br /> The Hill-Tribes effectively have to \u201cmake do\u201d with the poorest, least-productive lands in the Hundred Kingdoms, leading to a very thrifty culture of artisans. \u201cWaste not, want not\u201d is a common saying.<br /> <br /> Due to this frugality, the Hill-Tribes are somewhat paradoxically known as some of the best weavers and metalworkers in the region, and especially in regards to gold and silver-working, eclipse even the Vanehani. Hill-Tribe woolens fetch a hefty price in Lowlander markets, due to their high fine thread-work, sturdy weave and intricate patterns, and Hill-Tribe bronze-casting is of very high-quality as well. Their pendants and brooches, torcs and armrings are in high demand, even among high Lowlander nobility, and Hill-Tribe gold and silver filigree is used to decorate Lowlander sword-hilts, porcelainware, and other luxury goods.<br /> <br /> Despite (or, perhaps, because of) their bleak homelands, the Hill-Tribes are also skilled workers of stone and wood, leading to drystone buildings cunningly fitted together, so no mountain wind can pass through, and wooden beams with no nails supporting steep roofs and the worst of winter snows. In addition to Vanehani-influences, the style of the Hill-Tribes can still be seen in the architectural style of the lowlanders, with the steep roofs and curving stone footings.<br /> <br /> -Food and clothing.<br /> <br /> In their mountain-fastness, there isn\u2019t much flat land to grow crops, so the Hill-Tribes instead laboriously carve terraces into the sides of mountains, fertilizing them with manure and compost. This method of agriculture was stolen, amusingly enough, from the Vanehani.<br /> <br /> On these terraces, the Tribes grow corn and beans, squashes and pumpkins, potatoes and cabbage. Even with heavy terracing, there isn\u2019t enough arable land to support the people by itself, so each village is also heavily dependent on animal husbandry; usually sheep and goats, which are nimble enough to scramble over rock faces, all while providing wool and milk, on top of being small and requiring less fodder than cattle, and on hunting and foraging. Every single Hill-Tribe roundhouse will have at least one spear, brace of javelins or a bow, and someone skilled in its use.<br /> <br /> Clothing is suited for the rough vastness of the local terrain, and is in turn made from local materials. Wool and linen, leather and birchbark. Men and women both wear trousers reaching to the ankle, with leather shoes in the summer, with slip-on wooden clog-sandal-things (like <i>geta</i> or <i>patten</i>) in rain or winter. Undershirts and jackets are both long-sleeved, with high collars to turn aside cutting wind, and vests and coats made of sheepskins or leather/furs are also worn. <br /> <br /> This focus on hunting also heavily influences the Hill-Tribe religion.<br /> -Religion<br /> <br /> The Tribes do not follow the Immaculate Faith, instead keeping to their own gods: that of hill and stream, pool and grove, animal and plant. The \u201cThree Sisters\u201d, patron deities of corn, beans and pumpkin, are heavily worshipped, as well as the animal totems of deer and sheep, goat and wolf, bear and boar. The wolf, the bear, and the boar are widely revered as symbols and patrons of the hunter/warrior.<br /> <br /> However, the one deity the Hill-Tribes love and worship over all others, is Luna, the goddess of the moon, the wild places and the forsaken. Every hunter asks the Moon for a successful hunt before stalking into the trees, every warrior asks the Moon to watch over them as they pass under her gaze, and every maiden (and quite a few young men, too) ask the Moon to bless them with fertility.<br /> <br /> The Moon is a goddess of fertility, of war and harvest, of <i>nature</i>, red in tooth and claw, beautiful in her wild, unrestrained glory, and many, if not most, Hill-Tribespeople view Her as the patron of their people.<br /> <br /> The largest celebration of the Moon comes in the form of the Feast of the Moon, a tribe-wide ritual/celebration held every year in the middle of autumn (or, alternatively, Resplendent Water), after the harvest is done and the leaves begin to change. There, singing and dancing takes place, and hunters brave the wilds in order to bring back worthy sacrifices. The culmination of the ritual, however, occurs during the full moon, when a chosen warrior dons the skin and antlers of a great white stag, and after quaffing a sacred mixture, charges into the wilds armed with only a flint knife in order to find and kill another white hart. Some say Luna Herself takes the form of this deer, and leads the warrior on a merry chase. If the warrior is worthy, she \u201cembraces\u201d [I am using the classical term, here. I shouldn\u2019t have to spell it out]/sacrifices Herself (maybe both. Luna is <i>weird</i>) to the warrior, who is said to be forever changed by the experience. Regardless, if the hunter is successful, he ritually consumes the heart and liver of the animal, and hauls the carcass back to the ritual site, where the blood is then sprinkled into a sacred well, ensuring the lands are as fertile as they can be for the following year.<br /> <br /> Very few outsiders have been allowed to attend a Feast of the Moon, and none to date have been asked to participate in the central ritual.<br /> <br /> Warfare:<br /> <br /> Contrary to popular Lowlander opinion, the Hill-Tribes are not a bunch of frothing-at-the-mouth barbarian berserker morons, knowing only the mass-charge, nor are they cowardly sneak-thieves, preferring to avoid open confrontation in favor of shooting arrows from the forests and hills.<br /> <br /> Hill-Tribe warfare is based on personal bravery and skill, not on discipline and following orders. This is something they have in similar with the Oathsworn retainers of the Lowlanders, and in fact, the cultural idea of the Oathsworn (aka a heavily-armed follower) is partly-descended from the Hill-Tribe idea of the Chosen warrior (more on that later).<br /> <br /> In spite of this, Hill-Tribe warriors and warchiefs are aware of the value of \u201cproper\u201d formations and strategies\u2026.. it is just that they don\u2019t get to, nor have to, use them all that much. It isn\u2019t like the Lowlanders are going to invade <i>en masse</i>, nor can the other tribes send out warparties in enough numbers to warrant massing warriors all that often.<br /> <br /> Hill-Tribe warriors come in two \u201cclasses\u201d; young warriors, eager for status and glory, but poor in sense and equipment, and older warriors, skilled and proven. These older, more experienced warriors will usually take charge of a group of younger warriors, and use them to harry and harass the foe, throwing javelins, slinging rocks and loosing arrows either from a position of cover/ambush, or from the flanks of a Chosen warband.<br /> <br /> The warchief of a Hill-Tribe warparty will be composed of, obviously, the chief as well as their chosen (hence the name) warriors, hand-picked for their valor and skill. Such Chosen warriors are often older, married and by Hill-Tribe standards, wealthy. Equipped with shields, spears, short swords and axes, as well as helmets and armor of bronze or iron (including maille, \u201cchainmail\u201d to the uneducated, of which Kasai is equipped), they form the backbone of the warparty, engaging enemy forces in various formations, from rectilinear blocks to the famed \u201cBoar Snout\u201d, a large wedge for breaking open enemy formations.<br /> <br /> The infamous raids for which the Hill-Tribes are so well known and hated for, are usually the work of the younger warriors, eager as they are for wealth and status. A little-known fact among the Lowlanders is that the Hill-Tribes actually raid each other almost infinitely more often than they do the Lowlanders.<br /> <br /> -Relations with outside parties.<br /> <br /> Generally, one would be safe in saying that the Hill-Tribes hate the Lowlanders, the Lowlanders hate the Hill-Tribes, etc etc etc, but the reality is, of course, far more complex.<br /> Much of the peasantry of the Hundred Kingdoms is of partial (if long diluted, to the point of no longer expressing Wuld-mutations) Hill-Tribe descent, with the proportion of descent increasing as one gets closer to their lands. This is one reason why lowlander nobility tends to look down on the peasantry. In addition, in lands close to Hill-Tribe lands, trade, travel and even intermarriage is comparatively common.<br /> <br /> On the other hand, Hill-Tribespeople <u>do indeed</u> get discriminated against in the lowlands, mainly in the river-bottoms far away from their homelands, though generally not to the point where they will get lynched by a mob. Catcalls, insults and thrown garbage is about the extent of the abuse. They are also not prohibited from traveling in most domains, meaning you can find traders traveling around occasionally. However, since the majority of Hill-Tribespeople don\u2019t speak Riverspeak, they are few and far between.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-03-10T17:25:44-08:00","id":1061080,"title":"","content":"Just as an aside, I recently watched this movie, called <i>War of the Arrows</i>, set in Korea during Qing China. It is a pretty good representation of what I imagine the Hundred Kingdoms looking like, as well as how Oathsworn are equipped. Replace that funky-looking studded-leather thing with proper mail and lamellar, and you will be right on the money.<br /> <br /> I haven't been able to find an &quot;official&quot; English dub or sub, so you are gonna have to search Youtube or your favorite movie-site if you want to watch it. I strongly recommend it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11892,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","user":"Boston123","date":". 03-10-2017, 08:27 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-03-10T20:49:52-08:00","id":1061126,"title":"","content":"Chapter 3: The War of Broken Spears<br /> <br /> 2nd Descending Water, RY 769<br /> <br /> (In hindsight, letting you all know that the first chapter started in RY 768 probably would have helped. Anyhoo, it has been a year.)<br /> <br /> ----------------------<br /> \u201cAre you <i>sure</i> you want to do this, Kasai?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cSaiko, if I wasn\u2019t sure, I wouldn\u2019t have brought it up. Besides,\u201d you smirk, \u201cWhy the sudden concern? Worried that, even with this gimpy leg of mine, I\u2019m still going to put you on the floor?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cNot likely. I <i>have</i> been improving, you know. But\u2026.. <i>yeah</i>, it is your leg I am worried about. This will be the first time you actually fight on it, and what happens if it gets hurt again?\u201d<br /> <br /> You shrug, acknowledging her point but dismissing it all the same. \u201c Saiko, I\u2019ve been walking around under my own power for 5 months now, jogging and riding for 2. Hell, just yesterday I jogged around the entire castle-town wall, then rode through the countryside for a few hours! I <i>think</i> I will be fine, but if I never push it, I will never know, will I?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cHe\u2019s got a point, girl.\u201d Chimes in Blue Pines, lagging behind with sparring equipment. She grins, \u201cBesides, you can\u2019t back out now, I\u2019ve got money on this match.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cThat doesn\u2019t seem like a fair bet, Blue Pines. Kinda like taking sweets from a child, if you ask me.\u201d, you frown.<br /> <br /> \u201cHey, the castle-guards have never seen you fight, and what they don\u2019t know earns me some coppers, right?\u201d<br /> <br /> Saiko huffs, rolling her eyes, \u201cNice to know you have such faith in me, Blue. I hope you lose your bet, in the nicest possible way. Anyways\u2026\u2026 I just thought it was something worth mentioning, Kasai.\u201d<br /> <br /> She does have a point. Despite your assertions, 3 of those 5 months you walked on your own were with the aid of either crutches or a cane, and even with the recent addition of a cloth wrapping-brace, you still can\u2019t sit in a saddle (or sit at all, if your leg is bent) for much longer than an hour before the thigh-muscle starts to cramp and spasm. The cloth-brace <i>does</i> help with walking and even running, to the point where you almost have no limp to speak of, more of a swagger (much aided with the addition of a stylish cane to your wardrobe) but after sitting legs-bent for any appreciable period, it is all you can do to stand. There has to be <i>something</i> else you can do, because if you can\u2019t ride effectively, you can\u2019t fight effectively\u2026\u2026<br /> <br /> After a few more moments of banter, your group reaches the sparring field of Teriake Castle, set up against the wall in the upper bailey. There, in a sand-pit, you and Saiko stand opposite each other, the better to tighten ties and straps on padded vests and caps. From racks aside the pit, you select weapons; practice-spears, with shafts and \u201cblades\u201d of bamboo, to prevent injury more serious than bruising.<br /> <br /> Turning to your page, the two of you together work through several beginning techniques; slow measured strikes, parries and ripostes, more for warming-up and stretching than for anything else. When you are all limbered up, you drop into a low-guard, spear held pointed down toward her feet. She, in turn, adopts a high-guard, almost certainly because she is more proficient with the glaive and used to slashing attacks.<br /> <br /> The two of you watch each other for a moment, then with a cry, Saiko tries to take the initiative, leaping across the sand and bringing her spear down in a vertical arc. In response, you pivot to the side, noting with satisfaction that the leg-binding tightens and holds the limb upright. Whipping your spear down, to knock aside her blow, then up, to her throat, you \u201cwin\u201d the first point of the match.<br /> <br /> \u201cGood strike, but too aggressive. Such a move can only work when you have overwhelming initiative in the fight. Take control of me, or of my weapon, preferably both, <i>then </i>go for the kill.\u201d<br /> <br /> She nods, then sets up in a more defensive posture. This time, you play the aggressor, dropping down into a low stab to the stomach. She parries this, sliding the shafts of the spears along each other, and then shoving with all her might. Thrown off balance, you stumble back (not very far, you still outweigh her by a large magnitude), only to feel the point of her spear pressed to your side.<br /> \u201c\u2026..better. Again!\u201d<br /> <br /> The two of you trade blows back and forth, gradually working up to full battle-tempo, at which point Saiko is at a severe disadvantage. You are bigger, stronger, and even considering your handicap, faster than she is, and far more comfortable with mixing together different strikes and methods of combat. This comes to bite you in the ass, however, when you stiff-arm the girl in the chest and she flails around with her spear, trying to catch her balance, only to catch you upside your wounded thigh.<br /> <br /> Even through the layers of padding and cloth wrappings, the impact causes the muscle to cramp, dropping you to a knee with a growl and bit-off curse. Saiko yelps, dropping her spear and bringing her hands to her mouth in shock.<br /> <br /> \u201cOh gods! Are you alrigh..\u201d<br /> <br /> Her cry is cut short by the business end of your spear being driven into her stomach, driving the wind from her lungs and doubling her over. A strike to the side of the knee bowls her over into the sand, and a third strike punches into the sand next to her face.<br /> <br /> \u201cNever, <u>ever</u> turn your attention from an enemy in battle until they are dead! Don\u2019t I serve as a good example for that? Doing that in a <i>real</i> fight will get you killed!\u201d<br /> <br /> She lays silent on the sand for a moment, then, \u201cI\u2019m sorry, Kasai. I didn\u2019t expect to hit you.\u201d<br /> <br /> Still kneeling, \u201c\u2026..It\u2019s ok, but next time, don\u2019t try to catch your balance like that. As you can see, it didn\u2019t work. Just step back instead. It is immediately effective, and it takes you out of the range of most weapons.\u201d<br /> <br /> She nods, painfully climbing to her feet, \u201cDo you want a hand up?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cNo\u2026\u2026 just give me a minute.\u201d<br /> <br /> After a few moments of punching at your thigh, the cramp subsides, and you are able to clamber to your feet, leaning heavily on the spearshaft for support. Perturbed, you worry about what this means for future combat. Skilled opponents will almost-certainly take advantage of any obvious advantage, and it seems like any sort of impact will cause the muscle to cramp up. Maybe you should invest in some heavy padding, or some solid plates for the thighs, as opposed to your current quasi-flexible mail. Some considerations for tomorrow, perhaps?<br /> <br /> Getting back to the sparring, you and Saiko get back to full speed, where you proceed to give far more knocks than you get, although the few you received were well-given. She, like she said, has certainly improved since she joined you a year ago, although you unashamedly attribute that to your expert teaching as opposed to any real talent on her part. After all, she has only been practicing under arms for a short time, while you have been using weapons since you were seven, or 15 years. Still, for a former peasant girl, she gives a good show, and you will have plenty of bruises to find later on.<br /> <br /> She will have far more, however, especially since you are knocking her around so much. It is after a particularly-nasty takedown, ending with her faceplanted in the sand, that you get interrupted by the Lord Isyen and her daughter, Airi, who peers wide-eyed at Saiko, laid out on the ground<br /> <br /> \u201cMost impressive, Kasai. I have heard of your skill with the spear in passing, but this is the first time I have seen it.\u201d<br /> <br /> Standing straight and bowing at the waist, \u201cThank you, my Lord. I must admit, and please excuse my crudity, but I am <i>pretty damn good</i>, if I may say so myself.\u201d<br /> <br /> She laughs, \u201cQuite! I never was skilled with the spear; I prefer the bow myself. However, it is good to see you up and about, after so long convalescing.\u201d<br /> <br /> You nod, \u201cIndeed, my Lord. It is quite nice to be able to walk about, now without even the aid of crutches. Such a pain!\u201d<br /> <br /> She grins, clearly remembering the sorry sight you made stumping around Teriake Castle, cursing with every jarring step. The smile fades, and her countenance takes a turn to the serious.<br /> <br /> \u2018Are you\u2026\u2026 combat-ready?\u201d<br /> <br /> You wince,\u201d\u2026.. mostly, Lord. I still have pain and cramps every so often when in the saddle, but they are not serious. I can ride, if that is what you are asking.\u201d<br /> <br /> She relaxes, \u201c Good. If you will, let your page spar with my daughter, and walk with me a while. I have questions to ask of you.\u201d<br /> <br /> Silently, you make to follow her, up the stairs that lead to the wall-walk around the bailey. For a few minutes, you make your way around the walls, to the section that overlooks the river, where she leans against the parapet.<br /> <br /> She sighs, then, \u201cDo you understand how lucky we were last summer, never getting attacked by the force of Yue?\u201d<br /> <br /> You shrug,\u201dI guess. By the time Haichi Matsu probably got home from his failed plot, the campaign season was almost half-over. I\u2019m not really that surprised, to be honest.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cEven still, his father could have ordered some sort of raid or other minor attack. Haichi wasn\u2019t <i>that</i> skilled at warfare, so he wasn\u2019t required on the front. The fact that there wasn\u2019t even a token raid on a caravan or border village makes me concerned.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cWhy so?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cIt suggests two possibilities. 1: Yue requires a lot of time to organize an offensive, meaning he has more soldiers than I do. Or, 2: he was out making alliances against me. Of the two, I find the second far more likely. Xia and Yue are very similar in size, economy and terrain; he can\u2019t support <i>that</i> many more peasants than I can. \u201c<br /> <br /> She is quiet for a moment, \u201cI don\u2019t know if you know much about local history, but Xia is \u2026.. not well liked by its neighbors, for admittedly-good reasons. Firstly, the domain of Xia was brought about in a rebellion against Zhou, a state that once controlled the entire Red River valley. Zhou no longer exists, as a result of Xia and other states rebelling, but there are \u2018descendants\u201d of Zhou scattered around. The closest are the domain of Ji, a couple miles away from the northern border of Xia. There is a traditional grudge between our ruling families, nothing <i>serious</i>, but that is something Yue could build off of. Then there are several smaller domains scattered around, that could resent Xia\u2019s power and influence. Some of them were ruled over by Xia in the past, as recently as my grandfather\u2019s time. They might join Yue, in order to snatch a piece of the pie for themselves.We will have enough trouble fighting off Yue by itself as it is, much less with other domains joining in.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cWhat are you suggesting, my Lord?\u201d<br /> <br /> She looks out over the river, \u201cTell me, Kasai, when is a tiger most dangerous; when it is in front of you, snarling and full of fury, or when it is concealed in the forest, stalking you,?\u201d<br /> \u201c\u2026.when it is the forest, obviously.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c How about if you can\u2019t tell if there is one tiger or ten? What then do you do?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cBring friends, and stand back-to-back\u201d.<br /> <br /> \u201c<i>That</i> is what I mean to do. Be the attacking tiger, <i>and</i> bring friends.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cI see. I take it that you want me to go to the Hill-Tribes, then.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cYes, but\u2026..\u201d She sighs again, \u201c I have to admit that I have no idea what or how to communicate with the Hill-Tribes. I don\u2019t know much of anything about their culture, their social customs, or how they would even view a diplomatic effort. As you know, we tend to treat them\u2026.. well, pretty gods-dammed awfully. Hell, I don\u2019t even know what they <u>want</u>. Do they even use money? Would they accept it, or view it as a bribe, or just throw it out in disgust?<br /> <br /> \u201cWhat they want is largely irrelevant, because I am willing to pay whatever cost it takes to secure their neutrality, at the very least. I <u>need</u> them to leave Xia along when we go to war, or we won\u2019t have a domain to return to!\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cTell me, Kasai, and be honest, what will it take to stay the wrath of the Hill-Tribes?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c\u2026. You say you will give <u>anything</u>?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cYes, whatever it takes. Is that a problem?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c..No, you just might not like the cost. If something costs the full price of the thing you want to save, will you really have that thing at the end of the day?\u201d<br /> <br /> She frowns, \u201cwhat do you mean?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c\u2026\u2026. You say you know your history? Well, how much do you know of <u>theirs</u>?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cNot much. Only that the Vanehani drove them from these lands in righteous conquest several hundred years ago.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cThat is a \u2026\u2026 extremely sanitized and biased version of events, as far as my grandfather told me. They wouldn\u2019t describe the conquest as very \u201crighteous\u201d, what with the enslaving of entire villages, every man, woman and child. The desecration of their religious sites, the places where they buried their dead. So on and so forth, until the only way they could survive as a culture was to flee to the hills. And, even then, the oldest of storytellers told me that the current Hill-Tribe society is but a pale reflection of what it once was.\u201d<br /> <br /> She blinks, \u201cOkay, what does that have to do with what I could give them?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cPoint being, they want their land back. All of it. From the Red River to the Blue, from the Black to the Yellow. Every rock, field and stream.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cWell, I can\u2019t very well give that to them, can I?\u201d she starts, flustered.<br /> <br /> You nod, \u201cExactly. So, all that remains is what it would take for them to be\u2026\u2026 well, satisfied, enough to refrain from raiding your lands. That, in and of itself, would be incredibly expensive. They don\u2019t do that for <u>fun</u>, not entirely. They often appropriate needed supplies: food, metal, cloth, animals, new blood, etc.\u201d<br /> <br /> Lord Iysen groans, rubbing her eyes with the heels of her hands.<br /> <br /> \u201cOkay, what will <u>that</u> take?\u201d<br /> <br /> You hesitate; thinking for a moment, then reply with:<ol class=\"decimal\"><li>Money, specifically silver and gold. Money is the great social lubricator, and giving enough raw \u201ccash\u201d to the Hill-Tribes would allow them to essentially buy what they needed, in addition to being a welcome gift. The young warriors, eager for wealth and status, who often raid specifically for such, might be upset, but some silver might soothe their pride. On the other hand\u2026\u2026. This is pretty much going to clear out your treasury.</li> <li>Land. They want their ancestral lands back, after all, and while you can\u2019t exactly give it <u>all</u> back to them, there is a substantial portion of the domain of Xia that is largely underutilized, being rough and hilly terrain on the northern border, right up against the mountains where the Hill-Tribes live. It is already largely \u201ctheirs\u201d <i>de facto</i>, since it isn\u2019t largely settled by Lowlanders, but they tend to use it as hunting grounds as opposed to sites for new villages, since any new settlements get raided by your vassals, often specifically to <u>prevent</u> settlement. Ceding them the land, as well as preventing your vassals from bothering them, would be worth a <u>lot.</u> On the other hand, maybe 30-40% of Xia could be ceded to the Hill-Tribes in this fashion</li> <li>Give them a hostage, one of your children. While this wouldn\u2019t serve as an exchange in and of itself, giving one of your children as a hostage would essentially prove that you are taking them seriously, and could very well mean you have to give them less money or land. On the other hand\u2026.. you only have three children, Lord Iysen, and none of them will be happy serving as a hostage.</li> <li>Something else?</li> </ol><br /> OOC:<br /> <br /> I love me an Exalted meta joke<br /> <br /> Poor Saiko, girl just doesn\u2019t have the bulk to be a front-line combatant. Kasai throws her around like a ragdoll, just through sheer weight alone.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-03-11T01:22:25-08:00","id":1061164,"title":"","content":"A mix of 2and 3 strikes me as a good starting point. Silver will probably also needed to be given, but the mixture should limit the cost of each individual section. Money is the least likely to be cost effective but offering some as a token or a percentage of the spoils of any counter strike would help.<br /> <br /> Ceding some of the land should not be too much of an issue, but the amount given makes a huge impact and the local lords are a major issue in regards to the relative value of the land. Still our best thing to offer.<br /> <br /> Offering one of Iysen's children as a hostage <b>temporarily</b>, for say the duration of the conflict or until the Tribes/Confederation is paid in full is probably the best way to prove to the tribes that we are taking them seriously, that we intend to honour the bargain. Added bonus, said child will learn about the Hill Tribes and that is valuable as a learning experience. That said...from what we know of the Hil Folk, they might take offence to the idea of harming a non-combatant. Need more info.<br /> <br /> Any money offered would need to be divided between the Tribes...which makes it really expensive. A non-trivial sum becomes a lot smaller that way but offering something along those lines might not be awful...heck a full blown trade treaty might be better for both sides but I'm not sure how that works with limited interactions. I don't really have a handle on how common it is for the Kingdoms and the Tribal Confederacies to interact at high levels, let alone peacefully. I think trade between the occurs through intermediaries normally, travelling merchants and the like.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Thoughts ripple out, birthing others","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1624-falleneco","name":"FallenEco","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1624_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1927"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-03-11T07:56:53-08:00","id":1061245,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>FallenEco</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1061164#post1061164\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">-snip-.</div> </div> </div> </div><a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1624-falleneco\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar1624_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"1624\" data-vbnamecard=\"1624\">FallenEco</a><br /> <br /> Please note that we are only really dealing with the Dumoniapoccasset Confederacy, which is made up of 12 tribes, or about 20,000 people.<br /> <br /> Oh, and..... uh, no, if Xia was to renege on the deal, whoever the hostage was would almost certainly be killed. <u>Brutally</u>. Like, &quot;send home bits and pieces of their tanned skin periodically&quot; brutally.<br /> <br /> That is why giving a hostage would be so effective. Money, land, Lord Iysen can get more of both, but she can't really get another child. It is a curb on <u>her</u> behavior, something to ensure she keeps <u>her</u> side of the bargain, not really anything for the Hill-Tribes.<br /> <br /> Of course, the executing of a hostage in the case of a broken treaty isn't unique to the Hill-Tribes, not by a long shot. The Lowland families do it &quot;all the time&quot;, relatively speaking, and the exchange of hostages is a pretty common part of peace negotiations. Usually, the hostage (it doesn't always have to be a child) is treated gently, and in some cases, ends up marrying into the family, but in cases when the hostage's family acts up and breaks the treaty, well...... things get nasty. That is, well, <u>the point</u> of taking/giving a hostage; an assurance of good behavior, or the hostage gets killed.<br /> <br /> For example, let us presume that Yue gets defeated, and to ensure peace from the surviving members of the Matsu family (as well as vassals of the same, if she decides to let them retain their territory, which is possible depending on how they behave), Lord Iysen could very well decide to take hostages. The hostages would then be moved to Teriake Castle, where they would live from there on out. If the Matsu family/vassals rebel, Lord Iysen would kill the hostages in retaliation.<br /> <br /> Now, Lord Iysen wouldn't <u>like</u> to do so, not one bit (not many mothers are comfortable killing children), but politics is politics, and if you aren't willing to -ahem- &quot;do what is necessary&quot;, what is the point of taking a hostage?<br /> <br /> In some cases, marriagable hostages might be married to eligible vassals or retainers, both to ensure the loyalty of the vassal/retainer and to make sure any future progeny are loyal to the liege-lord, not the family of the defeated party. In many cases, this might be preferable to them living as a hostage. Sure, if the potential hostage was a member of a royal family, their status will be decreased...... but their status would disappear upon attainment <u>anyways</u>, and living as someone of lesser status is preferable in many ways to existing as a prisoner with the spectre of death looming over them. In fact, many noble families eyeing defeat on the horizon will offer marriageable children as part of the defeat-terms.<br /> <br /> Both parties have to have marriageable partners for that to work, though. It doesn't always happen, for many reasons.<br /> <br /> As for trade...... the Hundred Kingdoms and the Hill-Tribes don't really interact all that much on a high level. Most interactions occur in the hinterlands of both societies; lowlander villages in the hills, Tribe hunting parties buying supplies from said villages or travelling traders, so on and so forth. There are travelling Hill-Tribe merchants throughout the Hundred Kingdoms, but they tend to be few and far between, of partial descent from both cultures, and don't go to the Castle-Towns very much, working through intermediaries more often than not.<br /> <br /> This is mainly due to the language barrier, thought inter-cultural discrimination also plays a part. Very few lowlanders can speak Forest-tongue, and very few Hill-Tribespeople speak Riverspeak. You also aren't going to want to go to a place to sell goods if people mock you and treat you like shit.<br /> <br /> For example, most of the clothing and jewelry Kasai bought in the market is of Hill-Tribe manufacture, but he didn't buy it off a Hill-Tribesperson.<br /> <br /> If trade was actually opened up between Xia and the Dumoniapoccasset, both &quot;economies&quot; would grow as a result, since the Hill-Tribes are sitting on comparatively-large deposits of gold, silver and copper, not to mention animal byproducts (wool, leather, furs, meat, cheese, etc), while the Lowlanders have fine farming land, as well as more iron for tools and weapons. Most of the trade would pass through individual merchants, however, since there isn't any roads leading between the two areas, so if Xia wants to benefit, they would either have to build a road, or take advantage of rivers.<br /> <br /> If the Hill-Tribes move into the lower hills as a result of a treaty, then trade would become almost infinitely-easier by comparison. Of course, there are already Lowlanders living there, but they are few and far between. Partially due to the rough terrain making farming difficult in comparison to the river-bottoms, but also because the area is positively <u>lousy</u> with bandits, even by Hundred Kingdoms-standards.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11892,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","user":"Boston123","date":". 03-11-2017, 05:31 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-03-11T08:12:49-08:00","id":1061251,"title":"","content":"Land. Yue land, if possible. Plant the seed for a long term alliance, if possible.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Like my Avatar? Courtesy of<span style=\"color:#0000FF\"> Jen</span>! : <span style=\"font-size:12px\">Anybody want their characters to be experimented on ? </span>post 98<br /> <i>An Exalt is never unarmed.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4018-l-het-esh","name":"L'het'esh","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4018_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"521"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-03-11T15:35:07-08:00","id":1061361,"title":"","content":"Could we offer a share of Yule land if we grab us some?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11567-jairain","name":"Jairain","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11567_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"345"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-03-11T16:50:00-08:00","id":1061383,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Jairain</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1061361#post1061361\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Could we offer a share of Yule land if we grab us some?</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Technically, any land ceded to the Hill-Tribes is going to have to come from land owned by Lord Iysen herself. She can't exactly give away land belonging to her vassals.<br /> <br /> If Yue falls, all lands by right in Yue belong to her automatically, up until she doles it out. She is probably going to hold on to the more productive coastal parts, while much of the retaining land is going to be awarded to her vassals and faithful retainers. While most of Yue's vassals are going to be stripped of title when they die, any that bend the knee will likely stay in power, albeit diminished (and hostages taken, as above). That..... doesn't leave much leftover land.<br /> <br /> However, Yue, much like Xia, and every other domain in the Hundred Kingdoms, has marginal land that isn't used for very much. Most of it, as above, is going to be the hilly country right next to the mountains, while other parts is the forest-land well away from river valleys, comparatively unsettled by the Lowlanders.<br /> <br /> This marginal land is going to be what is ceded as part of any treaty. And, while it is rougher and less productive than river-bottom, it is <u>infinitely</u> more valuable than the current cliffs, crags and gullies the Hill-Tribes currently occupy. For example, the Jianghu Gap, a hilly lake valley where Yagura Castle (Lord Iysen's second castle) is located, is about 1/5 the size of the current territory of the Nemasket, Assawompsset, and Nasketucket tribes combined. It it also fertile enough to support 5 x their number, in terrain that, while still rough, is much less harsh.<br /> <br /> She isn't likely to give up her castle, however. It will either be destroyed, or more likely, given to a vassal to look over, <u>especially</u> if the land is ceded over. While the Lord Iysen is willing to cut a deal with the Hill-Tribes, she doesn't <u>trust</u> them, not long-term.<br /> <br /> The only reason she is willing to deal with them at all is because Kasai is available. If not, she would basically be forced to fight a defensive war with Yue, so her forces could remain at home.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11892,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","user":"Boston123","date":". 03-11-2017, 10:53 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-03-11T18:43:55-08:00","id":1061402,"title":"","content":"Just as a word of , well, not <i>forewarning</i>, but: you all who are proposing to give the Hill-Tribes conquered Yue territory..... what happens if Xia <i>loses</i>? The Hill-Tribes get <i>nothing</i> in that case. It is eminently possible, as Xia and Yue are extremely similar in power; the war, as is, will be a fair-fight. And fair battles equal losing wars.<br /> <br /> We are trying, first and foremost, to keep them off Xia's back. We can't pay them with stuff we don't yet have: put the cart before the horse, count the eggs before they hatch, so on and so forth.<br /> <br /> Now, ceding them conquered land <b><u><i>if they help</i></u></b> conquer it is indeed a worthwhile proposition, and will make them sit back from the diplomacy-table, but we have to actually get them to said table first.....","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-03-11T21:07:59-08:00","id":1061436,"title":"","content":"That's kinda what I was thinking but if I was them I would demand much more than small bits of shitty land for the blood of my warriors. Perhaps offering the hill lands of both Xia and Yue in exchange for active help in the war plus maybe some other spoils IFF we win. We should be willing to settle for a mutual non-aggression pact in exchange for a small combo of money and lands. I would prefer the hostage angle as that can occasionally lead to unforeseen long term benefits if we are willing to risk the royal blood but it seems unlikely that would be voted on.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11567-jairain","name":"Jairain","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11567_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"345"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-03-12T03:00:04-07:00","id":1061482,"title":"","content":"The lands may be shitty by the standards of the Hundred Kingdoms, it has been emphasized that they are significantly better than what they currently have. In order for this to work we need to be know what we are willing to give in total and what we will give as a minimum. Some of those foothills, the 'poor' borderlands are certainly something that qualifies as both. Offering some of the lower hills in Xia would be our best bet for securing neutrality.<br /> <br /> Offering some of those hills in Yue is a good start for actual military aid. As would a reminder that if we lose badly (say because they raided us) we might not even be able to follow through on our word, as we would be dead and our enemy would take all our lands. I'd play that one close to our chest but it would serve as an advantage if we already have an agreement to a non-aggression treaty.<br /> <br /> But both of those strike me as at the table arrangements. A hostage would make a fantastic insurance policy to prove our good faith. But on it's own it is worth little. Likewise offering some money would help us get there. Since the tribes are smaller than I thought it has become more viable. That said I thought offering a share of the spoils was to be assumed, but we should make that clear beforehand. Keep in mind, by this stage we are directing the targets of their raids to our enemies. I fully believe they would want to keep what they took and I don't have a problem with that.<br /> <br /> IMO, the best way to get what we want is to offer some of the lower hills of Xia with a hostage. Going for broke such as a military assistance or convincing them to raid Yue and their allies, will obviously require more. I think offering some of Yue's lower hills in the event of conquest would be a good start. Some discretionary funds to bribe/convince the council if they are are on the fence.<br /> Likewise (and this will be the hardest to ask for) we may well be asked for other things, deals, legal protections that we haven't considered so we may need the right to negotiate things on Isyen's behalf in an uncertain manner...honestly if we are going that route, I'd ask that the hostage be the one to authorize any such unknown dealings. We know what the big offer is but minor details are kind of an issue.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Thoughts ripple out, birthing others","edited":{"userid":1624,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1624-falleneco","user":"FallenEco","date":". 03-12-2017, 06:04 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1624-falleneco","name":"FallenEco","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1624_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1927"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-03-12T08:32:19-07:00","id":1061548,"title":"","content":"I want to say &quot;all of the above&quot;.<br /> <br /> Xia won't want to give up 1/3 to 1/2 of their turf, or go bankrupt, but ceding a smaller amount of money and land each might be bearable. Also, the tribes understandably will not trust us, and so providing a hostage would be helpful. We can also offer trade agreements, cultural exchanges, or more esoteric things.<br /> <br /> Bottom line - treaties are complicated, and we should be ready to offer a wide variety of things to balance Xia's needs and theirs.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>On the frontier of the Wild South, there's only one woman with the grit to take on its most dangerous outlaws and bring them <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/984977-back-alive-or-maybe-dead-quest\" target=\"_blank\">Back Alive, or Maybe Dead.</a><br /> <br /> Avatar by <a href=\"http://puzzlinghappenstance.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">K.S. Brenowitz</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/908-semicasual","name":"semicasual","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar908_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2499"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-03-12T10:09:20-07:00","id":1061566,"title":"","content":"When in doubt, barter a marriage agreement! Everything in life can be solved by enough matchmaking! We've established that the nobility pretty much has to marry whoever they get told to, so we'll just...tell a few of 'em to marry Hill Tribe VIPs. If we're gonna unify these warring cultures and forge them into a grand empire, we gotta get started healing the divide through nuptial agreements!<br /> <br /> .../bluesid.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>So I'm making <a href=\"http://godkickingboot.smackjeeves.com/\" target=\"_blank\">God-Kicking Boot</a>, an Exalted webcomic, now. Updates on Sundays. Full-color, mediocre but slowly improving art. It's a thing.<br /> <br /> The absence of a monument can, in its own way, be something of a monument also.</i> -Roger Zelazny","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/512-wise-old-guru","name":"Wise Old Guru","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar512_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1097"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-03-12T10:43:16-07:00","id":1061571,"title":"","content":"So..... I made a map in Hexographer, to clarify some of the geopolitical aspects of what we are going over, but I <u>can't attach the damn thing</u>. I've tried .png, .jpeg, .gif. Nothing. <br /> <br /> Very frustrating.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-03-12T11:43:27-07:00","id":1061578,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wise Old Guru</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1061566#post1061566\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">When in doubt, barter a marriage agreement! Everything in life can be solved by enough matchmaking! We've established that the nobility pretty much has to marry whoever they get told to, so we'll just...tell a few of 'em to marry Hill Tribe VIPs. If we're gonna unify these warring cultures and forge them into a grand empire, we gotta get started healing the divide through nuptial agreements!<br /> <br /> .../bluesid.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> The thing about that is: the Hill-Tribes don't <u>really</u> have a concept of nobility. Sure, sure, they understand the topic, but even tribal chiefs and elders are <i>technically</i> equal to the rest of the tribe. Functionally, they are not, since chiefs and elders have an ass-ton of social, political and military influence, but still. If a Lowland VIP were to marry a Hill-Tribe VIP, nothing <u>really</u> that earth-shaking would happen, as the Hill-Tribes don't practice positional-succession like the Lowlanders do (instead being a meritocracy of sorts), and the Lowland noble-families aren't about to accept the children of the Hill-Tribes as heirs.<br /> <br /> Plus, marriage as a whole would be iffy, since the overwhelming majority of Lowland noble familes are followers of the Immaculate Faith, and almost all Hill-Tribespeople are functional pagans. Neither party is likely going to convert. Not to mention the language barrier.<br /> <br /> Of course, this could change once the Hill-Tribes actually live on comparatively-good farming land, and therefore are able to rack up wealth. In Hill-Tribe society, men marry into the woman's familial clan, and it is the women of the clan that &quot;own&quot; agricultural land, passing terraces down to their daughters. If, once the Hill-Tribes get some decent arable land, some enterprising Lowland nobles take Hill-Tribe women as concubines, that would cement relations between the two peoples, as well as ensure more land &quot;stays within the family&quot;, as it were. On top of that, since the Hill-Tribe women would legally be considered concubines, said nobles will also be free to marry other nobles without the stigma of polygamy.<br /> <br /> Here are some marriage &quot;standards&quot; accepted by both cultures:<br /> - Hill-Tribes: usually monogamy, occasional polygamy accepted for certain circumstances. Children are always accepted into the mothers clan.<br /> - Lowlanders: monogamy, but &quot;official&quot; and &quot;unofficial&quot; concubines accepted. Only children from the &quot;official&quot; spouse can be accepted as heirs, but &quot;official&quot; (aka recognized and acknowledged in court) concubine-borne children are not considered bastards, just much lesser in status to the true-born children. &quot;Unoffical&quot; concubine-borne children are bastards, if the parent even recognizes them to begin with. (<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Concubinage#China\" target=\"_blank\">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Concubinage#China</a>)<br /> <br /> Of course, not too many Lowland nobles are actually going to want to take Hill-Tribeswomen as concubines, <u>official</u> concubines anyway. That might be &quot;too much, too fast&quot; for them to take. Allowing the heathen savages into the lowlands is bad enough as it is!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11892,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","user":"Boston123","date":". 03-12-2017, 03:04 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-03-15T12:18:35-07:00","id":1062908,"title":"","content":"I would wonder if we could do a number of things:<br /> <br /> 1. Offer them lands in Xia to stay neutral. Consider making small payments to help with resettlement but nothing more than necessary to do that. We dont want them having so much money they use it to pay off mercenarys and weapons suppliers. <br /> 2. Offer them lands in Yue if they help us to fight Yue. Make payments enough to arm their warriors with gear thats good but not better than that of the Army of Xia. <br /> 3. Make religious offerings to their gods and hand over a hostage so that they know we bring good faith to the bargain and are serious about what we say and that we respect them.<br /> <br /> Just as important if not more is considering the other kindgoms nearby. How do we mend those fences?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8897-joshopotamus","name":"joshopotamus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8897_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"97"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-03-15T15:24:09-07:00","id":1062998,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>joshopotamus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1062908#post1062908\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Just as important if not more is considering the other kindgoms nearby. How do we mend those fences?</div> </div> </div> </div>The two other major domains in the area are Yue (with whom we aren't going to be mending many fences soon, quite the opposite) to the southeast of Xia, and Ji, with whom Xia has had a traditional rivalry of sorts. Mainly, said rivalry involves turning down marriage proposals out of hand, but if pressed or given opportunity, Ji could very well try to &quot;split the pie&quot; with Yue. It isn't that likely, however: &quot;better the enemy you know&quot;, and all that, and if there is one thing Ji knows, it is Xia. The two realms were allied in the rebellion against Zhou, and while they had a falling out, they still somewhat have a level of respect for each other, even if said respect is downplayed in modern times.<br /> <br /> Ji is to the northwest of Xia.<br /> <br /> Along the borders of Xia, Ji and Yue, there are several smaller &quot;realms&quot;, roughly equal to one of Lord Iysen's vassals in size, power and influence. Some of them are antipathic towards Xia (due to Xia being &quot;the bully on the block&quot; a couple generations ago), some are happy trading partners, most are ambivalent. The important thing about these smaller realms is that 1) they &quot;occupy&quot; the most feasible sites to start invasions of either Xia or Yue, aka the river valleys, and 2) While they wouldn't be able to actually <u>stop</u> either Xia or Yue's forces, these realms have castles and forces of their own, and could impede the moving of troops for either side for significant amounts of time. (which is the main &quot;function&quot; of a castle, anyways).<br /> <br /> Either side would want to court these smaller Realms into 1) joining their side, or 2) leaving them be as the main opponents clash.<br /> <br /> If either side were to try to recruit these smaller realms, offers like; allowing them to retain their recognized and traditional rights and status (aka, make them powerful vassals, like Marcher-Lords, not some bog-standard landholder), grant them more lands, grant them charters for resources (like, rights to log a forest, mine a series of hills, etc), or arrange a marriage between the &quot;royal&quot; family of the major realm and the &quot;royal&quot; family of the lesser one would be reasonably effective.<br /> <br /> Such similar offers might sway the sworn vassals of an enemy realm, since the first and foremost loyalty of most Lords isn't to their liege, but to their own dynasty. If their family and name could be better off serving another realm, or going it alone, they might just abandon their liege-Lord. Of course, unlike the &quot;independent realms&quot; that join under their own free (or coerced by threat of attack) will, such behavior is viewed as suspicious, and hostages will almost certainly be taken. It also would be easier to coerce a vassal into renouncing their liege if you metaphorically smack the liege around some: by defeating them soundly in the field at least a couple of times.<br /> <br /> ..... This would all be almost-infinitely easier to explain if I could just post my damn map.<br /> <br /> EDIT: Fuck it, made an Imgur<br /> <br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"http://imgur.com/7JpSQye\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/google-material/image_placeholder.svg\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://imgur.com/7JpSQye\" target=\"_blank\"> </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://imgur.com/7JpSQye\" target=\"_blank\"> http://imgur.com/7JpSQye </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11892,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","user":"Boston123","date":". 03-15-2017, 10:26 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-03-19T20:10:18-07:00","id":1065065,"title":"","content":"It looks like most of you suggested a mix between land and hostages, with a few adding money/tribute.<br /> <br /> &lt;x&gt; denotes something said in Forest-tongue, from this point on<br /> -----------<br /> You sigh, trying to remember stories and conversations from over a decade ago.<br /> <br /> \u201cIn all honesty, Lord, money wouldn\u2019t be the best choice. Now, don\u2019t get me wrong, they would most certainly accept money in the form of <i>tribute</i>, but it would mainly be the principle of the thing as opposed to any real need for specie. They would likely just melt it down and reuse it in jewelry. Among the Hill-Tribes, wealth is worn, not hoarded.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cOn the other hand, giving them a supply of cash, while also opening up access to trade, would allow them to just buy the things they usually steal. That, in and of itself, might lessen the amount of raiding they do.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cLand\u2026.. that would definitely make them sit up and pay attention. They are land-starved, <i>especially</i> the younger men and women who make up the largest potential raiding groups, and ceding them some of the lands you or your vassals don\u2019t use would lessen both the intensity and number of raids.\u201d<br /> <br /> She frowns, \u201cWhat do you mean, \u2018land we don\u2019t use\u201d?<br /> <br /> \u201cThe lands in the hinterlands of Xia, Lord, right up against the mountains. Growing crops in that region is difficult with lowland methods of farming, and they are largely undersettled as a result. That, in turn, means bandits use the hills and gullies as hideouts. In fact, ceding them to the Hill-Tribes would take out two birds with one stone; you would eliminate the bandits hiding in those hills, and you would by-and-large get the Hill-Tribes off your back.\u201d<br /> <br /> Continuing, \u201cA hostage, on the other hand, woul\u2026\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cI am <u>not</u> giving up one of my children as a hostage, Kasai!\u201d, she interjects, passions inflamed.<br /> <br /> \u201c\u2026.If I may continue, Lord?\u201d, and after she masters herself, you continue, \u201cIt is probably the only way they are going to take any offer <i>seriously</i>, Lord. They have been burned by the lowland realms before, and they have long memories. Giving them a symbol that you deal with them in good faith and mean to keep your word would go a <i>long</i> way towards securing their neutrality. It doesn\u2019t have to be permanent, just for as long as the conflict lasts. And, while they have a different sense of honor that we do, they wouldn\u2019t kill the hostage so long as you kept your side of the bargain\u2026.. well, my grandfather wouldn\u2019t, anyways.\u201d<br /> <br /> After taking a moment to collect your thoughts, \u201cYes. Lord, in order to successfully cut a neutrality deal with the Hill-Tribes, I recommend ceding them land, opening up trade and offering them certain protections, as well as giving them a hostage for the duration of the war. A gift of money would help as well, but not be critical.\u201d<br /> <br /> Instead of answering, Lord Iysen walks over to the parapet, looking out over the river for quite some time. The sun is starting to descend towards the western horizon, and despite it being late spring, the evening wind is chilly. You start to shiver before Lord Iysen abruptly steps back out of her thoughts.<br /> <br /> \u201c\u2026you are right, Kasai. I am sorry for being cross with you for suggesting an option. I\u2026. don\u2019t like the choices, but I don\u2019t really have a choice, do I?\u201d She says bitterly. \u201cWhatever I can do to protect Xia will be done, my feelings on the matter are irrelevant.\u201d<br /> <br /> She starts back down the stairs, \u201cSo, I guess the next decision to make is who to choose\u2026\u2026\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cIf I may make another suggestion, Lord?\u201d, and with her nod of approval,\u201d Your daughters would be arguably be more politically useful as a marriage-candidate than as a hostage, if I may say so without being presumptuous. That leaves your son, Aketchi. He is too young to fight, too young to be wed, and on top of that, he showed a great deal of interest in the stories I told about my youth among the Tribes.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cHe is my only son, though! And so young\u2026..But, yes, I see your point. And he will require much less coercing. \u2026..Very well, it shall be done.\u201d<br /> <br /> As the two of you ascend through the keep, she turns to you, \u201cHow long do you think it will take you to get there and back?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cWell, it took about 5 days for my page and I to travel the length of the Tumblestone to Teriake, for the tournament. That was with one person on foot, and we weren\u2019t rushing. Depending on how we travel, and what we are bringing along\u2026. I guess it could take us a little more than a week to reach the mountains, so long as we stick to the post-roads. Once we get to the mountains, it could take gods-know-how-long. I was planning on travelling to my grandfather first, as he would likely be the most receptive to any diplomacy, but the Nemasket has territory primarily to the northwest of Xia. I know from my father\u2019s lands, it only took a few days to get to my grandfather\u2019s village, but that was from Que, which is on the opposite side of Ji from Xia. From Xia\u2026.. two weeks, maybe three, just to get to the Hill-Tribes, and a month or more to come back with a response? To be honest, I am not entirely sure; it isn\u2019t like there are roads in the mountains, and I will largely have to follow the mountains north until I see landmarks I recognize.\u201d<br /> <br /> She sighs, \u201cVery well. I was hoping you would be back in time for the campaign season, but the winds that carry travelers over the mountains exist only in stories. With that in mind, I suggest you get a good-nights-rest; you will be heading out tomorrow morning. I will handle the writing of the letter; your grandfather speaks Riverspeak, does he not?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cYes, Lord, he does, but the other elders will almost certainly not.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cWell enough, he was whom I was hoping to deal with anyways. Good night, Kasai.\u201d<br /> <br /> Upon making your way back to your quarters, you slide open the door to find Saiko and Blue Pines engaged in a lively dice game, the looks of which Saiko is winning.<br /> <br /> \u201cI\u2019m guessing Blue Pines won her bet?\u201d<br /> <br /> She nods morosely, \u201cYup. Now I\u2019m losing it all. For someone who has never thrown dice before, she sure has some luck!\u201d<br /> <br /> Saiko grins, \u201cThink of it as karma; you bet against me in the first place, now I am winning it all back!\u201d<br /> <br /> You chuckle, \u201cThat\u2026.. isn\u2019t what karma means, Saiko. Besides, I hate to break up the fun and all, but we have work to do.\u201d<br /> <br /> The two of them look confused, \u201cRight now, at night?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cYes, we are leaving in the morning on a diplomatic mission. That means we have to pack. And by \u2018we\u2019, I mean \u2018you\u2019. Saiko, pack up whatever we will need for an expedition into the northern mountains; it is going to be windy and rainy, so focus on clothing first. Blue Pines, I want you to go down to the stables and make sure the cart and mule are all set for travel.\u201d<br /> <br /> Groaning, the two of them get up and set about their appointed tasks, while you take the opportunity to actually sit back and relax for once. When it was just you, you would have had to do everything by yourself (obviously), so having people to do it for you is pretty nice. With this in mind, you ready up your own wargear, while Saiko takes care of everything else; clothing, equipment, weapons and armor. It only takes a few minutes, so by the time Blue Pines comes back up from the stables, everything is squared away and you are able to drift off to sleep quickly.<br /> <br /> In the morning (far too early for your tastes. The sun isn\u2019t even over the horizon, yet!), your entourage is woken by a servant tapping on the door, bringing you breakfast and an admonition to be ready for travel as soon as possible. After eating and gearing up, you head out to the upper bailey, where there you are surprised to see a mounted escort flanking both your cart and a larger wagon, drawn by a team of oxen and surmounted by a large banner, emblazoned with the heraldry of Xia. You are also greeted by Lord Iysen and her son (who seems rather put-off, as opposed to his usual cheerfulness), who present you with a tabard of green silk with yet more Xian heraldry on the breast.<br /> <br /> When Lord Iysen comes close, to pass over the tabard and other accoutrements, you ask, \u201cLord Iysen, what is all this? I thought we were aiming for speed!\u201d<br /> <br /> Eyebrow raised, \u201cYou are, Kasai, but you are also my official envoy. As a duly-recognized servant of mine on a mission of state, you need to have certain accompaniments, in line with your status. In turn, these things will let you effectively travel faster than if you were by yourself, even if you move more slowly.\u201d Gesturing to the banner and the escort, \u201cthose two will ensure you receive no trouble on the road, and will open most doors and post-stations to you, no questions asked.\u201d To the tabard, \u201cthis tells the world you are my official servant, and are not to be impeded.\u201d, and to the wagon, \u201cand this will allow you to carry more supplies, so you can travel longer without stopping.\u201d<br /> <br /> Moving to the wagon, she motions for you to follow her. At the foot of the bed, she pulls out a heavy bronze key and unlocks a chest set at the back. Inside, to your shock, you see ingot after silver ingot, 25 in total, as well as several smaller bags of coin.<br /> <br /> \u201cWh\u2026 what is this?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cYou <i>did</i> say tribute would be well-received, did you not? Well, here it is. The equivalent of 25 dirham of silver, a year and a halfs-worth of gate-taxes and berth-tolls. There is also some money for travel expenses. I will be putting the official <i>communique</i> in here as well, so don\u2019t lose the key!\u201d, and with that, she lays down a paper package, sealed with wax.<br /> <br /> Closing and locking the chest, she turns to her son, and after gazing at him for a moment, takes him in a tight embrace. A few moments later, she turns to you, \u201cI guess you had better get a move-on. The war will not wait on your behalf....&quot; Taking a deep breath,&quot; Hereby, I recognize you as my official envoy, able and allowed to speak in my name in this matter. &quot; And, with this, she turns and hurries back into the keep, and without much ado, your convoy heads out.<br /> <br /> To your surprise, having such an escort doesn\u2019t really slow you down. While the wagon, ox-drawn as it is, is indeed slower than a party all ahorse, between the Xian banner mounted on it and the ample supply of cash, you are able to travel up the banks of the White river essentially unimpeded, waved through post-stations by officials and able to change out horses and oxen as they get tired. In addition, the armed escort keeps away bandits and the other assorted riff-raff of the road well away, and the streets of the farming villages you pass through are cleared of traffic ahead of time, leading to less wagon-wrangling between crowds and stalls. Even your leg is considerate of your need for speed, acting up only infrequently due to your ability to switch between riding ahorse and steering the wagon as needed. After only a week of travelling, you are already starting to climb into the foothills, <u>very</u> good time by your reckoning.<br /> <br /> As you climb higher and higher, you leave the lowland woods behind, and start to see more sawtoothed pines, maples and alders, interspersed between the stands of bamboo. The humidity characteristic of the great Eastern river valleys drops off and the wind picks up, howling around the rocky outcroppings. The road thins and narrows, changing from gravel to dirt, then to a thin path following the river, now a rapid flow crashing down the rock faces. The villages get smaller and ruder, surrounded by terraces instead of stone-walled fields, and here and there you see the telltale signs of Hill-Tribe ancestry (aka mutations) interspersed among the peasants.<br /> <br /> It is in one of these villages that you are accosted by one of the local peasants, evidently the headman of the village.<br /> <br /> \u201cSorry, boss, but you aren\u2019t gonna wanna take that wagon any further.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cWhy not?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cThe trail gets rougher; you can only pass down it on a sure-footed beast. The cart might make it through. Why do you wanna go down there, anyways? All that leads to is the Hill-tribes.\u201d<br /> Shrugging, \u201cI just do what I am told, friend. Thanks for the advice.\u201d Turning to the escort, you call out, \u201cAll right, unhitch the wagon and start divvying up supplies, we are going to have to leave it behind.\u201d<br /> <br /> As the troops unpack then repack the supplies from the wagon-bed, you walk around and unlock the chest, stapled to the floor. As you pull out the silver ingots, you hear a gasp from behind you. Wheeling around, you see one of the locals, hair like a fox, gaping at the silver wealth so exposed. You snarl, partially at your own stupidity for bringing it out in the open, and make towards the man to tell him to mind his own bloody business. Before you reach him, he turns and runs back between the houses.<br /> <br /> Annoyed, you turn from the village and quickly pass the ingots to the waiting armsmen. Then, you remove the staples holding the chest down, and move it over to the cart. There, you load the ingots back up and lock it again, covering the chest with gear in lieu of drilling holes in the bed of your own cart.<br /> <br /> You pay the headman handsomely to watch over both the wagon and the oxen until you return, the small bronze coins you give him probably the most money he has ever seen in his life. Perturbed and paranoid about having exposed the amount of cash you are travelling with, you order the convoy to head out down the trail, as opposed to staying in the village for much longer. You don\u2019t like how the peasant looked at you when you had the silver spread out.<br /> <br /> Very shortly, you find out the headman wasn\u2019t exaggerating how rough the trail got. In short order, you find the party travelling in single-file down the rocky defile, in some spots having to dismount and lead your horses on foot between boulders that press close. The mule is perfectly capable of traversing the trail, but at several points the cart gets stuck between rocks or in ruts, and you all have to work together to lift it out/over the obstruction.<br /> <br /> At this rate, it takes several hours to travel less than a mile, and when you come across a wide-spot in the trail, you gladly order the group to stop for the night, even though it is only mid-afternoon. Evidently, the site is often used by travelers, with a stone firepit already dug and rudimentary shelters of stone and branches in place.<br /> <br /> Some of the armsmen go to gather firewood and set up camp, while Blue Pines starts to put dinner together. As night falls, you, Saiko and Aketchi practice sparring with lengths of bamboo staves.<br /> <br /> \u201cNow, Master Aketchi, how much training have you had with arms?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c\u2026.not very much, Mr. Kasai. I\u2019ve mostly practiced with wooden weapons, but that was just to build up strength.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cHuh, you started late, I guess. I started at seven. Anyways, do your best to try to hit me, and I will do the same.\u201d<br /> <br /> The boy steps forward, telegraphing his strikes even worse than Saiko does, and you parry them with ease. He is getting obviously frustrated, gritting his teeth, and he steps back to catch his breath. At this very moment, your eyes rise to the ridgeline of the gully, and\u2026. <i>You see movement!</i> Shadows moving in the trees, more than one!<br /> <br /> You:<ol class=\"decimal\"><li>Tackle Aketchi into one of the shelters. If he takes an arrow or spear through the throat, this diplomatic mission is worthless!</li> <li>If they want a fight, then a fight they\u2019ll get! Call the armsmen and prepare!</li> <li>Shout that your party is a diplomatic mission from Xia to the Hill-Tribes. Only a fool would attack an official envoy under a banner!</li> <li>Pull back down the trail. Here, they can fire/attack you from multiple angles. Staying here might be a bad decision\u2026.</li> <li>Run up the trail. The terrain is rough, you could lose them!</li> <li>Offer money to the attackers. Chances are this is what they are after, and if they might be satisfied with cash as opposed to blood.</li> <li>Something else?</li> </ol>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11892,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","user":"Boston123","date":". 03-19-2017, 11:13 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-03-19T20:35:26-07:00","id":1065074,"title":"","content":"Combine 1-3. Yell at Akatchi to take cover, yell at the armsmen to get armed, and yell at whoever is up there not to do anything stupid. Then, if there's time, yell at whatever gods Kasai worships for making life difficult. <br /> <br /> Two-to-one odds each its villagers from the place we just passed moonlighting as bandits, bandits tipped off by the villagers, or hill tribes.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>On the frontier of the Wild South, there's only one woman with the grit to take on its most dangerous outlaws and bring them <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/984977-back-alive-or-maybe-dead-quest\" target=\"_blank\">Back Alive, or Maybe Dead.</a><br /> <br /> Avatar by <a href=\"http://puzzlinghappenstance.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">K.S. Brenowitz</a>","edited":{"userid":908,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/908-semicasual","user":"semicasual","date":". 03-20-2017, 08:07 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/908-semicasual","name":"semicasual","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar908_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2499"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-03-19T22:13:17-07:00","id":1065103,"title":"","content":"I agree with semicasual","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11567-jairain","name":"Jairain","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11567_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"345"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-03-19T23:41:02-07:00","id":1065117,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Jairain</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1065103#post1065103\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I agree with semicasual</div> </div> </div> </div>We can't really get everyone moving fast enough, so semicasual has the right of it. I would stress Akatchi's safety along with our actions, but otherwise....","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Thoughts ripple out, birthing others","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1624-falleneco","name":"FallenEco","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1624_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1927"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-03-20T12:22:56-07:00","id":1065357,"title":"","content":"Tackle Aketchi into one of the shelters. If he takes an arrow or spear through the throat, this diplomatic mission is worthless!<br /> and call out to the armsmen. The fight will start soon enough; the villagers saw the size of the escort so it's a lot of them or it's daring Hill folk.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Like my Avatar? Courtesy of<span style=\"color:#0000FF\"> Jen</span>! : <span style=\"font-size:12px\">Anybody want their characters to be experimented on ? </span>post 98<br /> <i>An Exalt is never unarmed.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4018-l-het-esh","name":"L'het'esh","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4018_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"521"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-03-20T18:46:21-07:00","id":1065540,"title":"","content":"Gonna third semicasual's suggestion. Those three options seem like the best ones.<br /> <br /> I would also like to ask: what's the scale on the hexmap you have? Is every hex 30 miles across? I'm most familiar with hexmaps from pathfinder, which operated on a 1 hex = 12 miles scale. What scale do you use?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11619-kymmetheseventh","name":"KymmetheSeventh","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11619_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"151"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-03-20T19:01:53-07:00","id":1065542,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>KymmetheSeventh</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1065540#post1065540\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Gonna third semicasual's suggestion. Those three options seem like the best ones.<br /> <br /> I would also like to ask: what's the scale on the hexmap you have? Is every hex 30 miles across? I'm most familiar with hexmaps from pathfinder, which operated on a 1 hex = 12 miles scale. What scale do you use?</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Ehhhhhh, to be honest, I am playing rather hard and fast with that hex-map. I made it quick and dirty mainly to give context for the upcoming chapter. I didn't make it with a set scale in mind.<br /> <br /> For some context, however, there is a river that branches off to the &quot;northeast&quot; of Teriake Castle. Where the woods meet the river is where Saiko's village was located. From that village, it took Kasai and Saiko about ....... eh, 6-7 days or so, but one of them wasn't mounted, and they effectively faffed about training and such.<br /> <br /> Even with the wagon in tow, since the entire party was mounted, and able to travel on paved/well-maintained post roads, and switch out mounts regularly, the diplomatic escort made it to the border between Xia and the Hill-Tribes in about a week. Now, since they will be travelling through some pretty rough terrain, their pace will slow to a crawl.<br /> <br /> I envision that, if one were to follow the Red River, aka the &quot;big&quot; river on Xia's southern border, one could traverse the length of the realm in about a week and a half, two tops. The Hundred Kingdoms aren't that big, they are just heavily hilled and forested, and that slows down movement. In the flat river valleys, however, one can travel pretty quickly.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-03-26T15:38:33-07:00","id":1068446,"title":"","content":"Sorry ladies and gents, but I got back from a camping trip today and am pretty beat down as a result, so it looks like there will be no posting this week. I might make a post on Wednesday, or I might make next weekend's post extra-long.<br /> <br /> ...... This is why I can't have nice Quest threads, I suppose.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-03-27T16:01:39-07:00","id":1068919,"title":"","content":"First priority is Aketchi's safety. Then a call to arms, and warn the attackers (in both languages) that attacking the official envoys of the Lord of Xia is a really bad idea.<br /> <br /> Why the Hell didn't we set a watch?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">She/Her. I am literal-minded and write literally. If I don't say something explicitly, please never assume I implied it. The only exception is if I try to make a joke.</span></span></span></div> <span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">My point of view may be different from yours but is equally valid.</span></span></span><br /> <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1x1ycoxJF0G9niMt5D3Pp7owZ7tw8AJvL?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">Exalted-cWoD-ArM url mega-library</a>. Exalted <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/663334-random-name-generator\" target=\"_blank\">name-generators</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/896-erinys","name":"Erinys","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar896_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4235"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-03-27T17:39:40-07:00","id":1068946,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Erinys</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1068919#post1068919\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">First priority is Aketchi's safety. Then a call to arms, and warn the attackers (in both languages) that attacking the official envoys of the Lord of Xia is a really bad idea.<br /> <br /> Why the Hell didn't we set a watch?</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> We did, the unknown assailants just ...... uh, negated them. (It has been several hours since we set up camp, with all that implies: the setting up of tents, staking/watering/feeding/caring of horses, unpacking of gear, gathering of firewood, rotating of watches, etc. I felt that was implied, but if you feel that I should have outright said that, then I apologize for not being clear)) They could have sneaked around them, or they could have killed them, we don't really know. Hell, at this point, we aren't even sure if the unknown assailants are actually hostile, although they almost certainly are (nobody sneaks up on a camp for shits and giggles in bandit country), we almost literally <u>just</u> saw them, and only glimpses thus far.<br /> <br /> For future reference, we only have (possibly <i>had?</i>) 6 armsmen with us as part of our official entourage. It was all Lord Iysen could spare on short notice. They are tough, skilled fighters to a man, and quite good at their jobs (meaning, if a whole bunch of someones got around them, those someones are either <u>good</u>, or overwhelmed them before they could get off a shout)<u>,</u> equipped with brigandines, helmets, shields and spears, backswords and a single crossbow. 2 of them were on watch, so we very well might be down to 4, against an unknown number of assailants...... who, it might be worth mentioning, have concealment <u>and</u> the high ground.<br /> <br /> The Hill-Tribes <u>will</u> respect both the banner of Xia and our person as envoy....... if they aren't the &quot;young and dumb&quot; warriors of the Tribes, out to wreck shit and make a name for themselves. To say they probably wouldn't care is a simplification of the matter. They technically know that messing with us could/would bring down a <u>lot</u> of heat on the tribes, and on themselves from their chiefs..... but they aren't really thinking about consequences at this age. In fact, killing a recognized Lowlander noble and bringing back the <u>metric asston</u> (official measurement!) of money we are hauling would swell their reputations as warriors..... until their chief realizes who we were/who we represent, and shits their collective pants over the fact we were killed.<br /> <br /> Bandits wouldn't care about who were were or who we represent. They are in it for the lootz.<br /> <br /> Not to mention that if we are hypothetically stabmurdered on this mountainside, it is highly unlikely that anyone will ever know what happened to us. We are in the ass-end of nowhere, here.<br /> <br /> As a final addendum: we are not wearing armor, neither is Saiko, Blue Pines or Aketchi. The armsmen in camp are in partial panoply: underpadding and helmets, with brigandine-vests set aside. They have their weapons close at hand, however, as do you, Blue-Pines and Saiko. Aketchi has a little utility knife, but that is it, and he isn't even trained in combat.<br /> <br /> Wearing armor for a long period of time is a pain in the ass. Even though the weight of your mail is taken as tension across the chest rather than weight from your hips and shoulders, you can't really wear it for longer than a few hours: it is too insulating and encumbering. Even the comparatively light brigandines and gambesons worn by the other members of your party are too hot to just wear around for shits and giggles.<br /> <br /> Unless I specifically draw attention to us wearing armor (usually in a militaristic social function, to show we are a retainer of Lord Iysen), or we are engaged in a setpiece battle where we would have enough proper preparation time beforehand to get set up, you can almost always assume we aren't going to be in full array.<br /> <br /> We will, however, almost always have our sword and dagger on us 24/7, unless I specifically bring attention to us taking them off. Our spear and bow are much more iffy; the bow is almost-always mounted on Xuansu's saddle, and the spear usually stays there as well. Saiko and Blue-Pines will almost always have their weapons (unless they are in a social situation where it is inappropriate for non-nobility to be armed, like in an official function held by Lord Iysen), but their armor is only worn during combat (unless we get them some nice surcoats emblazoned with our personal arms..... which we don't have as of yet)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11892,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","user":"Boston123","date":". 03-27-2017, 10:23 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-03-28T04:48:38-07:00","id":1069249,"title":"","content":"Yeah, defensive positions. Protect the boy. Raise the alarm. Try to intimidate the 'bandits'.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Thoughts ripple out, birthing others","edited":{"userid":1624,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1624-falleneco","user":"FallenEco","date":". 03-28-2017, 07:52 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1624-falleneco","name":"FallenEco","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1624_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1927"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-03-28T07:23:30-07:00","id":1069329,"title":"","content":"((Thanks, that is helpful info!<br /> ((If we warn them we are an envoy in both languages, and they respond with juvenile idiot teenager taunts in Foresttongue, would it be helpful to tell them we are the Confederacy Warchief's grandson and killing us will piss him off? Or would that be risky?))","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">She/Her. I am literal-minded and write literally. If I don't say something explicitly, please never assume I implied it. The only exception is if I try to make a joke.</span></span></span></div> <span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">My point of view may be different from yours but is equally valid.</span></span></span><br /> <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1x1ycoxJF0G9niMt5D3Pp7owZ7tw8AJvL?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">Exalted-cWoD-ArM url mega-library</a>. Exalted <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/663334-random-name-generator\" target=\"_blank\">name-generators</a>.","edited":{"userid":896,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/896-erinys","user":"Erinys","date":". 03-28-2017, 10:28 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/896-erinys","name":"Erinys","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar896_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4235"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-03-28T13:53:16-07:00","id":1069597,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Erinys</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1069329#post1069329\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">((Thanks, that is helpful info!<br /> ((If we warn them we are an envoy in both languages, and they respond with juvenile idiot teenager taunts in Foresttongue, would it be helpful to tell them we are the Confederacy Warchief's grandson and killing us will piss him off? Or would that be risky?))</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> One of our grandfather's epithets/titles is &quot;the Beast of Baotsu&quot;, so if we oh-so-casually bring his name up (something along the lines of ,&quot; sure, attack the grandson of the guy who has personally collected over 100 enemy scalps, that will go over well!&quot;), the kids will probably metaphorically piss their pants. On the other hand, only the members of the Nemasket Tribe really know Ousamequin (his name) had a grandson, and only the people from the settlement he lives in know what we look like (er, more like <i>looked</i>: we left when we were seven or so, and only came back to visit occasionally) , so they might not believe us if we bring up our relationship.<br /> <br /> On the other hand, they would almost-certainly bring us to him straight away, in order to get to the truth of the matter. With a bunch of blustering and shit-talking, but that is what happens with hormonal teenagers everywhere.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11892,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","user":"Boston123","date":". 03-28-2017, 09:46 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-03-29T08:27:07-07:00","id":1070098,"title":"","content":"im all for the defensive position call to arms and warnings path set out by Erinys. In finding a defensive position i'd hope to find any sort of rocky out crop or cave like thing possible to make sure that any attackers would have to come at us one at a time essentially looking for a place where attackers would have a narrow approach and would have to fight through each other or terrain to get to us.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8897-joshopotamus","name":"joshopotamus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8897_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"97"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-03-29T22:23:32-07:00","id":1070488,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Boston123</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1069597#post1069597\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> One of our grandfather's epithets/titles is &quot;the Beast of Baotsu&quot;, so if we oh-so-casually bring his name up (something along the lines of ,&quot; sure, attack the grandson of the guy who has personally collected over 100 enemy scalps, that will go over well!&quot;), the kids will probably metaphorically piss their pants. On the other hand, only the members of the Nemasket Tribe really know Ousamequin (his name) had a grandson, and only the people from the settlement he lives in know what we look like (er, more like <i>looked</i>: we left when we were seven or so, and only came back to visit occasionally) , so they might not believe us if we bring up our relationship.<br /> <br /> On the other hand, they would almost-certainly bring us to him straight away, in order to get to the truth of the matter. With a bunch of blustering and shit-talking, but that is what happens with hormonal teenagers everywhere.</div> </div> </div> </div>So if these are Hill-Folk, it would be best if we tell them then. Got it","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Thoughts ripple out, birthing others","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1624-falleneco","name":"FallenEco","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1624_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1927"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-03-30T18:58:30-07:00","id":1070979,"title":"","content":"You know, if I spent as much time actually working on my Quest Thread as I did gleefully writing world-building lore, I would probably be done by now\u2026\u2026..<br /> <br /> The Oathsworn: Arms, Equipment, Training and Philosophy<br /> <br /> The Oathsworn, as they are so called, are the quasi-professional warriors of the Hundred Kingdoms feudal aristocracy. Stories are told about them, homage is paid to them, and battles are won or lost through their skill at arms. To be given the title of Oathsworn is to be held in great esteem, at least technically.<br /> <br /> But who are they <i>really</i>? Noble warriors upholding a valorous code of conduct through personal integrity and skill at arms? Vicious bullies that trod over the dignity and lives of those beneath them, demanding obsequiousness under threat of violence?<br /> <br /> In many cases, they are both. They are, after all, only human. Regardless, this treatise will attempt to decipher if only the smallest aspect of their traditional (and often confusing and contradicting) code of conduct, origins, and means of training.<br /> <br /> ---Origin:<br /> <br /> The Oathsworn, at least as an example of a professional warrior caste of society, is partially derived from two different and opposing forces: the ancient, yet still extant Vanehani martial tradition called &quot;Kenseiden&quot; (derived from the Shogunate Era Pre-High Realm terms for 'sword/means of aggression' [ken], 'paragon/saint' [sei], and 'tradition/philosophy' [den], many followers/practitioners of which accompanied the first Sword Prince, Kendorei, on his fateful final invasion of the proto-Hundred Kingdoms, and from the Chosen warriors of the proto-Hill Tribes warrior chieftaincies.<br /> <br /> From the Kenseiden warrior-cum-monks, the Oathsworn inherited their tradition of martial excellence, and from the Chosen warriors, the first lieges of what were to eventually become the Oathsworn took the idea of gathering a solid group of loyal, skilled and driven warriors (the Kenseiden practice largely being individual), and using them as heavy infantry in battle. After experiencing the brutal effects of the proto-Hill Tribe cavalry attacks, a beautiful idea was born, and the heavy infantry soon took up horses and turned into heavy shock cavalry.<br /> <br /> As the Hundred Kingdoms progressed from infancy to adolescence, and warfare changed from &quot;skirmishes between Lord and their retinues&quot; (scrums more akin to brutal back-alley gangfights than warfare) to &quot;massed combat between kingdoms&quot;, the proto-Oathsworn retainers changed from the primary combatants on a battlefield to supplementary heavy infantry and cavalry. For the price it takes to maintain and keep a single Oathsworn warrior, Lords could instead levy 5 or more peasants. This, coincidentally, was the start of feudalism in the Hundred Kingdoms, as when the lands of ruling Lords expanded, and they therefore needed more and more proto-Oathsworn to defend their holdings, many Lords were unable to support keeping such large retinues out of their own wallet. Therefore, each pre-Oathsworn retainer would instead be ceded land, so they could support themselves on the backs of the peasants that lived on their estates, and in turn swore loyalty to their liege, to support them in peace and in war.<br /> <br /> These oaths of loyalty would give these military retainers the title of Oathsworn. Initially, this was the only &quot;Oath&quot; the Oathsworn made.<br /> <br /> As the Hundred Kingdoms progressed from adolescence to adulthood, these Oathsworn warriors (now landed nobles), ever hungry for more lands and riches, became increasingly brutal in their search for the same. Initially, any warrior with enough equipment and skill could make an Oath of Loyalty to a lord in return for wealth and respect, leading as one might expect to plenty of bad apples in the bunch. The plundering of villages, brutal mistreatment of peasantry, and overwhelming preponderance of uncouth and rough behaviors led to a general mistrust of the group among the Hundred Kingdoms in general.<br /> <br /> One nameless Oathsworn, apparently in disgust at what their fellow comrades were doing to their reputation, rebelled against the then-current social norms; instead of swearing ill-kept Oaths for riches and homage, this individual instead traveled the Hundred Kingdoms, righting wrongs and behaving in general opposite what was expected of them.<br /> <br /> When an Oathsworn party attempted to raid a village for loot or fun, or stood by idly as bandits did the same, this Oathsworn was there to defend the peasants from violent death. Where a peasant might be beaten down, or even killed, by a passing Oathsworn for presuming to speak to them, this Oathsworn instead replied civilly and respectfully to people of all social status. Where most Oathsworn were concerned only with weapons, women and wine, this Oathsworn took up the arts of poetry, calligraphy, music and other arts.<br /> <br /> Eventually, this Oathsworn attracted followers, eager to learn from them. In turn, the Oathsworn bade them swear additional Oaths, instilling in them codes of conduct, a sense of honor and dignity, &quot;correct&quot; methods of training for war and peace, a sense of appreciation of the arts, as well as many other aspects, in a sense codifying the modern Oaths many Oathsworn swear. After making them swear these Oaths, their instructor had them meditate for a night and day in an Immaculate temple near their place of encampment, in order to fully reflect on what they were so swearing to do. If anyone felt, after a night reflecting on their Oaths, they they could not uphold them to their best, they would be allowed to leave, but they could not call themselves Oathsworn.<br /> <br /> The majority did, and thereafter left, travelling the Hundred Kingdoms much as their master had done, behaving in much the same way and speaking about the &quot;new&quot; Oathsworn. These warriors, in turn, attracted followers of their own, who attracted their own followers, etc etc etc. The Lords of the Hundred Kingdoms, happy to see a warrior-class that wasn't all about burning down villages indiscriminately, eagerly supplanted their earlier retainers with the 'new and improved&quot; Oathsworn........ who proceeded to rapidly become very similar to the rough-and-tumble asswipes they replaced. It would appear that material greed and desires beat out philosophical promises. However, the system of Oaths, methods of training and appreciation for the arts remained ingrained in the new class, and has remained so to the present day.<br /> <br /> ---Training:<br /> <br /> The Oathsworn are, first and foremost, semi-professional warriors. They are not fully professional (a good example of a professional warrior would be the Legions of the Scarlet Empire, or the maniples of Lookshy), since they do not receive a wage for fighting, nor do they spend the majority of their time at war. Instead, Oathsworn are instead retained (hence the term &quot;retainer&quot;) by Lords of the Hundred Kingdoms to serve as the heavy backbone of a fighting force. When they are not engaged in battle, many/most Oathsworn are either engaged in the running of their enfeoffed estates or training for future battles.<br /> <br /> One thing that might not be apparent to the modern, gentle reader, is that combat with weapons other than a crossbow or firewand is <u>difficult</u>. Using weapons like swords, spears and bows requires a great deal of physical strength, long periods of training, and a sense of aggression and drive that might be lacking in those not versed in combat.<br /> <br /> In response, many Oathsworn start training with arms at a very early age. The &quot;traditional&quot; age one might start &quot;official&quot; (start as a page to an Oathsworn instructor) is usually given as seven years of age, although many Oathsworn start physical training as soon as they are able to walk and run. As a youth, games and play that build stamina and strength (footraces, wrestling, whacking sticks together)are favored, and often chores involving physical labor are given as well, such as carrying heavy loads or gardening.<br /> <br /> At the age of seven, most prospective Oathsworn are apprenticed to an Oathsworn warrior for instruction and training, usually in how to maintain weapons and armor, some sparring bouts with blunted weapons, serve as go-fers and general labor (tents, firewood and water will not sort itself out!) as well as training in etiquette and other niceties (mainly: stand up straight, shut up, and listen to me when I am talking to you!). At this age, most pages do not accompany their lieges into battle, as they are far to young to be effective combatants, nor do they likely own weapons/armor.<br /> <br /> At the age of 14 (traditionally), or when the liege-Oathsworn deems them ready, usually several years after first starting as a page, the page becomes what is termed a &quot;footman&quot; (which comes from where the trainee sits, aka at the feet of their liege), which is basically a senior page. At this point, the footman will take a more active role in caring for the liege's equipment, as well as accompanying them both to battle and to court. In battle, they will usually hold their masters unused weapons ready for use, give up their horse if their masters is killed, as well as passing messages or bringing water (in battle, footmen are traditionally bare-headed and without hand protection, ostensibly to prevent them from being valid targets, although shit can always happen.) In court, they traditionally sit behind their master at the table, offering bowls of washwater or clean cloths when required, serving their master food and drink, and cleaning up after them.<br /> <br /> When a footman is deemed ready, both in combat skill and courtly skill, which is traditionally the age of 21, the liege-Oathsworn will ask the footman to accompany them into battle, fully armed and equipped. If they prove competence, they are thereafter allowed to swear the Oaths that make them Oathsworn, then stand a vigil overnight in an Immaculate Temple, in order to reflect on what the Oaths mean to them. Of course, the footman actually has to <u>own</u> their own weapons and armor, which isn't guaranteed. Some might be given equipment by their parents, others might be given arms and armor by their liege. Some, forever too poor to actually get equipment of their own, perpetually remain footmen to their masters.<br /> <br /> --- Equipment:<br /> <br /> --Armament:<br /> <br /> The three traditional &quot;noble weapons&quot; of the Hundred Kingdoms are the sword, the spear and the bow, and all Oathsworn are expected to be at least proficient with all three. There are also several important means of actually bearing the weapons that identifies an Oathsworn as a Oathsworn, as opposed to just a well-heeled retainer or noble.<br /> <br /> The main difference is: while most warriors in the Hundred Kingdoms will wear armor/clothing bound shut by a sash of cloth, only Oathsworn may wear their sword and dagger <u>thrust through</u> (aka wrapped up in the sash and bound to the body) the sash, while non-Oathsworn are to wear the above weapons <u>hanging from</u> (aka suspended via a cord or tie) from the sash. This difference is to symbolically represent how the Oathsworn is ever-ready to defend their Oath-sworn honor, and how the Oaths cannot be removed from their person, yadda yadda yadda. The difference might be seen as trivial, but bloody duels to the death have been fought over such disrespect.<br /> <br /> -The sword is a direct derivative of the ancient Vanehani-style straight sword (usually a concave diamond or flattened hexagon in cross-section), itself a descendant of a Shogunate cavalry blade adopted, amusingly enough, from the ancestors of the Hill-Tribes. In the Hundred Kingdoms, only those of noble birth are allowed to wield the double-edged straight sword, while those of lesser birth are to limit themselves to &quot;lesser&quot; single-edged backswords. Traditionally, the sword is made of three &quot;plates&quot; of steel, with a hard plate of steel sandwiched between two of softer steel. The softer steel plates form the flat of the blade, giving flexibility and protecting the inner spine from shock, while the hard central plate forms the hardened cutting edges and central spine. More expensive swords use 5 or seven plates instead, and most sword-steel is imported from Vaneha anyways.<br /> <br /> Most Oathsworn swords are about 3 feet in length, of which 24-30 inches of which are blade (depending on the size and preference of user) and the rest is hilt. The hilt is long enough to fit a hand and two or three fingers, allowing both single-handed and two-handed use as needed, and is usually wrapped in cord for better grip. The crosspiece is traditionally oval-shaped, although wing-shapes projecting both towards the blade and back towards the user are common. The hilt, handle and pommel are usually stacked onto the tang of the blade, which is in turn peened over the pommel, holding everything nice and tight.<br /> <br /> The blade usually exhibits both a profile and distal taper, meaning it both gets narrower and thinner closer to the tip, which itself is usually needle-pointed for piercing armor. Also, the blade is traditionally divided into three &quot;sections&quot; based on intended use: the section near the tip is razor-sharp, for stabbing, slashing and whip-cuts. The central portion of the blade is knife-sharp, for cleaving cuts or draw-cuts. The section near the hilt is either &quot;chisel-sharp&quot; (meaning a definite edge, but you can touch the edge with a finger) or left as an unsharped ricasso, and used for parrying, binding or other defensive moves.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jian\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/Sword_with_Scabbard_MET_DP119025_brightened_2x3.jpg/960px-Sword_with_Scabbard_MET_DP119025_brightened_2x3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=index&amp;utm_content=thumbnail\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jian\" target=\"_blank\"> Jian - Wikipedia </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jian\" target=\"_blank\"> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jian </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> While the sword is traditionally the most &quot;respected&quot; weapon of the Oathsworn, and in fact serves as the symbol of their social class, in practice the sword is usually a backup weapon, both on the battlefield and elsewhere. The &quot;main&quot; workhorse weapons of the Oathsworn are the spear and the bow.<br /> <br /> -The spear is, by far, the most used weapon of the entire Oathsworn arsenal. On foot, on horseback, in offense and in defense,the spear largely cannot be beat in practicality.<br /> <br /> The Oathsworn spear was actually invented by the invading Vanehani armies before the Hundred Kingdoms were formed. As the campaign progressed, the swords of the warriors broke in battle, and it wasn't like they were able to go home to get replacements. Therefore, in order to allow their armies to remain combat-effective, the clan-leaders of Vaneha ordered their warriors to reforge the broken blades into shorter weapons, suitable for use as spearheads or daggers.<br /> <br /> The spear is traditionally about 7 feet in length, or as high as the warrior can reach up with arm outstretched. From that point, the spearhead is traditionally one foot in length, with a dagger-shaped blade ending with a tang several inches long. This tang is inserted into a slot carved into the end of the shaft, over/through which a peg is inserted and a socket worked, to prevent the shaft from splitting. The dagger-shaped blade is long enough to allow for slashing and stabbing attacks to be worthwhile, while the shaft is long enough to be usable from horseback and short enough to not be unwieldy on foot.<br /> <br /> Traditionally (and pragmatically), the length of spearheads and dagger-blades allows one to reuse broken sword-blades in their construction.<br /> <br /> While the Oathsworn spearhead is dagger-shaped (due to them mainly being made from cut-down swords and daggers), spearheads used by commoners are simpler in construction, usually diamond-shaped and making use of less steel and more iron.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yari#/media/File:Yari02.JPG\" target=\"_blank\">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yari#/...ile:Yari02.JPG</a><br /> (Oathsworn spearhead)'<br /> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yari#/media/File:Ryo_shinogi_fukuro_yari.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yari#/...ukuro_yari.jpg</a><br /> (Commoner spearhead)<br /> <br /> -The bow is the final aspect of the Oathsworn arsenal, and is a blend of the traditional Vanehani asymmetrical composite bow and the laminated wooden longbow used by the Hill-Tribes. About 3 feet in length when strung, the Oathsworn bow is symmetrical, with deep limbs and a long draw-length, usually to the ear. Constructed of wood, sinew, rawhide, horn and bamboo laminates, the bow is capable of punching an arrow through both sides of a deer at 50 paces, and loosing an arrow faster than the eye can see out on a relatively flat trajectory.<br /> <br /> The arrows themselves are usually about 3 feet long, as long as the bow is, and made of coppiced willow and bamboo. The tang of the arrowhead is usually inserted into a tight-fitting section of bamboo, which is left free around the wooden shaft. That way, the arrowhead will be left behind when the penetrated enemy pulls on the wooden shaft.<br /> <br /> Usually, the string is made of linen or silk, and drawn via the aid of a carved ring worn on the thumb of the dominant hand.<br /> Like most composite/laminated bows, the glues and resins of the Oathsworn bow do not play well with water and moisture, meaning many battles are decided by the vagaries of the weather.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Composite_bow#/media/File:KaiyuanBowStrung.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Compos...nBowStrung.jpg</a><br /> <br /> --Other weapons<br /> <br /> - The dagger: the dagger is basically a shorter version of the sword, about a foot long and thrust through the sash. It is primarily a backup to the sword, but is retained for use in interior-combat, where a sword or spear might be caught up on the surroundings, or for use in a grapple. Traditionally, the sword and dagger are made in a matching set.<br /> <br /> -The glaive: Another Vanehani descendant, the glaive is basically a curved backsword blade set on a spearshaft. In the early days of the Vanehani invasion and the formation of the Hundred Kingdoms, the glaive was a favorite of the proto-Oathsworn, but is no longer very common. It requires too much space to use in the tightly-packed blocks common to modern Hundred Kingdoms combat. Instead, the glaive (and the spear) have found a new home in the hands of the daughters of nobility (and the nobility-aping <i>nouveau riche</i> town-dwelling middle class), who are trained to use the weapon in defense of their homes, and as a grace-building training tool.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Naginata\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9f/%E8%96%99%E5%88%80%2C_Naginata.jpg/1280px-%E8%96%99%E5%88%80%2C_Naginata.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=index&amp;utm_content=thumbnail\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Naginata\" target=\"_blank\"> Naginata - Wikipedia </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Naginata\" target=\"_blank\"> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Naginata </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> -The backsword: basically, a single-edged sword with a \u201cback\u201d that is perpendicular to the face. They come in many forms, from straight-bladed copies of the Oathsworn sword to curved blades for use from horseback. The backsword is used as a backup hand-weapon by wealthy Men-at-Arms/armsmen, and as self-defense tools for travelling merchants. Additionally, many nobles wear them as \u201cwalking about\u201d weapons, for when they don\u2019t want to possibly damage their expensive straight-swords, or as hunting weapons.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dao_(sword)\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/static/images/icons/enwiki-25.svg\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dao_(sword)\" target=\"_blank\"> Dao (disambiguation) - Wikipedia </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dao_(sword)\" target=\"_blank\"> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dao_(sword) </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> -The shield: The shield is used primarily by Oathsworn that wear lighter armor than their peers, or by Oathsworn intending to get into melee combat. The frame of the typical shield is made of steam-softened bamboo bent around wooden support laths. The shield is faced with more slats of bent bamboo, then covered with linen and sometimes laccquer. Convexed in final form, a well-made shield will cover from the shoulder to the knee, offering protection from arrows and melee weapons, all while being relatively lightweight.<br /> <br /> --Armor<br /> <br /> Oathsworn armor is derived from both the Vanehani styles the Hundred Kingdoms inherited and the Hill-Tribe styles they subsumed.<br /> <br /> Usually, in the wealthier river bottoms, nobles will wear lamellar supplemented by mail, while the poorer nobles from the hills and forests will wear just mail. Both are equally protective, all things considered, they differ mainly in the levels of maintenance required and maintenance methods.<br /> <br /> Lamellar is laced together, and if it gets wet must be unlaced then dried before the lacing starts to rot. Otherwise, it is largely self-supporting in the sense that it will bear its own weight.<br /> <br /> Mail, on the other hand, can get wet without (much) regard towards its welfare, as the links are open to the air and will dry comparatively quickly. However, it does not support its own weight, meaning it will hang from the shoulders if unsupported.<br /> <br /> Both mail and lamellar are worn with underpadding, usually made of wool or cotton stuffed between two layers of linen or canvas. This underpadding is what prevents spears, arrows and swords from breaking bones when impacting the armor, all the metal does is prevent the user from being stabbed/cut. And a strong enough cut/stab, or narrow enough point, <u>will</u> penetrate regardless. The underpadding prevents these from being lethal (most of the time). Mail, by and large, is probably going to be sewn directly to the underpadding so it doesn\u2019t just hang loose.<br /> <br /> Other than that, most armor worn by Oathsworn/nobility throughout the Hundred Kingdoms is going to be largely the same, and put on in much the same fashion. The heavier the armor, the more difficult it is to put on, hence why Oathsworn/nobles tend to have pages to help.<ol class=\"decimal\"><li>As a base layer, the warrior will don a loose shirt and trousers of linen or silk</li> <li>Over this, a tightly-wrapped sash of linen or silk is wound about the midsection, to give support to the torso. It is wrapped around itself with no knots</li> <li>The greaves are put on next, tied around the ankles and calves</li> <li>Next is the underpadding (lamellar) or mail-jacket.</li> <li>Around the waist go the thigh-guards, which are laced to the inside of the underpadding/jacket</li> <li>Next, the armored gloves and sleeves are donned. They are laced to the inside of the sleeves of the underpadding/jacket.</li> <li>If the warrior is wearing a heavy helmet, a padded shoulder/neck pad is worn. If not, skip this step</li> <li>Now the jacket is closed (through buttons, usually), or the lamellar vest is donned, and bound tight to the body with another sash. Through this sash is thrust the sword and dagger.</li> <li>Now comes the head protection. A coif may be donned, or a full helmet may be placed on the head. It is bound under the chin, while coifs are laced to the shoulders.</li> <li>Any extraneous armor, like pauldrons, poleynes, gorgets, face-masks,, crests, Coats-of-plates, etc are put on</li> <li>If the warrior has a tabard showing their personal heraldry or the heraldry of their lord, this is worn over the entire ensemble. Most of the time, these will be made of linen, but particularly-favored or wealthy retainers might wear silk.</li> </ol>Wealthy Oathsworn might very well own multiple sets of armor, a \u201cparade set\u201d of their finest pieces, and \u201cbattle sets\u201d of stuff they can accept being damaged.<br /> <br /> (For an idea of what the armor looks like, look up various Japanese armors.)<br /> <br /> --- Tactics:<br /> <br /> Oathsworn are, by and large, individual warriors, at-odds with the current means of warfare in the Hundred Kingdoms. Most fighters on a battlefield are going to be peasant Men-at-Arms, standing shoulder to shoulder in a tightly-packed formation, fighting in unison. Oathsworn, on the other hand, are probably going to be in a looser formation, as they require a bit more space to use their weapons to full-effectiveness. And, even when they do stand \u201cin formation\u201d, Oathsworn are almost always going to fight as individuals anyways, seeking out individual targets and fighting for their own honor and glory.<br /> <br /> The one exception is a full-on cavalry charge, which is a thing of blood-curdling beauty. Take several dozen heavily-armored warriors, put them on the backs of horses that weigh upwards of 1000 lbs, and pack them together tighter than sardines in a can. Now, point them in the general direction of the enemy.<br /> <br /> Contrary to popular knowledge, the Oathsworn charge isn\u2019t a full-on gallop. Only the last 50 or so yards are at a dead run, the rest is a trot or even a fast walk. The goal is to impact the enemy with maximum shock, with no gaps. Therefore, Oathsworn warhorses (and only warhorses. Horses untrained in this maneuver cannot be relied on) will charge literally shoulder-to-shoulder. Infantry will get run over and trampled underfoot, which is more than capable of killing the poor sap, especially when there are a couple more horses yet to step on them.<br /> <br /> Of course, the same goes for the Oathsworn who fall off their horse mid-charge (usually after getting hit by an arrow), or horses that get killed during the approach. Therefore, a heavy cavalry charge is reserved for the deciding point of a battle, not the opening stages. Infantry or light cavalry is usually used to sweep away missile troops.<br /> <br /> --- Arts and Etiquette<br /> <br /> Part of being an Oathsworn, or a noble in general, is a general appreciation for the arts and things <u>other</u> than battle. Someone who fixates wholly on warfare is generally viewed as a savage (which is one of the many reasons the Hill-Tribes are looked down upon, as they Hundred Kingdoms don\u2019t view their cultural aspects all that often, if at all).<br /> <br /> Traditionally, the \u201cnoble\u201d arts are calligraphy, poetry (spoken and written), musical skill (with the guzheng, the flute, or the drum) , painting, flower-arrangement, and dancing. Oathsworn, as nobility, are expected to at least be familiar, if not proficient, especially if they want to marry into their own social class. Other common pastimes include board-games, but they can be viewed as uncouth (especially cards and dice-gambling. UGH!)<br /> <br /> Also, as nobility, Oathsworn are expected to behave with \u201cdecorum suitable to one of their social status\u201d, aka not be an asshole. Nobility in general are expected to be rather stoic, with outward displays of emotion being looked down upon, and rudeness or crude language being <u>highly</u> discouraged. Hence, why most nobles won\u2019t react all that much when a peasant gets uppity, even though it is within their rights to kill them. Using proper diction, grammar, and honorifics are <u>very</u> important when it comes to courtly life, and the system of when to give respect, and <i>how much</i>, can be quite confusing. How deep one is supposed to bow, and how long, is dependent on the relative social status between two nobles.<br /> <br /> --- Oaths:<br /> <br /> Here we come to the meat of the matter. What makes Oathsworn, Oathsworn.<br /> <br /> When Oathsworn are made to stand vigil (again, traditionally in an Immaculate Temple for a full night and day), they are required to swear a number of oaths to as many witnesses as there are present (usually, these witnesses include their master Oathsworn and an Immaculate monk). The exact wording and the number of oaths vary among individuals, but the general gist is:<br /> <br /> Oath of Honor: I will bear myself with the dignity according to my station. I will treat others according to this dignity, and the dignity they deserve. I will not undertake any action that will bring me dishonor (aka fail to uphold an Oath, rob/steal/cheat, lie, commit a crime), and will not tolerate dishonor among my peers and servants. I will honor the gods and the Immaculate Faith.<br /> <br /> Oath of Compassion: I will protect the innocent. It is the duty of the strong to oppose those who threaten the weak. Through my own actions, I will not bring others to harm, or through my own inaction, allow others to come to harm. I will treat all with dignity and respect, and help whenever possible. I will punish the wicked.<br /> <br /> Oath of Loyalty: My word is my bond and my sacred honor. To break my word is to bring dishonor on myself. I will uphold my promises, to my superiors, to my peers, to my servants and to myself. I will work within the bounds of the law to bring the wicked to task, not subvert it for my own ends.<br /> <br /> So on and so forth. Generally, the \u201cbig 4\u201d standards one is trying to uphold when swearing an Oath is:<ol class=\"decimal\"><li>Protect Innocent</li> <li>Punish Wicked</li> <li>Uphold law and order</li> <li>Maintain Honor</li> </ol>Now, as you can see, most Oathsworn do not uphold the intent of their Oaths. Many do not try. Kasai does, but Kasai is an idealist in a pessimists world.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11892,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","user":"Boston123","date":". 03-30-2017, 10:35 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-03-31T05:05:41-07:00","id":1071191,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Boston123</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1070979#post1070979\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You know, if I spent as much time actually working on my Quest Thread as I did gleefully writing world-building lore, I would probably be done by now\u2026\u2026..</div> </div> </div> </div>Hey, you said it, not me. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/tongue.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Stick Out Tongue\" smilieid=\"5\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> That said, I do appreciate the additional context.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>On the frontier of the Wild South, there's only one woman with the grit to take on its most dangerous outlaws and bring them <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/984977-back-alive-or-maybe-dead-quest\" target=\"_blank\">Back Alive, or Maybe Dead.</a><br /> <br /> Avatar by <a href=\"http://puzzlinghappenstance.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">K.S. Brenowitz</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/908-semicasual","name":"semicasual","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar908_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2499"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-04T20:36:48-07:00","id":1073537,"title":"","content":"The decision is almost literally unanimous: get Aketchi to safety, order the armsmen to prepare, and identify yourself and you status to the assailants. You know...... in hindsight, that wasn't &quot;really&quot; a good decision point, and I apologize.<br /> <br /> Note: in response to what Semicasual stated: Kasai is nominally an Immaculate, at least if someone asks. He can tell the names of the Immaculate and Elemental Dragons, knows how to properly burn a prayer-strip, so on and so forth.<br /> <br /> He isn\u2019t very devout, due to the fact that the Immaculate Faith <i>technically</i> preaches that his ancestors, and by proxy, he himself, were morally/philosophically lacking, resulting in their mutations. He might go to a prayer-session every once in a while, but not every week, and doesn\u2019t put much stock in the Immaculate-upheld reincarnation-thing.<br /> <br /> When he lived among the Hill-Tribes, he was exposed to several aspects of their religion, mainly during festivals and feasts. Mainly animism/totemism, but nothing too extreme.<br /> <br /> When he lived at home with his father, he participated in limited ancestor-reverence; making minor offerings in a small cubbyhole shrine, acknowledgement of their deeds, etc.<br /> <br /> All in all, he certainly <i>recognizes</i> the power of the gods/spirits, but doesn\u2019t actually <u>worship</u> any with any sense of regularity. He does token things, like attend Immaculate prayer-sessions, make offerings to road-and-river deities when he travels, so on and so forth, but if he ever actually <u>met</u> a deity, he would probably metaphorically soil himself.<br /> <br /> -Authors Note: Jesus H Christ, this chapter was a goddamn bear. I had it done on FRIDAY, but I just\u2026\u2026 couldn\u2019t\u2026..get it right! The \u201cdialogue\u201d sounded perfectly fine in my head, but when putting it down on paper (as it were) it just kept on \u201csounding\u201d worse and worse, until I either had to just post it or delete it. To avoid another three week break between postings, I just finished it, \u201csound\u201d be damned, but I don\u2019t like how this one came out.<br /> <br /> Head &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; wall<br /> --------------------------------------- <br /> <br /> Cursing under your breath, you reach out to Aketchi.<br /> <br /> \u201c\u2026. Master Aketchi, can you get something for me from the wagon? It is <i>important</i>.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c What? Don\u2019t we have people f..\u201d<br /> <br /> Suddenly cut off by you grabbing and pushing him in the direction of the wagon. While it might not be big, the wooden siding might stop an arrow.<br /> <br /> As a result of Aketchi\u2019s protestations, the armsmen look up from where they are idling about. Realizing what you are looking at, they drop what they are doing almost as a man, grabbing weapons and shields. One manhandles Aketchi under the wagon (again, ignoring whatever protestations he brings forth), while the others form up tight around you.<br /> <br /> Stretching out your arms, to show you are unarmed (as unarmed as you can be, at least), you call out, \u201cCome, friends, come out into the light and sit by our fire. Dinner is almost ready, and the night air is chilled!\u201d<br /> <br /> There is no response, and you feel the hair on the back of your neck lift, imagining an untold amount of arrows being drawn and pointed directly at you. The armsmen shift uneasily, fingers tightening around spearshafts, and the air about the camp is thick with tension.<br /> <br /> Adding, \u201c Just as forewarning, this party is under the official protection of the Lord Xia, of the Hundr\u2026\u201d<br /> <br /> Suddenly, a figure slides down the face of the gully, bring with them a torrent of stones and dirt. As they stand up, they reveal themselves as one of the Hill-Tribes, bedecked in bangles, warpaint and a bobbing headdress, which upon further inspection appears to be porcupine quills interspersed through his hair.<br /> <br /> The warrior strides up to you, swaggering with every step. He comes up to <i>well</i> within your comfort zone, close enough to see the pores of his skin and smell the alcohol on his breath. You don\u2019t dare step back; showing weakness could be lethal.<br /> <br /> He grins, \u201cYour fire is <i>ours</i>, outlander. Food too. Horses <i>und</i> cart <i>und </i>tents. Weapons and clothes. Why we share?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt;Well, due to the fact that we are an official envoy, on a diplomatic mission, you <i>just might</i> not want to attack us. That could be a bad idea, not just for you but for your people as a whole.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> He is either inexperienced or drunk enough to be surprised at your fluency in Forest-tongue, stepping back (thank the gods!) and giving you the momentum in the conversation.<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt;\u2026. What do you mean, a \u2018bad idea\u2019? We don\u2019t care about what titles you lowlanders call yourselves, anyways!&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt; You might not, but your chief most certainly would. That is why you are out here, being a general nuisance, is it not? You are trying to win enough fame to be worthy in the eyes of your chieftain&gt;.\u201d You lean forward, adding emphasis,\u201d&lt; Now, how do you think they will view you being the cause of widespread warfare? Sure, you <i>might</i> win some honor for bringing back my scalp, but when my chief strikes your village with all the fury of a storm, will your chief be happy then? I think not.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> He snarls, the porcupine quills spreading out in ire. \u201c&lt; Who said anything about word getting back to my chief? If my crew and I put you under the knife right now and leave your corpses on this mountainside, none of that will matter!&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt; Oh of course, of course\u2026\u2026. But then you wouldn\u2019t get respect for the killing, would you? Are you and your boys ready to bleed for no reward? If I were in your place, I wouldn\u2019t be.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> Clasping your hands behind your back, you lean back, in order to let some of the metaphorical pressure off the lad. \u201c&lt; Now, on the other hand, if your chief were to find out that you met and escorted the diplomatic envoy of the Lord Xia to their destination, they would have every reason to shower you with honors, correct? <i>Especially</i> if they were to hear about how extraordinarily helpful you were\u2026..&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> His lip curls in a sneer,\u201d&lt; I would rather die than help the ancestral oppressors of my people, but nice try.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt;Well, to be honest, if you decide to attack us, that might very well happen. Asides from my party being willing and able to defend ourselves, my grandfather might not be too happy with you. You might have heard of him.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> Still sneering, \u201c&lt; Why should I care about what some wrinkled old ballsack thinks and feels?&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> Your eyebrow raises. \u201c&lt; I am sure if you call him a \u2013ahem- \u201cwrinkled old ballsack\u201d, he might not find it as amusing as I do. What tribe are you of? Not that it matters, <i>everyone</i> in the Confederacy has heard of him.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> Proudly, \u201d&lt; I have the honor of being of the Nemasket, outlander. Perhaps you soft weaklings know of our great chieftain, the mighty Ousamequin? The \u201cBeast of Baotsu?\u201d. Does his name strike terror into your heart?&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> To his surprise (and to the surprise of the armsmen and probably everyone watching), you instead start laughing almost hysterically, bent double at the waist. When you get your breath back, you stand back up and brush the tears from your eyes.<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt; Well, that solves that problem. You see; Ousamequin <i>is</i> my grandfather. I doubt he would take kindly to one of his warriors killing his relative.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> The warrior snorts in response. \u201c&lt; And I am the descendant of Tsenamatoric himself! Do you honestly expect me to believe that?&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> You shrug, \u201c&lt; Believe me or not. I just wouldn\u2019t want to be you when you show my scalp to him.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> Standing up straight, \u201c&lt; The way I see it; you\u2019ve got two choices. Help us in our travel to Ousamequin, and be lauded for the effort. Or, attack us, and quite possibly receive nothing besides blood and death.&gt;\u201d Spreading your hands, \u201c&lt; Your choice.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> He stares at you for a moment, then snarls and whirls about. Stalking back over to the embankment, he talks quickly with his warband for a few moments. After shouting down several objections, he comes back, eyes dark with anger and quills on full display.<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt; Here is the deal, outlander. We won\u2019t kill you, not yet. Instead, we will bring you before Ousamequin to learn the truth from your lies. When that happens, <i>I will put you under the knife myself</i>.&gt;\u201d. He smirks, \u201c&lt; Don\u2019t make me wait too long.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> Shrugging, \u201c&lt; Fair enough, I suppose. Although I must ask; there were two of my men on watch. Did you kill them?&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> He laughs coldly, not bothering to answer, but climbing up the embankment.<br /> <br /> After they are gone (from sight at least), you relax, sighing. If this can be taken as a sign of how you will be received\u2026\u2026.Hopefully, your grandfather will be more amenable.<br /> <br /> The sergeant in charge of the armsmen squad turns to you, \u201cIf I may ask, my Lord, what just happened? What about the two men on watch?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cStupid nonsensical bullshit just happened, sergeant.\u201d You reply bitterly. \u201cAs for the men, I would not think too much about them. It is probably better that way.\u201d<br /> <br /> His eyes flash, \u201cYou are telling me the bastards attacked a diplomatic envoy?!\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cYes, sergeant. And there is not all that much we can do about it, either, except for bringing it up to their chieftain. Which, <i>believe me</i>, I plan on doing.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cFrom now on, all watches will be held inside the camp perimeter. I would like to think they would be smarter than to attack us so blatantly, but then again, these are teenagers we are talking about. If one of us strays too far from the group, they might snag them.\u201d Raising your voice, you address the party. \u201cDoes everyone understand that? Do not go <i>anywhere</i> without either Sergeant Cheng or myself aware of your whereabouts.\u201d<br /> <br /> Everyone nods their assent, wide-eyed at the implications. After a few moments, you dismiss the group, who awkwardly go back to preparing dinner. You yourself go rummage through the contents of the cart, searching for your armor. If the reception and the escort are going to be like this, you want something rather solid covering your shoulder blades.<br /> <br /> In the morning, there is no sign of any watching Hill-Tribe warriors, although you are sure they must be present, and the atmosphere in the camp is heavy with tension. The group is silent as they break down the camp and ready for travel.<br /> The gloom sticks around for the next several days, as your party makes its way further and further up the trail. The terrain, if possible, gets even rougher, and at several points when navigating a particularly-technical section of the passage you look up to see a Hill-Tribe warrior watching your progress. They do point out the correct path when you come to forks, however, which is undeniably helpful, if only slightly.<br /> <br /> Suddenly, relatively speaking, your party rises over a crest in the trail, only to be greeted by the sight of one of the steep-sided alpine valleys the Hill-Tribes usually live in. The trail zig-zags back and forth down the slope of the mountain, and as you follow it down into the treeline, you start to dimly recognize landmarks here and there. That boulder was where you successfully hunted your first deer (and, unknown to anyone else, had to chase the damn thing across half the valley. You learned to make the shot \u201ccount\u201d after that!), the grove of scraggly trees where your grandfather taught you to live off the land, etc etc etc.<br /> <br /> Close to the bottom of the valley, you start to pass first pastures, then garden-terraces, thick with tribal laborers. The workers stare at you with barely-disguised suspicion, talking in low sullen tones.<br /> <br /> As you follow the path on, it gradually becomes wider (though you certainly wouldn\u2019t call it a road), and the terraces get wider and lusher closer to the valley-bottom. Eventually, you find yourself standing before the gates of your grandfathers home settlement.<br /> <br /> Passing through the gates, you can\u2019t quite help but notice just how\u2026.. <i>dilapidated</i> everything looks. The walls of the lodges are crumbling, the thatch and bark roofs are in serious need of repair. <i>Everything</i> stinks of shit, a phenomena that was rare in even the rudest of peasant villages back in the Hundred Kingdoms. You don\u2019t remember any of this!<br /> <br /> Finally, you find yourself in front of the longhouse, the central structure of the settlement. Around the door are clustered the young members of the warband. Now, in the light of day, their warpaint and bluster makes them seem rather laughable instead of the intimidating figures they cut a few mere days ago. The leader bows mockingly, gesturing to the door with one hand on his beltknife.<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt;After you, <i>your Lordship.&gt;\u201d</i><br /> <br /> Dismounting, you gesture for the armsmen to form up around you and lift the chest containing the tribute-silver from the bottom of the cart. The warband-leader\u2019s eyes bulge when he catches a glimpse of the chest, and how much the armsmen struggle to carry it.<br /> <br /> Pushing the door open, you stride purposefully into the hall. The building is long and narrow, with benches on either side of long hearths, and the air inside is hazy with woodsmoke and the smells of cooking food. People bustle back and forth, tending to haunches of meat and bubbling pots suspended on hooks, and you all-but have to push your way through the throng.<br /> <br /> At the far end of the hall, lounging between the high-seat pillars and flanked by his Chosen warriors, sits your grandfather. Even at this distance, he cuts an imposing figure: taller than you are, with arms and legs like tree trunks. Gold, silver and bronze glitter on throat, arm and chest, and his white hair only makes him look more distinguished.<br /> <br /> When you would ordinarily kneel before him, you instead throw back the edge of your coif, and his eyes widen in sudden recognition. In an instant, you find yourself swept up in a crushing embrace that drives the breath from your body.<br /> <br /> After a moment, he stands back, holding you at arm\u2019s length and looks you up and down.<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt; My gods, lad, you look such like your mother. And so tall and strong!&gt;\u201d (Is he tearing up?)<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt;I was <i>15</i>, grandfather, I think I was allowed to be a bit gangly. It <i>is</i> good to see you, after so long. For a second, I thought you didn\u2019t recognize me!&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt; I almost didn\u2019t! That just means you should come visit more often, hah!&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> You grin, but it fades, \u201c&lt; I\u2019m afraid this isn\u2019t entirely a heartfelt reunion, grandfather. I am here represe..&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> He waves you off, turning back to the high-seat, \u201c&lt; Yes, yes, I was told an envoy was coming to see me. If I had known they would be <i>family</i>, I would have brought out the good stuff! No time for business, not yet. For now, we will eat until our guts rumble, and our asses ache from overuse!&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> And so you do, long into the night. You feast (Until you soberly realize that this must be the last of the settlement\u2019s winter stores) on roast game and vegetable dishes, enough to make the trestle-tables groan underneath their weight, and enough liquor flows to coax even Aketchi out from his self-conscious shell. The language barrier is anything but: when you have several shots of corn moonshine resting in your gut, every word tends to come out as the same nonsense. As you eat, you take the time to catch up with your grandfather, trading tales of glory (the old bastard is doing extraordinarily well for someone of 66) and reminiscing about old times.<br /> <br /> It is in the small hours of the morning when you actually get down to what you trekked out here for (although meeting your only other relative was a definite plus). While the rest of the longhall sleeps in a jumbled pile on the benches, you fish out the documents from Lord Iysen, and your grandfather lights a pipe filled with tobacco.<br /> <br /> As you open the chest, he leans over your shoulder (you aren\u2019t used to people being taller than you) and catches a glimpse of the silver stashed therein, whistling in response.<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt; Gods be damned, Kasai. Something tells me this isn\u2019t just a \u2018how do you do\u2019 meeting.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt;No, grandfather, I am afraid not.&gt;\u201d. as you pass over the envelope of missives.<br /> <br /> He glances at the seal stamped into the wax, and then tears it open, pulling out the folded papers. Pulling out the actual communique, he starts reading it, only to mutter a curse when he gets to the actual meat of the letter. Scanning the offers causes him to sit down suddenly and pull the pipe from his mouth.<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt; Kasai, are\u2026.. are these offers <i>genuine</i>?&gt;\u201d<br /> Nodding, \u201c&lt; Yes.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> <br /> \u201c&lt;How can you be so sure?&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt;I was the one who suggested them. The words are hers, but the ideas were mine.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> He chuckles at that, \u201c&lt; Look at you, moving up in the world&gt;\u201d. More seriously, scanning the missive, \u201c&lt; This Lord Iysen, she trusts you?&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt; I would hope so, I saved her life, and that of her dynasty. She thinks enough of my opinion to <i>ask</i> me what I thought the best method of approach was, as opposed to <i>ordering</i> me.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt;Do you trust her?&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> You hesitate, \u201c&lt; Of all the nobles I\u2019ve seen so far in my travels through the Hundred Kingdoms, she seems to be one of the least shit. Not that she is perfect, but I can serve her and uphold my Oaths at the same time, which is a definite plus. I\u2026.. I think she respects that, my intention to uphold the Oaths. So\u2026.. yes, I suppose so.?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cSo, she will uphold her end of the bargain?&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cAs long as you keep her son safe, yes.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> His eyebrow raises, \u201c&lt; I was wondering who the kid was. Couldn\u2019t be yours, he doesn\u2019t have your coloring, and too old to boot. Nor any of the women in your band.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> You flush at that, but continue, \u201c&lt;Yes, the Lord Iysen offers her only son as a hostage, to ensure her continued good behavior. That, plus the offers of land and tribute are for <u>neutrality</u>, mind you. If the Confederation were to actually help in the fighting, then any land taken from Yue would be given to the Tribes as well.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> He shakes his head, the bangles in his braids ringing softly. \u201c&lt; Kasai, it is going to be difficult enough to get the Tribes to agree to <i>non-aggression</i>, much less an alliance.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> He stares sourly, pondering the politics of the Tribes. \u201c&lt; The Nemasket will agree, after some arguing and chest-thumping for show. I know my elders, and they will jump at this chance. Too many sons and daughters are land-hungry, and our territory is too small to support us. The fact that the offer is coming from a son of the tribe helps a <i>lot</i>.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> After a moment, \u201c&lt; And where the Nemasket go, the Assawompsset and the Nasketucket follow, such is the way of things. No&gt;\u201d, he says, standing up, \u201c&lt; The issue is the nine other tribes.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt; The Neponset are not going to want to cut a deal with you <i>at all</i>, not without some heavy ego-stroking and substantial tribute. Their elder, Iyannough, is an asshole through and through. He won his chieftaincy and his eldership through leading raids on Xia, and will probably view any sort of nonaggression pact with you as an assault on his personal legacy.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt;Same thing with the Assonet and the Mattepoisett, who are the \u2018younger brothers\u2019 of the Neponset. Their chiefs and elders are pretty intimidated by Iyannough, and will almost certainly support him.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> He sighs, staring morosely into the embers of the fire, \u201c&lt; The other 6 tribes are all up in the air. They both raid and trade the Hundred Kingdoms, but not enough to build up either grudges or relationships. On the other hand, they are just as hungry for good land as we are, so the offer might be interesting enough for them to take a bite\u2026\u2026&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> He shrugs,\u201d&lt; I dunno, Kasai. I just hope you have a silver tongue, and never get into politics. Killing men is easier; at least they are honest about their intentions. With that, I suggest you try to get as much sleep as you can, we have a few days of hard travel before we reach the Council site.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> You interrupt him as he turns, \u201c&lt; Grandfather, before you go to bed, I have to tell you something.&gt;\u201d At his raised eyebrow, \u201cMy diplomatic party was\u2026\u2026. well, we were attacked on the way here by a youth-led band. They killed two of the guards. My Lord is going to ask where the two men went\u2026\u2026.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> He growls, pulling back his lips to reveal his elongated fangs.\u201d&lt; I have an idea of who you are talking about. He is a bit of a shithead, always talking up himself around the village. I\u2019ve tolerated his nonsense for too long; attacking an official envoy is too far! Don\u2019t worry, your lord will be\u2026. <i>Mollified</i>. I\u2019ll see to it myself.&gt;\u201d, and stalks to his section of the sleeping-bench.<br /> <br /> It seems like it has only been a few hours (and it probably has) since you laid your head down on the bench (and you are confused as to how you slept on the benches when you were a kid. You aren\u2019t exactly old, but the wood/straw/furs made your back ache!), and you wake up to the morning light with a pounding headache.<br /> <br /> After dressing and leaving the longhall, you come upon the grisly sight of the principle leaders of the warband suspended from long spears in the center of the village, pointedly ignored by the tribespeople walking around. Your grandfather is watching them swing in the breeze, hands on hips, and as you get closer you can\u2019t help but notice his arms are red to the elbow.<br /> <br /> He looks over his shoulder, catching a glimpse of you approaching, and turns to address you.<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt;Good morning, Kasai. As you can see, I\u2019ve taken care of the troublemakers for you.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt;Wh\u2026.. what did you do to them?&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> He shrugs, \u201c&lt; Dragged them from their beds, removed their tribal and clan membership, had my Chosen break their legs and arms, then scalped them. By the time we return from the Elder Council, the scalps will be tanned, and your supporters will be avenged. Will that be good enough for your Lord, to avoid a diplomatic incident?&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> Blanching, \u201c&lt; I\u2026.. I <i>think</i> so, grandfather.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> He nods, \u201c&lt; Good. Are you ready to travel?&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt;About ready, but it will take some time to form the party up, an\u2026.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt;No, no, it won\u2019t be your entire party. Just you, me, and some Chosen to carry the tribute. The Elder council will take offense to any people not of the Tribes attending a council.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> He sighs, \u201c&lt; And, I am sorry Kasai, but you are going to have to be blindfolded. We can\u2019t have the risk of the lowlanders knowing where our government meets.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> Frowning, \u201c&lt; How am I going to be able to travel if I can\u2019t see where I am going?&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt;Don\u2019t worry, I won\u2019t let my only kin take a nosedive off a cliff.&gt;\u201d, he grins. \u201c&lt; I recommend we head out as soon as possible, these next few days are going to be hard going.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> After a few minutes of packing up (mainly involving leaving behind non-essential gear), you bid goodbye to your party and head out. On the trail, surrounded by stolid Chosen warriors in bronze and iron, your grandfather covers your eyes with a wrapping, preventing you from seeing your surroundings or even the trail in front of you.<br /> <br /> And so, you walk. On the flat-ish trails, you are able to be led by a hand on your shoulder, but then the going gets tough, your grandfather lifts you up (how?! You are 6 feet tall and weigh 12 stone!) and carries you bodily over a shoulder.<br /> <br /> By the passage of light and dark through the cloth over your eyes, you count up to 3 days before losing count. The steps blend together in a numbing monotony, but over time, you are able to sense you are steadily travelling upwards.<br /> <br /> Suddenly, almost in the middle of a step, the blindfold is removed, and light floods your vision. Rubbing the tears from your eyes, you look around. All around you, the mountains scrape the clouds like spears, their lower flanks saw-toothed with evergreens. In front of you, in a saddle between two outstretched ridges, are gathered the elders and chieftains of the Dumoniapoccasset Confederation. There, reflecting the light of a blazing bonfire, some look on you with various degrees of interest, while others don\u2019t even bother to hide their anger or suspicion.<br /> <br /> Your grandfather gently pushes you forward, into the flat section of ground around the bonfire, then goes to take his seat around the edge of the circle.<br /> <br /> One of the elders steps forwards, bearing a long staff decorated with beads and feathers. He bows low, then states somberly,<br /> <br /> \u201cWe, the elders who speak for the Dumoniapoccasset, welcome you, diplomat of the realm of Xia. Will you require a translator?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt; No, revered elder, but thank you.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> The spokesman nods in acknowledgement, then turns to address the gathered representatives.<br /> <br /> \u201cFellow elders, chosen chieftains, we gather here today to hear the words of the representative of Xia, in the aim of attaining a satisfactory result if possible for both sides. If anyone is unable to keep the peace of our Confederation, please excuse yourselves.&gt;\u201d One of the elders snorts, crossing his arms, but stays where he is. This must be Iyannough.<br /> <br /> Upon the gesture of the spokesman, you step forward and bow deeply towards the entire circle.<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt; Good evening, revered elders. My name is Kasai Harutane, and I am here as the duly-recognized diplomat of the Lord Xia, Lady Iysen Shiba. Tonight, I seek to broker a deal between our peoples, so that both of us may prosper.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> Gesturing to the Chosen warriors carrying the chest of silver, you pull out the missive from inside your coat. Upon setting the chest down, they open it so the light of the fire reflects on the lustrous tribute within. Many of the chiefs and elders lean forward for a better look.<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt; It is my honor to present to this council a tribute of 25 Dirhams of silver. For those not aware, a dirham is worth about 400 dinar coins, and a single dinar is the approximate value of the output of a lowland peasant farm for a month. This means, as long as my calculations are correct, in that chest there is about 2 weeks wages for every single person in the Confederation, every man, woman and child. That money is <i>yours</i>, regardless of the outcome of our deliberations, as a gesture of goodwill and respect from my people to yours.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> The asshole from earlier laughs, leaning forwards, \u201c&lt; Hear that, brothers and sisters, the lowlanders think to distract us with pretty baubles and trinkets, in effort to keep us away! As for I; I know how the saying goes. \u2018Where there is smoke, there is fire\u2019, and if the bitch-queen has this money to throw away, there must be even more held in reserve!&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> To your consternation, several of the elders nod and murmur in assent.<br /> To regain control of the situation, you continue, unfolding the missive as you do so,<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt;The gift of tribute is not all that is being offered, elders. Long have the Hundred Kingdoms held both a healthy sense of respect and yes, fear, for the prowess of the warriors of the Tribes. My Lord has a deep respect for the history and accomplishments of your people, learning the names and characteristics of each of the tribes, in order to fully understand how to most successfully approach you.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> Taking a deep breath, \u201c&lt; With that in mind, the Lord Xia seeks to negotiate a non-aggression pact with the Tribes, in light of the current war building in the lowland realms. To that end, here is what she is prepared to offer:<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt; The Hill-Tribes have riches aplenty; gold and silver, wool and wood, but if the goods cannot get to market, they serve no greater purpose, and the children cannot eat gold and wool when the winds of winter swirl about the door! To that end, the Lord Xia offers market benefits to traders and merchants of Tribal descent (You\u2026.. didn\u2019t know about this beforehand, only after reading the missive in your grandfathers hall), and will pay to build roads and bridges into tribal lands so that the goods can pass back between low and high-landers.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt; The Lord Xia also recognizes that the Hill-Tribes have been mistreated, marginalized and brutalized by her ancestors, forced into the hinterlands to starve. Contrary to expectations, the Tribes possess tougher souls, and have survived to the present day. In a gesture of friendship, the Lord Xia offers all hill-country in the lands held by her, to be forever ceded to the Hill-tribes. You would rule over the lands yourselves, with no interference by the lowland noble families, due to recognizance of raids and opposition from their warparties, and be free to practice your traditional ways of life, so long as her people are unharmed. A sense of friendship and camaraderie is sought by my Lord, and by recognizing the suffering and desires of your people, she seeks to grow relations.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt; Finally, in order to show that she is bargaining in good faith, the Lord Xia has offered up her young son, her <i>only</i> son, Aketchi Shiba, as a hostage. So long as the non-aggression pact is in place, the Master Aketchi will be fostered among the Tribes, in order to serve as a check on the Lord Xia\u2019s behavior, as well as to expose the boy to the finer aspects of Hill-Tribe culture. &gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> There is a moment of silence, then the assembled elders and chiefs burst into an explosion of noise, hand-waving and emotion. There are several angry shouts, but most of the noise appears to be questions aimed in your direction. The spokesman raises his hands, and gradually the circle grows quiet.<br /> <br /> He gestures to your grandfather, who has risen to his feet, \u201c Yes, Ousamequin, Warchief of the Confederation, and the Nemasket?&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> Your grandfather nods, and as he passes you to reach the center of the circle, he squeezes your shoulder reassuringly.<br /> \u201cCouncilmembers, let me get straight to the point. Yes, this confederation was formed several hundred years ago in order to more effectively resist the Vanehani invaders. You know this, but one thing many of us forget is that this council was formed in order to <i>keep peace</i>. Ensure that the tribes remain strong and united, in the face of outside aggression. In light of that\u2026. Have we done what we set out to do?&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt; As warchief of this confederation, I spend more time sorting out and preventing, or really, <i>attempting</i> to prevent, raids and intertribal squabbles, than I do leading our warbands into glorious victory against our ancestral enemies. Why is this? Some of you (he looks sideglance at Iyannough, who snarls in response) know: We do not have enough of <i>anything</i> to go around. Even elder daughters in the clans are not inheriting the land they need to keep the people fed, and our sons do not have enough lands to hunt upon without trodding on each other\u2019s feet. There is not enough metal, not enough water, not enough wood, not enough <i>land</i>\u00b8 to support our people. Accepting this deal would give enough for even the youngest daughter to grow her corn, and every young son to build a hall for himself and his family. What do we have to gain, and what do we have to lose?&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> Iyannough stands up at this, \u201c&lt; What do we have to gain? Nothing we don\u2019t already have, or can\u2019t take by force of arms! You would make our people lapdogs to the lowland invaders for, what, some square miles of earth and stone? Would you make us <i>slaves</i>?&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt;What are we <i>now</i>, elder Iyannough? The lowlanders already think of us as savage and primitive barbarians, and there is little we can do to change that. One of the few things we <i>can</i> do is not act like ravenous beasts!&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> Iyannough laughs, \u201c&lt; Strange words for a <i>warchief</i>. Are you a wolf or a sheep?&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> Your grandfather snarls, baring his fangs at the insult. \u201c&lt; Would you say that to me if you were down here, face-to-face?! I think not! I have led our people for many years, with much honor and glory to m\u2026&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> He is about to say more, but the spokesman steps forward and slams the end of his staff on the turf. With that, your grandfather abruptly shuts up, and with a final glare at Iyannough, goes back to his spot. One of the other elders stands up,<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt; For what it is worth, you carry yourself well, although I must ask: who are you, and why should we listen to you? Both the warchief Ousamequin and the elder Iyannough are well known, lauded and respected. So far, all you have are the words of a distant figure, whom, might I add, has waged war against us in the past.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> You stop, then say:<ol class=\"decimal\"><li>I am Oathsworn. Do you know what that means? I will swear an Oath here and now, if that is what it takes\u2026\u2026</li> <li>I am the hero of Teriake Castle. Surely you must have heard about the conflagration? If so, you know I have the interests of the people at heart.</li> <li>I am the scion of both the Tribes and the Vanehani. If anything, my ancestry shows that our peoples can work together!</li> <li>\u2026. What worthy task do you need done?</li> <li>Something else?</li> </ol>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-04T21:16:21-07:00","id":1073555,"title":"","content":"Scion followed by teriyaki followed by oathsworn. It's like a resume.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11567-jairain","name":"Jairain","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11567_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"345"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-04T21:52:21-07:00","id":1073568,"title":"","content":"I am Kasai, scion of both the Tribes and the Vanehani, son of a minor lord and grandson of Ousamequin. I know the ways of both, having lived in the Hills for years before going to my father's house. I have been hated, cursed and spit on there, for my Hill blood, as I am despised here for coming from the Lowlands. But I am Oathsworn, and can put these hates aside. Can you? Xia seeks peace and trade, knowing friendship will follow. This tribute is for now; real wealth is in the future, for your children and grandchildren.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Like my Avatar? Courtesy of<span style=\"color:#0000FF\"> Jen</span>! : <span style=\"font-size:12px\">Anybody want their characters to be experimented on ? </span>post 98<br /> <i>An Exalt is never unarmed.</i>","edited":{"userid":4018,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4018-l-het-esh","user":"L'het'esh","date":".  Kasai 04-05-2017, 03:09 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4018-l-het-esh","name":"L'het'esh","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4018_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"521"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-05T05:13:39-07:00","id":1073673,"title":"","content":"&quot;<span style=\"font-family:inherit\">What worthy task do you need done?&quot;<br /> <br /> Always, always, <i>always </i> go for the option that presents an epic journey/sidequest!<br /> <br /> (I don't think we'll be able to persuade </span>Iyannough regardless of what we do. He appears to be spinning this as a sign of weakness - &quot;hey, look at these stupid fat sheep, trying to bribe us not to kill them and take their stuff!&quot; We can't overcome that by waving our credentials around. But we might be able to sway the others with a demonstration of commitment.<br /> <br /> And if that doesn't work, maybe we can try challenging Iyannough to a duel.)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>On the frontier of the Wild South, there's only one woman with the grit to take on its most dangerous outlaws and bring them <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/984977-back-alive-or-maybe-dead-quest\" target=\"_blank\">Back Alive, or Maybe Dead.</a><br /> <br /> Avatar by <a href=\"http://puzzlinghappenstance.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">K.S. Brenowitz</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/908-semicasual","name":"semicasual","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar908_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2499"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-05T17:19:21-07:00","id":1073934,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>semicasual</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1073673#post1073673\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">&quot;<span style=\"font-family:inherit\">What worthy task do you need done?&quot;<br /> <br /> Always, always, <i>always </i> go for the option that presents an epic journey/sidequest!<br /> <br /> (I don't think we'll be able to persuade </span>Iyannough regardless of what we do. He appears to be spinning this as a sign of weakness - &quot;hey, look at these stupid fat sheep, trying to bribe us not to kill them and take their stuff!&quot; We can't overcome that by waving our credentials around. But we might be able to sway the others with a demonstration of commitment.<br /> <br /> And if that doesn't work, maybe we can try challenging Iyannough to a duel.)</div> </div> </div> </div>Exactly.<br /> <br /> Iyannough, as already stated, earned both his earlier chieftaincy and his current elder-dom by cashing influence he earned by, basically, being an antisocial git. While he was/is most famous for leading several very successful raids on Xia and other realms, he also &quot;spread the love&quot; among the other tribes. While he is amazingly popular in his own tribe (mainly because he gives a loot of booty away and throws some kickass feasts, both hallmarks of successful warleaders among the Tribes), the other tribes aren't so happy.<br /> <br /> Therefore, almost all of his social status is based on warfare. He would view peace between the Tribes themselves as a bad idea, much less peace between the Confederation and the Hundred Kingdoms.<br /> <br /> However, he is only one of two elders representing the Neponset, and there are 4 other elders representing the Assonet and the Mattepoisett, not to mention the chiefs for all three tribes. While he has all of them pretty firmly under his thumb (due to both respect and fear), they <i>technically</i> can vote however they want. He would almost certainly use his influence on his tribes' warriors to retaliate if they go against his wishes, however.<br /> <br /> Another important thing to recognize is the the Warchief of the Neponset is present (all the Warchief's are; they are here to present their points-of-view concerning warfare, strategy, logistics, etc to the Elder council, they just don't get to vote.), and <i>technically</i> he controls the warriors of Neponset, not Iyannough. Iyannough just has an asston of influence (&quot;hey, you boys should go hit the Assonet!&quot;-kinda thing), but he can't &quot;legally&quot; organize a warparty or lead warriors in battle. If we win the loyalty and respect of Neponset's warchief, there isn't much Iyannough can do about it. He can vote for war all he wants, but if the warriors refuse to fight (because they all left to settle the lowlands)..........<br /> <br /> Finally, as Warchief of the Confederation, our grandfather gets to vote. That means at least 3 certainly in favor of non-aggression/alliance.<br /> <br /> There are 25 possible votes, and we &quot;have&quot; at least 3 (from the elders of the Nemasket and our grandpappy). The elders of the Assawompsett and the Nasketucket traditionally follow the direction of the Nemasket, so there are 4 more votes &quot;on the fence&quot;, but leaning toward positive. There is at least one certain negative vote, with 5 more &quot;on the fence&quot;, but leaning toward negative. There are 12 votes that are completely up in the air, and depend on our actions here.<br /> <br /> As a final note: the decision made by this council could very well tear the Confederacy apart.<br /> <br /> As for duelling Iyannough..... it almost certainly isn't going to happen. The Tribes don't really have a dueling &quot;culture&quot;: while duels certainly happen, they aren't codified, and resemble muggings and beat-downs as opposed to the refined bouts the Hundred Kingdoms have. Most internecine affairs of honor in the tribes result in blood feuds between entire families and clans, not individuals, and are usually settled by the chief/elder stepping in and on the necks of those involved.<br /> <br /> Finally, Iyannough just plain isn't capable of fighting. He was....... 30 or even 20 years ago, not today. He isn't our grandpappy (our grandfather has a very special reason why he is so..... vigorous, for a lack of a better term, an explicitly-supernatural reason. Although, he is still 'naturally&quot; fit.), and he is an old man. His capabilities are social, not physical.<br /> <br /> Besides, he would refuse a fight anyways. He doesn't think we are worth it, and would find it hilarious.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11892,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","user":"Boston123","date":". 04-05-2017, 10:27 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-07T19:02:35-07:00","id":1074948,"title":"","content":"Start with:<br /> &quot;I am Kasai, son of _______ daughter of Ousamequin, and _________ Harutani of the Lowlanders. I know the ways of both peoples, having lived in the Nemasket tribe and in my father's house. I am here for the prosperity and strength for both the Dumoniapocassett and the Xia.&quot;<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:inherit\">((OOC: Has Xia waged offensive war on the Dumoniapocasset (ie, not just defending village<span style=\"font-family:inherit\">s from raiders) during the r<span style=\"font-family:inherit\">eign of Iysen Shiba? If she put a stop to that upo<span style=\"font-family:inherit\">n gaining <span style=\"font-family:inherit\">the throne we must <span style=\"font-family:inherit\">say so.))</span></span></span></span></span></span><br /> <br /> Then:<br /> &quot;<span style=\"font-family:inherit\">What worthy task do you need done?&quot;<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> At s<span style=\"font-family:inherit\">ome point we should mention that</span></span> if they go the route of war, and Xia is destroyed, Yue will conquer <b>every</b> acre of Xia, including the farmland Iysen is offering to cede to Dumoniapocassett, and the Tribes will receive NOTHING -- no land, no trade, not many spoils from raids into Xia. Unlike Xia, the Yue almost certainly intend the usual war against the Hill-Tribes, not trade. This will not be a positive outcome for the Confederacy -- at <b><i>best</i></b> they will gain nothing more than they own now.<br /> <br /> If they are neutral for the duration of this war, they have nothing to lose: if Yue conquers Xia, the tribes can always resume the usual raiding. If Xia survives, then they gain land, trade, and prosperity for their tribes for many generations.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> No, there's no point trying to persuade Iyannough, so we focus on everybody else, especially the Neponset warchief: what do their tribes need, and how are they most likely to get it?<br /> Splitting the Confederacy would be a bad thing, obviously. Except they might be better off with the Neponset kicked out, because they suck.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> OOC question:<br /> ((If the Vanehani know that the Hill-Tribes have gold and silver in the mountains, why hasn't there been a genocidal gold rush like what happened to California?))","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">She/Her. I am literal-minded and write literally. If I don't say something explicitly, please never assume I implied it. The only exception is if I try to make a joke.</span></span></span></div> <span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">My point of view may be different from yours but is equally valid.</span></span></span><br /> <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1x1ycoxJF0G9niMt5D3Pp7owZ7tw8AJvL?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">Exalted-cWoD-ArM url mega-library</a>. Exalted <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/663334-random-name-generator\" target=\"_blank\">name-generators</a>.","edited":{"userid":896,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/896-erinys","user":"Erinys","date":". 04-07-2017, 10:18 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/896-erinys","name":"Erinys","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar896_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4235"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-07T19:58:35-07:00","id":1074962,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Erinys</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1074948#post1074948\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Start with:<br /> &quot;I am Kasai, son of _______ daughter of Ousamequin, and _________ Harutani of the Vanehani. I know the ways of both peoples, having lived in the Hills for ______ years before going to my father's house. I am the chosen warrior and envoy of Lord Xia because I saved her life and the lives of her family from the treachery of Yue.&quot;<br /> <br /> Then:<br /> &quot;<span style=\"font-family:inherit\">What worthy task do you need done?&quot;<br /> <br /> <br /> At s<span style=\"font-family:inherit\">ome point we should mention that</span></span> if they go the route of war, and Xia is destroyed, Yue will conquer every acre of Xia, including the farmland Iysen is offering to cede to Dumoniapocassett, and the Tribes will receive NOTHING -- no land, no trade, not a lot of spoils from raids into Xia. Unlike Xia, the Yue almost certainly intend the usual war against the Hill-Tribes, not trade. This will not be a positive outcome for the Confederacy -- at <b><i>best</i></b> they will gain nothing more than they own now.<br /> <br /> If they are neutral for the duration of this war, they have nothing to lose: if Yue conquers Xia, the tribes can always resume the usual raiding. If Xia survives, then they gain land, trade, and prosperity for their tribes for many generations.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> No, there's no point trying to persuade Iyannough, so we focus on everybody else, especially the Neponset warchief: what do their tribes need, and how are they most likely to get it?<br /> Splitting the Confederacy would be a bad thing, obviously. Except they might be better off with the Neponset kicked out, because they suck.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> OOC question:<br /> ((If the Vanehani know that the Hill-Tribes have gold and silver in the mountains, why hasn't there been a genocidal gold rush like what happened to California?))</div> </div> </div> </div>1) Because the Hill-Tribes are more than capable of killing the ever-loving shit out of any Hundred Kingdoms-warparty that traipse into the Hill-Tribes territory, and the Vanehani know this.<br /> <br /> The Hill-Tribes are superb light skirmish infantry, while the <u>primary offensive</u> strength of the Hundred Kingdoms is based in the Oathsworn heavy cavalry. If either of each traveled too far into the other's territory, they would get destroyed in short order. as the territory of the opposing faction plays to that factions strengths. Of course, the Hill-Tribes have the edge, since the Hundred Kingdoms are hilly as shit.<br /> <br /> In order to mount an invasion of any size into Hill-Tribe lands, the Hundred Kingdoms would basically have to split up their forces into infinitely smaller units, as there isn't really any mountain passes wide enough for a regiment to travel as an entire unit. This would allow the Hill-Tribes to encircle and destroy the Hundred Kingdoms forces piecemeal.<br /> <br /> On the other hand, the Hundred Kingdoms are centered on the castles and castle-towns, and while the Hill-Tribes could <u>take</u> the fortifications <u>relatively</u> easily, (on a one vs one basis, a Hill-Tribe warrior is arguably superior to a Hundred Kingoms peasant Man-at-Arms, albeit more lightly equipped. In game terms, the Hill-Tribes would have higher stats, but lower Drill and effective morale (Willpower)), <u>holding</u> the forts would be an entirely different story. Forget actually standing in the field: an Oathsworn cavalry charge will scatter them in an instant.<br /> <br /> Something like 90+% of a Hill-Tribe warparty is light skirmish infantry (archers, slingers, javelinmen, attacking from ambush, cover, high ground, or preferably all three at the same time), and while they can fight in melee, they &quot;prefer&quot; ( its complicated. While the average Hill-Tribe warrior youth is more than eager to mix it up and gain some glory, and possibly/probably pretty good at it, it isn't their strong suit; they fight in loose order <i>at best</i>, and lack the discipline and morale for prolonged melee outside of ambush. ) not to. Only the Chosen warriors of the Chief are going to be in wargear that even approaches heavy, and only they will have the discipline to actually see orders through.<br /> <br /> Basically, Hill-Tribes are &quot;warriors&quot;, and Hundred Kingdoms are &quot;soldiers&quot; (<a href=\"http://tvtropes.org/pmwiki/pmwiki.php/Main/SoldierVsWarrior\" target=\"_blank\">http://tvtropes.org/pmwiki/pmwiki.ph...ldierVsWarrior</a>). In a drawn-out melee or charge, chances are the Hundred Kingdoms would come out on top, while the Hill-Tribes have the edge in ambushes and skirmishes.<br /> <br /> Finally, Hill-Tribe warbands have no effective logistics to speak of: each warrior is responsible for their own food, drink and armament, and you can only really carry so much. While each warrior is going to carry a few days worth of food on them, they are largely dependent on seizing the enemies stores for &quot;long-term&quot; attacks. Since most raids are undertaken in order to steal needed goods, they can't really &quot;afford&quot; to stay in the field for that long, on top of getting slowed down with the booty.<br /> <br /> On the other hand, the Hundred Kingdoms have, by comparison, effective logistics (although it pales in the face of the Shogunate or even the Realm [both Rome stand-ins in my games]). While each peasant Man-at-Arms and Oathsworn retainer is responsible for bringing their own wargear, chances are overwhelmingly likely that at the next castle there will be enough food for the army. (for example, Teriake Castle has enough dried beans and grain for a siege lasting over a year, and could support a couple hundred/thousandish men for a few weeks) On top of that, army quartermasters usually buy food from surrounding farms and the like, and in many cases, Oathsworn raids are undertaken to take food from enemy villages.<br /> <br /> However, said logistics (based largely on the wagon and cart) are only really effective in the lowland river bottoms, and will fall apart <i>very rapidly</i> once the terrain gets anywhere approaching rough. The Hundred Kingdoms armies are basically tied to their wagon-trains, and the Hill-Tribes would cut them to ribbons with hit-and-run attacks.<br /> <br /> TL;DR- the territory each culture lives in would make waging war <u>extremely</u> difficult for either side. There was a reason Kendorei didn't charge into the hills after them, and why the proto Hill-tribes were never able to reconquer their lands.<br /> <br /> 2) The other Hundred Kingdoms would be on the invading realm faster than white on rice.<br /> <br /> Basically, for the same reasons the &quot;campaign season&quot; in the Hundred Kingdoms only lasts 60 days or so. Each Lord <u>has</u> to keep their forces at home for significant periods of time, else they risk both famines and attacks by other Lords. <br /> <br /> The Hundred Kingdoms <i>really</i> need something like Qin Shi Huangdi or Tokugowa Ieyasu: until they get united, they will be little more than a squabbling backwater. Rulers have tried in the past, but &quot;tall poppy syndrome&quot; (<a href=\"http://tvtropes.org/pmwiki/pmwiki.php/Main/TallPoppySyndrome\" target=\"_blank\">http://tvtropes.org/pmwiki/pmwiki.ph...lPoppySyndrome</a>) often kicks in. Hell, Xia was formed as a result of that.<br /> <br /> 3) The Hundred Kingdoms <i>don't really</i> know the Hill-Tribes are sitting on deposits of gold and silver.<br /> <br /> Sure, they are <i>aware</i>, since the <u>very limited</u> trading between the two peoples involves said gold and silver, but they aren't really cognizant of the size or wealth of the deposits. Travelling between the two regions is <u>difficult as fuck</u>, to use the technical term, since there are no real roads or rivers between the two regions, and as a result most of the &quot;wealth&quot; of the Hill-Tribes stays 'at home' so-to-speak (even the poorest and youngest daughter in a Hill-Tribe clan will have jewelry of silver, for example, and our grandpappy has several hundred Dinar worth of bling on his person as &quot;wearing around the house&quot; stuff)<br /> <br /> It isn't just gold and silver, either. The Hill-Tribes also have access to good building-stone, some lumber, as well as the aforementioned wool and animal pelts. They have the <i>means</i> to be wealthy, but not really the <i>ability</i>, if that makes sense.<br /> <br /> If the deal goes through, and Lord Iysen builds roads and bridges into the Dumoniapoccasset territory, both economies will <u>explode</u>.<br /> <br /> As for the Confederacy....... it splitting apart is honestly the likeliest outcome, regardless of what happens. They have never really had to &quot;deal&quot; with a decision like this before, where the individual Tribes would be able to alternatively prosper or wither, depending on said choice.<br /> <br /> If/When the Confederacy splits apart, they could always just form a new one, or each Tribe could seek to go it alone, or join with another Tribe/Confederation. There are several hundred-thousand Hill-Tribespeople, split into many tens/hundreds of Tribes across the Hundred Kingdoms, the Dumoniapoccasset are only 12 of them.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-09T12:33:41-07:00","id":1075543,"title":"","content":"The Hundred Kingdoms<br /> <br /> A teeming morass of hundreds of petty kingdoms situated to the north of Vaneha, across the Yellow River, the Hundred Kingdoms region is largely viewed as a cultural backwater (especially by Vaneha), where brutish warriors and robber barons extort the fruits of the land from their peasant\u2019s brow. While the above description is \u2026.largely correct, if only in the technical sense of the term, one must dive deeper into the culture, social mores, and social strata of the region to fully understand why it is so.<br /> <br /> -History<br /> <br /> The Hundred Kingdoms were formed in the smoldering aftermath of Sword-Prince Kendorei\u2019s stalled invasion. Left behind by their warleader, the clan-leaders and subalterns started to carve out sections of the newly-conquered Red, Black and Blue river valleys, keeping their subjugated Hill-Tribe quasi-slaves in line with the traditional Vanehani Attendant forces and proto-Oathsworn retainers.<br /> <br /> Initially, the Hundred Kingdoms were ruled over by a Vanehani-appointed military dictator, called the Shogun, a term (appropriately enough) stolen from <u>The</u> Shogunate. After time, and as the power of the local landowning Lords waxed and the Shogun waned, the position of Shogun became less and less important, until in RY 351, the Shogun Tamura Emishi was sent back to Vaneha in various pieces for failing to bow before the Lord Han. Thus ended overt Vanehani overlordship of the Hundred Kingdoms.<br /> <br /> Almost as soon as the Vanehani yoke was overthrown, the various Lords and realm in the Hundred Kingdoms began squabbling between themselves for the overlordship, or for more localized power. To the outside world, this internecine infighting has continued for over 400 years, with no end in sight. Yet, if an outsider (usually Vaneha, who is still a mite sore about getting kicked out) attempts to intervene, the Hundred Kingdoms put aside their differences (at least in public), band together and take out the invaders. As soon as the Hundred Kingdoms are safe, the realms start infighting again in short order.<br /> <br /> Over the years, many realms, clans and rulers have fallen, and the Hundred Kingdoms region is littered with ruined castles, dilapidated manors and other detritus of the ages. One can find a poor family with little to their name living in a collapsing manor-house in the backwoods, claiming descent from Kendorei himself!<br /> <br /> -Phenotypes and physical characteristics:<br /> <br /> The peoples of the Hundred Kingdoms are largely descendent from two ethnic groups; the Vanehani and the Hill-Tribes. Almost overwhelmingly, the nobility is descended from the Vanehani, while the common-folk tend to be a mix between the Vanehani invaders (especially the lower ranking members of the clans) and the Hill-Tribes, with Hill-Tribe characteristics being more common the farther north and higher in elevation one travels.<br /> <br /> The nobility tend to be of lighter skin tone; yellow tan, pale brown and peach, (although most nobles tend to be rather pale anyways, as sun-tanned skin is emblematic of the working class), with straight, dark hair and eyes.<br /> <br /> The common-folk range from the pale skin tones of the nobility, to the dark huskiness of the Hill-Tribes, and anywhere in between (although, yet again, commoners tend to be darker in general, as they spend more time outside), with hair tending towards dark brown, although red can be common in Hill-Tribe influenced areas.<br /> <br /> -Climate<br /> <br /> Overall, the Hundred Kingdoms region can be thought of as being relatively warm, without the searing heat of the Southern deserts, chaparral or veldt, and without the bitter cold of the Northern taiga, tundra or seacoast.<br /> <br /> The Hundred Kingdoms, like most of the East, is amazingly fertile by the standards of most of Creation, capable of fielding three harvests a year, year after year. The limitation that lessens agricultural production in favor of the Blessed Isle isn\u2019t fertility, but weather.<br /> <br /> During the spring and fall (or, Seasons of Wood and Water), the Hundred Kingdoms are inundated with heavy rains, often jokingly referred to as \u201cDragon\u2019s Piss\u201d. During the summer (Season of Fire), the weather is relatively mild (aka about 70-80 degrees Fahrenheit), but humidity soars through the roof. Finally, during winter (Season of Air), snows sweep from the north, often burying the river valleys under several feet of snow.<br /> <br /> This often-extreme weather is a result of the unique geography of the Black, Red and Blue river valleys, and the mountains the Hill-Tribes live in. While the mountains are high from the Hundred Kingdoms-side, they are little more than high hills from the direction of the Thunder Plains (aka the grassland steppe several thousand miles across, to the north), and these heights serve as little more than speedbumps to the storms that sweep across the Thunder Plains. They cause the storms to rise into the atmosphere, then slow down and condense, dumping the accumulated precipitation onto the Hundred Kingdoms.<br /> <br /> In the summer, the mountains that cause the inclement weather serve to trap the humidity, making life miserable for all involved.<br /> <br /> In the low river-bottoms, trees are largely deciduous: beech and hickory, maple and elm, oak and ash. In the heights, conifers (pine, cedar, fir) start to become more common, along with birch and aspen, but do not replace the other species. Bamboo is everpresent, growing entire forests in the river bottoms and groves in the uplands.<br /> <br /> -Settlements:<br /> <br /> For the Hundred Kingdoms, \u201cNine out of Ten\u201d is a pretty good rule of thumb.<br /> <br /> That statement means that, on average, 9 out of 10 people live in the rural countryside, as opposed to in the urban castle-towns, as well as approximately 9 out of 10 people being commoners.<br /> <br /> By-and-large, most people in the Hundred Kingdoms are going to live within either 30 miles of the Black, Red or Blue Rivers, or within 5 miles of a tributary of the same. Most settlements are built on the banks of <i>some</i> body of water, be it even a small stream.<br /> <br /> This is due largely to the fact that most land in the Hundred Kingdoms is wrinkled worse than an elephant\u2019s backside, with most arable (or, really, <i>easily</i> arable) land being located on the banks of the major rivers. Some 70-odd percent of the population lives in these river-bottoms, and the woodlands, hills and mountains in-between the major river valleys are comparatively empty. Most of the river-bottoms are comparatively overcrowded, with little hope for younger sons and daughters to inherit farmland, leading to an exodus to the castle-towns (more on that later).<br /> <br /> Generally, most settlements in the Hundred Kingdoms will meet one of these gradations:<ul><li>Town: A town is a larger settlement, with a permanently-staffed Immaculate Temple and a designated market-square. Walls and bridges/dikes, as well as dockyards, are also important qualifiers. Most towns, including castle-towns, are located on the banks of the Red, Black and Blue rivers.</li> <li>Village: Smaller than a town, a village nonetheless has an Immaculate Shrine, although it might not be permanently staffed.</li> <li>Hamlet: Smaller still, hamlets are basically little more than wide places in the road, or clustered farmhouses in the countryside, and every inhabitant of a hamlet is possibly/probably related to everyone else.</li> </ul>Generally, hamlets are clustered around villages, which is where the farmers bring their goods for trade, and villages are clustered around towns.<br /> <br /> Regardless of the size of the settlement, most settlements are built around a core plaza/square, where social gatherings and market-fairs (in the larger settlements) take place, and around which craftsmen (if the settlement has permanent craftsmen) and \u201ccommon buildings\u201d, like tithe-barnes for the local Lord and Immaculate temples, are located.<br /> <br /> Around the core plaza are located the private dwellings of the inhabitants, which tend to be square structures, built of wattle-and-daub, fitted drystone, or bamboo, with roofs of thatch, bamboo/wood shingles, or slate or ceramic tiles. In the lowest of river-bottoms, houses and other buildings might be up on stilts to prevent flooding and dampness. In market-towns and castle-towns, the streets and buildings will be built to an ordered plan (usually oriented to defense), but in smaller rural villages, buildings tend to be built wherever the owners felt like, leading to cramped alleys between buildings.<br /> <br /> Farther out from the center are the agricultural fields and livestock pastures, as well as farm-related outbuildings (barns, sheepfolds, sheds, stables, etc), surrounded by the forests and hills.<br /> <br /> -Social Organization:<br /> <br /> The Hundred Kingdoms can be broadly divided into two social classes: the nobility and the commoners.<br /> <br /> The commoner-class is made up of peasants and serfs, with slaves beneath them. The main division between peasants and serfs is the ownership of land, with peasants owning land and serfs renting it from a landlord. Peasants tend to be wealthier, larger and stronger, better educated, and more \u201crespected\u201d by the nobility, while serfs are the opposite.<br /> <br /> Peasants, as expected, make up the bulk of the Hundred Kingdoms \u201cbureaucracy\u201d, limited as it is, serving their noble landlords as foremen, followers and armed soldiery (the Men-at-Arms are overwhelmingly drawn from the peasant class), while serfs are left to work the land (as it all they can be trusted with, in the minds of their lords). Peasants, as landowners, pay their landlord taxes on the land they own, while serfs instead pay rent <i>on top</i> of working on their landlords private lands; the amount of work depends on the lord and the realm.<br /> <br /> Nobles, on the other hand, run the gamut from the Great Lords of the major realms, to the Oathsworn landlord you pay your taxes to. The \u201clower\u201d one is on the nobility scale, the more common it is you will interact with the common-folk. A Great Lord, or a member of their clan, might go their entire lives (although that is difficult) without interacting at all with a commoner, while their Oathsworn retainer, granted land for their service, would probably interact with their tenants every day.<br /> <br /> As a result of the overcrowding of the farmlands, many younger sons and daughters of peasants (and even serfs, although serfs leaving their lords land without permission is illegal) pack up and head to the larger towns for work. This has led to the spontaneous creation of the \u201cmiddle class\u201d of townsmen; skilled craftsmen, merchants and guildsmen, not dependent on working the land for survival, and quite capable of hypothetically making money hand over fist. Most nobles find this middle class quite confusing; as they are <i>technically</i> legally serfs or peasants at best, they are nonetheless quite wealthy, with skilled merchants and craftsmen rivalling the Great Lords in wealth and influence. Most townsmen belong to some sort of guild (not <u>the</u> Guild); an organization of skilled workers who group up for protection and other benefits, and many Guilds have power, wealth and influence that far surpass their liege-lord. After all, the sword can only reach so far, whereas money is good wherever you go.<br /> <br /> As a result, many towns are ruled over by their landlords in name only, with the Guilds taking on most aspects of day-to-day running, and several Guilds have petitioned their liege-lords for \u201ccharters\u201d, or the right to administer, build and maintain not only towns, but harbor-facilities, walls, gates, bridges, etc. Not many Lords have granted charters as of yet, but the efficiency of the guild-run towns compared to the noble-run ones cannot be denied. After all, safe towns are <i>productive</i> towns\u2026<br /> <br /> -Hundred Kingdoms clans:<br /> <br /> All facets of Hundred Kingdom society, from the highest of nobles to the lowest of commoners, is determined by one\u2019s clan, or extended family. The clan is an individual\u2019s lifeline, social network, past and present and future. Actions are undertaken in order to bring honor to the clan, and to avoid bringing dishonor.<br /> <br /> Among nobles, the clan is represented by the \u201cfamily name\u201d (aka Shiba, Matsu, Harutane, etc), and noble clans can be quite large, with many dozens of members, cadet-branches and subfamilies. For example, the Shiba clan is divided into the Teriake Shibas (aka Lord Iysens family), the Iyana Shibas, the Wuyi Shibas and the Shino Shibas. Most noble clans will have a \u201csenior\u201d branch/family, the relatives of the head of the clan, and the one who makes the decisions for the clan.<br /> <br /> Among commoners, clans tend to be smaller, a couple dozen people at the most, and they tend to be much closer together, both in relations and in territory. For example, you might have several families in a clan live in the same village, with the entire clan spread across the villages in the valley, or in isolated hamlets, everyone in a village could be a member of the same clan.<br /> <br /> Regardless of if a clan is noble or commoner, the clan is by-and-large the social lifeline of the individual in the Hundred Kingdoms. Clanmembers stand by each other, assist each other in running businesses and the like, arrange marriages, look after children and invalid relatives, aka anything they can do to help each other. Filial piety is <u>everything</u> to a clan; leaving clanmates by the wayside is the ultimate dishonor, and clan ancestors are commonly worshipped. Clanmates are commonly called upon to represent them in court, to back one up in a fight, or anything else you need help with.<br /> <br /> If someone in the Hundred Kingdoms lacks a clan to back them up, that person is in <i>trouble</i>.<br /> <br /> -Food and Clothing:<br /> <br /> Most clothing throughout the Hundred Kingdoms is going to be broadly similar, even between social classes, differing mainly in textiles used, the fineness (threadcount, with higher threadcounts being finer and nicer cloth), and the length/ornamentation of layers of fabric.<br /> <br /> Most everyone wears undergarments around the groin to avoid rashes, with commoners wearing cotton and hemp, and nobles/the wealthy wearing linen or even silk. Over these, a pair of loose-fitting trousers and a open-breasted shirt are worn, again of cotton or canvas and linen or silk.<br /> <br /> The shirt, instead of being pulled over the head or buttoned up the front, is entirely open, with lapel-flaps being folded over each other and the entire garment bound shut with a belt or a sash.<br /> In colder or wetter weather, a jacket of quilted wool, canvas or linen is worn, fastened in much the same way as the undershirt. Conical hats, made of straw, are also worn in all weathers and seasons, to keep off the sun, the rain, and snow, as well as \u201ccoats\u201d made of the same.<br /> <br /> For show, a loose robe or mantle of finer cloth is worn over everything else, itself made of linen, velvet or even silk.<br /> <br /> As for footwear, a kind of cotton, canvas, wool or linen sock-thing is worn, except when one is asleep or working in the fields. In wet or cold weather, wooden sandals are added.<br /> <br /> Most clothing is layered, with underlayers being longer than the one above it. The higher the social class, or the less physical work one is expecting to do, the longer the skirts, sleeves and other layers of clothing will be. A peasant farmers shirt might fall to the mid-thigh, while a nobles shirt would go all the way to the ankles, with sleeves draped down to the waist.<br /> <br /> Difference in wealth and social class is instead inferred through material and ornamentation. Commoners usually wear rougher, harder-wearing fabrics (although a well-off peasant could certainly afford some comfortable cotton), with nobles wearing linen, velvet, and silk. Dyes and colors are not restricted, any everyone will generally wear clothing decorated in some fashion, but generally nobles (aka \u201cold money\u201d) are going to wear articles of surprisingly simple cut and coloration, usually single colors and muted prints (for example, Lord Iysens robe is a severe dark blue and white, but it is made of exceptionally-fine silk). The idea is that the nobility doesn\u2019t \u201cneed\u201d to show off their clothing in order to show their power.<br /> <br /> Instead, ostentatious clothing is the indicator of the town-dwelling middle class (aka \u201cnew money\u201d), with bold, contrasting colors, flashy patterns and prints, and extravagant materials the norm for all those that can afford it.<br /> <br /> (basically, look up Japanese clothing)<br /> <br /> As for food, it largely depends on where one lives in the Hundred Kingdoms. The most southerly realms are about the northern limits of where rice will grow <u>reliably<i>,</i></u> meaning most people subside off other grains, like wheat, corn, oats, etc, in addition to other vegetables. Vegetables include things like various legumes, cabbage, radishes and turnips, onions and leeks, potatoes, soybeans etc.<br /> <br /> Orchards are relatively common, mainly owned by landlords; cherry,apples, plums (always a favorite!), peaches and pears are all cultivated. The fruit is eaten fresh, preserved, cooked in soups and other dishes, made in tarts, pies and other deserts, and made into ciders and wines. The blooms are often related in poetry and other arts to the concept of mortality as a whole (especially the noble warrior, a concept/symbol inherited from Vaneha), and every spring it seems like every valley is filled with the slowly-drifting petals.<br /> <br /> As for meat: most everyone, commoners and nobles alike, eats fish, either fresh or smoked, and pork is by far the most common terrestrial animal consumed, valued for its fat and smooth taste. Pigs are intelligent enough to allow loose into the forest, put on weight nicely and produce large litters, all pluses to the farmers who must look after them. Other meats include chicken, goat/sheep, and wild game like deer, boar, pheasant, rabbit and, for the brave, tiger.<br /> <br /> The majority of the diet is made of three parts: bread, broth and brews. Bread aka grains is not usually baked in an oven, but usually made into dough, then into things like noodles or steamed dumplings. Vegetables and meat are put into soups, stews and stuffed into dumplings. Brews, or alcohol and tea, are consumed by everyone, although the only the nobility really drinks tea on a regular basis, as it has to largely be imported.<br /> <br /> Spices are mainly imported, with only salt, soy sauce, mustard, peppers of various heats, honey and vinegar being \u201cnative\u201d.<br /> <br /> -Religion:<br /> <br /> Overwhelmingly most people in the Hundred Kingdoms are going to be some degree of Immaculate-fearing, with an Immaculate Temple or shrine available in some fashion throughout the entire region. There are plenty of travelling monks to attend to the needs to the faithful, and the dates of festival-days are known by practically everyone.<br /> <br /> Spread across the Hundred Kingdoms are a multitude of monasteries and abbey\u2019s, usually fortified, and filled with monks devoting their lives to service (and, in times of war, desperate refugees). In many of these monasteries, martial training accompanies the spiritual, and there are as many folk-stories about righteous warrior-monks travelling the lands and righting wrongs as there are about Oathsworn. These warrior-monks usually train with the spear, the glaive or the bow, as the sword remains the symbol of the nobility.<br /> <br /> This does not mean, however, that other deities or spirits are not honored. Most travelers will make offering to spirits and gods of the roads and of the rivers before setting out, and ancestor-spirits are outright <b>worshipped</b>, in spite of the Immaculate Faith looking down on the practice. Even a peasants house will have a small ancestor-shrine, usually in a corner or in a cubbyhole, with candles/lantern, offering bowl and other votive objects on fully display. In noble manors, the ancestor-shrine can be an entire room in and of itself, with carved stone tablets, small bell or gong, and devotional objects.<br /> <br /> -Relations with outside parties:<br /> <br /> The only two groups the Hundred Kingdoms interact with to any large scale are the Hill-Tribes and the Vanehani, both of which are negative.<br /> The relationship with the Hill-Tribes has already been described, but the interactions with the Vanehani is <i>weird</i>. Like, the Hundred Kingdoms eagerly absorb Vanehani culture, yet otherwise want nothing to do with them. This is mainly due to the efforts of the Vanehani to subdue/conquer the Hundred Kingdoms in the past.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11892,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","user":"Boston123","date":". 04-09-2017, 03:38 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-09T21:04:55-07:00","id":1075710,"title":"","content":"I notice you don't seem to mention Dragonblooded, most I would assume would be Outcasts, how do they figure into everything?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1435-videopete","name":"videopete","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1435_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"111"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-10T03:36:11-07:00","id":1075823,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>videopete</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1075710#post1075710\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I notice you don't seem to mention Dragonblooded, most I would assume would be Outcasts, how do they figure into everything?</div> </div> </div> </div>I mean...... they are <i>around</i>, but even Outcaste Dragonblooded are going to be rare.<br /> <br /> Outside of the Realm and Lookshy, most (Outcaste) Dragonblooded are going to be more like wandering Youxia/Wuxia heroes, as opposed to something everybody knows about.........well, everybody <i>does</i> know about them, but more as the focus of stories and legends as opposed to that guy you see when you head to market every Sunday.<br /> <br /> People have this, well, not <i>misconception</i> per se, but I feel that they drastically overstate just how common Exalted are.<br /> <br /> For example, Saiko's home village, little more than a wide and muddy place on a trail? Equal to the amount of Solars in the world, if not more.<br /> <br /> Teriake Castle-Town, and the surrounding farms? More population than the Dragon-Blooded of the Realm, which has the highest concentration of Dragon-Blooded in Creation.<br /> <br /> So on and so forth. I am going to work right now, but I can go into greater detail later.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-10T18:16:05-07:00","id":1076222,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Erinys</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1074948#post1074948\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Start with:<br /> &quot;I am Kasai, son of _______ daughter of Ousamequin, and _________ Harutani of the Lowlanders. I know the ways of both peoples, having lived in the Nemasket tribe and in my father's house. I am here for the prosperity and strength for both the Dumoniapocassett and the Xia.&quot;<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:inherit\">((OOC: Has Xia waged offensive war on the Dumoniapocasset (ie, not just defending village<span style=\"font-family:inherit\">s from raiders) during the r<span style=\"font-family:inherit\">eign of Iysen Shiba? If she put a stop to that upo<span style=\"font-family:inherit\">n gaining <span style=\"font-family:inherit\">the throne we must <span style=\"font-family:inherit\">say so.))</span></span></span></span></span></span><br /> <br /> Then:<br /> &quot;<span style=\"font-family:inherit\">What worthy task do you need done?&quot;<br /> <br /> At s<span style=\"font-family:inherit\">ome point we should mention that</span></span> if they go the route of war, and Xia is destroyed, Yue will conquer <b>every</b> acre of Xia, including the farmland Iysen is offering to cede to Dumoniapocassett, and the Tribes will receive NOTHING -- no land, no trade, not many spoils from raids into Xia. Unlike Xia, the Yue almost certainly intend the usual war against the Hill-Tribes, not trade. This will not be a positive outcome for the Confederacy -- at <b><i>best</i></b> they will gain nothing more than they own now.<br /> <br /> If they are neutral for the duration of this war, they have nothing to lose: if Yue conquers Xia, the tribes can always resume the usual raiding. If Xia survives, then they gain land, trade, and prosperity for their tribes for many generations.<br /> <br /> No, there's no point trying to persuade Iyannough, so we focus on everybody else, especially the Neponset warchief: what do their tribes need, and how are they most likely to get it?<br /> Splitting the Confederacy would be a bad thing, obviously. Except they might be better off with the Neponset kicked out, because they suck.</div> </div> </div> </div>Seconded. Though i would stress that any &quot;Tasks&quot; should be coming from the 'unaligned' votes not the guy who will oppose us no matter what. And I would call him out on the conflict of intrest if he tries to push. &quot;I can't convince a man like you, who built everything on war. You don't understand how to be victorious in peace.&quot;","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Thoughts ripple out, birthing others","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1624-falleneco","name":"FallenEco","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1624_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1927"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-10T21:02:43-07:00","id":1076273,"title":"","content":"I have always put my Dragon Blooded as one in 10k as far as population density goes. This is far lower than say the Realm which is closer to one in 500. The idea that the Dragon Blooded were the soldiers of the Celestial Host.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1435-videopete","name":"videopete","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1435_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"111"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-11T13:50:36-07:00","id":1076653,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>videopete</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1076273#post1076273\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I have always put my Dragon Blooded as one in 10k as far as population density goes. This is far lower than say the Realm which is closer to one in 500. The idea that the Dragon Blooded were the soldiers of the Celestial Host.</div> </div> </div> </div>Generally, outside of my first campaign, when my players basically went out of their way to tangle with the Dragonblooded (they were not! Vikings, raiding Realm shipping, so got into a fight with a Dragonblood was all-but inevitable), my players will almost-never meet an actual Exalted. <br /> <br /> Sure, sure, they hear about them, but they never really actually meet them. The Solars and Lunars are ancient stories, fabled gods from the distant past, and Dragonblooded are wandering <i>wuxia</i> heroes.<br /> <br /> For example, Guan Yu in my games would be a Dragonblooded, while the Yellow Emperor was a Solar.<br /> <br /> The tend to meet <i>plenty</i> of Fair Folk, Ghosts and minor demons and deities, however. Mortal power and survivability got boosted somewhat in 3E, so I can safely throw some supernatural antagonists at my players without them getting stomped, so long as they prepare of course.<br /> <br /> I dunno: since I run almost solely-mortal campaigns, I tend to feel that if I throw Exalted in there, it will take the focus off the players a little bit, kinda like a GMPC turned up to 11. <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-15T18:47:55-07:00","id":1079157,"title":"","content":"It appears that most of you want to \u201cpresent our resume\u201d, so to speak, with the added addition of completing a \u201cworthy\u201d task.<br /> <br /> ----------<br /> <br /> You exhale, considering how best to respond. Then, reaching up and back, you tug at the tie binding your hair up. Shaking it free, you gesture to the gathered elders.<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt; As you can clearly tell, I am of the blood of both the Vanehani and the Tribes. I was raised under the shade of plum trees, training at arms with my father, and with my grandfather, I learned to stalk the deer and go among them unafraid! Who better to represent the needs and desires of both peoples?&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> Pacing around the circle of the fire, \u201c&lt;When I was very young, I was mistreated by both peoples, and was very sullen and misanthropic as a result. I would have given into my baser urges; unleashed violence and hatred, if not for my grandfather telling me an old parable. It involved two wolves, fighting for supremacy. Each wolf represented an aspect of our inner selves; one, love, serenity, empathy, compassion, humility, all positive aspects of our beings. The other, false pride, envy, sorrow, self-pity, resentment, anger, inferiority, all the worst parts of humanity brought together. The two wolves are constantly battling, inside you, inside me, and in every living person.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt; I asked him, \u2018which wolf will win?\u2019, only for he to tell me, \u2018the one you feed.\u2019.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt; The meaning and message of the parable was clear, and I made my choice. I let go of all the pain and negativity I felt towards my ancestry, and sought to improve both myself and the world.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u2018&lt; To that end, I returned home, asking my father to train me in the ways of the Oathsworn warrior. If I may ask without giving offense; who here is aware of what those are?&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> Another elder interjects, \u201c&lt; The warrior elite of the Vanehani, much like our Chosen warriors.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> Nodding, \u201cClose, but it is more involved than that. While they, and I, are indeed warriors first and foremost, existing as an Oathsworn is also a philosophical concept, and a moral one.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt; Before we get invested as Oathsworn, we have to swear Oaths pertaining to morality, honor, and respect. These Oaths, and our word, is a sacred bond, our honor and righteous will. Without the Oaths, we are little better than common bandits particularly skilled in bloodshed.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> Raising a hand, \u201c&lt; Now, I am aware that the Oathsworn of the Hundred Kingdoms have wronged the Hill-Tribes before, and, sadly, that the word and bond of most Oathsworn is worth about as much as warm spit, but I am <i>different</i>. I comport myself to the utmost degree of integrity, and I live to uphold and expand my Oaths as well as I am able. I <i>have</i> suffered because of this, but to give up as a result would be reprehensible in the extreme.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt;One of the Oaths I myself swore, in front of gods and men, was the Oath of Compassion. By uttering the words, I swore to forever defend the weak, uphold the righteous, and help whenever and wherever I can. To that end, I have wandered the Hundred Kingdoms for some years, aiding the downtrodden and abating the villainous. I am ready and willing to transfer the same aid to the Hill-Tribes. I will admit: when I arrived in the lands of the Nemasket\u2026\u2026. I was <i>ashamed</i>. Ashamed at how your people, <i>my</i> people, had to live as a result of our continued hatred. I would see such poverty and misery end, were it in my power.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cAnd it is! The offers presented might be the words of my liege, Lord Iysen Shiba, but the words, the heart and soul of the accords, they are mine! I myself suggested the terms and conditions of the treaty, in light of what both peoples could best use. The fact that Lord Shiba, a Vanehani through-and-through, trusts me, a bastard-born child of the Hill-Tribes, enough to use my suggestions in a binding treaty suggests several important things; that she indeed means to uphold her end of the treaty, and is bargaining with the Hill-Tribes in good faith, and that even one of the Tribes can rise high, based on their own merits, so long as they are honest!&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> Stopping and catching your breath, \u201c&lt;As for any tasks of note that add to my reputation, I must admit that I have only one. Sure you must be aware of the great and terrible conflagration at Teriake Castle, a years past?&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> One of the elders leans forwards, chin on fist,\u201d&lt; I have, but only the barest of details were brought back. We know that there was a fire, and that the ruler of Xia was injured, but that is about it. Are you saying you were involved with the event?&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> Nodding, \u201c&lt; Yes, elder, one could even say that I was a key figure. The fire was no accident; it was started by the agents of Yue during their efforts to escape, following the attempted assassination of the Lord Iysen. I was the one that originally discovered the treachery, and warned her Lordship about the assassins. More importantly, when my party and I got caught up in the blaze, instead of fleeing like other Oathsworn, I instead organized the survivors to safety. While doing that, I challenged another Oathsworn to a duel, over how he mistreated the common-born refugees.&gt;\u201d Gesturing to your leg, \u201c&lt; I took a grave injury as a result, and only recently have been able to walk, much less travel. <i>That</i> is how seriously I take this peace-treaty; I came here, across the hills and valleys, when I could be home comfortably convalescing!&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt; Of course, that just proves how little you can trust the realm of Yue. If they cannot be trusted to stick to a simple marriage-agreement, can they really be held to the standards of a peace-treaty? I think not!&gt;\u201d<br /> ]<br /> \u201c&lt; When I met and talked with Haichi Matsu, he did little but disparage my stories of life among the Hill-Tribes. He and his father, if they are successful in conquering Yue, will not be satisfied with the comparative level of coexistence that Lord Iysen has maintained. While, yes, the Tribes are capable of driving off invaders\u2026\u2026 is that worth the cost, in land and in lives? How many villages and farms will be burnt before they are satisfied? <i>At best</i>, the Tribes will be able to live as they do now.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt; Lord Iysen offers not only coexistence, but trade between our peoples, now and forever. Even if only you agree to neutrality, the Tribes will get land, wealth and prosperity, all of which are badly needed. If you decide to help us, you will earn something perhaps much more important: <i>respect</i>, and admiration!&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt; It is up to you, worthy and wise elders of the Dumoniapoccasset, to decide what you value more: your long-held pride, or the well-being of your people, not just the current generation but the generations to come. I ask of you; which wolf will you feed?&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> Lifting your head, to look as many elders as possible full in the face, \u201c&lt; Of course, if that speech of mine is insufficient to show my integrity and valor, I am perfectly willing to accept any task you set out for me to accomplish, if that be so needed to prove I mean what I say.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> The gathered elders lean towards each other, filling the circle with murmured conversation, too low to hear. Even Iyannough isn\u2019t ranting and raving, instead sitting back with an annoyed expression (evidently, he wasn\u2019t expecting you to be able to bear yourself so well). After a moment, the elder that questioned your credentials rises and, to your surprise, bows.<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt; I am sorry, Oathsworn Kasai of Xia, for questioning your right to speak. As you are, your deeds and integrity cannot be questioned. I, too, am familiar with the parable you refer to; it is one I tell my grandchildren, and if I may be honest \u2026.. you have a point. We have held onto our pride and resentment toward the lowlanders for so long, we have forgotten what it means to live in the present. Our history cannot fill the empty bellies of the children, and our pride cannot be wrapped around us to keep our the winter winds!&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> She bows again, \u201c&lt; Perhaps\u2026.. it is time for us to do a little less fighting, and a little more talking.&gt;\u201d She looks to her compatriot, who nods, \u201c&lt; Kasai of Xia, I am Anawan, this is Quadequina, and together we represent the Paskamansett in this council. You have our votes for neutrality. But\u2026\u2026.&gt;\u201d She sighs, \u201c&lt; As of right now, we cannot commit to lending you warriors, as attractive as the new lands might be. The lands of the Paskamansett are plagued by the anger of a great and terrible beast from the fringes of the world, and it takes all our warriors just to ward it away from our villages. We have already lost several of our bravest warriors to its maw, and while it haunts our hills, we cannot leave&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt; If were to kill this beast, would you be willing or able to lend warriors to my cause?&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> At this, the Paskamansett Warchief speaks up, \u201c&lt; Kasai of Xia, do you know of what you speak? This isn\u2019t just some animal, maddened by pain and hunger. It is a <i>god-beast</i>, which wears a thousand faces. Its breath spoils milk and pollutes the rivers; crops are withered by its passing! I do not want to cast doubt on your abilities as a warrior, but several of my oldest and most-able Chosen warriors have already tried fighting the beast. <i>None</i> of them have returned, and if I may bring up the mysteries, our shamans are about ready to ask the Gods themselves for intercession. To fight it is certain death!&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt; All death is certain, warchief, all that matters is how we greet it. And, to be honest, that is what they said about the fire at Teriake, and look where I am now.&gt;\u201d You shrug, \u201c&lt; I will need a guide to its lair.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> The chieftain gapes at you, then bows, evidently impressed at your resolve. \u201c&lt; I will show you myself.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> At this, the speaker for the council steps forwards, \u201c&lt;Kasai of Xia, the council will require some time to deliberate on your proposal.&gt;\u201d Turning to the Paskamansett warchief, \u201c&lt; How long will this escapade take?&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> He shrugs, \u201c&lt; About 2 weeks, maybe.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> The speaker nods, \u201c&lt; Then it is settled. Kasai of Xia, we will await your return for three weeks, when the moon is but a sliver in the sky.&gt;\u201d, and he turns back to the circle.<br /> <br /> You go to follow the Paskamansett warchief, only to get grabbed by your grandfather as you pass. He pulls you into an embrace, not minding the crowd watching, then hands you a pendant. A circle of wrought iron is webbed through with red yarn, and tied to a leather bag suspended from a strap.<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt; Listen\u2026. I don\u2019t agree with what you are doing, but if you are going to fight this beast, wear this at the very least. It is made from the purest iron, to ward against the formless chaos from the edges of the world, and should help.&gt;\u201d With that, and a final embrace, he turns without another word and heads back to the fire-ring. Perturbed, you follow the Paskamansett chieftain down the mountainside.<br /> <br /> While the going is rough, at least this time you can see where you are going, and because you can follow the long-but-winding mountain valleys, it takes but a few days to reach the territory of the Paskamansett. Almost as soon as you enter their territory, the atmosphere in the scattered villages you come across changes. While you were never exactly warmly welcomed, at least the other tribespeople were polite, but the Paskamansett are short-tempered even with their warchief, as well as with each other. The villages themselves are heavily fortified (by Hill-Tribe standards, at least, with high log palisades, earthworks and bushes with inch-long thorns surrounding each one), with each gate and door heavily warded by iron-and-feather fetishes, smoking with ritual incense. Only the fields immediately outside each settlement are worked, and you <i>never</i> meet anyone travelling along the river-bottom.<br /> <br /> After a few days of ominous travel, you eventually break down and ask the warchief just what the hell is going on. Why is everyone so downtrodden?<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt; This god-beast has, to put it bluntly, scared the hell out of everyone, for lack of a better term. When it first showed up, it took the form of a beautiful woman, lost in the woods. When a village took her in, she repaid the favor by slaughtering and <i>eating</i> everyone in the settlement. A travelling trader found the settlement full of corpses, hearts torn out of their chests. The beast tried the same thing in another village, but the warriors chased it out before she could do it again. Now, it haunts the woods and crags between settlements, attacking people at a whim. Most people it leaves alone, while others, usually the strongest hunters and warriors in the settlement, get killed and eaten. Worst of all, our totem-spirits <i>refuse</i> to do anything about it, according to the shamans. They don\u2019t even want to talk about it, apparently.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt;\u2026I\u2026..see. Has anybody volunteered to fight it, or am I the first?&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt; Before we knew what we were truly dealing with, there were younger warriors who thought they could best the beast.&gt;\u201d His jaw clenches, \u201c&lt; <i>Idiots</i>. They were killed to a man, for nothing!&gt;\u201d<br /> \u201c&lt; When the deaths got too much to bear, I led a party of my Chosen and tried to trap the beast in a gorge not too far from here. A muddy stream surrounded by high cliffs, the perfect ambush site, right? <i>Hah!</i> The thing godsdamned <i>jumped</i> right to the top, laughing all the way! It killed the entire warband, and I barely escaped with my life\u2026&gt;\u201d He indicates a long slash-scar running down his face.\u201d&lt;\u2026.. That <i>thing</i> killed all my Chosen, my blood-companions! I will see it dead if it is the last thing I do!&gt;\u201d, eyes burning with vehemence.<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt;Well, based on what you are telling me, you might just get your chance in the next few days. Does this thing have a lair, a place where it can regularly be found, or just it just wander around?&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt;There is a cave of sorts, where the beast can be found when it isn\u2019t out hunting. It <i>used</i> to be a shrine to the Moon-goddess, but it desecrated its sanctity with human corpses. Close quarters, with nowhere for the thing to run. We will be there in a day or so, and there is one more settlement a few hours away, so if you have need of anything, I suggest you pick it up there.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> True to his word, there is a final village down the valley, even more suspicious and fearful than the others, and it takes quite a while (and quite a lot of wheedling by the warchief) before they open the gates to you. Inside, you enjoy the sparse luxury of a hot meal and a cushion to sit on, and look over your weapons and armor. When you wake up in the morning (according to the few survivor\u2019s accounts, the beast isn\u2019t hindered by sunlight, like most monsters are, but you certainly don\u2019t want to fight the thing at night!), you head out downriver to the site of its lair.<br /> <br /> Contrary to what you expected, the area is \u2026\u2026. Pretty damn peaceful, actually. A babbling brook flows under the shade of a rocky outcropping, with trees and sweet-smelling grasses growing on its banks. There are even birds singing in the background. All in all, not that bad of a monster lair.<br /> <br /> Bemused, you turn to the warchief, who has his bronze waraxe raised and ready.<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt; Is\u2026\u2026 <i>this</i> the place?&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> He nods, \u201c&lt; Yes. The grotto is by the top of the outcropping. Just\u2026\u2026 just wait until we get inside, you will see.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt; \u2026. Very well. Are you ready?&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> He shrugs, \u201c&lt; About as ready as I will ever be.&gt;\u201d, and with that, you head on up.<br /> <br /> It takes but a few minutes to clamber up and around the rocky slope, and before you lies the grotto. Again, it isn\u2019t what you expected; not a dank cave-mouth, but a comparatively-wide portal into the hillside, framed by drystone blocks. Inside the door, the floor slopes down into the hillside, passing quickly out of sight into the darkness. After but a moment of consideration (hesitation?), you quietly make your way into the opening, followed by the warchief.<br /> <br /> Almost instantly, it gets too dark to see, and even with your hand on the wall, you have very little idea of where you are. You can tell the floor is sloping <i>down</i>, but that is about it.<br /> <br /> Almost as soon as it got dark, the wall peels away from your fingers, and a room opens up before you, bright under the light of day. In front of you, the passage spreads out into a circular chamber, with a pool of clear water in the center, lit up by a shaft of sunlight beaming down from the open ceiling above.<br /> <br /> On the other side of the pool, against the far wall, stands an altar of sorts. Drawn in chalk is a crude outline of a human figure standing beneath the image of a waxing/full/waning moon, and beneath the figure is set a slab of blackest slate, strewn with votive objects: deer skulls and antler-tines, animal pelts and hides, weapons of every shape and style, and <i>bones</i>. Bones upon bones upon bones. They spill over the sides of the slab, strewn all over the floor in dizzying patterns, piled in the corners like firewood.<br /> <br /> Not all of them are animal bones, not even most. And <i>all</i> of them are covered with teethmarks.<br /> <br /> \u201cHmm, I was wondering how long it would take the two of you to make your way here. Don\u2019t you realize it is incredibly <i>rude</i> to make a lady wait?\u201d drifts a voice through the darkness.<br /> \u201c&lt; <i>Don\u2019t listen to it!</i> It will use its siren-song to turn you to its side!&gt;\u201d, roared the warchief, axe raised high and eyes dancing from corner to corner.<br /> <br /> The voice laughs, silver bells pealing through the chamber, and a <i>very</i> female figure stands up from behind the slate altar, draping herself languidly over the stone. She is tall, dressed in a diaphanous gown of whitest silk, with long red hair. She grins, exposing her teeth-like-needles.<br /> <br /> \u201cCome now, little man, no need to be so angry. It doesn\u2019t matter, not for long. I <i>do</i> appreciate you coming out here for little old me; it means I don\u2019t hav\u2026\u201d<br /> <br /> You interrupt the Anathemic demon, holding out the talisman provided by your grandfather. \u201cCease thy blathering, demon! We have come here to put an end to your monstrous existence, in the name of peace and great justice!\u201d<br /> <br /> The Moon-mad creature recoils, but instead of crying out in pain against the hateful presence of pure iron, she starts <i>laughing </i>(way to kill the mood\u2026.), bent-over at the waist.<br /> <br /> Standing up and wiping the tears from her eyes, \u201cOh good gods, that was funny! Did you practice that line in the mirror before you came here? I\u2019m probably the farthest thing from a <i>raksha</i> within 1000 miles, for what it is worth. Hells, I <i>eat</i> <i>raksha</i>, you should be thanking me!\u201d<br /> <br /> You snarl, \u201cBe that as it may, demon, that does not mean that your crimes will go unpunished. You have committed murder, the cursed crime of cannibalism, and the defilement of a holy shrine and you will be brought to task!\u201d<br /> <br /> She smirks, \u201cWhat gives you any right to pass judgement over me, <i>mortal</i>? You aren\u2019t even the Wyld Hunt! They, at least, I could take partially-seriously. You? <i>pfffftttt</i>. I am a living god! You couldn\u2019t even begin to be able to challenge me in any fashion!\u201d<br /> <br /> She hops up onto the slate slab, idly combing her fingers through her hair, and gazes on you appraisingly.<br /> <br /> \u201cBesides, what I did wasn\u2019t murder, not in the slightest. I killed the warriors so that I might absorb their strength. I <i>honored</i> them, and ever-shifting Luna, and through their sacrifice, Creation itself might be better protected!\u201d She shrugs, \u201cNot that they really mattered in the long run. They\u2019ll reincarnate.\u201d<br /> <br /> You glare at her, gorge rising in indignation at her flippant disregard for human life. The Paskamansett warchief, not fluent in Riverspeak, watches warily, not sure of what exactly is going on.<br /> <br /> \u201cEven if the killing of the warriors was \u2018necessary\u2019, which I most vehemently doubt\u2026\u2026.. <i>why</i>? Isn\u2019t there other ways you could build your strength? Ways that do not involve murder?!\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cWhy?\u201d, she looks almost amused, \u2018The proper question is more like; <i>why not?</i>\u201d She spreads her arms, silver disk igniting on her brow. \u201cLuna gave me the power to be anything, to <i>do anything</i>. <b><i>Why limit myself</i></b>?!\u201d<br /> <br /> She smirks and eyes you appraisingly. Sashaying around you, she pokes and prods at certain parts of your anatomy, chuckling as you tense up in apprehension (-ahem-). Coming around to your front, she leans forward, face almost touching yours. All you smell is the rancid odor of dried blood.<br /> <br /> \u201cTell you what, mortal: ordinarily, I would just rip you into ever-so-many-quivering-pieces, but I am <i>bored</i>, and your spirit is just so <i>adorable</i>\u2026\u2026 I can respect that, in some fashion. You are here for my head, are you not? For some treaty?\u201d At your physical response, she rears back laughing again, cold and beautiful as the far-off moon.<br /> <br /> She grins, exposing her perfect teeth, \u201cWhat, surprised I know about that? <i>I have been following you since you entered the mountains</i>. I know <b>everything </b>worth knowing around here!\u201d She pats your cheek in consolation, sending an electric thrill coursing through your body.<br /> <br /> Leaning into you, lips to your ear, \u201c To be honest, I do not feel up to fighting, especially not a fine young specimen such as yourself. Do what I want, and I will <i>give</i> you what you want: some part of my person, as proof of my \u201cdeath\u201d. Hell, I\u2019ll even leave these mountains.\u201d<br /> <br /> She steps back, caressing your cheek, eyes and brow flashing silver, \u201cGet rid of the old man, so we can talk more privately, <i>if you know what I mean</i>.\u201d<br /> <br /> Trembling, you:<ol class=\"decimal\"><li>Fight! I will not be swayed by your silver (I can\u2019t help myself) tongue, Anathema! HAVE AT THEE!</li> <li>Flee! \u2026.. I\u2019m not cut out to deal with Anathemic moon-madded monsters. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5FjWe31S_0g\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5FjWe31S_0g</a></li> <li>Fuck! I, uh, accept your proposal \u2013huff + fan self-</li> <li>Something else?</li> </ol>--------------------<br /> <br /> Terrifying Lust Infliction, <i>gooooooooooo</i>!<br /> <br /> This particularly-pleasant Lunar is based off the Korean <i>Gumiho</i> and the Chinese <i>Huli Jing</i>. Her Spirit Totem is a red-and-white-furred fox\u2026.. so I guess she could pretty accurately be described as a <i>foxey lady</i>? (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_PVjcIO4MT4\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_PVjcIO4MT4</a>)<br /> <br /> Eh? <i>Eh?</i> (help me)<br /> <br /> On a more serious note, she is pretty much totally bugfuck insane. She is Casteless, and spent a fair portion of time cavorting through the Wyld. She isn\u2019t quite a Chimera just yet, but she is far from mentally stable.<br /> <br /> Of all the Exalted-types, Lunars are by far my favorite.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11892,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","user":"Boston123","date":". 04-15-2017, 09:55 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-15T19:34:41-07:00","id":1079177,"title":"","content":"<b>Fight!</b> I will not be swayed by your silver (I can\u2019t help myself) tongue, Anathema! HAVE AT THEE!<br /> <br /> That's the task, she is a murderer, and unlikely to keep any promise.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Like my Avatar? Courtesy of<span style=\"color:#0000FF\"> Jen</span>! : <span style=\"font-size:12px\">Anybody want their characters to be experimented on ? </span>post 98<br /> <i>An Exalt is never unarmed.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4018-l-het-esh","name":"L'het'esh","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4018_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"521"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-15T20:11:55-07:00","id":1079192,"title":"","content":"Oooookay. *deep breaths* Before I give my answer, I feel compelled to raise an objection. I feel like we've been dropped into a tactically unwinnable situation - not because we, the players, took a wrong turn, but because you wrote Kasai into this without any warning. <br /> <br /> At this juncture, I would say the odds of Kasai being able to solo a Lunar exalted (or, really, anything that is known to have killed dozens of people by itself) are <i>frighteningly low. </i>Not too long ago, Kasai fairly fought against a single, reasonably skilled mortal fighter who was willing to use some dirty tricks. Kasai almost died. That tells me that Kasai is, to be blunt, <i>not good enough </i>to have a fighting chance of winning against any supernaturally enabled enemy, unless he seizes on every advantage he can. But we're not here with a prepared ambush, or any special armament, or even a plan! We've got one angry guide (who apparently doesn't feel motivated to intervene yet) and the beast is already close enough to rip out Kasai's throat with her teeth. If Kasai runs, will she chase him? If Kasai gives in, will she tear out his tongue, heart, or something else anyway? It appears we might be doomed no matter what we choose, and <i>that is not fair. </i><br /> <br /> We only said we'd do the tribes a favor - not that we'd get killed senselessly. I think that last post could have been broken into 3 or 4 smaller ones, each with their own meaningful decisions. Perhaps you could have telegraphed a little better what we were getting into. Perhaps we could have gathered more resources to prepare for this encounter. But instead, we're suddenly here, and now we've got to improvise. <br /> <br /> And that brings us to my answer - <br /> <br /> <b>4. Pretend to give in to your lust and pull her to you. Bite and hold her bottom lip (this is important - it may momentarily prevent her from tearing Kasai's face off). Then, while she's distracted, grab your knife and stab her in the gut. After that, improvise. </b><br /> <br /> The only thing I can think to do here is to attack first, and hope to cripple her before she hits back. This might violate the Oath of Honor, but what good are the Oaths if we're just going to die?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>On the frontier of the Wild South, there's only one woman with the grit to take on its most dangerous outlaws and bring them <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/984977-back-alive-or-maybe-dead-quest\" target=\"_blank\">Back Alive, or Maybe Dead.</a><br /> <br /> Avatar by <a href=\"http://puzzlinghappenstance.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">K.S. Brenowitz</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/908-semicasual","name":"semicasual","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar908_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2499"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-15T20:27:48-07:00","id":1079200,"title":"","content":"Normally I would say join up with the lunar but it would violate the oaths to just betray the warchief. I guess fight, it will be an honorable death or the best win ever.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11567-jairain","name":"Jairain","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11567_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"345"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-15T21:33:36-07:00","id":1079220,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>semicasual</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1079192#post1079192\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">-snip-</div> </div> </div> </div>1) In hindsight, flat-out explaining just why Ousamequin is so kickass might have helped. I apologize for not doing so earlier.<br /> <br /> During the Feast of the Moon, he ritually hunted down and killed a Luna-touched animal (arguably, an aspect of the Incarna [her? him? hir?] themselves), consuming the heart, liver and blood of the stag. (if that sounds like the Lunar Blood-Hunt...... surprise! Guess where they learned it from). It gave him, basically, a shittier version of the Exalted Healing merit. Not permanently, but enough to give him the body of a 20 year old, when he is 66.<br /> <br /> Point being: blood is the key. Blood has power. K<u>eep that in mind</u>.<br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\">Blood consumed in a Lunar shrine? (Not a Manse, but a <u>consecrated shrine</u> to Luna) Hoo boy.</div></div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> 2) The Lunar isn't &quot;combat specced&quot;, for lack of a better term. While that is largely the difference between 100 and 90..... she isn't going to bust out a Deadly Beastman Transformation on us. She has a bunch of animal forms, as well as Hybrid Body Rearrangement, but that is pretty much it. Most of her &quot;stuff&quot; is heavily focused on manipulation, charisma, social-shenanigan-fu, in order words. She is also unarmed, as unarmed as one of the Exalted can be.<br /> <br /> I mean, my mortal players killed one of the Exalted (granted, they were Dragonblooded, but still), and it wasn't <i>that</i> tear-inducing.<br /> <br /> 3) Honor only applies to those <i>with</i> honor. Anathema, as demons, lack honor, and as such, merit no honorable treatment. On a related note: fighting &quot;honorably&quot; doesn't preclude fight <u>pragmatically</u>, at least in my book. On a battlefield, or in a fight for his life, Kasai can kick, bite, elbow-drop and eye-gouge to his hearts content.<br /> <br /> Honor, when you get down to the nitty-gritty, only really applies in duels.<br /> <br /> 4) If we decide to fight, she would almost-certainly toy with us, as opposed to ROFLstomping us into the ground off the bat. Give us a chance &quot;in the name of sport&quot;, and all that.<br /> <br /> 5) If she actually got meaningfully hurt, she would run, hands-down. She would be <i>pissed</i>, and we would have made an enemy, but she would run. Her possible-5000-years-of-life is worth her momentary pride any day of the week.<br /> <br /> 6) The warchief of the Paskamansett is with us, and he isn't exactly a trivial opponent, far from it. He just doesn't speak Riverspeak.<br /> <br /> 7) This might be spoileriffic, but<br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\">there is.........stuff, on and around the altar, that could be incredibly useful. I mean, weapons and other pointy things ritually sacrificed to the Incarnae of the Hunt? Yerp.</div></div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> 8) Also spoileriffic<br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\">I <u>always, ALWAYS</u><b><i>, </i></b><i>give my players the chance to run. Always. Of course, if my players decide not to take the chance, or are so single-minded they don't realize I am offering the chance.........<br /> <br /> Now, the Lunar would find it funny, and probably chase us for the fun of it, but in the end, we would almost-certainly get away, with a little bloodshed and luck. Remember: the Paskamansett already <u>promised</u> us neutrality. We could very well go back to the elder council and say &quot;sorry, no-can-do, big gribbley monster was too gribbley&quot;, as well as calling for a Wyld Hunt when we got back to &quot;civilization&quot;. There is <u>always</u> an out. If there wasn't/isn't, I wouldn't include it.<br /> <br /> I included the option to fight because...... well, sometimes you just need to go out and slay the goddamn dragon. </i><br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"><i>Finally, fighting on, in the face of probably-quite-probable death, because it is the right thing to do?...... Arguably worthy of Exaltation. Although Exaltation would send the story in an <u>entirely</u> different direction. Not a bad one, just....... different. </i></div></div> </div> </div><br /> </div></div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> Amusingly, my original plans called for Kasai to throw down with a <u>Chimera</u> (more specifically, a bear+snake+boar+tiger, 12 feet of muscle and bad attitude), but I changed it to a &quot;regular&quot; Lunar in effort to be more fair. We also weren't fighting said Chimera in a cave, but still.<br /> <br /> Finally, I'll just leave this here:<br /> <br /> <i>&quot;If you have to look along the shaft of an arrow from the wrong end, if a man has you entirely at his mercy, then hope like hell that man is an evil man. Because the evil like power, power over people, and they want to see you in fear. They want you to know you're going to die. So they'll talk. They'll gloat. They'll watch you squirm. They'll put off the moment of murder like another man will put off a good cigar. So hope like hell your captor is an evil man. A good man will kill you with hardly a word.&quot;</i> <span style=\"color:#333333\"><span style=\"font-family:sans-serif\">\u2014 Terry Pratchett, <i>Men at Arms</i><br /> <br /> Take all the time you want to think about it, come up with possible strategies, because it unfortunately looks like there will probably be two weeks before my next posting. I am going camping next weekend, and if plans hold out, I am almost certainly going to be pooped on Sunday.<br /> <br /> As always, I am learning to write as I go, and I certainly do appreciate the , er, advice when it comes to presenting a story. If you feel that I could have broken up the post a wee bit better, or explained background details more in-depth, than I shall certain attempt to do so in the future.<br /> <br /> If anything, more and more advice is certainly welcome, including criticism. I range between &quot;ceaselessly writing and deleting posts in despair over how they sound to me as the author&quot; and &quot;meh, good enough&quot;.<br /> <br /> If something <u>isn't</u> adequate, <b><i>please</i></b> tell me.</span></span>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11892,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","user":"Boston123","date":". 04-16-2017, 01:48 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-17T05:42:29-07:00","id":1079754,"title":"","content":"We can't let this broken soul, this monster live. Not after what she has done. We will fight.<br /> <br /> I am not against semicasual's forth option (deceive her and then strike while her guard is down); I feel her social fu may give the game away and I don't know if we could pull off the smooch and stab. Plus we don't know how the chieftain will react and every second counts in this situation.<br /> <br /> It has been an honour, but we will most certainly die here. Let's try to make this as painful for the child eating b!*$h as we can.<br /> (She ate a whole village. Villages have children)<br /> <br /> If a miracle happens, we are cutting her heart out. Possibly desecrating her corpse and salting the ground around whatever grave. This is an abomination. This is Anathema","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Thoughts ripple out, birthing others","edited":{"userid":1624,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1624-falleneco","user":"FallenEco","date":". 04-19-2017, 07:19 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1624-falleneco","name":"FallenEco","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1624_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1927"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-17T06:35:52-07:00","id":1079767,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Boston123</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1079220#post1079220\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Various clarifications</div> </div> </div> </div>Responding to your points in order:<br /> 1. There was no real reason to bring it up before, and I'm not sure what reason there is to bring it up now... unless you mean to say that Kasai is some kind of 2nd or 3rd generation Godblood? Which is nice and all, but probably won't help against an enemy that won't sit and wait patiently for Kasai to die. <br /> <br /> 2. If we're not supposed to worry about them, then I wouldn't introduce a character by saying &quot;This animal killed a whole village, and then multiple warbands who went after her, and some of those people were very capable.&quot; It gives an impression of overwhelming power. <br /> <br /> 3. Good to know!<br /> <br /> 4-5 . Now you've given us a lot of out-of-character knowledge about her abilities and temperament, I'm less concerned about our chance for survival, but I also feel that the versimilitude of this story has been damaged. :/<br /> <br /> 6-7. No real description has been provided of the warchief, and the weapons are alluded to only briefly. If you want objects in a story to have relevance, you need to define them at least enough to draw attention to them. Otherwise, readers/players tend to forget that those things exist, or dismiss the idea that they could be more than window-dressing. <br /> <br /> For example, the warchief <i>really</i> needs a name. What do we know about him other than... uh... he's a warchief? And he's got a bronze axe, that's very nice. And we may assume he doesn't like talking about himself. We've been walking around with the guy for days, for crying out loud! <br /> <br /> Those weapons, too, need to stand out more if we're supposed to notice them. They are &quot;of every shape and style&quot;, but what else is noteworthy about them? Are they in a heap? Are they shiny and polished, despite sitting in a cave? Are they made of wood, stone, or metal? Are they ornamental? If you <i>really</i> wanted us to use the weapons, you could have split the room description into multiple paragraphs and given 2-3 extra sentences to the cave-armory. The extent of description, and the <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-bs_idSD498\" target=\"_blank\">order in which you describe things</a>, informs the reader of what is or isn't important. <br /> <br /> 8. It's nice to have an &quot;out&quot;, but seriously... I'm not familiar with your style, and I don't have a tacit understanding of what you will or won't let fly in your games. All I have to work with is the text and, to me, the text says &quot;BAD END incoming!&quot;","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>On the frontier of the Wild South, there's only one woman with the grit to take on its most dangerous outlaws and bring them <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/984977-back-alive-or-maybe-dead-quest\" target=\"_blank\">Back Alive, or Maybe Dead.</a><br /> <br /> Avatar by <a href=\"http://puzzlinghappenstance.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">K.S. Brenowitz</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/908-semicasual","name":"semicasual","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar908_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2499"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-18T04:53:02-07:00","id":1080246,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>semicasual</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1079767#post1079767\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Responding to your points in order:<br /> 1. There was no real reason to bring it up before, and I'm not sure what reason there is to bring it up now... unless you mean to say that Kasai is some kind of 2nd or 3rd generation Godblood? Which is nice and all, but probably won't help against an enemy that won't sit and wait patiently for Kasai to die.<br /> <br /> 2. If we're not supposed to worry about them, then I wouldn't introduce a character by saying &quot;This animal killed a whole village, and then multiple warbands who went after her, and some of those people were very capable.&quot; It gives an impression of overwhelming power.<br /> <br /> 3. Good to know!<br /> <br /> 4-5 . Now you've given us a lot of out-of-character knowledge about her abilities and temperament, I'm less concerned about our chance for survival, but I also feel that the versimilitude of this story has been damaged. :/<br /> <br /> 6-7. No real description has been provided of the warchief, and the weapons are alluded to only briefly. If you want objects in a story to have relevance, you need to define them at least enough to draw attention to them. Otherwise, readers/players tend to forget that those things exist, or dismiss the idea that they could be more than window-dressing.<br /> <br /> For example, the warchief <i>really</i> needs a name. What do we know about him other than... uh... he's a warchief? And he's got a bronze axe, that's very nice. And we may assume he doesn't like talking about himself. We've been walking around with the guy for days, for crying out loud!<br /> <br /> Those weapons, too, need to stand out more if we're supposed to notice them. They are &quot;of every shape and style&quot;, but what else is noteworthy about them? Are they in a heap? Are they shiny and polished, despite sitting in a cave? Are they made of wood, stone, or metal? Are they ornamental? If you <i>really</i> wanted us to use the weapons, you could have split the room description into multiple paragraphs and given 2-3 extra sentences to the cave-armory. The extent of description, and the <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-bs_idSD498\" target=\"_blank\">order in which you describe things</a>, informs the reader of what is or isn't important.<br /> <br /> 8. It's nice to have an &quot;out&quot;, but seriously... I'm not familiar with your style, and I don't have a tacit understanding of what you will or won't let fly in your games. All I have to work with is the text and, to me, the text says &quot;BAD END incoming!&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> 1) No, it isn't something that is passed along through the blood. And even if it wasn't, Ousamequin partook in the ritual after his daughter was born, meaning she, and Kasai, would have recieved no benefit from it.<br /> <br /> I brought it up (I referred to it way back in the Hill-Tribe infodump) in order to hint to the idea that the fight was survivable, IF we/you play the cards right, as well as make some heavy decisions. I mean, consuming the blood and flesh of a creature is a Lunar heresy, on top of being, well, <u>cannibalism </u> (in this case, at least. Lunar Anathema are still human, at least in form)<br /> <br /> The Hill-Tribes do it because that is part of their normal worship process. Kasai..... ehh, not so much. He debatably doesn't even know that one could get power from the act. I was hoping that referring to both the Hill-Tribe ritual as well as the Lunar's culinary proclivities would be enough of a hint.<br /> <br /> 2) We aren't &quot;not supposed to worry about them&quot;, they are still a Lunar Anathema. It is just less &quot;almost-certain to get asshole stomped&quot; and more &quot;extremely-tough-fight that might be survivable if we play our cards right.&quot;<br /> <br /> EDIT: -removed- never mind, more spoilers<br /> <br /> 4-5) Almost as soon as I posted these, I had the same thought you did, but I couldn't take it back at that point. I apologize.<br /> <br /> 6-7) Very true. That is on me.<br /> <br /> Part of <u>my</u> problem is that I have all these things floating around in my head, and I will be the first one to admit it: I have trouble writing things from Kasai's POV without revealing stuff that, to him (and therefore, to you as the audience), he would have no way of knowing. For example: the weapons? To me, they <i>could be</i> important in the fight, as they have <span style=\"font-family:Courier\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\">kinda-sorta special abilities, like very-specific D&amp;D-style 'weapons of slaying' </div></div> </div> </div></span></span><br /> <br /> But <i>Kasai isn't going to know that</i>. The Warchief will, and will refer to them, but, yet again, I felt that if I went heavy into the details, it would basically slap a great big &quot;Look at this!&quot; marker on the things. Therefore, I prefer to downplay the &quot;importance&quot; of things. <u>evidently</u>, that isn't working too well, so that is something I will certainly change in the future.<br /> <br /> Now, admittedly, this is prreeeettttyyyyyyy much my first foray into actual writing for people outside my immediate circle of friends, so I expect there to be several massive bumps along the road. I do appreciate the writing advice, especially from someone that has two Quest threads under their belt.<br /> <br /> Amusingly enough, &quot;From Out of a Dream&quot; was pretty much the first thing I ever read on this forum, and it is what gave me the courage to start this bad boy, so, all the respect in the world to you.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11892,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","user":"Boston123","date":". 04-18-2017, 08:17 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-19T12:19:54-07:00","id":1081025,"title":"","content":"I feel much as semicasual did. We are fucked friends. I dont care if its not a combat master, it is still a god damn lunar with powers and a statistical bell curve we could never hope for. I think we have a few options but most likely all of them will lead to our deaths and if we're going to die, i hope we do it being as Kasai as possible. We fight the beast but we'll have to get creative. <br /> <br /> We have staked this deal on reputation and our resume, we can't just shirk that. If we die then at least we committed to that reputation as an oathsworn. Hopefully the confederacy will remember that when deciding the deal with Xia. Things we should be considering as we go into the fight:<br /> <br /> 1. How do we deal with the warchief? She is a social fu expert and having another person around to fight us is a bad idea. Telling him to leave might be best so it isn't 2 on 1, but we can't kill him as she suggests. On the other hand if he sticks around he could possibly help. If he does we could use him to help divide her attention long enough to possibly grab a weapon or some such mystical object or even take time praying to luna to weaken this bat shit evil monster.<br /> <br /> 2. Can we take time to scope out the actual temple through delaying tactics to get a better sense of what is around us that might be usable?<br /> <br /> 3. I assume the chamber makes all of this close quarters, something that will be awful for us to deal with especially with a being that can transform its hands and fingers into tentacles and sharp things.<br /> <br /> 4. Our only hope is injuring the damn thing first and hoping the penalties come in too early for it to be effective in eating us. <br /> <br /> It was an honor in being Kasai with you all <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8897-joshopotamus","name":"joshopotamus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8897_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"97"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-19T17:38:41-07:00","id":1081198,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>joshopotamus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1081025#post1081025\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I feel much as semicasual did. We are fucked friends. I dont care if its not a combat master, it is still a god damn lunar with powers and a statistical bell curve we could never hope for. I think we have a few options but most likely all of them will lead to our deaths and if we're going to die, i hope we do it being as Kasai as possible. We fight the beast but we'll have to get creative.<br /> <br /> We have staked this deal on reputation and our resume, we can't just shirk that. If we die then at least we committed to that reputation as an oathsworn. Hopefully the confederacy will remember that when deciding the deal with Xia. Things we should be considering as we go into the fight:<br /> <br /> 1. How do we deal with the warchief? She is a social fu expert and having another person around to fight us is a bad idea. Telling him to leave might be best so it isn't 2 on 1, but we can't kill him as she suggests. On the other hand if he sticks around he could possibly help. If he does we could use him to help divide her attention long enough to possibly grab a weapon or some such mystical object or even take time praying to luna to weaken this bat shit evil monster.<br /> <br /> 2. Can we take time to scope out the actual temple through delaying tactics to get a better sense of what is around us that might be usable?<br /> <br /> 3. I assume the chamber makes all of this close quarters, something that will be awful for us to deal with especially with a being that can transform its hands and fingers into tentacles and sharp things.<br /> <br /> 4. Our only hope is injuring the damn thing first and hoping the penalties come in too early for it to be effective in eating us.<br /> <br /> It was an honor in being Kasai with you all <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" /></div> </div> </div> </div><br /> 1) Arguably, the warchief is on Kasai's &quot;level&quot; of combat ability, if not a <i>wee</i> bit better (You don't get elected as the martial representative of several thousand people by being a slugass). He is less protected than Kasai is (wearing less armor, so less soak), but his weapon is big and gnarly, so he can cause some damage.<br /> <br /> As for mental resistance and such, Kasai has a pretty high Resolve ([Wits + Integrity + Specialty]/2) = ([ 2 {wits} + 4 {Integrity} + 1 {when Oaths are involved}] /2 = 4, plus he has a Defining concept of &quot;Upholding my Oaths is worth everything, even my life.&quot;, on top of having Willpower out the ass (on the quick and dirty character sheet I hammered out for him, I had him pegged at a 6, with room for a 7), so unless the Lunar goes all out on Kasai, he isn't going to break.<br /> <br /> The warchief, on the other hand, isn't nearly so driven, although he does have a Major tie of &quot;This Lunar bitch [hatred]&quot;, as well as &quot;Seek revenge for my slain bretheren&quot;, so that is something.<br /> <br /> Hint: I am a <u>big</u> fan of using Willpower as a 'resource&quot;, so to speak, in my mortal campaigns, to the point where it is basically the mortal version of Essence and motes. My players usually have a bunch of Intimacies, and (over time), learned to use stunts/reinforcement of Intimacies to basically farm for Willpower, which they would then use to supplement rolls and values.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://tvtropes.org/pmwiki/pmwiki.php/Main/HeroicSpirit\" target=\"_blank\">http://tvtropes.org/pmwiki/pmwiki.php/Main/HeroicSpirit</a> <br /> <br /> is one of my favorite tropes, for what it is worth.<br /> <br /> And that is all I am going to say about that.<br /> <br /> 2) Of course. Write in what you want to do/say, and I will roll with it.<br /> <br /> 3) The chamber is, well, enclosed, but it is about 30-40 feet in diameter, meaning there is room to move around, but most of the combat would be at Close and Short Range. The flagstone floor is flat, but it is covered in bones (like, seriously, bones for days), making the ground &quot;Rough Terrain&quot; for both parties. There is a pool of water in the middle of the chamber, so that is something we would have to avoid, but the only real cover in the chamber is the altar at the far end.<br /> <br /> Kasai is in full panoply (full mail, with spear, sword, dagger, shield and bow), so unless she has armor-piercing claws, or specifically aims for the area exposed by his coif (basically, his eyes and the bridge of his nose)<br /> <br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_armour#/media/File:Kusari_gusoku_11.JPG\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Samurai-armor-costume-2019-1-4.webm/1280px--Samurai-armor-costume-2019-1-4.webm.jpg\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_armour#/media/File:Kusari_gusoku_11.JPG\" target=\"_blank\"> Japanese armour - Wikipedia </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_armour#/media/File:Kusari_gusoku_11.JPG\" target=\"_blank\"> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_armour#/media/File:Kusari_gusoku_11.JPG </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> she isn't really going to just be able to plow through. Of course, she is a Full Moon right now, so if she decides to spend Essence, her fists are basically maces....<br /> <br /> When she isn't spending Essence, Kasai is, in all seriousness, taller and stronger than she is.<br /> <br /> As for animal forms: she is largely limited to what animals can be found in the East. That is about the extent of what I am going to reveal.<br /> <br /> 4) Onslaught penalties are a thing. I <u>love</u> me some Onslaught Penalties : )","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-19T22:07:18-07:00","id":1081295,"title":"","content":"I vote fight, we swore an oath to hunt down this monster, and die it shall. Legends don't write them selves.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1435-videopete","name":"videopete","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1435_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"111"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-21T06:25:05-07:00","id":1081804,"title":"","content":"*nods* We hit her hard, fast and with our 'friend' the warchief. Sucker punch/Surprise attack if possible but she needs to be overwhelmed before she can bring her 'god-body' essence against us. She is less of a threat if she is in Crash, or suffering wound penalties. Heck just getting some damage on her track before we roll for initiative would be fantastic.<br /> <br /> Once we start this, we don't stop. We can't let her get out either. It will be worse if she escapes and seeks revenge. We'll have to block the exit from a shapeshifting monster. joy.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Thoughts ripple out, birthing others","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1624-falleneco","name":"FallenEco","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1624_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1927"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-25T18:46:40-07:00","id":1083776,"title":"","content":"\u2026..guess we are gonna throw down and mix it up!<br /> <br /> ------<br /> (Just a quick baby update)<br /> <br /> Grinning happily, you respond with, \u201c Of <i>course</i>, my lady, but first, may I ask for a boon?\u201d<br /> <br /> The Lunar stands back, smirking and obviously pleased with herself. \u201cIt depends, but luckily for you, I am in a giving mood.\u201d<br /> <br /> Going to one knee and dropping your spear,\u201d Nothing more than a kiss, for your loyal servant? As a sign of good faith?\u201d<br /> <br /> She stares for a moment, then, shrugging, \u201c\u2026. I suppose, if you can\u2019t even wait for a minute or two\u2026\u201d<br /> <br /> As she steps forward, flipping her hair nonchalantly over her shoulder, you hear the Paskamansett warchief step back, muttering and tightening his grip on his waraxe. Even though you try desperately to keep your countenance impassive, <i>something</i>, some tightening of the eyes, some tensing of the body, must have tipped her off, and she leaps back, only just avoiding the sudden slash of your sword, drawn as you rose.<br /> <br /> The blade whips up, left to right, and draws a line through both the silken gown and her ivory skin, parting both with nary a whisper. She cries out, and for a moment, the slightest of heartbeats, she ceases to look like the monster she is, and instead resembles nothing more than a slighted girl. The image passes, and she grins, eyes gleaming liquid silver, as the skin across her breast seals up seamlessly.<br /> <br /> \u201cYou know\u2026.. I\u2019m not even <i>mad</i>. Ordinarily, I would at least expect you to take me out to dinner first before getting rough, but you went out of your way to bring it here! I really should thank you\u2026\u201d<br /> <br /> Snarling, with your sword-point between you and the demon, \u201c <i>Please</i>, demon! Your foul charms fill any righteous man with disgust, as they should! Prepare to die!\u201d<br /> <br /> The Lunar grins, and to your horror, her fingers elongate into tiger claws with the cracking of bone.<br /> <br /> As you start orbiting around the side, the warchief chimes in, \u201c&lt; You know, I was getting worried for a moment. What did it offer you?&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt; The bitch said she would lay with me if I killed you. Obviously, I didn\u2019t take the bait. We need to split up her atten..&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt; You know, it is <i>incredibly rude</i> to carry on a conversation in another language, when someone else is present. I mean, I am <i>right</i> here\u2026..&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> The warchief shrugs, \u201cSo sorry, but I don\u2019t pay much attention to the feelings of dead men!&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt;So confident! Maybe I should have started with you, instead of mr. \u2013stick-in-the-mud over there!&gt;\u201d She turns to the warchief, eyes and forehead gleaming. \u201c&lt; What do say, old buck? Kill the young one, and I will leave these lands and never return!&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> He stares, then shakes his head, \u201c&lt;N\u2026 No! You killed my warriors, killed my <i>people!</i> I would never give in to you!&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> The Anathema laughs, leaning forwards coyly and hiding her claws behind her back, \u201c&lt; You don\u2019t know what happened, do you? Your warriors attacked <i>me</i>, and I fought them in self-defense! Maybe they were smitten by my unearthly beauty, and couldn\u2019t help themselves? Regardless, I just want to live in peace! Don\u2019t you want peace? It is possible, and will only take your leaving this place at once\u2026..&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> Even though the Anathema\u2019s voice isn\u2019t directed at you, the throbbing power in her words make your knees quake and your heart pound. The warchief, feeling the brunt of the siren-song, trembles all over, sweating, his eyes rolling in his sockets, from you to the Anathema and back. His axe slowly falls, until it hangs uselessly at his side\u2026\u2026<br /> <br /> He stands there motionless, breathing heavily, until you suddenly leap towards the Lunar with a cry of fury leaving your lips. She twists sinuously, gracefully as a bolt of falling silk, and the point of your blade meets only air. The warchief starts, as if waking from a dream, and hefts his axe warily, wrath entering his eyes.<br /> <br /> She sneers, \u201cYou <i>really</i> need to learn to butt out of other people\u2019s conversations, asshole.\u201d<br /> <br /> You smirk, \u201cWhat was that about having conversations when others are present, again? I was just returning the favor!\u201d<br /> <br /> She snarls in response, suddenly leaping forwards across the chamber with a speed and grace beyond all mortal ken, colliding with you before you can get the point of your blade up to meet her. In spite of her slight (yet tremulous. <i>Oh so tremulous</i>) frame, she impacts with the force of a battering ram, knocking you both to the floor and sending your sword skittering through the bones. She grins widely (looking more and more like some bestial creature baring her fangs by the second), sharp teeth snapping scant millimeters away from your face, and her claws scrap over your mail. To your surprise (and terror), the claws rend the links, popping rivets and bending steel, although the underpadding holds firm. Her arms are as strong as steel as well, and you fight a losing battle keeping her teeth from your throat.<br /> <br /> Only a few heartbeats pass, but it feels like an eternity before the warchief charges across the bone-strewn floor, driving his axe into the shoulder of the crazed animal-woman and kicking her in the ribs, ripping her iron-grip from your shoulders and sending her sprawling across the floor. She screams as the wound heals, the combination of human, fox and other beasts turning your blood to ice.<br /> <br /> He laughs as he helps you up, \u201c&lt;Surprised you did I? Stupid bitch, forget there are two of us again, and it will be the end of you!&gt;\u201d Aside, to you, \u201c&lt; Run for the altar, there are sacrificial weapons there, weapons of the sacred hunt, blessed by the Moon-Goddess herself! If anything can take this demon down, it will be them!&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> She laughs (giggles, really) as she rises into a half-crouch, silver and black, purple and blue swirling about her like trees in a storm. \u201c&lt; You old fool, the Moon-Goddess <i>is my patron</i>. What makes you think weapons dedicated to her will do me any harm?!&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> He shrugs, presenting his waraxe, \u201c&lt; I dunno, <i>demon</i>, we seem to be kicking your ass with mere mortal weapons, we might not even need the blessed ones!&gt;\u201d Raising the axe over his head, \u201c&lt; For as long as you live, I want you to remember this: for all your power, your \u201cgodhood\u201d, you are <b>losing</b>. How many times have we bled, compared to you?! I, Corbitant of the Paskamansett, have made you <i>scream in pain</i>! Remember that!&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> She screams again, in fury and hatred, and leaps across the chamber faster than the eyes can see. The warch\u2026. Corbitant makes a valiant effort to step aside, to no avail, and they make the bones fly in their wake. The Lunar Anathema lifts the man almost effortlessly, in one hand, and slams him bodily into the wall, then wraps her clawed hands around his neck. She is smiling again, but there is no amusement in her eyes, and her bared teeth look more like the fangs of a fox than any human expression.<br /> <br /> Her fingers tighten inexorably, irresistibly, and Corbitant\u2019s face turns from red, to purple, to blue, and his eyes bulge from his skull. Kick at her as he might (and he is breaking ribs, you can hear that from across the room), the Lunar doesn\u2019t let up her grip, and he rolls his eyes at you in mute appeal.<br /> <br /> She giggles again, high-pitched and demented, and when she opens her mouth, instead of words, from between her lips slithers a snake, fangs dripping with venom. As it rears back to strike, you,<ol class=\"decimal\"><li>Run forwards to attack the Lunar Anathema! Her back is turned, you could stab her in the neck, the kidneys, or even the heart!</li> <li>Jump forward and save Corbitant! He saved you, you can\u2019t just let him die! Besides, the more help you have for this fight, the better!</li> <li>Go look at the altar, while her focus isn\u2019t on you! If what Corbitant says is right, you might find something to even the odds a bit!</li> <li>Something else?</li> </ol>She has got between 7 and 14 dice (with excellencies) of Read Intentions, so fooling her really wasn't going to happen. Not with Kasai's shit-tastic Guile rating, that is.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11892,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","user":"Boston123","date":". 04-25-2017, 09:50 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-25T19:20:04-07:00","id":1083784,"title":"","content":"Back turned means a shot at her neck, especially with a snake tongue. Can you imagine how distracting it would be to have a snake for a tongue working it's way out of your mouth? Take the head and save the warchief who has a name so is no longer a red shirt.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11567-jairain","name":"Jairain","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11567_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"345"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-25T20:39:48-07:00","id":1083802,"title":"","content":"1 and 2 are pretty much the same, so both!<br /> <br /> &quot;When morale is low, attack. When the weather is bad, attack. When you are outnumbered, attack. Attack! <u>Attack</u>!! <b>Attack!!</b>!&quot;, <i>the Star Khan of Radha</i>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Like my Avatar? Courtesy of<span style=\"color:#0000FF\"> Jen</span>! : <span style=\"font-size:12px\">Anybody want their characters to be experimented on ? </span>post 98<br /> <i>An Exalt is never unarmed.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4018-l-het-esh","name":"L'het'esh","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4018_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"521"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-26T05:01:40-07:00","id":1083921,"title":"","content":"I would like to draw everyone's attention to these two sentences:<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Boston123</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1083776#post1083776\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">...The image passes, and she grins, eyes gleaming liquid silver, as the skin across her breast seals up seamlessly....<br /> <br /> .... She screams as the wound heals, the combination of human, fox and other beasts turning your blood to ice..</div> </div> </div> </div>3. Go for the altar. Attacking might buy Corbitant some time, but we won't win this fight if we can't find something to inflict wounds that she cannot immediately heal. <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>On the frontier of the Wild South, there's only one woman with the grit to take on its most dangerous outlaws and bring them <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/984977-back-alive-or-maybe-dead-quest\" target=\"_blank\">Back Alive, or Maybe Dead.</a><br /> <br /> Avatar by <a href=\"http://puzzlinghappenstance.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">K.S. Brenowitz</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/908-semicasual","name":"semicasual","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar908_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2499"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-30T17:53:47-07:00","id":1085973,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8897-joshopotamus\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar8897_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"8897\" data-vbnamecard=\"8897\">joshopotamus</a> <br /> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1435-videopete\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar1435_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"1435\" data-vbnamecard=\"1435\">videopete</a> <br /> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1624-falleneco\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar1624_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"1624\" data-vbnamecard=\"1624\">FallenEco</a> <br /> \u200b<br /> What do you think?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-30T21:14:28-07:00","id":1086048,"title":"","content":"I vote...have our cake and eat it too!<br /> <br /> Corbitant didn't get to be warchief by being an idiot. If he thinks these sacred weapons might help...well, then we wont' need this one.<br /> <br /> We're a good shot. I say we utter a quiet prayer and throw our sword like a spear. We put it through her in the hopes she'll drop our new friend...or at least that having a sword stuck through her from behind will slow her down enough for him to take advantage of the opening and get loose...and then dash for the sacred weapons while she's distracted.<br /> <br /> Flurries are our friend!<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>So I'm making <a href=\"http://godkickingboot.smackjeeves.com/\" target=\"_blank\">God-Kicking Boot</a>, an Exalted webcomic, now. Updates on Sundays. Full-color, mediocre but slowly improving art. It's a thing.<br /> <br /> The absence of a monument can, in its own way, be something of a monument also.</i> -Roger Zelazny","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/512-wise-old-guru","name":"Wise Old Guru","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar512_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1097"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-01T03:26:37-07:00","id":1086122,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wise Old Guru</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1086048#post1086048\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I vote...have our cake and eat it too!<br /> <br /> Corbitant didn't get to be warchief by being an idiot. If he thinks these sacred weapons might help...well, then we wont' need this one.<br /> <br /> We're a good shot. I say we utter a quiet prayer and throw our sword like a spear. We put it through her in the hopes she'll drop our new friend...or at least that having a sword stuck through her from behind will slow her down enough for him to take advantage of the opening and get loose...and then dash for the sacred weapons while she's distracted.<br /> <br /> Flurries are our friend!</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Kasai's sword is currently <i>somewhere</i> in the piles of bones, as is his spear. All he has on him at this point is his dagger and his bow.<br /> <br /> 1) His bow is in a lacquered wood sheath, bound to the right hip /small of the back. He could draw it and knock an arrow in but a moment. <br /> 2) His dagger is bound across the front of the torso, angled in such a fashion that he could draw it with either the left or right hand. It isn't designed for throwing, nor is Kasai all that skilled at throwing knives, but taking a combination of sharp steel and heavy bronze to the arm or face would be a distraction, Anathema or not.<br /> <br /> For what it is worth, we are equidistant between both the altar and the scuffle.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-01T05:49:28-07:00","id":1086152,"title":"","content":"If he's got the bow on him, even better! Put an arrow in her arm while backing away toward the altar. It's probably harder to heal wounds when there are arrows stuck in them...and pulling the arrows out takes time. This way we help Corbitant, move towards the altar, and hopefully continue to hinder our foe without getting close enough for her to murder us.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>So I'm making <a href=\"http://godkickingboot.smackjeeves.com/\" target=\"_blank\">God-Kicking Boot</a>, an Exalted webcomic, now. Updates on Sundays. Full-color, mediocre but slowly improving art. It's a thing.<br /> <br /> The absence of a monument can, in its own way, be something of a monument also.</i> -Roger Zelazny","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/512-wise-old-guru","name":"Wise Old Guru","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar512_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1097"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-01T09:50:31-07:00","id":1086234,"title":"","content":"Throw the knife run for the alter. If the knife doesn't hit maybe it will distract her? Either way corbitant is screwed and we aren't going to really be capable of doing much with a knife against this beast. The bow would probably take forever and i doubt it would be all that effective. I say throw the knife and hunt through the alter for weapons we probably wont be capable of wielding <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/frown.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Frown\" smilieid=\"11\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8897-joshopotamus","name":"joshopotamus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8897_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"97"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-01T15:18:20-07:00","id":1086354,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>joshopotamus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1086234#post1086234\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Throw the knife run for the alter. If the knife doesn't hit maybe it will distract her? Either way corbitant is screwed and we aren't going to really be capable of doing much with a knife against this beast. The bow would probably take forever and i doubt it would be all that effective. I say throw the knife and hunt through the alter for weapons we probably wont be capable of wielding <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/frown.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Frown\" smilieid=\"11\" class=\"inlineimg\" /></div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Nah, they will be all stuff Kasai is capable of using.<br /> <br /> Overwhelmingly, outside of bronze or steel shortswords (which Kasai can use anyways), most Hill-Tribe weapons are intended for hunting use. So: spears, javelins, bows and arrows, knives, clubs/axes, etc.<br /> <br /> The only Hill-Tribe weapon Kasai isn't proficient with would be the sling.<br /> <br /> Besides, Kasai is a <b>warrior</b>, and has been training with arms since he was 7-and-younger. If there is something there he doesn't immediately recognize..... He can make do.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11892,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","user":"Boston123","date":". 05-01-2017, 06:21 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-01T20:58:22-07:00","id":1086453,"title":"","content":"Start shooting arrows to draw the monsters attention while running to the Altar, while screaming songs to the Moon Goddess. Drown out her siren's voice with ritousness. Show her what Luna thinks of those who abandon thier charge.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1435-videopete","name":"videopete","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1435_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"111"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-06T20:36:33-07:00","id":1088988,"title":"","content":"Hmmm\u2026.<br /> <br /> Several of you want to go attack the Lunar, and save Corbitant at the same time<br /> <br /> Several others want to break for the blessed weapons ASAP<br /> <br /> Others want to attack the Lunar in order to save Corbitant, <i>then</i> go for the weapons<br /> \u2026. Very well. I will work with what you\u2019ve given me.<br /> <br /> --------------------------------------------------<br /> <br /> Hurriedly, you snatch at the dagger bound up in your sash. As the snake-tongue flashes forwards, you throw it. You\u2019ve never had much skill at knife-throwing, and your dagger isn\u2019t weighted for throwing anyways, but you can\u2019t help but feel a small glow of satisfaction as the dagger impacts the body of the snake.<br /> <br /> Distracted, the Lunar whips her head about, hissing at the interruption. As you make for the altar, sending bones skittering in front of you, you draw your bow from its cover. Mid-stride, you draw and loose an arrow, not your best shot! Loosed quickly, and scarcely aimed at all, the arrow zooms across the chamber, and as you reach the altar, you hear two screams of pain from behind you!<br /> <br /> Chancing a glance up from the altar, you see that the arrow, as poorly aimed as it was, <i>somehow</i> managed to connect not with the Lunar, but with her snake-tongue, uncoiled in mid-strike, and the serpent flails around wildly, spewing blood and venom in its death-throes. The Lunar dropped Corbitant in her pain, who is curled up against the wall, wailing in fright. After but a moment, she yanks the arrow out from the snake, and turns to you, eyes blazing with fury and a froth of blood and saliva heavy on her lips. Another arrow loosed in her direction merely shatters, deflected off her skin as easily as harmlessly as a reed.<br /> <br /> In near-panic, you quickly scan the slate tablet, heaped with votive objects and weapons, for something, <i>anything</i>, you can use! A bronze dagger\u2026 no. An iron spearhead, bent into a zig-zag\u2026. Useless. A flint spearhead\u2026. Maybe\u2026.. there! Arrows, of flint and bone and bronze, fletched with eagle plumes! You snatch up a handful of the projectiles, and hurriedly fit the nocks of one to your string.<br /> <br /> As you draw the arrow back, you look up and see in terror that the Lunar is all-but 10 feet from you. She draws back her claws to strike, and as the razor-sharp talons descend, you loose. At this range, you pretty much can\u2019t miss, yet she twists and flows like a drop of quicksilver\u2026.. not enough! The arrow impacts her shoulder with a wet <i>thok</i>, and the scream that leaves her throat makes any earlier cries of pain sound gentle by comparison. She drops to her knees, grasping at the arrow-shaft punched through both sides of her shoulder, and to your surprise, the wound (and the blood running from it, down her body and dripping from the point) is sizzling, <i>smoking, <b>burning</b></i>. The sight is so much, for a moment you forget what you were doing, bow-and-arrows held numbly by your side<br /> <br /> She cries out in agony, and with titanic effort, yanks the flint-tipped arrow from her body. Unlike the rest of the other wounds you have inflicted over this battle, <i>this one doesn\u2019t close</i>. She screams again, in pain and fear, argent corona condensing into foxfire tails spread out behind her, yet the wound remains fresh, bleeding. She turns to you, eyes filled with tears, and cries almost piteously,<br /> \u201cWhat have you done to me!? WHAT HAVE YOU DONE TO ME!? Why am I bleeding, I\u2019m not <b><i><u>supposed</u></i></b> to bleed! Luna, why have you forsaken me?!\u201d<br /> <br /> Your only response is another arrow, drawn once you collected your wits. She makes no effort to dodge this one, in the middle of her panic attack, and the arrow punches into her thigh easily. With nothing more than a soft grunt, she sinks down to the floor, lying deathly still.<br /> <br /> Shocked, you remain still for a few moments, your heartbeat pounding. It\u2026. it couldn\u2019t have been <i>that</i> easy? Yet it looks like the Lunar isn\u2019t even breathing, and she is lying so still\u2026\u2026<br /> <br /> Cautiously, to avoid disturbing her if she is only unconscious, you make your way to within a few feet before drawing back your final scavenged arrow. You want to be able to put an arrow directly through her black heart, and if she is just unconscious, you might not have time to search for more, what with her Anathema powers and all.<br /> <br /> This close, the stench of burning flesh is overpowering, and you can see the flesh blackened and charred where the two arrows penetrated. Ordinarily, you would be confused and fearful at the sight of something so overtly supernatural, but at this point, you will take whatever help you can get!<br /> <br /> As you draw the arrow back to your ear, the bow-lathe creaks, and right before you loose, you suddenly catch a glimpse of her face, silver light escaping from the slits of her eyes. Before you can jump away, she rises to a knee, and you scream in pain as a line of fire traces its way across your chest, slicing through your mail and underpadding as easily as a knife through paper.<br /> <br /> Staggering back, hands to your chest, you are in no way capable of stopping the blow to your face, were you even capable of seeing it coming. Your head snaps around, hard and fast enough to make the ligaments <i>crack</i>, and you hear your teeth and nose break before the pain comes.<br /> <br /> You don\u2019t even feel the punch to the ribs, nor the impact with the wall across the chamber. The final impact, with the stone floor, is like something out of a dream. Nothing even really <i>hurts</i>, just\u2026. numb, like an icy winter fog, and your thoughts are slow and sluggish.<br /> <br /> After what feels like an eternity, feeling comes back to you, and the first thing you can feel for real is <i>something</i> resting uncomfortably under your head and neck. Feeling around clumsily (your left hand doesn\u2019t want to work, for some reason), you discover the uncomfortable pillow is nothing other than your spear, laid on the floor at the beginning of the fight. With immense effort, you manage to get yourself up on your knees (your entire right arm went icy-numb again, when you tried to use it to lift yourself), keeping one hand on the spear, and spit out a torrent of blood and shattered teeth.<br /> <br /> Across the chamber, you see the Lunar on her knees, trying to pull the arrow from her upper thigh. At the slightest touch, she groans in pain, yet after a moment, she yanks this arrow free as well. Just like before, this wound remains open, and even from this distance, you smell the blood. She screams in frustration, silver disc and corona flaring, making the chamber as bright as day, but to no avail. With the crack of bone and wet sound of tearing flesh, she <i>transforms</i>, from a woman to a fox, which immediately drops to the ground, two legs of four useless and limp. With another flare of light, she changes back, sobbing, dropping back against the wall.<br /> <br /> She is startled from her breakdown by your laughter. In a voice, hoarse with pain, you call out, \u201cLooks like \u2018your patron\u2019 doesn\u2019t agree with the whole \u2018living god\u201d part, Anathema!\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c<i>Shut. Up.\u201d</i><br /> <br /> Instead, you laugh again, \u201cYou know, I know where I shot you. If that bleeding continues, you only have a few minutes before you bleed out and die. I suggest you stop crying and make your peace.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cI\u2019m not the one who is going to die, <b>mortal</b>. I am a god, I can\u2019t die from mere blood loss\u2026.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cYou don\u2019t sound too confident, Anathema! What is your problem with dying? Are you a coward? You must be, considering how you are here, hiding in the mountains, away from the Wyld Hunt! I find that <i>immensely</i> funny. You see\u2026 here I am, a mere mortal in your terms, yet I came to my death with no fear, while you weep and scream at it! <i>Hypocrite</i>! That makes me, in a way, \u2018better than you\u2019, doesn\u2019t it?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cStop talking, STOP TALKING, <b><i><u>STOP TALKING!</u></i></b>\u201d<br /> <br /> You grin, grasping your spear in preparation, \u201c\u2026..<i>make me</i>, Anathema.\u201d<br /> <br /> With a scream of fury, she wrenches herself upright, and charges into a shambling run. Stumbling through the bones, her claws raised, her teeth bared, she staggers towards you. A few feet away, she pounces, all grace and fluidity gone.<br /> <br /> At the last moment, you grab your spear and brace it against the wall behind you. With the backspike firmly set, you lift it and hold the point out in front of you.<br /> <br /> Directly in the path of the Lunar.<br /> <br /> This close, you can see her eyes widen, and she makes an effort to twist and avoid the glittering edge. Inexorably, the point connects with the base of her throat, right between the collarbones, and punches through her marble skin. You feel the spearshaft shudder as the point, a full foot of Vanehani steel, cracks her spine in half and cuts through the back side of her neck.<br /> Due to her momentum, she almost pulls the spear from her grasp when the two connect, and she shaft <i>snaps</i> under her weight. She impacts the wall beside you, breaking something else (the spearhead or her neck, you can\u2019t quite tell), and she slides down next to you.<br /> <br /> At this close range, you can see her eyes tremble and her lips quiver, and she gasps for air. The silver disc on her forehead <i>burns</i>, and before she can heal from that final effort (you are certain you won\u2019t be able to do that again!), you raise the broken spearshaft in two hands, and with a cry of effort, bring it down on her sternum. The bronze backspike punches through her skin and sternum, and for a moment you can feel her heart trembling on its point, fluttering like a bird. With a savage wrench, you tear it open, and the silver-and-purple corona surrounding the Lunar Anathema cuts out, covering the chamber in a layer of dew and hoarfrost.<br /> <br /> You gasp for air, all strength suddenly gone, and with the weakness comes back the fog, creeping over your senses and slowing your thoughts. Finding it hard to concentrate, you shake your head dumbly, and go to take your feet. As soon as you put weight on your left leg, the numbness changes to cold fire, and all goes black.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> It is dark and cold, and for a moment, you don\u2019t remember who you are. You are afraid\u2026.. and wet. Very wet. Why the hell are you so wet?<br /> <br /> After a moment, cold water dribbles on your forehead, and your eyes snap open. Almost instantly, you close them in agony, groaning. Your head <i>throbs</i>, and you feel like you might vomit. With your eyes clamped shut, the nausea slowly passes, and you carefully crack your eyes open.<br /> <br /> It is dark in the chamber, the only light coming from the full moon above, beaming light down into the pool, and it takes a few moments for your eyes to acclimate to the darkness. Looming over you is a human figure, features obscured by shadow, and you start in terror. The figure hears you move, and shifts back into the moonlight. It is Corbitant, kneeling by your side.<br /> <br /> Relief plain in his voice, \u201c&lt; Good, I was starting to wonder if the two of you killed each other.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> Confused, \u201c&lt;What do you mean?&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> He laughs, \u201c&lt; What do I mean? What do you think? The Lunar is dead, that is what I mean!&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt;\u2026.. really? Huh&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> He nods, \u201c&lt; Yes, cold and dead, the best kind of dead.&gt;\u201d He turns his face from you, \u201c&lt; I am guessing you passed out, too? I just woke up a few minutes ago.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> Your only response is a groan of pain, as the fog starts to get replaced with embers. It feels like your entire body has been beaten with a big ol\u2019 stick, a wet one.<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt; Wh\u2026. Why am I wet?&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt; I was dribbling water on you, to see if it would help you wake up.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> Giving a grunt in response, you gingerly start feeling your body, to see just what the hell happened to you. It hurts to breathe too deeply, and your side aches, both your ribs and the slash on your chest. Your nose is definitely broken, but the bleeding has stopped (and the clots are inside your nose, ow!), and your entire left jawline is a mass of splintered teeth and agony, and all you can taste is blood. Your right arm is almost-certainly broken as well, telling from the pain, and based on that some fingers on your left hand seem to be broken as well; at the very least, two of the fingers don\u2019t want to bend, and hurt then you try to make them. Your neck aches, and it feels stiff and weird when you try to turn your head. There are many other more minor cuts, scrapes and bruises, too many to count, but they appear to be minor upon cursory inspection.<br /> <br /> No, the worst of your injuries appears to be your left leg (damn, that was the \u2018good\u2019 one!) and your head.<br /> <br /> Gingerly, you feel around the back of your head, stomach sinking when you feel a mass of matted hair and clotted blood. The bleeding has apparently stopped, but your coif and underpadding is absolutely <i>soaked</i>, and upon thinking on how much blood you may have lost, you feel weak and sick. Exploring around the wound some more, you come across the edge of the wound, and even when applying the gentlest of pressures, you feel the plates of your skull suddenly <i>shift</i>, and the nausea rises up, hot and acidic. You vomit, spewing breakfast (that was so long ago!) across the floor, and Corbitant gently passes you his helmet, filled with water from the pool, to rinse out your mouth.<br /> <br /> After rinsing out your mouth, you still feel weak and sick, and the room spins around you. After a few long heartbeats, the spinning stops, and Corbitant leans over.<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt; What is wrong?&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> Blood acrid on your mouth, \u201c&lt; I\u2026\u2026 I cracked my skull. It must have been when the Lunar threw me into the wall.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt;\u2026.. Damn, there isn\u2019t much I do about that. Anything else?&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt; Yes, my left leg. It hurts really bad.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> Feeling up and down the limb, it is readily apparent that the leg isn\u2019t actually broken, but instead the ball of the hip is projecting forwards, straining against the skin, burning with cold fire. You tremble and shake, and not just from the cold.<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt; I think my hip is dislocated.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt; Let me see&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> He leans over you, and gently pokes at your left hip. Even the gentle touch, little more than a fingertip, makes you groan in pain and break out in a sweat.<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt; \u2026. Yes, sorry Kasai, but your hip is popped out of place. You said she threw you into a wall? You must have hit wrong. Fortunately for you, I know how to put it back in, but\u2026\u2026 well, it is going to hurt.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt; How bad are we talking?&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt;Likely worse than anything you have ever felt. But, if you want to walk, it is going to have to happen.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> You nod, but he doesn\u2019t respond (odd). \u201c&lt; I guess you might as well go ahead, then.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> In response, he takes a deep breath, and shuffles around you, feeling his way with his hands.<br /> <br /> Confused, you ask, \u201c&lt; Corbitant, can\u2019t you see? Is it too dark?&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> He laughs darkly, \u201c&lt; No, Kasai, I can\u2019t see. The Lunar bitch spat some sort of venom at me, and it got in my eyes. &gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> The blood drains from your face, and you feel immensely guily. \u201c&lt; I\u2026 I am so sorry Corbitant, but I think that was my fault. I shot an arrow at her, and I hit the snake.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> He is quiet for a moment, then shrugging, \u201c&lt; Eh, I would rather be blind than dead any day of the week. I came into this fight expecting to die, so anything other than that is a plus.&gt;\u201d Grabbing ahold of your foot and placing his foot into your crotch, \u201c&lt; Are you ready?&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> Taking a cloth from your sash, you stuff it between your teeth, \u201c&lt; About as ready as I will ever be.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> With that, he <i>pulls</i>, and the cold fire burning in your hip turns hot, arcing up and down your body like hot sunlight on steel. Your spine arcs involuntarily, and your scream behind the gag. Just as your vision starts to blacken around the edges, the knob of your leg pops back into place, and the white fire subsides to a deep, aching throb. Still painful, of course, but far preferable to what came before, and after you catch your breath, you find you can move the leg.<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt;\u2026. Thank you.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt; No problem. I owe you my life, and the spirits of my dead people thank you.&gt;\u201d, pressing his forehead to the floor.<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt; Corbitant, if it wasn\u2019t for you, I would have gotten killed in the first 5 minutes of the fight! I should be thanking you!\u201d&gt;<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt; Ah\u2026. What is a \u2018minute\u2019?&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt; \u2026\u2026 never mind.&gt;\u201d Sitting up, wincing at the pain in your ribs, \u201c&lt; I guess the right thing to ask is, \u2018what are we going to do now\u2019?\u201d<br /> <br /> He sits back, kneeling, \u201c I think we should sacrifice the Lunar to Luna, and consume her heart. If She accepts the sacrifice, the sacrificed\u2019 strength will flow into us, healing our wounds.&gt;\u201d Slowly, cautiously, \u201c&lt; Your grandfather did that, 30 years ago during the Feast of the Moon. Look at him now!&gt;\u201d Then, viciously, \u201c&lt; Plus, it would be retribution for my slaughtered warriors, my people who were killed by this monster! Giving it a taste of its own medicine would be karma!&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> You balk at the suggestion, \u201c&lt; Corbitant, that is \u2026\u2026. well, cannibalism, man! Isn\u2019t that ritual done on <i>animals</i>?!&gt;<br /> <br /> He snarls, \u201c&lt; Yes, but what this bitch did <i>makes</i> her an animal! She killed <i>children</i>, Kasai, and ate their hearts!&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> He takes several deep breaths, to control himself, then \u201c&lt; Of course, you were the one to strike the final blow, so you get the final say in what we do.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> After a moment of thought, you suggest you;<ol class=\"decimal\"><li>Take the head and leave. You don\u2019t know if the Lunar has friends or allies, and you <b><i><u>definitely</u></i></b> need medical attention! Let\u2019s not hang around any longer than we have to\u2026.</li> <li>Cut out the Lunar\u2019s heart and ritually consume it. Getting the strength of a demigod sounds\u2026\u2026 pretty good, actually. But\u2026. do you want to commit the sin of cannibalism?</li> <li>Re-consecrate this shrine of Luna by sacrificing both the heart of the Lunar and your broken spear. She undoubtedly helped you in this fight, and you would be amiss if you didn\u2019t show the proper respect in turn\u2026\u2026</li> </ol>OOC:<ol class=\"decimal\"><li>This was the <u>golden ending<i>, </i></u>believe me or not. Everything else would have resulted in either 1- Kasai losing <i>something</i>, 2- the Lunar escaping, and swearing revenge, or 3- some combination of the two.</li> </ol>Kasai and Corbitant were actually extremely lucky the Lunar was a Full Moon, not a Changing Moon. If she was a Changing Moon, Kasai likely wouldn\u2019t have been able to resist her charms (and Charms), and would have fought Corbitant. Even still, it was close.<br /> <br /> Here is a list of Kasai\u2019s injuries.<ol class=\"decimal\"><li>Concussion</li> <li>Cracked skull</li> <li>Moderate blood loss</li> <li>Dislocated left hip</li> <li>Several broken fingers and bones in left hand</li> <li>Broken right arm</li> <li>Several broken ribs</li> <li>Broken nose</li> <li>Broken/knocked-out teeth</li> <li>Claw-slash across chest</li> <li><u>Numerous</u> other minor scrapes, cuts, bumps and bruises.</li> </ol>Yet again, he will be out of the running for a while.<ol class=\"decimal\"><li>The weapons were indeed blessed by Luna, and they are basically the sole reason we didn\u2019t get even more fucked up.</li> </ol>The Hill-Tribes usually sacrifice weapons after a successful hunt, but a wise hunter might also want to be on the Moon-Goddess\u2019 \u2018good side\u201d, and ask Her to bless their weapons ahead of time by leaving them in a shrine.<br /> <br /> In that case, Luna might send visons of things she <u>really</u> would like to see dead in the dreams of the hunters, as well as blessing their weapons against that particular target.<br /> <br /> That is, by and large, what happened here. Functionally, the weapons acted much like Holy weapons vs Creatures of Darkness, and in game terms they would have dealt Aggravated damage, as well as causing bleeding (aka, for all intents and purposes, the Lunar was mortal-and-then-some, so long as she was struck with the weapons from this shrine)<ol class=\"decimal\"><li>If we consume the heart of the Lunar, we will indeed be healed\u2026\u2026. But we will also take on other aspects of the Lunar\u2019s personality.</li> </ol>By ritually consuming the heart, you take on <b><i><u>all</u></i></b> of the strength of the creature, not just the \u201cgood\u201d parts.<br /> <br /> For example, look at Kasai\u2019s grandfather. His behavior isn\u2019t\u2026\u2026. natural, for lack of a better term. His snarling, growling, bearing-of-teeth, all of that is bestial behavior, ingrained as a result of partaking in the Feast of the Moon. And that was from a deity that is largely benevolent and arguably, the most human of all the Incarnae. That was a <u>reward</u>.<br /> <br /> Now think about what would happen if Kasai consumed the heart of the Lunar. What sort of crazy, sociopathic tendencies would he develop? Now, of course, Kasai wouldn\u2019t do a complete 180, changing from a morally-upstanding individual to a man-eating insane monster\u2026\u2026.. but there would be a personality change, a marked one. More bestial, more savage. Quick to anger, slow to forgive, that sort of thing.<br /> <br /> So, he would gain power, but at what cost?<br /> <br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11892,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","user":"Boston123","date":".  that grammar and diction, tho 05-07-2017, 01:12 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-07T12:45:44-07:00","id":1089318,"title":"","content":"3) Re-consecrate this shrine of Luna by sacrificing both the heart of the Lunar and your broken spear. She undoubtedly helped you in this fight, and you would be amiss if you didn\u2019t show the proper respect in turn\u2026\u2026","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Like my Avatar? Courtesy of<span style=\"color:#0000FF\"> Jen</span>! : <span style=\"font-size:12px\">Anybody want their characters to be experimented on ? </span>post 98<br /> <i>An Exalt is never unarmed.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4018-l-het-esh","name":"L'het'esh","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4018_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"521"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-07T15:09:24-07:00","id":1089416,"title":"","content":"3, fix the alter and make offering but let's not consume the heart","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11567-jairain","name":"Jairain","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11567_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"345"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-07T15:27:08-07:00","id":1089430,"title":"","content":"I say 2 and 3, this was a hunt, and deserves all of the respect and devotion Luna deserves.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1435-videopete","name":"videopete","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1435_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"111"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-07T17:09:34-07:00","id":1089476,"title":"","content":"3. Regeneration would be very welcome right now, but I don't think its worth losing Kasai's self. <br /> <br /> We probably also should not walk home with these injuries.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>On the frontier of the Wild South, there's only one woman with the grit to take on its most dangerous outlaws and bring them <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/984977-back-alive-or-maybe-dead-quest\" target=\"_blank\">Back Alive, or Maybe Dead.</a><br /> <br /> Avatar by <a href=\"http://puzzlinghappenstance.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">K.S. Brenowitz</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/908-semicasual","name":"semicasual","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar908_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2499"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-07T19:07:40-07:00","id":1089525,"title":"","content":"Thing is him eating the heart would be no more of a loss than him not eating it. All of these injuries are career enders, and duty still calls.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1435-videopete","name":"videopete","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1435_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"111"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-07T19:35:08-07:00","id":1089533,"title":"","content":"Don't eat the heart.<br /> <br /> We may not be a super-devout Immaculate...but we ARE familiar with their theology, and we just slew one of their Anathema. It's fine to offer some thanks to the goddess who helped you slay it...but don't eat its heart to gain its strength. That's...that's not clean strength like eating the heart of a boar or a lion or something. That's tainted strength. That's unholy demonic strength. We don't want any part of that.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>So I'm making <a href=\"http://godkickingboot.smackjeeves.com/\" target=\"_blank\">God-Kicking Boot</a>, an Exalted webcomic, now. Updates on Sundays. Full-color, mediocre but slowly improving art. It's a thing.<br /> <br /> The absence of a monument can, in its own way, be something of a monument also.</i> -Roger Zelazny","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/512-wise-old-guru","name":"Wise Old Guru","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar512_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1097"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-08T04:28:02-07:00","id":1089702,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>videopete</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1089525#post1089525\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Thing is him eating the heart would be no more of a loss than him not eating it. All of these injuries are career enders, and duty still calls.</div> </div> </div> </div>Actually, NONE of these injuries are career enders, provided they receive adequate treatment. The real challenge here is how to get that treatment. <br /> <br /> You'd be surprised what you can live through or what you can learn to live with.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>On the frontier of the Wild South, there's only one woman with the grit to take on its most dangerous outlaws and bring them <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/984977-back-alive-or-maybe-dead-quest\" target=\"_blank\">Back Alive, or Maybe Dead.</a><br /> <br /> Avatar by <a href=\"http://puzzlinghappenstance.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">K.S. Brenowitz</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/908-semicasual","name":"semicasual","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar908_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2499"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-08T06:52:29-07:00","id":1089751,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>semicasual</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1089702#post1089702\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Actually, NONE of these injuries are career enders, provided they receive adequate treatment. The real challenge here is how to get that treatment. <br /> <br /> You'd be surprised what you can live through or what you can learn to live with.</div> </div> </div> </div>In a modern day emergency trauma center you would be right, but in a back woods iron age maybe bronze age with limited access to various types of advanced healing techniques, Kasai is pretty much out of the fight for a long time not even mentioning the risk of inner cranial pressure, internal bleeding, those broken ribs can easily turn into a flail chest with either tension pneumo thorax or hemotension thorax which takes a chest tube. The man is in dire need of surgery, with these injuries the best he can hope for is six months of recuperation followed with orthopedic and physical therapy and a life time of being a cripple. <br /> <br /> Sorry my real life job is in emergency medicine and he needs to be seen in under a hour for best chances of survival and that is in a modern conditions.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1435-videopete","name":"videopete","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1435_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"111"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-08T07:56:12-07:00","id":1089782,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>videopete</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1089751#post1089751\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> In a modern day emergency trauma center you would be right, but in a back woods iron age maybe bronze age with limited access to various types of advanced healing techniques, Kasai is pretty much out of the fight for a long time not even mentioning the risk of inner cranial pressure, internal bleeding, those broken ribs can easily turn into a flail chest with either tension pneumo thorax or hemotension thorax which takes a chest tube. The man is in dire need of surgery, with these injuries the best he can hope for is six months of recuperation followed with orthopedic and physical therapy and a life time of being a cripple. <br /> <br /> Sorry my real life job is in emergency medicine and he needs to be seen in under a hour for best chances of survival and that is in a modern conditions.</div> </div> </div> </div>Kasai also lives in a setting with magic. Now, I am not saying Elixir of Life is easy to get on short notice, but I'd rather bank on the blessings of thankful spirits than risk the side effects of consuming a lunar heart.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>On the frontier of the Wild South, there's only one woman with the grit to take on its most dangerous outlaws and bring them <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/984977-back-alive-or-maybe-dead-quest\" target=\"_blank\">Back Alive, or Maybe Dead.</a><br /> <br /> Avatar by <a href=\"http://puzzlinghappenstance.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">K.S. Brenowitz</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/908-semicasual","name":"semicasual","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar908_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2499"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-08T14:42:17-07:00","id":1089972,"title":"","content":"In my mind, his most serious injuries are the concussion and the dislocated hip. Everything else is manageable, to a given extent: Kasai is young, and in excellent physical shape. While he won't be running around and fighting for the next few weeks, he will bounce back from the various broken bones pretty well, barring chronic pain and stiffness.<br /> <br /> -With regards to the concussion: He is actually lucky he cracked the skull open, as there is less chance for blood to put pressure on the brain. Most of his &quot;issues&quot; will come from the concussion itself, not from the skull fracture. Remember (and I don't want to reveal too much), he didn't pass out from the concussion itself, but from pain due to putting body weight on the dislocated hip, and he can remember before, during, and after he got thrown into a wall. His head <u>was </u> protected, after all, by the padding of his armored coif, the Lunar just threw him into a wall <u>that</u> hard.<br /> <br /> He <u>is</u> going to be confused, irritable, nauseous and have trouble balancing (as well as other symptoms) in a few minutes, however.<br /> <br /> - Dislocated Hip: This is the big one. As Oathsworn, he is expected to serve as a cavalryman, or at least be able to. He already has issues riding, as a result of the injury to his right leg. He can walk (hobble, really, but over time the deep-tissue bruising will heal), but depending on the treatment he receives, this will plague him for the rest of his life. Chronic pain, stiffness, the works.<br /> <br /> -ribs: he actually isn't too concerned about these. So long as he isn't coughing up blood, and can still breathe, he assumes he is OK, relatively speaking. Remember: he wears a thick and tightly-woven <i>sarashi</i> about his midsection almost-literally for this, aka supporting the midsection and limiting the effects of injury.<br /> <br /> -bleeding: Kasai <u>has</u> lost a decent amount of blood, not enough to make him pass out like last time, but enough where he is weak and woozy. The good thing is: his bleeding wounds have already closed, mainly due to the pressure of his armor underpadding, so as long as he doesn't break them open, he won't lose any more.<br /> <br /> shattered teeth: meh. Both the Hundred Kingdoms and the Hill-Tribes know what dentures are. The stumps are certainly going to have to be pulled out, and that will be <i>fun (</i> no solid foods for the next month or so!), but he can have dentures made in a few weeks.<br /> <br /> broken bones: as above. So long as they get set, not <i>too</i> big of a concern. He just won't be capable of fighting for 6 or so weeks.<br /> <br /> Treatment is key, here. If he gets good enough treatment, he will be fine, relatively speaking, even regarding chronic long-term effects. It is just, well, he is a couple hours away from the closest village, at night, cold and wet, and he doesn't know the way, and his companion is blinded, etc etc etc.<br /> <br /> Lord Iysen's chirugeon is <u>very</u> capable (and will almost-certainly want to give him a physical when he gets back to Teriake Castle, regardless of what happens), and the Hill-Tribes aren't chucklefucks either. The main issues he is facing at this point isn't the fact that he is injured <i>per se</i>, more that he has to actually <i>get</i> to someone who can treat him.<br /> <br /> +++ Spirits:<br /> <br /> Getting help from some form of spirit or deity is ...... possible, even plausible, depending on how we play our cards. Most Essence-using beings within a couple miles felt the Lunar go Totemic, then cut out, and probably recognized the implication.<br /> <br /> The Hill-Tribe animistic totem spirits, which are worshiped in various ways through the East and Northeast (and have corresponding levels of power), are probably going to be <i>fucking ecstatic</i> this Lunar is gone. They certainly didn't appreciate her killing their worshipers in particular, and resent/resented the presence of Exalted in general. The thing about them is: their domains, and corresponding powers, are largely limited to what their animistic qualities are. They probably aren't going to be able to heal Kasai (or Corbitant, for that matter), not in any major capacity (jolts of Essence akin to adrenaline, maybe?), but they could certainly offer other forms of aid, largely limited to what you fine people come up with, if that is what you wish.<br /> <br /> Of course, Kasai would have to make contact with the totems first, and he has no fucking idea how to do that. Corbitant has some semblance of what the rituals entail, but he is no shaman. Traditionally, the shamans of a tribe would be the ones to handle communication with the totem spirits, and they guard the rituals <u>very</u> closely.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11892,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","user":"Boston123","date":". 05-08-2017, 05:46 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-08T15:12:07-07:00","id":1089989,"title":"","content":"I say we eat the heart for numerous reasons<br /> <br /> 1. Luna saved us from certain death. The personality she gives us should be her choice. <br /> 2. That's assuming kasai would even know that eating the heart would cause massive personality changes. Only one other person in our experience has done it and thats not exactly a great scientific survey. <br /> 3. Luna is a big powerful spirit by taking the time to honor her in her way might either see our healing factor raised or kasai completely healed. If it doesn't then we just ate something gross but if it does nothing then the only thing lost is all important time.<br /> 4. I was starting to find kasai's personality both white knight-ish and stale. I feel it might be exciting to explore the personality changes<br /> 5. Bestial traits might actually be helpful in Kasai's duties to his lord. If he is able to sniff out intruders and assassins he might be more useful than any other retainer she has. <br /> <br /> Just my thoughts","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8897-joshopotamus","name":"joshopotamus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8897_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"97"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-08T17:57:16-07:00","id":1090062,"title":"","content":"You know...... over these last few hours, I was not-so-idly musing on the possibility of Exigence. One of the animist totem-spirits could be ....... particularly grateful for the assistance.<br /> <br /> I actually came up with a brutally-OP quasi-martial art based on the unholy union of a pack of wolves and White Reaper style<br /> <br /> Hmmm... of course, this would be difficult to write for, as my web-chart hasn't had Exalted/Exaltation brought up yet.....<br /> <br /> ..... I personally find that when campaigns go off the rails, they either go <u>really well</u>, or crash and burn.<br /> <br /> So far, this is the list of animist totem-spirits: ( I like Celtic stuff. Most of my tribal groups in the East, Northeast and North will have a decent level of Celtic influence)<br /> <br /> - Kerne, the White Wolf, Hunting Hound of Luna<div style=\"margin-left:40px\">- themes/purview: cooperation (esp. organized warbands, but clans and tribes as a whole), law and hierarchy, glory of the group, perseverance, hunting (esp. the Fair Folk)</div> <br /> - Magda, the Moon-Hunting Bear<div style=\"margin-left:40px\">- themes/purview: the lone warrior, solitude (esp. a chieftain or elder), strength, poetry, stubborn single-mindedness, glory of the individual</div> <br /> - Branwen , Raven-tressed<div style=\"margin-left:40px\">- themes/purview: death, reincarnation, sorcery/magic, the spirit-world, archery, secrets, the spirits of the dead (malevolent/neutral)</div> <br /> - Oichieach, the Guardian of Night<div style=\"margin-left:40px\">-animal: Owls<br /> - themes/purview: night, activities taking place at night (hunting, raiding, etc), bad luck, good luck, the spirits of the dead (benevolent)</div> <br /> -Cullach, Red-Maned, Red Boar of the Woods<div style=\"margin-left:40px\">- themes/purview: Berserkers, the mad/insane, death in the line of duty, battle (esp. the frenzied charge), protection of family and people</div> <br /> - Fiannach, King of the Wild<div style=\"margin-left:40px\">- animal: deer, moose, reindeer, elk, (especially stags)<br /> - themes/purview: royalty, duels, honor, purity, valor, excellence</div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-08T17:59:26-07:00","id":1090064,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>joshopotamus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1089989#post1089989\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I say we eat the heart for numerous reasons<br /> <br /> 1. Luna saved us from certain death. The personality she gives us should be her choice.<br /> 2. That's assuming kasai would even know that eating the heart would cause massive personality changes. Only one other person in our experience has done it and thats not exactly a great scientific survey.<br /> 3. Luna is a big powerful spirit by taking the time to honor her in her way might either see our healing factor raised or kasai completely healed. If it doesn't then we just ate something gross but if it does nothing then the only thing lost is all important time.<br /> 4. I was starting to find kasai's personality both white knight-ish and stale. I feel it might be exciting to explore the personality changes<br /> 5. Bestial traits might actually be helpful in Kasai's duties to his lord. If he is able to sniff out intruders and assassins he might be more useful than any other retainer she has.<br /> <br /> Just my thoughts</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> If we consume the heart of the Luna(r), Luna will have nothing to do with it.<br /> <br /> You get the strength of the consumed, and in this case, the consumed is a crazed, man-eating (in more ways than one), manipulative monster fully deserving of the moniker Anathema.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-08T19:46:20-07:00","id":1090094,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Boston123</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1090062#post1090062\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You know......<br /> <br /> - <u>Kerne, the White Wolf</u>, Hunting Hound of Luna<br /> - themes/purview: cooperation (esp. organized warbands, but clans and tribes as a whole), law and hierarchy, glory of the group, perseverance, hunting (esp. the Fair Folk)</div> </div> </div> </div>I think Kerne would be appropriate.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Like my Avatar? Courtesy of<span style=\"color:#0000FF\"> Jen</span>! : <span style=\"font-size:12px\">Anybody want their characters to be experimented on ? </span>post 98<br /> <i>An Exalt is never unarmed.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4018-l-het-esh","name":"L'het'esh","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4018_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"521"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-09T14:18:43-07:00","id":1090477,"title":"","content":"Hnnnggggg..... now I kinda-sorta want to make Kasai some sort of Exigent, but that would mean I would have to finagle a way for him to actually meet the deity, as well as come up with plotlines more suited for an Exalted storyline....<br /> <br /> ..... like any other fancy, it too will pass.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-09T17:21:44-07:00","id":1090529,"title":"","content":"I like Fiannach, if that matters.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>On the frontier of the Wild South, there's only one woman with the grit to take on its most dangerous outlaws and bring them <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/984977-back-alive-or-maybe-dead-quest\" target=\"_blank\">Back Alive, or Maybe Dead.</a><br /> <br /> Avatar by <a href=\"http://puzzlinghappenstance.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">K.S. Brenowitz</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/908-semicasual","name":"semicasual","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar908_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2499"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-09T17:52:09-07:00","id":1090537,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>semicasual</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1090529#post1090529\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I like Fiannach, if that matters.</div> </div> </div> </div>Figured, since it involves duelling.<br /> <br /> That one, however, is probably going to be the most challenging to make Charms for. Other than Duels and general Presence stuff, in my mind there isn't that much to it, nothing really &quot;exciting&quot;, anyways.<br /> <br /> If you folks decide to go for Exigence, I am ....... well, I might need some help coming up with Charms. They don't have to be &quot;game -functional&quot;, but I've never actually homebrewed Charms before, so I don't really know where to start, to be blunt.<br /> <br /> The wolf-themed Charms I've already started working on (I will be blunt: I am biased towards wolves), as they are the easiest for me to conceptualize, and the bear and the boar are pretty straightforward as well. So far, I have been basing Charms off the abilities of the animals in the Corebook.<br /> <br /> So, wolves are based off cooperation, in all walks of life: war, community, etc, and the Charms themed for them foster Intimacies among a group, and cooperative actions<br /> Bears : strength, both physical and mental, boosting Resolve and Willpower, etc<br /> Boars: pure martial skill, if not outright berserkerism: quasi-damage reduction/soak, increased damage, recklessness, etc<br /> <br /> There are no stag-creatures in the Corebook, so I have to come up with stuff from the bottom up.<br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-11T13:36:43-07:00","id":1091254,"title":"","content":"Can Exigents gain power from more than one deity, or does the transplanted power have to all come from one deity?<br /> <br /> I am mainly asking this (and, probably, will go through with it regardless) because while idly brainstorming at work, came up with themes for a whole charm list, with different aspects of the list based on the different totemic animals. I just don't know if it is &quot;legal&quot; or not.<br /> <br /> Functionally, based on what I have come up with so far, this Exaltation would be the knockoff version of a Lunar and a Solar, with certain Charms boosting Attributes (and acting as quasi-Excellencies), and others boosting Abilities in <u>specific</u> circumstances.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11892,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","user":"Boston123","date":". 05-11-2017, 05:54 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-11T15:10:55-07:00","id":1091296,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Boston123</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1091254#post1091254\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Can Exigents gain power from more than one deity, or does the transplanted power have to all come from one deity?<br /> <br /> I am mainly asking this (and, probably, will go through with it regardless) because while idly brainstorming at work, came up with themes for a whole charm list, with different aspects of the list based on the different totemic animals. I just don't know if it is &quot;legal&quot; or not.<br /> <br /> Functionally, based on what I have come up with so far, this Exaltation would be the knockoff version of a Lunar and a Solar, with certain Charms boosting Attributes (and acting as quasi-Excellencies), and others boosting Abilities in <u>specific</u> circumstances.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes*<br /> <br /> *Early talk about Exigents included Black Market exaltations (presumably because whatever calamity they were needed for either didn't occur, or the god changed its mind halfway through) which could lead to trading the exigence &amp; &quot;dirty bomb&quot; exaltations which to the best of my understanding is one empowered by multiple gods. It's probably possible for a pantheon-style local setup to all chip in some power straight up as well though.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/649-nightwinder","name":"Nightwinder","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"154"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-12T16:54:54-07:00","id":1091762,"title":"","content":"You know, I have been thinking about it for the last few days, and I don't think I am quite capable of writing a <b>believable</b> way for Kasai to receive an Exigent Exaltation at this point.<br /> <br /> I have the god (really, gods), I have the rationale, the only thing I don't have is the <u>story</u>. Everything I have come up with so far has come across as half-assed and rushed.<br /> <br /> Which, honestly, it largely has been. I didn't have a plan for Kasai to possibly receive Exaltation until 2 or so chapters down the road. And I am not going to put it in if it &quot;sounds&quot; so crap.<br /> <br /> So ..... no Exaltation. Not at this point. A classic case of &quot;jumping the gun&quot; and &quot;eyes too big for the stomach&quot;, sadly.<br /> <br /> ...........<br /> <br /> So far, it looks like there is 4-to-2 in favor of making a sacrifice to Luna. I'll leave it open for a while longer, as I have to get my thoughts straight.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11892,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","user":"Boston123","date":". 05-12-2017, 09:20 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-15T18:34:07-07:00","id":1093052,"title":"","content":"It seems like most of you would rather forgo some god-given regeneration in favor of keeping Kasai entirely sane. Solid choice.<br /> <br /> --------<br /> A long moment passes, and you shake your head.<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt; I am sorry, Corbitant, but no. I am not comfortable committing cannibalism for the sake of revenge, regardless of what power we could get from it. Your people are dead and gone, and desecrating our souls will not bring them back, no matter how much closure it will bring. The Anathema is <i>dead</i>, that is enough.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt; But we\u2026. !&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt; <i>No</i>. You may do as you wish, but I will not partake.&gt;\u201d Drawing in a deep breath, \u201c&lt; Instead, I will make sacrifice to the Horned Huntress, as is Her due as the patron of hunters. This was a hunt, and I am willing to bet Her help is the only reason we survived, much less won.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt; \u2026. Not that I am complaining, but what do you mean?&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt; During the fight, I did as you said, and ran for the altar and the sacrificed weapons. They struck with more violence than I ever could, and burned the Anathema at a touch. Two arrows were enough to bring her down to our level, and I finished her off with my spear. That \u2026\u2026 wasn\u2019t natural, and <i>someone</i> was interfering. I prefer to think that it was Luna.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> Slowly, agonizingly, you make your way to your feet, using the grotto-wall and climbing as much as standing, and look around. \u201c&lt; Therefore, in thanks and deference, I plan on re-consecrating this sacred space and sacrificing both the heart and my spear, or try to at least. I am no priest.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u2018&lt; What ritual are you thinking of using?&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> You shrug, before remembering that Corbitant can\u2019t see. \u201c&lt; I dunno \u2026\u2026 the ritual for a successful hunt, I guess? The one where you cut out the heart of the animal and place it in a pool of water? That is the only Hill-Tribe ritual I know for hunting, and the Immaculate Faith prohibits the veneration of the Incarnae\u2026\u2026\u2026 The one thing I do know, however, is that I need to change into dry clothing, before this cold sets in any further.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> He shrugs weakly, \u201c&lt; I\u2019ve got a wool cloak, if you want it\u2026.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt; No, you wear it. I had one, it must have gotten torn off in the fight. It\u2019ll be around somewhere, and I need to find my weapons anyway.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> After stumping around for a few minutes, you find both your weapons and your cloak. Your weapons are unharmed, barring a few scratches and nicks, and your cloak, aside from a few small rips (thank the gods for Hill-Tribe craftsmanship!) is warm and dry. Before throwing it around your shoulders, you peel off the ragged remnants of your armor underpadding. Soaked with blood and water as they are, keeping them on will do more harm than good. Naked, shivering in the mountain cold, it is almost by accident that you stumble over a nest of sorts behind the altar.<br /> <br /> Tucked behind and partially below the slate slab, is piled a truly-staggering amount of clothing. Most of it is bunched or rolled into a crude bedroll, but other pieces, along with what appears to be pieces of armor and weapons, are neatly folded underneath human skulls, gleaming dull-white in the moonlight. Several of the skulls are disconcertingly small, and you turn away in repugnance. Pulling at the piled clothes and muttering a quick apology to the assembled dead for disturbing them, you eventually find a jacket and trousers that will fit, as well as other strips of cloth to use as bandages and a sling for your arm.<br /> <br /> Dressed in warm and dry clothes, you start to feel a little better (relatively speaking, of course.), and it comes time to actually get to work.<br /> <br /> The ritual you are going to perform was one your grandfather taught you long ago when he took you hunting, and it is pretty basic, comparatively speaking. When the hunter is successful at bringing down their quarry, they must show proper thanks and reverence to the prey for giving up its life so that others may live. The hunter cuts off the head of the animal and suspends it in the crux of a tree, preferably oak. The heart, along with the killing-weapon or a facsimile, gets laid in a calm pool of water (preferably, a sacred well, but any pool the moon shines in is acceptable), and a prayer of thanks is given to the Horned Huntress. Pretty simple, and it only takes a few minutes. This is the one religious ritual you maintained with any regularity, performing it even when in your father\u2019s house, although not when he was present (he was a comparatively-devout Immaculate, one of the few things the two of you couldn\u2019t see eye-to-eye on)<br /> <br /> It isn\u2019t the ritual you are unsure about, but the \u201csetting\u201d. While the Hill-Tribes are largely not concerned about ritual cleanliness (they eat the raw hearts of animals, for godssakes!), the Immaculate Faith sure is (practitioners are expected to bathe and come to prayer-sessions in clean clothing, ritually rinse the hands and mouth with blessed water before entering a temple, and dead bodies are ritually unclean, for starters), and despite your irreverence to the philosophy \u2026\u2026 they might have a point. While this might be a shrine to the Argent Madonna, this grotto reeks of blood and death, and you can almost feel the spirits of the dead crowding around you (<i>something</i> has to explain this miasma of fog clouding your thoughts!).<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt; Corbitant, do you mind helping me move these bones to the sacred well? We need to dispose of them before we can start the ritual, and it isn\u2019t like either of us can dig graves or build cairns.\u201d&gt;<br /> <br /> He hitches back the folds of his cloak, \u201c&lt;\u2026 Let me do it. They are my people, I should be responsible for them. You should sit for a while, anyways, you are the badly-hurt one, not I! &gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> Sinking to the ground, almost glad Corbitant can\u2019t see how weak you are, you lean back up against the wall. You don\u2019t remember closing your eyes, even for a moment, but you abruptly find yourself getting shaken awake, quite roughly in fact.<br /> <br /> \u201c.. Wazzat?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt; Kasai, don\u2019t go to sleep! With your wound, you might not wake up!&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> Groggily, you look around the chamber, seeing that the floor is cleared of all bones. That must have taken him a while, especially without the benefit of sight.<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt; How\u2026. How long was I out?&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> He shrugs, \u201c&lt; Long enough for me to clean the floor and say a few prayers. An hour, maybe? It\u2019s hard to tell. I didn\u2019t realize you were asleep until a few minutes ago.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> Shaking your head in effort to clear the fog (and largely failing), you climb to your feet again. Your head throbs, and you are vaguely irritated. Just what were you doing again?<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt;Are you ready to start, Kasai?&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt;\u2026. Yeah.&gt;\u201d. Passing a hand in front of your face, you stumble over to the corpse of the Lunar Anathema, sprawled over the floor. Kneeling down beside the body, trying to avoid most of the tacky blood, you make the mistake of looking at her face. In this state, she looks entirely human, eyes closed and fox-fangs hidden. That doesn\u2019t stop you from remembering the sheer <i>hatred</i> on her face when you fought, nor the child-sized skulls behind the altar, and you suddenly shiver, at once fully cognizant of <i>just how lucky you were</i> to be able to kneel here, injuries nonwithstanding\u2026\u2026.. you need to stop doing things like this, or at least think things through before jumping into them! You want to help people, but if you get eaten by an Anathema, what will you be able to do then?<br /> <br /> In the middle of your glimpse into your own mortality, you suddenly realize that you won\u2019t be able to complete the ritual as you are, not without help.<br /> <br /> After all, in order to butcher game and remove the heart, you need to have functioning hands. Your right arm is bound up in a sling, and some of the fingers on your left hand are broken. You can\u2019t hold a knife, much less crack the ribs open.<br /> <br /> Turning your head (and wincing at the sudden lightning-bolt of pain that arcs around in your skull), you call out, \u201c&lt; Corbitant, can you come over here? I\u2019ve got a problem.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> Shuffling over, directed by your voice, \u201c&lt; What is the issue?&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt; My hands are injured, and I can\u2019t hold a knife. You are going to have to cut the heart out. Will your lack of sight prevent you from doing that?&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> You are surprised to hear him laugh, \u201c&lt; Boy, I have been butchering game for longer than you have been alive. I don\u2019t need to see.&gt;\u201d, and he pulls out a sheath knife.<br /> <br /> And he is correct. Despite his lack of vision, he quickly and cleanly dresses the Lunar, cutting open the chest cavity and after yanking aside broken ribs, pulls out the heart. Removing your spearhead from the spine takes a bit more effort, and he has to put all his body weight into tugging on the broken tang to work it free of the bone. At last, with a mighty heave, the steel breaks free with a <i>snap</i>, and he lays it down beside the heart.<br /> <br /> Turning to you, wiping his hands on his trousers, \u201c&lt;Whenever you are ready.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> Taking a deep breath, you shuffle over to the edge of the pool on hands and knees, pulling the sacrificial objects with you. Here, right by the water\u2019s edge, the air is cold and your breath fogs, and the full moon reflects pristinely in the black water, almost to the edge of the pool. For a moment, the pool and reflected moon look like nothing less than a great eye emblazoned in the heavens, and you can\u2019t look away, even if you wanted to. <i>Something</i> is going on, either a god is watching, or a severe head injury is acting up, and you have to take a moment to stop your hands and voice from trembling..<br /> <br /> Lifting both the broken spearhead and the bloody heart in front of you, you chant, slowly at first, then more strongly as you gain confidence.<br /> <br /> \u201c Great Luna, Horned Huntress, Argent Madonna, patron of hunters and the hunted, the wilderness and the Wyld, the lost and the forsaken, hear me!<br /> <br /> My companion and I have slain a great and terrible monster, here in your sacred shrine, using weapons you yourself have touched! In thanks, both for our survival and for your assistance, to you I sacrifice the heart of the terror, and the weapon that dealt the killing blow.<br /> <br /> The heart, the power and strength, is yours, as is only right and proper, and so that both the soul of the Anathema and the souls of the people it took might be guided safely to the underworld, by the glow of your majesty.<br /> <br /> The spear \u2026.. it was a parting gift from my father, and has served me well in battle for many years. It ended the reign of terror of the Anathema before you, and as such is well-suited for sacrifice under your purview. May you find it as useful as I did.\u201d<br /> <br /> Clasping your hands before you, ignoring the pain, \u201cGreat Goddess, I re-consecrate your shrine, cleaned of corpses and death, so that it may be used for its intended and rightful purpose.\u201d<br /> <br /> Reaching down beside you, you pick up the spearhead and hold it up to the moon overhead. Gently, you let it slip down into the water, sliding it beneath the surface without even a ripple. Doing the same with the heart, you watch as the entire pool is stained crimson, and when the dye reaches the edge of the pool, you press your hands to the floor and bring your forehead down upon them.<br /> <br /> Your heart beats once, and again. You raise your eyes from the floor\u2026.<br /> <br /> Nothing. Other than the moon reflecting in the pool, and the shuffling of Corbitant behind you, the grotto is silent and empty, and for a moment you are slightly surprised, and then feel vaguely foolish. Did you honestly expect anything to happen? Luna already helped you out once today, was She going to come down from the sky and thank you personally, maybe offer a lift back to town? Not bloody likely.<br /> <br /> Grumbling to yourself, you clamber back to your feet, grabbing onto the longer section of your broken spearshaft for assistance.<br /> <br /> Corbitant looks up, \u201c&lt;That was it?&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt; That is about as much as I know&gt;\u201d, you admit through gritted teeth. \u201c&lt; If Luna doesn\u2019t like it, She can write a letter or something. All I know is: we can\u2019t stay here. Can you cut off the head?\u201d<br /> <br /> He grunts, \u201c&lt; I was just thinking the same thing. To me, it makes no difference whether I leave at night or during the day, I can\u2019t see regardless. But you \u2026. You can\u2019t fall asleep, at least until we have a medicine-man look at you\u2026\u2026&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> Sighing, you mutter almost to yourself, \u201c&lt; This is going to <i>suck</i>.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> And it does. Even with walking and balancing-aids (Corbitant and the spearshaft), and with a full moon rising bright and brilliant over the mountains, your passage back to the Paskamansett village is slow, and the term \u201carduous\u201d only touches at the difficulty.<br /> <br /> The trail is rough, rougher than you remember, and you stumble constantly, threatening to spill both yourself and Corbitant to the stone-strewn earth. Your side, head and hip ache ever more with every step, driving you to tears at several points, and your vision fades in and out with the pain. At several points, you have to stop walking , to catch your breath and let the agony in your hip fade away. It only took a few hours to make you way to the Lunar shrine from the village when you were whole and hearty, but you are so slow-going that the sun has risen several inches over the horizon by the time you reach the outskirts of the village. Worst yet, the few tribespeople you see flee before you, throwing down their tools and sprinting away, chased by your curses. Left alone, you stumble along, until (finally!) you are intercepted by a group of chanting priests, waving burning incense of red-cedar in your general direction. The priests are backed up by skittish-looking hunters armed with various weapons, and the whole ensemble is so godsdamned <i>funny</i> that you can\u2019t help but laugh, even though it hurts.<br /> <br /> The chief-priest steps forwards, wafting red cedar smoke in front of him. \u201c&lt; What do you want, spirits-of-the-dead? If it is in our power, we will give it, so long as you leave us in peace.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> Still giggling, \u201c&lt; Well, to be honest, hot food and a warm bed would be nice to start. Oh, and medical attention.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> Set aback by your demands, \u201c&lt;\u2026.. what?&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> Suddenly enraged, \u201c&lt; We aren\u2019t dead, old man! We got out and kill your monster for you, and this is the thanks we get?!&gt;\u201d You go to untie the head from your sash, only to fall over at the movement. The impact with the ground drives all air from your lungs, and your vision blackens, constricts, until all you can see is the dirt of the path. Slowly, even that fades out, and you pass into unconsciousness almost gratefully. At least things don\u2019t hurt anymore.<br /> <br /> It only feels like you are asleep for a few minutes before you gradually rise up out of unconsciousness. The first thing you are aware of is the smell of more burning cedar. Cracking open your eyes slowly, the inside of a Hill-Tribe longhouse is revealed. Spread all around and hanging from the ceiling are bunches of herbs, and in the corner, merrily working away with a mortar and pestle, is an elderly Hill-Tribesman, the same shaman you yelled at earlier (awkward). When he hears you shuffling around, he sets the pestle aside and hurries over.<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt; Be careful, warrior! You were, and still are, gravely injured!&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt; Yeah, tell me something I don\u2019t know\u2026. Ugh!&gt;\u201d grunting as you sit up on the bench. Patting yourself down, you come across numerous bandages, lathes and casts, all covering your various wounds.<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt; How long was I asleep for? I must have been out like a rock to not notice this.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> The priest shifts shrugs, \u201c&lt;About four days&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> You stare for a moment, and then laugh. Shaking your head, \u201c&lt; You know, I\u2019m not even surprised anymore.&gt;\u201d Still exploring the bandages and wrappings, \u201c&lt; So, tell me. How badly did I cripple myself this time?&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt; <i>This</i> time?&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt; Yes, this is a habit for me. So, lay it on me; what is broken?&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt;Ah\u2026. Most of the ribs on your left side are cracked, though not severely. Your upper right arm is broken clean through, hence the cast. Several fingers on your left hand are broken, as well as your nose and several teeth. I yanked out the broken teeth and filled the holes with pine-pitch (which explains why your mouth tastes like you just sucked on a tree root). There are several cuts and scrapes over your body, which were sewn up without much issue.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> He takes a deep breath, \u201c&lt; No, the most serious injuries were the cracked skull and the hip. The hip I can largely do little about, asides from bracing it for additional support, but the skull was treatable.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt; When we brought you in, I found the crack on the back of your head, and thought your brain might be swelling or bleeding. Luckily, I know how to trepan the skull, and when I drilled a hole above your ear, a rush of blood told me I was right. Lucky for you, there wasn\u2019t that much, so the long-term effects should be minor.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt; You \u2026\u2026 drilled a hole in my skull?&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> Nodding,\u201d&lt; Yes, a small one. Above and behind your left ear.&gt;\u201d He takes your hand and guides it to the spot, where you feel a small bumpy scar, about the size of a large arrow-shaft. \u201c&lt; Do you want the bone disc? Many people use it as a talisman or amulet.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> Mildly amused, \u201c&lt; Sure, why not wear a piece of my own skull around my neck?&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> Before he passes over the amulet, \u201c&lt; Now, a few words of forewarning. Both the hip and the skull may very well cause long-term effects, minor as they are. Most of the deep-bruising around the hip has faded, but there will still be pain and stiffness, probably for the rest of your life, especially when it is cold and wet. As for the head, you will probably have headaches. There isn\u2019t much you can do for either, asides from smoking the flowers-of-hemp or chewing on poppy seeds.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt;I\u2019ll look into it. Can I walk? And where is my equipment?&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt; You walked here, didn\u2019t you? Most impressive, might I add. I would advise against running or jumping, at least until you get a better idea of how badly damaged the joint is, but you can certainly hobble around. And your equipment is stored in the central longhall, I believe.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> After thanking the medicine man, and apologizing for losing your temper with him (as well as accepting a packet of poppy seeds), you head out to find your gear. Almost immediately, you notice a significant change in the atmosphere in the settlement. Windows are thrown open, the warding fetishes are gone, and people are happy (comparatively), hard at work and conversing with their neighbors. As you pass by, the tribespeople mutter thanks and bow their heads, making you feel vaguely uncomfortable.<br /> <br /> The village is small, and it takes but a few minutes to reach the central longhall. Inside, you are greeted by the chief of the village, who welcomes you back to his table warmly (almost a complete 180 from just a week ago!), and passes over your equipment. While you are eating (painfully, even though it is just broth and cornbread), he talks about how the village has been breathing easier since the god-beast died, now that they don\u2019t have to worry about a demon wearing the skins of loved ones, about what happened to Corbitant (he was brought back to the Elder Council to report what happened), and several other comparatively-inane topics you, quite frankly, don\u2019t really care about. One topic <i>does</i> catch your attention, however.<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt; Oh, and I heard from travelers that the warchief of the whole Confederation itself is coming here to congratulate and thank you, for killing the god-beast. Imagine that; someone so influential coming to our little ass-end-of-nowhere! Hah!&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> You freeze, spoon halfway to your mouth, \u201c&lt; Is it \u2026.. confirmed, that he is coming?&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt; Oh yes! Last I heard, he was just a few days away.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u2026. Well damn.<br /> <br /> The next few days pass by in a blur, of walking around the village and sleeping (undoubtedly helped by your ample consumption of the poppy seeds), until, all of a sudden in his usual fashion (the man can be quite sneaky, in spite of being over 6 feet tall), you find yourself being woken up by your grandfather, looming over your sleeping bench. Silently, he turns to the shaman, who all-but scurries out the door. Turning back to you, you quickly see several conflicting emotions on his face, all before he folds you into a crushing embrace, letting go only when you grunt in pain.<br /> He only lets go for a split-second before you find yourself lifted into the air, suspended by the front of your jacket. His lips pull back from his teeth in a snarl, and he growls, \u201cTell me, Kasai, were you knocked on the head one too many times training with your father, or were you always this <i>stupid</i>?!\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c\u2026.. What?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cChallenging a Lunar to a fight at <i>all</i>, much less marching to it in its own lair and fighting it on its own turf, <i>alone</i>?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cI wasn\u2019t alone, I had Corbitant, an\u2026\u201d<br /> <br /> He laughs, waving his hands in the air, \u201c Oh, <i>excuse me</i>, two people, <b>two</b>, against the wrath and rage of an <i>insane demigod</i>! I would have sent a full warparty to drive it to earth, and you just walked in there, dick swinging!\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cI didn\u2019t know it was an Anathema until I got there! By that point, I couldn\u2019t just walk away!\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cIf you didn\u2019t know what it was, why did you offer to go fight it?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c\u2026.. You\u2019ve got a point. By the way the Paskamansett described it, I didn\u2019t think it was anything more than a Wyld-touched animal. Corbitant only described it in full when we got <i>here</i>. Besides, if you knew it was an Anathema, why didn\u2019t you tell me?!\u201d<br /> <br /> He averts his eyes, \u201c\u2026 I didn\u2019t know either, not until after you left, and I asked the elders for more info. I <i>tried</i> to send someone after you, but they must have missed you. I couldn\u2019t leave to come get you myself; until the Council is closed, voting representatives cannot leave.\u201d<br /> <br /> A moment of silence, then you add, \u201cIt doesn\u2019t really matter anyways. The beast is dead, and..\u201d<br /> <br /> He growls, \u201cNo, <i>you aren\u2019t getting my point</i>. Eventually, there <b>won\u2019t</b> be a next time! You keep throwing yourself into trouble like this, and it <u>will</u> kill you.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cMy Oaths demand tha..\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cYour Oaths!\u201d He sneers, \u201c<i>Your Oaths</i>! Your father always prattled on about \u2018my Oaths this\u2019 and \u2018my Oaths that\u201d, what good did they ever do him, besides land him and his family in almost-exile in the backwoods?! Tell me, Kasai, are those pretty words and phrases <i>worth your life?</i>\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c<b><i>Yes</i></b>, unequivocally.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c\u2026 Why? Please, explain it to me, make me understand, because there must be something I am missing.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c If I don\u2019t do what I do, who will? If the people who would be heroes never stand up, who will help?\u201d<br /> <br /> Your grandfather is silent for a long time, and when you look up to him, his eyes are sparkling in barely-restrained amusement.<br /> <br /> \u201cThat is \u2026\u2026 amazingly na\u00efve, Kasai. Where did you get such a worldview?\u201d<br /> <br /> Sniffing, \u201cI prefer to think of it as optimism, myself. And I think like that almost-certainly because of the stories my father used to tell me as a kid. You know: Ryu Jiang and the Peach Grove, Honda of the Five-Dragon Fist, stuff like that.\u201d<br /> <br /> His face falls, filled with an expression of dawning horror. Pressing his face into his hands \u201c\u2026 My grandson based his morality and worldview on <i>fairy tales</i>? \u2026\u2026. I\u2019m never going to have great-grandchildren, am I?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cNot for any lack of trying on my part!\u201d you protest. \u201cBesides, I haven\u2019t exactly found the right girl yet.\u201d<br /> <br /> He chuckles, kneeling down beside the bench. \u201cWell, then you had better take hold of that \u201coptimism\u201d of yours, hold it close, because the real world is <i>cold</i>, and it doesn\u2019t care about spirit, or the \u2018right thing\u2019.\u201d<br /> <br /> You shrug, \u201cIt has worked for me for this long\u2026\u201d Looking away, \u201cand\u2026 I\u2019ve noticed that. Believe me, I had a panic attack when I realized just what I had gotten myself into. That was probably the closest I have come to dying in my life\u2026...\u201d<br /> <br /> He sighs, \u201cKasai, I\u2019m sorry to be so short with you, it is just that\u2026.. well, when I found out what was happening, and how badly you go injured\u2026\u2026 I\u2019m not used to feeling like that, so weak, so <i>helpless</i>. There was <i>nothing</i> I could do, and I hated it. You are the last of my line, my only grandchild, and I would fight the gods themselves before I willingly let you come to harm. Just\u2026. next time, don\u2019t just jump into things? Use your head, and not your \u2018spear\u2019, and <i>ask for help</i>. If you want to go do foolish things\u2026. Well, you are an adult, and can make your own choices, but <i>bring friends</i>. Me, your companions\u2026\u201d<br /> <br /> Sudden dawning horror. \u201cI forgot about them! Oh hell\u2026.\u201d<br /> <br /> He grins viciously, \u201cYep! The little one apparently said she was going to \u201chit you with a shoe\u201d when she saw you next, from what my man told me.\u201d<br /> <br /> Burying your face in your hands, \u201c\u2026. I\u2019m dead.\u201d<br /> <br /> He laughs, \u2018Come now, it can\u2019t be <i>that</i> bad!\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cYou haven\u2019t heard Saiko when she gets going. She <i>screeches</i>, like a cat.\u201d<br /> <br /> He claps you on the shoulder, all good spirits, \u201cThat, my young lad, sounds an awful lot like <i>your</i> problem.\u201d Helping you to your feet, \u2018Come, let us go home, might as well get it over with.\u201d<br /> <br /> Suddenly realizing the implications of his visit, \u201cWait, if you couldn\u2019t leave until the Council was done\u2026\u2026 the Council came to a decision!\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cYes\u201d<br /> <br /> Excitedly, \u201cWhat was it?\u201d<br /> <br /> He grins, \u201c You, <i>somehow</i>, brought seven of the twelve tribes over to the side of Xia. The Nemasket, the Assawompsset, and the Nasketucket as expected, as well as the Paskamansett, but you also got the Nauset, Onset and the Assonet, right out from under Iyannough\u2019s nose!\u201d He laughs, \u201cCorbitant bringing back the head of the Lunar, as well as what went down, almost certainly helped. As for the rest, the Mattepoisett, the Narragansett, Cohasset and the Poccasset, all agreed to neutrality, almost-certainly because they could get the lands of the tribes that were leaving. I don\u2019t think they trust the lowland Vanehani enough to move down there. Only the Neponset and Iyannough declared war, but that doesn\u2019t mean very much in the long run.\u201d His smile fades, \u201cThe Confederacy broke up as a result, of course. It was inevitable, I suppose.\u201d<br /> <br /> Stunned, \u201cthat is great! \u2026.. not the Confederacy breaking apart, but\u2026\u2026 \u201c A long silence, then \u201c So, what will happen now?\u201d<br /> <br /> He shrugs, \u201cthe Tribes march to war. Now, it will take a while for the full forces of the tribes to move into the lowlands, but I\u2019ve already started mustering the Nemasket together. Back at Dun Paunuxet, my Chosen are already gathering, and you will have one hell of an entrance when you get to Xia.\u201d<br /> <br /> That news bolsters your spirits, making the trip back to your grandfather\u2019s village seem to fly by, regardless of how much your injuries act up. A few hours away from the settlement, you start to feel a sense of dread, worrying about just what sort of tirade Saiko and Blue Pines have managed to cook up between them.<br /> <br /> It\u2026.. actually isn\u2019t that bad. Saiko, asides from a death-glare that would put your grandfather to shame, says nothing other than that you are, \u201can <i>asshole</i>\u201d, and that she isn\u2019t speaking to you until further notice. Blue Pines, on the other hand, is a little more vocal.<br /> <br /> \u201c You know, if you were going to go through all the effort of buying me all this gear, why wouldn\u2019t you at least take me with you when you get into fights?\u201d<br /> <br /> You sigh, \u201cYou can blame my grandfather for that one. He was the one who told me outsiders couldn\u2019t see where the Elder Council met. Hell, even I had to be blindfolded. As for the fight\u2026.. you probably wouldn\u2019t have wanted to be there. If anything, more people being there might have made her take the whole thing more seriously, and be more dangerous.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cWait, \u2018her\u2019?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cYeah\u2026.. I fought a Lunar Anathema. It was a <u>dumb</u> decision, I know. I only survived because I got lucky.\u201d<br /> <br /> She slowly lowers her face into her hands. \u201c\u2026. A Lunar Anathema. <i>You fought a Lunar Anathema<u>.</u></i> Gods save me!..... Listen, boss, with that in mind, I\u2019m <i>glad</i> you ended up not taking me, but \u2026\u2026 seriously, you need to pick and choose your battles a little more carefully, you know what I mean? I knew guys like you, who thought with your dicks, and where did that get them? In the river, floating face-down.\u201d<br /> <br /> You say, with a wry smile, \u201cMy grandfather already gave me this particular speech. Several times, in fact. But I appreciate the concern. I\u2019ll bring you the next time I challenge a man-eating demon-possessed abomination to a face-off.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cYour grandfather is a smart man\u201d, as she shakes her head.<br /> <br /> Over the next few days, more and more warriors of the Nemasket filter into the valley, until the whole rift glows at night from the light of many campfires. When all the Chosen warriors are gathered, your grandfather gives the order to move out, and the army starts making its way out of the mountains, in an entirely different direction from where you entered.<br /> <br /> This passage must be wider and flatter than the rough mountain trail you took to get here, and more suited to the travel of troops, but it takes you longer to get back to Xia; a little over two weeks. By the time you start descending from the mountains, summer is in full-spread\u2026.. and so is the humidity. It isn\u2019t too bad in the hill-country close to the mountains, but in the lower valleys, the air is so thick you can almost cut it with your sword.<br /> <br /> As your small army descends from the mountain passes into the lowland valleys, you start passing peasant villages, small and crude at first, but getting larger as you travel farther from the mountains. All of them, every single one, is deserted, not surprising when you march at the head of a proper Hill-Tribe warhost, something not seen in the Hundred Kingdoms for many years.<br /> When you reach the fork of the White river, where Azu Castle stands, you find an army before you, preventing you from advancing. With the banners blowing in the breeze, it is an easy task to identify the forces of Sanada Kotaro, Lord Wulin, his vassals, and right in front, the forces of Xia. As you look, the Xian banner surges forward, held aloft in the middle of a party of mounted Oathsworn, and you ride forward to meet it.<br /> <br /> There, in the middle of the two armies, you dismount from Xuansu, staggering as you do so, and bow as low as you are able before your liege. She peers at you from her saddle, then lifts her facemask in order to get a better look.<br /> <br /> As soon as she is able to clearly see you face (as well as all your other injuries), her stoic fa\u00e7ade drops, if only for a moment.<br /> <br /> \u201cKasai, is that you?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cYes, my Lord. I have returned from the diplomatic mission to the Dumoniapoccasset Confederacy, and bri\u2026\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c<i>Dragons Tits,</i> Kasai! What the hell happened to you, get in a fight with an angry bear?\u201d<br /> <br /> You chuckle, \u201cNot quite, I will tell you later, after the introductions.\u201d<br /> <br /> She shakes her head, \u201cNo, never mind that, you need to see a doctor. I can handle things on my own.\u201d<br /> <br /> In spite of your (admittedly weak and short-lived) protestations, you are all-but dragged away by one of Lord Iysen\u2019s retainers deep into Azu Castle, where in a particularly-cold room you meet, yet again, the doctor from Teriake. When he sees you, his eyebrows shoot up somewhere close to the ceiling, and he sighs the sigh of the long-suffering.<br /> <br /> \u201cYou know, the Lord doesn\u2019t pay me nearly enough\u2026\u2026 when you see her, tell I said that. For you, I want <i>double</i>.\u201d<br /> <br /> Resigning yourself to a long period of discomfort, you are pleasantly surprised to find that he only gives you a quick once-over, poking at casts and bandages and hemming and hawing all the while. The only truly-painful bit is when he jams his hand upside your crotch, feeling around the hip as you rotate (painfully!) the joint around.<br /> <br /> \u201cHow badly did you hit your head?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c Not that bad, I think? I remember hitting my head, as well as after it, and while I passed out, I think it was more me trying to stand on the leg than the cracked skull.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cWhat is this?\u201d. Pointing above your ear.<br /> <br /> \u201c The Hill-Tribe shaman said there was swelling, and they cut a hole in the skull to let it out.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cWas there blood?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cA \u2026. little?\u201d<br /> <br /> He rolls his eyes. \u201cWell, congratulations, you won\u2019t be doing much of anything for the next 3 months at least, maybe longer. Do you know what a \u2018concussion\u2019 is?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cNo.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cA concussion is when the brain gets knocked around in the skull, gets bruised and swells up. There can be both physical and emotional effects, short and long term. After you hit your head, did you have trouble balancing? Seem confused? Get emotional?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c\u2026. Yes, I had all of those things and more.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cThere we go, you have a concussion. Since you remember before, during and after the injury, it likely wasn\u2019t that bad, but I always prefer to play it safe. Nothing, other than light activity, for the next three months, then come see me. If, at <i>any</i> point, you have trouble sleeping, remembering things, or have fits, send me for me <i>right away</i>. There could be long-term effects.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cHow long will it take to heal?\u201d<br /> <br /> He shrugs, \u201cIt depends on the injury and the person. Some people get better after a month or two, others have problems for years. Avoid salt, fatty meat, and alcohol to excess, and any headaches that show up should be mitigated. As for the leg, there isn\u2019t much I can do that already hasn\u2019t been done. The ball is back in its socket, and the tissues are starting to heal. Have any pain or stiffness, especially when it is cold or wet out?\u201d<br /> <br /> Sighing, \u201cyes, a little. But when it isn\u2019t, there is none.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cYep, that is one likely long-term affect. The tissue around the bone gets damaged, and doesn\u2019t heal properly. I can give you something for the pain, but you will just have to deal with it. The teeth\u2026.. eh. I can commission a set of dentures for you, but it will take a while to carve, and until then the only thing I can tell you is to chew on the right side of your mouth, and avoid hard foods.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cNow, <i>go to sleep</i>. Sleep is the best medicine.\u201d<br /> <br /> At that, and armed with a jar of opium tablets, you toddle off to bed, led by an Azu-castle servant. Only a few hours later, Saiko wakes you up with particular grace (she didn\u2019t even shake you that hard!) and tells you Lord Iysen is waiting for you. When she holds out the crutches, you try to refuse, but her frosty glare indicates that wouldn\u2019t be a smart decision. Even with the crutches, you need help up the stairs, and Saiko even helps you (when you thank her, she acknowledges it with an eyeroll. She isn\u2019t <i>that</i> mad, evidently), and only a few stiff-and-sore minutes later, you find yourself before Lord Iysen and your grandfather, in one of the side-halls of the castle.<br /> <br /> Evidently, they are already partway through a bottle of moonshine, and the conversation between the two continues unabated while you eat: noodles in a beef broth, with beef slices and fresh vegetables. It is arguably the most expensive food you have ever eaten, tarnished only by the fact that you can\u2019t quite taste it over the pine resin covering half your mouth. Annoyed, you set the bowl aside just in time for Lord Iysen to question you.<br /> <br /> \u201cSo, why exactly didn\u2019t you tell me your grandfather was so \u2026.. <i>virile</i>, Kasai?\u201d<br /> <br /> You freeze, hand halfway to a cup of tea, before you comprehend exactly what she was getting at.<br /> <br /> <i>Gross</i>.<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt; Grandfather, <i>please</i>, don\u2019t hit on my boss\u2026.&gt;.\u201d<br /> <br /> Indignantly, \u201c&lt; Why the hell not?! She is a good looking woman! You need to step up your game, boy.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> She chimes in, \u201cWhat is he saying? I am assuming it is about me, since he went to Forestongue.\u201d<br /> <br /> He laughs, \u201cHe is asking me to not flirt with you. I can\u2019t understand why, a distinguished woman such as yourself deserves the attention. I would be <i>disrespecting</i> you if I did not!\u201d<br /> <br /> Your liege-lord, the ruling queen of a major kingdom, titters at the compliment. Shaking your head in disbelief, you eye the jar of moonshine on the table.<br /> <br /> \u201cHow much of that have you had?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cOnly half, between the two of us\u201d (well, that explains it. Moonshine is rotgut.) Lord Iysen leans over the table,<br /> <br /> \u201cSo, Kasai, about these wounds, how the hell did they happen?\u201d<br /> <br /> You take a deep breath, and after a moment, \u201c I went out and fought a Lunar Anathema. Yes, <i>I know</i>, it was a stupid choice. Everyone I have told it to has said it, so I would <i>appreciate</i> it if you don\u2019t lay it on too heavily.\u201d<br /> <br /> Flatly, \u201cYou fought a Lunar Anathema. And you are still alive?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c More like \u201cgot thrown around a room, got lucky when it counted\u201d, but yes, to both counts.\u201d<br /> <br /> Her eyebrow shoots up, \u201c\u2026 very well. I don\u2019t know whether to be impressed at your bravery or angry at your foolishness. The demon <i>was</i> dead, right?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cYes, I cut off its head and cut out its heart, to be sure.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c\u2026. Impressive. How did it happen?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c I walked into its cave, it tried to seduce me, I turned it down. We fought, and by \u2018fought\u201d, I really mean \u201cIt thrashed me seven ways till Sunday\u201d, breaking my armor and spear, until I managed to get ahold of ritually-sacrificed weapons, sacred to the Fickle Lady. They wounded the demon enough to where I was able to finish it off with my spear. In thanks, I sacrificed both my spear and the heart to Luna. That \u2026. about covers it, I think.\u201d<br /> <br /> She looks confused. \u201cWhat do you mean by \u201csacrificed\u2019?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cI dropped the broken spearhead and the heart into a sacred well of L..\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cA well? Underground, filled with black water and a full moon overhead?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cY\u2026 Yes?\u201d<br /> <br /> Lord Isyen sits back, looking dumbfounded. \u201c\u2026. How long ago was this?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cI dunno, about a month. Why?\u201d<br /> <br /> She stands up, walking over to a travelling chest. \u201cI had a dream about a month ago, about a well of black water, surrounded by black stone. The full moon shone in the water, and as I watched, the moon turned red. The water rippled, spilling over the floor, and a spearhead rose up from the water. I woke up after that, but I remembered enough to have the thing commissioned.\u201d<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:arial\">She turns around, unwrapping a long object. As the cloth rolls back, a spearhead is revealed, gleaming softly in the light of the lanterns. A little over a foot long, gracefully curving, with two wicked side-blades projecting towards the point. The socket is decorated with elaborate spirals of silver and niello, fading into each other across the feathersteel, and the blade itself has both the fuller and an inset carving filled with red lacquer. The inset has the Dragontongue characters \u6708\u5149\u904e\u6c34 \u201cMoonlight Over Water\u201d within it, outlined in more silver and niello. An amazing piece.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:arial\">She holds it out to you, \u201c\u2026 I guess the Argent Lady wanted to thank you, for what you did.\u201d</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:arial\">Stunned, you feel a quick bite of quilt. The gods <i>do</i> work in mysterious ways, after all, and you shouldn\u2019t really question their judgement.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:arial\">\u201c\u2026 Thank you, my Lord, this is a princely gift.\u201d</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:arial\">She sits back, taking a deep breath. \u201cIt is fitting, then, for you to own such a weapon.\u201d</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:arial\">Something in her voice catches your attention, and you drag your eyes away from your new weapon to actually look at your liege. She is tense, holding her fingers tightly cinched together.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:arial\">You grandfather notices as well. \u201c\u2026.. You want to do it now? I thought you meant <i>after</i> the war?\u201d</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:arial\">\u201cLook at him, Ousamequin! (evidently, they are on a first-name-basis already, they must be getting along well) He can barely walk, much less fight! What else can I do? I need the help, and every second I defer it is another knife aimed at my back!\u201d</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:arial\">Carefully, you set the spearhead aside, \u201cJust what the hell is going on?\u201d</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:arial\">She takes several more breaths, then, quietly and slowly, where you have to lean close to hear, \u201c I \u2026. have reason to believe that several of my vassals are plotting against me. Some of my far-flung lesser vassals, greedy for more land, or swayed by Yue or Ji. Sanada Kotaro, most of all. He was always the most truculent of my vassals, but this deal with the Hill-Tribes must have hit him off. I don\u2019t know how he found out, but he did, and he <i>hates</i> your people. He probably views an alliance with the Tribes as nothing less than treason against everything he holds dear.\u201d</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:arial\">\u201cAre we \u2026. <i>safe</i> here, in that case?\u201d</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:arial\">She smiles thinly, \u201cOh yes, for now at least. My forces are only a few days downriver, and we are about equal in manpower, so it wouldn\u2019t be an easy win. From what I have learned via my spies, and admittedly, it isn\u2019t much, Sanada evidently plans on waiting for the main bulk of the army to maneuver against Yue, before declaring independence and striking for Teriake.\u201d</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:arial\">You sigh, \u201cSo\u2026. Civil war, then?\u201d</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:arial\">\u201cHopefully not.\u201d</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:arial\">\u201cBut I thought you just said\u2026\u201d</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:arial\">\u201cIf all goes to plan, Kasai, not all of my forces will move downriver. That is where you come in.\u201d</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:arial\">Resting your cheek on your fist, you say, \u201cLet me guess, you want me to lead these forces?\u201d</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:arial\">\u201c No, Kasai, I want you to <i>rule</i> them.\u201d</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:arial\">\u201c\u2026.. wait, what?\u201d</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:arial\">Your grandfather joins in, \u201c Kasai, this was part of the alliance agreement that you weren\u2019t privy to. The main area the Tribes are going to settle in is the region called Jianghu. There is a castle there, and Lady Isyen wasn\u2019t about to give it up <i>or</i> destroy it. Instead, she would cede it to a loyal and <i>capable</i> vassal, who would supervise the integration of the Vanehani and the Tribes.\u201d</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:arial\">Pointing across the table at you, \u201cBoth of us agreed that you should be that vassal. A member of both cultures, skilled in both languages, morally upstanding and respected by all, who better?\u201d</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:arial\">Lord Isyen smiles, knotting the tablecloth. \u201cOf course, it isn\u2019t <i>entirely</i> a reward. In the case of a rebellion led by Sanada Kotaro, you would be the first loyalist on the scene, \u2018leading the charge\u2019, so to speak.\u201d</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:arial\">She pulls out map of Xia. \u201cJianghu and Yagura Castle is just to the east of here, several days travel up the White River. In such a strategic position, you would outflank both the Wulin Marches, held by Lord Sanada, and the Iruma Marches, held by Lord Jubei Imoto. You would, in effect, be my Gateway piece, both preventing movement and threatening all who threaten me. So long as you hold Jianghu, either of them cannot commit entirely to war.\u201d</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:arial\">\u201cAnd, of course, since you are wounded, you cannot commit to an offensive as of yet. It is your right to travel to your new home at Yagura, and to raise troops to wipe out the bandits infesting the region, as well as prepare for the arrival of the Hill-Tribes.\u201d</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:arial\">\u201cKasai, I would ennoble you, set you at the head of your own clan, and make you a peer of the realm, so long as you agree to these three conditions.\u201d</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:arial\">She holds up a finger, \u201cOne: You triple the amount of Men-at-Arms under the Jianghu levy. Right now, Jianghu has a population of about 3000 people, and can support about 300 or so soldiers. By next campaign season, I want 1000, if not more. How you raise them is up to you.\u201d</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:arial\">She holds up a second finger. \u201cTwo: Since you are apparently so hell-bent on committing suicide-by-heroism, I also want to see you wedded, with heirs on the way, by next summer.\u201d She grins, \u201c I have several young ladies in the lesser branches of my family looking to get married, and I can set something up if you want. Otherwise, there are other nobles, even wealthy commoners if you feel like defying social convention. Hell, Lord Sanada has a daughter of marrying age, if amusing irony is something you appreciate. However, so long as you have children, and as many as you are able, all that is up to you.\u201d</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:arial\">Finally, the thumb, and her expression turns deadly-serious, \u201c Lastly, I want you to swear an Oath to me, that as long as both of us live, you will be nothing more than my loyal vassal. I want your support in <i>everything</i>, in war and peace, even in deciding what color the drapes will be.\u201d</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:arial\">\u201cIf you will do those three things for me, I would make you the Marcher Lord of Jianghu, entitled to all its rents, rights and privileges. As Marcher Lord, you would be independent in all but name, with the rights to write your own laws, collect your own taxes, and make your own vassals, if you so desire.\u201d</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:arial\">She sits up, back straight, and looks you full in the eyes. \u201c Kasai, would you be my man?\u201d</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:arial\">Stunned beyond all measure, you take a few moments to collect your thoughts, then:</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:arial\">1: Yes! \u201cMy Lord, I am your man, for as long as I live\u201d.</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:arial\">2: No. \u201c I am no puppet. If I will rule, I will do it on my own merits, not for anyone elses benefit.\u201d</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:arial\">3: Maybe? \u201cI\u2026. don\u2019t think I am up to such a task, honestly.\u201d</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:arial\">4: Something else? (write in)</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:arial\">OOC:</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:arial\">Jesus Christ this one was long.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:arial\">Kasai: Grandpa no! D:</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:arial\">Lord Iysen : Grandpa <i>yes</i>! ;D</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:arial\">Throwing the dog a bone on this one, for getting everyone all worked up about Exaltation.<br /> <br /> This is what Kasai's new spearhead looks like: </span>https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yari#/media/File:Yari-p1000609.jpg<br /> <br /> And Yagura Castle is on this map. The new Jianghu Marches extends off to the west, to the next river fork.<br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"http://imgur.com/7JpSQye\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/google-material/image_placeholder.svg\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://imgur.com/7JpSQye\" target=\"_blank\"> </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://imgur.com/7JpSQye\" target=\"_blank\"> http://imgur.com/7JpSQye </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> Here is a closer view<br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"http://imgur.com/hLDrK4y\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/google-material/image_placeholder.svg\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://imgur.com/hLDrK4y\" target=\"_blank\"> </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://imgur.com/hLDrK4y\" target=\"_blank\"> http://imgur.com/hLDrK4y </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:arial\">NOTE: IF there is a problem with how this post is formatted, let me know. I just spent about 10 minutes trying to reformat it, but I think things are still messed up. </span><br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11892,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","user":"Boston123","date":". 05-15-2017, 10:04 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-16T11:07:38-07:00","id":1093343,"title":"","content":"Yes!<br /> <br /> (We made it! And all we had to do was almost die over and over again!)<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>On the frontier of the Wild South, there's only one woman with the grit to take on its most dangerous outlaws and bring them <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/984977-back-alive-or-maybe-dead-quest\" target=\"_blank\">Back Alive, or Maybe Dead.</a><br /> <br /> Avatar by <a href=\"http://puzzlinghappenstance.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">K.S. Brenowitz</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/908-semicasual","name":"semicasual","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar908_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2499"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-16T14:40:33-07:00","id":1093420,"title":"","content":"I mean ..... just like Lord Iysen said, it isn't <i>entirely</i> a reward.<br /> <br /> A whole lotta responsibility, a big ol' target on his back, so on and so forth.<br /> <br /> Up to this point, the most complicated thing Kasai has ever organized has been a dinner setting, or drilling some peasants. Most nobles, especially heirs, get &quot;training&quot; for rulership when they are children from their parents. Kasai got <u>none</u> of that. Partially because he lived with his grandfather for some time, and partially because his stepmother didn't want him to be his father's heir. Instead, he got the &quot;standard&quot; Oathsworn training from his father, which ........ <i>touches</i> on <i>some</i> of the aspects of running a feudal domain, but doesn't cover the topic in any sort of depth.<br /> <br /> Now (if you say yes, but I'll admit this is a pretty straightforward choice, by design), he will in charge of 3000 people outright, and almost-certainly more. He will have to recruit , train and lead 1000+ soldiers in war, and develop logistical trends to that effort. He has to supervise the integration of the Hill-Tribes into their new territories, several thousand particularly-rambunctious people all-told, with quite-different ideas of culture,social norms, and religion than what he is used to. (While he isn't ruling directly over the Hill-Tribes, he is the go-to-guy when there are problems between the Vanehani and the Tribes, working with the Tribal elders and chiefs), as well as supervising and implementing the construction of treaty-promised roads and bridges (this <i>will</i> get contracted out, however). He has to levy taxes, collect taxes, implement laws and see them through, etc etc etc.<br /> <br /> Now, both Lord Iysen and Ousamequin aren't just going to throw Kasai in the river and scream &quot;SWIM OR DIE MOTHERFUCKER&quot; at him. They will both offer advice and lend him advisers ..... but the final choices are entirely up to him.<br /> <br /> On the other hand...... he <i>would be</i> the in-universe equivalent to a Duke. If he accepts, he would get the following Merits:<br /> -Resources 4 (my version, not the books), just from agricultural production alone,<br /> -Resources 2 from taxes,<br /> -Resources 2 from tariffs and fees<br /> (All of the Resources ratings can, and likely will, go up if/when Jianghu increases in population, or depending on how he sets domestic policies)<br /> - Command 4 (using the 2E rating, which is 500 troops) [I find it easier to use that system, as opposed to 3E, just for numbers]<div style=\"margin-left:40px\">= 3 spear regiments, of 60 men each<br /> = 2 longbow regiments, of 60 men each<br /> =2 halberd regiments, of 50 men each<br /> = 2 light cavalry regiments, of 30 men each<br /> = a compliment of 40-ish Oathsworn, although most of these will serve as regimental officers<br /> = (Most of the time, not all soldiers would be mustered. The above numbers would strip Jianghu of most fighting-men, not just the able-bodied)<br /> <br /> - In the 3E Battlegroup system, these would be equal to Size 2 BG's. Stats are forthcoming<br /> <br /> - And, Lord Iysen wants Jianghu to march to war with 1000 soldiers, so he would be in charge of more (She probably expects him to be able to levy more than 1000, once he exploits the space available in Jianghu to the fullest. Jianghu is a rural backwater, and there is a lot of space to expand. Secondly, both the Lord Wulin and the Lord Iruma have around 3000-5000 soldiers, between themselves and their vassals, so Kasai should want to at least come close to that)</div>- Influence 3<br /> - Ally 1, depending on who he weds<br /> <br /> So on and so forth.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11892,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","user":"Boston123","date":". 05-16-2017, 07:26 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-16T15:55:18-07:00","id":1093433,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"font-family:arial\">Yes! \u201cMy Lord, I am your man, for as long as I live\u201d.<br /> <br /> The point of going to the tournament was to get lucky and a position. This is a position. <i>A chance to put the Oaths to work on a large scale</i>.</span><br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Like my Avatar? Courtesy of<span style=\"color:#0000FF\"> Jen</span>! : <span style=\"font-size:12px\">Anybody want their characters to be experimented on ? </span>post 98<br /> <i>An Exalt is never unarmed.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4018-l-het-esh","name":"L'het'esh","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4018_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"521"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-16T19:35:27-07:00","id":1093498,"title":"","content":"Hmmmmmmmmmmmmm. Tough. Can we say yes to 1 and 2 and a modified 3 for the terms. I don't like the thought of an absentee landlord picking my drape colors. We can swear loyalty but she's asking for much more than that. Also may I suggest stealing some veteran soldiers from the neighbors by offering them the chance to have land? This would grab a few extra soldiers but potentially experienced soldiers and lieutenants. Just thinking out loud.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11567-jairain","name":"Jairain","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11567_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"345"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-16T20:18:47-07:00","id":1093515,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Jairain</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1093498#post1093498\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Hmmmmmmmmmmmmm. Tough. Can we say yes to 1 and 2 and a modified 3 for the terms. I don't like the thought of an absentee landlord picking my drape colors. We can swear loyalty but she's asking for much more than that. Also may I suggest stealing some veteran soldiers from the neighbors by offering them the chance to have land? This would grab a few extra soldiers but potentially experienced soldiers and lieutenants. Just thinking out loud.</div> </div> </div> </div>She is, by and large, being hyperbolic. <br /> <br /> In essence, what she wants is a vassal that is loyal to <u>her</u>, as opposed to one that is loyal to her so long as it is convenient, aka what her vassals are now.<br /> <br /> Most Lords primary loyalty is, first and foremost, to their family and clan, their dynasty. All of Lord Iysen's primary vassals: the Lords Wulin, Iruma and Aikawa, serve under her as long as doing so is politically and economically expedient. If leaving her service would lead to increased prestige or power, they would likely jump on the chance to leave, or take power for themselves. She has to play a careful balancing game, playing the clans against each other, from without and within, keep them happy in her service, to ensure they don't declare independence, or leave and join another domain.<br /> <br /> Kasai, on his own, has no clan, no dynasty, no lands or wealth or ancestors, no power of his own. If Lord Iysen is going to raise him up, she wants him to be mindful that <u>everything</u> he now has comes from her. If she is removed from power, he would likely lose <u>everything</u>. Therefore, he should want to keep her in power above almost all else. That is basically what she wants: s<i><b>omeone she can count on, when all else fails.</b></i><br /> <br /> As for the &quot;support in everything&quot; line, that ties into loyalty. Since the three Lords mentioned above are Marcher Lords (as Kasai would be if you accept), they are, again, functionally independent in all-but-name already. She can <u>ask</u> them to summon their forces and march to war, but they would be well-within their rights to say &quot;nah, something came up, can't come&quot;, and there is <u>very little</u> she can do about it, asides from declaring war on them...... which would almost-certainly destroy Xia. She has to toe &quot;that line&quot; <b><u><i>very carefully</i></u></b>.<br /> <br /> That is the double-edged sword of having Marcher Lords. The borders of your realm will be well-defended, but it comes at a cost of vassal loyalty. If Lord Iysen could strip them of Marcher-Lord powers, she likely would. The clans have been vassals to Xia as long as Xia has been around, and been Marcher-Lords for almost as long. If she tried to strip them of power, they would certainly rebel.<br /> <br /> Stuff like this happened <u>all the goddamn time</u>, both in Europe and in Asia. <br /> <br /> As for stealing the men-at-arms from other domains..... it's possible, although it won't make Kasai many friends among the nobility. Peasants can leave and go wherever they want, while serfs need permission from their landlord (and they usually don't get it).<br /> <br /> If Kasai wants to increase the population of Jianghu, he will need to do one, or several, things:<br /> <br /> 1) Make it economically beneficial to move to Jianghu. Lower taxes/rents, give out larger parcels of land, give &quot;sign-on&quot; bonuses for moving, that sort of thing. They are going to have to be significant (or aimed at a populace that lacks opportunity, like younger sons and daughters with no inheritance), because uprooting your life is expensive and potentially catastrophic, and most Men-at-Arms are already well-established.<br /> 2) Make Jianghu a shining beacon of progress compared to other domains. If your landlord is a right asshole, who makes you pay heavy taxes, mistreats you, so on and so forth, and you hear about this new domain where people are treated nicely .... you might try to make a break for it.<br /> 3) Discover some untapped natural resource and exploit it. Jianghu has large forests, deposits of stone and iron and coal, all untouched because of the remoteness of the area and the sheer <i>ass-ton </i>of bandits infesting the region. If Kasai cleans up the region (and he pretty much is going to have to. A great way to train troops and gain skill in commanding at the same time!), he can exploit these resources, drawing people in search of work to the region.<br /> <br /> So on and so forth.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-16T22:16:55-07:00","id":1093541,"title":"","content":"I say we take the deal, Option Number 1! This is what we've been trying to do this whole time. We've finally made it up in the world!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11619-kymmetheseventh","name":"KymmetheSeventh","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11619_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"151"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-17T15:23:33-07:00","id":1093860,"title":"","content":"I would take option 1, but with the caveat that service must be in ways that do not violate the oaths, and if she wants him to get married she must let the wife pick the drapes.<br /> second since we will be settling the area with Hill Tribes, I suggest we recruit from there and build a force I call Iron Wolves, essentially the skill of the Hill Tribes Warriors but with discipline and better equipment, a focus on team work and a deemphasis on seeking personal glory and honor.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1435-videopete","name":"videopete","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1435_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"111"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-17T16:22:04-07:00","id":1093888,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>videopete</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1093860#post1093860\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I would take option 1, but with the caveat that service must be in ways that do not violate the oaths, and if she wants him to get married she must let the wife pick the drapes.<br /> second since we will be settling the area with Hill Tribes, I suggest we recruit from there and build a force I call Iron Wolves, essentially the skill of the Hill Tribes Warriors but with discipline and better equipment, a focus on team work and a deemphasis on seeking personal glory and honor.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> The drapes-thing was a joke, guys. Lord Iysen was exaggerating for effect.<br /> <br /> As for recruiting Hill-Tribe warriors, that is entirely possible, but it will take a bit of work.<br /> <br /> Unlike the Vanehani, who serve in war because their social class demands it (due of oaths of obligation), the Hill-Tribes follow warleaders due to the leaders being successful, generous (both in feasts and in weapons, jewelry, etc), and through personal charisma.<br /> <br /> If a Hill-Tribe warleader does little other than rest in town, eating and drinking, they will find their support withering away, as warriors leave to support a leader who will lead them to glory. On the other hand, a warleader who leads their warriors to victory after victory, but doesn't give gifts or feasts to share their success, will have the same thing happen.<br /> <br /> The younger, unproven warriors are more likely to join Kasai, but they won't respond all that positively to any attempts to install order or discipline. Hill-Tribe youth bands are akin to college frat parties: a bunch of young men (and women!) who are out looking for a good time, a way to boost their reputations and show off their bravery and skill, so on and so forth. Both events involve the ample consumption of alcohol to excess.<br /> <br /> The older warriors, the Chosen, are less likely to join Kasai (they are likely already married, with spouses that are wealthy by Hill-Tribe standards, and don't need to show off how big their metaphorical dicks are), but they would be the easiest to train and integrate into Vanehani-style forces.<br /> <br /> The two cultures are ..... different, enough to a degree that it would take generations of intermixing before the two approach homogeneous. There is already going to be a difficult amount of culture-shock as it is, and the populace of Jianghu are &quot;used&quot; to the Hill-Tribes already, having traded with (and intermarried with, sometimes) them for years. The Hill-Tribes, to put it bluntly, are not 'used' to peace, and almost every aspect of their culture is organized around endemic warfare (<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Endemic_warfare\" target=\"_blank\">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Endemic_warfare</a>). The constant, albeit relatively low-intensity, raids and skirmishes between the Tribes is one of the reasons their population is so low.<br /> <br /> I don't want to dissuade you from the idea, but it will be <i>very</i> difficult to beat (maybe literally) the Hill-Tribes into organized forces. It would be easier just to use them &quot;as is&quot;, as light skirmish infantry supporting the heavier Hundred Kingdoms forces.<br /> <br /> Of course, it bears mentioning that Kasai, as Lord Jianghu, will have <i>no</i> control over the Hill-Tribes, as part of the peace-treaty stated that they would remain entirely independent. Of course, another part of the agreement stated that Lord Iysen (and by proxy, Kasai) wouldn't abide attacks on their people, so he can get involved if they persist in being annoying cockholes.<br /> <br /> On that matter, the Elders and Chiefs of the Tribes are a hell of a lot less battlehappy than the young warriors, and <i>actually want</i> this peace treaty to work out; they know a good deal when they see one. They would drop the hammer like the wrath of god on any troublemakers, likely before Kasai got around to bringing it up. Ousamequin is going to be <i>really good</i> at stamping out troublemakers.<br /> <br /> ..... That is one reason why Lord Iysen chose Kasai to be the ruler of where the Tribes were going to settle. He is both a shield and a sword against Hill-Tribe aggression. Ousamequin isn't going to want his grandson's prestige/holdings/what-have-you damaged, and try to stamp out issues before they arise. And, the other Tribes aren't going to want to piss off Ousamequin, even if they don't care what Kasai thinks.<br /> <br /> Ousamequin is a big fan of &quot;head and scalps on pikes&quot; as a form of deterrent.<br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11892,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","user":"Boston123","date":". 05-17-2017, 09:57 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-18T14:18:24-07:00","id":1094303,"title":"","content":"1. I think nows an appropriate time to become a vassal. <br /> <br /> I'd like to suggest that we do demand this possible traitors daughters hand in marriage. If it could be worked out, it would serve as a massive deterrent on his end to raiding you or even moving on our Lord. Integrating communities will be really damn hard and im kind of hard pressed in imagining how to do so successfully. Even greater still is gaining the numbers and dealing with bandits. How do you exert your power over those bandits? I worry trying to pay them outright wont be all that successful. Maybe killing their leaders and showing what our force could do given proper motivation might help us kotow those disinterested in serving?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8897-joshopotamus","name":"joshopotamus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8897_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"97"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-18T15:14:41-07:00","id":1094316,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>joshopotamus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1094303#post1094303\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">1. I think nows an appropriate time to become a vassal.<br /> <br /> I'd like to suggest that we do demand this possible traitors daughters hand in marriage. If it could be worked out, it would serve as a massive deterrent on his end to raiding you or even moving on our Lord. Integrating communities will be really damn hard and im kind of hard pressed in imagining how to do so successfully. Even greater still is gaining the numbers and dealing with bandits. How do you exert your power over those bandits? I worry trying to pay them outright wont be all that successful. Maybe killing their leaders and showing what our force could do given proper motivation might help us kotow those disinterested in serving?</div> </div> </div> </div>1) Kasai can't &quot;demand&quot; anything of the Lord Wulin (Sanada Kotaro). The guy hasn't exactly rebelled yet; to the outside world he is nothing less than the perfect vassal. Lord Iysen is hoping promoting Kasai to the peerage will be enough to stem any possibility of rebellion, in a kind-of &quot;I know what you are up to&quot; way.<br /> <br /> Lord Iysen is many things, but she isn't stupid. She wouldn't survive as a ruler in the &quot;Sengoku Jidai on crack&quot; Hundred Kingdoms if she was<br /> <br /> 2) He can certainly <i>ask</i> for permission to court the girl. If the two get married, he will have to pay a bride-price (which for the daughter of a Duke-equivalent is pretty steep), but will get a correspondingly large dowry (lump sums of cash, grants of land, etc) in return.<br /> <br /> 3) Marrying the girl will almost-certainly prevent the Lord Wulin from making a move on Kasai. Hell, it might even make him try to bring him into the fold!<br /> <br /> 4) How else do you exert power over people outside the law?<br /> <br /> Bring them inside the law ...... on the end of a rope.<br /> <br /> On a less murdery-note, most of the bandits in the Hundred Kingdoms are 1) dispossessed commoners, driven from their homes via war or politics, and turned to raiding to survive, or 2) deserted soldiers, who turned to raiding to survive. They aren't mindless antagonists (they certainly are antagonistic, however, just not mindless) like Raiders in Fallout games, they <i>want</i> things. Mostly, stuff that will let them live to see another day (food, weapons), or things to enjoy themselves with, in aim to forget the things they have done. (Money, booze, women/men, drugs, etc).<br /> <br /> I certainly wouldn't trust them, but if Kasai kills their leaders and browbeats the rest in giving up (and promises not to hang them), they will either leave Jianghu and plague some other domain, or surrender and try make something of their new lives...... which almost certainly won't work out, since most law-abiding folks <b><u><i>hate</i></u></b> bandits.<br /> <br /> They are pretty decent light infantry, however, and the deserters are at least familiar with massed-unit tactics, so that is a thing.<br /> <br /> If you come up with an idea, let it out, by all means.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-18T17:04:33-07:00","id":1094346,"title":"","content":"Instilling discipline and team work is not as hard as you make it out to be, ask any one who had to go through boot camp. The whole process is about breaking the individual down, and rebuilding them up. The main techniques in this is mass punishment, when one fails or messes up they all mess up. Combine this with sleep deprivation, hunger (In basic you eat close to or over 3000 calories a day and still hungry, so not being starved). Same idea.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1435-videopete","name":"videopete","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1435_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"111"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-19T13:54:20-07:00","id":1094670,"title":"","content":"I apologize, demand was obviously the wrong word. Respectfully request that she aid us in courting his daughter. I think the dowry would be worth it. As far as what comes after this is mainly thoughts and not my intended actions past the &quot;yes lord we will be your man&quot; option i've already suggested we take. <br /> <br /> <b>Dealing with Hill People and Finding Natural Resources</b><br /> Maybe creating some token of value or worth for our hill tribe allies that would give them great glory if they were to find a resource for us. Kind of the eagle feather equivalent in counting coup to have our hill tribe friends search for valuable resources. Who ever finds the most useful resource gets the largest feather/valuable show of ones glorie or worth or whatever mcguffin they use to show status as warriors/scouts/etc.<br /> <br /> <b>Dealing with Bandits</b><br /> As far as the bandits are concerned, i doubt any of their leaders would be willing to serve and who ever leads the most fearsome group of bandits will have to be made an example. Maybe an initial parlay meant to show our force of arms (the five hundred we have on hand which could either make them fearful or band together, that's a gamble maybe?). and offer bandits the opportunity to exploit the natural resources found by our hill people scouts? I assume at that point head bandit group leader will make a fuss and we'll have to bait him into getting aggressive but as boston has suggested they may hate everything we have to say and just leave hopefully not coming back to raid us. We'd have to see and obviously all of this is rather far out.<br /> <br /> <b>Choosing a wife</b><br /> I really stress the other guys daughter if there might be any way we can gain her hand because if it doesn't deter this other lord from splitting it would at least give us an idea into where he stands and give us plenty of opportunity to watch his dealings more closely. Marrying any of Lord Iysen's relatives seems like a poor decision if we're fearful about the other marcher lords loyalty. Other questions are how public would our swearing of oaths to lord iysen be? If the other lords didn't know we swore oaths then we could make it seem as though kasai did her a big favor but only has loyalty to himself.<br /> <br /> Right now again all of this is just thinking out loud wanting to hear other people's thoughts<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8897-joshopotamus","name":"joshopotamus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8897_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"97"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-19T15:14:00-07:00","id":1094695,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>joshopotamus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1094670#post1094670\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">-snip-</div> </div> </div> </div>1)There are a multitude of potential spouses that Kasai (and you readers) can choose from. I .... wouldn't lock yourself into a choice just yet. Different spouses give different benefits, which will be revealed when I actually come up with the list.<br /> <br /> 2) Whatever resources that are located on the Hill-Tribes ceded-territory is theirs by default. I might not have understood this paragraph, so if that wasn't what you meant, please explain.<br /> <br /> 3) Kasai can most certainly recruit Hill-Tribe warriors, he just isn't going to recruit massive units of light infantry. He will get a couple, maybe enough to form a small bodyguard cadre, but he isn't going to recruit them with a pitch of &quot;come and serve me, learn discipline and honor!&quot;.<br /> <br /> Hill-Tribe warriors serve because they <i>want</i> to serve, and they generally want to serve specific leaders because 1) the leader has reknown (now, Kasai is actually pretty well known among the Tribes. He is the grandson of Ousamequin, and killed a friggen god-beast. He could probably score a couple followers on that alone), 2) the leaders are successful in war, meaning being attached to their coattails brings status, 3) the leader is generous: influential chieftains give <i>a lot</i> of feasts and gifts away, and 4) the leader has a degree of influence over the warrior (relative, trained them, etc).<br /> <br /> If a leader starts to lack any of those things, they will find themselves alone in short order.<br /> <br /> Are... are you guys just not understanding the difference between Hundred-Kingdom warleaders and Hill-Tribe warleaders? I'll admit; the style of recruiting and leadership is different from what many used to an organized army uses, and it could lead to a great deal of confusion.<br /> <br /> The Hill-Tribes are largely based off both Native American and Celtic groups. The Chiefs are based off this (<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sachem\" target=\"_blank\">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sachem</a>)<br /> <br /> 4) Most bandit-groups are going to fade away into the backwoods whenever anything even approaching a fair fight comes their way. It is how they survive.<br /> <br /> Burn their hideouts, hang them when they get caught, and the threat will diminish. Hell, there is probably going to be a mass-migration of the bandits in the hills, because the Hill-Tribes are certainly not going to put up with their shit.<br /> <br /> Man, am I going to have <i>fun</i> with these bandits : )<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-19T19:20:45-07:00","id":1094750,"title":"","content":"Actually, setting the Hill Tribe warriors loose on the bandits with a bounty per head would probably thin the bandits down a lot. Perhaps promising them tools would be better than just money?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Like my Avatar? Courtesy of<span style=\"color:#0000FF\"> Jen</span>! : <span style=\"font-size:12px\">Anybody want their characters to be experimented on ? </span>post 98<br /> <i>An Exalt is never unarmed.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4018-l-het-esh","name":"L'het'esh","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4018_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"521"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-22T18:13:19-07:00","id":1095694,"title":"","content":"Seems pretty cut and dried.<br /> -------<br /> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8897-joshopotamus\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar8897_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"8897\" data-vbnamecard=\"8897\">joshopotamus</a> The thing doesn\u2019t have to be public. It certainly can be, with a whole ceremony (which is usually what happens when someone new ascends to the throne, and wants to show everyone how powerful they are), but that is mainly to show off how wealthy the people involved are. At its barest form, all the situation demands is the swear-er and the swear-ee.<br /> --------<br /> <br /> After collecting your thoughts (and closing your mouth), you bow your head.<br /> <br /> \u201cMy Lord, I am your man, for as long as I may live.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cGood!\u201d She grins, standing again and walking over to her armor-stand, pulling out her sword as she returns.<br /> <br /> \u201cUh\u2026. It is traditional for you to kneel. Are you capable? If you can\u2019t, I understand. Never been much for tradition, anyways\u2026\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cI\u2019d prefer not to, honestly. If I did, I would need help standing again.\u201d<br /> <br /> She shrugs, \u201cFair enough.\u201d Walking over in front of you, she takes a moment to compose herself, then intones, \u201c\u2026. Kasai, Oathsworn, scion of Clan\u2026. <i>well, we\u2019ll get to that</i>\u2026\u2026 do you swear homage to me, as rightful ruler of Xia, to uphold my laws and protect my holdings, to defend my people and honor, until the world ends?\u201d<br /> <br /> Mouth dry, \u201cI do.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c Then swear to me, by steel and silk, for honor and integrity, and in front of the gods and men, an Oath of Fealty.\u201d, and she kneels in front of you, burying the point of her blade into the wooden floor, with the crosspiece in between you.<br /> <br /> Placing your hands over hers on the handle, you softly recite, \u201c On my honor, I hereby swear eternal homage to the Lady Iysen Shiba, Lord Xia, for as long as I am able. I will support her in war, uphold her rule in peace, and defend her from all enemies. This I swear!\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cAnd to you, Kasai, I gift to you the fiefdom of the Jianghu Marches, and all the traditional rights and privileges therein. I name you the Lord Jianghu, Lord Paramount, and entrust to you Xia\u2019s northern border, in defense against all that will do me harm. I raise you up to noble-status, a peer of the realm, and head of your own clan. I, in turn, will defend you and your rights for as long as I am able. This I swear!\u201d<br /> <br /> In unison, both of you draw the palms of your hands along the edge of the steel, enough to draw blood. Once the blood is running, you clasp her hand with yours (gently! your fingers are still broken), mingling blood and sealing the Oath.<br /> <br /> There is a long moment of silence, then \u201cWell, that brings back memories!\u201d, Lord Iysen says as she rises. \u201cThe last time I formally swore an Oath was over twenty years ago, I had almost forgotten how!\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c\u2026.. What now?\u201d, you ask, still a mite overwhelmed by what just happened.<br /> <br /> \u201cWell, first things first, you have a drink, to celebrate this momentous occasion.\u201d, she grins, \u201cThen, you head to your new home, and start carrying out your new responsibilities. I am assuming you are going to want help? Running a fiefdom isn\u2019t much like anything else, even a small one like Jianghu\u2026.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c<b><i>Yes</i></b>. I will be the first one to admit that I have no idea what I am doing.\u201d Lord Iysen laughs at this.<br /> <br /> \u201cI understand, believe me or not. I was about your age when I had to take over Xia. If anything, I was even less prepared than you were. I was my fathers fifth child, so I thought I was going to have an easy life as an Oathsworn, wandering around and righting wrongs\u2026.\u201d She shakes her head, \u201c Life changes so fast\u2026. I was the terror of every bandit in Xia, the \u201cTiger of Kai\u201d, now look at me, everything I hated as a young woman.\u201d Her face turns morose.<br /> <br /> \u201cDon\u2019t let this change you, Kasai. You will be tempted, by the <i>gods</i> will you be tempted, to take the easy way through things. Don\u2019t, even if they seem, well, easier. Stick to the Oaths, even if people tell you they aren\u2019t applicable to the \u2018real world\u2019. I wish I did\u2026\u2026 These are trying times, and we need the spirit of the Oaths more than ever.\u201d<br /> <br /> Another long moment of silence, \u201c\u2026 Forgive me, that must be the drink. I get maudlin when I drink.\u201d<br /> <br /> Your grandfather lines up a rounds of moonshine shots. \u201cThat just means you need to drink <i>more,</i> Lady Iysen, until the sentimentality stops!\u201d<br /> <br /> She smiles again, blinking way tears, \u201c A man after my own heart. I\u2019ve already ruined Lord Kotaro\u2019s carpet, what with the blood and the stabbings and whatnot, what is a little more?\u201d<br /> <br /> Taking the small glass, the three of you raise them high, clinking them together.<br /> <br /> \u201cTo the new alliance!\u201d, tossing it back.<br /> <br /> \u201cTo Xia!\u201d, and again.<br /> <br /> \u201cTo the new Lord Jianghu!\u201d, for a final time. The spirit burns in your mouth and on the way down, setting your nose and gums to stinging. In spite of the pain, you smile.<br /> <br /> Feels good.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <b><i><u>Jianghu Domestic Policies</u></i></b><br /> <br /> Kasai, as the Lord Paramount of Jianghu, can basically run his new fiefdom however the hell he pleases. Free peasantry or bonded serfs? High or low taxes? Centralized or spread out? Those choices and more are entirely his.<br /> So, what I am asking is for you fine folks to decide on the domestic policies governing the new region, on a 1 to 5 scale.<ul><li>Centralization: Whether Kasai holds onto judicial and legislative powers<ol class=\"decimal\"><li>Decentralized (local landed retainers have authorization to hear and decide on court cases, as well as law implementation) <b>[this is what is in effect right now in Jianghu]</b></li> <li>Little decentralized (local landed retainers have authorization to hear and decide on court cases)</li> <li>Mix: dependent on situation</li> <li>Little centralization (law and court cases must be brought to a specialized representative of Kasai, aka a Magistrate of his choosing)</li> <li>Highly centralized (all legislative and judicial rulings come from Kasai himself)</li> </ol></li> <li>Aristocracy vs Plutocracy: Whether wealth is favored in agriculture or in trade, aka price of raw goods vs finished goods. Has effect on economy<ol class=\"decimal\"><li>Highly Aristocratic (Strongly agriculturally favored, attract more Oathsworn)<b> [Current policy in effect in Jianghu]</b></li> <li>Slightly Aristocratic ( Slightly agriculturally favored)</li> <li>Mix</li> <li>Slightly Plutocratic ( Prices favor finished goods and tradesmen)</li> <li>Highly Plutocratic (Trading centers strongly influential, give Liangzhou market-town a charter, allow them to run themselves)</li> </ol></li> <li>Commoners: generally, the more peasants, the more money Kasai can collect in taxes, and the more peasants, the more food he gets<ol class=\"decimal\"><li>All peasants (free landowners, Resources (agriculture) goes down to 0 {effectively, Kasai will have to pay people to work his lands}, Resources (taxes) will go up to 4, as well as more people being eligible to levy</li> <li>Mostly peasants ( 7 to 3 split, Resources (agriculture) goes down to 2, Resources (taxes) goes up to 3)</li> <li>Even mix (5 on 5 split, Resources (agriculture) goes to 3, Resources (taxes) goes to 3)</li> <li>Mostly serfs (7 to 3 split, Resources (agriculture) goes to 4, Resources (taxes) goes to 2) <b>[current policy]</b></li> <li>All serfs (bonded land-workers, Resources (agriculture) goes to 4, Resources (taxes) go to 0, no levyable Men-at-Arms, probably rebellions)</li> </ol></li> <li>Mercantilism vs Free-Trade:<ol class=\"decimal\"><li>Free-Trade favored: encourages external trade. Boost external economy</li> <li>Mercantilism favored: protectionist, encourages local trade. Boost local economy</li> </ol></li> <li>Attracting new settlers<ol class=\"decimal\"><li>Offer tax/food-rent breaks to new settlers who move</li> <li>Offer lump sums of cash to new settlers who move</li> <li>If someone joins your army and serves for 5 years, give them 40 acres (aka \u2018standard\u2019 peasant farm size) and an ox (expensive draft animal) when they muster out</li> <li>If people move to hinterlands of Jianghu where there are natural resources, give the settlement shares of profits when resource is exploited</li> </ol></li> <li>Army composition<ol class=\"decimal\"><li>\u201cstandard\u201d : Peasants buy whatever weapons they want and can afford, and show up for muster (no effect) [<b>current policy]</b></li> <li>Give weapons to new settlers: expensive, but Kasai can guarantee weapon quality</li> <li>Assize of Arms edict: dependent on the value of the land one holds, they have to show up with certain weapons</li> <li>Militia edict: every household, regardless of status, has to have a spear and a crossbow, with at least one person trained in their use</li> </ol></li> <li>Army training<ol class=\"decimal\"><li>\u201cstandard\u201d: men-at-arms are trained by their Oathsworn landlords whenever they get the time [<b>current policy</b>]</li> <li>Regular Muster: Every Sunday, every landowner has to show up at the local village center for drill</li> <li>Summer Maneuvers: every summer, after the harvest and planting is done, every landowner shows up for 6 weeks of long-term drill at Yagura Castle</li> </ol></li> </ul>Finally,<ul><li>Kasai's new clan name:<ul><li>Gekokujo: Dragontongue phrase meaning &quot; the low overcomes the high&quot;, or more poetically, &quot;surpassing one's superiors&quot;</li> <li>Qianke: Dragontongue word meaning &quot;spearman&quot;, or more poetically, &quot;one of the way of the spear&quot;</li> <li>Matsuoka : Dragontongue word meaning &quot;Pine-tree covered hill&quot;, an apt description of Jianghu</li> <li>Yamaue: Dragontongue word meaning &quot;from above the mountains&quot;</li> <li>Ikashi: Dragontongue word meaning &quot; forested pool&quot;, a reference to Yari Lake, a notable feature in Jianghu</li> <li>Noburuma : Dragontongue word meaning &quot;figure of real nobility&quot;</li> <li>Write in</li> </ul></li> </ul><br /> <br /> If anyone is confused about what I am asking, ask me.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-22T18:52:50-07:00","id":1095708,"title":"","content":"Hoo boy. Well, we don't want to be an unfair master, but we're also about to go to war. So I figure that means we should balance boosting the local economy with levying soldiers, and keep a relatively firm hand on the reigns.<br /> <br /> Centralization: <i>Mix</i><br /> Aristocracy vs. Plutocracy: <i>Mix</i><br /> Commoners: <i>Even Mix</i><br /> Mercantilism vs. Free Trade: <i>Mercantilism.</i><br /> Attracting New Settlers: <u><i><b>3. Army Service Reward</b></i></u><br /> Army Composition: <i>Combine 2 and 4 </i>- Kasai will provide weapons to people who don't have any, but every household has to maintain their own arms.<br /> Army Training: <i>Regular Muster. </i>Bonus points if you hold worship ceremonies before or after drilling, to make everyone's religious obligations more convenient and reinforce a sense of community.<br /> <br /> Clan Name: <i>Matsuoka.</i> Something simple, humble, and straightforward would be best, I think.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>On the frontier of the Wild South, there's only one woman with the grit to take on its most dangerous outlaws and bring them <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/984977-back-alive-or-maybe-dead-quest\" target=\"_blank\">Back Alive, or Maybe Dead.</a><br /> <br /> Avatar by <a href=\"http://puzzlinghappenstance.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">K.S. Brenowitz</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/908-semicasual","name":"semicasual","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar908_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2499"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-22T19:49:37-07:00","id":1095725,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>semicasual</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1095708#post1095708\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Hoo boy. Well, we don't want to be an unfair master, but we're also about to go to war. So I figure that means we should balance boosting the local economy with levying soldiers, and keep a relatively firm hand on the reigns.<br /> <br /> Centralization: <i>Mix</i><br /> Aristocracy vs. Plutocracy: <i>Mix</i><br /> Commoners: <i>Even Mix</i><br /> Mercantilism vs. Free Trade: <i>Mercantilism.</i><br /> Attracting New Settlers: <u><i><b>3. Army Service Reward</b></i></u><br /> Army Composition: <i>Combine 2 and 4 </i>- Kasai will provide weapons to people who don't have any, but every household has to maintain their own arms.<br /> Army Training: <i>Regular Muster. </i>Bonus points if you hold worship ceremonies before or after drilling, to make everyone's religious obligations more convenient and reinforce a sense of community.<br /> <br /> Clan Name: <i>Matsuoka.</i> Something simple, humble, and straightforward would be best, I think.</div> </div> </div> </div>Unless Sanada Kotaro decides to rebel, we aren't going to war until next summer. <br /> <br /> Just clearing that up, if there is any misconceptions.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-22T20:01:48-07:00","id":1095726,"title":"","content":"Centralization: <i>3 </i>(Local landowners should be able to handle most things that happen on their land, anything on a larger scale can be dealt with by a magistrate)<br /> Aristocracy vs. Plutocracy: <i>2</i>: A shift away from agriculture is going to have be be gradual as Jianghu grows in wealth and power; as things currently stand I'd lean towards ensuring food production &amp; storage, especially when we get called up to go on campaign.<br /> Commoners: <i>Mostly Peasants</i> (Needing to triple the amount of troops we can muster means needing more peasants to levy)<br /> Mercantilism vs. Free Trade: <i>Mercantilism.</i><br /> Attracting New Settlers: <i>3 and/or 4</i>: Will help bolster the army size, as well as settle the periphery of the holding<br /> Army Composition: <i>2 and 3:</i> New settlers coming out of the regulars will already have arms, but at a bare minimum every eligible household should follow the militia standard<br /> Army Training: <i>2: </i><i>Regular Muster. </i>Agreed with <b>semicasual</b>, if it worked for the English, it can work for us.<br /> Clan Name: <i>Qianke </i>takes it for me; it ties in with our banner, the symbolism and implications wrap it up into a neat little package.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/649-nightwinder","name":"Nightwinder","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"154"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-22T20:02:50-07:00","id":1095727,"title":"","content":"Centralization: Little decentralized (local landed retainers have authorization to hear and decide on court cases). <i> Kasai, as Lord Paramount, should be taking over the major decisions.</i><br /> Aristocracy vs Plutocracy: Slightly Aristocratic. <i>Small change (for the moment) towards more trade and manufacture</i>.<br /> Commoners: Even mix. <i>Again, small tilt towards trade ergo taxes</i>.<br /> Mercantilism vs Free-Trade : Mercantilism. <i>Build up the fief!</i><br /> Attracting new settlers: Offer tax/food-rent breaks to new settlers who move. <i>Doesn't cost anything out of pocket. If it doesn't draw enough settlers, something else can be tried.</i><br /> Army composition: Assize of Arms edict <i>Hopefully, as the fief prospers, the better this will work out.</i><br /> Army training: Regular Muster <i>At least until Kasai has confidence in the troops.</i><br /> Either Qianke or Matsuoka is fine.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Like my Avatar? Courtesy of<span style=\"color:#0000FF\"> Jen</span>! : <span style=\"font-size:12px\">Anybody want their characters to be experimented on ? </span>post 98<br /> <i>An Exalt is never unarmed.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4018-l-het-esh","name":"L'het'esh","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4018_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"521"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-23T15:42:13-07:00","id":1096062,"title":"","content":"If Kasai is going to offer weapons to prospective settlers in return for military service, or due to a Militia edict, you folks have to think about what kinds of weapons he is going to buy. I mean, he is basically the 5th or so richest man in Xia at this point, but arms and armor are expensive, and just because he is &quot;rated&quot; at a certain amount of income doesn't mean he is swimming in actual cash.<br /> <br /> If he does buy up a stock of weapons, either to give out or just to stockpile in case of emergency (many castles and manors have an armory, both for use and to show off wealth), he would almost certainly order them from the Honorable Guild of Weaponcrafters, aka the place he traded with back in the beginning of the Quest. He knows them, they know him, and would probably give him both reasonable rates and a loan if he needed it.<br /> <br /> ** Weapons<br /> <br /> -Spears:<br /> <br /> Spears are almost-certainly the most common weapon in use across the Hundred Kingdoms, for many reasons. They are cheap (requiring little steel), easy/fast to make (if stock is available, even an apprentice can hammer out a spearhead in an hour or two), easy to use (if you can stand up straight, you can use a spear), and omni-applicable, functioning well against cavalry and infantry, mounted and on foot.<br /> <br /> On the battlefield, spear-regiments are primarily used as &quot;defensive&quot; units, presenting spears and shields to the enemy, holding them in place while other troops flank. They have staying power, but not very much offense (that is relative. A spear-regiment can and will 'kill' other units, just not as fast as halberdiers, archers or cavalry.) [think of Tanks in an MMO]<br /> <br /> Cost: ~2 dinar for a single spearhead retail, ~150 dinar for a lot of 100 spearheads wholesale<br /> <br /> -Halberds<br /> <br /> Halberds are probably the most 'effective&quot; weapons of all time; the reach of a spear, the impact of an axe, the cutting capability of a sword. Such effective weapons are surprisingly simple, often times being little more than a spear with an axe or sickle-blade bolted on,and are effective against many different targets; cavalry, infantry, heavy armor, etc . They are popular with guardsmen, town-miltias, peasant Men-at-Arms, and even some Oathsworn.<br /> <br /> On the battlefield, halberd-regiments are primarily used as &quot;offensive&quot; units, due to the fact that halberd require two hands to use, preventing the use of a shield and therefore, leaving the halberdier and their comrades comparatively weak to missile fire and the crush of melee. (This is often mitigated by the halberdiers, who tend to be comparatively wealthier than their peers, owning and using heavier armor). While spear-regiments hold the enemy in place, halberd-regiments charge the flank, chewing through enemy units with aplomb. Halberdiers are also often <i>big guys</i>, strong and hearty, who can hold a screamed battle-cry while charging full-tilt across a field while waving a sharp stick. [Think DPS]<br /> <br /> Cost: ~5 dinar for a single halberd-head retail, ~400 dinar for a lot of 100 halberd-heads wholesale<br /> <br /> -Longbows<br /> <br /> After spears, the longbow is probably the second most common weapon on the Hundred Kingdoms battlefield. Nothing, asides from an Oathsworn cavalry charge, is more terrifying than seeing 100+ arrows blot out the sun, and said arrows can decimate an enemy unit, killing men <i>en masse</i> with a sigh like the winter wind.<br /> <br /> Most non-Oathsworn longbows are comparatively-simple laminates of wood and bamboo, between 5 and 6 feet in length. Most archers draw to the ear, loosing arrows around 3 feet in length, which are comparatively heavy and when fired in a plunging arc (<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plunging_fire\" target=\"_blank\">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plunging_fire</a>, essentially adding gravity to the impact) are capable of punching through light armor and flesh with ease.<br /> <br /> The main 'problem' with longbow regiments and longbowmen in general is that there always isn't enough of them. While hunting is pretty common, a common saying said by many Lords is &quot;if you want a good archer, start with his grandfather&quot;. Archery, even disregarding accuracy and instead using massed-fire, is <b>hard</b>, and building up the necessary muscle and proper techniques takes lots of time and lots of practice. Generally, unless you recruit people already skilled with the bow, you aren't going to have effective archers...... leading many Lords to enact laws mandating weekly training in archery for peasants of specific wealth-ratings. Most peasants just buy spears instead.<br /> <br /> On the battlefield, longbow-regiments are used as skirmishers, plunging mass-fire (more artillery than accuracy), fire over intervening terrain (can loose arrows over a river or swamp, or over friends heads), or as flankers.<br /> <br /> Cost: ~70 dinars for a finished longbow from a bowyer (bows are individual pieces, almost works of art, and can't be 'mass-produced'), but most peasants make them themselves, ~1 dinar for a sheaf of 20 broadheaded war-arrows (which are thicker and heavier than hunting arrows).<br /> <br /> -Crossbow<br /> <br /> Crossbows are a comparatively-rare sight on the battlefield, despite their proven effectiveness. This is mainly due to their long reload times (in the time it takes to load and loose a single crossbow bolt, a skilled longbowman can loose 6 or more arrows, meaning less pressure is put on the enemy), and due to their expense (most peasants can't afford crossbows, as they require a lot of metal and skilled carpentry). Therefore, crossbows are more commonly seen in the hands of town-miltias (due to ease of use. If you can kneel and stand, you can use a crossbow), serf-levies and castle-guardsmen.<br /> <br /> The crossbow really shines in siege warfare. Any Lord with even a modicum of intelligence stockpiles crossbows to arm refugees, and even an untrained crossbow-user can aim at a target and pull the trigger. A mass of crossbow-levies can put out a terrifying rain of steel, even if most miss or are ineffective. In addition, a crossbow can be used inside a castle or other building, even one with low ceilings and tight walls, where longbows cannot. At such close ranges, a crossbow with a steel prod will make obsolete even the thickest of Oathsworn armor, meaning a few armsmen covering a doorway can hold off an army, so long as the bolts don't run out.<br /> <br /> On the battlefield, when they do get used, crossbow-regiments are primarily used for direct-fire missile attacks, usually within 100 or so meters, as crossbows are hard to use for plunging fire. This means they are often used as close-range skirmishers and as flanking troops.<br /> <br /> Cost: ~50 dinar for a steel-prod crossbow, ~ 1 dinar for a box of 20 bolts<br /> <br /> -Other weapons<br /> <br /> Other weapons, like backswords, daggers, maces, etc, are usually individually picked for use as backup sidearms.<br /> <br /> **Armor<br /> <br /> - Body armor<br /> <br /> Most peasants make due with garments called &quot;arming jackets&quot;, thickly-quilted jackets made of multiple layers of canvas and wool-padding, essentially a thicker standalone version of the underpadding worn under Oathsworn mail/lamellar. Long-sleeved, with high collars, overlapping lapels (to give a double thickness over the front of the chest), and extending to the knee, arming jackets are hot (especially in the Eastern summer), heavy, hard to dry and keep clean ..... but they are incredibly protective, capable of bouncing spent arrows and turning aside blade-cuts. Only stabs from close range are really capable of punching through the layers of cloth, but even those tend to be blunted unless they strike perpendicular to the weave.<br /> <br /> In most cases, an arming jacket and a helmet might be all a peasant Man-at-Arms has to protect themselves with ........ but they are often &quot;good enough&quot;.<br /> <br /> Cost: ~5 dinar for an arming jacket of canvas and wool.<br /> <br /> For those Men-at-Arms with more disposable income, there is the Brigandine. A Brigandine is basically an arming jacket with metal plates and mail sewn onto the front and back. The metal plates are sewn directly to the cloth backing, and linked with mail rings. The whole ensemble is much more protective than a &quot;regular&quot; arming jacket, for only a slight increase in weight. (<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Karuta_(armour\" target=\"_blank\">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Karuta_(armour</a>) ) Or, what these folks are wearing (<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ashigaru#/media/File:The_Ieyasu_Parade_by_sachakun_3.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ashiga...sachakun_3.jpg</a>)<br /> <br /> Cost: ~25 dinar<br /> <br /> -Head armor:<br /> <br /> Most Men-at-Arms (and even poorer Oathsworn) use helmets styled along the lines of the familiar conical hats worn every day, albeit militarized. A straw hat can be coated in several layers of lacquer, making it relatively bladeproof while retaining its light weight. Or, for those with more money, a facsimile of the conical hat can be made from lacquered leather or even metal, these of course being more protective.<br /> <br /> These styles of helmet are popular because they are relatively cheap (especially compared to a full-helm), while being easy to maintain and comfortable. Many Men-at-Arms drape canvas or other cloth around the inside rim of the helmet to help keep off wind and weather, or paint regiment insignia on the front for ease-of-recognition. (<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kasa_(hat)#/media/File:Return_of_the_Samurai_14.JPG\" target=\"_blank\">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kasa_(...Samurai_14.JPG</a>), the top one especially<br /> <br /> Cost: ~2 for a lacquered straw helmet, ~7 for a lacquered leather or iron helmet<br /> <br /> -Personal armor:<br /> <br /> If able to, (aka spearmen or light-cavalry), Men-at-Arms will usually bear shields very similar to their Oathsworn overlords. Made of bamboo, wooden slats and layers of canvas, the shield is probably the easiest and most effective form of protection found on the battlefield, capable of deflecting arrows and blocking blades. In some cases, shields may be painted or decorated with regiment markings, ferocious animals or insults to the enemy.<br /> <br /> Cost: ~ 5 - 10 dinar, depending on materials.<br /> <br /> <br /> *** Unit TO&amp;E<br /> <br /> While, when using game mechanics, peasant Men-at-Arms will all be Resources 2, in-universe there often exists a gradation between peasants that dictates which weapons, armor and equipment they will have.<br /> <br /> -Bottom: spears, arming jackets, shields, lacquered straw helmets. This is the cheapest equipment, so spear-regiments are most common<br /> -Middle: halberds or longbows, arming jackets (common) or brigandines (rarer), lacquered metal or leather helmets. Halberdiers will often be veterans of prior campaigns, with more productive lands, and longbowmen will hail from the hinterlands and frontiers of a lords domain.<br /> -Top: spears, shields, backswords, longbows, brigandines, lacquered metal helmets, light horses. The &quot;peasant aristocracy&quot;, made up of village headmen, accomplished yeomen farmers, or the elder sons of a commoner clan, these warriors are often used as light cavalry (to chase off missile troops, run down routing/wavering units, or as scouts and skirmishers), or as mounted dragoon-style infantry and longbowmen.<br /> <br /> Most domains will naturally tend to have a mix of weapons, trending towards spears predominating, due to peasants purchasing whatever they can afford and prefer.<br /> <br /> I guess what I am trying to get at is: what sort of weapons do we want Kasai to purchase, if we end up doing that? Combined arms is arguably best, as it allows tactical flexibility....<br /> <br /> Any questions, don't be afraid to ask.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11892,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","user":"Boston123","date":". 05-23-2017, 09:28 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-23T19:09:49-07:00","id":1096133,"title":"","content":"Centralization: 4, its better with trained magisters but dipping into mixed is ok occasionally, maybe only certain crimes require magister intervention.<br /> Aristocracy: 2, making change towards more trade without major upset, might free some citizens to become soldiers too<br /> Commoners: 3, nice mix<br /> Mercantilism over free trade I think<br /> Settlers: 3, army reward. This is just a good idea with so much land available but will need to change in the future.<br /> Army comp: I agree with the 2 and 4 suggestion mentioned earlier<br /> Training: regular muster, might make an incentive for advanced techniques such as military style festival &quot;games&quot; getting people training through non traditional methods and making a cool cultural contribution.<br /> Ikashi cause I like water","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11567-jairain","name":"Jairain","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11567_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"345"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-24T06:07:55-07:00","id":1096261,"title":"","content":"Been awhile, need to make this quick...<br /> <br /> Centralization: <i>Mix</i><br /> Aristocracy vs. Plutocracy: <i>2;</i> give the freemen/merchants a bit more room.<br /> Commoners: <i>Even Mix</i><br /> Mercantilism vs. Free Trade: <i>Free Trade.</i> We've got new Hill Tribe neighbours we want to convince not to rob us. Offering trade was hinted at as part of the Treaty.<br /> Attracting New Settlers: <u><i><b>3. Army Service Reward</b></i></u><br /> Army Composition: <i>2</i>- Halberds, Spears and longbows, with head armour and body armour for new settlers. Halberds only for the first 200, Join Now!<br /> Army Training: <i>Regular Muster. </i>Bonus points if you hold worship ceremonies before or after drilling, to make everyone's religious obligations more convenient and reinforce a sense of community.<br /> <br /> Clan Name: <i>Yamaue</i> From the Hills, we came. Always remember.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Thoughts ripple out, birthing others","edited":{"userid":1624,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1624-falleneco","user":"FallenEco","date":". 05-24-2017, 07:12 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1624-falleneco","name":"FallenEco","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1624_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1927"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-24T06:11:46-07:00","id":1096263,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Boston123</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1096062#post1096062\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I guess what I am trying to get at is: what sort of weapons do we want Kasai to purchase, if we end up doing that? Combined arms is arguably best, as it allows tactical flexibility....</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, it depends.<br /> 1. What's our budget?<br /> 2. How many people are we equipping?<br /> 3. What kind of war do we expect to wage? (i.e. are we more of a raid-and-skirmish army, a capture-the-point-and-hold-it-until-the-main-force-arrives army, a build-defenses-and-occupy-territory army, a besieging army, or something else?)<br /> <br /> I don't expect answers to all of these questions now, since they all depend on how our economy grows. But if I had make plans on the spot, without any additional information...<br /> <br /> ...I'd say focus on spearmen and halberdiers. They're inexpensive and can be deployed in a variety of contexts. Everything else would either take too much time to train* or costs too much.<br /> <br /> *<div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\">Modern army basic takes 10 weeks, but volunteers in boot camp can train full-time. Peasant levies can only train one day a week, so stretch that out to 70 weeks... yeah, we're not going to get to advanced infantry training for the raw recruits. That means no bowmen, no cavalry (can we even afford horses?), and no engineers.<br /> <br /> Actually, crossbows would be ideal for these kinds of soldiers, but for what it takes to buy one of those we could get 10 halberds or 25 spears. And that's <i>before</i> ammunition. Better to stick with infantry, unless our recruit pool is so small that we can afford to buy expensive gear for everyone.</div></div> </div> </div>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>On the frontier of the Wild South, there's only one woman with the grit to take on its most dangerous outlaws and bring them <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/984977-back-alive-or-maybe-dead-quest\" target=\"_blank\">Back Alive, or Maybe Dead.</a><br /> <br /> Avatar by <a href=\"http://puzzlinghappenstance.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">K.S. Brenowitz</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/908-semicasual","name":"semicasual","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar908_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2499"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-24T14:47:14-07:00","id":1096491,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>semicasual</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1096263#post1096263\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Well, it depends.<br /> 1. What's our budget?<br /> 2. How many people are we equipping?<br /> 3. What kind of war do we expect to wage? (i.e. are we more of a raid-and-skirmish army, a capture-the-point-and-hold-it-until-the-main-force-arrives army, a build-defenses-and-occupy-territory army, a besieging army, or something else?)<br /> <br /> I don't expect answers to all of these questions now, since they all depend on how our economy grows. But if I had make plans on the spot, without any additional information...<br /> <br /> ...I'd say focus on spearmen and halberdiers. They're inexpensive and can be deployed in a variety of contexts. Everything else would either take too much time to train* or costs too much.<br /> <br /> *<div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\">Modern army basic takes 10 weeks, but volunteers in boot camp can train full-time. Peasant levies can only train one day a week, so stretch that out to 70 weeks... yeah, we're not going to get to advanced infantry training for the raw recruits. That means no bowmen, no cavalry (can we even afford horses?), and no engineers.<br /> <br /> Actually, crossbows would be ideal for these kinds of soldiers, but for what it takes to buy one of those we could get 10 halberds or 25 spears. And that's <i>before</i> ammunition. Better to stick with infantry, unless our recruit pool is so small that we can afford to buy expensive gear for everyone.</div></div> </div> </div></div> </div> </div> </div><br /> 1) Jianghu is both relatively poor and underdeveloped, the first resulting largely from the second. While it has Resources 4 (right now, and can/will change depending on domestic policies, when the land actually gets put to plow, and when trade with the Hill-Tribes opens up) with regards to agricultural output, it is on the low end of Resources 4. In 'my&quot; version of Resources, the system is exponential, meaning a rating can cover a wide range of values.<br /> <br /> It generally goes:<br /> <br /> Resources 1: the farm held by a serf family, generally up to 40 acres, not counting that paid for land-rent to the overlord<br /> Resources 2: the farm held by a peasant family, generally up to 40 acres. Taxes generally amount to less than land-rent, so peasants tend to have more disposable income.<br /> Resources 3: the value of the land held by a landed Oathsworn, who usually oversee between 1 and 5 villages (~100 - 500 people).<br /> Resources 4: the value of the land held by a vassal; several thousand people<br /> Resources 5: the value of the land owned by the ruler of a kingdom.<br /> <br /> After land-rent and taxes (both of which go to Kasai, as the Marcher Lord/Lord Paramount [so many titles!]), serfs will generally make ~1 dinar a year, peasants will make ~2, Oathsworn will make between 5 and 25, etc etc etc.<br /> <br /> Of course, Resources (agriculture) only really measures the value of <u>food production</u>. Many farmers, both peasants and serfs, have/try to have additional sources of income: taking up minor crafts (basketry, sewing, pottery, etc), grow and sell cash &quot;crops&quot; (tobacco, hemp, linen, etc), manufacture trade goods (locate and fell workable lumber, make charcoal, keep sheep and goats for wool, etc), or work for someone else in their spare time (children are good for this, which is one of the many reasons farming families tend to be large).<br /> <br /> With regards to Kasai's Resource (agriculture) rating, that largely comes from the labors of his serfs, who both pay part of their own harvest in food-rent and work on Kasai's <i>demense</i> for a day a week. It is an <i>immense</i> amount of food..... most of which gets sold for cash(really, shipped downriver for credit. Kasai doesn't actually have Resources 4 in actual specie, it is just what his land is valued at), or stockpiled in case of war or hardship. Also something to remember: the money Kasai gets from agriculture only comes from the lands he owns <i>personally</i>. The produce from peasant farms and Oathsworn estates belongs entirely to them, most of which is used to pay taxes (again, usually in kind, not in silver).<br /> <br /> With regards to Kasai's Resources (taxes) rating, that largely comes from the taxes levied on landowners, aka peasants and Oathsworn. The more of those Kasai attracts, the more money Kasai gets in taxes.<br /> <br /> With regards to Kasai's Resources (fees) rating, that comes from things like road-tolls, gate-tolls, bridge-tolls, docking-fees, inheritance-fees, court-cases-fees, etc. Much of this goes towards actually maintaining the infrastructure of Jianghu.<br /> <br /> If you want things in hard numbers, <i>ehhhhhhhhhhhhhhhhhh. </i>I am leery about hard numbers, but here goes.<br /> <br /> Look at it this way: Lord Iysen is the queen of Xia. Xia (that is, the <u>entire</u> kingdom) is valued at <i>around</i> 10 million dinar a year....... the amount of actual money Lord Iysen makes a year, after accounting for upkeep (roads, bridges, the harbor at Teriake Castle-Town, which requires regular dredging) and what isn't actually held by her (Wulin, Iruma, Jianghu and Ikawa, as well as her smaller vassals)? Probably around 5000-10000 dinar a year. Granted, she has a lot of expenses Kasai won't. (like road maintenance. It is easier and cheaper to maintain a dirt road than a stone-paved one, with post-stations and such. Or, harbor-dredging. That is <b><u><i>expensive as fuck</i></u></b>)<br /> <br /> So............... Jianghu is smaller, but it has fewer expenses than Xia, so let us say that it makes around 90,000 - 100,000 a year gross income. Net? Maybe 1000, 2000?<br /> <br /> Of course, that will increase when 1) the region gets tamed, by exterminating bandits (aka encouraging trade, increasing Res(fees)) and putting more acres under the plow. 2) the population increases, both serfs (aka increase Res(agriculture)) and Peasants (aka increase Res(taxes)), 3) natural resources get exploited (Jianghu has deposits of iron, stone and coal, as well as lumber, not exploited due to interference from bandits and the Hill-Tribes), and 4) trade with the Hill-Tribes picks up.<br /> <br /> Jianghu was a goddamn <i>backwater</i>. Lord Iysen gave it to Kasai as a test.<br /> <br /> Also, since Kasai is a landowner with a regular source of income, he could very well just get a loan to cover any costs. Since he, again, has a regular source of income, he could get a decent-sized loan and just pay it off over a few years.<br /> <br /> 2) Right now, the Jianghu Marches can support around 500 Men-at-Arms, aka part time semi-professional soldiers of the Peasant class. That would however, strip the region of most Peasant men (think old whitebeards and beardless boys), not just the able-bodied. Most Lords will only levy between 1/4 and 1/3 the Peasant population, leaving behind the too-old, the too-young, and enough soldiers to leave the region with a token defense force.<br /> <br /> Jianghu has a population of about 3000. With a 7 serf-to-3 peasant split, and subtracting the Oathsworn (about 40), we are left with a little over 2000 (2072, to be exact) serfs and about 900 (888) peasants across the region. 1/4 to 1/3 of 900 is 225 - 300 soldiers.<br /> <br /> However, Lord Iysen wants Kasai to be able to levy at least 1000 Men-at-Arms from Jianghu before next summer, and preferably more, meaning Kasai is going to have to double/triple his levyable population. Both Lord Wulin and Lord Iruma have around 3000-5000 soldiers, and Kasai should want to be able to match that, but that is for the future.<br /> <br /> Note: If Jianghu is <u>invaded</u>, the Hill-Tribes will almost-certainly help fight off the invaders. They understand that Kasai is their effective &quot;meal ticket&quot;, and that if he gets removed from power, any treaty them have with him is null and void. That would give Kasai at least a thousand or two warriors.<br /> <br /> 3) Uh...... pretty much all three of those 'styles&quot; of war is probable.<br /> <br /> The &quot;most famous&quot; (aka the battles stories and songs are sung about) 'type&quot; of war in the Hundred Kingdoms are field battles, where rectilinear blocks of men march at each other over terrain, spears flashing and arrows flying........ except that &quot;style&quot; is avoided wherever possible, as it is exorbitantly expensive both in soldiers lives (which are extremely hard to replace) and in material.<br /> <br /> Most battles start out as skirmishes, where light troops (longbowmen or light cavalry) stumble across each other and fight, while the main armies maneuver and jockey for position. These skirmishes are <b><u><i>FAR</i></u></b> more common than open battles, which only really happen when an army gets ambushed, forced into terrain where it can't escape (or won't leave undefended, like a bridge or river-ford), or some other unavoidable situation.<br /> <br /> Most wars usually degenerate into sieges, where armies camp outside and inside a castle, waiting for either the food to run out or for reinforcements to come, or into a series of constant skirmishes and raids on border-villages, where light troops attack villages and caravans belonging to the enemy side, until one side can't take any more and capitulates.<br /> <br /> Think about it like the E3 combat system. Skirmishes are like Withering attacks, pitched-battle is like Clash attacks, and Sieges are like Hold-At-Bay. You want to make as many withering attacks as you can, until the enemy can't really fight back (Initiative Crash). You want to avoid Clash attacks, unless you are sure of overwhelming the enemy, and Holding them At Bay is safe.<br /> <br /> Finally, if Kasai decides to try and attract the younger sons and daughters of peasants to Jianghu, to settle and to serve, some if not many of them will likely come with weapons. The river lowland valleys are hideously overcrowded, and most younger sons/daughters lack much opportunity to gain an inheritance worth very much (Try dividing 40 acres between 5+ kids!). These younger people are land-hungry (can't make much of a life, or get married, without income) and <i>bored</i>, for a lack of a better term, and parents anxious to see them succeed [and get them out of their hair] might send them off to Jianghu (&quot;Go north, my son, go north!&quot;) with hand-me-down weapons and equipment. Depending on what Kasai offers, he could be <u>flooded</u> with potential settlers and soldiers.<br /> <br /> Please note that I am assuming you will only be giving weapons and equipment to settlers/soldiers that don't already have them. Bowmen and cavalrymen are only going to be &quot;recruitable&quot; from people already skilled in archery or in horseback riding. Those aren't things that can be trained, at least not in a couple months (for mortals, at least). <br /> <br /> And, even discounting weekly drill-musters, most Peasant Men-at-Arms are going to be <i>decent</i> (aka 2's where they count, Melee for melee troops, Archery for archers. Strength and Dexterity). They are used to fighting off bandits and other assorted nonsense, after all. Their children are going to be familiar with weapons, even if just through practice and not combat (aka 1s in abilities).<br /> <br /> Of course, he has to clear out the bandits first...... which would be an excellent way to train new recruits.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11892,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","user":"Boston123","date":". 05-24-2017, 06:55 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-24T17:17:24-07:00","id":1096532,"title":"","content":"For the arms being bought? Spears, spears as far as the eye can see. As sexy as halberds are, the utility of a shield to the peasant levy is just too good. Halberds can always be mixed into a spear-block to provide a touch more utility. <br /> <br /> If there's any sort of archery extant in Jianghu already, then it should be supported with the bow as the primary ranged weapon, but if nothing else Kasai should lay in a supply of crossbows for his castle.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/649-nightwinder","name":"Nightwinder","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"154"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-24T17:45:35-07:00","id":1096540,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nightwinder</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1096532#post1096532\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">For the arms being bought? Spears, spears as far as the eye can see. As sexy as halberds are, the utility of a shield to the peasant levy is just too good. Halberds can always be mixed into a spear-block to provide a touch more utility.<br /> <br /> If there's any sort of archery extant in Jianghu already, then it should be supported with the bow as the primary ranged weapon, but if nothing else Kasai should lay in a supply of crossbows for his castle.</div> </div> </div> </div>Remember: these are the peasant levies available in Jianghu<br /> <br /> = 3 spear regiments, of 60 men each<br /> = 2 longbow regiments, of 60 men each<br /> =2 halberd regiments, of 50 men each<br /> = 2 light cavalry regiments, of 30 men each<br /> = a compliment of 40-ish Oathsworn, although most of these will serve as regimental officers<br /> <br /> The 120-or-so longbowmen are those who either 1) own enough land to hunt &quot;legally&quot; (Technically, Kasai owns all land not otherwise owned, and people that hunt on it without his permission are poachers, which is largely a hanging offense if caught), 2) live in the hinterlands where they are unlikely to get caught.<br /> <br /> Jianghu is a gigantic mess with regards to the social order. The populace is <u>very</u> self-sufficient; they have to be, Jianghu is isolated, with few supplies coming in from 'outside'. The social order is &quot;squashed&quot;; the classes are still separate, but they socialize and work together far more often than in other domains. Again, they have to. The winters are especially harsh, with several feet of snow being common, and if people don't band together, they die. And that is without bringing up the bandits and the Hill-Tribes.<br /> <br /> In Jianghu, <u>everyone</u> is at least familiar with weaponry (when the bandits or Tribes come a-calling, you want to know how to fight back, especially when your Oathsworn landlord is several hours away), even serfs, who largely aren't supposed to own weapons, and they often hunt as well. They hide the bows and deny it whenever an Oathsworn asks...... but even most, if not all, Oathsworn are in the know, and officially turn their heads whenever they see a serf walking in from the woods with a brace of game. Unofficially, they cheer them on, so long as the serfs regularly give them cuts of game.<br /> <br /> In game terms, Jianghu serfs will have 2s in appropriate Attributes, and 1s in Melee and Archery. They know enough to be able to put food on the table, but don't have the practice of Men-at-Arms.<br /> <br /> Of course, the &quot;whole point&quot; of the serfs/peasants divide is so people can stay at home and keep the economy going while war is going on. Serfs, <i>technically</i>, are exempt from military service, as they don't own land. (although in invasion all bets are off). If Kasai wants to tap that manpower-resource, he has to lower/abolish serfdom in Jianghu........ which would affect the economy.<br /> <br /> Peasants are based off a combination of Yeomen (<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yeoman\" target=\"_blank\">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yeoman</a>) and Ashigaru (<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ashigaru\" target=\"_blank\">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ashigaru</a>)<br /> Serfs are, well, based off serfdom (<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Serfdom\" target=\"_blank\">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Serfdom</a>)<br /> <br /> Jianghu has been without a central figure of authority for so long, and so many laws have been ignored, Kasai is going to have -fun- (<a href=\"http://i.imgur.com/mHXiz.png\" target=\"_blank\">http://i.imgur.com/mHXiz.png</a>) trying to get the place to 'behave'.<br /> <br /> Or, he could adapt to Jianghu, instead of trying to make Jianghu adapt to him.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charter_of_the_Forest\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/Coat_of_Arms_of_England_%28-1340%29.svg/960px-Coat_of_Arms_of_England_%28-1340%29.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=index&amp;utm_content=thumbnail\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charter_of_the_Forest\" target=\"_blank\"> Charter of the Forest - Wikipedia </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charter_of_the_Forest\" target=\"_blank\"> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charter_of_the_Forest </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> </div> </div> </div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11892,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","user":"Boston123","date":". 05-24-2017, 09:38 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-24T19:43:15-07:00","id":1096564,"title":"","content":"Then, there is the matter of marrying a suitable girl and siring heirs. Kasai..... probably wants to do this relatively soon, before he gets involved in fights with bandits or enemy rebels. Having a shiny new clan-name won't mean very much when he takes an arrow through the throat.<br /> <br /> .... Speaking of which, he might want to invest in some armor as well. A helmet for sure, he tends to get knocked in the head a lot.<br /> <br /> Soooooo, here is a list of potential marriage candidates. All the ladies on this list are of &quot;equal status&quot; to Kasai, as Lord of the Jianghu Marches (aka a Duke-equivalent).<br /> <br /> +Cousins (really, the daughters of cousins, of lesser branches of the Shiba family) of Lord Isyen. Kasai would arrange betrothals and weddings via Lord Iysen<br /> - Ayaka Shiba, 14<br /> - Miyu Shiba, 16<br /> - Masoko Shiba, 17<br /> <br /> + Daughters of Xian vassals. Kasai would arrange things with their fathers<br /> - Ren Kotaro, 18, Lord Sanada Kotaro's daughter<br /> -Tamiko Imoto, 19, Lord Jubei Imoto's daughter<br /> <br /> +Other girls of ducal-status. Kasai would arrange things with their fathers<br /> -Hakari Daomaru, 15, daughter of Akio Daomaru, Lord of Yan<br /> -Sayuri Ryota, 17, daughter of Takao Ryota, Lord of Chen<br /> - Emi Osamu, 14, adopted daughter of Kaede Osamu, Lord of Dai<br /> <br /> (if it matters to you readers, Kasai is 22? 23? He was 21 at the start of the Quest. It doesn't matter in-universe. The more noble the girl, the younger she tends to marry, often to a man a decade or more her senior)<br /> <br /> +Then, of course, there are other ladies. Oathsworn and the daughers of Oathsworn. Even commoners, although he would take some flak for marrying outside/below his station. Hell, there is a girl, Saya Azuki, that is the daughter of the engineer (the head of the Engineering Guild and more, in Teriake) that is contracted to build the roads in Jianghu to the Hill-Tribes, who is rather wealthy, <i>very</i> intelligent and &quot;well-bred&quot;, to use a patronizing term.<br /> <br /> Kasai can marry whomever he (and therefore you) wants. He doesn't <i>have</i> to marry inside his station, for example. Shit, both Mino and Airi Shiba (Lord Iysens daughters) still aren't married......<br /> <br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"http://imgur.com/7JpSQye\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/google-material/image_placeholder.svg\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://imgur.com/7JpSQye\" target=\"_blank\"> </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://imgur.com/7JpSQye\" target=\"_blank\"> http://imgur.com/7JpSQye </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> Now, Kasai has some ...... hang-ups, if you will, concerning marriage. Nothing serious, he just, well, hasn't ever really thought about it, to be blunt. He is the bastard child of a minor (read, poor) marginalized noble family, so his prospects were never good, even without taking his Hill-Tribe ancestry into account.<br /> <br /> Now, of course, he is pretty much one of the more desirable bachelors in Xia, and probably beyond. Young, relatively good looking (aka Appearance 2, in good shape, tall, that sort of thing), well-liked by the Lord Iysen, newly raised to not!Ducal status, with ample (if poor-for-now) holdings. So long as he wasn't a deliberate asshole, he could pretty much walk into any house with an eligible girl and leave with her hand in betrothal.<br /> <br /> He <i>has</i> been intimate, mainly with girls thankful for his 'services' (-ahem-) to their home villages (much like Saiko, back in the beginning of the Quest, but those girls were consenting, unlike Saiko who was coerced), but he hasn't been in a single place long enough for an actual emotional relationship.<br /> <br /> Since Kasai doesn't have a marriage arranged since his early childhood, he would basically travel around and court a girl he is interested in. If the two like each other (or, if Kasai/her father thinks he could get some political benefit from the marriage), the topic of betrothal would be brought up. If both parties agree, the two are considered engaged, with marriage following generally within the year.<br /> <br /> Kasai would pay a bride-price to the girls family, dependent on her status, and the family would give Kasai a dowry, also dependent on her status. In some cases, this might be a lump sum of cash, but a dowry can also be a plot of land, a head of horses or cattle, a deed to a series of mines or mills, or anything that has long-term value. The dowry is technically for the support of the girl, if something happens to Kasai, but overwhelmingly-most couples share the funds.<br /> <br /> Most noble-born (and the nobility-aping middle classes, for that matter) girls are going to generally be various flavors of <a href=\"http://tvtropes.org/pmwiki/pmwiki.php/Main/YamatoNadeshiko\" target=\"_blank\">Yamato Nadeshiko</a>, at least in public (for example, Airi Shiba is the very definition of a 'proper lady&quot; in public, but in private is a sarcastic jokester). Grace, humility, maturity, etc. Most girls are going to have some form of training with glaives or spears, bows and daggers, all of which are part of the dowry. They also have training in the organizing and running of households, some medical knowledge, various forms of entertainment (singing, dancing, instruments, tea ceremonies, etc) the organizing of finances, and various domestic skills (weaving, especially <i>fine</i> weaving, like lace and silk, cooking [although they tend to have servants for that]), etc.<br /> <br /> Generally, the &quot;lower&quot; the birth of the girl, the more 'practical&quot; skillset she will have, something to keep in mind in a frontier like Jianghu.<br /> <br /> So on and so forth<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11892,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","user":"Boston123","date":". 05-24-2017, 10:46 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-24T19:59:44-07:00","id":1096570,"title":"","content":"Sayuri Ryota is an interesting option; not too young, not one of the 'problem lords' children but her father's castle is close to one. Also close to the Yue, so...<br /> <br /> Saya Azuki, the engineer's daughter also sounds promising.<br /> <br /> Would really like more info on the candidates. Choosing a wife is important and that is one of the reasons why the aristocracy choose them so early. We don't have the luxury of time when it comes to making this choice, but there has got to be more information than just their names (and families) and ages.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Thoughts ripple out, birthing others","edited":{"userid":1624,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1624-falleneco","user":"FallenEco","date":". 05-24-2017, 11:02 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1624-falleneco","name":"FallenEco","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1624_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1927"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-24T20:13:50-07:00","id":1096575,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>FallenEco</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1096570#post1096570\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Sayuri Ryota is an interesting option; not too young, not one of the 'problem lords' children but her father's castle is close to one. Also close to the Yue, so...<br /> <br /> Saya Azuki, the engineer's daughter also sounds promising.<br /> <br /> Would really like more info on the candidates. Choosing a wife is important and that is one of the reasons why the aristocracy choose them so early. We don't have the luxury of time when it comes to making this choice, but there has got to be more information than just their names (and families) and ages.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> You don't have to <i>choose a spouse<u> right</u></i>now. If you want, you can potentially court all of them, and more.<br /> <br /> Kasai just has to be married before next summer. That is a little over a year away. Granted, he has a <i>bunch</i> of stuff to do in the meantime, but ....<br /> <br /> You'll meet Saya Azuki soon, as she is actually in charge of drafting up the plans for a few of the bridges to the Hill-Tribes (She is a bit of a <a href=\"http://tvtropes.org/pmwiki/pmwiki.php/Main/RenaissanceMan\" target=\"_blank\">Renaissance (wo)man</a> ). Either Kasai will be asked to go down to Teriake, or she (and her father, who is in charge of the entire project) will come up to Yagura Castle. <br /> <br /> ..... Thank the gods Kasai doesn't have to pay for those roads and bridges.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-24T20:22:58-07:00","id":1096579,"title":"","content":"As far as army-building goes, we can set broad policies now but I don't think it's reasonable to craft a detailed budget before we've even visited the territory. Let's save that for later. <br /> <br /> Now, marriage. I'm with FallenEco - we really should <i>meet </i>some of these people before proposing. And, if I may add one more to the list, how about Airi Shiba? I thought she might have a thing for Kasai.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>On the frontier of the Wild South, there's only one woman with the grit to take on its most dangerous outlaws and bring them <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/984977-back-alive-or-maybe-dead-quest\" target=\"_blank\">Back Alive, or Maybe Dead.</a><br /> <br /> Avatar by <a href=\"http://puzzlinghappenstance.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">K.S. Brenowitz</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/908-semicasual","name":"semicasual","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar908_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2499"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-24T23:06:07-07:00","id":1096624,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>semicasual</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1096579#post1096579\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">As far as army-building goes, we can set broad policies now but I don't think it's reasonable to craft a detailed budget before we've even visited the territory. Let's save that for later.<br /> <br /> Now, marriage. I'm with FallenEco - we really should <i>meet </i>some of these people before proposing. And, if I may add one more to the list, how about Airi Shiba? I thought she might have a thing for Kasai.</div> </div> </div> </div>Agreed. We can only really set outline goals, small plans and long term ideals until we get more information ' We do not have eyes on the ground, just broad strokes from reports at this stage.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Thoughts ripple out, birthing others","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1624-falleneco","name":"FallenEco","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1624_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1927"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-25T14:41:15-07:00","id":1096871,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>semicasual</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1096579#post1096579\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Now, marriage. I'm with FallenEco - we really should <i>meet </i>some of these people before proposing. And, if I may add one more to the list, how about Airi Shiba? I thought she might have a thing for Kasai.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Hoo boy. That one is .... complicated.<br /> <br /> If you want to know the truth, she did/does. (She thought he was gallant and suchlike, while he mostly just appreciated the attention) He did live in her house full-time for pretty much a year (on top of her basically taking care of him when he was unconscious), and the two of them did flirt about as much as was socially acceptable for a not!princess to flirt with their mom's underling.<br /> <br /> Both of them made sure to leave several inches of space between their &quot;toes on the line&quot;, as it were, because Airi knows what is expected of her as a not!princess, and Kasai would much rather prefer his head, hands and genitals remain firmly attached to his body, not mounted on a pike over the city gate. They were friends, however, about as much as Airi had friends.<br /> <br /> Airi and Saiko, on the other hand, got on along very well, to the point where the two considered each other close friends (Airi doesn't really have a stick up her ass about nobility, and was happy to have another girl her age to talk to about girl-problems with). Airi confided in Saiko (mainly about how such a love was never to be, due to the demands of politics and courtly life. How tragic!), and Saiko inadvertently told Kasai (she is bad at keeping secrets). Kasai already had an inkling, because he isn't Jaune Arc-levels of dense, but she doesn't know that he knows. (With this in mind, he found their interactions afterwards hilarious)<br /> <br /> Of course, both parties didn't/couldn't actually act on their feelings, due to politics.<br /> <br /> Airi, as a not!princess, could only really wed someone of equal social status. In the &quot;immediate area&quot; around Xia, that leaves only two possible choices (3 really, but Haichi Matsu was out of the running for several obvious reasons); Kei Hiroki, the heir-apparent of Qian, which is south across the Red River, and Nobu Sora, heir-apparent of Ji. Kei Hiroki is 9, and still thinks girls are gross, and since Nobu Sora is from Ji, and Ji and Xia don't get along, he would probably turn down the marriage proposal out of hand, even if he wasn't 44 and gay.<br /> <br /> So, a marriage of equal status is largely out. That brings us down to the next step, those of not!ducal status, which is what Kasai is now..... of course, the matter of bride-price comes into play. And, to be blunt, Kasai almost-certainly couldn't afford it.<br /> <br /> Airi Shiba's bride-price is 8,000 dinar, and her dowry (that is, the amount Kasai would get) is 15,000. In physical specie at Yagura Castle (Lord Iysen was remarkably generous and didn't clear the place out when she bequeathed it to him, mainly because she didn't want to gimp his recruitment of soldiers), Kasai has about 3,000-4,000 dinar, the accumulated net profit of several years taxes and fees. At his current rate of net profit gain, it would take him about 4 years to earn enough.<br /> <br /> Of course, that is if Lord Iysen even allows Airi to marry, after what happened to her sister. Lord Iysen was turned various shades of paranoid as a result of that shit-show. (it is one of the reasons Airi and Mino haven't married yet)<br /> <br /> I mean, don't get me wrong, Lord Iysen wants her children to be happy ..... but she also knows she has to do what is best for Xia. That whole aspect of her life has caused her great pain and suffering, which is darkly-amusing because she swore she would never put her children through what she went through. Lord Iysen, before she was the ruler of Xia or even the heir-apparent, basically ran away from her arranged marriage and played at being an Oathsworn hero (which is one of the reasons she likes Kasai so much). When her older brothers died, she sucked it up and came home to 'do her duty', thanklessly ruling her kingdom in a loveless marriage.<br /> <br /> So, she would <i>prefer</i> if her children got to at least enjoy life a little, but she also knows the kingdom would be better served if they were used as pawns in cold, hard realpolitik. If a way was found for her to fulfill both qualifiers, she would do it in a heartbeat. And if Kasai somehow came up with enough money, or cut another deal with her ..... who knows?<br /> <br /> And stuff.<br /> <br /> Of course, Kasai would get some pretty impressive benefits from being wed to Airi, on top of the obvious benefits of marriage to someone you like and find physically attractive (-ahem-). Since Airi would be marrying down, the marriage would be <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Morganatic_marriage\" target=\"_blank\">morganatic</a>: that is, no children born of the marriage would be in the line of succession for the throne of Xia, but Kasai would still become the son-in-law of the most powerful figure in Xia, which in game-terms would give him Allies 1 (Lord Iysen Shiba), Influence 4, and Mentor 2.<br /> <br /> On the other hand, marrying into such a powerful family would put an even greater target on Kasai's back. If he is already 'under the gun' now, with regards to plots, politics and other assorted nastiness (and he is, as a recently-promoted Lord), it would get turned up to 11 by being affiliated with Lord Iysen. People seeking to undermine Lord Iysens efforts might try to sway him, they might try to hurt or kill him to hurt her, so on and so forth.<br /> <br /> Welcome to the Sengoku Jidai, motherfucker.<br /> <br /> @ Nightwinder<br /> <br /> There is already an armory of weapons at Yagura Castle. There is a force of 10 armsmen stationed there, 6 infantry and 4 crossbowmen led by a Captain (whose position Kasai gets to fill), as well as a small armory in case of attack. The armory contains 50 spears, another 10 crossbows, boxes of bolts, a few dozen helmets, and several shields, as well as plenty of rocks to drop from the walls. Most of the stuff is in various states of disrepair, although the spears are in decent shape.<br /> <br /> @semicasual<br /> <br /> The reason crossbows are so expensive is because they require a lot of metal in their manufacture, especially spring steel, which is expensive even when talking about metal. In a &quot;war crossbow&quot; or arbalest, aka the crossbow as depicted in the Corebook, the prod (the bow part) of the crossbow is made of spring-steel, which is steel that, well, &quot;springs&quot; back into shape after being bent. The steel prod gives the crossbow it's power, and an arbalest can have a draw-weight of 600+ pounds.<br /> <br /> Upon doing some further research, I came across the existence of other crossbows, with prods of different materials, aka the same materials that are used to make &quot;regular&quot; bows, like wood, horn, sinew and suchlike. These crossbows are much wider (basically a 3-4 foot bow mounted on a stock, as opposed to the foot or so on a steel prod crossbow) and weaker than a steel-prod crossbow, but they are also likely to be much less expensive.<br /> <br /> In game terms, I would consider them Light Archery weapons, otherwise with crossbow stats, but they would cost 20-30 dinar instead of the 50 of an arbalest. Would those interest you?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11892,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","user":"Boston123","date":". 05-25-2017, 07:30 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-25T17:57:39-07:00","id":1096922,"title":"","content":"I would go a daughter of other of vassals, knitting Xia together a little better and having any possible assistance closer to hand:<br /> - Ren Kotaro, 18, Lord Sanada Kotaro's daughter<br /> -Tamiko Imoto, 19, Lord Jubei Imoto's daughter","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Like my Avatar? Courtesy of<span style=\"color:#0000FF\"> Jen</span>! : <span style=\"font-size:12px\">Anybody want their characters to be experimented on ? </span>post 98<br /> <i>An Exalt is never unarmed.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4018-l-het-esh","name":"L'het'esh","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4018_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"521"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-25T18:12:11-07:00","id":1096930,"title":"","content":"Hm.<br /> <br /> We have a year until we have to be married, eh?<br /> <br /> I propose that we spend the next year ROCKING IT and end up with enough cash, troops, and influence to be a solid choice for either Airi Shiba...or her mom, because frankly I think Lord Iysen is absolutely the sort of personality that Kasai needs in a partner. We are a noble and honorable knight, and if we're not being heroic enough to prove ourselves a worthy match for royalty...well, we need to up our game!<br /> <br /> ...also, apropos of nothing, if we run into anybody with blue eyes we ought to be super nice to them.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>So I'm making <a href=\"http://godkickingboot.smackjeeves.com/\" target=\"_blank\">God-Kicking Boot</a>, an Exalted webcomic, now. Updates on Sundays. Full-color, mediocre but slowly improving art. It's a thing.<br /> <br /> The absence of a monument can, in its own way, be something of a monument also.</i> -Roger Zelazny","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/512-wise-old-guru","name":"Wise Old Guru","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar512_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1097"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-25T18:16:25-07:00","id":1096934,"title":"","content":"I like guru's thinking. But do we have a whole year?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11567-jairain","name":"Jairain","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11567_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"345"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-25T18:19:59-07:00","id":1096938,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Jairain</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1096934#post1096934\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I like guru's thinking. But do we have a whole year?</div> </div> </div> </div>It is the first few weeks of Ascending Fire, aka summer (In my games, I use a 12 month year for philosophical reasons), and Lord Iysen wants Kasai to 1) have 1000 troops, and 2) be married by the start of next summer.<br /> <br /> So yeah, a year.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-25T18:53:24-07:00","id":1096946,"title":"","content":"I thought Lord Shiba is already married? Although, her husband doesn't seem to be around.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>On the frontier of the Wild South, there's only one woman with the grit to take on its most dangerous outlaws and bring them <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/984977-back-alive-or-maybe-dead-quest\" target=\"_blank\">Back Alive, or Maybe Dead.</a><br /> <br /> Avatar by <a href=\"http://puzzlinghappenstance.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">K.S. Brenowitz</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/908-semicasual","name":"semicasual","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar908_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2499"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-25T19:11:04-07:00","id":1096951,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>semicasual</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1096946#post1096946\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I thought Lord Shiba is already married? Although, her husband doesn't seem to be around.</div> </div> </div> </div>He's dead, and has been for about 10 years.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-26T05:00:08-07:00","id":1097078,"title":"","content":"We are not marrying our Boss. It defeats the purpose of making us a Marcher-Lord.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Thoughts ripple out, birthing others","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1624-falleneco","name":"FallenEco","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1624_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1927"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-26T06:55:56-07:00","id":1097108,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>FallenEco</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1097078#post1097078\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">We are not marrying our Boss. It defeats the purpose of making us a Marcher-Lord.</div> 