outlaws and bring them <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/984977-back-alive-or-maybe-dead-quest\" target=\"_blank\">Back Alive, or Maybe Dead.</a><br /> <br /> Avatar by <a href=\"http://puzzlinghappenstance.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">K.S. Brenowitz</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/908-semicasual","name":"semicasual","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar908_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2499"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-26T08:34:17-07:00","id":1097134,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>semicasual</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1097113#post1097113\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">...I don't want to consider the legal and social complications would come from that. You just made a powerful argument against pursuing Airi, Masako, Miyu, or Ayaka.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Keep in mind that getting involved in political shithattery comes with the position of Lord of Jianghu, and really with any form of authority in the Hundred Kingdoms. And, while marrying into high families would turn that up to 11, as it were, the members of those families are also the most prepared for said shithattery, Airi Shiba arguably most of all.<br /> <br /> She has been training to rule Xia since she was about 5, after all. Even if she isn't a master manipulator (and she isn't , but she is pretty good), she <i>is</i> 100% aware of inter-and-intra family alliances, who can be pushed into doing what, who can be relied on to act in certain ways, so on and so forth.<br /> <br /> Basically, she (and most other ladies of noble birth) has all the skill in Socialize, Lore, Bureaucracy and other stuff that Kasai doesn't.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11892,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","user":"Boston123","date":". 05-26-2017, 11:38 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-26T17:47:27-07:00","id":1097371,"title":"","content":"The part about having to raise 1000 troops makes me want to change my settlement pitch from reduced taxes to 5 years service for 40 acres and a draft animal.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Like my Avatar? Courtesy of<span style=\"color:#0000FF\"> Jen</span>! : <span style=\"font-size:12px\">Anybody want their characters to be experimented on ? </span>post 98<br /> <i>An Exalt is never unarmed.</i>","edited":{"userid":4018,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4018-l-het-esh","user":"L'het'esh","date":".  spelingk, as uzual 05-29-2017, 03:25 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4018-l-het-esh","name":"L'het'esh","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4018_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"521"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-26T18:16:50-07:00","id":1097380,"title":"","content":"The Jianghu Marches: Xia\u2019s frontier<br /> <br /> The Jianghu Marches, formerly known as the Jianghu Gap, is a region of Xia scarcely talked about in the esteemed halls in Teriake. Distant, underdeveloped, poor and harsh, the region has been largely \u201cunder the radar\u201d for most Lords for quite some time\u2026. until now. With the recent elevation of Kasai {x} to the Lordship, and the peace agreement with the Hill-Tribes to the north, the fate of the region is of sudden and immediate concern to all involved, most of all the people who actually live there.<br /> <br /> History:<br /> <br /> The Jianghu Gap has been inhabited for almost as long as the Hundred Kingdoms and the Hill-Tribes have been around. Before the Hundred Kingdoms, the proto Hill-Tribes reared sheep across the hills of the region, and when the Vanehani came, they kicked out the Tribes and built terraces where sheep once roamed.<br /> <br /> The region would pass through many hands as the nascent Hundred Kingdoms rose and fell, until about 200 years before the present date. The region was owned by the Shiba clan, a comparatively-important Oathsworn family, who served under the royal family of Zhou. Zhou was a powerful kingdom that ruled over most of the Red River valley, a state that builds grudges like weeds.<br /> <br /> To make a long story short, the Shiba rose up in rebellion against Zhou, and along with others, annihilating the kingdom and, through both leadership in war and clever politicking, established the kingdom of Xia. Once the Shibas took control of the region, they basically abandoned their holdings in Jianghu in favor of Teriake, but never relinquished the region to another. Either because the family wanted a fall-back point if things went south, or it just was never considered, Jianghu has been held by the Shiba family since the founding of Xia, but visited only infrequently.<br /> <br /> Yagura Castle was the clan\u2019s ancestral stronghold, but even still it has never been popular with the family. The current Lord, Iysen Shiba, only visited the castle 3 times in her life (after she was given ownership of it as a wedding gift), and before her the castle was functionally empty for 50 years. None of the younger members of the Shiba clan has ever been to Yagura, or visited the region.<br /> <br /> As a result, the region has been <i>de-facto</i> independent for almost as long as Xia has been a state. There have been castellans responsible for maintaining Yagura Castle, as well as Oathsworn retainers living in the region, but neither made much effort to govern the entire region. Both the Oathsworn and the commoners are largely used to doing what they want\u2026.. what will happen now the region has a Lord who actually will live and govern it?<br /> <br /> Geography:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"http://imgur.com/hLDrK4y\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/google-material/image_placeholder.svg\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://imgur.com/hLDrK4y\" target=\"_blank\"> </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://imgur.com/hLDrK4y\" target=\"_blank\"> http://imgur.com/hLDrK4y </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> The Jianghu Marches are roughly V-shaped, following the banks of the upper reaches of the White River. The river falls over fast-flowing cataracts and rapids, steeply descending from the surrounding hills and bluffs of the mountains, and the White River stops being navigable at the market-village of Liangzhou. From the river\u2019s edge, the land rises quickly into ridges and outcroppings of granite and slate, making conventional agriculture difficult at best, and leading the farmers of Jianghu to make heavy use of terraces and suchlike.<br /> <br /> Abutted up against the mountains as it is, the region is harsh to say the least. While still temperate, Jianghu tends to be windier and colder than the lower river bottoms, and people living in the region make more use of wool and quilted garments than is usual.<br /> <br /> Due to the geography of the region, and the mountains to the north, west and east, Jianghu tends to magnify the weather and climate of the seasons: Spring has truly astronomical amounts of rain, Autumn has fog and mists in abundance, and Winter often sees several feet of snow. On the other hand, summer is comparatively pleasant, with milder temperatures and diminished humidity compared to other regions.<br /> <br /> In Dragontongue, \u201cJianghu\u201d roughly means \u201crivers and lakes\u201d, and \u2026.. well, Jianghu has a lot of rivers and lakes! The two branches of the White river are the major ones, but there are many subtributaries as well, not to mention Yari Lake. Yari Lake is located at the head of one of the branches of the White river, and is where Yagura Castle is located. Deep and cold, Yari Lake is home to many fish, from trout to bass to pike, the last of which can reach sizes as long as a man. In addition, there are many hundreds of smaller lakes and ponds scattered throughout the domains, several of which are sacred to the Hill-Tribes that inhabited this area.<br /> <br /> Vegetation and animal life is representative of the general Hundred Kingdoms, if skewed towards species that prefer higher elevations and colder temperatures. Maple and Ash, Oak and Pine are all common, with pine and other conifers tending to cover the upland ridges and hills. Wild animals include white-tailed-deer, turkey, raccoons and skunks, wolves and bear and boar, as well as many others. Berries like blueberry, cranberry, raspberry and others are <i>extremely</i> common, serving as food for both animals and man.<br /> <br /> In autumn, the forests and fields are resplendent with a multitude of colors, making for quite the beautiful scenery.<br /> <br /> People:<br /> <br /> The people of Jianghu are an odd lot, influenced mainly by their isolation and harsh living conditions.<br /> <br /> Phenotypically, they mainly exhibit traits from the Vanehani, but Hill-Tribe characteristics (darker skin tones, taller statures, red hair, etc) are common as well, and in the northern parts of Jianghu one can sometimes see actual mutations signifying direct descendance from intermarriage. The main language spoken is Riverspeak, but the region has a distinctive accent with the lilting flavors of Forest-tongue, and many people know a few words in that language.<br /> <br /> People have a tendency to be pragmatic and taciturn, hard-working and focused. Self-sufficiency is prized, and people that can come up with solutions to problems without the assistance of others (not that others won\u2019t help, of course) are valued highly.<br /> <br /> In Jianghu, the social order is diminished in importance, relatively speaking. While Oathsworn landlords are still nobility, and therefore treated with respect and deference, the various social classes interact <i>far</i> more often, and work together more closely, than in other domains. This is necessary: if everyone didn\u2019t band together, surviving the winter would be almost impossible. Oathsworn more often serve as direct mediators and judges (even though they don\u2019t have the technical legal authority to do so) than as distant and disconnected authority figures, and it isn\u2019t uncommon to see a landlord directly talking with their tenants. Peasants serve more as jurors and village headmen than as an \u2018insulating\u2019 class between the nobility and the dirty serfs, and in many cases can claim descent from their Oathsworn landlords. Some particularly-wealthy and powerful peasants might even have tenants of their own, making them a quasi \u201cpeasant aristocracy\u201d.<br /> <br /> In addition, the \u201ccommoner clan\u201d is of more importance in Jianghu than what is usual in other domains. They function as support networks in the face of the harsh terrain, and many an Oathsworn landlord has to balance the needs of their fief and the wants (if not demands) of the commoner-clans inhabiting it.<br /> <br /> With the absence of any real authority figures, most of the usual laws and customs of the Hundred Kingdoms are modified or outright ignored. Some of the more prominent examples include the owning of weapons and the preponderance of hunting.<br /> <br /> Due to the high level of bandits plaguing the region, most every household will have at least one person familiar, if not skilled, with arms. This includes serfs, who by-and-large are prohibited from owning weapons in other domains. Common weapons include the spear, the club, and the bow. Club-fights are a common way of sorting out issues with neighbors, and asides from some broken bones and teeth, are usually nonlethal.<br /> <br /> The ownership and usage of weapons is important for another reason: hunting. Almost everyone in Jianghu hunts, for the simple fact that to not make use of an ample food source would be foolish. Again, this often includes serfs, and said hunting often takes place in the privately-owned forests, most of which are owned by the Lord of Jianghu. Hunting in these without the Lords express permission is considered poaching, largely a hanging offence in other domains, and in said domains most nobles viciously retain their right to hunt. In Jianghu, things are different, and almost all Oathsworn landlords turn a blind eye, at the very least. As far as they are concerned, the fact that the common folk aren\u2019t starving to death as often is enough benefit to give up some of their noble rights, and the fact that they are better able to defend themselves against bandits and raiders is a plus on top of that.<br /> <br /> Most people live in the comparatively-less harsh river bottoms, and all settlements of any size are located along the White River. Both settlements and farmsteads are compact, with little wasted space. Houses and outbuildings are solidly made, almost-always with footings of stone and steeply-sloping roofs to shed winter snow, and buildings tend to be built close together, if not touching, to prevent snow and wind from whipping between them. Many of the isolated farmsteads are fortified, with palisades and thorn-walls, to protect them from both bandits and wild animals.<br /> <br /> Economy:<br /> <br /> Like in most of the Hundred Kingdoms, most of the people in Jianghu are agriculturalists.<br /> <br /> Crops grown in Jianghu include potatoes, maize, beans, onions and leeks, pumpkins and other gourds, carrots, cabbage (which is easy to preserve for long periods of time), oats and wheat, etc etc etc. Cotton doesn\u2019t grow well in the thin, rocky soil, so most clothing is made from linen or nettlecloth.<br /> <br /> Fruit trees are especially common, with many houses having at least one tree, and great orchards stretching over the upper terraces. Most of these orchards are technically owned by the Lord of the region, but in the absence of said figure, most of them are cultivated freely by the commoners. Fruit raised in the orchards includes cherries, plums and apples, all of which are eaten both fresh and cooked, as well as turned into cider. A common method of making alcoholic beverages stronger is through freezing, leaving barrels of cider outside over winter and regularly scooping out the ice that forms. Such \u201cice-wines\u201d are usually around 25-40% alcohol, rivalling wine in potency and in the hinterlands is commonly used as a currency.<br /> <br /> Asides from fruit orchards and farm-fields, much of the arable land in Jianghu is never put to plow, instead being used as pasture for animals like sheep, goats and (rarely) cattle. The milk is used to make cheese, including particularly fine cheddar, and the fiber is used for clothing, warm against the wind from the hills.<br /> <br /> Except for fruit, dairy and wool, the region doesn\u2019t really export much besides the occasional timber, which is floated through the rapids to Liangzhou. There are deposits of iron, workable stone and coal, as well as great stands of lumber in the forests, but these aren\u2019t exploited mainly due to the bandits infesting the region.<br /> <br /> Liangzhou is the largest settlement in the region, and the only market town. A comparatively-sleepy hamlet of only 300 people, Liangzhou nevertheless has the only dock-facilities in the region, as well as the largest concentration of craftspeople. Everyone else lives in scattered farmsteads on the estates of Oathsworn overlords, with a few scattered hamlets, more concentrations of farms than actual towns.<br /> <br /> Religion:<br /> <br /> Like overwhelmingly-most of the Hundred Kingdoms, Jianghu is officially Immaculate-fearing. There is a shrine in Liangzhou, and the smaller hamlets and isolated farmsteads are (sporadically) services by roaming priests.<br /> <br /> However, due to the isolation, several other religions have crept into the area. Field-gods, while not worshipped outright, are acknowledged, with small shrines present on most farms, and the animal-totems of the Hill-Tribes are often acknowledged whenever someone enters the woods, especially by hunters. There is a shrine to Luna, left over from the Hill-Tribes, that still sees regular offerings, though if they are made by the Tribes or by the Vanehani is unclear.<br /> <br /> This mish-mash of religions has led to several holidays that would otherwise be proscribed by the Immaculate Faith.<br /> <br /> First of these is the Blossom Festival, made in springtime when the flowers of the fruit trees are in bloom. Garlands and crowns of ephemeral flowers are laid on the graves of the honored dead and around the necks of newly-married couples, in symbolism of the circle of life and death.<br /> <br /> The other holiday is the Feast of the Moon, distantly related to the Hill-Tribe holiday of the same name. Essentially a harvest festival, it takes place in the middle of autumn, after the harvest is done and the leaves begin to change. Communities gather together and welcome the coming winter, acknowledge the dead (usually through the carving of pumpkins and other gourds with faces and such, in order to \u2018scare away the dead\u201d), and pass around treats (fruit candied with maple sugar, pastries made of fruit and chopped nuts, as well as ample supplies of alcohol) to enjoy. Young children run around with masks and such, to frighten away the Fair Folk, or the dead (nobody is sure) and otherwise celebrate another year of toil.<br /> <br /> [fuck yea Halloween!]<br /> <br /> Troublemakers:<br /> <br /> Jianghu has a bandit problem.<br /> <br /> This is mainly due to the region\u2019s isolation and lack of central authority than anything else. The hills and forests make <i>excellent</i> places for the ruffians to hide, and the scattered and often divided Oathsworn can\u2019t muster enough to meaningfully drive the bandits out. Sure, they can defend against a bandit attack on their own holdings, but the scruffians don\u2019t exactly respect fiefdom borders and often melt away when faced with any sort of meaningful resistance.<br /> <br /> Recently, however, there have been reports of several bandit gangs being forcibly consolidated into one, led by a disgraced Oathsworn warrior who calls himself \u201cLord Masanori of Jianghu\u201d, who has occupied (or built up, reports are conflicting) a ruined hillfort deep in the central forests of Jianghu. Masanori has used his bandit gang to demand tribute from the outlying farmsteads on the fringes of the forest, and the Oathsworn of Jianghu are concerned he might have pretensions of glory and might try to take the region for his own. Of course, the Oathsworn can\u2019t agree on the proper response (unify? Call the Lord Iysen? Appease?), and in the face of their indecision the bandit-lord only grows more bold.<br /> <br /> Of course, the Men-at-Arms of Jianghu being called away to war doesn\u2019t exactly help matters.<br /> <br /> The Hill-Tribes are another problem. Life is hard enough in Jianghu as it is, even without screaming barbarian raiders stealing your sheep and crops in the middle of the night. However, other than the raids by young warriors out to prove themselves, the residents of Jianghu and the Tribes get along well, comparatively speaking. The two peoples trade together as much as fight, and have even intermarried in the past. As far as the populace of Jianghu is concerned, at least a Tribesman will stab you in the front, not the back, and be trusted to keep their word.<br /> <br /> However, the recent rumors about the Tribes moving into the hills is \u2026.. concerning. While they get along, they don\u2019t get along <i>that</i> well. What will happen remains to be seen.<br /> <br /> Yagura Castle:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"http://imgur.com/ptwtYlt\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/google-material/image_placeholder.svg\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://imgur.com/ptwtYlt\" target=\"_blank\"> </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://imgur.com/ptwtYlt\" target=\"_blank\"> http://imgur.com/ptwtYlt </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"http://imgur.com/C4MORiK\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/google-material/image_placeholder.svg\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://imgur.com/C4MORiK\" target=\"_blank\"> </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://imgur.com/C4MORiK\" target=\"_blank\"> http://imgur.com/C4MORiK </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> Yagura Castle is the ancestral stronghold of the Shiba family, and the single strongest point in the Jianghu region. Built of local granite and slate, and located on a bluff overlooking Yari Lake, the fortification is more than capable of holding off a force many times the number of the armsmen it holds.<br /> <br /> \u201cYagura\u201d is the Dragontongue word for \u201ctower\u201d, and indeed Yagura Castle is primarily made up of towers. The three towers, while small compared to the likes of Teriake or Azu, are solid and well-built, shedding heavy snows and deflecting strong winds as easily as arrows.<br /> <br /> Since the Shiba clan doesn\u2019t live at the castle, it is overseen by a castellan appointed by the Lord Iysen. The current holder of the position, Akio Masumi, is an incompetent fat slob who should probably be hung on principle alone. Due to his principle of noninterference (due to chronic drunkenness), the armsmen at Yagura are relatively lazy and out of fighting trim, and the armory is degraded. Not to mention the state of Jianghu\u2026.<br /> <br /> Yagura Castle currently has a force of 10 armsmen permanently stationed, 6 infantrymen and 4 crossbowmen, with helmets, backswords, halberds and crossbows. The armory holds 50 spears, 10 crossbows, with plenty of bolts for the same, several dozen helmets, several shields, and several piles (several hundred or so) fist-and-head-sized rocks, for dropping from machiolations and the walls. Most of the armory is in various states of disarray, but the spears are in decent shape, and if the stuff <u>needed</u> to be used, it probably could.<br /> <br /> Edit: Kasai still need a clan name, folks. So far, it is a 2-on-2 tie between Matsuoka and Qianke. Some more votes to break the tie would be much appreciated.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11892,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","user":"Boston123","date":". 05-26-2017, 10:30 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-27T04:33:24-07:00","id":1097484,"title":"","content":"Out of the two tied clan names, I favour Qianke.<br /> <br /> Meanwhile we should consider our pick for our new castellan, since it sounds like the guy is incompetent. If he is sober and shapes up once we're there then maybe he keeps his job. We need to get our house in order. On the plus side, we have a decent, non-violent punishment detail for our men-at-arms; maintenance and repair of those 'house weapons'.<br /> <br /> On the other hand, punishing poachers with military service could be a quick way to boost the standing army size. Given that poachers should be punished via death, the technically counts as mercy, but given how reliant the area is on hunting at the moment, it would be a bad idea to start enforcing that. This is a sort-of middle ground and to be honest, I would still suggest paying full wages to those thusly conscripted. Just an idea. I am less confidant about the merit of this approach having now articulated it but that is still a good idea to share. If only to spark others.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Thoughts ripple out, birthing others","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1624-falleneco","name":"FallenEco","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1624_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1927"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-27T05:34:42-07:00","id":1097495,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>FallenEco</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1097484#post1097484\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Out of the two tied clan names, I favour Qianke.<br /> <br /> Meanwhile we should consider our pick for our new castellan, since it sounds like the guy is incompetent. If he is sober and shapes up once we're there then maybe he keeps his job. We need to get our house in order. On the plus side, we have a decent, non-violent punishment detail for our men-at-arms; maintenance and repair of those 'house weapons'.<br /> <br /> On the other hand, punishing poachers with military service could be a quick way to boost the standing army size. Given that poachers should be punished via death, the technically counts as mercy, but given how reliant the area is on hunting at the moment, it would be a bad idea to start enforcing that. This is a sort-of middle ground and to be honest, I would still suggest paying full wages to those thusly conscripted. Just an idea. I am less confidant about the merit of this approach having now articulated it but that is still a good idea to share. If only to spark others.<br /> <br /> .</div> </div> </div> </div>Kasai only needs a castellan if he isn't going to live at Yagura. Now that he is here, and I am assuming you lot wants him to live there, the guy is out of a job.<br /> <br /> Edit: Also, don't forget, most of those poaching are those who are also working Kasai's fields. Conscript them, and Kasai has both less food and less money from food.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11892,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","user":"Boston123","date":". 05-27-2017, 08:48 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-27T21:38:10-07:00","id":1097686,"title":"","content":"Given how reliant the populace is on hunting - what about formalising the arrangement with bag limits &amp; foresters? In addition to managing logging &amp; hunting, having some sort of formalised presence in the woods should help identify where the bandits might be lurking. (Side note: did exemption from hanging in exchange for military service come up in regard to dealing with our brigands?) &quot;Lord&quot; Masanori is going to have to be dealt with, either by bringing him to heel under Kasai or by pulling his fort apart stone-by-stone and leaving a trail of heads every hundred yards. Specifically, he's probably the number one priority when it comes to lawlessness, so some sort of fact-finding mission will have to be up on the agenda.<br /> <br /> The present castellan is definitely going to get given the boot, one way or another, but we'll probably need to investigate to see if he's just incompetent, or incompetent AND corrupt; if he's more than just lazy it's going to be a bad idea to let him run around freely before we get our house in order, but we've got oaths to uphold, hanging may or may not be a bit extreme. Regarding the politics of marriage, Kasai should probably look to the daughters of those lords Lord Shiba is most worried about rebelling or failing to answer the call to war simply to try and bring them further into line.<br /> <br /> More thoughts to come when they happen<br /> <br /> Edit: Dereliction of duty probably is a hanging offence come to think of it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":649,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/649-nightwinder","user":"Nightwinder","date":". 05-28-2017, 08:53 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/649-nightwinder","name":"Nightwinder","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"154"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-29T13:47:38-07:00","id":1098222,"title":"","content":"sorry folks, but I have been working overnight, on 12 hour shifts, yesterday, today and tomorrow. So, rather obviously, I haven't been able to get anything done with regards to the Quest. <br /> <br /> THankfully, I have a 5 day &quot;weekend&quot; coming up, so I can post then.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-29T14:05:12-07:00","id":1098232,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nightwinder</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1097686#post1097686\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Given how reliant the populace is on hunting - what about formalising the arrangement with bag limits &amp; foresters? In addition to managing logging &amp; hunting, having some sort of formalised presence in the woods should help identify where the bandits might be lurking. (Side note: did exemption from hanging in exchange for military service come up in regard to dealing with our brigands?) &quot;Lord&quot; Masanori is going to have to be dealt with, either by bringing him to heel under Kasai or by pulling his fort apart stone-by-stone and leaving a trail of heads every hundred yards. Specifically, he's probably the number one priority when it comes to lawlessness, so some sort of fact-finding mission will have to be up on the agenda.<br /> <br /> The present castellan is definitely going to get given the boot, one way or another, but we'll probably need to investigate to see if he's just incompetent, or incompetent AND corrupt; if he's more than just lazy it's going to be a bad idea to let him run around freely before we get our house in order, but we've got oaths to uphold, hanging may or may not be a bit extreme. Regarding the politics of marriage, Kasai should probably look to the daughters of those lords Lord Shiba is most worried about rebelling or failing to answer the call to war simply to try and bring them further into line.<br /> <br /> More thoughts to come when they happen<br /> <br /> Edit: Dereliction of duty probably is a hanging offence come to think of it.</div> </div> </div> </div>That is ...... a very good point.<br /> <br /> As Castellan of Yagura Castle, Akio Masumi was technically responsible for not only maintaining the castle, but also for maintaining the castle's lands. Obviously, he hasn't been doing a very good job, since bandits in Jianghu are thicker than fleas on a dogs ass.<br /> <br /> Technically, he is Lord Iysens retainer, not Kasai's. While she isn't likely to care all that much, (She sent Masumi out here to get him out of her hair and her way, because he is part of a relatively-important noble family)....... Kasai can't really do what he wants with the guy. Fire him, harshly even? Certainly (again, he was going to lose the job once Kasai showed up anyways, something with he certainly isn't going to be happy about, as he loses a decent salary). <i>Execute </i>him?<br /> <br /> Politics, politics.<br /> <br /> Besides, nobles &quot;aren't supposed to&quot; be hanged anyways. That form of execution is for commoners, and is usually public. Nobles, traditionally, are supposed to be beheaded, usually privately. <br /> <br /> And, while the Hundred Kingdoms doesn't have the culture of seppuku that Japan did (for example, if Kasai failed Lord Iysen in some way, she wouldn't order him to commit seppuku), many nobles will commit suicide as opposed to being executed. &quot;Better to die than be killed&quot;, and less embarrassing and painful.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11892,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","user":"Boston123","date":". 05-29-2017, 05:20 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-30T11:46:04-07:00","id":1098500,"title":"","content":"<b>My opinions on the matter of how we run Jianghu (love the name by the way as both a martial artist and wuxia enthusiast). Hope i'm not too late<br /> <br /> <br /> <i><u>Jianghu Domestic Policies</u></i></b><ul><li>Centralization: 4. Little centralization (law and court cases must be brought to a specialized representative of Kasai, aka a Magistrate of his choosing). I say this because it keeps us from doing all the ruling but also allows us to build loyal underlings who clearly would have to strive to appease Kasai.</li> <li>Aristocracy vs Plutocracy: 2. Slightly Aristocratic ( Slightly agriculturally favored) we have a lot of natural resources that we can take advantage of right now and by having other oathsworn vassals or retainers or whatever distinction it is, we'd be building a class of people to support us as we go while being able to get our economy moving now.</li> <li>Commoners: 3. Even mix (5 on 5 split, Resources (agriculture) goes to 3, Resources (taxes) goes to 3) lots of money and allows us to grow in either direction depending on whether we find the slightly aristocratic way of doing things useful. If not we could easily switch back. Either way its good to divide people so they aren't all capable of uniting against you.</li> </ul><ul><li>Mercantilism vs Free-Trade: 1. Free-Trade favored: encourages external trade. Boost external economy. Seems to me like local economy is almost non-existant beyond subsistence. We'll need to trade outside our boundaries to grow the economy. We'll want investment from outsiders but that also comes with a risk of spies and sabatours. Either way i still think its the better option for a po-dunk territory like ours.</li> <li>Attracting new settlers: A mix of 3 and 4 if possible?</li> <li>Army composition: 3.Assize of Arms edict: dependent on the value of the land one holds, they have to show up with certain weapons (I guess? I'm happy for others vote to override me on this one)</li> <li>Army training: Mix of 1 &amp; 2 with greater preference to 2</li> <li>Kasai's new clan name: Qianke: Dragontongue word meaning &quot;spearman&quot;, or more poetically, &quot;one of the way of the spear&quot;</li> </ul><span style=\"font-family:inherit\">Question of Army Composition: A unit or two of cavalry and archers would be cool, otherwise i second the MAWR SPEARMEN method of staffing our army.<br /> <br /> Wife Selection: Obviously i prefer the daughters of our other marcher lords who could get rebellious. I see going after our lords children as mostly pointless and a lot of wasted time and effort for someone we wouldn't be certain would make kasai happy. Its also a rather large gamble given we could screw up our relationship with our lord by trying to marry her children. We also dont want to overplay our hand by making our other possibly mutinous marcher lords an inkling of our sworn loyalty to iysen if they dont already know. If we can't get with the daughters of our other marcher lords i'd suggest going for the daughters of Chen or Yan given that marrying their daughters might keep them from making war on Xia or at least give us the possibility of acquiring intel early. </span><br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":8897,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8897-joshopotamus","user":"joshopotamus","date":". 05-30-2017, 02:50 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8897-joshopotamus","name":"joshopotamus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8897_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"97"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-31T14:53:31-07:00","id":1098907,"title":"","content":"You know..... it seems rather poor form as of now, but &quot;Qianke&quot; is really sticking out to me.<br /> <br /> Having the one Chinese name in the middle of a bunch of Japanese names is ....... awkward. In order to stick with the theme (High Realm is Chinese, Dragontonue is Japanese), the name should really be something more along the lines of Yarichi.<br /> <br /> I just realized this now, after 2 weeks of looking at it. : (<br /> <br /> I am just vaguely annoyed, ignore my ramblings.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11892,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","user":"Boston123","date":". 05-31-2017, 05:56 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-01T03:44:06-07:00","id":1099092,"title":"","content":"Pretty sure that was the one that was winning, too.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Thoughts ripple out, birthing others","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1624-falleneco","name":"FallenEco","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1624_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1927"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-01T03:54:57-07:00","id":1099096,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Boston123</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1098907#post1098907\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You know..... it seems rather poor form as of now, but &quot;Qianke&quot; is really sticking out to me.<br /> <br /> Having the one Chinese name in the middle of a bunch of Japanese names is ....... awkward. In order to stick with the theme (High Realm is Chinese, Dragontonue is Japanese), the name should really be something more along the lines of Yarichi.<br /> <br /> I just realized this now, after 2 weeks of looking at it. : (<br /> <br /> I am just vaguely annoyed, ignore my ramblings.</div> </div> </div> </div>I know for me at least, the appeal was the meaning of the name rather than the sound, and thus would happily convert to Yarichi. As a semi-related thought, is Yarichi the Japanese equivalent of the same characters, or just a near enough equivalent?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/649-nightwinder","name":"Nightwinder","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"154"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-01T09:35:00-07:00","id":1099186,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>FallenEco</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1099092#post1099092\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Pretty sure that was the one that was winning, too.</div> </div> </div> </div>That is why it suddenly stook out to me. I was going over the suggested names, and I noticed that it didn't &quot;look&quot; right.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nightwinder</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1099096#post1099096\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I know for me at least, the appeal was the meaning of the name rather than the sound, and thus would happily convert to Yarichi. As a semi-related thought, is Yarichi the Japanese equivalent of the same characters, or just a near enough equivalent?</div> </div> </div> </div>&quot;Yarichi&quot; is a portmanteau of the Japanese <i>yari no michi</i>, which google-translates to &quot;way of the spear&quot;. I took <i>qianke</i> from <i>jianke, </i>the pinyin word for &quot;swordsman&quot;. (<i>jian</i> is Chinese for &quot;a double-edged straight sword&quot;, and <i>qiang</i> is the stereotypical Chinese spear).<br /> <br /> Ultimately, Kasai's clan-name is 100% irrelevant. Whatever the choice is, it will have no effect on the story at all. It is just me quibbling over inconsequential things, things that I, myself, actually chose and am now second-guessing why I chose them.<br /> <br /> Its kinda like ordering spicy chicken wings, only to get the same number of spicy chicken tenders instead. Same price, same amount, still tasty, just ...... annoying.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-01T20:42:03-07:00","id":1099379,"title":"","content":"Wikipedia gives me S\u00f5jutsu as Spear-fencing (Well, &quot;Art of the spear&quot;, but whatever), so S\u00f5do (It's supposed to be a macron, but apparently Win10 is the dumb) would be &quot;Spear-way&quot; - don't ask me how that would then become a name, but its a start towards consistency <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/649-nightwinder","name":"Nightwinder","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"154"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-01T22:05:01-07:00","id":1099392,"title":"","content":"Here we go, after taking a tally of everyone\u2019s input.<br /> <br /> ---------<br /> <br /> The next morning, your party sets out from Azu Castle, following the banks of the White River to the northeast. Only a few miles away from the castle, the post-stations become fewer, and as the land rises into forested mountains, stop entirely, as do the farming villages. As the elevation increases, your rate of travel decreases, and you walk Xuansu as often as you ride.<br /> <br /> Of course, your rate of travel isn\u2019t helped by your companions.<br /> <br /> You are accompanied by the men-at-arms of Jianghu, released from their levy by the Lord Iysen. As a result, the men (and not a few women) are in high spirits, jovial as they march home, and you take the time to try and get acquainted with your new tenants and Oathsworn vassals.<br /> <br /> The peasants are friendly and respectful, if a bit standoffish, and the Oathsworn nobles are polite, even if they don\u2019t believe your claims of nobility at first (you do have to admit, without armor or even fine clothing, you don\u2019t cut that regal of a figure), and it takes repeated showings of Lord Iysens seal and letter for them to come around to the idea that they have a new liegelord.<br /> <br /> All in all, they seem like a pretty decent lot, standoffishness nonewithstanding. Nobody even gave you a second glance concerning your Hill-Tribe features, which is \u2026.. odd, to say the least. Come to think of it, many of the men-at-arms had red hair and wood-grained skin themselves. Maybe integrating the Tribes will be easier than you thought?<br /> <br /> As a result of both the army on your heels and the poor road, it takes a little over a week to get from Azu Castle to the margins of Jianghu. Increasingly, the land rises steeper and steeper, even to the banks of the river, and the farm-fields you pass are criss-crossed with rough-laid walls of fieldstones.<br /> <br /> You cross the White river at the market-village of Liangzhou, where the term \u201csleepy\u201d is the kindest phrase you would use to describe it (\u2018po\u2019dunk\u2019 or \u2018deader than its own cemetery\u201d are better fits, in your mind). Almost as soon as you leave Liangzhou, the road changes from rough cobbles to dirt, and the land becomes wild. Even the cultivated land close to the river is rough compared to the domains you just left, with copses of trees and rugged heathland breaking up the farmhouses, and you only half-jokingly say you can see more sheep than people. More than one line of hills is dark, uncultivated and empty, and half the orchards you pass (there are a <i>lot</i> of orchards, come to think about it) are wild and overgrown, grass and brambles slowly choking out the fruit trees.<br /> <br /> You realize with growing disquiet that Lord Iysens demands are going to be a lot harder than you realized. Just what have you got yourself into?<br /> <br /> As you progress farther and farther into Jianghu, you start to see that the farmhouses are incredibly fortified, along the levels of noble manor-houses in other domains, with heavy stone walled houses, palisades of logs, watch towers, dry-moats and earthen embankments. When you ask, the one common reply, heavy with dry amusement and unspoken long-borne resignation\u2026.<br /> <br /> \u201cBandits\u201d.<br /> <br /> Well, that explains <i>everything</i>.<br /> <br /> Over time, in groups of ones and twos and threes, the men-at-arms and Oathsworn peel off from the travelling army, trudging up dirt paths to farmhouses and estates. The latter, you request that they meet you at Yagura Castle in a week, for a general meeting and debriefing. By the time you reach Yari Lake, dark and cold even in the middle of high summer, you are accompanied by only a scant few armed soldiers, and the knowledge that the region is heavy with bandit activity sets a weight deep in your stomach.<br /> <br /> Eventually, you come up over a rise in the road, catching a glimpse of Yagura Castle for the first time. Even bone-tired as you are, stiff and sore and worried sick, the sight nevertheless fails to drag a gasp from your lips, and Saiko and Blue-Pines cease their chatter in shock and surprise.<br /> <br /> Even at this distance, the whitewashed walls gleam in the noonday sunlight, and the towers rise almost gracefully over the water and forests surrounding it. Yagura Castle commands the entire valley, rising up almost to the level of the mountains in the background (not really, you chide yourself. Just a trick of perspective), and the tower on top of the keep has line-of-sight across the entire lake.<br /> <br /> You realize, with sudden recognition, that that is <i>yours</i>. Yagura Castle, almost all the farmhouses, orchards and pastures, the allegiance of all the soldiers and noble Oathsworn. All of it. Never, not once in your wildest dreams, could you have ever imagined this would happen. Sure, you\u2019ve idly daydreamed about winning an estate, running a manor-house and maybe someday a family, but not this! According to Lord Iysen, you are, technically, the 4<sup>th</sup> most powerful person in Xia, her right hand\u2026\u2026.. you, the bastard child of some backwoods noble, and half-savage besides!<br /> <br /> You shake your head, chuckling. Yes, this surpasses your wildest dreams, but from the looks of things, you sure as hell have your work cut out for you here in Jianghu. Saiko touches your knee at the sound.<br /> <br /> \u201cWhat\u2019s so funny, Kasai?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c\u2026. Just laughing at circumstances, Saiko. Did you think that, a year ago, we would be moving into a castle, one <i>I</i> <b>own</b>, or owning a fief that would take several days to cross? I sure as hell didn\u2019t.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cNo, not really.\u201d She admits, turning back to the castle gleaming on the bluff. \u201cI\u2019m still trying to take it all in, so I can only wonder how you feel. Still, this is all like what happens in the fairy-tales\u2026.\u201d<br /> <br /> Blue Pines grunts, \u201cBetter hope there isn\u2019t a troll, or a dragon. Kasai doesn\u2019t really look up to fighting monsters right yet.\u201d<br /> <br /> Grinning, \u201cNope, not at all. I\u2019ll take a frog, though. Maybe if I kiss it, it will turn into a princess!\u201d<br /> <br /> Saiko throws grass at you, \u201cThe frog turns into a handsome <i>prince</i>, you numbskull! Besides, that is how you get warts!\u201d<br /> <br /> You draw yourself up in quiet indignation, \u201cI thought I already had the \u2018handsome prince\u2019 side of things covered.\u201d<br /> <br /> She giggles and rolls her eyes, and Blue Pines shakes her head, \u201cAnd then you woke up.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cSure, sure, insult the guy that pays your salary.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c\u2026. You <i>don\u2019t</i> pay either of us a salary!\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cMy point still stands. It\u2019s the principle of the thing.\u201d\u2026..<br /> <br /> You bicker with your retainers as you move down the bluff. At the base of the cliff Yagura stands on, there is a \u2018town\u2019, more a collection of farmhouses around a central plaza. When you pass into the plaza, you get buttonholed by two men, one grey-haired in noble robes and the other in well-worn armor. Both the men bow deeply.<br /> <br /> The noble speaks first, \u201cGood day, my Lord Jianghu. I hope the travel was not too grueling?\u201d<br /> <br /> You shrug, \u201cEasier than some and worse than some. Might I ask who you gentlemen are?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cOf course\u201d. Still bent over, he places a hand to his chest, \u201cMy name is Kasahiro Manabu, one of the Lord Shiba\u2019s vassals and an accountant by trade. I was asked by my liege to come here and assist you in the running and ordering of your fief.\u201d<br /> <br /> The warrior steps forwards, knuckling his forehead in salute. \u201cI\u2019m Oxblood, Sergeant and drillmaster of her levies, M\u2019Lord.\u201d He grins, \u201cI reckon I\u2019ve trained more soldiers than you\u2019ve had hot dinners, begging your pardon, and the boss sent me here to give you a hand with that.\u201d<br /> <br /> Kasahiro continues, \u201cOf course, my Lord, both our jobs would be made much easier if we actually had access to the castle\u2026\u201d<br /> <br /> You frown, \u2018What do you mean?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c Akio Masumi, the castellan in charge of the castle and the domain, refused us entry, even when I showed him the letter from the Lord Xia. He told us he would send for us when he was, and I quote, \u201cgood and ready\u201d.\u201d He sniffs, \u201cthat was 2 days ago.\u201d<br /> <br /> Sgt. Oxblood grunts, \u201cBastard was as drunk as a skunk, at 9-bells even! That takes some dedication.\u201d<br /> <br /> You sigh, glaring up at the castle overhead. <i>Of course</i> things have to be difficult. All you want at this point is a hot meal, a bath, and a nice soft bed, but <i>nooooooo</i>.<br /> <br /> Grumbling, \u201cFollow me, if you will. I will see things straightened out.\u201d<br /> <br /> Followed by Kasahiro and Oxblood, you ride Xuansu up the winding road to the castle gate. At least the guards were competent and watchful enough to notice your approach, and you are met by several (which is not how things should be. Another force could sneak up while the armsmen were distracted) guards watching interestedly from the gatehouse wallwalk. One of them calls down to you,\u2019<br /> <br /> \u201cCan I help you, sir?\u201d<br /> <br /> You draw yourself up to your full height, \u201cMy name is Kasai Qianke, and by the edict of the Lady Iysen Shiba, Lord Xia, I am the duly-recognized Lord of the Jianghu Marches. As such, I would like these gates to be opened, so that I might enter my home.\u201d<br /> <br /> After he picks up his jaw from the parapet, \u201cI\u2026.. I\u2019m sorry my Lord, but I can\u2019t just open the gate to anyone who asks. I don\u2019t have the key, first of all, and \u2026. Uh\u2026.. how do I know who you are who you say you are?\u201d<br /> <br /> You smile. The man has a point. \u201cCan you read?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cYes, my Lord.\u201d<br /> <br /> Reaching into your jacket, you pull out the missive from Lord Iysen, signed and stamped with her seal. \u201cThis should clear things up.\u201d<br /> <br /> He unfolds the paper, glancing at the wax seal at the bottom and skimming the rest. Sighing, he folds it up and tosses it back to you, \u201c\u2026. Looks like everything checks out, my Lord.\u201d He bows, as well as the rest of the armsmen, \u201cBut I still don\u2019t have the key to the gate. The castellan, Akio Masumi, does, and I will have to get it from him. I\u2026.. well, I\u2019ve got to warn you. He has a temper, on top of being drunk, and he <i>hates</i> any dismissal of his authority. This could get <i>interesting</i>, if you catch my drift.\u201d<br /> <br /> You grin, \u201cNothing can be simple, my good man. Bring him to me, and we will see things out.\u201d<br /> <br /> It only takes a few minutes before you can hear the shouting echoing from within the castle walls. The argument gets louder and louder, until the face of the castellan, red and angry, appears over the parapet.<br /> <br /> Seething, spittle forming at the corners of his lips, \u201cWho the hell are you, to tell me to open the gates to my castle?!\u201d<br /> <br /> You refrain from letting your irritation show, instead lifting an eyebrow, \u201cTechnically, my good sir, this is <i>my</i> castle. You are just watching over it for me. As such, I think that allows me to open whatever I please, does it not?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c\u2026.What?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cMy name is Kasai Qianke, and I am the Lord Jianghu. <i>You may bow</i>.\u201d<br /> <br /> He reels back in shock, then after regaining his composure (and his balance. You can smell the drink on him from here), \u201c\u2026..<i>bullshit</i>. The Lord Iysen would have told me about this!\u201d<br /> You toss the letter up again, \u201cTechnically, she did. That letter has everything you need.\u201d<br /> <br /> He reads through the missive, \u201c\u2026.. You\u2019ve got to be shitting me.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cNo, indeed I am not. Please open the gates.\u201d<br /> <br /> He stares at you for a moment, grinding his teeth, then mutters a curse and heads down out of sight. A few more moments pass, then the air is filled with the sound of chains clanking and rusted metal screeching. Slowly, with much creaking and groaning, the gate to Yagura Castle grinds open.<br /> <br /> As you ride Xuansu through the open gate (more of a tunnel through the stone footing of the wall, really), you take immediate notice of the numerous impediments to access. The ceiling, really the floor of the gatehouse above, is lined with murder holes and machicolations, and the walls of the tunnel slope inwards, getting close enough at a point where a wagon would have to go slow to avoid getting caught up. Any enemy force trying to enter would have to be crammed shoulder to shoulder, easy targets for rocks or arrows from above, and a ram would be slowed to a crawl. The gate itself is a foot thick, made from iron-banded white oak beams, and supported by a porticullis.<br /> <br /> On the other side of the gate stands Akio Masumi, fuming.<br /> <br /> From on top of Xuansu, you lift your other eyebrow, finding it more and more difficult to withhold your irritation. \u201c\u2026. I am still waiting for that bow, sir.\u201d<br /> <br /> He stares in confusion, then offers a perfunctory bow, little more than a nod and a twitch of the waist.<br /> <br /> \u201cNo, no, a <i>real</i> bow, one accorded to my station.\u201d<br /> <br /> He glares, \u201cIn this mud?\u201d<br /> <br /> Okay, that\u2019s it.<br /> <br /> \u201c <i>Yes</i>, Akio Masumi, in this mud. I am the <i>Lord Jianghu</i>, and I will see the respect accorded to my station!\u201d Your voice is filled with ice. \u201cDon\u2019t make me come over there to <i>show you how</i>.\u201d<br /> <br /> He wavers, internally debating what to do, then slowly, hatefully, he sinks to his knees in the muck and presses his forehead to the ground. He holds the position until you give him permission to rise.<br /> <br /> \u201cGood!\u201d, you smile beatifically. Dismounting (painfully, with help from Saiko), you pass the reins to an armsman, \u201cNow that that is all squared away, I would like a tour.\u201d<br /> <br /> As you follow Akio into the keep, you buttonhole the armsman who helped you get inside. \u201cI am sorry, I didn\u2019t catch your name.\u201d<br /> <br /> Dubiously, \u201cMy name is Tadao, my Lord.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cExcellent. Thank you for the help, Tadao.\u201d, and he bows quickly before heading back to the wallwalk.<br /> <br /> Slowly, Akio Masumi leads you through the keep, pointing out the various rooms and functions. There is a general level of unkempt-ness through the castle, making your skin crawl. Dust piled in the corners, dirt ground into the wooden floors, cobwebs by the ceiling. People <i>live</i> here?<br /> <br /> When you get to the armory, your jaw drops. The place is a mess: spears are jumbled haphazardly in the racks, the crossbows are piled on the table, etc. You pick up one of the arbalests and pass it over to Blue Pines. \u201cWhat do you think of this?\u201d<br /> <br /> Taking the weapon, she gives it a quick once-over. \u201cThe stock is fine, but the trigger is sticky, which means the nut probably has to be replaced. The prod is ok, but the string is fraying at the nocks. I wouldn\u2019t want to shoot it.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cLater on tonight, I want you to go through all this stuff, see what condition it is in.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cGot it.\u201d<br /> <br /> Turning to Akio, \u201cTell me, sir, why are these arms such a mess?\u201d<br /> <br /> He shrugs, \u201cWe\u2019ve never been attacked.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cDoesn\u2019t explain why they are in such bad condition.\u201d All you get is another shrug. Seething, you follow him onwards and upwards.<br /> <br /> The great hall is \u2026.. ok, although the walls are faded and the floor is dusty. The guest-rooms, under the eaves, are a mess, filled with cobwebs and mouse droppings. The Lords chambers\u2026\u2026<br /> <br /> You stare. \u201c\u2026.. What the <i>fuck</i> have you done in here?!\u201d<br /> <br /> He starts, \u201cExcuse me?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cYou heard me!\u201d You slowly make your way between the piled empty wine bottles and beer jars. This place <i>stinks</i>, of stale beer, old food, sweat and piss, to the point where your eyes water.<br /> <br /> Going over to a window, where the smell is at least diminished, you pull out another letter.<br /> <br /> \u201cAccording to this letter from Lord Iysen, there should be keys for the main gate, the keep gatehouse, as well as a safe in these chambers, two of each. Where are they?\u201d<br /> <br /> Sullenly, \u201cIn my room.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cGet them.\u201d<br /> <br /> He stalks off to the Lords chamber (gods be merciful, you have to sleep in there!), coming back a few minutes later with two keyrings.<br /> <br /> \u201cHow much personal equipment do you have in here?\u201d<br /> <br /> Confused, \u201cNot much, why?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cGather it. You have one hour, I want you gone by then. I can handle the rest of the tour on my own.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u2018What! You\u2026. I am a retainer of the Lord Iysen, she will hear of this!\u201d<br /> <br /> You grin, pulling your lips back from your teeth, happy to see him recoil. \u201c<i>Go ahead</i>, tell her. What do you think she is going to say? You are <i>fired</i>, Akio Masumi, and based on your gross incompetence and impertinence, you should consider yourself lucky I don\u2019t send you away with a <i>horsewhipping.</i>\u201d You make to go into the office, \u201cRemember, Masumi: <i>one hour</i>. Don\u2019t make me wait.\u201d<br /> <br /> In the office, after pushing aside yet more trash, you find the safe, made of cast iron, under the desk. Inside, you are happy to find 10 dirham in silver, stamped with the mark of Xia. At least you aren\u2019t a <i>pauper</i>, and running a domain of this size will be <i>expensive</i>. On top of the desk are several messy piles of papers, and you start going through them idly at first. When some of them indicate the food-rent and tax records of Jianghu, you read them in earnest.<br /> <br /> Evidently, you got quite invested in your reading, and get startled when you hear Saiko curse at the level of garbage.<br /> <br /> \u201cLots of shit, huh?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cNo godsdamned kidding! This is awful!\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cYour room is over there. I haven\u2019t even been in there yet, so be prepared for anything\u2026\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cMy room?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cYeah,\u201d , untangling yourself from the hug, \u201cWhen you are done jumping around, go down to the town and see if you can\u2019t find some people willing to help clean this place up. I\u2019ll pay, if that helps. The meeting with my vassals is in 3 days, and I don\u2019t want this place looking like a pigsty.\u201d<br /> <br /> Grabbing what papers you see as critical, you head back downstairs to the armory hall, easily the cleanest part of the keep. Tucked in a corner with Kasahiro, you go over the records in detail, trying to hammer out some semblance of sense of the lines upon lines of numbers. Eventually, Saiko comes back with several young villagers in tow, who cry out in dismay at the mess (although the copper you offer quiets them nicely).<br /> <br /> Over the next few days, you go over records with Kasahiro, discussing different levels of taxation, the traditional expected amounts of food-rent and labor-service required of serfs, just how much land you now own (a <u>lot</u>, only 2/3 of which is actually used), what economic effect releasing serfs from servitude would have, as well as many other topics. When the numbers and figures start swirling senselessly through your brain, you take a \u201cbreak\u201d by helping the peasant girls clean. Once the garbage is thrown away and the floors and walls scrubbed with ammonia, the place actually looks rather nice. Better yet, some of the girls found various carpets and tapestries in a linen closet, so now the place doesn\u2019t look so empty. You can even move into the master bedroom, which actually has a <b>bed</b>, with linen sheets and quilted blankets. Even in Teriake Castle, you didn\u2019t have a bed.<br /> <br /> So far, it is good to be the boss.<br /> <br /> Eventually, your various Oathsworn vassals start trickling in, where they get directed up to the Great Hall. There, at a long trestle table set up by your new servants, you sit and greet them as they come in (you actually agonized about how to set the thing up; whether you should sit on the dais at the head of the hall, be humble and sit with the retainers, or somesuch. Eventually, you decide to sit with them, albeit at the head of the table.). The servants bring out refreshments, including an ample supply of cider; as you noticed on the way here, Jianghu has quite a high number of orchards, which produce great amounts of fruit.<br /> <br /> When everyone is seated and sated, you stand up and introduce yourself to your assembled retainers.<br /> <br /> \u201cGood evening, everyone. I have already met a great number of you, but for those who have yet to speak to me, my name is Kasai Qianke, and by the edict of the Great Lord Xia, Lady Iysen Shiba, I am now the Lord Paramount of the Jianghu Marches.<br /> <br /> They rise and bow, evidently a great deal more polite than Akio Masumi (something you are glad to note. If everyone acted like that asshole, there were going to be <i>problems</i>). One of the Oathsworn, an older gentlemen, asks,<br /> <br /> \u201cMy Lord, if I may ask without giving offense: how old are you?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c I am two-and-twenty years of age, sir.\u201d<br /> <br /> He shakes his head, in amusement or despair. \u201cDragons, my youngest children are older than you, my Lord\u2026\u2026.. Again, if I can ask without giving offense: why would so young a man be entitled to such a prominent position?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c Of course you can ask. I served our Lord Iysen faithfully for a year, and saved her life in the assassination attempt before that, during the Fire in Teriake. Most recently, I was instrumental in securing peace and a military alliance with the Dumnoniapoccasset Confederation of the Hill-Tribes, that for which I was granted the Lordship.\u201d<br /> <br /> The last line is met with blank stares.<br /> <br /> \u201c\u2026..Do you not know what is happening?\u201d<br /> <br /> The older gentlemen answers, \u201cForgive us, my Lord, but here in Jianghu we are isolated at the best of times. News comes late and slow, if at all. Could you please tell us what this agreement is?\u201d<br /> After taking a sip of your cider (which is really quite good, you should try to export this), \u201cAs a result of the ongoing war with Yue, the Great Lord Xia did not want to potentially fight a war on two fronts, and therefore sought peace with the Tribes.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cI was tasked with travelling to their chief, the warlord Ousamequin, and discussing the possibility of a non-aggression pact in return for ceding certain parcels of land, trade agreements, so on and so forth. \u201c<br /> <br /> \u201cSeven of the twelve tribes agreed to join forces with Xia, and four of the rest agreed to neutrality. The only tribe that declared war is on the other side of the Confederation, making it by-and-large a paper tiger.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cThe lands ceded to the Tribes include most of the hill-country and uplands on the border of Xia. In the case of Jianghu, this would include the mountains and highlands to the north and east. I expect them to start moving into those territories comparatively soon.\u201d<br /> <br /> Your statement is met with stunned silence. \u201cIs \u2026..Is this going to be a problem? If so, there isn\u2019t much I can do about it. The Lord Iysen was the one to decide which territories to cede, not I. Anyways, part of the treaty spelled out in specific terms that the people of Xia were not to be harmed.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cWell, to be honest, my Lord\u2026\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cIf you are going to be the only one talking, sir, I would like to at least know your name.\u201d, you say softly.<br /> <br /> \u201c-ahem- Of course. My name is Shin Takeshi.\u201d<br /> <br /> You nod, acknowledging it. \u2018And while we are at it, you may refer to me as \u201cLord Kasai\u201d. To be honest,\u201d You admit, almost sheepishly, \u201cI am still trying to get used to this whole \u201crulership\u201d thing, and would kind of prefer to be eased into it.\u201d<br /> <br /> Shin laughs, good-naturedly, \u201cVery well, Lord Kasai. \u2026.. As I was saying, the people of Jianghu have a \u2026.. complicated relationship with the Tribes. While they do raid us, and we do fight them, we also have largely coexisted with them for quite some time. Many of the peasants are of partial Tribe descent\u2026. Hell, even some of us have the Hill-blood within our veins.\u201d He stops, the says quietly, \u201cMy first wife was of the Tribes, and my children from her are half-blooded, like you, Lord Kasai.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cOh? What totem do they show?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt;The fox, Lord Kasai&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> Impressive. Most lowlanders garble Forestongue beyond all recognition, but he is fluent despite a slight accent. You might want to keep him around.<br /> <br /> Shin Takeshi continues, \u201cAnyways\u2026.. having the Tribes move so close is \u2026. <i>concerning</i>, but more because any large body of people on the move is cause for concern, especially when they are as warlike and confrontational as the Tribes. However, if you say there is a peace agreement, then there shouldn\u2019t be <i>too</i> many issues, Lord Kasai. So long as the agreement is kept, of course.\u201d<br /> <br /> Pleased, \u201cThat is good to hear. Of course, if there is any trouble with the Tribes, bring it to my attention as soon as possible. I would like to hammer out any cross-cultural kerfluffles before they cause serious damage.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cAh\u2026\u2026 actually, my Lord. There has been some troubles, some minor raids on the isolated northern farmsteads. Nothing serious; no casualties, just scared farmers, that is all.\u201d<br /> <br /> You frown. The Tribal elders were supposed to keep a tight lid on stuff like that. You\u2019ll have to talk to them. On to the next!<br /> <br /> Refilling your cup, \u201c Part of my agreement with the Lord Iysen was that, in order to me to retain this Lordship, I have to be able to levy 1000 Men-at-Arms by next summer. Now, I already know the answer, but tell me; how many men are under arms in Jianghu?\u201d<br /> <br /> The landed retainers turn and converse with each other. After a few minutes of muttered conversation, Shin Takeshi turns back to you, \u201cTechnically, between our combined holdings and your lands, Jianghu can levy about 500 or so soldiers. At least, that is what we marched to war with several weeks ago. Functionally, the number is probably less, as the Lord Iysen demanded we levy as many soldiers as possible; old men, bare-faced boys, that sort of thing. We recommend about 300 be the maximum in a non-emergency situation.\u201d<br /> <br /> You nod. \u201cThat is what I was told. Now, on my personal holdings, I am planning on releasing a couple hundred serfs from serfdom, in order to pad those numbers.\u201d Taking the slip of paper offered by Kasahiro, \u201cBy these calculations, that would bring the numbers of functionally-levyable soldiers to \u2026\u2026 about 500. According to the terms of the agreement, I still need 500 more, and I would like to <i>eventually</i> at least compete with the Lords Wulin and Iruma, who are both capable of at least 3000. What sort of actions do you recommend to increase these numbers?\u201d<br /> <br /> Again, your retainers turn and discuss with each other, this time for far longer. You take the opportunity to go over several of the figures with Kasahiro.<br /> <br /> After several minutes of deliberation, Shin Takeshi rises again, \u201c Surely as you travelled here to Yagura Castle, you noticed just how much land was empty, uncultivated?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cYes, I did, actually. Why is that?\u201d<br /> <br /> Another of your retainers (Aya Cho, you remember now) snorts in derision, \u201cThat useless waste of meat, Akio Masumi\u201d.<br /> <br /> You freeze, blood turning to ice. \u201c\u2026.. Just what has that blowhard done to damage my people?\u201d<br /> <br /> She chuckles, \u201cI see you met him.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cMet him? I sent him packing, and spent the last three days cleaning his mess.\u201d<br /> <br /> Her smile fades, \u2018Well, he took the same amount of care in governing this domain as he did in keeping this castle clean. Technically, he was responsible for administering all the lands now held by you, Lord Kasai, and we were not to interfere. You can imagine how that went.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c\u2026..total anarchy?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cMore like \u2018total apathy\u2019. I do not think he actually ever went to the places he technically oversaw. Entire regions were left to their own devices, in the face of bandits and the Tribes. Asides from local militias, they were entirely defenseless. Worse, when we tried to help, we were censured, and told to \u201cmind our own business!\u201d<br /> <br /> She continues, quietly, \u201cI still think he was taking kickbacks. Nobody can give that little of a shit.\u201d<br /> <br /> Softly, in shock, \u201cWhat happened to the people?\u201d<br /> <br /> She shrugs, \u201cSome of them moved to our holdings, some of them left, the rest died.\u201d<br /> <br /> You snarl, causing some of the closest retainers to scoot back.<br /> <br /> \u201cDo you have any proof of collaberation?!\u201d<br /> <br /> She shakes her head, \u201cNo, sadly, or we would have dealt with it ourselves. He never really talked to us; this is the first time I have been in Yagura Castle for years.\u201d<br /> <br /> You are quiet for a long time, simmering over this revelation.<br /> <br /> \u201cSo \u2026. What you are telling me is ; one, there are regions of my domain that are severely underpopulated, and two, you have basically been left alone for the last several years?\u201d<br /> <br /> Shin nods, \u201cYes, to both counts. Jianghu as a whole is mostly empty, the hinterlands of the forests and the hills even more so. The bandits and lack of governance only compounded the problem.&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;As for \u2018being left alone\u2019\u2026. Yes. Most of us have had to implement legal codes on our own.\u201d<br /> <br /> You rest your chin on your fist, \u201cHow has law and order been?\u201d<br /> <br /> He shrugs, \u201cNot bad, which isn\u2019t surprising. There isn\u2019t any large urban areas for crime to grow in, and life is hard enough out here already without adding to it, leading to most people being well-behaved. Most of the commoners have strong clan-ties, and those clans largely police themselves. We serve as moderators and go-betweens, mostly.\u201d<br /> <br /> You nod. \u201cVery well. Carry on with your cases, I give you the authority to decide cases and punishments. I do want to at least hear about <i>major</i> cases, however. Murders, clan-feuds, that sort of thing. Petty cases are all yours.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cAs for the underdeveloped and undersettled regions, I am planning on implementing several types of settlement and suchlike. For one, both to increase population and to pump up the size of the army, I am planning on offer land to prospective soldiers, so long as they serve for 5 years. My first tentative thoughts involved 40 acres and a head of oxen to start. How does that sound to you?\u201d<br /> <br /> They turn and deliberate.<br /> <br /> \u201cLord Kasai, you come from the lowland valleys, do you not?\u201d<br /> <br /> Amused, \u201cTechnically. I lived in Teriake for a year, but I am ultimately from Tsen, to the northwest. My families holdings are geographically similar to Jianghu.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cRegardless, how heavily-settled are the lowland river valleys?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cHeavily settled, to the point of being crowded\u2026\u2026.. Oh, I see what you are getting at. Yes, many of the children of lowland peasant families lack much in the way of inheritance, and might jump at the chance to gain substantial land of their own. Was there something else?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cYes. Now, while there isn\u2019t anything wrong with spearmen, currently Jianghu has a dearth of combined-arms. Most of the prospective settler-soldiers are going to arrive with spears, which will only compound the issue. Maybe if you offer different grants of land to recruits with different weapons\u2026..\u201d Shin scratches out some computations on a slip of paper. \u201cLet us say that 40 acres is the baseline. If a soldier brings their own weapons, they get 1.25 times that grant. If they are skilled in archery, they get 1.5 times, and cavalry would get 2 times. So\u2026\u2026.. A \u2018base\u2019 soldier would get 40 acres, a soldier with their own equipment would get 50 acres, a longbowman would get 75, and a light cavalryman would get 120-150 acres. That might attract more specialized soldiers, my Lord.\u201d<br /> <br /> You rub your chin, \u201cAnd Jianghu could support such grants?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cFrom what land I have seen not under plow\u2026. Easily. Not counting what will happen when forests and such get cleared.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u2018Excellent idea, Shin Takeshi.\u201d You raise your glass in salute, praise he receives graciously.<br /> <br /> You continue, \u201cNow\u2026\u2026 what is this I hear about commoner militias?\u201d The guilty looks on their faces tells you all you need to know.<br /> <br /> \u201cThe serfs have weapons, don\u2019t they?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cThey <i>have</i> to, Lord Kasai! We Oathsworn are usually several hours away, and..\u201d She is interrupted by your hand.<br /> <br /> \u201c Believe me, I understand. Before I served the Lord Iysen, I wandered the Hundred Kingdoms. I know how \u201creality works\u201d. I have <i>zero</i> issue with the people protecting themselves. I am assuming they hunt as well?\u201d<br /> <br /> The silence is, yet again, all the answer you need.<br /> <br /> You lean back in your chair, resting your head on your hands (it is nice for the casts to be finally off, although the last two fingers on your left hand are stiff and purplish still).<br /> <br /> \u201cSo\u2026. What you are telling me is that there is a substantial population here in Jianghu that owns arms and is somewhat practiced in woodcraft?...... Very well.\u201d You lean forward, looking your retainers in the eyes. \u2018Again, I have <i>no issues</i> with people owning weapons to protect themselves, or even hunting in \u201cmy\u201d woods. The gods know I can\u2019t eat <i>every</i> deer. With that in mind\u2026\u2026 I want every single household in Jianghu, be they peasant or serf, to own at least a spear or a bow, and have at least one person from each household skilled in their use. From what you tell me, this is already a reality, so implementing this shouldn\u2019t be that hard.\u201d<br /> <br /> Aya frowns, \u201cwhat are you getting at, Lord Kasai?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cI want to institute a formal militia to Jianghu. The Men-at-Arms, while doughty, are evidently not enough to drive the ruffians from our lands. This milita would serve to reinforce them, as well as legally protect the right, yes, <i>right</i>, of people to own arms. These militias would wait for a call-up, either from me or from you, and they could only serve within the boundaries of Jianghu itself. And that is an excellent way to segway to my next topic.\u201d<br /> <br /> You twist in your chair, singling out Sergeant Oxblood. \u201cLadies and gentlemen, this is Sergeant Oxblood, on loan from the Lord Iysen\u2019s forces. He is here to help me train and order the required soldiery. With that in mind, he is going to draw up a system of drills and exercises for us. I must ask; how do the Men-at-Arms receive training as of now?\u201d<br /> <br /> Another one of your retainers shrugs, \u201cTo be honest, Lord Kasai, they drill whenever they can, and whenever we can get around to it. Most of us help with the planting and harvest, at least with the planning thereof, which doesn\u2019t really leave a lot of free time. Other than that, the peasants train with each other, by actually using the skills, or through necessity in bandit attacks.\u201d<br /> <br /> You nod, \u201cUnderstandable. Fortunately or unfortunately, that is going to change. I am assuming at least a significant portion of Jianghu is Immaculate-fearing? I saw a shrine in Liangzhou. Regardless, what I want is for you landowners to call a general muster, every Sun-day, probably after Immaculate services for sheer practicality. There, there will be <i>ordered</i> drills, for both the Men-at-Arms and the new Militia. Marching drills, combat training, archery, whatever. So long as it is regular, and it is <i>efficient</i>. Sergeant Oxblood will see to the last, and I want you to see to the first. The sooner we start this, the earlier it becomes a habit. Is that possible?\u201d<br /> <br /> They converse among themselves quickly. \u201cIt should be, Lord Kasai. It will be a change of pace, but both we and the commoners will manage.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cExcellent\u2026\u2026.. excellent. Was there anything else?\u201d<br /> <br /> The retainers look across the table at each other. Aya stands up, \u201cYes, Lord Kasai, there is, and this is the most serious at all.\u201d She takes a deep breath, then ,\u201d Before the call to war came, early in the spring, we heard rumors that several bandit-gangs in the Akibara Forest, in the center of Jianghu, were forcibly consolidated into one by a charismatic leader. Eventually, we learned that this leader is, well, Oathsworn like ourselves, one who has the gall to call himself the \u201cLord of Jianghu\u201d.<br /> <br /> It is almost funny.\u201d Has this bandit-lord done anything yet?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cAsides from lean on outlying farmsteads for supplies\u2026.. no, not really. If anything, his presence is moderating the effects of other bandit gangs. The others are attacking less, and I personally think he is either drawing them to his banner, or wiping out those that will not follow him.\u201d<br /> <br /> You frown, sitting up and paying attention, \u201cHow many warriors follow him?\u201d<br /> <br /> She shrugs, \u201cIt is hard to know for sure. A few bandit-gangs, at least. A few hundred, centered on a ruined Hill-Tribe hill-fort in the Toge hills.\u201d<br /> <br /> That is serious. If he gathered enough strength, or fortified his ruined stronghold, he would be <i>very difficult</i> to remove\u2026\u2026.<br /> <br /> You sigh. \u201cVery well, I shall have to think about what to do about this. Keep me informed if you learn anything new.\u201d<br /> <br /> Turning to the rest of the gathered retainers. \u201cIs there any other intelligence you wish to share with me? Questions? Concerns?\u201d Nobody answers. \u201cVery well, I thank you all for coming, and I appreciate your support. You are dismissed; if anyone wishes to stay the night, inform one of my servants, and a bedroll will be set up in a guest-room for you. Have a good night.\u201d<br /> <br /> As the group gets up to leave, talking among themselves, you sit deep in thought. So far, so good, you guess. Things haven\u2019t caught fire yet\u2026\u2026.. but then again, this is only really your first day on the job, plenty of time to mess up\u2026..<br /> <br /> You are startled from your thoughts by Kasahiro tapping on your shoulder. In his hands he holds a few letters.<br /> <br /> \u201cSorry to interrupt, my Lord, but various couriers have dropped these off for you. I took the liberty of accepting them.\u201d<br /> <br /> Thanking the man, you pull the letters from their envelopes. You do notice that all of them are on fine paper, close-grained and bleached white.<br /> <br /> The first reads:<br /> <br /> -<i>To the Lord Jianghu,</i><br /> <i>I am most humbly and respectfully requesting a meeting at your earliest convenience, so that we might discuss the planning and construction of several roads and bridges, between your domain and the newly-ceded territory to the Hill-Tribes. If you are not able to travel to Teriake Castle-Town at this time, I am willing and able to travel to Yagura Castle.</i><br /> <br /> <i>Respectfully yours, Satoshi Azuki, Head Architect of the Most Virtuous and Puissant Engineers Guild, Teriake Castle Town</i><br /> <br /> <br /> The other letters are from the Lords Chen, Yan and the Wuyi branch of the Shiba clan, all offering various offers to dinner, ostensibly to meet the family and discuss a betrothal.<br /> <br /> \u2026.. They certainly don\u2019t wait around very long!<br /> <br /> You sigh. You are going to be busy as hell for the next couple months, but you should probably decide to focus on one thing first.<ol class=\"decimal\"><li>Focus on taking out this rogue Oathsworn bandit-lord. Summon the army!</li> <li>Meet with the elders of the Tribes. Let\u2019s put out any fires before they start\u2026..</li> <li>Travel around your new holdings. You should probably take a look at what you are giving away\u2026.</li> <li>Travel to Teriake Castle-Town, both to meet this Architect and to order weapons for your new army. Kill two birds with one stone.</li> <li>Have dinner with the Lord Chen, the Ryota family</li> <li>Have dinner with the Lord Yan, the Daomaru family</li> <li>Have dinner with the Wuyi Shiba family</li> </ol>OOC:<br /> <br /> Aiko Masumi is a dickwaffle.<br /> <br /> Secondly, we now have a council! There are several positions that <i>probably</i> should be filled, to offer advice. They don't have to be, and some Lords don't have councils at all.<br /> <br /> -Chancellor: in charge of diplomacy to other domains: who is who, seating arrangements at feasts, writing letters, etc<br /> -Chamberlain: in charge of finances: taxes, food-rent, fees, etc. <u>Kasahiro Manabu is your current Chamberlain</u><br /> -Constable: In charge of drilling troops, maintaining armories, stockpiles, etc. <u>Sergeant-Major Oxblood is your current Constable</u><br /> - Captain of the Castle Guard: In charge of the defense of Yagura Castle, maintaining the castle and supplies<br /> -Personal Retainer. Bodyguard, like Nori was to Lord Iysen<br /> -Magistrate: in charge of laws, hearing court-cases, etc<br /> -any others you want to appoint.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11892,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","user":"Boston123","date":". 06-02-2017, 01:15 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-02T06:18:09-07:00","id":1099481,"title":"","content":"Well, Kasai's liege requires 1000 troops and an heir by next year. Cynical as it sounds, the army can be worked on before, during, and after the actual marriage. Like a crop, an heir has to be on the way by <i>X</i> to meet schedule, while recruiting and training will probably be ongoing. Therefore, look over a map and see if 5, 6 and 7 can be arranged in short order. If that can be combined with sweeping up the young and landless for the army (without irritating other lords), so much the better.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Like my Avatar? Courtesy of<span style=\"color:#0000FF\"> Jen</span>! : <span style=\"font-size:12px\">Anybody want their characters to be experimented on ? </span>post 98<br /> <i>An Exalt is never unarmed.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4018-l-het-esh","name":"L'het'esh","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4018_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"521"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-02T07:47:42-07:00","id":1099514,"title":"","content":"<i>1. Focus on taking out this rogue Oathsworn bandit-lord. </i>If we rally everyone we can muster right now, we will have the advantage of numbers, but I think we can safely assume that the bandits will grow in strength if they are left alone. Everything we're trying to achieve here could fall apart if we have to spend a lot of military resources keeping this province under control <i>at the same time</i> we're trying to build up those resources to ready for war. Besides which, taking down this false lord would be a big PR win with both the peasantry and the nobility, which is something the new Lord Qianke will badly need if he's going to keep making big, sweeping changes to the existing social order of the province.<br /> <br /> As for the council - can we appoint just anyone to these positions? Right now, it feels like Kasai barely knows enough named characters to fill every post, and most of them already have important jobs that demand all of their attention.<br /> <br /> EDIT<br /> Re - options 4-7: We should ask the Satoshis, Ryutas, Daomarus, and Shibas to come to Yaruba rather than us coming to them. That might be a trifle inconsiderate, but we've got a lot of things to do here and taking too many weeks off to visit people in other provinces is really going to cut into our buildup-time.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>On the frontier of the Wild South, there's only one woman with the grit to take on its most dangerous outlaws and bring them <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/984977-back-alive-or-maybe-dead-quest\" target=\"_blank\">Back Alive, or Maybe Dead.</a><br /> <br /> Avatar by <a href=\"http://puzzlinghappenstance.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">K.S. Brenowitz</a>","edited":{"userid":908,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/908-semicasual","user":"semicasual","date":". 06-02-2017, 12:54 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/908-semicasual","name":"semicasual","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar908_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2499"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-02T11:10:24-07:00","id":1099583,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>semicasual</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1099514#post1099514\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><i>1. Focus on taking out this rogue Oathsworn bandit-lord. </i><br /> <br /> As for the council - can we appoint just anyone to these positions? Right now, it feels like Kasai barely knows enough named characters to fill every post, and most of them already have important jobs that demand all of their attention.<br /> <br /> EDIT<br /> Re - options 4-7: We should ask the Satoshis, Ryutas, Daomarus, and Shibas to come to Yaruba rather than us coming to them. That might be a trifle inconsiderate, but we've got a lot of things to do here and taking too many weeks off to visit people in other provinces is really going to cut into our buildup-time.</div> </div> </div> </div>Council: Yep, Kasai (aka you) can appoint whomever he likes, although when I use the term &quot;appoint&quot; I really mean &quot;ask&quot;.<br /> <br /> With regards to people having other jobs.... yeah, that would affect their decision, but &quot;absentee landlordism&quot; is a relatively common thing in the Hundred Kingdoms, especially when you get higher up in the social ranks. I mean, look at Kasahiro Manabu. His land-holdings are on the literal other side of Xia, on the banks of the Red River, yet here he is. Mainly because Lord Iysen isn't one to say no to, but that isn't here nor there.<br /> <br /> Having a council isn't <u>required</u> (Lord Iysen doesn't have one, but that is her own choice, as well as something that causes her a great deal of stress), it is just helpful. Kasai has none of the usual training in running a domain noble children gets, so he is basically foundering in the deep end of the pool at this point. Having a council to help out would be like putting on floaties: immensely helpful, but he still has to learn how to swim on his own.<br /> <br /> Dinner dates at home: ....... that would certainly raise more than a few eyebrows, but they would come. They aren't going to give up the possibility of social advancement just because Kasai doesn't stick with the traditional courtship methods.<br /> <br /> EDIT: Don't forget, and this goes for everyone, not just Semicasual..... once the Hill-Tribes start moving down from the mountains to the highlands, the bandit problem in Jianghu (in most domains, actually, but we only really care about Jianghu) will multiply tenfold. The Tribes will mow through the bandits like a scythe through dry grass, and many of the bandits will run away as opposed to fight them. (The Tribes aren't nice to captured combatants), and where is the closest place they can run to?<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11892,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","user":"Boston123","date":". 06-02-2017, 03:03 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-02T17:09:14-07:00","id":1099688,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Boston123</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1099583#post1099583\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> EDIT: Don't forget, and this goes for everyone, not just Semicasual..... once the Hill-Tribes start moving down from the mountains to the highlands, the bandit problem in Jianghu (in most domains, actually, but we only really care about Jianghu) will multiply tenfold. The Tribes will mow through the bandits like a scythe through dry grass, and many of the bandits will run away as opposed to fight them. (The Tribes aren't nice to captured combatants), and where is the closest place they can run to?<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>Let us solve three problems with one stroke.<br /> <br /> Let us contact this bandit &quot;lord.&quot;<br /> <br /> Bandits are usually dispossessed and desperate souls experienced in violence, who have for one reason or another been forced to leave their homes and live in the woods. It's a tough existence-you're surrounded by other violent desperate people, food and money are inconsistent at best, you're roughing it in the woods...there's a reason banding together under a bandit lord is attractive.<br /> <br /> As it turns out, we have need of people who already have their own weapons, know how to use them, and might be willing to use them in exchange for an increase in wealth or status. We have need of people who are charismatic and capable of forging bands of random bandits into a small army, especially if they actually know how to lead that army in battle! We have the ability to offer the wealth that the bandits want-food, money, stability...a permanent and stable home. Lands to call their own.<br /> <br /> Instead of just wiping the bandits out, why not turn them into your army? Offer this self-styled bandit lord a REAL title in your service, and your help marrying into the lower ranks of the nobility, and estates, and herds! Offer forgiveness from past crimes in exchange for loyal service.<br /> <br /> If he turns out to be evil and just out to rape and pillage, we can kill him off...but based on what the &quot;law&quot; looked like around here until we arrived, a former Oathsworn gathering a following of loyal bandits to him and wiping out bandits who won't join...might well be a Robin Hood looking to bring order and honor to the outlaws and eventually overthrow the distant and &quot;illegitimate&quot; authority of your current Lord and her local and corrupt vassal. It's the sort of thing Kasai might have ended up doing himself, if his story had gone a little differently.<br /> <br /> It's at least worth sending a messenger. I bet there's 500 warriors worth of bandits out there.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>So I'm making <a href=\"http://godkickingboot.smackjeeves.com/\" target=\"_blank\">God-Kicking Boot</a>, an Exalted webcomic, now. Updates on Sundays. Full-color, mediocre but slowly improving art. It's a thing.<br /> <br /> The absence of a monument can, in its own way, be something of a monument also.</i> -Roger Zelazny","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/512-wise-old-guru","name":"Wise Old Guru","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar512_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1097"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-02T19:57:12-07:00","id":1099727,"title":"","content":"Wise old guru might be on to something, we should invite the engineer out to us if he's willing and use his travel times to inspect our holdings as well. Get our face out there a bit. Not sure what's best for the dinners and such.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11567-jairain","name":"Jairain","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11567_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"345"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-02T20:55:26-07:00","id":1099740,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wise Old Guru</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1099688#post1099688\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Let us solve three problems with one stroke.<br /> <br /> Let us contact this bandit &quot;lord.&quot;<br /> <br /> Bandits are usually dispossessed and desperate souls experienced in violence, who have for one reason or another been forced to leave their homes and live in the woods. It's a tough existence-you're surrounded by other violent desperate people, food and money are inconsistent at best, you're roughing it in the woods...there's a reason banding together under a bandit lord is attractive.<br /> <br /> As it turns out, we have need of people who already have their own weapons, know how to use them, and might be willing to use them in exchange for an increase in wealth or status. We have need of people who are charismatic and capable of forging bands of random bandits into a small army, especially if they actually know how to lead that army in battle! We have the ability to offer the wealth that the bandits want-food, money, stability...a permanent and stable home. Lands to call their own.<br /> <br /> Instead of just wiping the bandits out, why not turn them into your army? Offer this self-styled bandit lord a REAL title in your service, and your help marrying into the lower ranks of the nobility, and estates, and herds! Offer forgiveness from past crimes in exchange for loyal service.<br /> <br /> If he turns out to be evil and just out to rape and pillage, we can kill him off...but based on what the &quot;law&quot; looked like around here until we arrived, a former Oathsworn gathering a following of loyal bandits to him and wiping out bandits who won't join...might well be a Robin Hood looking to bring order and honor to the outlaws and eventually overthrow the distant and &quot;illegitimate&quot; authority of your current Lord and her local and corrupt vassal. It's the sort of thing Kasai might have ended up doing himself, if his story had gone a little differently.<br /> <br /> It's at least worth sending a messenger. I bet there's 500 warriors worth of bandits out there.</div> </div> </div> </div>I can't say much without revealing necessary background info, but I like the way you think.<br /> <br /> However, who are we going to send with a message?<br /> <br /> Remember: as far as most people are concerned, traipsing into a bandit camp is basically a drawn-out form of suicide. Most people aren't going to want to go, and is Kasai -orders- someone, they are going to resent it, if they even survive.<br /> <br /> Even if the Oathsworn bandit-king is Robin hood-esque (again, can't say much)......bandits are still bandits.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-02T21:39:57-07:00","id":1099749,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Boston123</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1099740#post1099740\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I can't say much without revealing necessary background info, but I like the way you think.<br /> <br /> However, who are we going to send with a message?<br /> <br /> Remember: as far as most people are concerned, traipsing into a bandit camp is basically a drawn-out form of suicide. Most people aren't going to want to go, and is Kasai -orders- someone, they are going to resent it, if they even survive.<br /> <br /> Even if the Oathsworn bandit-king is Robin hood-esque (again, can't say much)......bandits are still bandits.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, maybe this is just the fact that I'm a star-eyed schemer myself...<br /> <br /> But, well...<br /> <br /> Kasai isn't actually KNOWN in these lands yet. The HEROIC thing to do here is to show up pretending to be just another bandit recruit, scout the place out a little, and then go meet with the Big Boss pretending to be a messenger from the new Lord Jianghu. You can get a good impression of the man, you don't ask an underling to do anything you wouldn't do, and you continue our established pattern of making brave-but-incredibly-stupid decisions!<br /> <br /> Worst case we have to singlehandedly fight our way out of a bandit fortress while still recovering from our previous injuries, and I can't see anything possibly going wrong with that plan!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>So I'm making <a href=\"http://godkickingboot.smackjeeves.com/\" target=\"_blank\">God-Kicking Boot</a>, an Exalted webcomic, now. Updates on Sundays. Full-color, mediocre but slowly improving art. It's a thing.<br /> <br /> The absence of a monument can, in its own way, be something of a monument also.</i> -Roger Zelazny","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/512-wise-old-guru","name":"Wise Old Guru","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar512_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1097"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-03T11:50:56-07:00","id":1099871,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wise Old Guru</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1099749#post1099749\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> Worst case we have to singlehandedly fight our way out of a bandit fortress while still recovering from our previous injuries, and I can't see anything possibly going wrong with that plan!</div> </div> </div> </div>That might be the biggest problem with this plan.<br /> <br /> While Kasai has the casts off his broken arm/fingers, they are still recovering. He has trouble gripping chopsticks or a spoon for any appreciable length of time, much less a weapon clashing and slashing about. There are 'physical therapy&quot;-type things he can, and is, doing, but ..... still. Recovering.<br /> <br /> Plus, his ribs are still broken-ish. He needs help getting out of bed in the morning, for reference (bending at the waist and twisting is difficult/painful)<br /> <br /> Finally, he still needs a cane to walk around. On flat ground, he doesn't <i>need</i> it, but it is necessary going up or down inclines/stairs. Its going to be like that for years, at the very least.<br /> <br /> I mean..... one of the reasons Lord Iysen gave him this is <i>specifically because</i> he can't really fight, not for right now. The 3-month moratorium on fighting due to his concussion still isn't up, either. (Which reminds me; he still has to go back to Teriake Castle in 3 months to get checked out).<br /> <br /> Of course, if shit were to hit the fan, he would still be <i>capable</i> of combat, albeit not that well (in-game, he would likely have some hefty penalties, probably -2). He doesn't have armor either. Yet another thing he has to spend money on.<br /> <br /> Again, however, I do like the way you think, and I wish more people did write-ins. If people don't do write-ins, and choose what I put down, then I basically do what I 'want'. And what I want is 'boring'.<br /> <br /> See, I never would have thought about your plan, for example.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11892,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","user":"Boston123","date":". 06-03-2017, 03:54 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-03T17:02:30-07:00","id":1099957,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Boston123</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1099871#post1099871\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> That might be the biggest problem with this plan.<br /> <br /> While Kasai has the casts off his broken arm/fingers, they are still recovering. He has trouble gripping chopsticks or a spoon for any appreciable length of time, much less a weapon clashing and slashing about. There are 'physical therapy&quot;-type things he can, and is, doing, but ..... still. Recovering.<br /> <br /> Plus, his ribs are still broken-ish. He needs help getting out of bed in the morning, for reference (bending at the waist and twisting is difficult/painful)<br /> <br /> Finally, he still needs a cane to walk around. On flat ground, he doesn't <i>need</i> it, but it is necessary going up or down inclines/stairs. Its going to be like that for years, at the very least.<br /> <br /> I mean..... one of the reasons Lord Iysen gave him this is <i>specifically because</i> he can't really fight, not for right now. The 3-month moratorium on fighting due to his concussion still isn't up, either. (Which reminds me; he still has to go back to Teriake Castle in 3 months to get checked out).<br /> <br /> Of course, if shit were to hit the fan, he would still be <i>capable</i> of combat, albeit not that well (in-game, he would likely have some hefty penalties, probably -2). He doesn't have armor either. Yet another thing he has to spend money on.<br /> <br /> Again, however, I do like the way you think, and I wish more people did write-ins. If people don't do write-ins, and choose what I put down, then I basically do what I 'want'. And what I want is 'boring'.<br /> <br /> See, I never would have thought about your plan, for example.</div> </div> </div> </div>Hm. Doubt they're gonna take us on as a bandit if we're not in fighting form, just one more mouth to feed.<br /> <br /> I still like the &quot;infiltrate, scout, join up, and then offer an alliance&quot; option. I just don't like the thought of risking one of our small number of competent and loyal minions on it...but hey, that's what minions are for. We ought to ask if one of them is okay going on that mission. It's a big risk...but it's potentially also a big payoff.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>So I'm making <a href=\"http://godkickingboot.smackjeeves.com/\" target=\"_blank\">God-Kicking Boot</a>, an Exalted webcomic, now. Updates on Sundays. Full-color, mediocre but slowly improving art. It's a thing.<br /> <br /> The absence of a monument can, in its own way, be something of a monument also.</i> -Roger Zelazny","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/512-wise-old-guru","name":"Wise Old Guru","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar512_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1097"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-03T17:21:58-07:00","id":1099961,"title":"","content":"We need a Chancellor, so very much...<br /> <br /> The Bandit-Lord needs to be dealt with, as do the Tribes. The other things can wait a <b>little</b> while, though we can send a messenger the Architect now. Doing so might be seen as a slight snub to the nobles awaiting our reply to their dinner invitations ('you replied to the commoner but not us'). I think we can get away with it but it had to be said.<br /> <br /> We could send a runner requesting a meeting with the Tribal Elders while we try to deal with bandit problem, so that at least something is being done stop that issue getting worse while we deal with the others.<br /> <br /> As for how to deal with the bandits...<br /> <br /> Compromise between Wise Old Guru and semicasual?<br /> Gather our forces and approach their stronghold; don't start a fight and call for a parlay. Either this &quot;Lord&quot; will see us or not, though the show of strength should put some pressure on him. If the shit hits the fan, WE HAVE AN ARMY backing us up. Heck at the very least we can offer the same rewards for military service as anyone else in addition to clemency for their crimes. At least some of the bandits will be interested.<br /> Since this individual is supposed to be Oathsworn, any chance we could find out who they are before we march on them? Rumours, description, first appearance in the area, histories of the families, etc?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Thoughts ripple out, birthing others","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1624-falleneco","name":"FallenEco","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1624_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1927"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-03T17:52:46-07:00","id":1099968,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>FallenEco</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1099961#post1099961\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> Since this individual is supposed to be Oathsworn, any chance we could find out who they are before we march on them? Rumours, description, first appearance in the area, histories of the families, etc?</div> </div> </div> </div>From me, or in story?<br /> <br /> In-story, nobody knows very much. It isn't like he personally rides into the villages he extorts food from. What people do know is what the bandits tell them.<br /> <br /> 1) Rumors:<br /> <br /> It is suspected that he is from outside the region, as none of the noble familes from Jianghu have, or know, anybody that go/went by the name Masanori. Masanori is his given name, too, so nobody knows what,if anything, his clan-name is.<br /> <br /> 2) Description.<br /> <br /> Nobody knows. Only the bandits have seen him, and even they don't see him all that often. He works through their gang-bosses.<br /> <br /> 3) Appearance in the area:<br /> <br /> Concrete evidence of his actions came to light about a year ago, although in hindsight it is evident he has been operating in Jianghu for at least a few years.<br /> <br /> 4) MO<br /> <br /> He......well, actually he is pretty well-behaved, as well as bandit-lords go. Sure, sure, he extorts food from the farms on the boundaries of the Akibara forest, but it isn't all that much more than what the food-rent already is, and the bandits are comparatively well-behaved (no rape and murder, and what has occurred was dealt with harshly).<br /> <br /> Asides from extorting food, Masanori and his goons spend most of their time attacking other bandits, which is a blessing in disguise. If a bandit-gang doesn't start following him and doing what he says, he wipes them out, and Masanori's bandits spend as much time fighting other bandits as they do extorting stuff from the peasants. Bandit attacks in general (except for Masanori's crew, of course) along the fringes of the Akibara forest have dropped precipitously in frequency and intensity.<br /> <br /> If anything, and even Kasai's Oathsworn vassals admit this: he is technically doing the same thing any landlord does, that is, protecting farmers in return for food. The <u>main</u> problem is that he is doing it illegally, while Oathsworn retainers are given the legal right to do so. Plus, he is affiliated with bandits and other scum-of-the-earth as opposed to &quot;proper&quot; Men-at-Arms, but what-have-you.<br /> <br /> The commoners are more pissed they are paying taxes/land-rent &quot;twice&quot; than anything else. They fought back initially, but after Masanori's bandits hung those who resisted, they started paying. They also sent word to Akio Masumi (who did fuck-all, as one might expect), and to the Oathsworn landlords, but the Oathsworn technically aren't supposed to do anything, as the land is legally Kasai's.<br /> <br /> 5) Base of Operations<br /> <br /> It is suspected that he is operating out of an old Hill-Tribe ringfort, Dun Nashuon, itself located in the Toge hills, in the middle of the Akibara forest. The place has been a haven for neer-do-wells for years, and it is &quot;close', relatively speaking, to the farmsteads that have been imposed upon, which means it only makes sense for him to be there. While the place is largely ruined, it <b><u><i>is</i></u></b> a fortification in rough terrain, which means it would be comparatively difficult to assault. And even bandits can hold ground when it is on a hill a hundred feet tall.<br /> <br /> There are no roads to Dun Nashuon, and the region is heavy forest, so it is going to be a pain in the <u>ass</u> getting an army out there. Bandits would be able to conduct hit-and-run attacks with ease on our slowly-moving regiments. By comparison, that would be the terrain the Hill-Tribes <u>excel</u> in fighting in......<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11892,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","user":"Boston123","date":". 06-03-2017, 09:23 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-03T18:46:34-07:00","id":1099973,"title":"","content":"On an aside, Kasai's name and title in Dragontongue would be something like: <i>Koshaku Qianke Kasai, Kugo-Qianke, Daimyo Jianghu-han</i><br /> <br /> meaning, roughly, Marcher-Lord (or, Duke) Kasai Qianke, founder (or, member of the founding family) of the Qianke clan, ruler of the Jianghu domain<br /> <br /> In Riverspeak, the given name comes first and the family name comes last, hence Kasai Qianke or Iysen Shiba.<br /> <br /> In Dragontongue (or High-and-Low Realm, which Dragontongue is largely derived from), the family name comes first and the given name goes last, so <i>Qianke Kasai </i> or <i>Shiba Iysen</i>.<br /> <br /> In formal events and soirees, nobles are probably going to be introduced in Dragontongue. 99.99999% of commoners don't speak Dragontongue (Riverspeak has 36 characters, while Dragontongue has hundreds, if not thousands), so most nobles go by the Riverspeak version of their names. Hell, most nobles only 'know' Dragontongue with it's importance to the arts. In most places, it isn't a 'conversational&quot; language, but the language of calligraphy, poetry, high-literature, etc etc etc.<br /> <br /> Kasai can read (and write) Dragontongue, as well as speak it, but he is rusty in both.<br /> <br /> High Realm: Traditional Chinese + Classical Latin<br /> Low Realm: Simplified Chinese + Vulgar Latin, well on its way to becoming proto/psuedo not!Romance languages<br /> Dragontongue: uberintricate Japanese Kanji + flowery Elizabethan English, like something from Shakespeare. Lots of thee's, thou's and forasmuch'es","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11892,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","user":"Boston123","date":". 06-03-2017, 10:12 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-03T20:49:27-07:00","id":1099999,"title":"","content":"Ok...so if we can get to talking Masanori, odds are decent that they would at least willing to listen to us. Bad news, we can't just march on and call for an audience...<br /> Can we try to figure out which village will get hit next? Is there a pattern to their 'attacks'? If we can get ahead, we might have small contingent of men at arms there to deliver a letter and facilitate the peaceful transfer of goods (said village would be excused from their next 'rent' to us, rather than have to pay twice.)<br /> <br /> We can't always be stupid!brave...we have people counting on us.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Thoughts ripple out, birthing others","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1624-falleneco","name":"FallenEco","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1624_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1927"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-03T21:37:00-07:00","id":1100005,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>FallenEco</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1099999#post1099999\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Ok...so if we can get to talking Masanori, odds are decent that they would at least willing to listen to us. Bad news, we can't just march on and call for an audience...<br /> Can we try to figure out which village will get hit next? Is there a pattern to their 'attacks'? If we can get ahead, we might have small contingent of men at arms there to deliver a letter and facilitate the peaceful transfer of goods (said village would be excused from their next 'rent' to us, rather than have to pay twice.)<br /> <br /> We can't always be stupid!brave...we have people counting on us.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> There is only one settlement of appreciable size in Jianghu; Liangzhou, which has 300 people. Everyone else lives on scattered farms and homesteads. There are conglomerations of homesteads, usually a commoner-clan-holding, but they are more 'farms close together&quot; than an actual village.<br /> <br /> As for if there is a pattern...... hmmmm. -flips through notes- Not...... really? There is a decent amount of farms along the borders of the Akibara, and it isn't like Masanori is hitting them west-to-east or something like that. Crackdowns usually happen when the harvests are done (In Jianghu, there are generally three harvests a year: late spring, later summer and late autumn), and if a farmstead has already been extorted, Masanori shows the remarkable foresight and mercy of not taking stuff again, at least not until next harvest.<br /> <br /> We could just leave letters with the farms along the border of the Akibara, with instructions to give them to the bandits when they appear. Exempting the farms from taxes or land-rent for a single harvest won't impact the economy long-term.<br /> <br /> Don't forget: the Men-at-Arms of Jianghu, and all the Hundred Kingdoms, are farmers first and soldiers second. &quot;Campaign season&quot; is only 60 or so days long for a reason. We can't just leave forces in a single spot for an appreciable length of time.<br /> <br /> Or....... don't forget, with Kasai's new method of recruiting, the soldiers will serve for 5 years before being granted territory. That is 5 years Kasai could have -X- amounts of soldiers in the field, basically <u>year round</u>. Of course, it would be expensive, but the idea is that the soldiers would &quot;pay for themselves&quot; in but a few years, by both destroying bandits, building roads, clearing land, that sort of thing.<br /> <br /> Of course, Kasai doesn't have these new recruits yet.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-04T12:57:30-07:00","id":1100192,"title":"","content":"I am not opposed to recruiting this bandit if possible, but we should deal with him ASAP in any case.<br /> <br /> And we should definitely invite the other noble families to visit. The architect, too.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>On the frontier of the Wild South, there's only one woman with the grit to take on its most dangerous outlaws and bring them <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/984977-back-alive-or-maybe-dead-quest\" target=\"_blank\">Back Alive, or Maybe Dead.</a><br /> <br /> Avatar by <a href=\"http://puzzlinghappenstance.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">K.S. Brenowitz</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/908-semicasual","name":"semicasual","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar908_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2499"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-04T18:09:39-07:00","id":1100282,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>semicasual</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1100192#post1100192\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I am not opposed to recruiting this bandit if possible, but we should deal with him ASAP in any case.<br /> <br /> And we should definitely invite the other noble families to visit. The architect, too.</div> </div> </div> </div>We are not fit to host such a gathering yet. It would guarantee negotiating the marriage from a disadvantaged position assuming they would still wish to do so. Remember how we had to clean the place for our retainers visiting and how we barely got it presentable to them? We haven't even started on the outside yet. We don't really have the required food stocked for a fancy dinner or the facilities for guests to stay the night. We could only offer a cot for 'our' Oathsworn.<br /> <br /> We just aren't ready to be a host yet and the preparations would eat up too much time while other things are going on.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Thoughts ripple out, birthing others","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1624-falleneco","name":"FallenEco","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1624_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1927"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-04T18:35:58-07:00","id":1100288,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>FallenEco</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1100282#post1100282\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> We are not fit to host such a gathering yet. It would guarantee negotiating the marriage from a disadvantaged position assuming they would still wish to do so. Remember how we had to clean the place for our retainers visiting and how we barely got it presentable to them? We haven't even started on the outside yet. We don't really have the required food stocked for a fancy dinner or the facilities for guests to stay the night. We could only offer a cot for 'our' Oathsworn.<br /> <br /> We just aren't ready to be a host yet and the preparations would eat up too much time while other things are going on.</div> </div> </div> </div>Er..... just to be particular, but most people in the Hundred Kingdoms don't even sleep on cots. <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Futon\" target=\"_blank\">Futons</a>, really. Actual beds that we would recognize are rare, and usually only used by the wealthy, and even then they are just futons lifted off the ground on a wooden frame, to allow air circulation and keep dirt/dust off the thing<br /> <br /> 99.999% of people sleep on the floor, even nobility. The material that goes into a futon/pallet depends on wealth. Commoners will use straw mats or mattresses, while low-nobility will use mattresses stuffed with wool or cotton. High nobility will sleep on mattresses stuffed with down or somesuch. Blankets are universal.<br /> <br /> Yagura Castle also does indeed have &quot;guest-rooms&quot; (although they are really secondary bedrooms in general, not just for guests), and they aren't <i>shitty</i> by any standard. They are about what Kasai had back at Teriake, maybe a little better. Wood floors, covered with rugs and mats, lath-and-plaster whitewashed walls, comfortably furnished, with windows that look out on the surroundings.<br /> <br /> There are 6 guest-rooms total, with 4 open. Plenty of room, so long as Kasai doesn't invite every prospective family at once.<br /> <br /> You do have a point about the fancy-dinner-stuff, although that varies. It isn't like it would be <i>that </i>hard to procure supplies: 99% of the food for a dinner could come from Kasai's farms, same with alcohol, the only things that would need to be bought from 'outside' is stuff like tea (which would be all-but necessary) and wine.<br /> <br /> In the Hundred Kingdoms, the food nobility eats isn't <i>that</i> different from the commoners, just ...... of a higher quality, and of more refined ingredients. Think white wheat bread and noodles, instead of darker barley and oats. Beef and pork, instead of chicken or fish (although nobility eats that as well. nobles just eat more meat in general). So on and so forth.<br /> <br /> And it isn't like the prospective families don't live in similar environments or situations. While they and Kasai are of the same social rank, he actually technically holds more land than they do, and the climate is roughly the same in Yan and Chen as it is in Jianghu. Only the Wuyi Shiba's hold land close to the Red River, and even then, they are just going to think it is cold more than anything else (on top of them being actually pretty poor)<br /> <br /> Finally..... Kasai does have servants for stuff like this. It isn't like he is cooking his own meals anymore. He can also hire help as needed from the homesteads around Yagura.<br /> <br /> The prospective families are going to be more taken-aback by his asking them to come to him, as opposed to the facilities he offers. Traditonally, grooms go to the clan of the bride, not vice-versa.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11892,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","user":"Boston123","date":". 06-04-2017, 09:38 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-05T16:10:51-07:00","id":1100630,"title":"","content":"I got a number of opinions so hold on to your butts everybody<br /> <div class=\"videocontainer with-aspect-ratio videocontainer-horizontal\" style=\"aspect-ratio: 16/9;\"> <a class=\"video-frame\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-W6as8oVcuM\" data-code=\"-W6as8oVcuM\" data-provider=\"youtube\" data-height=\"0\" data-width=\"0\" itemprop=\"video\"> <div class=\"videothumbnail\" style=\"\"> <img src=\"https://img.youtube.com/vi/-W6as8oVcuM/hqdefault.jpg\" alt=\"\" /> <div class=\"videothumbnailoverlay\"></div> </div> </a> </div><br /> <br /> 1. (Primary Priority) We should deal with the bandits but in a manner similar to what wise old guru said. I would recommend the following:<br /> A. Soften the bandit lords power by giving the rank and file bandits the opportunity at receiving amnesty. We do this by going to our local settlements/farmsteads/villages/etc (giving us the chance to survey our new land) and spreading the message that everyone can get on the right side of the law by leaving the bandits and joining our local military. We also spread the message about hill tribe settlement and that any bandit that seeks amnesty will have your protection. Give them the same deal we'd give anyone hoping to settle their own land. If they are local, then the social pressure from their moms/wives/brothers/neighbors/etc as well as the hope they can feed themselves while being on the right side of the law might be enough to push them to right action <br /> B. We put a dead line of how long people have to consider the amnesty. Something a little bit further out so that the bandits have time to deliberate among themselves about the possibility. Maybe a couple weeks to a month or so? If it is successful, i'd assume the bandit lord would come to us either trying to kill us or by seeking some sort of parlay.<br /> C. (optional) we could include missives and messengers to said &quot;lord&quot; stating that he would be allowed to join as an advisor or some other position of importance if he were willing to fold his forces into ours. Obviously we should send someone who is well liked enough by the locals that just killing this person out right would be really bad for our adversary.<br /> <br /> (Of secondary importance)<br /> 2. We should seek to have our possible brides from Chen and Yan come to see us. We should use the excuse of newly gained lands and a time of crisis and ask their understanding. I dont remember if either one is warring with us or is neutral currently but obviously entertaining those families and forcing them to travel from their lands would stall any possibility of them entertaining the thought of joining our enemies.<br /> <br /> We should also have letters written to our neighbor marcher lords with an invitation for some date not in consideration for the lords of chen and yan to come stay with us in the hopes of building good relationships with our neighbors. <br /> <br /> 3. Invite the road builder. Maybe we can sic an underling to deal with this? Maybe the older oathsworn/man at arms who has grandchildren older than us? <br /> <br /> 4. I'd say blue pines can go to teriake town on our behalf for weapons. <br /> <br /> I love this quest<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8897-joshopotamus","name":"joshopotamus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8897_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"97"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-05T16:13:33-07:00","id":1100632,"title":"","content":"I was thinking we should give our retainers more responsibility, sending blue pines makes sense, with guards of course.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11567-jairain","name":"Jairain","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11567_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"345"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-05T17:39:41-07:00","id":1100656,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>joshopotamus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1100630#post1100630\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">-snip-<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>1A) Many of the bandits from any/every domain <u>won't</u> be local. Some of them will, but most bandits are usually deserted soldiers from the losing side of a war. Jianghu is infested with the detritus largely because 1- other domains force them here, and 2- local authority was weak<br /> <br /> 1B) The offer of &quot;land for military service&quot; is going to be open for at least a couple of months, if not a year, from sheer necessity, and probably longer. Remember: Creation is a world overwhelmingly-without fast, let alone instant communication or travel. It isn't like Kasai can call people up over the telephone and order things. It takes time to travel: It takes at least 2 weeks to get from Yagura Castle in Jianghu to Teriake Castle, and that is using the post-roads, which 'normal' people aren't allowed to do. Now, if we give someone either the seal of Jianghu (not fucking likely. Kasai isn't letting that thing out of his sight. Most Lords wear their seals as rings or necklaces), or a letter stamped with said seal, they can pass through the post-offices with no issues.<br /> <br /> Other than that, prospective soldiers interested in the land-grants are going to take longer to get here, since they aren't legally allowed to use the post-roads. Since most of them aren't going to be mounted, or ride on a wagon, you would be safe in assuming a month in travel time.<br /> <br /> I'll be straight: due to the means of recruitment you folks chose, we are almost certainly going to end up with more than 1000 soldiers. Which is a <u>good thing</u>. The two Xian vassals closest to Jianghu have about 3000 apiece, and we want to at least try to match that.<br /> <br /> 1C) A possibility<br /> <br /> 2) Also a good idea.<br /> <br /> Technically, the only realm Jianghu is warring with is Yue, and that is because Kasai's liegelord (Iysen Shiba, aka Xia) is warring with them. Xia is, right now, at peace with Chen, Yan, Sui, Dai and Yang, although that probably won't last as soon as 'real&quot; war breaks out between Yue and Xia. Sides will be picked, lines will be drawn.<br /> <br /> Feasts are a general thing that happens across the Hundred Kingdoms, and is one of the main ways vassals actually meet each other. Other than that, they tend to be too busy. The procedure for having a feast is similar to the procedure for courting: the host sends out invitations, people show up, the party gets started.<br /> <br /> They can get <b><u><i>expensive</i></u></b>though; many nobles use feasts and soirees to show off their wealth, and many go overboard. To not have a lavish feast is worse than not having one at all.<br /> <br /> 3) If you are sending someone down to Teriake to commission weapons, why not just scoop them up at the same time? Save a trip, kill two birds with one stone.<br /> <br /> 4) That works, but Blue-Pines is, at best, only semi-literate (she can read and write her name, as well as a few other words, but not much else), so Kasai would have to send someone that can actually read. She <i>does</i> have an in with the guild we would order weapons from, however.<br /> <br /> Also, don't forget: we still have to at least respond to the fact that the Tribes are poking around in the northern parts of Jianghu, activities that were<i> expressly</i> forbidden by the peace accords. Now, it is almost certainly just kids being kids (&quot;oooh, wat's dat!?&quot;), but still.<br /> <br /> Most of the warriors of the Tribes, at least the Tribes that are helping Xia in the war vs Yue, are on their way down to the Xia/Yue border, to link up with Lord Iysens forces (and <u>scaring the shit </u>out of everyone they come across, but nevermind that), meaning most of the people moving into the hinterlands of Jianghu are noncombatants. Still, there are some younger warriors (think kids in their mid-teens) who are bored and stuff.<br /> <br /> As Lord Jianghu, Kasai is in charge of keeping the peace between the Tribes and the people already living in Jianghu. The buck stops at him, basically.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11892,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","user":"Boston123","date":". 06-05-2017, 08:42 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-05T17:55:18-07:00","id":1100661,"title":"","content":"Thoughts to your response:<br /> 1A - Still spread message to town and whatever major population centers exist? MIght be useful as bandits might send infiltrators to town in the guise of travelers from time to time for supplies/rumors/what have you?<br /> <br /> 1B - I think often our problem is communication as im not very exact in the language i use and should have stated that amnesty and military service for bandits should exist for a limited time to force them to make a decision quickly? After that grace period we move on them. Military service would obviously be open to general public for a longer period of time.<br /> <br /> 2 - i assume we dont know any kingdoms particular leaning?<br /> <br /> 3 - Noted and a reasonable suggestion. I say lets do that<br /> <br /> 4 - Also noted and agreed on needing a literate person. Find a reasonably trust worthy person to send with blue pines is my vote. As was stated by someone else i dont have a large memory or knowledge of named characters living here yet to immediately make appointments.<br /> <br /> The hill tribes i have no idea how to deal with until we actually have grandpa around honestly outside of sending some guards to make occasional patrols?<br /> <br /> Thanks for your patience and clarification.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8897-joshopotamus","name":"joshopotamus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8897_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"97"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-05T18:00:05-07:00","id":1100662,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>joshopotamus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1100661#post1100661\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Thoughts to your response:<br /> 1A - Still spread message to town and whatever major population centers exist? MIght be useful as bandits might send infiltrators to town in the guise of travelers from time to time for supplies/rumors/what have you?<br /> <br /> 1B - I think often our problem is communication as im not very exact in the language i use and should have stated that amnesty and military service for bandits should exist for a limited time to force them to make a decision quickly? After that grace period we move on them. Military service would obviously be open to general public for a longer period of time.<br /> <br /> 2 - i assume we dont know any kingdoms particular leaning?<br /> <br /> 3 - Noted and a reasonable suggestion. I say lets do that<br /> <br /> 4 - Also noted and agreed on needing a literate person. Find a reasonably trust worthy person to send with blue pines is my vote. As was stated by someone else i dont have a large memory or knowledge of named characters living here yet to immediately make appointments.<br /> <br /> The hill tribes i have no idea how to deal with until we actually have grandpa around honestly outside of sending some guards to make occasional patrols?<br /> <br /> Thanks for your patience and clarification.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> 1a- messengers are probably better. Generally, only Peasants will be literate.<br /> <br /> 1b- Ah, gotcha<br /> <br /> 2- Not really. Most realms, unless already allied, will play their cards close to their chests. Loyalties are generally discovered (by outside parties, at least. Communication between realms happens) either on the battlefield, or when troops start marching.<br /> <br /> However, since Yan and Chen sent letters to Jianghu, you could be safe in assuming they aren't going to attack when our pants are down, and are at least interested in alliance. If they weren't, they wouldn't have sent anything at all.<br /> <br /> As for the Tribes, Kasai basically needs to talk to the Tribal elders, who will handle things from that point. However, as far as he knows, only he and Shin Takeshi (the old Oathsworn retainer who did all the talking) actually speak Forestongue.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11892,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","user":"Boston123","date":". 06-05-2017, 09:07 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-10T20:20:47-07:00","id":1102420,"title":"","content":"Ok. It appears most of you want to deal with Masanori the bandit-king first. However, many of you brought up the idea of talking with him first, as opposed to marching in and taking names.<br /> <br /> Interesting<br /> <br /> -------------------------------<br /> <br /> After sleeping on it, you decide that focusing your efforts on this bandit-lord Masanori would be the most worthwhile use of time and energy. It would solidify your position while giving you an opportunity to view your troops in combat, <i>and</i> make Jianghu safer in the long run.<br /> <br /> After all, you can\u2019t really attract new settlers, or expand your holdings, if they can\u2019t have at least a semblance of security. But\u2026.. how to deal with the bandit-king?<br /> <br /> From what it sounds like (and it isn\u2019t much), Masanori already has \u2018several hundred\u201d bandits following him, as well as a comparatively-well-fortified position. You only have several hundred troops (professional troops, that is) yourself, so you won\u2019t have the benefit of numbers, which is something you understand, even as unskilled in warmaking as you are. Add in the benefits of a fortified position, and he could very well have effectively <i>more</i> soldiers than you do\u2026\u2026 and the longer you wait, the more bandits and neer-do-wells will likely be attracted to his banner.<br /> <br /> No, you don\u2019t have time to wait, not for prospective recruits to be trained, much less show up. You have to deal with this problem <i>now</i>, before it has the opportunity to grow any more. But the question still remains of how to approach the issue\u2026.<br /> <br /> A direct assault on Dun Nashuon is largely out of the picture. Even you know that marching a bunch of men through difficult terrain, much less the home terrain of the opposing force, is a <i>bad idea</i>. Hit-and-run attacks on your army will cut it to pieces long before you reach the hillfort, and you need numbers in order to run an effective siege. Coupled with that: logistics. There are no roads to Dun Nashuon, and your troops would have to carry everything with them: food, materiel, etc. That would slow down the marching even more, and make them more vulnerable to skirmishers.<br /> <br /> Of course, you could call up your new militia, and have them cut a road through the Akibara while your Men-at-Arms provide security\u2026.. but that would take a <i>long</i> time, time that you probably don\u2019t really have. Summer is halfway over, leaving you with only about 4 weeks for meaningful military maneuvers, and it will take at least a week to gather the army as it is. As much as you want this bandit-problem dealt with, it probably isn\u2019t worth having crop failures and angry peasants. You just got here, you don\u2019t want to mess up that badly just yet.<br /> <br /> However\u2026.. you shouldn\u2019t really leave such a strong defensive position \u2018out in the open\u2019, so to speak. At the very least, you shouldn\u2019t just \u2018let\u201d the place be occupied by bandits. You are going to have to take the point <i>eventually</i>, preferably before it gets built up any more, but how?<br /> <br /> You could\u2026. call in the Tribes? While you don\u2019t have any actual authority over them, they <i>are</i> messing with your people, actions which were clearly proscribed in the peace-treaties. In return for you \u2026..\u2019overlooking\u2019 those actions, they could help you out in return. While the forests of Akibara would be a death-trap to your own troops, it is the territory the Tribes excel in fighting in. They would have <i>no</i> problem engaging, much less wiping the floor with, the bandits. Plus, the fact that the bandits are occupying an ancient Hill-Tribe ringfort would be reason enough for the Tribes to call for blood. Tribal ringforts are/were almost-always \u2018multi-purpose\u201d structures. Defensive strongpoints, yes, but also the seat of tribal chief-kings, manufacturing centers, religious sites\u2026.. and burial grounds. You would be willing to bet a substantial supply of silver that the bandits in Dun Nashuon are walking about but a few feet over the head of untold numbers of buried barrows. And the desecration of burial grounds is a <i>big</i> \u201cno-no\u201d in Hill-Tribe culture (and probably in many cultures, come to think of it), especially by the Vanehani. During the establishment of the Hundred Kingdoms, the Vanehani often went out of their way to destroy Tribal burial grounds and sacred spaces. Of course, the Tribes would probably want Dun Nashuon back in their hands, if that were the case\u2026\u2026<br /> <br /> You sigh, rubbing at your temples with the heels of your palms. It is still before 9-Bells, but you already have the beginnings of a massive headache brewing. While some of that may be the results of your concussion, you are certain most of the headache stems from stress. You haven\u2019t been sleeping <i>or</i> eating well (having half your teeth knocked out makes eating painful, and you haven\u2019t had much of an appetite anyways) since you got to Jianghu, and you are fairly certain you have lost weight. On top of that\u2026\u2026 your hair is falling out. It does that <i>anyways</i>, of course, molting in spring and autumn to make way for warm or cold-weather coats, but not in <i>summer</i>. Nevertheless, you keep pulling mats of hair from your head, twisted tangles of tan and grey hairs that itch until they are removed.<br /> <br /> Pulling out another chunk, you examine it sullenly before flicking it out the window. At this rate, you are going to be bald by years end.<br /> <br /> No, the \u2018best\u2019 choice is to take out Masanori directly. However, according to both your retainers and the reports of the peasants, Masanori never actually directly interacts with the people he robs, instead acting through go-betweens and subordinates. Hell, nobody even really knows what the man looks like! In order to assassinate someone, you generally have to have an idea of who the target is\u2026..<br /> <br /> On the other hand, his actions do not sound very \u201cbandit-y\u201d, for a lack of a better term. Most bandits are out for loot and excitement, and when they lean on villages and such for supplies, they tend to be \u201call or nothing\u201d affairs, coupled with bloodshed and mayhem. Masanori\u2019s crew, on the other hand, has been comparatively well-behaved, only taking what the farmers can spare and treating them with relative grace and gentility, with what boorish behavior that has occurred being punished (brutally, apparently) in short order. Maybe the man has a sense of honor?<br /> At the very least, it is readily apparent, even to you, that the bandit-king views the farms along the edges of the Akibara forest as \u2018his\u2019. Asides from regularly extorting food from the farmsteads, he also protects them from other bandits. If anything can be said about his actions, it is that he is doing a pretty good job of it: bandit attacks, other than Masanori\u2019s crew of course, along the reaches of the Akibara have declined precipitously. The farmsteads report that they haven\u2019t seen bandits other than Masanori\u2019s in <i>months</i>.<br /> <br /> Generally, most bandits take poorly to organized leadership, even the ones that stem from army deserters. If Masanori is able to beat not one, but multiple bandit gangs into compliance, what does that tell you about his organizational abilities, or his force of charisma? Bandits\u2026.. tend to not \u201cplay well\u201d with others, especially other bands that intrude on their turf. If this guy was able to get several bands to work together towards a common goal\u2026\u2026<br /> <br /> \u2026. He sounds like someone you would like as a vassal, no lie. Leadership ability, apparent motivation towards gaining authority, the only thing you don\u2019t quite recognize is his warmaking skills\u2026. And you suppose you are going to see them soon enough. If he could be recruited, and is actually loyal, you could get not only an effective ally, but someone who knows the bandit-situation in Jianghu inside and out. Regardless, he is <i>dangerous</i>, and should be removed from the situation in one way or the other.<br /> <br /> \u2026. You are gonna have to meet the guy to make any sort of decision along those lines. Of course, he actually has to be made aware of a meeting, as well as actually <i>want</i> to meet with you in the first place\u2026..<br /> <br /> You rest your head on the desk for a moment, trying to collect your thoughts, then arduously begin writing various letters. This takes up most of the morning, and it is well after noontime before you call Blue-Pines and several of your guardsmen into your office.<br /> <br /> Turing to Blue-Pines, you hold out the first of many letters.<br /> <br /> \u201cBlue-Pines, you have a very important job, with many aspects. Firstly, I want you to travel down to Teriake Castle-Town and place an order for bulk armaments. Secondly, along the way, I want you to pass along the word about my recruitment rally going on, here in Jianghu. Try to get some people to come up here. Then finally, I want you to scoop up the architect Satoshi Azuchi, from the architects guild. He is apparently supposed to come up here and build roads and suchlike. \u201c<br /> <br /> \u201cFor expenses and the ordering of armaments, I am giving you two Dirham in silver. Try to focus on cheaper stuff like spears and shields, but if you can order some helmets or arming jackets too, that would be nice. I would love some crossbows, but they tend to be expensive. Other than that, buy what you think is best.\u201d<br /> <br /> She nods and takes the letter, \u201cWhat is this mark, right here?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cThat is my seal, as Lord Jianghu. That will get you through any post-stations without much hassle. Take the cart and some horses, along with two of the guardsmen, you will make good time.\u201d, and she nods again, stepping back.<br /> <br /> You turn to the rest of the guardsmen, \u201cThe rest of you are to ride across Jianghu, spreading the word about mustering. I want the army, both the Men-at-Arms and the new militia, to muster at the market-village of Liangzhou as soon as possible. Along the way, drop off these letters at the farmsteads along the margins of the Akibara forests, with instructions to give them to the bandits when they appear. Any and all farms being extorted are freed from the next harvest-rent, until this bandit situation is resolved.\u201d<br /> <br /> Turning to the final guardsman, \u201cAnd you are to travel directly to the estate of Shin Takeshi. I am asking him totake this letter north, to the settling Hill-Tribes, and present it to their elder. The letter has other instructions for him, as well.\u201d<br /> <br /> Sitting back, \u201call right, let\u2019s get to work. We all have plenty of work to do\u2026\u201d<br /> <br /> And so it begins. After a few days of waiting for the letters to be delivered, you head out, travelling west downriver towards Lianghzhou. Due to your stopping at the manor of every retainer you pass, it takes a little over a week to make it to the outskirts of town. You are pleased to note that a decent proportion of your Men-at-Arms and militiamen are already encamped on the outskirts of town, drilling under the watchful eye of Sergeant-Major Oxblood. Even from your admittedly-unfamiliar perspective, the troops seem to be \u201cgetting in step\u201d rather quickly, even the militia, although they are far from ready for battle.<br /> <br /> In camp, you meet back up with several of your Oathsworn retainers, the ones you talked to on your way here. Gathered around a trestle-table set up in your encampment, you key them into the plan.<br /> <br /> Taking a deep breath, \u201cI am planning on meeting with Masanori here, in Liangzhou.\u201d<br /> <br /> The table explodes, and after a minute of chatter, Aya Cho interrupts with a wince, \u201cMy Lord, is \u2026. Is that wise?\u201d<br /> <br /> You shrug, \u201cI would rather meet with him, try to work out his motives, as opposed to charging into the Akibara with my dick swinging. I\u2019ve done enough of that for one life.\u201d<br /> <br /> Another Oathsworn asks, \u201cLord, what exactly do you mean, \u2018find out his motives\u2019?\u201d<br /> <br /> You sit back in your chair, \u201c Tell me, what do you think of his actions, ignoring the fact that he is a bandit? The fact that he is apparently protecting farmers, taking food in return for his protection? Punishing his own troops when they attack the farmers? So on and so forth. If you were in his position, what would you be doing?\u201d<br /> <br /> After a long moment, Aya chimes in, \u201c\u2026.. it sounds like he is trying to build a power base. Gain the support of the agricultural class, and prevent others from accessing it.\u201d<br /> <br /> You ask, although you already think you know the answer, \u201c Why would he be doing that?\u201d<br /> <br /> Slowly, she continues, \u201cHe wants to be a figure of authority, a <i>ruler</i>.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cMy thoughts exactly.\u201d Sitting forwards, \u201cNow, most Oathsworn nobles in general don\u2019t really lack other opportunities in life, to the point where they <i>have</i> to go live in the backwoods with a bunch of neer-do-wells, especially someone with potential or skills. Since Masanori is apparently quite good at organizing and leading troops, especially troops so defiant and misbehaved as your average bandit, that tells me he should theoretically be in high-demand as a retainer or even a vassal. Since he is most emphatically <i>not</i>, what does that tell me?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cSomething happened to prevent him from serving where he should.\u201d, adds another of your retainers.<br /> <br /> \u201cAgain, exactly. For some reason, Masanori was either never allowed to hold, or removed from, a position of power and authority, one he thought he should rightfully hold. I am guessing a failed coup-attempt that he escaped from, but any other event could have led to the loss of status. What it was is, for right now, largely irrelevant. What matters, however, is <i>what he cares about</i>. To me, he wants to hold a position of power\u2026\u2026 I am planning on offering him one.\u201d<br /> <br /> The table again explodes, and Shin Takeshi, who has just walked in, says with a sigh after offering a bow, \u201c\u2026. What did I just walk into, my Lord? Why, pray tell, are you offering vassalage to this bandit?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cI thought you weren\u2019t going to make it, sir Shin Takeshi. How did the meeting with the Tribes go?\u201d<br /> <br /> He shrugs, and after your gesture, takes a seat, \u201cAs far as I know, all they said was \u2018yes\u2019. They <i>did</i> get excited when I brought up your name however, as you instructed.\u201d<br /> <br /> Pleased, \u201cExcellent. Did you happen to catch the name of the Tribe?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cI believe they called themselves the Paskamansett, my Lord. Is that significant?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cQuite so. Their warleader and I fought together, so we have a good working relationship. Did they give you any indication as to when they would be available?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cNo, my Lord. All they said to me was, \u201cTell your chieftain it will be handled.\u201d I am guessing that refers to the raids on the northern lands?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cAnd then some.\u201d You pull out your map of Jianghu, with Akibara forest and the Toge hills in the center. \u201cAs one might expect, the war-methods of the Hundred Kingdoms are not suited to rough terrain, forests and hills the least of all. They break up regiments, allow for unceasing hit-and-run attacks, and deny effective logistics. I don\u2019t know about you, but I for one would <i>not</i> want to have to lead an army to lay siege against the fortress of Dun Nashuon, especially not with the little time we have before the summer harvest and autumn planting.\u201d<br /> <br /> Several of your retainers nod in assent, relief evident. Shin interjects, \u201cSo, Lord Kasai, what does this have to do with the Hill-Tribes?\u201d<br /> <br /> Smiling, \u201cI have just killed two birds with one stone, Takeshi. The raids on the northern farms are mainly because the younger hunters and warriors of the Tribes have little to do, as the married women of the Tribes are the ones to construct houses and farms. I have just given them \u2018things to do\u2019.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cFor every bandit-head they take and bring to me, I will give the warrior a spearhead of steel, or a number of sheet-iron arrowheads. Iron and steel are rare and expensive among the Tribes, and most younger warriors and hunters use weapons of bronze or even flint. Giving them weapons is arguably better than giving them money.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cAlso, they have agreed to patrol and harass the area around Dun Nashuon, as well as assisting when we actually get around to besieging it, in return for the fortress when it gets taken.\u201d<br /> <br /> Aya rests her face in her hands, \u201cYou\u2019ve outright given the Tribes a fortified stronghold, so close to our borders? Lord Kasai, I say this with all due respect, but \u2026\u2026 what were you thinking?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c Miss Aya Cho, the issues between our two peoples will never get resolved until we actually learn to <i>trust</i> each other. Fighting together on the field of battle is but one way to build that trust. The other is to, well, act neighborly. Dun Nashuon used to belong to the Tribes long ago, it holds part of their history and culture. By ceding it to them, I show that we respect them, and trust them enough to hold such a stronghold close to us. Honey catches more flies than vinegar, and treating the Tribes with respect will win us more favors than treating them with suspicion.\u201d<br /> <br /> She says nothing, but bows her head, in either acceptance or acquiescence.<br /> <br /> \u201cAnyways.\u201d, you continue with your earlier conversation.\u201d I am planning on meeting with the bandit-king Masanori tomorrow at midday, in the taphouse in Liangzhou. There, I plan on offering him a position by my side, with the potential for promotion.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cAgain, my Lord, why?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cHe can lead troops, effectively and in numbers. He doesn\u2019t sound like the average bandit, out for blood and plunder, and from what I am told, he might just have a shred of honor. Compared to many of the rulers in the Hundred Kingdoms, that is impressive, even more so for a bandit. Besides, he very well might have information on the other bandits operating in the region, information that makes taking them out faster and easier.\u201d You shrug, \u201cit is worth a shot, in my opinion.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cNow, I have been called \u2018overly-optimistic\u201d before, but that doesn\u2019t mean I am a <i>fool</i>. I fully expect Masanori, if he even shows up, to bring backup in case this meeting goes south. I am asking you to accompany me, <i>not overtly</i>, to the meeting, just in case.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cWhy not openly, Lord?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cSeeing a bunch of Oathsworn warriors might scare him off. Again: more flies with honey than vinegar, and I would at least like to talk to him, find out why he is here in Jianghu.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cWhat if he attacks, or doesn\u2019t even show up?\u201d<br /> <br /> You shrug, \u201cThen we kill him. Straightforward enough. And, if he doesn\u2019t show up, then we proceed with the muster-drills as planned. Either way, our bandit-problem here in Jianghu will be dealt with, one way or the other. Working with the Hill-Tribes has given us a bit of breathing room, and it is only a matter of time.\u201d<br /> <br /> Looking around at your retainers, \u201cAny questions? No? Then let us watch the Sergeant at work, and get involved if possible. We will all have to lead these soldiers, in some form or another, and being familiar with them may help on the battlefield.\u201d<br /> <br /> The next day, you walk over to the taphouse in Liangzhou. One of the largest buildings in town, three stories all told, it is hard to miss.<br /> <br /> You just hope this isn\u2019t a waste of time. You have plenty of other things to do, and wasting a day, let alone a week, wasn\u2019t one of them.<br /> <br /> The first thing that strikes you as soon as you walk onto the taproom floor is how <i>crowded</i> it is. Bargemen up from the docks, farmers and tradesmen in for a liquid lunch, your retainers in various clothes (who pointedly ignore you, as per your orders). There are several men you don\u2019t know (which isn\u2019t surprising at all), who take great interest in you as you walk in, unlike the rest of the patrons. If you intuition is right, these must be Masanori\u2019s men. You give one of the more obvious a two-fingered salute as you claim a corner table.<br /> <br /> As a waitress scurries over to take your order, you also notice that the ceiling is <i>very</i> low, barely over your head, and there are solid wooden pillars supporting the beams. Close quarters, tight spaces, using a sword would be awkward: important to know if violence is imminent. You check the hilt of your dagger, to make sure it is clear of obstruction\u2026..just in case.<br /> <br /> As you sip on a small glass of plum wine (also <i>very</i> good. One of the few good things you\u2019ve found in Jianghu has been the alcohol), you watch the door, occasionally casting your eyes about the room to see if you missed anyone. You aren\u2019t all that concerned about being spotted for who you are, that is <i>the</i> point.<br /> <br /> Nevertheless, <i>somehow</i>, a figure manages to swoop in while you are looking around. You don\u2019t jump (but you do curse silently), and the figure takes a seat across from you.<br /> <br /> \u201c\u2026.Aren\u2019t you a little young to be the Lord of Jianghu? If I wasn\u2019t told otherwise, I would have expected someone older.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cWho told you about me?\u201d, shifting your attention over to the man, who grins.<br /> <br /> \u201cAkio Masumi\u201d.<br /> <br /> It takes all the effort you can muster to not show your wrath. \u201cReally now? What <i>else</i> did he tell you about me?\u201d<br /> <br /> He shrugs, \u201cNot much. Asides from how you kicked him out of \u2018his\u2019 castle, and how he would help me get inside. He was too much of a blubbering idiot to say much more. I wouldn\u2019t worry about him; I slit his throat and threw his body in a ditch.\u201d<br /> <br /> You raise an eyebrow, \u201cEven if he could get you into Yagura Castle?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cI doubt it. Besides, it is the <i>principle</i> of the thing! I <i>despise</i> a traitor\u2026.. and besides, if he betrayed you, he would have betrayed me. It was the practical thing to do.\u201d<br /> <br /> You say nothing, taking the opportunity to study this bandit-king sitting across the table. Middling height (for a lowlander, at least, you\u2019ve got several inches on him), middling age: a face anywhere from late twenties to forties, with only the slight grey at the temples revealing his \u2018true\u2019 age, no scars, no disfiguring marks, no ostentatious clothing or jewelry, with only the straight sword by his side revealing his status.<br /> <br /> The man is entirely unremarkable. If you saw him twice in a crowd, you wouldn\u2019t recognize him\u2026\u2026 that is concerning.<br /> <br /> He reaches a hand across the table, and you take it in the old warrior\u2019s style, forearm to forearm.<br /> <br /> \u201cMasanori, formerly of clan Mitsuhide.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c Kasai Qianke, Lord of Jianghu.\u201d He grins again at this last statement.<br /> <br /> \u201cSo, my <i>Lord Jianghu</i>, why did you summon me here? What, exactly, could we talk about?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cTo be honest, I want to know more about the enigmatic figure everyone calls \u2018the bandit-lord of Jianghu\u2019\u201d<br /> <br /> Masanori laughs, then summons the waitress with a twitch of the finger. After she brings him his drink, he asks, \u201cWhat do you want to know? Where should I start? And, perhaps most importantly, <i>why should </i>I<i> tell you</i>?\u201d<br /> <br /> You shrug. \u201c\u2026..you impress me, if I may be blunt. Not many people can organize bandits into an effective fighting force, much less multiple groups, nor actually get them to behave in public. Akin to herding cats, or so I have been told. I wish to learn how you did it.\u201d<br /> <br /> He smirks, but continues talking, \u201cIt is a long story.\u201d He takes a sip of his drink, then looks out the doorway.<br /> <br /> \u201cClan Mitsuhide were, and always have been , the retainers of the clan Miso, who rule over the realm of Song, to the south of Ji. I was no different, and I was raised to be a warrior from a young age, eternally faithful to my liege-lords. And I was.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cAnything they wanted, I did. They were happy when their enemies died, so I killed them. I brought them enemy heads by the bucketful, then by the wheelbarrow-full. I stacked skull on top of skull, and they just asked for more. It got to the point where I couldn\u2019t kill enough to satisfy them with my own hands, so I ended up leading their armies. Small groups at first, single regiments, then more. Battle groups, armies, I led them all. Against bandits within Song, against their enemies in other realms, even in power plays against other members of the clan. I was the red right hand of Lord Sasaki Miso himself!\u201d He laughs, but there is no emotion in it.<br /> <br /> \u201cHell, I was even sent to go sneak into the houses of other families, to slit their throats while they slept. <i>And I did</i>. Not <i>once</i> did I complain, not once did I falter!\u201d His hands tighten on his cup, and his shifts into an ugly leer. \u201cAnd <i>not once</i> was I ever rewarded for what I did\u2026..\u201d<br /> <br /> He leans forwards, over the table, \u201cNot. Once. Not even a single word of encouragement, a word of thanks, from my lord. That was all I ever wanted, nothing much, not a castle, or a fief, just some form of thanks for steeping myself in blood for <i>his</i> sake. When he eventually, <i>finally</i>, summoned me into his throne room, I thought all my prayers had been answered\u2026. No such luck!\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cI was told that I was, and I quote, \u2018making him look inferior\u2019 through my actions, and that I was getting stripped of my rank as retainer. I was fired because I <i>made him look bad</i>.\u201d He laughs again, high and cold, causing many of the people in the taproom to look askance in your direction.<br /> <br /> He takes several deep breaths, sitting back in his chair. \u201cPardon me, that is still a rather sore spot\u2026\u2026.anyways, I told him that everything I did, I did on <i>his</i> command. He told me to be silent, insolent pretentious dog that I was, and to accept my punishment with whatever grace I had.\u201d He smirks again, \u201cI made him look worse after that.\u201d<br /> <br /> Quietly, \u201cSo the Miso clan is\u2026.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cNo more, at least the main family isn\u2019t.\u201d He dismisses the idea with a wave of his hand.<br /> <br /> <i>Dragons</i>, you think to yourself. By comparison, Lord Iysen has all-but given into your every demand\u2026. You should probably thank her the next time you see her.<br /> <br /> \u201cSo\u2026\u2026 how did you get here?\u201d<br /> <br /> Refilling his cup, \u201cWell, after I woke up from my madness fit in the house of Miso, I, well, I kind of panicked. I fled to the one place that was honor and family-bound to protect me: my ancestral clan. I\u2019ll give you three guesses as to what they did, and the first two won\u2019t count. \u201c<br /> <br /> You wince, \u201cThey refused you entry?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cWorse, they tried to arrest me, to hand me over to the infuriated Miso families, in order to save themselves. I had to fight my way out. Do you know what it is like, having to fight your younger cousin to the death in order to escape?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c\u2026. No, and I hope I never have to. What next?\u201d<br /> <br /> He shrugs, \u201cI fled into the wilderness. I had several more madness fits, and I woke up in a serfs house in the middle of bumfuck nowhere. Nobody knew who I was, nor where I was from. They\u2026. treated me with kindness, and respect, the first time in my life. I was actually content there, even happy, and put up my sword. Up until the raid, that is.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cThe village got raided? By whom?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cSome Oathsworn from Song. They weren\u2019t looking for me, just raiding an enemy village. I led the serfs in an effort to drive them off, killing several of the Oathsworn in the process. The serfs asked, even <i>begged</i>, for me to stay, but I started to have bad dreams again, and I left before I could kill anyone else.\u201d<br /> <br /> He leans forward again, \u201cYou know what I realized, then and there? The Lords don\u2019t care about the little people, about you and me, about Tama, the little serf-girl who liked the wildflowers by the stream, and took a spear through the head for it. All they care about is their thrones, their glory, and whoever they have to step on to stay above the rest, well, <i>fuck</i> them, right?\u201d<br /> <br /> He shrugs again, \u201cSo, eventually, I decided to do the same, only I would do it <i>my way</i>. After all, back when the Hundred Kingdoms were new, how did the first Lords form their kingdoms?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c\u2026..Through clever politicking and diplomacy?\u201d<br /> <br /> Smirking, \u201cNo, <i>by the sword</i>. All the fancy stuff, the thrones, the robes, the tea-ceremonies, all that is <i>useless</i> when the people in a domain are downtrodden. If you can\u2019t protect your people, can you really call yourself their ruler? <i>That</i>, my young friend, is why I am here. The Lord Iysen didn\u2019t care enough about Jianghu to <i>visit</i>, much less protect the people. Akio Masumi \u2026\u2026 well, you met him, so I don\u2019t have to go into detail. The other Oathsworn here? They <i>try</i>, but they are too concerned about rights and legalities to be worthy of the name. If they <i>actually</i> cared (and you see you closest retainer shift angrily), they would fight me off, land-boundaries be damned!\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c When I got here, five years ago, Jianghu was a mess. People were afraid to leave their homes, and bandits and the Tribes forced away or killed many of the others. Large regions of Jianghu were laid barren. Now? A maiden girl of 16 can walk from Yagura to Liangzhou, naked as her name day and with a bag of silver around her neck, and not get accosted. <i>I</i> take credit for that, me! Atonement, for my actions earlier in life.....\u201d<br /> <br /> This time, you yourself lean across the table, \u201cAbout time. About <i>goddamn</i> time. Do you know how long I wandered the Hundred Kingdoms, waiting for someone in authority to say those words? My Lord, Isyen Shiba, is \u2026\u2026<i>alright</i>, but she still has her moments. I feel the same way you do. Without the Oaths, we are little more than well-heeled murderers.\u201d<br /> <br /> He raises an eyebrow, \u201cOkay, alright, that is nice and all, but what does that have to do with anything?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cYou said you want to do things \u2018your way\u2019, you feel a sense of responsibility towards the people of Jianghu, and the Hundred Kingdoms? You seek out a position of authority, and make the land safe for them? <i>That is exactly what I need</i> in a vassal!\u201d<br /> <br /> His face falls, \u201cAre you\u2026\u2026.trying to hire me?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cYes.\u201d<br /> <br /> He sits back, stunned, then collapses in a gale of uncontrollable laughter. After a moment, he composes himself (mostly, a chuckle still escapes here and there), \u201cWhy the <i>hell</i> would I agree to serve you?\u201d<br /> <br /> You say, stone-faced, \u201cBecause I will kill you if you don\u2019t.\u201d<br /> <br /> This brings forth more laughter. \u201cYou and what army? The one outside? <i>Please</i>. Farmers with spears. I have twice, no, <i>three</i> times as many men as you, hardened killers to a man.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cDid you count the militia?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cWh\u2026.yes.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cOr the recruits coming up from the rest of Xia, serving in return for land? Or the Hill-Tribes, who want to see you gone as much as I do?\u201d You rise, placing your hands on the table (to give support, you can already feel your hip quivering), \u201cDo I need to tell you just what the Tribes do to prisoners, <i>especially</i> prisoners that deface their shrines and defile their burial grounds?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cJust\u2026.. what the hell are you talking about, the Hill-Tribes? They are <i>miles</i> away!\u201d<br /> <br /> You give him a quick grin of your own, pulling back your lips to expose the elongated incisors. \u201cSomething tells me you are a little behind the times, Masanori.\u201d You rise to your feet and draw yourself up to your full height, brushing the ceiling with your pony-tail and looming about 2 feet over the seated bandit-king.<br /> <br /> \u201cAs part of the recent agreement between Xia and the Tribes, the Tribes are going to be moving down into the hinterlands of Jianghu. Hell, they are <i>already here</i>. With that in mind, what do you know of Dun Nashuon, your base of operations?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cIt\u2019s a Hill-Tribe ringfort, but the Tribes haven\u2019t used it in centuries!\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cAnd now they want it back, with back-rent.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cThey have already agreed to help me take it, and take you out, in return for the fortress. As many warriors as you may have, the Tribes have <i>more</i>, and I doubt you have ever seen anything like the ferocity of an enraged Tribesman. Add in the militia, and the new recruits coming up from Xia, and, well, let\u2019s see, carry the one, and \u2026\u2026 <i>no</i>, sir, you actually do <i>not</i> have more fighters than I do.\u201d<br /> <br /> He snarls, \u201cAnd?! Are you going to fight me for Jianghu?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cIndeed, if it comes to that, although I would prefer not to if it may be avoided. I have been called many things throughout my life, but \u2018gentle\u201d or \u201ckind\u2019 is probably the ones I am most fond of. With that in mind, I am prepared to make the following offer.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c Anyone who joins me, and serves in my army for five years, will be granted a plot of land, acreage dependent on the experience and equipment of the soldier. That applies to your fighters as well, Masanori. If they join me, all crimes will be forgiven, at least by me. They serve their five years, then get to rejoin civilized society, remade men with a new lease on life. I am willing to bet many will jump at the chance. Living in the woods <i>sucks</i>, Masanori; I would know, I\u2019ve done it for the last three years.<br /> <br /> \u201cThe same offer applies to you. Serve me for 5 years, and I will raise you up as a vassal, with holdings, title and prestige <i>worthy</i> of your actions. I would make you the Lord you so dearly seek to be, give you the <i>recognition</i> you so clearly deserve!\u201d<br /> <br /> Your face settles, stone-like. \u201cBut do not mistake my kindness for weakness, Masanori. I have been called an optimist, even an <i>idealist</i>, but that does not mean I am a fool. This offer is only open to your men for a month, and to you, <i>for this very hour</i>. The bandit-problem in Jianghu is <i>over</i>, one way or another. I will pile body upon body, stack heads above the treetops, hang so many men from trees it would look as though new orchards had grown overnight. I will burn the forests down and piss on the ashes, if that is what I have to do in order to <i>keep my people safe</i>.\u201d<br /> <br /> You look deeply into his eyes, \u201cYou can either join me, or die with the rest of them.\u201d<br /> <br /> In spite of your fiery statements, he looks positively <i>amused</i>. \u201cBrave, man, and well spoken. But are you willing to die for them? In a flash, he stands up, and the room is filled with the singing of steel.<br /> <br /> His dagger flies towards your face, yet you do not move. He stiffens, at the very end of his blow, with his dagger mere fractions of an inch away from your eye, and looks down.<br /> <br /> Your own dagger is drawn and laid, almost neatly, along the line of his thigh.<br /> <br /> \u201cYes, in fact I would, although, yet again, I would prefer not to.\u201d You smile thinly, \u201cAgain, I am not a fool, Masanori.\u201d<br /> <br /> He laughs, in spite of the razor-sharp edge resting above his femoral artery, \u201c\u2026.. Evidently not. Balls of steel, too. Did you even flinch?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cI had to resist the urge not to, I\u2019ll admit.\u201d<br /> <br /> He shakes his head, looking around the taproom floor. Both your retainers and his backup have leapt to their feet and drawn steel, sending tables, chairs and drinks flying. The civilian proprietors are cowering amidst the furniture, and the barkeep has pulled a crossbow out from under the bar, coolly moving the steel tip from target to target.<br /> <br /> \u201cNobody move! The militia is on their way, and they\u2019ll sort the lot of you out right quick.\u201d<br /> <br /> Masanori stands still for quite a few moments, then abruptly, \u201cStand down, boys!\u201d<br /> <br /> Almost as a man (which is a credit to both their skill and Masanori\u2019s leadership), the bandit toughs put up their weapons, and when they do, your Oathsworn do the same, albeit much more reluctantly. They tense up again, however, when Masanori suddenly draws his sword, dropping to a knee in front of you.<br /> <br /> \u201c\u2026\u2026 You\u2019ve made your point.\u201d He chuckles at his small pun, \u201cYour optimism is\u2026\u2026\u2026 valiant, and admirable, if a bit misplaced. Such optimism is \u2026.. dangerous, when faced with the reality of life in the Hundred Kingdoms, and with my experience I feel that it would be dangerously close to murder if I let such rampant naivety continue unabated.\u201d His face saddens for a moment, \u201cThe gods only know I have committed enough murder in my life. That, coupled with your impressive resolve to see things <i>right</i>, leads me to make the only possible choice. This seems quite a bit hypocritical, but......I swear loyalty to you, <i>Lord Jianghu</i>. My blade is yours \u2026.. so long as you remain moral.\u201d<br /> <br /> Before you can say or do anything, he coughs, \u201c Of course, it bears mentioning that I am not a fool either. If I so happened to not return from this meeting, my men were ordered to raze Liangzhou to the ground in retaliation for my assumed death\u2026\u2026 In hindsight, that was\u2026\u2026 not my best decision, I\u2019ll admit.\u201d<br /> <br /> You freeze, blood going cold, \u201cHow many soldiers are coming?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cAbout 900 or so.\u201d At your look of shock, he shrugs, embarrassed. \u201cHey, I don\u2019t do things half-assed, okay?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c\u2026\u2026 How long do we have until they attack?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cSundown. Although, my Lord, if I may make a suggestion. If I return to my men, an attack <i>can</i> be prevented tonight, and we will return to Dun Nashuon. Once there, you can lay siege to the fortress, and I will escape the fortress with any others who want to desert and take up your offer, as well as gaining intelligence on the other bandits in the area.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cWhat if I show you to the bandits, will that prevent an attack?\u201d<br /> <br /> He shakes his head, \u201cShow me off as a traitor who sold them out? Really?\u201d<br /> <br /> You bite your lip, pondering your options. After a moment of thought, you,<ol class=\"decimal\"><li>Accept Masanori\u2019s service, and let him infiltrate Dun Nashuon. That could end this \u2018war\u2019 with the bandits with <i>ease</i>!</li> <li>Accept Masanori\u2019s service, but keep him with you. To be honest\u2026.. you don\u2019t entirely trust him yet</li> <li>Take his sword and kill him. He agreed to your offer <i>far</i> too quickly, and you can cut off the head of the snake right here and now.</li> <li>Something else? (write in)</li> </ol>NOTE: Only option 1 prevents battle. 2 and 3 results in battle<br /> <br /> OOC:<br /> <br /> Masanori is basically an older Kasai \u2026\u2026 if Kasai were a maladjusted child-soldier with a raging case of PTSD and occasional berserk mental breakdowns/blackouts. Also kind of a ninja, albeit not the Hollywood-ized version, but the 'realistic&quot; version: aka a not!samurai not only skilled in open battle and warfare, but also in subterfuge and assassination.<br /> <br /> Also, his statement of &quot; so long as you remain moral.&quot; is <u>totally</u> a threat.<br /> <br /> Battle of Liangzhou: The bandits have orders to attack the town of Liangzhou at sundown, razing the settlement to the ground in retaliation for their \u2018Lords\u2019 death.<br /> <br /> There are ~900 bandits, stats forthcoming.<br /> <br /> For friendly forces, there are the soldiers mustered for drill, both Men-at-Arms and Militia. Not everyone is here yet, but the rest of the forces will be here in a day or two.<br /> <br /> The Jianghu forces are: (stats forthcoming)<br /> <br /> 100 spearmen, of 180<br /> 60 longbowmen, of 120<br /> 25 halberdiers, of 100<br /> 0 light cavalry, of 60<br /> 25 Oathsworn, of 40<br /> 180 militiamen, of 600<br /> So\u2026\u2026 390 troops, vs ~900.<br /> <br /> Liangzhou <u>is</u> fortified, however, meaning so long as we keep our forces inside the town walls (palisade, really), they get relatively hefty Defensive bonuses, and the attacking troops get funneled through the two town-gates, meaning their effective Size gets reduced. However, said fortifications are made from wood,as is most of the town, and with some concentrated effort, they <i>could</i> be set ablaze.......<br /> <br /> Up to you folks.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11892,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","user":"Boston123","date":". 06-11-2017, 12:24 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-10T21:41:42-07:00","id":1102432,"title":"","content":"Either the man is honest, in which case we can trust him to do as he says and come back to serve us if we let him go...<br /> <br /> Or he's not, and he'll go back to his men and won't come back to serve us.<br /> <br /> Either way, I say we let him go back to his forces. He and his handful of men won't make a huge difference if we do wind up having to fight them, and if he can prevent them attacking it saves us a lot of trouble. After we let him go we should prepare for immediate assault anyway-we ought to assume the attack is imminent until we have reason to believe otherwise. Masanori might be good...but talking 900 guys OUT of a raid can be tough.<br /> <br /> EDIT: These &quot;Madness Fits&quot; have me a bit concerned. It's all well and good to have a dangerous madman working for you as long as he's pointed in the right direction...but I'm a bit concerned about giving him lands and lordhood when we know he has violent bouts of madness, especially as that sort of thing tends to get worse with age. Not a reason to betray him or not take him on as a vassal...but once he's actually entered our service we'll want to keep him close, so we can gauge how stable he is.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>So I'm making <a href=\"http://godkickingboot.smackjeeves.com/\" target=\"_blank\">God-Kicking Boot</a>, an Exalted webcomic, now. Updates on Sundays. Full-color, mediocre but slowly improving art. It's a thing.<br /> <br /> The absence of a monument can, in its own way, be something of a monument also.</i> -Roger Zelazny","edited":{"userid":512,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/512-wise-old-guru","user":"Wise Old Guru","date":". 06-11-2017, 12:43 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/512-wise-old-guru","name":"Wise Old Guru","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar512_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1097"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-11T09:41:24-07:00","id":1102539,"title":"","content":"Sure, option 1. It removes or lessens the bandit problem, while bolstering the troop tally.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Like my Avatar? Courtesy of<span style=\"color:#0000FF\"> Jen</span>! : <span style=\"font-size:12px\">Anybody want their characters to be experimented on ? </span>post 98<br /> <i>An Exalt is never unarmed.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4018-l-het-esh","name":"L'het'esh","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4018_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"521"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-11T15:14:15-07:00","id":1102621,"title":"","content":"pleased to see a number of my suggestions were taken into consideration. I hope i haven't doomed us all. <br /> <br /> I agree with everything old wise guru said. He is used to distrust from his lord. If we prove that we are both willing to trust our vassal and reward them for good service it might further grow our trust. I'd suggest he be made aware that his reward will be equal to his strength of leadership. If we are attacked, his rewards will quickly dry up the longer the violence lasts. Maybe an exchange of oaths now or a later date to one another might be necessary as well?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8897-joshopotamus","name":"joshopotamus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8897_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"97"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-11T15:41:41-07:00","id":1102631,"title":"","content":"I forgot to say it sounds like Kasai is suffering from radiation poisoning....","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Like my Avatar? Courtesy of<span style=\"color:#0000FF\"> Jen</span>! : <span style=\"font-size:12px\">Anybody want their characters to be experimented on ? </span>post 98<br /> <i>An Exalt is never unarmed.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4018-l-het-esh","name":"L'het'esh","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4018_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"521"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-11T15:46:42-07:00","id":1102634,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>L'het'esh</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1102631#post1102631\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I forgot to say it sounds like Kasai is suffering from radiation poisoning....</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> More stress. Canines shed when stressed.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-11T17:26:08-07:00","id":1102656,"title":"","content":"Trust the man, even if he lies that doesn't change the situation and we have much to gain if he is telling the truth.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11567-jairain","name":"Jairain","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11567_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"345"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-12T04:42:27-07:00","id":1102761,"title":"","content":"1. Accept Masanori's service and send him off. <br /> <br /> ...I'm a little surprised that we have to go through the rigmarole of a &quot;siege&quot; to recruit him after that. Kasai's offer is pretty good, considering - does Masanori believe that a majority of his men won't go for it?<br /> <br /> Also, we should spend more time gathering intelligence before we do something like this again. 900 is not what I'd call &quot;a few.&quot;","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>On the frontier of the Wild South, there's only one woman with the grit to take on its most dangerous outlaws and bring them <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/984977-back-alive-or-maybe-dead-quest\" target=\"_blank\">Back Alive, or Maybe Dead.</a><br /> <br /> Avatar by <a href=\"http://puzzlinghappenstance.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">K.S. Brenowitz</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/908-semicasual","name":"semicasual","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar908_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2499"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-12T14:03:30-07:00","id":1102910,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>semicasual</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1102761#post1102761\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">1. Accept Masanori's service and send him off.<br /> <br /> ...I'm a little surprised that we have to go through the rigmarole of a &quot;siege&quot; to recruit him after that. Kasai's offer is pretty good, considering - does Masanori believe that a majority of his men won't go for it?<br /> <br /> Also, we should spend more time gathering intelligence before we do something like this again. 900 is not what I'd call &quot;a few.&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>1) The rigaramole is to ensure all of his (Masanori's) forces are in one place, so Kasai doesn't have to play whack-a-bandit across the Akibara for the next few months. It is also so he can present the offer to the bandits he actually trusts with relative secrecy. If other bandit-captains get a whiff he might leave them up shit creek without a paddle, they might try to kill him.<br /> <br /> <i>That is, of course, if you believe him. </i>He very well might just be trying to get away.<br /> <br /> By letting the bandits here leave, Kasai just ensures he has to face upwards of 1500-1600 bandits holed up in Dun Nashuon. Don't get me wrong..... a decent proportion will change coats once his offer gets out...... but that doesn't mean the Battle of Dun Nashuon is going to be <u>easy</u>, not by a long shot, even with the Tribes helping.<br /> <br /> To be honest, I am a bit surprised nobody wants to fight, not that I want to sway opinions, of course. Yes, I'll admit: in pure numbers alone, we seem to be pretty outnumbered. But the fortifications of Liangzhou are hefty enough to make quite the difference, to the point where, <i>so long as Kasai (you<u>)</u> fights intelligently (</i>And we have Sgt. Maj. Oxblood here to whisper in Kasai's ear, so that is almost a given)<u>,</u> the odds are actually pretty even, if not weighted towards our side.<br /> <br /> If you kill them now, you don't have to kill them later, is what I am saying.<br /> <br /> 2) The bandits are dispersed along the fringes of the Akibara, the smaller projections of the larger forest, and throughout the small hills and dales in the upper pasturelands and orchards overlooking the town. It isn't like they are standing around in formation where people can see them from far off, and they can vanish from scouting parties with relative ease. Once they get the sign to attack, the dispersed groups will come together.<br /> <br /> Such is the problem with owning such an underpopulated and underdeveloped region. If the region was more heavily settled, or the wildlands were cut back, bandits and other riff-raff would have a <u>much</u> harder time sneaking up on stuff.<br /> <br /> But.... yes, 900 is a lot. Like he said, he wanted to send a message if Kasai proved untrustworthy. Liangzhou has a stout palisade and 300 souls inside, and he wanted the razing to be a 'done deal&quot;. More so once he got here and saw the troopers drilling.<br /> <br /> Here is a map, for your consideration.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"http://imgur.com/WVFQ7hJ\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/google-material/image_placeholder.svg\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://imgur.com/WVFQ7hJ\" target=\"_blank\"> </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://imgur.com/WVFQ7hJ\" target=\"_blank\"> http://imgur.com/WVFQ7hJ </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> </div> </div> </div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11892,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","user":"Boston123","date":". 06-12-2017, 05:05 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-12T17:48:17-07:00","id":1102963,"title":"","content":"We've done a lot of fighting and need to grow to consider alternatives. Especially now that we are responsible for so much more than ourselves and a couple retainers. Still we can fight when the alternatives fail or become too costly.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11567-jairain","name":"Jairain","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11567_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"345"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-13T05:06:21-07:00","id":1103083,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Boston123</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1102910#post1102910\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If you kill them now, you don't have to kill them later, is what I am saying.</div> </div> </div> </div>Fighting a siege battle and having to follow up with bandit whack-a-moles doesn't sound like a winning strategy.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>On the frontier of the Wild South, there's only one woman with the grit to take on its most dangerous outlaws and bring them <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/984977-back-alive-or-maybe-dead-quest\" target=\"_blank\">Back Alive, or Maybe Dead.</a><br /> <br /> Avatar by <a href=\"http://puzzlinghappenstance.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">K.S. Brenowitz</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/908-semicasual","name":"semicasual","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar908_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2499"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-13T13:46:07-07:00","id":1103256,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>semicasual</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1103083#post1103083\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Fighting a siege battle and having to follow up with bandit whack-a-moles doesn't sound like a winning strategy.</div> </div> </div> </div>True, point taken.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-14T17:20:09-07:00","id":1103663,"title":"","content":"I guess the main question at this point is: Do you folks want to make a push for Dun Nashuon ASAP, once the rest of the army gets here, before Masanori has a chance to reconsider his vow of service?<br /> <br /> I mean, the rest of the Jianghu levy will get here &quot;tomorrow&quot; in various stages (meaning the army won't set out until the day after), and from there it will take about a week-ish (I'm aiming for about 4-6 days) to get to the closest point to the Toge Hills and Dun Nashuon. From there, it will take [REDACTED] days to get to Dun Nashuon, depending on your choices.<br /> <br /> Once the entire Jianghu levy musters, Kasai will have about 1100 men under his command, Men-at-Arms + Militia.<br /> <br /> The reason why I am asking is: Due to the entire Jianghu levy being made up of farmers, both Men-at-Arms and Militia, Kasai can't keep them in the field past the end of summer, and that is pushing it. Functionally, there is about 3 weeks of &quot;campaign season&quot; left, or 3 weeks before the end of summer (aka summer harvest) and the start of autumn (aka autumn planting). He <u>really</u> wants the levy home and workin' the fields before those start.<br /> <br /> There will be more plot and decision-making along these lines in the upcoming posts<br /> <br /> While missing the summer harvest, or getting home late to the same, won't <u>kill</u> people due to starvation.... it will make Kasai look pretty bad, which is the exact opposite of what he wants to do as a young, relatively-unknown new figure of authority.. He got kinda metaphorically-cockblocked due to showing up in Jianghu in midsummer.<br /> <br /> Once he gets the land-grant soldiers, he won't really have to worry about time constraints regarding campaigning anymore. Since the new recruits won't be farmers, they can stay in the field almost indefinitely. Unfortunately, the recruits won't start showing up for about another two weeks at least.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11892,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","user":"Boston123","date":". 06-14-2017, 10:08 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-15T05:56:10-07:00","id":1103781,"title":"","content":"1100 vs 900 isn't great odds, especially if the bandits occupy a fortified position and the Jianghu levy does not. But we might be able to get reinforcements from the hill tribes, and anyway we're not <i>really</i> going there to siege. We're going there to parley with a big stick. I say we set off as soon as possible.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>On the frontier of the Wild South, there's only one woman with the grit to take on its most dangerous outlaws and bring them <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/984977-back-alive-or-maybe-dead-quest\" target=\"_blank\">Back Alive, or Maybe Dead.</a><br /> <br /> Avatar by <a href=\"http://puzzlinghappenstance.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">K.S. Brenowitz</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/908-semicasual","name":"semicasual","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar908_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2499"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-15T09:10:37-07:00","id":1103827,"title":"","content":"Go, go, go. A year isn't much time for all Kasai is supposed to do.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Like my Avatar? Courtesy of<span style=\"color:#0000FF\"> Jen</span>! : <span style=\"font-size:12px\">Anybody want their characters to be experimented on ? </span>post 98<br /> <i>An Exalt is never unarmed.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4018-l-het-esh","name":"L'het'esh","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4018_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"521"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-15T14:48:37-07:00","id":1103950,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>semicasual</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1103781#post1103781\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">1100 vs 900 isn't great odds, especially if the bandits occupy a fortified position and the Jianghu levy does not. But we might be able to get reinforcements from the hill tribes, and anyway we're not <i>really</i> going there to siege. We're going there to parley with a big stick. I say we set off as soon as possible.</div> </div> </div> </div>It is more like 1100 (+ whatever forces Masanori brings over, + the Hill-tribes) vs ~1500 (- whatever Masanori takes with him, - whomever else wants to see the light, <u>if</u> we make them the same offer)<br /> <br /> And.... yes. One way or another, Dun Nashuon will be in friendly hands before the summer is out, unless you fine folks decide to retreat. You can't really &quot;afford&quot; to let such a defensive position be held by unfriendly hands for much longer, especially since it is so close to your territory.<br /> <br /> The situation is more &quot;parley with a big stick, then kill whomever doesn't comply&quot;.<br /> <br /> More details will be forthcoming, that very well might influence your thought-process.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-15T18:05:56-07:00","id":1104066,"title":"","content":"Go for the goal. Let's see what happens","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11567-jairain","name":"Jairain","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11567_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"345"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-15T20:42:42-07:00","id":1104137,"title":"","content":"stick parley time!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8897-joshopotamus","name":"joshopotamus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8897_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"97"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-18T16:53:28-07:00","id":1105225,"title":"","content":"It seems that most people want to send Masanori off, to avoid fighting right now.<br /> Sounds good.<br /> <br /> -------------------------<br /> <br /> You turn from the doorway, where you were lost in thought.<br /> <br /> \u201c\u2026.. Go back to your men, Masanori. Prevent this attack.\u201d As he starts to rise, you grab him by the shoulder and jerk his face up close to yours, eyes inches apart.<br /> <br /> \u201cDo <i>not</i> betray me, Masanori. I take to those who abuse my trust about as well as you could expect\u2026\u201d<br /> <br /> He smiles slightly, nodding, \u201cyes, yes, of course my Lord. <i>If</i> I were to betray you, you would capture me, do various unpleasantries to my body, and show my broken corpse in public. I know the drill, have no fear.\u201d<br /> <br /> You let go, standing back ,\u201d Just about how many men do you think would cross sides?\u201d<br /> <br /> He shrugs, \u201c I dunno, probably two hundred or so? Maybe more? I am willing to bet most of those who cross sides come from my own crew. They are the only ones I would trust, anyways, and that is saying a lot.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c\u2026.Alright. I expect to see you in about a week. Dismissed.\u201d And, with a grandiose bow, Masanori and his men leave, in quite a bit of a hurry.<br /> <br /> As soon as they leave, Aya Cho rushes over , \u201cAre you wounded, Lord Kasai?\u201d<br /> <br /> You shrug, feeling around your cheek, \u201cNo, but it was close. Remind me to stop doing stuff like that\u2026..\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cWas letting him go a \u2026.wise decision, my Lord?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cMaybe, maybe not. He kind of put me on the spot\u2026. Which is almost-certainly why he brought an army here. He is smarter than he looks.\u201d You look over to the barkeep, who is still agitatedly waving his crossbow about.<br /> <br /> \u201cWant to put that away, goodman? I\u2019d rather not take a crossbow bolt to the stomach.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cNot until someone tells me what is going on!\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cYou insolent son of a peasant dog! Do you know to whom you speak? I should take your head for your words alone, much less thre..\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cAya, <i>relax. </i>I did almost just cause a bloodbath in his bar, his suspicion is warranted.\u201d Walking over to the barkeep, \u201c I am Kasai Qianke, and I am the Lord of Jianghu.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cI\u2026..what\u2026.oh <i>fuck</i>.\u201d He looks at the crossbow in his hand, as if seeing it for the first time. Hurriedly, he puts it away back under the bar. \u201cMy deepest apologies, My Lord.\u201d<br /> <br /> You wave him off. \u201cNot a problem.\u201d Turning back to your retainers, \u201c Now, I may be an optimist, but I am not <i>that</i> na\u00efve. Just because we let Masanori go doesn\u2019t necessarily mean he will keep his side of the bargain. That said, I want the mustered levy brought inside the palisade, as soon as possible, just in ca\u2026\u201d<br /> <br /> It is at this point that the Liangzhou militia shows up, armed with clubs and staves. They flood the taproom, meaning it takes quite a few more minutes before any meaningful work is done (You have to explain who you are more than seven times!). Finally, the militia starts to filter out, and you hurriedly rush back to the muster camp.<br /> <br /> There, you snag Sgt. Maj. Oxblood and explain the situation. He curses liberally, glaring up at the heaths overlooking the town, and at the assembled levy.<br /> <br /> \u201cHow they managed to sneak that close, I don\u2019t know. I <i>had</i> patrols around the perimeter, Lord Qianke, evidently they weren\u2019t paying attention\u2026.\u201d, fixing the levy with a glare that would\u2019ve soured milk and made women barren.<br /> <br /> \u201cFrom what Masanori said, he had his troops dispersed in groves and hills across a few square miles of empty country. I am not surprised they weren\u2019t found, especially if the patrols only kept close to the Liangzhou walls. No harm, no foul, at least this time.\u201d<br /> <br /> Once Sgt. Oxblood relaxes a bit, you continue, \u201c Once the rest of the levy and militia gets here, I want to make a move on the bandit-held fortress of Dun Nashuon. How feasible is that, and how long do you think we can get there?\u201d<br /> <br /> He rubs his chin, \u201c Attacking a fortified point? Eh\u2026\u2026 the Men-at-Arms levy is ready, and the militia is moving along nicely, although I wouldn\u2019t say they are quite ready yet. It isn\u2019t that they lack skill, more that they lack cohesion, drill. They aren\u2019t used to fighting as a unit yet, and their officers and sergeants lack experience and knowledge of the unit both. But\u2026.. so long as you don\u2019t just throw them into a battle-line, they should be fine.\u201d<br /> <br /> You nod, \u201c Understood. How long will it take to make a move on the fortress?\u201d<br /> <br /> He shrugs, \u201cHow far away is it, and what sort of terrain do we have to march through?\u201d<br /> <br /> Shin Takeshi chimes in, \u201cThe closest point to the Toge Hills is the Bulrush Creek, which is several days away, about halfway between Liangzhou and Yagura Castle. The lower parts of the flow are open, surrounded by farms and heathland, but the upper reaches are forested until the hills themselves.\u201d<br /> <br /> Sgt. Oxblood ponders that for a moment, \u201cWell\u2026.. a good rule of thumb to keep in mind when marching armies is that the \u2018base\u2019 marching rate for trained troops is about ten to fifteen miles a day, over good terrain and with weather permitting. If the roads suck, or if it rains or is too hot, or if the troops aren\u2019t trained in long-distance marching, the army slows down. Other than that, you have to keep logistics in mind. \u2018An army marches on it\u2019s stomach\u2019, and all that, and you almost-always never want to leave your beans, bandages and boots straggling behind your forces if possible.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cI prefer to keep a slow march, so the army doesn\u2019t pull ahead of it\u2019s own baggage train, or get all strung-out, and even then, if it is possible, have the troops carry a supply of materiel on them, like the Shogunate-of-old did. If every soldier has five days of food on them, you can afford to leave the wagons behind a few days. Most commanders don\u2019t like that, they want to <i>go go gogogogogogogo</i>, marching troops to the bone and effectively making them fight tired and hungry. That is\u2026\u2026inefficient, to say the least, but being able to strategically outflank the enemy with a forced march could be worth it\u2026\u2026\u201d<br /> <br /> Despite the rather dry subject matter, you pay rapt attention, trying to soak up as much knowledge as possible. After all, you <i>will</i> be leading these forces one day, and you will take as much help as you can get! After listening to Sgt. Oxblood ramble on for a few minutes, mainly about logistics (In your old and dog-eared copy of the <i>Thousand Correct Actions</i>, the topic of logistics was referred to as \u201cHeaven and Earth\u201d), you politely interrupt.<br /> <br /> \u201cSo\u2026.. since most of the roads in Jianghu are made of packed earth, it will take slightly longer for the men to march over them. When we get to the Akibara, the men will get slowed down even more, especially since the wagons will almost-certainly trail behind\u2026\u201d<br /> <br /> He says to you, eyebrow raised, \u201cWith that in mind, M\u2019Lord, how long do you think it will take to march to this fortress?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c\u2026\u2026 Erring on the side of caution, probably about a week to get to the Akibara forest, and another few days to pass through the forest?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cMaybe less, M\u2019Lord. I\u2019ve travelled on the road between Liangzhou and Yagura Castle, it isn\u2019t <i>that</i> bad, we could make good time. The forest\u2026\u2026..ehhhh.\u201d He shrugs. \u201cWe\u2019ll have to see.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cWill there be time to take the fortress, before the harvest season starts?\u201d<br /> <br /> He shrugs again, \u201cIt all depends on how fast we move. A siege will be \u2026\u2026iffy, but it is probably the safest option, with the least loss of life. An assault could be fast, over and done with in a day or two, but more people would die. We will have to see what the best option is when we get there.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cAlright. Before we move out, however, we have to wait until the rest of the levy gets here. Plus, moving materiel beforehand would be preferable. <i>Finally</i>, I don\u2019t quite trust Masanori Mitsuhide yet, and I still fear an attack. Let us move the muster inside the walls of Liangzhou.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cGood idea, M\u2019Lord. I\u2019ll see to it.\u201d<br /> <br /> Thankfully, either Masanori was genuine, or he was scared off by your fortifications, and you watch the sunset pass with a sigh of relief. Over the course of the next day, the rest of the levy and militia wanders in, confused at how the rest of the army is standing guard over the palisade of Liangzhou. They settle in, with more than a little direction from Sgt Oxblood bawling orders (audible even from the town center), and when the sergeant is free, you sit with him to plan out the movement of your (you still aren\u2019t used to the idea of such \u2018things\u2019 actually \u2018belonging\u2019 to you, even after a few weeks in Jianghu.) army. In addition, you dispatch Shin Takeshi, with a retinue of 100 miltiamen, to travel to Yagura Castle and pack up as much food from the vaults as possible, and to link up with the rest of the levy at the mouth of the Bulrush creek.<br /> <br /> The next day, Sgt. Oxblood musters the whole levy in what he refers to as \u201cparade formation\u201d, and the town takes on an almost festival air. Citizens gather on top of the palisade wallwalk to watch the soldiers form up, and farmers at work in the fields stop to watch. Even you have to admit as you ride up mounted on Xuansu, that there is a <i>hell</i> of a lot of people here. When you bring this up to the Sergeant, he chuckles,<br /> <br /> \u201cM\u2019Lord, begging your pardon, but this isn\u2019t that many soldiers. The Lord Iysen has about 10,000 Men-at-Arms, and believe you me, <i>that</i> is a sight to see when they get fully mustered. The ground <i>shakes</i> when they march.\u201d<br /> <br /> You stay silent, instead nudging Xuansu forwards. You ride out in front of the assembled regiments. It feels like every eye is on you (they must be, in all seriousness), and it takes you a moment to find your voice.<br /> <br /> \u201c Good evening. My name is Kasai Qianke, and I am the Lord Paramount of the Jianghu Marches.\u201d At this, everyone, with a rattle of arms and armor, takes a knee. \u201c I am sure you are wondering why you have been mustered? The answer is this: The bandits in Jianghu have been ever-bold, marauding across the countryside, stealing crops and burning farms. For too long have you suffered under their rapacity, and it ends now!\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cThe largest, boldest and most troublesome group of bandits has a stronghold in the Toge Hills, an ancient hillfort known as Dun Nashuon. By taking it, we both deny the fortress to the enemy, and send a message to the other bandit-gangs in Jianghu: \u2018We will not be cowed any longer!\u201d. We will <i>break </i> their backs here, my soldiers, and make Jianghu safe for your families and children!\u201d<br /> <br /> At this, the assembled soldiers break out cheering, and Sgt. Oxblood blows a brassy blast on a bugle. The signal is taken up by the other buglers in the army, and the sound echoes across the river valley. With some shouted orders, the levy heads out, and you buttonhole the Sergeant as you pass by at the head of the column.<br /> <br /> \u201cHow was that, Sergeant?\u201d<br /> <br /> He grins, \u201cAlright, M\u2019Lord. A little short, with a lot of big words, but good enthusiasm.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cUgh. I <i>hate</i> public speaking.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cBetter get used to it. Most troops expect a speech before, after and even sometimes <i>during</i> a battle. When they <i>don\u2019t</i> get the speech is when they get worked up. The familiar is comforting, you know?\u201d<br /> <br /> You sigh, already ruing what is to come.<br /> <br /> Surprisingly, the clear weather holds, and even the humidity stays at bay (which is usual for Jianghu, you are told), and the levy makes good time. Only a day or so after leaving Liangzhou, however, you start coming across farmers and fieldsmen streaming west back towards Liangzhou, terror and panic writ large in their body language. When you stop a few of them and ask them just what the hell they are running from, the universal reply is:<br /> <br /> \u201cThe Tribes!\u201d<br /> <br /> A little perturbed (just what the hell have the Tribes been doing?), you keep the army moving east, and in spite of your predictions, the army isn\u2019t slowed down all that much by the dirt track you call a road, even with the refugees travelling in the opposite direction. Only 5 days after leaving Liangzhou, you crest over a hill and take in the sight of the Bulrush running into the White River. Lifting your eyes from the river to the hill beyond, you can\u2019t help but let your jaw drop in surprise.<br /> <br /> That has to be the largest number of Hill-Tribe warriors you have ever seen!<br /> <br /> The entire eastern side of the Bulrush, from shore to forest, is one massive encampment, with fires and shelters sprouted like weeds. Your militia under Shin Takeshi is already here, as evidenced by the white canvas tents in orderly rows, down by the ford, but the Hill-Tribe warriors look to outnumber the Vanehani several times over.<br /> <br /> Your Oathsworn retainers, rising over the crest behind you, catch a glimpse of what drew you up short, and you hear several curses of various degrees of blasphemy. Aya Cho rides up, and in a stunned tone mutters,<br /> <br /> \u201cWhen you said the Tribes would help take the fortress, My Lord, I didn\u2019t think they meant <i>this</i> level of help!\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cI know it.\u201d you answer, still in shock. \u201cI\u2026..I guess they <i>really</i> want Dun Nashuon. There must be 500 warriors here, at least!\u201d<br /> <br /> After hanging up on top of the rise, you direct the army down towards the ford. Once you get closer, you see Shin Takeshi ride towards you, accompanied by several others, whom you guess to be representatives of the Tribes.<br /> <br /> Once they get closer, you entrust Sgt. Oxblood with the setting-up of camp and go to meet with the worthies. In the shade of a larger pavilion-tent, you meet with the representatives of the Paskamansett, the Nemasket and the Onset.<br /> <br /> One by one, the sub-chiefs of the tribes kneel before you (which is a surprise in and of itself, to be honest), and the sub-chief of the Paskamansett intones,<br /> <br /> \u201cHail, <i>sachem</i> (&lt;chief&gt;). Us warriors join you to take back the ancient home of Dun Nashuon.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cUh\u2026..&lt;Well, thank you for coming, but I must say that I am surprised by just how many people showed up. I thought most of the warriors were with Ousamequin and the Vanehani?&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> He grins, exposing the boar-tusks \u201c&lt; Most of the warriors fighting with Ousamequin are those with something to prove. While some of the Chosen went with our chiefs, most stayed home, to assist and protect the relocation of our people. They, along with the youngbloods that have been causing problems, are here now.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt; But why do many?&gt;\u201d, asks Shin Takeshi.<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt; Your chief helped us, Vanehani. We are just repaying the favor. As for the Nemasket and the Onset\u2026..&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt; In addition to giving us back a piece of our history and culture, Chief of Jianghu, you are seeking to make your land safe, both for your people and for ours. Why would we not help?&gt;<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt; Besides,&gt;\u201d says the Onset sub-chief with a vicious smirk, \u201c&lt; You <i>did</i> promise a spearhead or arrowheads for every bandit-head we bring you. Why go through the effort of hunting them down when they are all here?&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> You take this in over a couple of moments.<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt;So\u2026. What happened to make my people flee in terror?&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> The sub-chiefs share a look, then, \u201c&lt;Nothing we did, Chief. All we have done so far was encamp here, the farmers fled on their own.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt;And\u2026. How are you feeding yourselves?&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt; We have brought food, from the last of the winter stores. On top of that, the youngbloods are hunting in the woods for game, as well as cooling their ardor by wiping out any of the bandit-scum they come across. The last tally was\u2026\u2026. 100 spears, I think.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> Walking out of the pavilion, you look up at the Bulrush creek and the Akibara rising behind. Only a few feet into the treeline, the light dims and sight becomes almost impossible.<br /> <br /> Turning back to the assembled sub-chiefs, you ask, \u201c&lt;Are you fully prepared to move out? Harvest-time is in two weeks, and I would rather not be late\u2026..&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt; We have been waiting for you, to be honest.&gt;\u201d At your look of surprise, he continues, \u201c&lt; You Vanehani are slow, and we have not forgotten the forest-tracks, nor the gods that walk them. It only took us a week to get here from the mountains, and we have been here for 4 days already.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt;Well, we won\u2019t be ready to move again until tomorrow.&gt; Shin, please inform the men to be ready to move by daybreak.\u201d After he leaves, \u201c&lt; You said you had your youth-bands in the Akibara already? Have they brought back word of what the terrain is like?&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt; Indeed, Chief. They say almost all of the wood is severely overgrown, with trees and bamboo and brambles, and only the banks of the creek itself are passable without a great deal of brushcutting. Even those open areas took a great deal of time to pass through, and the youth-bands are faster and more agile than your Vanehani fighters. It <i>is</i> largely flat, however, until you get to the hills at least.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> You sigh, looking back at the Akibara again. This forest could make or break the campaign.<br /> <br /> You:<ol class=\"decimal\"><li>Ask the youth-bands to push ahead of the combined army, clearing brush and building a trail. This will cut the travel time down significantly</li> <li>Have the youth-bands cover the flanks and rear of the army. You would rather not get ambushed! However, this will slow down the army.</li> <li>\u201c&lt; Just what are these \u2018track-gods\u2019, exactly?&gt;\u201d If they sped the travel of the Hill-Tribes, might they do the same? But\u2026.. what would they want for sacrifice?</li> <li>Something else? (Write in)</li> </ol>OOC:<br /> <br /> Short post this week, and probably next week as well. I've got my Level 2 Water Treatment Test on Thursday, and I have been distracted as all hell.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11892,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","user":"Boston123","date":". 06-18-2017, 07:56 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-18T17:12:13-07:00","id":1105227,"title":"","content":"Just what are these \u2018track-gods\u2019, exactly? If they sped the travel of the Hill-Tribes, might they do the same? But\u2026.. what would they want for sacrifice?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Like my Avatar? Courtesy of<span style=\"color:#0000FF\"> Jen</span>! : <span style=\"font-size:12px\">Anybody want their characters to be experimented on ? </span>post 98<br /> <i>An Exalt is never unarmed.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4018-l-het-esh","name":"L'het'esh","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4018_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"521"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-18T17:16:18-07:00","id":1105228,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>L'het'esh</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1105227#post1105227\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Just what are these \u2018track-gods\u2019, exactly? If they sped the travel of the Hill-Tribes, might they do the same? But\u2026.. what would they want for sacrifice?</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Jesus, that was fast.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-18T17:56:26-07:00","id":1105233,"title":"","content":"I also want to know about the track gods, but I will probably choose #1","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>On the frontier of the Wild South, there's only one woman with the grit to take on its most dangerous outlaws and bring them <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/984977-back-alive-or-maybe-dead-quest\" target=\"_blank\">Back Alive, or Maybe Dead.</a><br /> <br /> Avatar by <a href=\"http://puzzlinghappenstance.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">K.S. Brenowitz</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/908-semicasual","name":"semicasual","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar908_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2499"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-18T19:07:54-07:00","id":1105255,"title":"","content":"Learn about the gods but if it is too much or too weird then have the youth clear the path ahead. Our forces should be capable of repelling ambush.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11567-jairain","name":"Jairain","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11567_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"345"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-18T19:59:04-07:00","id":1105261,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Boston123</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1105228#post1105228\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Jesus, that was fast.</div> </div> </div> </div> Just lucky, I guess, to check the forum right when you were finishing up...","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Like my Avatar? Courtesy of<span style=\"color:#0000FF\"> Jen</span>! : <span style=\"font-size:12px\">Anybody want their characters to be experimented on ? </span>post 98<br /> <i>An Exalt is never unarmed.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4018-l-het-esh","name":"L'het'esh","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4018_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"521"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-18T22:32:40-07:00","id":1105289,"title":"","content":"id say combo 2 and 3. It would be disasterous for both our forces and our scouts to get hit while moving to the fortress. Would be rather disasterous and by working together our folks might learn how to use the track gods to help move us fast enough to remain effective","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8897-joshopotamus","name":"joshopotamus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8897_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"97"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-19T08:31:04-07:00","id":1105405,"title":"","content":"FINALLY I'm caught up with you guys.<br /> Just came back to the forums and started reading this. Awesome story so far Boston123.<br /> I'm going to echo in everyone asking about the track-gods, with the added possibility of doing 2 if the track gods are too crazy. We seem to be making okay time as is, and don't we want Masori's bandits to have enough time to slip out anyway?<br /> Though if we could do some combo of 1,2,3, that might also be best, as we might just need a lil' of each, speed and protection.<br /> <br /> Again, fun read!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3752-mushroom-king","name":"Mushroom King","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3752_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"221"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-19T13:44:09-07:00","id":1105505,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Mushroom King</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1105405#post1105405\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">FINALLY I'm caught up with you guys.<br /> Just came back to the forums and started reading this. Awesome story so far Boston123.<br /> I'm going to echo in everyone asking about the track-gods, with the added possibility of doing 2 if the track gods are too crazy. We seem to be making okay time as is, and don't we want Masori's bandits to have enough time to slip out anyway?<br /> Though if we could do some combo of 1,2,3, that might also be best, as we might just need a lil' of each, speed and protection.<br /> <br /> Again, fun read!</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Thanks! I do appreciate those who read my stuff, especially since it may be more 'boring&quot; than other Exaltified Quests.....<br /> <br /> EDIT: the bandits are going to slip out when we get there. You don't spring a trap until <u>all</u> the pieces are in place, after all.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11892,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","user":"Boston123","date":". 06-19-2017, 08:18 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-19T15:26:18-07:00","id":1105534,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Boston123</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1105505#post1105505\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> Thanks! I do appreciate those who read my stuff, especially since it may be more 'boring&amp;quot; than other Exaltified Quests.....</div> </div> </div> </div>I like it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11567-jairain","name":"Jairain","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11567_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"345"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-19T16:24:09-07:00","id":1105546,"title":"","content":"honestly its refreshing to see a more realistic depiction of creation. Most mortals probably wont see dbs or lunars or really any exalt type as i assume most mortals live in some tiny back water. Your depiction of creation for the most part has been supernatural light with touches of exalted. Its been a lot of fun to read","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8897-joshopotamus","name":"joshopotamus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8897_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"97"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-24T07:13:56-07:00","id":1107301,"title":"","content":"By the way: questions!<br /> 1. Do we have our new spear yet? The Luna dream spear?<br /> 2. Did Kazai ever get his armor fixed (I know you recommended doing something like that)?<br /> 3. What is our current &quot;beat to hell level&quot; i.e. how much have we healed from the Lunar fight?<br /> Not that I think we should be fighting, but still curious.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3752-mushroom-king","name":"Mushroom King","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3752_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"221"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-24T08:24:34-07:00","id":1107319,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Mushroom King</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1107301#post1107301\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">By the way: questions!<br /> 1. Do we have our new spear yet? The Luna dream spear?<br /> 2. Did Kazai ever get his armor fixed (I know you recommended doing something like that)?<br /> 3. What is our current &quot;beat to hell level&quot; i.e. how much have we healed from the Lunar fight?<br /> Not that I think we should be fighting, but still curious.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> 1) We have the spear. Lord Iysen gave it to him, after Luna sent her a dream. Like with his sword, bow and dagger, it travels with him everywhere<br /> <br /> 2) Kasai's old mail armor is back in the Lunar shrine, deep within the old Paskamansett territory. It got <i>wrecked</i> in the fight with the Lunar, to the point where it would be cheaper to buy a new set than get it repaired. He doesn't have his own set of new armor, yet, but his retainers will lend him bits and pieces until he commissions his own.<br /> <br /> Since he can't jump and dodge like he could before his injury, his new armor is better off being heavier.<br /> <br /> 3) Kasai is <i>........ mostly</i> healed from the Lunar fight, in the sense that he can use his hands and walk. He still has a few weeks until everything is &quot;back to normal&quot;, or as normal as he is going to be, however; some fingers on his left hand are stiff and nonresponsive (and will be for quite some time), his right arm still aches, his ribs still hurt (and twisting and turning his upper body is painful, meaning he needs help rising from bed in the morning, much less fighting), his concussion gives him headaches occasionally, and his dislocated hip troubles him on inclines and in cold/wet weather.<br /> <br /> He <i>can</i> fight, but it will be with decent penalties, at least -2 in game-terms.<br /> <br /> Note that Kasai's days of fighting directly are...... well, I'm not going to say that they are over, but he has people to do that for him now. While he is still Oathsworn, and therefore expected to fight when the situation calls for it, as a commander he isn't expected to charge into battle. And, while he is expected to fight in his own defense, he <i>technically</i> is &quot;too important&quot; to fight things like honor-duels anymore, although he certainly could if he wanted.<br /> <br /> Most Lords have retinues of Oathsworn retainers and armsmen for stuff like that, as well as general work. If/when Masanori swaps sides, that will be his position.<br /> <br /> Lord Iysen doesn't fight her own duels, for example. She had Nori for that, as well as her unlanded Oathsworn retainers in Teriake Castle (which is what Kasai was)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-24T09:44:30-07:00","id":1107339,"title":"","content":"er, had Nori? I thought he was found dead in the arena during the fire. But I understand the idea of a champion.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Like my Avatar? Courtesy of<span style=\"color:#0000FF\"> Jen</span>! : <span style=\"font-size:12px\">Anybody want their characters to be experimented on ? </span>post 98<br /> <i>An Exalt is never unarmed.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4018-l-het-esh","name":"L'het'esh","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4018_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"521"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-24T10:08:55-07:00","id":1107350,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>L'het'esh</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1107339#post1107339\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">er, had Nori? I thought he was found dead in the arena during the fire. But I understand the idea of a champion.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Yeah, Nori is dead, hence the 'had&quot;. When he was alive, he was her personal bodyguard, retainer, champion, etc (among other things)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-24T11:44:30-07:00","id":1107387,"title":"","content":"It would appear that most of you want some combination of 1 (push ahead) and 2 (protect flanks), on top of at least seeing what the track-gods are. Coolio<br /> <br /> -----------------------<br /> <br /> You take a long moment to ponder the best approach to this problem. Then, turning to the assembled Tribesmen, you ask,<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt; Just who, or what, are these \u2018track-gods\u2019 of which you speak?&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> The sub-chiefs glance at each other almost incredulously. \u201c&lt; Are you saying that you Vanehani do not know of the black spirits of the deep woods? We knew you didn\u2019t worship them, but <i>forgetting </i>them\u2026.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt;No, most Vanehani settlements are in the lowland river valleys. Most people stay out of the woods, for many good reasons. Tell me; what do these spirits do? How did they move your warband so fast? What did you do to appease them?&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> The Onset war-leader shrugs, \u201c&lt; They patrol the deep wilds, the lands untouched by humankind. If someone disturbs them without the proper ceremonies, they tend to retaliate violently, as they hate civilization and what it stands for. Whenever a new steading or path is built, we make offerings so they stay calmed. To appease them\u2026.. well, it depends on what we are doing. Constructing a new farm or clan-stead involves some lengthy ceremonies anyways, so we usually just fold the appeasement into those. For new trails or paths, we usually leave offerings of grain-spirits, bread and flesh at the edges of the forest. I don\u2019t know if the gods themselves take them, or other animals, but so long as the offerings are sufficient, they tend to leave us alone.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt; As for how they moved us so far, so fast\u2026.&gt;\u201d He chuckles uneasily. \u201c&lt; The Tribes might be more familiar with the actions of gods than you Vanehani, but that was \u2026. Something else. All the bushes and trees and rocks in the path of our travel <i>moved</i> out from before us, then moved back into their old positions behind us. We basically traveled as the crow flies from our new steadings to here, through the heart of the forest.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> You turn and look back at the Akibara again. The darkness under the trees has taken a decidedly unfriendly turn. \u201c&lt; These spirits\u2026. Are they <i>everywhere</i>? If I walk under the margins of the trees right now, will I meet one?&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> The Nemasket subchief shakes her head in affirmation. \u201c&lt; A possibility. At the very least, they will know you are there. You know how, when you go hunting in the deep woods, you sometimes get this feeling like you are being watched, like eyes are fixed on you, yet when you turn around, there is nothing there? That is the spirit, watching you to see what you do.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt; Will they respond to a summons?&gt;\u201d, only to get a shrug.<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt; We usually just leave offerings out for them, meeting them is <i>rare</i>. Generally, if you meet one face-to-face, they are unhappy about something. I don\u2019t think that has happened in living memory.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> You draw yourself upright, pulling <i>Moonlight Over Water</i> from its sheath. \u201c&lt; Well then, we had best get started. Will you join me in meeting with these spirits?&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> The warleaders agree, albeit reluctantly, and you call up one of the militiamen to fetch you \u201clunch\u201d from the camp canteen; a large kettle of noodles in broth, a jar of ale sweetened with apples, and a rack of pork ribs.<br /> <br /> It occurs to you as you walk into the margins of the Akibara, burdened under your load, that you might not be the best Immaculate.<br /> <br /> Almost as soon as you pass under the branches of the trees, the light dims substantially, and you have to squint to see more than a couple feet in front of you in the gloom. Unlike other forests, which are usually filled with the sounds of birds and other animals, interspersed with clearings and sunlight, the Akibara is \u2026\u2026 <i>dense</i>. Light struggles to penetrate the canopy above, and the thick turf and moss swallows the sound of your footsteps. In the gloom, there is little underbrush, and the trees and bamboo grows close and thick. The only sound you can hear is the whispering of wind, passing through the upper branches of the forest.<br /> <br /> Only a few moments after passing into the forest, you start to feel a crawling on your skin, like someone is watching, with great interest as to what your insides look like. You curse, drawing your lips back from your teeth in a snarl, and the Nemasket warchief lays a hand on her axe almost instinctively. She removes her hand, embarrassed, but the feeling doesn\u2019t abate.<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt; Well, they obviously know we are here, Chieftain.&gt;\u201d She says in a whisper.<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt; <i>Indeed</i>.&gt;\u201d Lips still drawn back from teeth, as you glance around the trees. You\u2019ve been in <i>plenty</i> of forests in your time, but never felt anything like this before!<br /> <br /> After a few more moments of struggling through the trees, you come into the closest thing the Akibara has to a clearing: a falling tree cleared a path through the canopy, letting some small amount of sunlight through. Instead of trees, the space is filled with a dense thicket of brambles, thornbushes, and small saplings, intertwined and struggling for the limited sunlight.<br /> <br /> A good a place as any.<br /> <br /> Setting down the packed offering, you spike your spear deep into the turf and, going down on both knees before the trees, speak loudly.<br /> <br /> \u201cI am the <i>Koshaku Qianke Kasai</i>, <i>Diamyo Jianghu-han</i>, the legally-recognized ruler of these lands. I call upon the spirits of the forest to hear my pleas, and appear before me now!\u201d<br /> <br /> The words are swallowed up by the woods without even an echo, and the feeling of being watched only intensifies. Regardless, nothing else happens, and you are about to stand up before the wind starts blowing. At first, you think it is \u2018just\u2019 the wind rustling through the exposed branches of the clearing, but as you listen, the rustling and creaking of leaves and branches starts forming words, a toneless voice coming from nowhere and everywhere at once.<br /> <br /> \u201c<i>I care not about your name, titles or laws, mortal, but I will hear your words. Leave your offerings and say your piece.\u201d</i><br /> <br /> Mouth suddenly dry, it takes you a moment to find your words.<br /> <br /> \u201cIn these woods, there is a grand encampment, of bandits and other law-breakers, murderers and criminals. I seek to wipe them out, in effort to protect my people. To that end, I need to move troops to their location, an action greatly hindered by the forest surrounding the hills. If you would help me, our presence in these woods would be minimal, and over-and-done with quickly.\u201d<br /> <br /> There is no answer from the whispering wind, and if it weren\u2019t for the skin crawling up and down your back, you would have thought the god left. Instead, the wind suddenly picks up, whipping the trees and leaves into a flurry of movement, shrieking and moaning in the gale. You lift an arm to protect yourself from the wind-raised leaves and detritus, but stop and stare in awe at the sight taking place before you.<br /> <br /> In the center of the clearing, the wind <i>condenses</i>, drawing scattered leaves, pine needles and twigs to itself. As the cyclone gathers strength, it pulls in black bog-water, larger sticks and branches, and the skull and antlers of a long-dead stag into itself.<br /> <br /> After but a few moments of shrieking wind, the gale subsides, and before you stands a large (easily horse-sized!) dog-like <i>thing</i> of the accumulated forest detritus. Long and lean, its \u2018fur\u201d is made of pine needles, its flesh of rotten leaves and bog-mud, its bones of white-and-black-mottled decaying branches. Its head is crowned by the skull and antlers of the deer, and as you watch in mounting awe (and not a spot of terror), the eyes of the skull blaze with a deep green light, the color of bamboo or oak leaves. Even ten feet away, all you can smell is the ripe odor of rotting vegetation and stagnant water long cut off from sunlight. It\u2026\u2026 doesn\u2019t look healthy (can gods even get sick?), with its \u2018ribs\u2019 clearly outlined under its \u2018skin\u2019, even exposed in some spots.<br /> <br /> The forest god huffs, stalking around you on silent footfalls, before sitting back on its haunches in front of the offerings. Silently, not from an unmoving mouth, but through the rustling leaves, it speaks;<br /> <br /> \u201c<i>Well then, mortal. I accept your offerings, at least for now, and show myself before you. Your words, and your intended actions, are of great concern to me. Long have I watched these interlopers, who cut down my trees and shit in my rivers. Due to ancient binding agreements with the place-gods and ancestor-ghosts of Dun Nashuon, as you call it, I have not been able to remove them from my forest.\u201d</i><br /> <br /> The god\u2019s lips pull back from its teeth in a feral grin, exposing the straggly-teeth of white-bleached branches, and you are blasted by it\u2019s fetid breath. It takes all you can to not show your disgust on your face, much less pull back.<br /> <br /> \u201c<i>You are not bound by the same agreements as I, are you?&quot;</i> It was not a question. \u201c<i>You can freely enter the boundaries of the walls, forcibly remove them from the fortress\u2026\u2026.\u201d</i><br /> <br /> The god looks away from you and starts feasting on the offerings, ravenously devouring the food and beer.<br /> <br /> \u201c<i>Fine, I will allow you to move through my forest unhindered. Although\u2026\u2026.</i>\u201d It pulls its head from the kettle, noodles and broth running in rivulets down through the muck.<br /> <br /> \u201c<i>I will make you the following proposition.\u201d</i> The blazing green eyes turn from you to the assembled Hill-Tribe chieftains, who all-but wither under its gaze.<br /> <br /> \u201c<i>These mortals made great sacrifice to me, so that they may pass through my territory at great speed. I would make you the same offer, if you sacrifice to me as they did.\u201d</i><br /> <br /> You gesture to the food and drink, confused, \u201cForgive me, spirit, but as far as I know, these foodstuffs are the correct offering. What, pray tell, did the Tribes offer?\u201d<br /> <br /> The god snorts, resuming its feast. \u201c<i>This offering was sufficient for me to allow you to enter these woods unmolested , nothing more. For anything else, I require a sacrifice with a bit more \u2026.. body.\u201d</i><br /> <br /> You shrug, still confused, \u201cI have draft-animals, oxen and donkeys. Would those be sufficient?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c<i>No, but they would be most welcome.\u201d</i> It grins again, then continues \u201c<i> In order to move the forest around your path, I require \u2026\u2026 more. More breath, more blood, more body. I require <b>Essence</b>.</i>\u201d<br /> <br /> It\u2019s jaws snap shut, shattering branch-teeth and scattering droplets of broth and bog-water over you.<br /> <br /> \u201c<i> As even you mortals can clearly tell, I am \u2026. Unwell. My physical corpus reflects the health of the Akibara, which is polluted and unclean with the spirits of the dead. The rotten stench of the Underworld permeates my soil, pollutes the water and kills the trees.\u201d</i><br /> <br /> You swallow, mouth dry. \u201cSpirit, what caused the Underworld to enter the lands of the living?\u201d<br /> <br /> The god snarls, \u201c<i>The spirits of the restless dead draw the Underworld to them, like moths to a flame. While the spirits walk, the Akibara is unclean, and I remain weak. Until I regain Essence, I cannot help you, not truly, as much as I would wish, for I want the mortal interlopers gone as much as you do.\u201d</i><br /> <br /> \u201cSo, what does this have to do with sacrifices, spirit?\u201d<br /> <br /> The god grins again, \u201c<i>The mortals behind you offered me the sacrifice of a still-living human, blood and bone, body and breath, so that they might move quickly and unhindered through the Akibara. If you make sacrifice of five mortals, that will be enough for me to move the very land itself, at least for a short while.</i>\u201d<br /> <br /> In sudden horror, you recoil, then glare at the assembled Tribesmen behind you. \u201c&lt;What the <i>fuck</i> did you do?&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt;What do you mean? None of us speak Riverspeak, so we couldn\u2019t follow along&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt; The god says you made <i>human sacrifice</i> to it!&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt;Oh, that.&gt;\u201d The chief shrugs, \u201c&lt; Yeah, we gave the spirit a bandit that was captured. They were going to be killed <i>anyways</i>, so we figured we might as well get some use from them.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> Disgusted, you turn your face away from the Tribesmen. Human sacrifice is <i>Anathemic</i> to civilized society.<br /> <br /> After regaining control of your emotions, you speak again to the god, \u2018Spirit, is there perhaps <i>any</i> other way we might come to this deal, so you might regain the necessary power?\u201d<br /> <br /> It rests its head on its forepaws, staring balefully at you, \u201c<i>Hmm\u2026. So long as the spirits of the dead wander the trees, I cannot regain Essence at a meaningful rate.\u201d</i><br /> <br /> \u201cJust\u2026.. where are these spirits of the dead coming from, exactly?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c<i>The tombs under Dun Nashuon groan with the spirits of the dead, mortal, disturbed from their slumber by the interlopers. Other than that, for the last few years, mortals have been leaving their old and weak in these woods to die, especially during the cold of winter. Most of <b>those</b> ghosts pass on quietly, but several have stayed here, protected from the light of the Unconquered Sun by the shade of the trees. If anything, those ghosts are more troublesome than the ones in Dun Nashuon, as they ceaselessly wander in wrath and sorrow, and spread the Essence of the Underworld wherever they travel.\u201d</i><br /> <br /> You sit, lost in thought. You\u2019ve never dealt with a ghost before, but the priests in the Immaculate Shrine in Liangzhou should know funerary rights. Or\u2026\u2026 the Akibara is one of the main places you planned to expand to, when the population of Jianghu grew too large for the river-valleys to hold. Maybe\u2026.. if you offer to build this god a shrine, and dedicate the farms and fields of the new homesteads to it, it would regain power that way? But those would take <i>time</i>.<br /> <br /> After a moment, you speak to the god.<ol class=\"decimal\"><li>I will offer you human sacrifice, as distasteful as I find it. You will have your \u2018Essence\u201d, as you put it, and in return, you will move the very land itself out of the way of my army. I need the fortress of Dun Nashuon in my hands within 2 weeks!</li> <li>I will put these spirits of the dead to rest, so you might regain \u201cEssence\u201d with the healing of the Akibara. In return, you will allow my army to build a road to Dun Nashuon.</li> <li>I eventually plan to settle these lands, spirit. If you allow my army to build a road to Dun Nashuon, I will make you the patron god of the new settlements, and of the Akibara Forest as a whole. I will build you a shrine in the town of Liangzhou, with regular worshippers, as well as shrines along the road. You will gain much more power through worship, as well as regular sacrifices of beer and grain, as much as you can eat!</li> <li>Yeah \u2026.. no, I\u2019m out. Human sacrifice and ghosts are too much for me.</li> <li>Something else? (write in)</li> </ol>OOC:<br /> <br /> The Akibara is based largely off the <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aokigahara\" target=\"_blank\">Aokigahara Forest</a> in Japan. It isn't a Shadowland yet, not <i>quite</i>, but things are ........not kosher.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11892,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","user":"Boston123","date":". 06-25-2017, 07:12 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-24T15:18:30-07:00","id":1107425,"title":"","content":"A combination of 1, 2 and 3. I reworked this once, so I hope on rereading later that I will think it came out okay:<br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\">I will offer you human sacrifice; although it is against my upbringing. You are part of Creation, so I will abide by your ways inside Akibara, and help you as I can. I do this partly because I plan to settle these lands, spirit. For now, with these who have already know your ways and have already sacrificed. Later there will be other setters. I will make you the patron god of the new settlements, and of the Akibara Forest as a whole. I will build you a shrine in each settlement. You will gain much more power through worship, and I will seek the aid of priests and shamans to send the wandering ghosts on. The abandoning of the sick and old will be forbidden. I would build a road to Dun Nashuon as well, so people could travel without disturbing you. But all of that is for later. For now, the other part is that I would have Dun Nashuon in my hands, as soon as possible. Speed my troops and the interlopers will be routed. The wakened spirits there will be put to rest.<br /> <br /> <u>I would be your ally, I and all of my line, as long as it continues.</u> I will make binding pacts, if you wish it. And if you prefer to wait and see how things turn out, I will also wait, upon your pleasure.</span>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Like my Avatar? Courtesy of<span style=\"color:#0000FF\"> Jen</span>! : <span style=\"font-size:12px\">Anybody want their characters to be experimented on ? </span>post 98<br /> <i>An Exalt is never unarmed.</i>","edited":{"userid":4018,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4018-l-het-esh","user":"L'het'esh","date":". 06-24-2017, 06:24 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4018-l-het-esh","name":"L'het'esh","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4018_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"521"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-24T16:34:48-07:00","id":1107443,"title":"","content":"Has this spirit given us any reason to believe it hates seeing the land cultivated? if not, then no 3 is a possibility.<br /> No to human sacrifices.<br /> Yes to getting rid of ghosts that are going to be a problem later anyway.<br /> Break out the salt and find either a shaman or a monk.<br /> Or another Goddamn Lunar.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3752-mushroom-king","name":"Mushroom King","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3752_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"221"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-24T17:08:22-07:00","id":1107451,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Mushroom King</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1107443#post1107443\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Has this spirit given us any reason to believe it hates seeing the land cultivated? if not, then no 3 is a possibility.<br /> No to human sacrifices.<br /> Yes to getting rid of ghosts that are going to be a problem later anyway.<br /> Break out the salt and find either a shaman or a monk.<br /> Or another Goddamn Lunar.</div> </div> </div> </div>OOC: It <i>is</i> a Dog of the Unbroken Earth (Corebook pg 511), a small-god of the wild places, so they tend to dislike roads and farms, progress of that nature, in general principle. Kasai has no idea what this is, and only knows that the deity doesn't like 'civilization' because the Tribesmen told him.<br /> <br /> They <i>can</i> ,however, be propitiated to ignore things like that for regular sacrifices of meat and alcohol. Which is what the Tribes (and many others) do, and what Kasai did.<br /> <br /> Plus, if Kasai actually orders a shrine/shrines built to it, which is <i>far</i> more 'worship' it would get, even for ignoring roads and farmsteads, it might be bribed to look the other way. Key word on the 'might'. They are <u>really</u> touchy about things like that.<br /> <br /> The Dog would ordinarily be able to deal with the ghosts, even unruly ones, without much trouble. They, in and of themselves, aren't <i>that</i> powerful: even in undeath, an angry old peasant woman is still an old peasant woman.<br /> <br /> It is the necrotic Essence of the Underworld permeating around the unburied bodies of the dead that is causing the problem, mostly. Again, the Akibara isn't a Shadowland, and overwhelmingly most of the forest is healthy, albeit creepy-as-fuck. The Dog knows where the bodies are, if just by the necrotic Essence surrounding them.<br /> <br /> Basically, this is like anything else: Kasai does the dog-god a favor, and it does him one. Functionally, he already got it's 'permission' to travel to Dun Nashuon, all this is extra.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11892,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","user":"Boston123","date":". 06-24-2017, 08:18 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-24T18:44:21-07:00","id":1107479,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Boston123</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1107451#post1107451\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> OOC: It <i>is</i> a Dog of the Unbroken Earth (Corebook pg 511), a small-god of the wild places, so they tend to dislike roads and farms, progress of that nature, in general principle. Kasai has no idea what this is, and only knows that the deity doesn't like 'civilization' because the Tribesmen told him.<br /> <br /> They <i>can</i> ,however, be propitiated to ignore things like that for regular sacrifices of meat and alcohol. Which is what the Tribes (and many others) do, and what Kasai did.<br /> <br /> Plus, if Kasai actually orders a shrine/shrines built to it, which is <i>far</i> more 'worship' it would get, even for ignoring roads and farmsteads, it might be bribed to look the other way. Key word on the 'might'. They are <u>really</u> touchy about things like that.<br /> <br /> The Dog would ordinarily be able to deal with the ghosts, even unruly ones, without much trouble. They, in and of themselves, aren't <i>that</i> powerful: even in undeath, an angry old peasant woman is still an old peasant woman.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /></div> </div> </div> </div>Aha! That's what I was wondering. I had actually used that exact creature in my only 2E campaign on my players but I didn't want to meta-game<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> (meta-quest? meta-story?). Well in that case, since we actually are planning to develop this area, we'd have to either kill or convert this guy to go along. I say we carefully make our sales-pitch and agree to deal with the necrotic essence, since both things help us out in the long run.<br /> Just my two cents.<br /> And still a fun read!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3752,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3752-mushroom-king","user":"Mushroom King","date":". 06-24-2017, 10:05 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3752-mushroom-king","name":"Mushroom King","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3752_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"221"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-24T23:22:08-07:00","id":1107537,"title":"","content":"2: I will put the spirits of the dead to rest.<br /> <br /> Human sacrifice is way uncool, even for the piss-poor excuse for an Immaculate we are, but dealing with the restless dead is definitely worth doing to start with, let alone for a road. Appeasing this god when it comes time to settle Akibara is a problem for future Kasai.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/649-nightwinder","name":"Nightwinder","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"154"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-25T07:06:41-07:00","id":1107624,"title":"","content":"Is there a reason we could've just make human sacrifice the method of execution for capital punishment? I doubt it's any cooler than any other form of execution. We could probably even stipulate some kind of humane execution method.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11567-jairain","name":"Jairain","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11567_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"345"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-25T07:53:24-07:00","id":1107633,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Jairain</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1107624#post1107624\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Is there a reason we could've just make human sacrifice the method of execution for capital punishment? I doubt it's any cooler than any other form of execution. We could probably even stipulate some kind of humane execution method.</div> </div> </div> </div>Considering how the Immaculate Faith finds the idea of human sacrifice abhorrent, it would probably be a bad idea.<br /> <br /> If it got out that Kasai was using capital executions as sacrifices to gods, he would almost certainly be declared Anathema. If a Wyld Hunt didn't come a-knockin', a lynch mob would certainly take him down.<br /> <br /> The Hill-Tribes (and other cultures that practice human sacrifice) can 'get away with it' specifically because they <u>aren't</u> Immaculate, and live a goodly distance away from Immaculates, on top of not practicing human sacrifice openly, or even all that often.<br /> <br /> Generally, capital punishment in the Hundred Kingdoms depends on what social class the perpetrator belongs to.<br /> <br /> Commoners will be hanged, generally speaking, using either the s<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hanging#Methods_of_judicial_hanging\" target=\"_blank\">uspension or short-drop method as opposed to the long-drop method</a> (WARNING: pictures of people being hanged) [this means the condemned will expire from strangulation as opposed to a broken neck], almost-always in public. Sometimes, but only rarely, commoners will be decapitated instead of hanged, but this is viewed as being more merciful than hanging, which is why it is far less common.<br /> <br /> Nobles, on the other hand, will almost always be decapitated in the rare case they get convicted of a capital offense, usually privately. A noble would have to fuck up <u>bad</u> in order to be executed publicly, like &quot;grand treason' fuck up. Anyways, many if not most nobles will commit suicide if they get word that they are going to be executed, as a private suicide is less painful, faster, and less embarrassing than an execution. Usually, a retainer will 'assist' them by decapitating the suicidal liegelord, and in fact many Magistrates will, effectively, execute people by sending an Oathsworn retainer to their place of residence, in a form of &quot;the writing on the wall&quot;.<br /> <br /> While the Hundred Kingdoms are based on feudal Japan, it doesn't have the same culture of suicide. Nobles committing suicide isn't <a href=\"http://tvtropes.org/pmwiki/pmwiki.php/Main/Seppuku\" target=\"_blank\">Seppuku</a> <i>per se</i>, but a means of avoiding unnecessary pain and embarrassment. If Kasai fucked up, pissed off the Lord Iysen, or dishonored himself, he wouldn't be ordered to commit suicide, for example.<br /> <br /> As the Marcher-Lord of Jianghu, Kasai is functionally 'above the law', in a sense. In Jianghu, he <u><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=itmNiTwHOsM\" target=\"_blank\">is</a></u> the law, and while people <i>can</i> bring cases against him...... he controls the Magistrate, and people would basically have to go to his boss, Lord Iysen, to have any real chance of getting a case heard. And, even then, there are functionally very few things he could actually get convicted for. He would have to basically stroll through town, cutting people down while singing <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hHsDKqKE_kc\" target=\"_blank\">&quot;I'm a Dentist&quot;</a> and giggling to himself before he could get slapped with a murder charge. Rape is another one. In other domains, he has less 'immunity' to conviction, but he is still a Lord (Duke-equivalent), and most charges against him will likely be dropped or settled out of court.<br /> <br /> Bandits, as people effectively outside the law (which is where the term 'outlaw' comes from) are, well, outside the protection of the law. In the Hundred Kingdoms, even the rudest of serfs is theoretically protected by the laws of the land, from murder and thievery and stuff like that. Bandits are not.<br /> <br /> Bandits in general are assumed to be murderers. rapists, thieves and scum like that, and hanging is the traditional method of dealing with them. Most bandits ultimately end up being the aforementioned scum, due to lack of legal protection (and associated ability to work within the law), and while many/most bandits are deserted soldiers, a decent portion of them are 'regular&quot; people fleeing from the law. If Papa gets charged with the murder of some guy in an accidental drunken taphouse brawl, he very well might flee the retainers of the Magistrate and go live in the woods for a while. Since he can't exactly work on the farm anymore (and while his family might try to support him as much as they can, illegal as that is), he overwhelmingly-likely is going to have to steal in order to survive.<br /> <br /> That is why Kasai offering a blanket pardon in return for military service is <u>such a goddamn good deal</u>. Overwhelmingly-most, close to all, bandits will end up dying either from wounds, sickness, or at the end of a rope. What he is offering is, basically, a new beginning.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11892,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","user":"Boston123","date":". 06-25-2017, 11:08 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-25T16:20:46-07:00","id":1107787,"title":"","content":"No human sacrifice, and no building temples to wild gods. Kasai isn't a great immaculate, but he has *some* standards.<br /> <br /> #2. Send a priest to cleanse the place. Probably should do that for the benefit of hunters and foragers anyhow.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>On the frontier of the Wild South, there's only one woman with the grit to take on its most dangerous outlaws and bring them <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/984977-back-alive-or-maybe-dead-quest\" target=\"_blank\">Back Alive, or Maybe Dead.</a><br /> <br /> Avatar by <a href=\"http://puzzlinghappenstance.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">K.S. Brenowitz</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/908-semicasual","name":"semicasual","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar908_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2499"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-26T13:59:52-07:00","id":1108136,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>semicasual</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1107787#post1107787\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">No human sacrifice, and no building temples to wild gods. Kasai isn't a great immaculate, but he has *some* standards.<br /> <br /> #2. Send a priest to cleanse the place. Probably should do that for the benefit of hunters and foragers anyhow.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Imma say hard pass to this option because we dont need to piss off the hill tribes who do utilize the god. I'd say we offer to petition the local immaculates to include this god on the calendar for feasts and shit within our region. If they're cool with it, we can maybe even establish small shrines or something.<br /> <br /> My real answers are:<br /> -Offer to petition local immaculates to put this guy on the local worship calendar<br /> -Offer to make a small sacrifice now but no humans. Maybe a horse or goat or something but nothing that gets us banned from the immaculate temples<br /> -Offer to put the ghosts and unruly dead to rest.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8897-joshopotamus","name":"joshopotamus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8897_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"97"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-26T17:08:10-07:00","id":1108191,"title":"","content":"I say we promise to put the spirits of the dead in the area to rest. Getting a monk or a priest to offer them an honorable burial should be relatively cheap...and if there are actually dangerous/maddened ghosts wandering our countryside it's our duty to deal with them anyway, and it's probably not a bad idea to hire a court exorcist/priest anyway-we're going to be making a lot more dead people and it's a good idea NOT to have ghost-bandits haunting our castle, y'know?<br /> <br /> No human sacrifices. Offer to propitiate the dead in exchange for fast travel and impeding the travel of your enemies. If the god questions either our intent to fulfill your end of the bargain or our capability to do so, swear to it upon our name and your spear, granted to you by your Lord and by Luna...and if we break our word may they both hunt us.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>So I'm making <a href=\"http://godkickingboot.smackjeeves.com/\" target=\"_blank\">God-Kicking Boot</a>, an Exalted webcomic, now. Updates on Sundays. Full-color, mediocre but slowly improving art. It's a thing.<br /> <br /> The absence of a monument can, in its own way, be something of a monument also.</i> -Roger Zelazny","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/512-wise-old-guru","name":"Wise Old Guru","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar512_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1097"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-26T17:31:08-07:00","id":1108194,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wise Old Guru</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1108191#post1108191\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I say we promise to put the spirits of the dead in the area to rest. Getting a monk or a priest to offer them an honorable burial should be relatively cheap...and if there are actually dangerous/maddened ghosts wandering our countryside it's our duty to deal with them anyway, and it's probably not a bad idea to hire a court exorcist/priest anyway-we're going to be making a lot more dead people and it's a good idea NOT to have ghost-bandits haunting our castle, y'know?<br /> <br /> No human sacrifices. Offer to propitiate the dead in exchange for fast travel and impeding the travel of your enemies. If the god questions either our intent to fulfill your end of the bargain or our capability to do so, swear to it upon our name and your spear, granted to you by your Lord and by Luna...and if we break our word may they both hunt us.</div> </div> </div> </div>This. But additionally we can petition the order to have him added to the schedule of worship as was mentioned earlier in exchange for future him not messing with future expansion.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11567-jairain","name":"Jairain","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11567_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"345"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-27T16:04:48-07:00","id":1108537,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wise Old Guru</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1108191#post1108191\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I say we promise to put the spirits of the dead in the area to rest. Getting a monk or a priest to offer them an honorable burial should be relatively cheap...and if there are actually dangerous/maddened ghosts wandering our countryside it's our duty to deal with them anyway, and it's probably not a bad idea to hire a court exorcist/priest anyway-we're going to be making a lot more dead people and it's a good idea NOT to have ghost-bandits haunting our castle, y'know?<br /> <br /> No human sacrifices. Offer to propitiate the dead in exchange for fast travel and impeding the travel of your enemies. If the god questions either our intent to fulfill your end of the bargain or our capability to do so, swear to it upon our name and your spear, granted to you by your Lord and by Luna...and if we break our word may they both hunt us.</div> </div> </div> </div>thirded. plus 10 characters.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3752-mushroom-king","name":"Mushroom King","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3752_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"221"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-27T16:17:26-07:00","id":1108539,"title":"","content":"<i>Technically</i>, the Dog-God can't do much in the way to actually help, as it lacks Essence as a result of the necrotic Underworld Essence seeping into the Akibara. More than that, it lacks a means to regain Essence, so long as the bodies remain unburied.<br /> <br /> Sacrifices and prayers will help the Dog-God regain Essence, human sacrifices the most of all. Just one sacrificed bandit was enough for the Dog-God to move 500 warriors across the forest, with a little to spare. 5 would be enough to let the Dog-God be, well, &quot;normal&quot; for quite some time. A ritually-sacrificed animal like an ox or a donkey wouldn't be enough for the above, but would definitely put Kasai on it's good side.<br /> <br /> For a deity (aka something that is already pretty inhuman in mindset) that is almost 100% antithetical to the desires and even needs of most humans, the Dog of the Unbroken Earth is actually being.... pretty nice, for a lack of a better term, even helpful. Mainly because it actually needs Kasai's help pretty bad, but.....","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11892,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","user":"Boston123","date":". 06-27-2017, 07:22 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-27T18:27:52-07:00","id":1108578,"title":"","content":"Just so we're clear, would dealing with the dead right now help us move afterwards, and would getting him some kind of worship or veneration help us with his mood in the future?<br /> And yeah, Dogs of the Unbroken Earth <i>hate</i> civilization, if I recall correctly. I'm just glad we're not fighting him right now.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3752-mushroom-king","name":"Mushroom King","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3752_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"221"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-27T19:09:13-07:00","id":1108592,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Mushroom King</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1108578#post1108578\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Just so we're clear, would dealing with the dead right now help us move afterwards, and would getting him some kind of worship or veneration help us with his mood in the future?<br /> And yeah, Dogs of the Unbroken Earth <i>hate</i> civilization, if I recall correctly. I'm just glad we're not fighting him right now.</div> </div> </div> </div>1) Would it allow the Dog-God to move our army <i>right now</i>? No. It still has to regain Essence, and that is dependent on the Akibara 'healing' from the necrotic Essence infesting it. Think of this whole thing like an infection (which is a pretty apt comparison). Even when an infection is treated, the body still has to recover.<br /> <br /> Whereas, sacrificing a/multiple humans to the Dog-God would be the metaphorical Essence equivalent of a booster-shot, or a shot of morphine or epinephrine: The 'infection' is still there, and the cause of the 'disease' is still there. All you are doing is treating symptoms ...... but it will certainly make the patient feel better, take the edge off.<br /> <br /> 2) Dealing with the dead isn't going to happen <i>right now</i> either. There is an Immaculate priest back at the Shrine in Liangzhou, but that is, even if Kasai sends a retainer on a fast horse, at least a week away, there and back.<br /> <br /> Kasai doesn't have a week, or, more truthfully, a week to putt around and wait for the priest to show up, much less lead the priest to where the bodies are located, which are scattered across the margins of the Akibara forest almost the length of Jianghu.<br /> <br /> Dealing with these restless ghosts is a <i>long-term</i> thing, not something Kasai can handle on the way back to camp. <br /> <br /> The whole premise of dealing with the restless ghosts is functionally a &quot;I'll scratch your back, you scratch mine&quot; type-deal. Kasai will (eventually) restore the Akibara forest to its proper status, allowing the Dog-God to 'heal', and the Dog-God will allow Kasai to clear a path to Dun Nashuon, and eventually build a road. That is about the maximum to which the Dog of the Unbroken Earth will agree to at this point in time. As far as it is concerned, it is on the 'giving' side of the deal already<br /> <br /> 3) Certainly. <u>All</u> gods appreciate worship/veneration, as it directly ties into their Essence and power. For example; the food and drink Kasai offered as sacrifices? As a deity, the Dog-God doesn't actually <u>have</u> to eat. It just 1) likes to, and 2) gains a small amount of Essence from the offering. Larger offerings = more Essence.<br /> <br /> 4) Now..... this is complicated.<br /> <br /> As a general rule, Dogs of the Unbroken Earth <u>strongly dislike</u> civilization, you are correct. However, as long as they are properly propititated, they will <u>generally</u> leave development alone. Generally.<br /> <br /> The level of development to which Kasai is planning on putting the Akibara under (not clear-cutting, but clearing land for small-scale farming, hunting, logging and building mills, etc), are the type and level of things that the Dog-God will almost-certainly flat-out not agree to, probably violently. However........ if Kasai were to declare the Dog-God the patron spirit of the Akibara, and the activities therein (or, more likely, pressured the local Immaculate priest into giving it a holy festival day and building some shrines, etc etc etc) the Dog-God would be ..... more amenable. <u>Especially</u> if Kasai did more favors for the Dog-God (made more and regular sacrifices, took out its competition, etc).<br /> <br /> As for fighting the Dog of the Unbroken Earth.......it <u>is</u> pretty weak right now. For a metaphorical equivalent, picture yourself trying to fight with pneumonia and a case of the flu.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-02T13:34:51-07:00","id":1110645,"title":"","content":"Most of you want to deal with the wandering wrathful ghosts in return for a road. Other than that, installing the Dog of the Unbroken Earth as a guardian/patron-god of the new settlement is largely up to the local Immaculate Faith. Kasai will have to cross that bridge when he comes to it.<br /> ---------<br /> You shake your head vehemently, as much to banish the idea of human sacrifice from your mind as to get your point across.<br /> <br /> \u201cSpirit, even were I not a follower of the Immaculate Faith, I would not be willing, much less comfortable, with the idea of human sacrifice. \u201c<br /> <br /> \u201c<i>Pah, I do not understand you mortals, and likely never will. You live such short lives, and you all die in the end. Why not make something useful from that death?\u201d</i><br /> <br /> You bristle, reminded closely of the mocking words of the Lunar Anathema. Are all gods so callous? Either the god doesn\u2019t notice, or doesn\u2019t care, and goes back to eating the offerings.<br /> <br /> It ignores you kneeling there for a few long moments, then ,\u201c<i>I do believe we are done here, mortal. We have made our choices \u2026\u2026 or was there something else?\u201d</i><br /> <br /> \u201cThere was, spirit. My soldiers are not the woodswise warriors of the Dumoniapocasset, that can slip between the trees and not get lost. If you want these interlopers removed from your woods, we will have to build a road. To th\u2026\u201d<br /> <br /> You stop when you hear the muted growling of the Dog-God rumbling from deep within the brass kettle of soup. Slowly, almost nonchalantly, it pulls its head from within the container and cants it at an angle, staring at you all the while. While its ears fall flat against its skull, and the muzzle pulls back in wrinkles and folds, its voice betrays no emotion, staying even and level all the while.<br /> <br /> \u201c<i>A road? When the interlopers pollute the Akibara already with their very presence, you want, even <u>expect</u>, me to allow you to build a <u>road</u>?\u201d </i>It growls through exposed snag-teeth \u201c<i>Such impertinence! Where I not weighed down by your offerings, I would slay you where you kneel\u2026..\u201d</i><br /> <br /> You shrug, \u201cNo deal, then. Unfortunate.\u201d<br /> <br /> Its ears prick up. \u201c<i>What do you mean, \u2018no deal\u2019?</i>\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cI will not force my soldiers to bushwhack through the forests, subject to ambush and assault at any time, all for a god that won\u2019t even offer a fair trade. If you would let me continue?.....\u201d<br /> <br /> The growling continues, but the god stays quiet.<br /> <br /> \u201cAs I was saying; In return for you letting my soldiers build a road, I will order these bodies and restless ghosts laid to rest. The Akibara will heal, in time, and you will regain this \u201cEssence\u201d, as you put it.\u201d<br /> <br /> You gesture towards the north. \u201cYou want these interlopers gone, the ghosts laid to rest? Good, <i>so do I</i>. We each have something the other wants, why not work together? Plus, it isn\u2019t like I am expecting you to build the damn thing, or even help. One road, <i>one</i>, in return for the removal of the polluters, and the exorcism of the angry spirits of the dead.\u201d<br /> <br /> The growling slowly stops, and the god stands and paces back and forth, dripping muck and brown pine needles as it walks. It stops within a few feet of you and leans close.<br /> <br /> \u201c<i>One road, that is all you want?\u201d</i><br /> <br /> \u201cFor now, yes.\u201d You admit. You would rather ask for permission, in a sense, than beg for forgiveness.<br /> <br /> Its fangs snap shut again, \u201c<i>For now?</i>\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cYes, spirit, for now. I fully expect the Tribes to settle in Dun Nashuon, and I have no control over what they do. Other than that, the Akibara forest is one of the places I expect new settlers to Jianghu to move to, and, if I may be honest, I have functionally no control over what they do either. New roads, new farms, new homes, all are coming, spirit. It is your choice whether you want to join the progress \u2026. Or get swept aside.\u201d<br /> <br /> It snarls, snapping its jaws mere inches away from your face. In spite of the smell and passing death, you keep your face as stoic as possible, wiping the mud and broth from your brow with little more expression than mild disdain. To show weakness now is death, you learned that from the Lunar.<br /> <br /> \u201c<i>Are you <b><u>threatening me</u></b>, mortal?\u201d</i><br /> <br /> \u201cI would never dream of it, spirit. Perhaps my words were unclear, and I apologize for the confusion. Believe you me; this is a <i>warning</i>, a friendly one, not a <i>threat</i>.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cI can prohibit people from entering the Akibara Forest, enact laws and mete out strict punishments, but\u2026.. well, people are going to do it <i>anyways</i>, something both you and I need to recognize. It <i>will</i> happen, spirit\u2026\u2026 and, mayhaps, if you were to allow these developments, you would be lauded, both by the inhabitants and by the Immaculate Faith, as the patron of the Akibara Forest, with feast-days and regular offerings both <i>far</i> outstripping your current measly fare. Of course\u2026.. such an occurance would depend on our relationship remaining\u2026. cordial, a course of action I am <i>very</i> interested in pursuing.\u201d<br /> <br /> The god pulls its jaws away from your face and resumes its pacing, snarling and growling to itself, the trees and leaves above roiling in sympathy. After a long time (as in, the sun has sank closer to the horizon), the god abruptly ceases its pacing and turns its head to you.<br /> <br /> \u201c<i>Feast days? Days, as in plural?\u201d</i><br /> <br /> \u201cSure.\u201d You shrug, \u201cAll of this is ultimately dependent on how the Immaculate Faith receives word of your behavior, both in general and towards the new development. All I can do is pass along information, I have no control over them.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c<i>Is not one of your titles \u2018Lord of Jianghu\u2019? How can you have no control?\u201d</i><br /> <br /> You shrug again, \u201cMy hands are largely bound, spirit, by circumstance, tradition and jurisdiction. My realm is the physical, while the Faith deals with the spiritual.\u201d<br /> <br /> You are surprised to hear the god laugh, \u201c<i>At least I can gain some comfort from knowing everything is alike. The Unconquered Sun rules in Heaven, yet is restricted by His duties more tightly than any other god, or so I am told\u2026\u201d</i><br /> <br /> The god goes silent again, looking off into the woods. \u201c<i>\u2026..I really have no choice, do I? I give up part of my domain, for the <b>chance</b> of reimbursement in the future. Or I resist\u2026\u2026 and the Wyld Hunt comes and slays me. That is no true choice\u2026..\u201d</i><br /> <br /> The god sighs, \u201c<i>Very well, you may have your road, in return for exorcising the restless ghosts and removing the interlopers. And\u2026..\u201d</i> It looks side-glance at you, slyly. \u201c<i>Two, no <b>three</b>, oxen. As for these future developments\u2026.that remains to be seen. We will be in touch.\u201d</i>And, without another word, the god goes back to eating, its body language clearly demonstrating that the conversation was over. After a few moments of waiting, just to be sure, you rise (slowly and painfully, using your spear as support) to your feet and start making your way back to camp.<br /> <br /> Either your facial expression or your angry outburst cowed them, because the Tribal warleaders do little other than fall into line behind you. Your little group is silent until you reach camp and your pavilion, where you call for a meeting with your retainers and captains.<br /> <br /> When everyone is gathered, you start to speak, with Shin Takeshi providing a running translation to the Dumoniapocasset warleaders.<br /> <br /> \u201c So\u2026. It appears that the Hill-Tribes have decided to be neighborly and provide some hefty assistance in our undertaking to capture Dun Nashuon. Several of you mentioned your \u2026.. unease with their presence, but be assured that they will keep a <b>tight</b> (you glance at the warchiefs, who quickly nod in assurance when your words are translated) control over their warriors. I expect you captains and officers to maintain a high degree of order and discipline over the upcoming campaign, and if there are any issues, I want to hear about them before they become a problem. The last thing I want or need is a brawl happening in the middle of the Akibara forest, while we are cut off from reinforcement or supply. Is that understood?\u201d<br /> <br /> Your unit-commanders quickly affirm their understanding, and you continue.<br /> <br /> \u201cExcellent. Now\u2026.. any business I should know about? How goes the setting up of camp? How is our materiel situation? The morale of the men?\u201d<br /> <br /> Several of your Oathsworn retainers and Sergeant Oxblood step forwards.<br /> <br /> \u201cMy Lord, the camp is set up, with pickets and regular sentry-patrols. The men are getting bedded down, but we haven\u2019t had a chance to order latrines or anything of that nature built as of yet.\u201d<br /> <br /> You nod, and another retainer speaks, checking from a slip of paper.<br /> <br /> \u201cConcerning materiel and logistics\u2026. Between the vaults at Yagura Castle and the surrounding tax-barns, we have roughly four weeks of food packed and ready to move out, five and a half if we enact restricted-rations.\u201d<br /> <br /> You interrupt, \u201cDo those numbers include the milita and the Hill-Tribes?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c\u2026. No, my Lord. Forgive me.\u201d, as his expression fills with consternation.<br /> <br /> After Shin Takeshi translates, the Nemasket warleader pipes up, \u201c&lt; Chieftain, our warriors have food enough for us, and the youth-bands can hunt and forage as we travel.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> You frown, \u201c&lt; Will that be enough to feed 1500-plus warriors for possibly two weeks?&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> She shrugs, \u201cQuite possibily, the Akibara is rich, richer than the mountains, with game aplenty.\u201d She takes a moment to think, \u201c&lt; Now, we don\u2019t gather ordinarily in such numbers to wage war, but even still\u2026\u2026 they should be able to bring in enough game to feed everyone.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> You sigh, \u201c&lt;\u2019Should\u2019 is the operative word there. I am not comfortable ordering an expedition if there is the possibility we can\u2019t sustain i..&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> The retainer with the list stands up abruptly from where he was scratching on the paper slip.<br /> <br /> \u201cMy Lord, forgive my interruption, but even with the addition of the militia <i>and</i> the Tribes, we still have enough food for \u2026\u2026. Ah, about ten days, two weeks on half-rations.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cDid you take from <i>all</i> the tax-barns, or just from the ones surrounding Yagura Castle?\u201d<br /> <br /> Shin Takeshi chimes in, \u201cAll the tax-barns this side of the White River, Lord Qianke. While bringing the militia back from Yagura, I took the liberty of ordering the food and other supplies packed for use.\u201d<br /> <br /> You nod, \u201cSo, there is other materiel available on the south banks of the White River?\u201d<br /> <br /> He nods in response, \u201cYes, Lord. We didn\u2019t even touch that.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cVery well. Shin, take two regiments of militia at first light tomorrow and gather up whatever materiel remains south of the White River. By the time you get back to the Bulrush, the army should hopefully be engaged at Dun Nashuon, so proceed with all possible haste.\u201d<br /> <br /> He acknowledges your order with a bow, and you continue. \u201c Very well. How is the morale of the army?\u201d<br /> <br /> Sergeant Oxblood answers, \u201cPretty average, M\u2019Lord. They are annoyed to be campaigning at the end of summer, <i>after</i> they were already released from their duties earlier, but on the other hand, they are excited to be taking the fight to the enemy. \u2018About godsdamned time\u2019, was how one soldier put it. So long as the weather and food holds, we <i>should</i> be all-set with regards to morale.\u201d<br /> <br /> You grunt, \u201cGood to hear, Sergeant, thank you.\u201d After taking a moment to organize your thoughts, you speak, \u201cWell, captain, it is evidently a good thing you didn\u2019t have the time to build latrines or a palisade yet, as we won\u2019t be getting very much use of them.\u201d Stepping back from the camp-table, you pace back and forth in front of your officers.<br /> <br /> \u201cDun Nashuon lies almost directly to the north of this encampment, about 20 miles through the Akibara Forest. According to scouting reports from the Tribes, the forest is <i>severely</i> overgrown, making travel exceedingly difficult at the best of times. Even the land next to the banks of the Bulrush are thick and wild, and even the Tribes had trouble moving through them.\u201d<br /> <br /> One captain raises his hand, \u201cMy Lord, how then are we going to attack the fortress? The wagons will not make it through the scrub\u2026.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cSimply put; we will build a road through the forest. And, we will not use wagons. Instead, the soldiers will carry necessary materiel, and the wagons will be left behind, so that sir Shin Takeshi can use them.\u201d<br /> <br /> There is a sudden rush of chatter from the officers, and you silence it with an upraised hand.<br /> <br /> \u201cAs the good Sergeant Oxblood describes, the Shogunate-of-old had its warriors carry a supply of necessary materiel of their persons. Not just food, but camp-building supplies, tools, etc. That is what I am planning on having the army do. Those soldiers that do not wish to carry materiel will be tasked with clearing a track for travel. The choice is theirs. Any questions?\u201d<br /> <br /> This time, there is little chatter. \u201cExcellent! I plan on heading out as soon as possible, with the call-to-order being given at dawn. With that in mind, you are dismissed. With hard work and not a little luck, we can very possibly be at Dun Nashuon in 4 days. Keep the men motivated and driven, and that could be even less.\u201d<br /> <br /> As your retainers file out, to see to their regiments, you buttonhole the Hill-Tribe warleaders, as well as Aya Cho. Apprehensively, they wait for you to speak.<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt; I know that I have no legitimate command over your forces, but if possible I would like for your youth-bands to assist in this road-building effort.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> They blink in surprise, then sag in relief, \u201c&lt;\u2026.. Not a problem, Chief. What exactly did you have in mind?&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt; Your warriors know the terrain a bit better, and can move through it faster, than mine own. They can pick out the fastest path possible, meaning it will take less time to build this road. On top of that, they can also provide flank security. Getting ambushed in the middle of the Akibara doesn\u2019t sound like a good time in the least.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt;What about the Chosen? We are already carrying our own supplies\u2026&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt;Eh\u2026. Central security, rear-guard? Whatever works best, I suppose.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> They bow again, \u201c&lt; It will be done, Chief.&gt;\u201d, and they file out.<br /> <br /> Turning to Aya, \u201c I have a specific task for you, Miss Cho. I need you to travel to Liangzhou and meet with the Immaculate priests there. I would like to meet with them as soon as possible, so if they would agree to travel with you, please bring them back to me with all haste.\u201d<br /> <br /> She raises an eyebrow, \u201cOf course, Lord Qianke \u2026. But what do you require an Immaculate monk for?\u201d<br /> <br /> You shake your head, \u201cI do not want to reveal too much, Miss Cho, not until I meet with the monks. Be assured that the issue will be handled, but please treat this with the utmost secrecy! I do not want this getting out to the army\u2026.\u201d<br /> <br /> She nods, \u201cOf course, my Lord. Do you want me to bring the monk to you, or to Yagura Castle?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c\u2026.. Yagura Castle would probably be for the best. Thank you\u201d, and she heads off.<br /> <br /> Apprehensively, you retire for the night, yet can\u2019t seem to get to sleep very easily or well, and you toss and turn all night. Either you have too much on your mind, or you have become soft and spoiled with your featherbed back in Yagura Castle, and you are awoken by the blast of reveille on bugles and trumpets. Grumbling, you stagger from your pallet and pull on clothing, ears already filled with the shouts and curses of those breaking down camp.<br /> <br /> After breakfast (wintergreen tea with trailbread.), you mount Xuansu and ride over to the storage depot. There, you order the release of the three promised oxen, for the Dog-God, and lead them to the clearing from yesterday. You ignore the mystified looks from several of the soldiers, and leave the oxen tied to the trees in the middle of the clearing. You are sure the Dog-God will be able to figure out what to do with them, and you want to get to work.<br /> <br /> Back at the now-broken-down encampment, you find the soldiers hard at work under the instruction of your officers. Surprisingly, almost-certainly due to the pathfinding of the Tribal youth-bands, the army has already cut a trail several hundred feet into the Akibara. Granted, the trail is precisely that: a track, little more than 20 feet across, looping around trees too large to fell, but, hey, progress is progress. You certainly aren\u2019t going to complain.<br /> <br /> Upon a closer look, it would appear that the youth-bands and track-cutters chose their path wisely, as the track follows the course of the Bulrush as close as possible, where the sunlight and thinner soil prevented many trees of appreciable size from growing. Instead, the workers cut and pull out masses on masses of bushes and saplings, which while certainly more painful to remove than proper trees, are <i>much</i> faster. The few trees and boulders that are in the way are used to mark the sides of the track, or to fill in low spots and mud-holes. You even see more than a few Hill-Tribe Chosen among the workforce, and can hear a rough pidgin of Riverspeak and Forest-tongue being spoken, especially between the younger members of both peoples.<br /> <br /> While the two cultures can\u2019t quite \u2018get along\u2019 on a large scale (although you are certainly going to try to change that if you can!), it would appear that on the small scale, things can be \u2026.. alright. Maybe by fighting together, both sides might be able to appreciate each other a little more, although that might just be your idealism talking.<br /> <br /> Every so often (mainly when you get bored and sore from riding up and down the new track), an odd mood strikes you and you go to help the workers. Initially, the commoners are\u2026..leery about having nobility so close and so familiar, but, shirtless and swordless as you are (you took off your sword to work, but it remains close by. You aren\u2019t stupid), they eventually warm up to your presence. After a few jokes and pulls on a bottle of liquor and a smoldering pipe, they all-but forget you are there, at least until the work-gang goes on break and new workers come up. Then, arm and fingers aching, you return to your inspections, but try to at least help out every so often.<br /> <br /> The first day, you are surprised to find out the army has cut a path a little less than 5 miles into the Akibara, a little under a third of the way to the Toge Hills and Dun Nashuon. When you bring this up to Sgt. Oxblood, he grins.<br /> <br /> \u201c Well, it certainly helps that the ground we are marching through is flat. If it was hilly <i>and</i> forested, we would probably still be within a couple hundred feet of the edge of the treeline.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cBut still.\u201d,you protest, \u201cThat doesn\u2019t explain how we are moving so fast.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cAre you complaining, M\u2019Lord?\u201d He laughs, \u201cTrust me, sometimes things just work out, <i>especially</i> when you stack the deck in your favor, like we did here.\u201d<br /> <br /> Frowning, \u201cWhat do you mean?\u201d<br /> <br /> He holds up a finger, \u201cOne: You have <i>damn good</i> forward scouts, who can pick out a good trail and provide terrain information at the same time. Two: You have <i>damn good</i> flank screens. You haven\u2019t heard a peep about any bandits, much less word of an ambush, have you?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c\u2026. No, as a matter of fact, I haven\u2019t.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cAnd they would tell you if they encountered trouble, right?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cI would hope so!\u201d<br /> <br /> He waves the thought off, \u2018Either way, having proficient forward scouts and flank screens means the army can move faster in general, as both command and the rank-and-file can be confident in their security. Men not afraid of getting attacked march faster, and in this case, work harder. I, personally, prefer to have a <i>lot</i> of lighter troops, so they can serve as scouting-and-screening in-depth. Having a lot of heavier troops for offense is important, don\u2019t get me wrong, but many commanders, the Lord Iysen included, have a tendency to \u2026\u2026..<i>neglect</i> their lighter troops. I don\u2019t understand that. Scouts and screens are the eyes and ears of any army, and having a strong punch means nothing if you don\u2019t know where to put it. Not to mention that it is easier to knock out an opponent if they are bleeding and terrified first.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c If possible, M\u2019Lord, I strongly recommend you try to recruit some of these boys, or at least train some soldiers like them. They are arguably some of the best light infantry I have seen, and I have seen a <i>lot</i>. Dragons, they move like ghosts!\u201d<br /> <br /> You grin, \u201cI will be sure to pass along the compliments, Sergeant. Any other words of wisdom?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cHmm\u2026.. well, the fact that the scouts and screens are of the Tribes certainly helps, as it allows you to put more of <i>your</i> forces to work building this road. On top of that, you are effectively freed from logistical problems, since the soldiers are carrying most of what they need. I\u2019m glad you decided to follow my advice, not too many nobles like to listen to their lessers.\u201d<br /> <br /> Chuckling, \u201cMy father always told me that nobility comes from one\u2019s character, not from one\u2019s birth. Besides, I put my pants on the same way you do, and I have worked with my hands and head before. I would like to think I am not some airheaded aristocrat, prattling about poetry and suchlike.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cOn top of that, not heeding expert advice is\u2026\u2026<i>foolish</i>. If I want to grow crops, I would ask a farmer what they thought. If I want to lead an army, why not ask the expert? The gods know I don\u2019t know what I am doing. In a sense, it is almost like you are commanding me.\u201d<br /> <br /> He laughs, \u201cWell, to be honest M\u2019Lord, that is usually how it works. The nobles command the army, but it is the sergeants that <i>run</i> the army, if you catch my drift. In a way, it is the sergeants of a regiment that command it, only instead of giving commands like we do to the men, we phrase them like suggestions and end them with \u2018My Lord\u2019. A good commander <i>always </i>listens to the advice and suggestions of his sergeants, and while a regiment can survive a bad officer, you want to have as many good sergeants as possible.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cI will keep that in mind, Sergeant.&quot;<br /> <br /> The army camps right on the track, packed in tightly along the trail for over half a mile. At least wood and water are plentiful. The next day, they get right back to work, and this day is more of the same; cutting and pulling aside bushes and saplings, yanking out rocks and roots. This evening, you are a little over 12 miles, with not a single hide nor hair of a bandit yet.<br /> <br /> This last bit leaves you a little concerned. While you are certainly <i>glad</i> to have not been ambushed yet, this yet-unbroken string of good, even <i>great</i>, luck has to have an end. After all, doesn\u2019t your book on philosophy say, \u201cAll things must end\u201d?<br /> <br /> You just hope it doesn\u2019t end at Dun Nashuon. Privately, away from the eyes and ears of the soldiers, you say a few quick prayers to Mercury and Saturn, Maidens of Journeys and of Endings.<br /> Hell, you already pray to Luna, what is another two Incarnae? You are already a piss-poor Immaculate, why not add to the pile\u2026.. at this rate, you are going to be a first-class heretic before the year is out.<br /> <br /> The next day, it rains, a late-summer thunderstorm that drums on the Bulrush and sets the forest to howling. Several trees <i>explode</i> in the tempest, and your army does little more than huddle in their tents and shelters, waiting for the rain to stop.<br /> <br /> Next morning, the skies mostly clear, and the soldiers set off into the sucking muck, eager to make up lost time. The mud pulls at boots and roots, but even you notice a definite increase in the slope of the track. Boulders rise up into outcroppings that soon rival the trees in height, and said trees start slowly shrinking, becoming more gnarled and twisted by wind. The Bulrush picks up speed between the outcroppings, dropping over several waterfalls in leaps and bounds.<br /> <br /> Eventually, the land itself starts rising up into hills and ridges, smaller at first, then larger, and you actually start catching up to the Tribal forward scouts. They tell you that they can smell and see the smoke from Dun Nashuon from the bare tops of the ridges, and you eagerly join them in scrambling to the top (which you realize to be a mistake about halfway up, as your arm and hips start screaming). However, once you do reach the exposed summit, you marvel at the view.<br /> <br /> Away from you in all directions, the Akibara falls away in great undulating waves, broken only by the bare rock of ridges and hills, which increase in height to the north. There, squinting and shielding your eyes from the sun, you can <i>just</i> make out a grey smudge above the horizon.<br /> <br /> Smoke, from a multitude of campfires.<br /> <br /> While you are exhilarated to finally see your target at last, you can\u2019t help but feel a sense of trepidation. If you can see the smoke from fires this far off\u2026\u2026 how many fighters are encamped there?<br /> <br /> The skirmishers of the Tribal youth-bands tell you that you can\u2019t be much farther than a day or two from Dun Nashuon, and that several of the bands have actually been within sight of the fortress.<br /> \u201c&lt; What do you mean, \u2018within sight\u2019? Were you spotted?!&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> The warrior laughs, and you have to remember you are dealing with <i>teenagers</i> here. You certainly thought you were invincible at that age \u2026\u2026 and only stopped thinking so very recently.<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt; No, Vanehani. We kept to the trees.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt;\u2026.. I see. Any information for me?&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> The warrior shrugs, \u201c&lt; The fortress is on top of a hill, obviously, with a stone-and-wood palisade. The wall is only strong in several spots, but it looks like they have been trying to fix it. This stream flows beneath the hill, and there are several smaller hills and forests around it.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt; Any sign of the fighters?&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt;Sure. There are a lot of people inside the fortress,, but they don\u2019t appear to be doing very much.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt;Anything else? Any buildings?&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> He shrugs again, \u201c&lt; There was a <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Broch\" target=\"_blank\"><i>broch</i></a> at the highest point, but it was mostly ruined. There were also a bunch of stone buildings inside the palisade, but they appeared to be mostly ruined as well. A lot of bamboo and wooden shelters inside the walls, from what I could see at least. It was hard to see <i>inside</i> the walls, as the hill the fortress is on is the highest in the area.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt;\u2026.. very good. How much longer do you think it will take us to get there?&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt;It would only take us a few hours to get there from here, but we are faster than you are. The army?..... probably a day, maybe a little more. The trees start clearing out up ahead, and so long as we stick close to the river, things are pretty easy-going.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> You bring this info back to your captains (after <i>slowly</i> scrambling down the ridge, mostly on your ass. There goes a good pair of pants!), who are elated to hear the good news. You allow them to disseminate the word among the men, in effort to try to pick up their pace. Thankfully, they oblige, eager to feel the sunlight on their face after the gloom of the Akibara.<br /> <br /> True to the word of the scouts, the terrain opens up, even as it rises up and becomes rougher, and the army picks up speed as it no longer has to cut a path through the woods. After but a day of following the river between the hills (\u2018<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tor_(rock_formation)\" target=\"_blank\">tors</a>\u2019, in the local dialect), you find yourself riding Xuansu out into a relatively-broad valley, surrounded by the rocky, wind-swept hills. Dominating the entire valley is the fortress of Dun Nashuon.<br /> <br /> The description of the scouts does not do the place justice, to be frank. Even with its mostly-ruined palisade, the stronghold projects an aura of strength fully capable of drawing the eye. As your army sets up camp well outside of bowshot, you glare at the fortress, pondering the best course of action.<br /> <br /> How the <i>hell</i> are you going to do this?<br /> <br /> You are startled from your reverie by the shouts of one of your retainers.<br /> <br /> \u201cWhat is it?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cRiders approaching, my Lord, from the north!\u201d<br /> <br /> <i>\u201cWell</i>\u201d, you think to yourself as you mount Xuansu, \u201c<i>Let us see if Masanori is true to his word</i>.\u201d<br /> <br /> Once you are firmly in the saddle, wearing bits of borrowed armor, you ride out surrounded by your retainers and the Hill-Tribe warleaders, with the banner of Jianghu flying. In the middle of the valley, equidistant between Dun Nashuon and your encampment, your entourage stops, glaring at the other group slowly riding towards you.<br /> <br /> It is a \u2026\u2026. motley group, to say the least. The one in the middle is almost certainly Masanori, in the heaviest armor and mounted on the most capable horse. Everyone else is in odds and ends, and mounted on a disparate collection of draft horses, donkeys and even mountain-ponies.<br /> <br /> The other group stops, and Masanori rides forwards. You do the same, meeting him between your two retinues. He does nothing other than take you in for a moment, then says,<br /> <br /> \u201cWell, you weren\u2019t kidding about the number of troops. How many Hill-Tribe warriors are there? <i>Dragons</i>\u2026\u2026\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cThere is enough, Masanori. I trust you are keeping to your Oath?\u201d<br /> <br /> He waves it off, \u201cOf course, of course. Let me fall back, and we will join you soon enough. Any longer, and my captains will get suspicious.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c\u2026 Very well. I expect to see you soon.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cCount on it, my Lord.\u201d, and he turns his horse about.<br /> <br /> In a way, you are thankful for your suspicions, as you keep Xuansu firmly pointed at the bandit-entourage. This allows you to see Masanori pluck his bow and an arrow from his saddle, wheel about in the stirrups, and draw and loose in a single smooth motion. Even with the forewarning, it is all you can do to bring your shield up in time, and the arrow impacts the lenticular face with an odd, wet <i>smack</i>. As you lower the shield, level with your nose, you see Masanori urge his horse into a dead run, and hear the sudden hoofbeats of your retainers charging up behind you.<br /> Furiously, they cover you with their own shields, and you can hear the bandits laughing even from here.<br /> <br /> \u201cAre you wounded, Lord Qianke?!\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cNo\u2026. I\u2019m good.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cInfamous bastard! Vile perfidy!\u201d<br /> <br /> The Oathsworn continues insulting Masanori, and you focus on keeping your balance in the saddle as you ride back towards the camp. On the outskirts, you are met by Sergeant Oxblood.<br /> <br /> \u201c Scouts and pickets, M\u2019Lord?\u201d It isn\u2019t a suggestion.<br /> <br /> \u201cOf course.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cA palisade and abatis, as well?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c\u2026..yes. See to it, Sergeant.\u201d, and he strides off, bawling orders at the top of his lungs. You take the time to catch your breath and see just what the hell Masanori did.<br /> <br /> To your surprise, the arrow that impacted your shield has no head, instead having a glob of sticky pine-pitch molded onto the tip. The glob allowed the shaft to stick to the canvas of your shield, and around the shaft is wrapped a piece of paper. Unwrapping it, you expose a note, little larger than the palm of your hand. In small, cramped handwriting, you see written,<br /> <br /> \u201c<i>Lord Jianghu,</i><br /> <i>I apologize for the misdirection, but I had to make the meeting \u2018look real\u2019 in front of the other bandit-captains. I still intend to join you, and at sundown, I will be leading a \u2018surprise attack\u2019 from the forest to the east. About 300 men took up your offer, almost all of my personal crew. Veterans, all told.</i><br /> <i>Forewarn your men, I would rather not catch an arrow to the face. Again, sorry about that.</i><br /> <i>Masanori.\u201d</i><br /> <br /> As soon as you finish and reread the note, you call out at the top of your lungs, \u201cCommand meeting, all captains to my pavilion as soon as possible!\u201d, and hurriedly dismount, staggering towards your tent.<br /> <br /> After a few minutes, all of your regimental officers file in, and you explain just what is happening, in plain and simple terms.<br /> <br /> \u201cMasanori the bandit-king is planning on defecting, along with 300 or so fighters.\u201d<br /> <br /> The pavilion erupts into noise, quieting only when you pass around the note. One Oathsworn retainer asks,<br /> <br /> \u201cTo be honest, my Lord\u2026\u2026. Do you trust him? I do not, especially after what he just did\u2026\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cHis defection is what we came here for, partially. Depriving the bandits of their most capable leader was always my first and foremost goal. The extra troops are just gravy. But, to answer your question\u2026.. I do. I trust him. But, then again, I am also an idealist, and could be called an optimist. That does <i>not</i> mean, however, that I am going to welcome this turn of events with open arms. Sergeant, how goes the fortifications?\u201d<br /> <br /> Sergeant Oxblood, who has just walked in, \u201cWell enough, M\u2019Lord. I have several of my own boys on it, it will be done within the hour. Can I ask what is going on?\u201d<br /> <br /> You wordlessly hand him the letter, which he reads quickly. Once done, he closes his eyes and waits.<br /> <br /> \u201c\u2026..Sergeant, move the three spear-regiments to the eastern flank of the encampment, along with the two longbow-regiments. Use the milita-regiments to build the palisade, but keep them nearby if possible.\u201d<br /> <br /> He nods, almost imperceptibly, and you continue \u201cKeep the halberd-regiments on high alert, as well. Captains, see to your men. If we get attacked, repel them and sound the general-alarm. Light Cavalry, I want you to scout around the fortress, see if you see anything of note. Weakened walls, odd terrain, things like that. Report <i>directly</i> to me, and I want a report before sundown. Understood?\u201d<br /> <br /> The pavilion rings with the sound of gauntlets on breastplates, and the officers you mention leave with a bow. You are left with the Oathsworn of your reserve.<br /> <br /> \u201cIs there anything you would have us do, my Lord?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c\u2026.No.\u201d, you sigh. \u201cNow all we can really do is wait, I suppose\u2026\u2026. You could bring me a list of all our materiel, but that is just busy-work, really.\u201d<br /> <br /> Instead, they bring you dinner, which you largely pick at, appetite largely gone. Now that you <i>know</i> battle is quite-possibly imminent, you are anxious and unsettled. Images of failure and loss flicker through your mind, and you push away the noodle-soup. Walking through the camp (which is already surrounded by a sturdy palisade of bamboo. Sergeant Major Oxblood <i>gets shit done</i>, apparently), you can\u2019t help but notice that the same emotions are running rampant among the soldiers, the serf-militiamen most of all. Tempers are flaring, men are pacing, and the entire camp feels uneasy. You ask Sgt. Oxblood about it.<br /> <br /> \u201cWell, that is just nerves, M\u2019Lord. Everyone gets them, <i>especially</i> when a fight is going to happen. In a way, they would almost prefer to fight.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cWhy\u201d, you ask, genuinely mystified.<br /> <br /> \u201cBecause at least you know what can happen during a battle. In a fight, there really isn\u2019t \u2018enough time\u2019 to think about what can happen, and you just have to do what you sergeant and officers tell you. It\u2019s\u2026\u2026. easier, in a way.\u201d He shrugs.<br /> <br /> \u201cI can find them something to do, if you want.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cThat might be for the best, Sergeant.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c\u2026..How are you holding up, M\u2019Lord?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cPoorly\u201d, you sigh. \u201cI can\u2019t help but feel like I am making nothing but mistakes\u2026..like I am leading over a thousand men to their deaths\u2026\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cNo, none of that.\u201d He catches your eyes, \u201cMy Lord, you will be <i>fine</i>, you will do <i>fine</i>. Believe me, this is far from the worst I\u2019ve ever seen. We have a defensible position, we outnumber the enemy, and we know they are coming. Even if they do attack, fending them off will be easy, relatively speaking. Besides, I wouldn\u2019t let you make a major mistake. If you did, I would have said something.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c\u2026Thanks.\u201d You turn to the fortress looming overhead, \u201cbut\u2026. what are we going to do about that?\u201d<br /> <br /> He shrugs, \u201cDon\u2019t know yet. And we\u2019ll deal with it when we come to it. Don\u2019t worry about it for now. Have you eaten yet?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cI \u2026tried\u2026\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cGo eat. It will make you feel better. Trust me.\u201d<br /> <br /> Taking his advice, you return to your tent, but cold noodles taste even worse, and you can\u2019t take in more than a few spoonfuls.<br /> <br /> As the day ticks on, you wander around camp again, ostensibly to inspect the palisade. Tensions inside the camp have risen to a fever pitch, and pretty much everyone is aware that an attack is possibly imminent. As the sun slowly sinks towards the horizon, you are startled by a clamor erupting from the \u2018main gate\u2019 of the camp. Walking over, leaning heavily on your spear, you see one of the regiments of light cavalry dismounting. Their subofficer walks over to you, backsword swinging from his belt.<br /> <br /> \u201cMy Lord, you said you wanted a reconnaissance report in your hands before sundown?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cThat is correct. What do you have for me?\u201d<br /> <br /> You lead the man back into your pavilion, where he pulls out a sheet of paper from within his belt-pouch. Unfolding it and passing it to you, you see that there is a rough sketch of the surrounding terrain, centered on Dun Nashuon with hills, forests and other features marked out.<br /> <br /> Peering at the drawing, you ask, \u201cSo, what else have you got for me, sergeant?\u201d<br /> <br /> The soldier pulls off his helmet before continuing, \u201cAsides from the forests and hills, the ground is mostly clear and open, my Lord. The \u2018blank spots\u2019 on the map represent terrain that has no distinguishing features, being mostly mountain grasses, open meadows or heathland. Our horses could get through it no problem, so the groundpounders shouldn\u2019t be too caught up. \u201c<br /> <br /> \u201cThe tors themselves are mainly clear, with large amounts of bare rock and some gnarled trees. I guess that with some luck, one could arc an arrow from the peak into the fortress, but the wind blows pretty hard up there.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cThere is a swamp at the base of the tor the fortress is built on, and we swung wide to avoid getting bogged down. It basically covers one whole flank of the fortress, and anyone trying to approach from the south will have to swing about a half-mile out of the way, skirting around a tor and some heavy woods.\u201d<br /> <br /> He takes a deep breath, then continues, \u201cWe also found several \u2026. well, caves, for lack of a better term. Mostly on the north side of the tor, they look like old dilapidated piles of stone, and they drive straight into the hillside. I didn\u2019t send any men into them, but we got close enough to smell and feel them.\u201d<br /> <br /> Your frown, \u201cWhat does that mean, exactly?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cAir is moving <i>out</i> of those openings, my Lord. Fast enough to make a breeze, and the air is cold and wet.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cAny\u2026. uh, noises?\u201d<br /> <br /> Confused, \u201cNo, my Lord. We didn\u2019t stick around too long to hear anything.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cHmm\u201d. If you wanted to make an educated guess, you would say those are ventilation-shafts from when the barrows under the hillfort were constructed. Narrow, certainly, but they might be wide enough for a man to crawl down\u2026.. that is, if you wanted to send a man down deep into the earth, straight to the bosom of angry ancestor-ghosts.<br /> <br /> You look back up at the man, \u201cThat will be all, sergeant, thank you.\u201d, and the soldier leaves with a bow.<br /> <br /> You go back to scouring the map, considering calling in the Sergeant Oxblood to offer his opinion, when you get suddenly distracted by the sound of a bugle wildly blowing.<br /> <br /> Sounding the general-alarm.<br /> <br /> Cursing, you grab <i>Moonlight Over Water</i> from its rack and hurriedly leave the pavilion, heading towards the sound of the horn. Soldiers rush to and fro around you, and at some points, have to wait for the men to clear a path between the tents and supply depots. Long before you reach the embankment, you hear the Sergeant\u2019s parade-voice loud and clear over the clatter of weapons and the patter of footfalls. From what you can make out, he is yelling for people to \u201cstop rubbernecking and get back to their posts.\u201d Spurred on, you pick up the pace as much as you are able.<br /> When you finally reach the eastern embankment, you are greeted with the sight of not just your Men-at-Arms standing in formation, ready for battle, but several dozens of militiamen wandering about, trying to catch a glimpse of something outside the palisade. You limp forward and arduously climb up onto the wallwalk, peering between the uprights.<br /> <br /> Marching out from the treeline are several blocks of men, standing in formation, with weapons at the ready. As you watch, three regiments of spearmen emerge first, locked into a shieldwall, followed by two groups of archers in skirmish formation. For Men-at-Arms, they appear to be well-disciplined, much less for bandits!<br /> <br /> A path opens up between the formations, and Masanori rides out. A distance in front of the regiments, he dismounts and waits.<br /> <br /> You turn to the Sergeant, who nods and points at several of the halberdiers. Quickly, they form up around you, and led by Sergeant Oxblood, you push open the gate and make your way outside the fortress.<br /> <br /> As you walk up to Masanori, he goes down on one knee, and with a clatter, so does the rest of the soldiers behind him. Once you get closer, he looks up, and even from this distance, you can see his face pale once he sees your limp.<br /> <br /> \u201cMy Lord, here I am, as promised, with my regiments\u2026\u2026..<i>please</i> tell me I didn\u2019t actually hurt you.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cNo, no, this was from before, have no fear.\u201d<br /> <br /> You cast your gaze over the gathered soldiers, then look back at Masanori, \u201cSo\u2026.. you pulled through. How did you get out of the fortress, might I ask?\u201d<br /> <br /> He grins, \u201cBelieve me, that is a story worth telling\u2026..inside the encampment. I have plenty of other intelligence you will find interesting as well.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cVery well. Sergeant, find room for these additional soldiers. If you have to expand the palisade, go ahead.\u201d<br /> <br /> On your walk back to your pavilion, word evidently travels fast, and most of your Oathsworn officers and retinue are already there, with more filing in behind you. Once they are all settled, you gesture to Masanori,<br /> <br /> \u201c Oathsworn officers and vassals, I would like to introduce you to Masanori \u2026\u2026 ah, how would you prefer to be named?\u201d<br /> <br /> He shrugs, \u201c I have no clan-name as of yet, so Masanori will be sufficient, if it please you.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cMasanori it is. For those who don\u2019t know, Masanori was the leader of the largest and most active bandit-group in Jianghu, which is currently encamped in the fortress of Dun Nashuon. As I have said before, I met with Masanori a little over a week ago, and we came to an agreement. In return for his desertion of the bandit-group, he and whomever he could persuade to join us would receive a full pardon for any and all crimes, as well as the standard land-grant, in return for five years of military service. Now then, just how many soldiers did you manage to bring with you?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cAbout three hundred, my Lord Jianghu. Most of them are from the bandit-crew that followed me, as I found them the most skilled, not to mention the most trustworthy, for what my word is worth. Most of them are deserted soldiers from other domains, so they are already well-drilled and equipped. Three regiments of spears and two of longbows, I believe.\u201d<br /> <br /> One of your vassals speaks out, \u201cJust how did you manage to leave the fortress unnoticed?\u201d<br /> <br /> Masanori grins, \u201cI did not, if you want to be particular. The soldiers were known to be on a \u201cflanking maneuver\u2019 by my former bandit-captains, and so their absence was expected. Myself, on the other hand\u2026\u2026\u201d He points in the direction of the fortress, illuminated by the rays of the setting sun. \u201cYou can probably hear the screams from here if you listen closely.\u201d<br /> <br /> You frown, \u201cWhat exactly do you mean, Masanori?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cI \u2026.. well, took the liberty of sabotaging as much of the defense as much as possible, my Lord, before I escaped.\u201d<br /> <br /> The assembled officers erupt into noise, and you wait for them to calm before you question Masanori.<br /> <br /> \u201cCare to go into detail?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cOf course, my Lord.\u201d He pulls out several pieces of paper from the inside of his sash and passes them over to you.<br /> <br /> \u201cThis map is a detailed drawing of the defenses of Dun Nashuon; where the walls are ruined, where they are strong, the internal details of the encampment, so on and so forth.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cThis list details the forces remaining inside Dun Nashuon; their makeup, their morale, their commanders and their dispositions.\u201d He points at several names on the list, \u201c You can ignore these commanders, I killed them in my escape.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cAnd this list details the amount of materiel stockpiled in Dun Nashuon. Days of food stored, the number of arrows and projectiles stockpiled, etc.\u201d<br /> <br /> The last list is <i>criminally</i> short. \u201cExcellent work, Masanori, but I must ask \u2026. Why is there so little food stored in Dun Nashuon?\u201d<br /> <br /> He shrugs, \u201c By-and-large, we were dependent on the food stores we could extort from the farms along the border of the Akibara. It is the end of summer, and stores were winding down. I was at the point of ordering men into the woods to forage and hunt, but I recalled them all when you told me you were coming.\u201d<br /> <br /> Confused, \u2018Why is that?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cI <i>wanted</i> to burn through whatever food stores I had left, my Lord. While Dun Nashuon is a ruin, it is a <i>defensible</i> ruin, and I assumed you would want a siege over quickly. Based on how quickly we used the food stored in the souterrains, there can\u2019t be much more than 5 days of food stored in the fortress.\u201d<br /> <br /> You nod, and Sergeant Oxblood pipes up, \u201cHow about water? You can go a week or more without food, but only a couple of days without water. Does the fortress have its own water supply, or did you have to use the river?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cThe fortress had several wells on the surface level, but they either went dry or were filled long ago. I spent two weeks having my troops excavate one, to no success. 30 feet of rubble, at least. We were forced to crack open the vault under the stone tower, but that cistern was not large enough for our purposes. Eventually, we found a larger well, in the undercaverns.\u201d<br /> <br /> This last bit, when translated to the Hill-Tribe warleaders, causes them to react in horror and outrage.<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt;That sounds like the <i>sacred well</i>! Tell me this ignorant flatlander didn\u2019t contaminate the sacred well!&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> When you relay this info to Masanori, he winces. \u201cYou know, in hindsight, I regret ordering that thing opened. There were many artifacts; weapons, jewelry, things like that in there, and much of it was \u2026.. uh\u2026..looted, when the well was in use. In addition, there are several passages down there, and I lost several men who went wandering down there. Eventually, I ordered the passages sealed up, but I am almost certain I didn\u2019t get all of them. Will that be a problem?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cAlmost certainly\u201d, you reply with a sigh, \u201cBy removing the artifacts, you desecrated the well, and the men who wandered through the catacombs almost-certainly disturbed the dead\u2026 \u201c&lt;Will the desecration of the well and tombs be a problem, at least regarding this attack?&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt;Hopefully not. If anything, removing the ones who disturbed the sacred site will mollify the ancestor-ghosts. If we can, however, take some of the interlopers alive\u2026..&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> You shudder and turn away. The Tribes are terrifying when brought to wrath.<br /> <br /> \u201cBack on topic.\u201d You glance at the list detailing troop numbers.\u201d So, Masanori, just how many fighters do you have inside the fortress?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cAbout 1200, my Lord, divided into several different and often-infighting gangs. They were loyal, for a given sense, to me when I was present, but now that I am gone, they will be widely suspicious of each other. They don\u2019t \u2018play well\u2019 together, and when I led them, I often had to keep them wide apart from each other.\u201d<br /> <br /> You turn to your retainers and officers, \u201cAnd how many men do we have here?\u201d<br /> <br /> Sergeant Oxblood speaks up, \u201c 500 Men-at-Arms, plus the 300 sir Masanori brought over, so 800. 500 miltiamen, plus 500 or so Hill-Tribe warriors. In total \u2026\u2026\u2026 1800, not counting the Oathsworn officers and retinue. Close to 1900 fighters, all told, and when the two regiments of militia under sir Shin Takeshi gets here, we will be close to 2000.\u201d<br /> <br /> Back to Masanori, \u201cWould any of the fighters left in the fortress take me up on my offer?\u201d<br /> <br /> He shrugs, \u201cAlmost certainly, but \u2026\u2026 if I may speak plainly, my Lord, I would not trust them. The men who joined me did so because they <i>want \u2018out\u2019</i> of the bandit-life. Men who are interested in saving their own skins will say <i>anything</i>, but they will also do anything. The men who joined me are disciplined; the men left in the fortress are \u2026\u2026not. More like wild dogs than men.\u201d<br /> \u201c Hmmm\u2026.. How much food and materiel do we have stockpiled here?\u201d<br /> <br /> One of your retinue speaks up, reading from a list, \u201cIn dried goods, we have about 5 days of food. If you dispatch the Tribes to hunt and forage, that number can be safely increased to \u2026. Ah\u2026\u2026 probably a week at least, ten days if we skimp. Plus, sir Shin Takeshi should only be a few days behind, with the materiel from the rest of Jianghu.\u201d<br /> <br /> You sit down in your camp-chair, pondering the best course of action.<br /> <br /> \u201cAlright, so by the looks of things, we have two main courses of action.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cOne\u201d, you hold up a finger, \u201cWe can assault the fortress. We have the advantage of numbers, if only slightly, and the benefits of a unified and central command. If we strike while they are disjointed and confused, we might just be able to seize the day. That will, however, take a toll in blood and lives.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cTwo\u201d, holding up another finger, \u201cWe lay siege to the fortress. We have the advantage of food supply on our hands, and can outlast them if need be. Plus, we have more food and more soldiers on the way. On the other hand\u2026\u2026. Just how long do we have before the army has to leave, in order to make ready for the harvest?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c Truthfully, Lord Qianke, there is about two weeks left before the summer harvest actually begins, and that will be pushing it. If we lay siege to the fortress, we <i>very well might</i> not have time to effectively get the troops back in the fields before the harvest starts, and that is if the fortress capitulates in 5 days. Anything longer and the harvest-yield will start to suffer.\u201d<br /> <br /> You think about what the best course of action to take is:<ol class=\"decimal\"><li>Meet with the current commanders of the fortress, and make the same offer you made to Masanori. If you can get more fighters to change sides\u2026.</li> <li>Prepare equipment for an assault: mantlets, a ram, ladders, etc<ol class=\"decimal\"><li>Order the troops to attack without you. You are in no shape to charge up a hill, much less fight! (\u2018standard\u2019 morale)</li> <li>Lead your army into battle. You wouldn\u2019t order someone to do something you wouldn\u2019t do! (units close to Kasai will receive a hefty morale boost)</li> </ol></li> <li>Prepare equipment for a siege: mantlets, sanitary facilities for the encampment, more advanced defenses, so on and so forth.</li> <li>Ask the Hill-Tribes if they would be willing to sneak into the underbelly of the fortress through the ventilation-shafts your scouts found. Since the ghosts are kin to the Tribes, they might be willing to forgive a trespass, especially if the desecrators are removed, and putting a couple dozen angry Tribesmen up the enemies ass will cause a <i>lot</i> of damage.</li> <li>Something else? (write in)</li> </ol>Battle of Dun Nashuon map: <a href=\"http://imgur.com/ZSyAq9s\" target=\"_blank\">http://imgur.com/ZSyAq9s</a>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11892,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","user":"Boston123","date":". 07-02-2017, 05:19 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-02T15:01:23-07:00","id":1110668,"title":"","content":"I am opposed to a siege - even if we could spare the time, we don't really have any plan for dealing with the bandits if they surrender. I also trust Masanori's judgement to the extent that he believes no other defector would be reliable (and given how dubious Masanori himself has been, that's saying something). So that leaves us with the option of assault. It's a shame Masanori is already out - if we had coordinated some kind of attack with him, perhaps he could have opened the fortress gates for us. <br /> <br /> Can we try to knock down some of the more structurally unsound parts of the walls with catapults or sappers? If we can open one or more good entrances into Dun Nashuon, that would be preferable to trying to climb the walls.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>On the frontier of the Wild South, there's only one woman with the grit to take on its most dangerous outlaws and bring them <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/984977-back-alive-or-maybe-dead-quest\" target=\"_blank\">Back Alive, or Maybe Dead.</a><br /> <br /> Avatar by <a href=\"http://puzzlinghappenstance.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">K.S. Brenowitz</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/908-semicasual","name":"semicasual","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar908_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2499"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-02T15:47:49-07:00","id":1110679,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>semicasual</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1110668#post1110668\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I am opposed to a siege - even if we could spare the time, we don't really have any plan for dealing with the bandits if they surrender. I also trust Masanori's judgement to the extent that he believes no other defector would be reliable (and given how dubious Masanori himself has been, that's saying something). So that leaves us with the option of assault. It's a shame Masanori is already out - if we had coordinated some kind of attack with him, perhaps he could have opened the fortress gates for us.<br /> <br /> Can we try to knock down some of the more structurally unsound parts of the walls with catapults or sappers? If we can open one or more good entrances into Dun Nashuon, that would be preferable to trying to climb the walls.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Overwhelmingly most of the walls are ruined, being little more than piles of cobbles and mounds of earth at this point. Masanori was attempting to rebuild them, but he only really managed to set up <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Palisade\" target=\"_blank\">palisades</a> on the most important bits: the areas around the gate, and where the slope is gentlest, which would be the western side of the fortress. The eastern and northern flanks are defended by the steeper slopes of the hill, meaning they need 'less' defenses, and he could afford to pay less attention to. He did, however, pile things like thornbushes and the like on top of the cobbles to form an impromptu defense, but it isn't particularly impressive or effective.<br /> <br /> The western side of the fortress, which is where the gentlest slope of the hill and the approach to the gate are located, is also where the most earthworks are located. They are, basically, berms and trenches in the side of the hill, designed to slow down attacking forces. There are fewer, even no, earthworks on the south, east, and north sides of the fortress, just the remains of the drystone wall and Masanori's palisade.<br /> <br /> Finally, Dun Nashuon <u>isn't</u> a castle in the Vanehani tradition. <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hillfort#/media/File:British_camp_central_mound_2005.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">This</a> is more along the lines of what it currently looks like. And, even in it's heyday, when it was used as the seat of a Tribal chief-kingdom, it was more of an <i><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oppidum\" target=\"_blank\">oppidum</a> (</i>the diorama of the earthen wall is a very nice representation).<br /> <br /> Dun Nashuon's main defense, throughout its entire existence, was the fact that it was located on top of a hill, with uncontested fields of fire surrounding it. And that last bit is severely diminished in the present day. All the forests that are visible on the map were not present when Dun Nashuon was occupied by the Tribes, and would not be allowed to grow that close. Almost all of the land on the map, plus more off the edges, was farmland 700 years ago. If Kasai decides to move troops through the woods, they are going to trip over old stone walls.<br /> <br /> Said forests will provide excellent cover, both for troops and against missile fire.<br /> <br /> Finally..... sappers wouldn't really work, since Dun Nashuon is built on top of a hill. Any attempts to undermine the walls would lead the sappers right into the barrows under the fortress, and they would have a Bad Time.<br /> <br /> Also finally, the Hundred Kingdoms doesn't have a strongly-developed tradition of siege-engine-craft. Most fortifications are built on top of hills/rocky outcrops/mountains, and have walls made of sloping earthworks faced with stone, surmounted by wooden palisades covered in plaster. Siege engines won't really knock them down, and even if they did, the palisades are easy to repair.<br /> <br /> Don't get me wrong, there <i>are</i> catapults and the like in the Hundred Kingdoms, but they are small and mostly used as anti-personnel weapons as opposed to anti-fortification. Most castles are taken through starvation, disease, direct assault, or by fire. Almost every castle in the Hundred Kingdoms is built of wood covered in plaster/wattle-and-daub, and fire is always a fear.<br /> <br /> On top of that, Kasai doesn't have any siege-engines with him. There weren't any just laying around in Jianghu, and he doesn't have the engineering knowledge to build any.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"http://www.jcastle.info/resources/view/78-Structures\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/google-material/image_placeholder.svg\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://www.jcastle.info/resources/view/78-Structures\" target=\"_blank\"> 404 Not Found </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://www.jcastle.info/resources/view/78-Structures\" target=\"_blank\"> http://www.jcastle.info/resources/view/78-Structures </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-02T17:38:28-07:00","id":1110702,"title":"","content":"I say start a &quot;siege&quot; while sending hill tribesman in through the bottom. We attack in force on day 4. Panicked selfish bandits with no loyalty outside of their smaller groups should be fighting over a too small food supply. They will be weakened if not by an actual lack of food then by the infighting over resources. Thoughts?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11567-jairain","name":"Jairain","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11567_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"345"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-02T18:23:40-07:00","id":1110715,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Jairain</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1110702#post1110702\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I say start a &quot;siege&quot; while sending hill tribesman in through the bottom. We attack in force on day 4. Panicked selfish bandits with no loyalty outside of their smaller groups should be fighting over a too small food supply. They will be weakened if not by an actual lack of food then by the infighting over resources. Thoughts?</div> </div> </div> </div>Provided the tribesmen agree, I support this plan.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>On the frontier of the Wild South, there's only one woman with the grit to take on its most dangerous outlaws and bring them <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/984977-back-alive-or-maybe-dead-quest\" target=\"_blank\">Back Alive, or Maybe Dead.</a><br /> <br /> Avatar by <a href=\"http://puzzlinghappenstance.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">K.S. Brenowitz</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/908-semicasual","name":"semicasual","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar908_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2499"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-02T20:03:13-07:00","id":1110735,"title":"","content":"Do NOT send the Hill Tribes into the barrows. Leave them to be restored by the correct rituals after the battle.<br /> <br /> 2.2 <b>order the equipment built for an attack</b>, and lead one, probably the distraction at the 'front' , and have the real attack be up one of the less guarded slopes.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Like my Avatar? Courtesy of<span style=\"color:#0000FF\"> Jen</span>! : <span style=\"font-size:12px\">Anybody want their characters to be experimented on ? </span>post 98<br /> <i>An Exalt is never unarmed.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4018-l-het-esh","name":"L'het'esh","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4018_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"521"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-03T14:01:29-07:00","id":1110995,"title":"","content":"One thing I must say:<br /> <br /> I am actually rather impressed, if that is the proper term.<br /> <br /> I originally planned for Masanori to be, essentially, &quot;babby's first antagonist&quot;, in a sense. Instead of killing him, you went and recruited him.<br /> <br /> See what happens when you folks do write-ins?<br /> <br /> Cool stuff. Props to WiseOldGuru, as well as anyone else that proposed it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-03T14:14:53-07:00","id":1111001,"title":"","content":"I say its far too risky to send hill tribes into the catacombs. As boston said they are LESS likely to be taken and eaten, but it could still happen and if it did, we'd be down a quarter of our force. NOT OK. I say we surround the hill with the highest concentration at the west gentle slope and another group near the catacomb entrance. We spend a couple days pelting them with arrows if we can manage it and then scout three weak points in the wall to pour in through. I say we seige long enough for them to go hungry and thirsty and to soften them up, maybe even set part of the fort on fire if its possible but once they hit hungry/thirsty we assault. Just my thoughts.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8897-joshopotamus","name":"joshopotamus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8897_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"97"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-03T22:03:07-07:00","id":1111159,"title":"","content":"I'd say spend a day or two ramping up the psychological warfare anyway we could, to dampen the morale. Here are some ideas:<br /> 1. Lots and lots of fire arrows onto any location we can reach.<br /> 2. Is there a way to convince the <i>bandits</i> that the catacombs are haunted? maybe have the tribes declare that the bandits have defiled their dead, and have the tribes openly do rituals to spook the bandits somehow, as if the tribes could talk to their dead.<br /> 3. Can the dog of the earth be summoned here? He doesn't have to fight, he doesn't have to do anything at all. Just summon him openly in broad daylight in front of the bandits, feed him some oxen sacrifice, and then casually ask him<i>, </i>just curious, <i>but which bandit on the wall looks the tastiest?</i> Or just anything to imply (whether it's true or not) that we have some rotting abomination on our side, that might do horrible things to the bandits when the walls fall if they don't surrender, and by the way did we mention the ruins are haunted too?<br /> <br /> Wrap all that up by the third day. <i>Then </i>attack, if the bandits haven't already surrendered.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3752-mushroom-king","name":"Mushroom King","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3752_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"221"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-05T11:21:30-07:00","id":1111735,"title":"","content":"No arrows yet: we know they're low on ammo and there's no reason to give them more. Settle in for a siege-they have low organization/morale, they're gonna focus on their own lack of supplies rather than think about how we have to leave to make the harvest.<br /> <br /> They'll probably attempt to sally forth and charge us under cover of night-they're bandits, they won't like getting idea of a pitched battle without Masanori and his elites, and they're going to think it better to melt into the woods and lay low than try to hold a fort without food. Encourage them to make this tactical error by making your camp superficially appear to be weak at night-feasting and music and merriment should make you a tempting target while also hurting their morale by rubbing in that you don't have a food shortage. If you can set up your mess upwind of them so they smell your roast boar it whatever, so much the better. Be ready for them to swarm out at like two am, maybe try a pincer maneuver. THEN we use our arrows.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>So I'm making <a href=\"http://godkickingboot.smackjeeves.com/\" target=\"_blank\">God-Kicking Boot</a>, an Exalted webcomic, now. Updates on Sundays. Full-color, mediocre but slowly improving art. It's a thing.<br /> <br /> The absence of a monument can, in its own way, be something of a monument also.</i> -Roger Zelazny","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/512-wise-old-guru","name":"Wise Old Guru","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar512_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1097"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-05T17:50:30-07:00","id":1111910,"title":"","content":"There are a lot of good points. How hard would it be to taunt then into attacking outside of the fort considering they are poorly trained, disorganized, and likely have a higher than average incidence of intermittent explosive disorder and impulse control issues.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11567-jairain","name":"Jairain","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11567_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"345"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-05T19:00:03-07:00","id":1111932,"title":"","content":"The bandits <u>as a whole</u> are generally not going to attack us. They might be disorganized, but that doesn't mean they are stupid. Even the slowest of dullards is capable of recognizing the defensive benefits of Dun Nashuon, and conversely, the likely effects of leaving it.<br /> <br /> 1) The bandits, in general, are not actually that poorly trained. A decent proportion of them are actually deserted soldiers, and the bandits that weren't deserted soldiers learned quick, aka &quot;swim or die&quot;-type thing. In-game, a bandit battlegroup will have Poor drill not because, well, they are poorly drilled, but because they are not used to fighting in groups of any appreciable size and/or (usually 'and') lack effective means of coordination and communication between groups.<br /> <br /> For example: Kasai's army (and the overwhelming majority of the Hundred Kingdoms military) has systems like <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flag_semaphore\" target=\"_blank\">semaphore</a>, <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bugle_call\" target=\"_blank\">bugle/drum/trumpet calls</a>, or <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Runner_(soldier)\" target=\"_blank\">runners</a>, meaning communication and the passing-along of orders can be, for the time and technological period, pretty damn fast and effective. Overwhelmingly most bandit groups don't have them, either because they work in small enough groups so they would be unnecessary, or they lack trained personnel.<br /> <br /> 2) They <u>are</u> disorganized, and this is the main 'benefit' that &quot;the law&quot; (aka Kasai's army, or organized forces in general) have when fighting bandits.<br /> <br /> Even the freshest of Spear-Regiments is going to have an Oathsworn commander, and if a regiment is worth it's salt (and most are, outside of freshly-formed regiments), they will generally have a decent-to-damn-good sergeant/subofficer corps, made up of older, more experienced veterans. The Oathsworn commander is a semi-professional, and the sergeants/sub-officers in the regiment allow them to keep discipline in the ranks pretty well.<br /> <br /> Generally, Oathsworn officers are going to be throwing out (2 [War] + 2 [Charisma/Appearance] + 2 [sergeant/sub-officer bonus] + X [ Army commander Command roll successes] + X [Miscellaneous stunts, like having a battle-plan drawn up, etc]) dice on a command roll.<br /> <br /> It helps that Hundred-Kingdoms forces almost always have a single commander. In this case, it would be Kasai. The bandits .... do not. (If Masanori was still there, it would have been him, which would be terrifying.) Kasai can spread his Command Roll successes to effectively every unit in the army, while the bandits have 3,4,5 dudes jockeying for supremacy, and no Command Roll successes.<br /> <br /> On top of that, Kasai is going to come up with a plan-of-battle for the attack (ghostwritten and proofread by the good Sgt Maj Oxblood of course), which in game terms will give his forces a +2 dice stunt bonus to their rolls, so long as they follow the plan (and things go to plan, of course). The bandits plan is &quot;don't die, you shits&quot;.<br /> <br /> Overwhelmingly-most bandit-groups stay under 100, or even 50 fighters in size. Logistics plays a part in that, but another part is ....... uh, &quot;comparing dicks&quot;, to be crude. Chances are if you are a bandit and outlaw, you didn't play well with authority to begin with, and even if you were, living among others with issues-with-authority tends to rub off. Leaders of bandit-groups tend to rise either through coercion ('follow me or I'll kill you') or cooperation ('follow me and you can get some sweet shit!'), and they tend to jockey for position <u>a lot</u>. They spend as much time consolidating power within their own crews as they do between other crews. Generally, if a bandit-group gets too large, it will split (usually violently) into several smaller groups under their own leaders.<br /> <br /> Of course, the fact that Masanori managed to wrangle about 1500 individuals with authority-issues to work together, for a given sense of the term, means he is either terrifyingly charismatic, terrifying good at intimidating people, or terrifyingly good at leading forces in battle (the answer is &quot;all of the above&quot;.)<br /> <br /> I'll be straight; I'm glad you folks decided to recruit him. This fight would have been <u>much</u> harder if you didn't. Probably harder than the Lunar fight, if you can compare massed-combat to individual. Now, it is going to be ........ easier.<br /> <br /> <br /> .","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11892,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","user":"Boston123","date":". 07-05-2017, 10:04 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-07T13:53:07-07:00","id":1112902,"title":"","content":"So, I'm going camping with my Scout Troop for the week. I'll <u>try</u> to get the post out tomorrow, but since it is a long one, I just might have to push it back to next weekend.....","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-07T14:18:40-07:00","id":1112913,"title":"","content":"Have a good week!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Like my Avatar? Courtesy of<span style=\"color:#0000FF\"> Jen</span>! : <span style=\"font-size:12px\">Anybody want their characters to be experimented on ? </span>post 98<br /> <i>An Exalt is never unarmed.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4018-l-het-esh","name":"L'het'esh","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4018_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"521"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-07T22:39:58-07:00","id":1113119,"title":"","content":"I... don't like our options.<br /> Siege-wise, we can't do it for long. Best we can afford is a couple of days, before we start an assault if we don't want the harvest to suffer.<br /> Unless the Chosen give us a reason otherwise, the Tomb-Barrow is too dangerous for the Tribesmen to risk it. Even then, volunteers only.<br /> <br /> We need to talk to our commanders to make a plan. Let's include the Chosen of the Tribes, since they will leading a different aspect of our force. We need to know who is ready to fulfil which roles. We will need a diversionary attack in addition to our serious push, preferably more than one (but if we have a sneaky in to the castle via the barrow we might not need more than one).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Thoughts ripple out, birthing others","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1624-falleneco","name":"FallenEco","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1624_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1927"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-08T12:46:20-07:00","id":1113329,"title":"","content":"Yeah, it's really hard to figure the way to go here. Do we risk a late harvest, potentially having long-term effects? Or do we risk casualties we may or may not be able to afford in the upcoming war?<br /> I'd still like to know if it's possible to summon the Dog of Earth in the area, just for intimidation purposes.<br /> Maybe we could offer the first three bandit gangs that accept surrender into serfdom? They'd surrender their weapons, get separated, etc.?<br /> Maybe let the bandits know &quot;you're enduring a siege on haunted ground, you sure you want to do this?&quot;<br /> If the ghosts are somewhat reasonable, get them to avenge themselves in the attack?<br /> No idea how easy of those would be.<br /> And have a good weekend Boston123!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3752-mushroom-king","name":"Mushroom King","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3752_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"221"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-08T13:47:46-07:00","id":1113356,"title":"","content":"-[REDACTED]-","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-14T23:31:23-07:00","id":1116265,"title":"","content":"We are going to play it close, but we don't need to brake the bank. We should try too get this done within 5 days.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Thoughts ripple out, birthing others","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1624-falleneco","name":"FallenEco","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1624_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1927"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-16T10:45:06-07:00","id":1116897,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Mushroom King</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1111159#post1111159\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'd say spend a day or two ramping up the psychological warfare anyway we could, to dampen the morale. Here are some ideas:<br /> 1. Lots and lots of fire arrows onto any location we can reach.<br /> 2. Is there a way to convince the <i>bandits</i> that the catacombs are haunted? maybe have the tribes declare that the bandits have defiled their dead, and have the tribes openly do rituals to spook the bandits somehow, as if the tribes could talk to their dead.<br /> 3. Can the dog of the earth be summoned here? He doesn't have to fight, he doesn't have to do anything at all. Just summon him openly in broad daylight in front of the bandits, feed him some oxen sacrifice, and then casually ask him<i>, </i>just curious, <i>but which bandit on the wall looks the tastiest?</i> Or just anything to imply (whether it's true or not) that we have some rotting abomination on our side, that might do horrible things to the bandits when the walls fall if they don't surrender, and by the way did we mention the ruins are haunted too?<br /> <br /> Wrap all that up by the third day. <i>Then </i>attack, if the bandits haven't already surrendered.</div> </div> </div> </div>1) Fire arrows are certainly possible, but there are several...... well, not drawbacks, more caveats, with using them.<br /> <br /> In the Hundred Kingdoms, Men-at-Arms are responsible for owning, maintaining and supplying the majority of their own equipment. This includes ammunition. It isn't an official law (although Kasai could certainly make it so, especially since he has mandated ownership of spears and bows for every household), but generally each Man-at-Arms is going to show up to a muster with at least 24 arrows. The militia are going to have less, especially since 1) arrows are expensive, 2) you don't need that many to hunt animals, which is what the men drawn for militia-duty were using their bows for, but even still, chances are they are going to have at least 10.<br /> <br /> The more forward-thinking Longbowmen are going to bring more, probably at least 60, and the more forward-thinking commanders are going to bring arrows along with the food for their army.<br /> <br /> Please note that those numbers might sound like criminally-few, but they really aren't. Yes, well-trained longbowmen can loose arrows at about 12/minute, but that is an 'OH SHIT' rate of fire, really only used in a pitched battle when the enemy is advancing. More 'usual' rates of fire are more along the lines of 6/minute or even 4/minute, which is still pretty goddamn fast, especially when there are 60 arrows aimed in your general direction, and when you consider that the average longbow is 5+ feet long and has a draw-weight approaching 100lbs. Using a longbow is <i>exhausting</i>, and the officers of longbow regiments will usually institute 'rotating fire' drills, where different sections of the regiment alternate fire, so as to give the archers a break.<br /> <br /> What this has to do with fire-arrows, I have no idea. But, still.....<br /> <br /> Fire-arrows are probably the most common anti-fortification weapon in the Hundred Kingdoms, as most of the buildings, castles included, are built of wood and wattle-and-daub. However, they are pretty uncommon, especially in field-battles, as they require a high degree of preparation beforehand to be used.<br /> <br /> &quot;fire arrows&quot; are longer, thicker (and heavier) than regular 'war arrows', and almost always have a <a href=\"http://www.longbowandarrow.co.uk/ekmps/shops/richardhead/images/-cage-fire-head-medieval-style-arrowhead-9931-p.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">cage-like head</a> as opposed to a stabbing point. This means they 1) take longer to load, 2) have less killing potential, and 3) weigh more than regular arrows, so the soldiers can carry less. There aren't that many in the supply depot of Kasai's army, for example.<br /> <br /> However, arrows-that-are-on-fire can be improvised pretty simply, although they suffer the same drawbacks. if not more, as 'real' fire arrows. Take small shafts of bamboo, tie on some feathers or even leaves to one end, and jam some pine-pitch or other flammable substance into the open front. They don't have to be good or even decent as arrows, just 'enough'.<br /> <br /> Anyways, Shin Takeshi will be along with more food and materiel, including arrows, in a few days.<br /> <br /> 2) Even the stupidest of bandits is cognizant of the hauntings by now, and likely would be even if Masanori didn't order the catacombs sealed up. Even still, they likely aren't going to leave: the ghosts stay within the catacombs, even at night (and <u>everyone</u> knows monsters don't like sunlight, so they are 'safe' during the day), and even those who go down to get water don't stay very long. It is, and this is an official unit of measure, <i>creepy as fuck</i> down there.<br /> <br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\">Technically, the ghosts actually <u>can't</u> leave the barrows; they were bound there by wards set into the stones. The wards weren't there for the ghosts (or, really, <i>those</i> ghosts) specifically, but the binding was certainly a happy little accident that nobody really minds. You will find out just what/whom the wards were for when the fortress gets taken, along with some more plot. </div></div> </div> </div> Even with the bandits knowing about the ghosts, they aren't going to leave the 'safety' of the fortress (at least, not <i>en masse)</i><br /> <br /> 3) No.<br /> <br /> Even though the Hill-Tribes haven't lived in this region for a few hundred years, their field-gods still have shrines and attachments to the area. Through ancient agreements, the dog-god can't enter the boundaries of the stone walls encircling forgotten farm-fields, much less approach Dun Nashuon.<br /> <br /> The field-gods are weak, weaker than even the Dog-God, but collectively they could overpower it, even more so now that the Dog-God is weakened. It is ........ a bit miffed about that, and with the whole situation in general. That, coupled with Kasai's revealing of future developments, has pretty much led to the Dog-God going &quot;peace out!&quot; and retreating to, effectively, sulk for a little while.<br /> <br /> The fact that the Dog-God had to basically ask a mortal for help was pretty embarrassing.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-16T10:55:40-07:00","id":1116900,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wise Old Guru</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1111735#post1111735\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">No arrows yet: we know they're low on ammo and there's no reason to give them more. Settle in for a siege-they have low organization/morale, they're gonna focus on their own lack of supplies rather than think about how we have to leave to make the harvest.<br /> <br /> They'll probably attempt to sally forth and charge us under cover of night-they're bandits, they won't like getting idea of a pitched battle without Masanori and his elites, and they're going to think it better to melt into the woods and lay low than try to hold a fort without food. Encourage them to make this tactical error by making your camp superficially appear to be weak at night-feasting and music and merriment should make you a tempting target while also hurting their morale by rubbing in that you don't have a food shortage. If you can set up your mess upwind of them so they smell your roast boar it whatever, so much the better. Be ready for them to swarm out at like two am, maybe try a pincer maneuver. THEN we use our arrows.</div> </div> </div> </div>Night attacks are ...... rare, to say the least. Creation is a world overwhelmingly without effective artificial light, Kasai's army included. Generally, most light (and heat) is going to come from campfires or oil lanterns, both of which are difficult to use with an army in motion.<br /> <br /> It is terribly easy to get turned around and lost during a night battle, much less attack.<br /> <br /> In the Hundred Kingdoms, and probably across Creation, overwhelmingly most battles take place during the day, in dry/good weather, preferentially at dawn. Now, since we are in summer, albeit at the end of the season, sunrise probably happens around 6am-ish.<br /> <br /> If we <i>were</i> to fight a battle at night, we would have to wait for a full moon. It was a full moon during the fight with the lunar, and it has been about a month and a half since then. Meaning the moon is actually getting pretty close to New (if I have fucked up the Lunar cycle, please tell me).<br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-16T19:11:08-07:00","id":1117040,"title":"","content":"Since the camping break provided a bit of time - we could look at sending say, 200 of the bandit &quot;surprise attackers&quot; after making some battle noises back into Dun Nashuon to hold the gates open for an assault - bonus points if they say they caught Masanori skulking around in our camp and put an arrow into him on their way back out after doing some damage to our forces.<br /> <br /> It is a pretty significant risk for the poor bastards bluffing their way back into the gate, but would make an assault much, much easier.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/649-nightwinder","name":"Nightwinder","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"154"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-17T18:20:36-07:00","id":1117524,"title":"","content":"Camp was much like losing your virginity: hot, sweaty, at times uncomfortable \u2026\u2026 and over and done with far too quickly. I had a good time, thanks for the well-wishes.<br /> <br /> \u2026.It appears that most of you want some combination of \u2018siege + assault\u201d; starve them out for a couple days, try some morale-attacks, and attack before the week is out.<br /> Let\u2019s see what happens.<br /> <br /> ---------------------------<br /> <br /> You sit back in your camp-stool, pondering the options laid out in front of you. After a few long minutes, you call out:<br /> <br /> \u201cTell me what you all think. &lt;That includes you, as you will be part of this. Any recommendations, based on what you know of your own forces?&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> The war-leader from the Paskamansett pipes up, \u201c&lt; Well, the youngbloods would serve as excellent flanking attackers. Of all the forces here, they would be the most capable of climbing the sides of the hillfort, and the best suited to sneaking around through the forests and over the hills to the sides of the fort. The only thing is, they don\u2019t have very much \u2018staying power\u2019&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt;What do you mean?&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> He shrugs, \u201c&lt; Generally, only the Chosen will have anything approaching armor, much less battlefield discipline. The youth-bands are the very definition of \u2018impetuous\u2019, and will be truly savage in the attack, eager for glory and reputation as they are\u2026.. but chances are they will rout if faced with any serious resistance. They don\u2019t have armor, and don\u2019t fight in formation, so their ability to defend themselves is hampered. Generally, we use the youth-bands as skirmishers and ambushers, screens along the line of battle.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> You frown, \u201c&lt;Would they be willing to serve as a flanking force?&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> The other chiefs laugh, \u201c&lt; Chief, if you gave them enough booze, they would charge into the very gates of hell. They are more than willing to do whatever we tell them to do, especially if there is blood and glory involved. Getting them to stay there and to stop is the hard part.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> You translate this for the benefit of your other captains, who murmur in agreement.<br /> <br /> One of your regimental officers leans over and points at the map, \u201cAt risk of stating the obvious, the western side of the hill seems to be the best avenue of attack. Masanori, what form of defenses are located there?\u201d<br /> <br /> Masanori rouses himself, \u201cAlong that section of hillside, which, again, is the flattest and gentlest, there is a series of earthen embankments, ditches and the like. Most of them were here when I got here, and I didn\u2019t focus too much effort into building them up, instead focusing effort on repairing what I could of the wall.\u201d<br /> <br /> Another officer asks, \u201cAnd those were\u2026..?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cNot very much, to my embarrassment. After 5 years, I had built a palisade around the western perimeter and about the gatehouse, which is on the southern side of the fortress. Pine logs, set on end into earthen berms, supported by drystone and thornbushes. The rest of the wall circumference was, and still is, little more than piles of rubble and earth. I reinforced that with piles of brush and more thornbushes, but that was mainly it.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c\u2026..why so little reinforcement?\u201d<br /> <br /> Masanori sighs, \u201cBecause it takes a great deal of efforts to make bandits do <i>anything</i>, much less work together. A great portion of those 5 years involved me stomping on other bandit-groups across the Akibara, and their attention-spans for anything other than wine, women or song was \u2026lacking. Even with the crucifixions.\u201d<br /> <br /> You interrupt Masanori\u2019s ruminations, \u201cSo, what you are telling me is that the eastern and northern sides of the fortress are basically unfortified?\u201d<br /> <br /> He shakes his head, \u201cNo, my Lord. While all those sides of the fortress have are berms and bushes, they will still take some effort to overcome. The thorns around here grow an inch long and more, and I piled them <i>deep</i> on the embankments.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cWill they burn?\u201d<br /> <br /> He blinks, \u201c\u2026..Probably. I planted most of the bushes, but whatever didn\u2019t fit I just piled on top. They should be good and dried by now\u2026.. do you have any mangonels, to launch fire?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c\u2026..Do I have any what?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c\u2026. I see.\u201d He rubs his chin in thought, \u201c I suppose if you use enough fire-arrows, you could set the bushes alight. It hasn\u2019t rained for a few days, and what water there is should be well-drained by now\u2026.\u201d<br /> <br /> Masanori continues, \u201c Anyways, the only \u2018real\u2019 way into the fortress is the main gate, and is where I concentrated most of my rebuilding efforts. The palisade is strongest there, and I ordered a watchtower built. The gate is made of slabs of pine, reinforced with oak beams. A good ram should be able to take it down, with a decent amount of effort\u2026. The problem is getting to the gate in the first place.\u201d<br /> <br /> He points again to the map, \u201c there <i>is</i> a road, of sorts, leading to the main gate. About 30 feet wide, it was even paved at one point, and any number of troops could travel along it without getting hung up. The main issue is that the entirety of the road, from start to finish, is within sight and bowshot of the fortress. The berms block in both sides of the road, looping across the face of the hill, but they don\u2019t provide cover from the fortress. So, while your Lordships troops push a ram up the hill, they will be under fire from archers the entire way. Whomever built this fortress knew what they were doing\u2026.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cJust how many archers are there in the fortress, Masanori?\u2019<br /> <br /> He sighs again, \u201cThe problem with your question , my Lord, is that bandits don\u2019t really organize themselves into regiments and sections. Trust me, it was hard enough organizing the troops I brought over to you, much less other bandit-groups under my banner. On top of that, most bandits don\u2019t have any form of standardization, especially when it comes to weaponry. Everyone fights as an individual. There isn\u2019t going to be any massed-volleys of arrows coming at you, just a whole bunch of individual ones.\u201d He stares at the fortress for a moment, \u201c Ahhhh, I estimate there is maybe \u2026\u2026 400 fighters with ranged weapons in the fortress?\u201d<br /> <br /> You turn to your officers, \u201cAnd how many do we have?\u201d<br /> <br /> After a few moments of conversation, \u201c \u2026.. About 240 Men-at-Arms with longbows, and <i>technically</i> 600 or so militiamen. Most of them showed up with spears and bows, but I wouldn\u2019t want to rely on them. Those numbers don\u2019t include the Tribes, either.\u201d<br /> <br /> Turning to the assembled Tribal chieftains, \u201c&lt; Of your combined forces, just how many ranged troops do you have?&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> They converse for a few moments, then \u201c&lt; Most Chosen do not fight with the bow, but instead use the javelin. We throw them right before a charge. Other than that\u2026.. most of the youth-bands will have some sort of long-range missile weapon, usually a sling or a bow. Others carry javelins.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> Nodding, \u201c&lt; And how many youth-bands are there?&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt;\u2026.probably about 2/3 of the force. There are \u2018only\u2019 about 150 Chosen here, so that leaves 250? 300? Youths&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> You sit back on your stool, \u201cSo, we outnumber them in archers and other ranged weapons\u2026\u201d<br /> <br /> You turn again to the Tribesmen, \u201c&lt;\u2026. My scouts found what they described as \u2018caves\u2019, leading into the hillside. I am willing to bet those are ventilation-shafts leading straight into the barrows below the hillfort. If your men would be willing \u2026\u2026 we could attack them from underneath.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> They blanch under their tanned skin, \u2018&lt; Chief, that \u2026\u2026 we should probably leave the ancestor-ghosts alone, until they can be properly propagated. But, if that is what you want\u2026..&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> You wave them off, \u201c&lt; No, nevermind. I do not want to potentially pay such a large price for \u2018maybe\u2019. Do you know the proper rituals to appease them?&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> They shrug, \u201c&lt;Not really. Usually, that gets left to the shamans and smokewalkers. But\u2026.killing those that disturbed them would certainly mollify them, as will returning the offerings to the sacred well.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt;Very well, but that will have to wait until we take the fortress\u2026&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> You sit quietly for some time, musing, and Sgt. Oxblood steps forwards,<br /> <br /> \u201cM\u2019 Lord, why not both?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c\u2026 What do you mean?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cAccording to the beancounters, we have 5 days before we need to start considering pulling out. According to Masanori here, they have, at most, 5 days of food in the fortress.\u201d He shrugs, \u201cSeems simple enough to me. We sit here for 4 days, feasting and enjoying ourselves, while they start eating roots and rats. They will see it, and that is going to start hurting <i>real bad</i>, if you get my drift. They will go to their bosses and demand more food, and the bosses will start arguing and fighting with each other. They certainly won\u2019t be working together.\u201d<br /> <br /> Masanori nods, \u201cThe bandit-captains in charge were the ones who got along the least, so that, coupled with the assassinations of other captains I did, could mean they are already highly suspicious of each other. The lack of food could very well push them over the edge.\u201d<br /> <br /> Sgt. Oxblood continues, \u201cSo, then, right before we have to leave, and as they run out of food, we attack. They will be demoralized, hungry, divided, while we will be fresh, reinforced, and eager. We will need the time to build a ram and mantlets, anyways, so why not make the most use of that time?\u201d<br /> <br /> After thinking over the plan for a long while, you nod. There is no need to rush into an attack while you can wait them out.<br /> <br /> You turn to the rest of your officers, \u201c The rest of you\u2026. settle in. Let us fortify the camp a bit more, put in some proper facilities; a field kitchen, some latrines, that sort of thing. If the kitchen could be placed upwind of Dun Nashuon, all the better. Set the troops on full-rations; give the bandits something to salivate over, and Sir Shin Takeshi will be along in a few days with more food anyways. Other than that, supervise the construction of assault and siege-equipment; a ram, mantlets, ladders, the works.\u201d<br /> <br /> Turning to the Tribal warleaders, \u201c&lt; As for you\u2026.. please have your youth-bands hunt and forage around the fortress. The more food we have, the better, and it will give them a chance to scout out where they would prefer to attack.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> To all of the assembled officers, \u201cOver the next few days, I will be coming up with an overall plan of battle. If anyone comes up with any pertinent information, do not hesitate to come to me.\u201d<br /> And so begins the Battle of Dun Nashuon, the next morning at least.<br /> <br /> To your surprise, you find that there isn\u2019t all that much for you to do, not personally at least. The expanding of camp and the building of siege-equipment is by-and-large handled by your retainers. The scouting and hunting of game is done almost automatically, and brace after brace of deer, mountain goat and rabbit are sent to the camp kitchen like clockwork. Trees are cleared from the paths of approach, setting up a staging area for the coming assault.<br /> <br /> All the while, you watch the fortress, and those in the fortress watch you. Mostly, you are just \u2026.. bored? Bored. Granted, you have the battle-plan to write, but that is mostly copying down the suggestions of Sgt. Oxblood, writing and revising and rewriting, until your hands ache. The only bit of excitement you get for the first two days is the arrival of Shin Takeshi with his milita-units and the food-reserves, and you knew about that for two days beforehand, forewarned by your scouts.<br /> <br /> All of this changes the third night.<br /> <br /> After a long day of reading scout reports, writing, rewriting and revising your battle-plan some more, and light sparring with <i>Moonlight Over Water</i> (the thing is perfectly balanced and perfectly sized for you. Having a God/Goddess as the commissioner has its benefits, apparently, and you can\u2019t wait to actually try the thing in battle, moratorium on fighting or no), you finally settle in to get some sleep, only to be awoken by the clarion call of a bugle sounding the picket alarm: enemies on the perimeter!<br /> <br /> Cursing, you rise from the cot, clutching at your side as the half-healed ribs burn, and stumble over to your weapon-rack. You don\u2019t have time to pull on your full panoply, and instead grab your dagger, thrusting it through the cord holding your undershirt shut, and pull <i>Moonlight Over Water</i> from it\u2019s sheath. The white-oak shaft is surprisingly cold, as the night is warm, and your fingers numb at the touch. Puzzled, you go to examine the weapon in the dim light of your desk-lamp, but get startled from your reverie by the repeated bugle-blast and the throwing-back of the door-flap of your pavilion. Standing on the other side of the opening is one of the soldiers assigned to your personal-guard, halberd held at the ready.<br /> <br /> \u201cForgive me, Lord Jianghu, but there reports of enemies attacking!\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cI am aware, thank you\u2026. How many and where?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c Northern side of the palisade, by the main gate, it sounds like. No idea on the number.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c Understood. Send my retainer, Masanori, in to assist me in arming myself.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cMy Lord, sir Masanori isn\u2019t present. I don\u2019t know where he would be found, either.\u201d<br /> <br /> You curse, strangely-behaving spear all but forgotten. \u201cFuck!...... Help me with this, then.\u201d<br /> <br /> After a few minutes of fumbling, the guard manages to get you suited up to some degree of effectiveness, and you head off in the direction of the bugle calls, followed by an impromptu guard-section of halberdiers.<br /> <br /> The camp is a frenzy of movement, as soldiers are woken up by the sound of the bugle, stumbling from their bedrolls and struggling to equip themselves by the light of banked fires. Most of the Men-at-Arms are relatively calm, having seen this before, but the militiamen are all-but panicking. Generally, an attack at night means a burned farmstead and dead family members, and the several of the militiamen you pass are in the throes of terror at the prospect of combat. You do your best to settle them with calm words and a steady hand, but you find yourself almost as unsettled as they.<br /> <br /> Why? You have seen combat before, killed your fair share of men. What is the difference now?...<br /> <br /> Regardless, as you pass through the camp, you gather up those soldiers and militiamen who are most ready to fight, and as you come out into the open parade-field by the main-gate of the encampment, you are accompanied by a not-insignificant number of troops; spearmen, archers, even a few of your Oathsworn officers you came across. They attempt to organize your impromptu force into some semblance of order, with varying degrees of success.<br /> <br /> In the parade-field, you find most of the troops assigned to night-watch, assembled on the wall-walk of the palisade, looking out over the mist-filled river valley. The moon is mostly dark, with but a thin sliver of silver peeking through the black of night, and the trampled meadow is but dimly-lit by the light of oil lanterns suspended along the wall-walk. The mist swallows everything a few scant feet away from the palisade, and as you peer into it, you feel your heart start to pound.<br /> <br /> On the small platform overlooking the gate, you catch a glimpse of both Masanori and Sergeant Oxblood, and as you approach, you can hear Masanori <i>laughing</i>. It is a cold sound, and your skin crawls, already set on edge as you are. Getting closer, you can hear him speaking to someone in the fog, voice heavy with cold disdain.<br /> <br /> \u201c\u2026be honest, Three-Leaves, I expected something of the like of you, but this, <i>this</i> is a new level of cowardice. Did you just expect to walk in and be welc\u2026\u201d<br /> <br /> You interrupt Masanori, making your irritation plain, \u201c\u2026Would someone kindly tell me what is going on, here? I\u2019ve got alarm-calls going off, an army in panic, and my retainer talking shit with some unknown enemy?\u201d You turn to the fog and call out sharply, \u201cPresent yourself, or be destroyed!\u201d<br /> <br /> Masanori smirks, cold amusement evident, \u201cJust a rat following its base urges, my Lord. When the ship sinks, it is every rat for itself, and they will drown each other in order to stay afloat a minute longer. There is no true danger here.\u201d<br /> <br /> You turn to Masanori, irritation blossoming into anger, \u201cSince you know so much, care to fill me in? And where were you? I don\u2019t expect you to be on my ass every minute, but at least let <i>someone</i> know where you are, in case I do need you!\u201d<br /> <br /> His expression turns remorseful, \u201c\u2026 Forgive me, my Lord Jianghu, but I \u2026.. sometimes have bad dreams, and do not sleep well even in the best of times. Walking usually helps, and I was walking the ramparts when I heard the alarm sound. I was one of the first ones here. I will be more responsible in the future.\u201d<br /> <br /> You soften a bit, \u201c\u2026. Understood, and understandable.\u201d Turning to the dark river valley, \u201cWhat, and who, is this?\u201d<br /> <br /> Masanori chuckles, \u201cThree-Leaves is one of the bandit-captains that followed me. He is greedy for status, yet a coward at heart, and will suck your dick only so long as it takes him to stab you in the back. The other bandit-leaders likely didn\u2019t take any of his shit; he wasn\u2019t very popular, and he has apparently run here with his tail between his legs.\u201d<br /> <br /> A voice calls out from the fog, \u201c<i>Go fuck yourself</i>, Masanori! What is the difference between you and me? We both turned against our comrades, that makes us <i>both</i> traitors! You just got here first!\u201d<br /> <br /> Masanori leans up against the railing, lips spreading into a cold grin, \u201cThe difference, you little scumbucket, between you and I is that I actually <i>believe</i> in the principles espoused by the Lord Jianghu. You\u2026.. well, you just don\u2019t want to die, simple as that. I joined him to protect people from men like you, who would slit their own mother\u2019s throat if you thought it would get you some respect.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c\u2026.<i>Bullshit</i>. Not a week ago, you were talking a big game about how you were going to kick \u2018the kids\u2019 ass! Now you drink his piss and call it wine?\u201d<br /> <br /> Masanori glances at you apologetically, \u201c\u2026..Sorry, I had to make my defection a surprise. That meant I had to make you seem lesser than you are.\u201d Turning again to the field, \u201c Regardless, what does that have to do with your defection? What, pray tell, do you bring to the bargaining table?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cI bring my followers, the same as you!\u201d<br /> <br /> Masanori says nothing, then turns to you and speaks quietly, \u201cWell, that changes things, my Lord.\u201d<br /> <br /> You frown, \u201cDoes he have a lot of troops?\u201d<br /> <br /> Masanori shakes his head, \u201cNo. He was a mid-level bandit-leader, little more. He was a glorified toady, attaching himself to larger, more skillful bandit-captains. They didn\u2019t like him very much, especially since he betrayed them as soon as a better position came along, but they couldn\u2019t turn down the bodies he brought with him. As for troops\u2026\u2026 eh. He had a lot of \u2018fresh-meat\u2019 under him. Good for shaking down peasants, not so good in a fight. But\u2026.. that can be amended with proper training, and they won\u2019t have that many bad habits to break\u2026\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cSo, can I join your side yet? We are hungry, and the night is cold\u2026.\u201d<br /> <br /> You step forward, calling out, \u201cCome forwards, into the light, let me get a look at you!\u201d, while giving Sergeant Oxblood a significant glance. He nods, and gestures to the archers in the parade-ground. They step up onto the wall-walk, and at the command \u201cNOCK\u201d, draw arrows and set them to bowstrings.<br /> <br /> As they do so, the bandits slowly, as if filled with trepidation, file forwards out of the fog. When they enter the range of the lantern\u2019s light, they kneel and lay their weapons at their feet.<br /> <br /> You pan your gaze across the assembled ruffians, several dozen all-told, noting that they wear a varied ensemble of armor and gear, with most of the fighters wearing little-to-no armor. Weapons are mostly spears, in various degrees of poor quality, with a few bows and only one or two sidearms among the throng. One of the bandits, the one in the heaviest armor (although that doesn\u2019t amount to much!), remains standing, and when he catches a glimpse of you, leaning heavily on your spear, he laughs out loud.<br /> <br /> \u201cMasanori, <i>come on</i>! Tell me you didn\u2019t betray our trust to follow this <i>beardless boy!\u201d</i><br /> <br /> Masanori growls, hand falling to the hilt of his sword, and Sergeant Oxblood lifts his hand, ready to give the command for the archers to draw and loose their projectiles. You lift your hand, stopping both of them from continuing, and call out to the man.<br /> <br /> \u201cMy name, <i>sir</i>, is Kasai Qianke, and I am the Lord of Jianghu, beard or not! If you wish to serve me, you will be <i>speaking to me with more respect</i>!\u201d<br /> <br /> At this close range, you can see his eyes flicker, from you, to the archers with bows half-drawn, to Masanori, hand-trembling on his blade. He drops to a knee, yet keeps looking you full in the face, and under his half-helm you see an ugly leer spread across his face. As your anger rises, you note in sudden shock that the shaft of <i>Moonlight Over Water </i>is <b>frigid<i>, </i></b>making your left arm go numb to the elbow, and the exposed skin of your face is pale and prickly with the cold. Suddenly remembering the event from earlier, you glance at the feathersteel of the spearhead, and note that the edges and filigree covering the weapon are gleaming softly, not lit by the flames of oil lamps, but by cold moonlight, flickering and shifting like its namesake\u2026.. yet there is no moon in the sky?<br /> <br /> <i>Just what the hell is going on</i>?<br /> <br /> You are torn from your reverie by the sudden speech of Three-Leaves, \u201c\u2026. Of course. I apologize, Lord Jianghu. I meant no offense, just expressed by my surprise at such a young figure occupying such an <i>important</i> position. As promised, I turn my command over to you\u2026. if you would accept me, of course.\u201d<br /> <br /> Masanori scoffs, exhaling forcefully, and you, distracted, take a long moment to reply.<br /> <br /> You,<ol class=\"decimal\"><li>Accept them! The more troops you have, the better, and any \u2018irregularities\u201d can be dealt with as they happen\u2026.</li> <li>Accept them, but have Masanori kill Three-Leaves quietly. With their leader gone, the men will fall into line quickly!</li> <li>Accept them \u2026\u2026 <b><i><u>if</u></i></b> Three-Leaves goes back to Dun Nashuon and returns with the head of another bandit-chieftain. Masanori proved his worth, and so do you!</li> <li>Accept them\u2026. If they go back to Dun Nashuon and open the gates for you, fighting on the inside. Having a turncoat inside the fortress could make a big difference!</li> <li>Turn them away\u2026.. after cutting off three fingers from each hand, leaving the index and the thumb. I do not want you, but you can go free\u2026.so long as I make it so you can never lift a weapon again!\u201d</li> <li>Kill them! They, poorly armed and poorly-disciplined as they are, will be more trouble than they are worth, and\u2026.. I do not trust them.</li> <li>Duel Three-Leaves! You insult me, yet try to get in my good graces? No, No!</li> <li>Something else? (Write in)</li> </ol>OOC:<br /> <br /> <i>Moonlight Over Water</i> is doing some <b>weird shit!</b><br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-17T20:50:17-07:00","id":1117576,"title":"","content":"2 is not an option. We are not going to give Masanori the same less than honourable jobs as the last guy. That would bring up a lot of bad memories for him that we don't want associated with us. If we accept them Three-Leaves is not to be in charge. Don't want to kill anyone we don't have too. We can't let them go back Dun Nashuon either (3-Leaves isn't exactly inspiring confidence).<br /> <br /> Our best option seems to be doing stupid s@#t (Accepting them, then dueling Three-Leaves for his disrespect and killing him in the duel). I am sick of doing stupid stuff.<br /> <br /> Will think on this more.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Thoughts ripple out, birthing others","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1624-falleneco","name":"FallenEco","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1624_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1927"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-17T21:15:35-07:00","id":1117585,"title":"","content":"I rather like 4) Accept them\u2026. If they go back to Dun Nashuon and open the gates for you, fighting on the inside. Having a turncoat inside the fortress could make a big difference!<br /> Make them earn a position in the new army. Survivors get split up, and may be trouble down the road, but Three Leaves won't last 5 years in an army; he'll desert and run away.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Like my Avatar? Courtesy of<span style=\"color:#0000FF\"> Jen</span>! : <span style=\"font-size:12px\">Anybody want their characters to be experimented on ? </span>post 98<br /> <i>An Exalt is never unarmed.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4018-l-het-esh","name":"L'het'esh","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4018_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"521"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-17T21:25:49-07:00","id":1117592,"title":"","content":"Accept their service. We intend to be a FAIR Lord, and that means accepting an honorable surrender. It wouldn't be right to offer clemency to Masanori and not to others.<br /> <br /> That being said, we trust Masanori's judgement. These men are to be disarmed and more importantly split up! If they stick with their friends they're going to fall into old habits and plotting. Ideally we should put each man into a squad of strangers who are our more loyal or experienced troops, maybe the troops under command of our Oathsworn or their trusted subordinates. Surround them with good influences and make each of them into the New Guy who needs to prove himself and human nature will take care of the rest. Their leader you put under Masanori's command: Masanori is going to be your Dragon, so you may as well give him dominion over lesser serpents. Plus, this gives Masanori the right to punish/execute the guy if he acts up.<br /> <br /> Which further cements Masanori's reputation as a traitor, which makes it harder for him to go back to banditry or rebuild his power base if he and his new Lord have any disagreements down the line.<br /> <br /> I wouldn't use them to open the gates, but we should get Intel on conditions inside the fortress.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>So I'm making <a href=\"http://godkickingboot.smackjeeves.com/\" target=\"_blank\">God-Kicking Boot</a>, an Exalted webcomic, now. Updates on Sundays. Full-color, mediocre but slowly improving art. It's a thing.<br /> <br /> The absence of a monument can, in its own way, be something of a monument also.</i> -Roger Zelazny","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/512-wise-old-guru","name":"Wise Old Guru","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar512_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1097"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-17T22:40:44-07:00","id":1117613,"title":"","content":"as usual guru speaks my mind. I considered forcing them to camp outside our walls but in retrospect his reasons for splitting up the group have won me over","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8897-joshopotamus","name":"joshopotamus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8897_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"97"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-18T04:44:58-07:00","id":1117672,"title":"","content":"Echo Guru's plan, except I also really feel like dueling. Or at least find out some way as to why our spear is acting up.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3752-mushroom-king","name":"Mushroom King","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3752_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"221"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-18T05:55:37-07:00","id":1117688,"title":"","content":"I'm also going to go with WiseOldGuru's plan. <br /> <br /> I hope we don't get any more defectors. This is starting to become a real management problem. Where are all these former bandits going to go when the the battle is over? Our peasant conscripts and Oathsworn all have lands or trades to attend to, but these guys...?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>On the frontier of the Wild South, there's only one woman with the grit to take on its most dangerous outlaws and bring them <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/984977-back-alive-or-maybe-dead-quest\" target=\"_blank\">Back Alive, or Maybe Dead.</a><br /> <br /> Avatar by <a href=\"http://puzzlinghappenstance.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">K.S. Brenowitz</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/908-semicasual","name":"semicasual","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar908_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2499"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-18T07:47:59-07:00","id":1117733,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>semicasual</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1117688#post1117688\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm also going to go with WiseOldGuru's plan.<br /> <br /> I hope we don't get any more defectors. This is starting to become a real management problem. Where are all these former bandits going to go when the the battle is over? Our peasant conscripts and Oathsworn all have lands or trades to attend to, but these guys...?</div> </div> </div> </div>I thought we had some kind of garrison or service to put most of them in, and after a certain point they're given land of their own I thought?<br /> That said, what about serfdom? For those that surrender but don't want to serve? We take their weapons, spread them out and put them to work on our personal fields? And if they act up, we'd theoretically have more rights to punish/execute them?<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3752-mushroom-king","name":"Mushroom King","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3752_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"221"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-18T07:53:46-07:00","id":1117734,"title":"","content":"Hmmmm, is it wise to have a bunch of cut throats dispersed in our ranks? Also Masanori said he took the only worthwhile soldiers from the bandits. It feels like Luna has weighed in on this too. I say kill them but if we do take them they need to be not directly involved in the battle or armed, preferably locked up but I doubt we have that option right now.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11567-jairain","name":"Jairain","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11567_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"345"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-18T13:54:25-07:00","id":1117922,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>semicasual</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1117688#post1117688\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm also going to go with WiseOldGuru's plan.<br /> <br /> I hope we don't get any more defectors. This is starting to become a real management problem. Where are all these former bandits going to go when the the battle is over? Our peasant conscripts and Oathsworn all have lands or trades to attend to, but these guys...?</div> </div> </div> </div>You fine folks chose &quot;army service reward&quot; as part of your recruitment efforts, meaning after 5 years of military service, the mustered-out soldiers will be granted a 40-acre plot of land and an ox.<br /> <br /> By that, I basically sought to emulate the Roman Empire after the Marian Reforms: serve in the army, and receive lands after mustering out, usually in regions they conquer, along with citizenship. This served many purposes: 1) decrease the amount of urban poor that had no lands of their own, 2) increase the population in frontier regions, especially with people that can defend themselves, so on and so forth. Citizenship on top that was just gravy.<br /> <br /> In Jianghu, about 1/3 of the land isn't even touched; fields lay fallow, orchards choked with overgrowth,pastures lay barren. And that isn't even touching the lands that will become available when the Akibara gets cut back, or the hills around Jianghu get settled.<br /> <br /> After 5 years of service, Kasai will cede these lands to the veterans of his armies, who will then do what anyone with a new lease on life would do: start a family, pick up a trade, grow crops. Remember: 40 acres is a pretty nice plot of land in the Hundred Kingdoms, and an ox is a pretty expensive draft animal, useful for pulling a plow or carrying goods to market.<br /> <br /> Once the lands are actually put to good use, the economy of Jianghu will expand: food will be shipped out, trade-goods will be produced, and perhaps most importantly, <i>taxes will be paid</i>. Part of the deal is that the mustered-out soldiers will be granted membership in the Peasant class. Peasants pay taxes on the land they own, and <u>also</u> serve as soldiers in time of war.<br /> <br /> However, <u>before</u> the 5 years of service is up, Kasai functionally has an army that he can do whatever he wants with, <u>whenever he likes</u>. Remember: Peasant Men-at-Arms are farmers first, and fighters second, and the 'campaign season' in the Hundred Kingdoms is only about 60 days long. Since these new soldiers are <u>not yet farmers</u>, instead being 'professionals&quot; who soldier full-time..... they don't have to go home at the end of the summer to prevent crop failures. All this nonsense about Kasai worrying about the time left before he has to send the army home <u>won't be an issue at all</u> when the new soldiers start arriving.<br /> <br /> Want to surprise-attack the forces of Yue in the beginning of spring? Sure, go ahead! ROFLStomp your way through Sanada Kotaro's domain, while the peasants are working at the autumn harvest? Have fun! Be an utterly-crazy bastard, and order a march in the dead of winter? Entirely possible!<br /> <br /> That sort of behavior will make the other Lords in the Hundred Kingdoms piss their fine silk kimono's, as that screws with the traditional military balance of &quot;campaign during summer <u>ONLY</u>&quot;. Almost all aspects of military methodology in the Hundred Kingdoms is oriented around that little fact of reality ..... and Kasai just broke it over his knee.<br /> <br /> When they aren't being used, Kasai could station the army at points across Jianghu, allowing him to be able to intercept threats before they become major, or work on road-building, land-clearing, bandit-killing, aka stuff that needs to get done.<br /> <br /> Technically, unless you say otherwise, any bandits that defect are going to be folded into this same body of troops. They serve for 5 years, and get a new lease on life; that is the offer Kasai made....... which is a pretty damn good offer, which is why Masanori showed up with 300 troops. And...... also technically, the bandits also will have pertinent life-skills, albeit ones that weren't applicable when they were bandit-ing it up. Most bandits are deserted soldiers, aka peasant Men-at-Arms, and they had skills before they deserted, mostly oriented around farming, minor crafts, carpentry, blacksmithing, etc. Even the ones that weren't deserted soldiers, mostly those who had trouble with the law, still have applicable skills.<br /> <br /> Of course, folding them into polite, civilized society at the end of the 5 year term will be ....... difficult. Best have the Sergeant Oxblood beat the shitheadedness out of them before then.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-18T14:40:55-07:00","id":1117936,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Mushroom King</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1117733#post1117733\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I thought we had some kind of garrison or service to put most of them in, and after a certain point they're given land of their own I thought?<br /> That said, what about serfdom? For those that surrender but don't want to serve? We take their weapons, spread them out and put them to work on our personal fields? And if they act up, we'd theoretically have more rights to punish/execute them?</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Technically, since they are bandits, the usual go-to punishment is death by hanging.<br /> <br /> Making them into serfs would be, in comparison, an <u>amazingly merciful</u> sentencing. Most Lords, if they went that route, would make them slaves instead, and take precautionary measures to prevent them from ever causing trouble again.<br /> <br /> Usually, that involves maiming. Hands will get removed, brands will be applied, so on and so forth. Kasai's option, the removal of all but the index finger and the thumb, is, yet again, amazingly merciful. Yes, it is painful, and debilitating, but you can still work, and won't be forced into begging like <a href=\"http://godkickingboot.smackjeeves.com/comics/2379046/page-85-sharp-edged-principles-stance/\" target=\"_blank\">this dude</a>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-18T15:06:15-07:00","id":1117946,"title":"","content":"Shit ya'll gonna make me change my vote. I'm thinking duel the douschebag. Integrate his forces into ours when he is dead. He'd only lead them to some other shit bag when he got the chance anyway.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8897-joshopotamus","name":"joshopotamus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8897_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"97"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-18T15:23:51-07:00","id":1117956,"title":"","content":"Definitely don't duel him. We have nothing to gain and much to lose by doing so. If we win... nobody is impressed and we've killed a man that for some reason these bandits respect and that's going to give a few of them reason to hold a grudge.<br /> <br /> More importantly...we have sworn to our liege that we will be serving her in battle. We already have injuries from our last two duels! Our life is not ours to risk right now. We have a duty to our liege and our people that is more important than our being miffed at one impertinent bandit!<br /> <br /> Also... getting a reputation as a man who executes his enemies when they surrender is a good way to wind up with enemies who fight ferociously and to the death. Always give the other guy a way out, if only to make him-and his soldiers-hesitate with indecision.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>So I'm making <a href=\"http://godkickingboot.smackjeeves.com/\" target=\"_blank\">God-Kicking Boot</a>, an Exalted webcomic, now. Updates on Sundays. Full-color, mediocre but slowly improving art. It's a thing.<br /> <br /> The absence of a monument can, in its own way, be something of a monument also.</i> -Roger Zelazny","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/512-wise-old-guru","name":"Wise Old Guru","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar512_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1097"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-18T17:47:09-07:00","id":1118010,"title":"","content":"I also vote against dueling Three-Leaves. It's kind of pointless to do that when he's basically at Kasai's mercy already, regardless of how he offends Kasai.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>On the frontier of the Wild South, there's only one woman with the grit to take on its most dangerous outlaws and bring them <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/984977-back-alive-or-maybe-dead-quest\" target=\"_blank\">Back Alive, or Maybe Dead.</a><br /> <br /> Avatar by <a href=\"http://puzzlinghappenstance.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">K.S. Brenowitz</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/908-semicasual","name":"semicasual","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar908_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2499"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-18T18:27:48-07:00","id":1118017,"title":"","content":"...yeah, good point about the duelling. It is not worth it.<br /> <br /> Wise Old Guru's plan is solid though. Accept their service and split them up as evenly as we can, preferably in different squads. We don't trust them but we do want to be fair. Placing Three-Leaves under Masanori is just good sense","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Thoughts ripple out, birthing others","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1624-falleneco","name":"FallenEco","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1624_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1927"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-19T13:01:52-07:00","id":1118252,"title":"","content":"Couple new thoughts:<br /> 1) still think Guru has a great plan (okay, not a new thought). That still largely has my vote.<br /> 2) what else is going on right now? We still have our guards posted and looking around right? I'd hate to think this guy was serving as a distraction or trap. And we can see him laughing at us under his helm. And <b>something</b> has our spear freaking out.<br /> 3) Yeah, okay, dueling is risky and almost benefit-less... but what about <i>testing </i>him? Masanori convinced us he would be loyal to the same ideas we hold sacred. Prove both your skill by staying alive and your loyalty to your new job by not slaying or wounding your opponent (either Kasai or Masanori). If your opponent is unharmed and you are still alive after 30 seconds, you pass. We keep our five-year policy, but if you give Kasai reason to doubt <i>right in front of him</i>, he reserves the right to confirm your loyalty. Then, if he survives, put him under Masanori's command.<br /> 4) Really want to know what's up with our spear. it feels like the closer it gets to this guy the colder it becomes.<br /> 5) ask one of Three-Leaves' defectors what's going on in the camp. Regardless of what happens to Three-Leaves.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3752-mushroom-king","name":"Mushroom King","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3752_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"221"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-19T13:51:35-07:00","id":1118269,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Mushroom King</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1118252#post1118252\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Couple new thoughts:<br /> 1) still think Guru has a great plan (okay, not a new thought). That still largely has my vote.<br /> 2) what else is going on right now? We still have our guards posted and looking around right? I'd hate to think this guy was serving as a distraction or trap. And we can see him laughing at us under his helm. And <b>something</b> has our spear freaking out.<br /> 3) Yeah, okay, dueling is risky and almost benefit-less... but what about <i>testing </i>him? Masanori convinced us he would be loyal to the same ideas we hold sacred. Prove both your skill by staying alive and your loyalty to your new job by not slaying or wounding your opponent (either Kasai or Masanori). If your opponent is unharmed and you are still alive after 30 seconds, you pass. We keep our five-year policy, but if you give Kasai reason to doubt <i>right in front of him</i>, he reserves the right to confirm your loyalty. Then, if he survives, put him under Masanori's command.<br /> 4) Really want to know what's up with our spear. it feels like the closer it gets to this guy the colder it becomes.<br /> 5) ask one of Three-Leaves' defectors what's going on in the camp. Regardless of what happens to Three-Leaves.</div> </div> </div> </div>1) Around the army encampment is a palisade. <u>Outside</u> the palisade, distance dependent on terrain, is a line of <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Picket_(military)\" target=\"_blank\">pickets,</a> or forward scouts. They are on watch for, well, enemy movement. When they spot/hear movement, they give calls via bugle or trumpet.<br /> <br /> They are stationed all around the encampment, and get relieved regularly, so if one goes missing it will be known about in short order.<br /> <br /> 99.999% of the logistical stuff involved in this campaign was set up by Sergeant Oxblood (obviously, Kasai was there, since he is getting tutored), and the man is <u>very</u> good at his job.<br /> <br /> 2) A possibility.<br /> <br /> 4) ..... You will see, now or later : D<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-19T14:14:55-07:00","id":1118275,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Boston123</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1118269#post1118269\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> 1) Around the army encampment is a palisade. <u>Outside</u> the palisade, distance dependent on terrain, is a line of <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Picket_(military)\" target=\"_blank\">pickets,</a> or forward scouts. They are on watch for, well, enemy movement. When they spot/hear movement, they give calls via bugle or trumpet.<br /> <br /> They are stationed all around the encampment, and get relieved regularly, so if one goes missing it will be known about in short order.<br /> <br /> 99.999% of the logistical stuff involved in this campaign was set up by Sergeant Oxblood (obviously, Kasai was there, since he is getting tutored), and the man is <u>very</u> good at his job.<br /> <br /> 2) A possibility.<br /> <br /> 4) ..... You will see, now or later : D</div> </div> </div> </div>are these numbers right? did you mean, 2) and 3), or 1) and 2)?<br /> Because if you meant 1) and 2), it feels like we should double-check the alarms<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/tongue.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Stick Out Tongue\" smilieid=\"5\" class=\"inlineimg\" />.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3752,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3752-mushroom-king","user":"Mushroom King","date":". 07-19-2017, 05:28 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3752-mushroom-king","name":"Mushroom King","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3752_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"221"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-19T14:34:56-07:00","id":1118280,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Mushroom King</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1118275#post1118275\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> are these numbers right? did you mean, 2) and 3), or 1) and 2)?<br /> Because if you meant 1) and 2), it feels like we should double-check the alarms<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/tongue.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Stick Out Tongue\" smilieid=\"5\" class=\"inlineimg\" />.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Yeah.... I screwed up on the numbers.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-24T06:43:43-07:00","id":1119862,"title":"","content":"No worries! Just checking! Still a really fun read! <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3752-mushroom-king","name":"Mushroom King","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3752_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"221"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-26T20:13:08-07:00","id":1121166,"title":"","content":"Most of you want to accept Three-Leaves into our command, so long as we divide his forces among our own, to prevent any undue plotting or scheming, as well as placing Three-Leaves himself under Masanori\u2019s heel. Others suggested various tests of loyalty.<br /> We shall see how things play out.<br /> <br /> -----------------------<br /> <br /> After a long moment of thought, you call out, \u201cVery well. I accept you into my service, pending an oath of loyalty. Once you are inside, come to my pavilion, and we will discuss terms.\u201d Turning to Sg. Oxblood, \u201cOpen the gates.\u201d<br /> <br /> With a short shrug, he gives the order, and Masanori steps forward, \u201cNow, Lord Jianghu, I do not wish to tell you what to do, but\u2026.. do you <i>really</i> think this is wise? Three-Leaves is about as trustworthy as..\u201d<br /> <br /> You cut him off, firmly yet politely, \u201cMasanori, I can\u2019t very well offer you absolution, and not offer it to others who wish to submit. That would make me a liar and a hypocrite, both epithets that I do not want, nor can afford. I intend to be known as a fair ruler, and that means keeping my word, even if it would seem foolish. Besides, even I know that killing those who surrender will do nothing but make it less likely in the future.\u201d<br /> <br /> He stares, \u201c\u2026 I can understand that, my Lord, but \u2026\u2026 Three-Leaves, really? What, exactly, do you expect him to do? He is barely a leader, and will almost-certainly betray you as soon as it is convenient\u2026.\u201d<br /> <br /> You raise an eyebrow, \u201cI expect him to follow my every order to the letter, never faulting or shirking. If he does, then our agreement is off, and I will punish him for his past crimes. As will you.\u201d<br /> He looks confused, \u201c\u2026.say again?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cI am placing Three-Leaves under your command, Masanori. You are my first retainer, and as such, you will have superiority over the other retainers under me. You will manage him, and if you find him wanting\u2026\u2026.\u201d, you raise your hands in a gesture of finality. \u201cJust come to me first.\u201d<br /> <br /> He looks shocked, then amused, \u201cI must admit, I am a mite touched you trust me so early on, my Lord.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cJust an appeal to your better nature, Masanori.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cMy better nature?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cSure. You are a good man at heart, Masanori, even if you yourself don\u2019t believe it. Your \u2018defection\u2019 wasn\u2019t entirely pragmatic.\u201d<br /> <br /> He rolls his eyes in response, yet a small smile remains ghostlike on his lips.<br /> <br /> Turning to Sgt Oxblood, \u201cSergeant, please ensure our most recent recruits\u2026.. shall we say, start seeing new people?\u201d<br /> <br /> He nods, \u201cGood idea, M\u2019Lord. Whenever there is a troublesome group, it is best to split them up before the trouble gets worse. Any preferences for how you want them divided?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cNot really, so long as they can\u2019t collaborate effectively with each other. Splitting them up between the regiments Masanori brought over would be a good start. Other than that\u2026.. keep the night-watch on high-alert for now, for a few more hours at least. I would hate for this to be an ambush in disguise.\u201d<br /> <br /> The Sergeant knuckles his brow, \u201cBy your command, M\u2019Lord.\u201d, and starts dragging men from the line walking into the encampment. As Three-Leaves passes by, you and Masanori bracket him, with you up front and Masanori taking the rear.<br /> <br /> As you limp back to your pavilion, you feel the frost ebb from <i>Moonlight Over Water</i>, until all that remains is a slight tingle in your fingers. Evidently, the weapon responds to your emotional state, or, at least, that is what this has been happening thus far. Suddenly, you think back to when you fought the Lunar, and used the weapons blessed by the Horned Huntress. Granted, they smoked and burned instead of freezing and gleaming, but\u2026\u2026 could this be the same thing, or something similar?<br /> <br /> Perturbed, you wonder just what this could mean. Supernatural stuff like this is <i>supposed</i> to be the purview of the Dragon-Blooded Princes of the Earth, but \u2026.. this spear was a gift, from one of the Most High Rulers of Heaven! It can\u2019t be <i>bad</i>, or unholy Anathemic sorcery\u2026.. can it?<br /> <br /> You push the thought to the back of your mind when you come up to your pavilion. You will cross that bridge when you come to it, and you have more pressing concerns at the moment.<br /> Inside your pavilion, you turn and face Three-Leaves, who remains standing until Masanori presses in the back of a knee. He drops to a knee with a bitten-off curse and a glare at Masanori, and barks,<br /> <br /> \u201cWhat\u2019s this about, then? I turned over my forces to you, didn\u2019t I?\u201d<br /> <br /> You say nothing, dropping into a camp-stool and looking the man in the face for a long moment. Then, as you point across the tent, to a jar of plum wine, you mildly ask,<br /> <br /> \u201cTell me, sir Three-Leaves\u2026.. do you know what the term \u2018retainer\u2019 means?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c\u2026.huh?\u201d, and Masanori rolls his eyes in exasperation, pouring cups of drink.<br /> <br /> \u201cA retainer, my good man, is someone in service to a figure of authority. It means, almost literally, \u2018one who serves\u2019.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c\u2026.ok. What\u2019s this got to do with me?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c That is our agreement. In return for becoming one of my retainers for 5 years, serving me with distinction and efficacy, I will absolve you for any and all crimes you may have committed, both to me and to my people. Thuggery, thievery, assault and murder, all will be washed away\u2026.<i>if</i> you serve.\u201d<br /> <br /> His mouth gapes, \u201c<i>5 years!</i>\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cYes, 5 years. Not enough to negate a life of crime, but a good start. What say you?\u201d<br /> <br /> He broods for a long moment, \u201c\u2026. And if I work for you for 5 years, I\u2019ll get what Masanori gets?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cIt depends.\u201d<br /> <br /> Angrily, \u201cWhat does that mean?\u201d<br /> <br /> You stand up, knees shaking, using <i>Moonlight Over Water</i> for support. The shaft goes ice-cold again, numbing your fingers, and you take solace from the apparent solidarity.<br /> <br /> \u201cMasanori has already both proven his loyalty, and proven his worth. He brought 300 soldiers with him, <i>detailed</i> information regarding the forces, resources and fortifications inside Dun Nashuon, and taken out several key commanders on the enemy side. In essence, he has all-but won this war for me, and <i>that</i>, among other things, is why he is getting a promotion. You, on the other hand\u2026 have brought with you a scant few dozen men, <i>beardless boys</i> as you put it, and no information. Do you think that is worth an offer of vassalage?\u201d<br /> <br /> Quietly, you accept the cup of wine from Masanori, then continue.<br /> <br /> \u201cRegardless, I wish to be known as an honest ruler, one who appreciates and rewards his faithful servants, and if you prove to be worthy of vassalage, then by the gods that is what you will get\u2026.. at the end of 5 years of service. Masanori is bound to the same terms as you, so do not feel left out. But\u2026 I still question your loyalty.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cIn the interests of appearing magnanimous, I am going to offer you the choice how you prove your loyalty, as opposed to simply ordering you. Hmmm\u2026\u2026 you can fight, either Masanori or I, proving both your skill and your ability to follow instructions, you can bring me the head of one of the bandit-leaders in Dun Nashuon, to prove your defection true, or\u2026. You can push the ram in the assault, to prove your dedication. Your choice.\u201d<br /> <br /> Three-Leaves shakes his head almost instantly. \u201cIf I go back to the fort, they\u2019ll kill me before I say, \u2018How do you do.\u2019\u201d\u2026.. He glances at Masanori, blanching at his sudden feral grin, then turns his eyes to you. As he takes in your trembling knees, weight leant on your spear, the corners of his lips start to twitch up in a sneer, and as he opens his mouth to reply, you lean close and whisper.<br /> <br /> \u201cBefore you speak, let me tell you how I received these injuries, and <i>just why</i> the Tribes are here in the assault. Tell me \u2026.. what do you know of the Lunar Anathema?\u201d<br /> <br /> His face blanches again, and Masanori frowns, lifting an eyebrow.<br /> <br /> \u201cThere was a Lunar Anathema plaguing the lands of the Paskamansett Tribe, who make up one of the warbands here. In return for their support, not only here, but in the war with Yue, I ventured out to slay the beast.\u201d<br /> <br /> Masanori interjects, \u201cYou\u2026.fought a <i>Lunar Anathema</i>? And you survived?\u201d<br /> <br /> You grin, pulling back your lips to expose the enlongated canines, appreciating how Three-Leaves draws back (you begin to understand why your grandfather does it so much), \u201cMy clan-name is <i>Qianke</i>, Masanori. Would you please inform Three-Leaves just what that stands for?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c\u2026. One of the way of the spear.\u201d, he breathes.<br /> <br /> \u201cExactly. I <i>did</i> survive, albeit broken and bloodied, but more, I was <i>rewarded</i> with the spear I now hold. Its name is <i>Moonlight Over Water</i>, and per my clan-name, I am <i>very</i> skilled with it.\u201d<br /> <br /> You abruptly toss you cup of wine in the air, watching both Masanori and Three-Leaves jerk back. Before the cup can fall, or the wine slosh from the rim, you whirl the spear around, intercepting the bottom of the cup and balancing the whole ensemble on the blade. Sliding the shaft smoothly between your fingers, you bring the blade up to your chin and sip from the captured cup.<br /> <br /> Perfectly chilled. Wonderful.<br /> <br /> You turn back to the two men, \u201cJust to \u2026.disabuse the notion that I may be incompetent at combat.\u201d<br /> <br /> Aghast, Three-Leaves mutters, \u201cI\u2019ll\u2026. uh, I\u2019ll be at the front of the ram tomorrow, if it please you, m\u2019Lord.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c<i>Excellent</i>. For now, you can set up your equipment here, in my pavilion, where I can call you if I need you. Why don\u2019t you head down to the quartermaster, to pick up what you might need?\u201d<br /> <br /> He nods hurriedly, eager to be out of the range of your weapon. Walking backwards, he quickly leaves the tent, bowing every other step. A few moments pass after he leaves, and you sink into the stool with a sigh. Masanori stays quiet for another moment, then,<br /> <br /> \u201c\u2026 That was quite a show.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cI hope so. I was trying to intimidate him. Technically, I am not supposed to fight in earnest for another month or so, so I don\u2019t know what would have happened if he called my bluff.\u201d<br /> <br /> Masanori stays quiet again, then says, \u201c Do you honestly expect him to last five years?\u201d<br /> <br /> You recline back in the stool, stretching out your legs with a wince, \u201cMasanori, I don\u2019t expect him to last a <i>week</i>. But, that lack of character is on him, not I. I gave him the chance for absolution, it is up to him if he wants to keep his side of the bargain. I, to be honest, am not even worrying about it. For all I know, he could catch an arrow in the throat tomorrow.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cDo you <i>want</i> him to?\u201d, as he gives you a meaningful glance.<br /> <br /> It takes you a moment to actually understand what Masanori is getting at. \u201cNo. No\u2026.. I\u2019m willing to give him the benefit of the doubt.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cVery well.\u201d He is quiet again, then suddenly interjects, surprising you as you almost fall asleep. \u201cIf I may ask, what is the plan for tomorrow?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cOh! Uh\u2026. Here, there is a map over here\u2026. Somewhere\u2026\u201d<br /> <br /> Rousing yourself from the stool, you limp over to your desk and start shifting aside papers. Discipline reports\u2026.. materiel reports\u2026. various minutiae\u2026.. there! You pull out a redrawn sketch of the map given to you by your light cavalrymen, and Masanori pulls an oil lamp close.<br /> <br /> He looks over the map for a long while, then points at several markings on the map.<br /> <br /> \u201cThese units here, what are they?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c The Jianghu Men-at-Arms.\u201d<br /> <br /> He frowns, \u201c<i>All</i> of them?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cYes.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c\u2026.Why?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cThey are going to be the distracting force.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cBut they have the ram and the siege ladders, as well as the Militia in support?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cThey are going to be one hell of a distracting force.\u201d<br /> <br /> He studies the map for another long moment, then nods, \u201cAlright, in that case, who is the <i>attacking</i> force?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cThe Hill-Tribes, specifically the younger bands.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cAh.\u201d He breathes, \u201cI see. They are going to flank, while you make a lot of flash and fire from the gate-side.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c\u2026. Is that something you wouldn\u2019t do?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cNo, that is a pretty \u2018standard\u2019 strategy, actually. Not bad thinking, however. Several commanders have problems understanding \u2018fix and flank\u2019, believe me or not. In this case, you are making the <i>distracting</i> force appear to be the <i>attacking</i> force, all the while as your <i>real</i> attacking force swoops in from the \u2026. east?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cAnd the north. The forest creeps up right to the flanks of the hill.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201chmmm\u2026. When are they supposed to make their move?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cIdeally, when they hear the gate crash down, but we will be blowing orders via bugle as well.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cWill they heed the commands?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cThe youth-bands\u2026 probably not.\u201d You say with a sigh, \u201cThat is why I am sticking the Hill-Tribe Chosen over there with them, to provide some discipline, as well as support if the attack doesn\u2019t carry the day.\u201d<br /> <br /> He steps back from the table, running his chin, \u201cSo\u2026. You have two different forces, both of ample size, gearing to hit the enemy from two different directions. Not bad. A pretty \u2018standard\u2019 maneuver, as I said, but not a bad one. It certainly helps that the enemy will be divided and disorganized\u2026\u2026 when is the attack?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cTomorrow, at midday. I originally planned to assault at dawn, but Three-Leaves little stunt here forced me to push it back.\u201d<br /> <br /> Masanori scoffs, \u201cDawn-attacks are \u2026. Overrated and clich\u00e9d, if those terms can apply. <i>Everyone</i> does them, and so everyone is prepared for them.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cWhy does everyone attack at dawn?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cIt gives you the most time to pursue an attack, or bring about a retreat. Don\u2019t get me wrong, a dawn-attack <i>is</i> practical, I just \u2026.. preferred shaking things up a bit, to keep the enemy on their toes.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cWhen did you prefer to attack?\u201d, you ask, genuinely interested.<br /> <br /> He shrugs, \u201cAbout mid-morning, when the enemy is preparing and eating breakfast.\u201d At you confused look, he continues, \u201cThink about it. A little before dawn, in preparation for an attack, a commander will rouse a decent portion of their troops. You know this; you have seen it done at this very encampment.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cThese troops will, basically, sit there and wait for either an attack, or the all-clear signal. <i>While</i> they wait, however, they are getting more and more strung out, more tired, more hungry. When breakfast is ready, these troops will stand down to rest, and everyone will be freshened for the day. Right before then is when I attack, before the dawn-watch gets to stand down, but as the rest of camp is still in the process of waking up.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cAnd you found it to be more effective?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cUnbelievably so. It pissed off the enemies I fought to no end, but I didn\u2019t really care about what they thought, of them or their \u2018rules\u2019. My methods saved lives, both my soldiers and theirs, and to me, that was worth the \u2018dishonor\u2019\u201d. This last word is accompanied with an eyeroll and air-quotes.<br /> <br /> \u201cWhat else did you do?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cUsed a lot of siege engines in field battles, mostly. Not to diminish my prowess as a field commander, but ample use of siege engines turned the tide of Fate in my favor more than once.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cSiege engines?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cBig, spindly things, throw rocks, that sort of thing.\u201d<br /> <br /> You sit back, thinking if you have ever seen any siege engines period, much less one getting dragged out to battle. As far as you remember, the first siege engine you saw in your entire life was at Teriake Castle, and that was a little thing for throwing shrapnel at the gate approach.<br /> <br /> \u201cHow did you use them? Can you build some now?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cNot in the timeframe we have left, Lord Qianke. I\u2019ll need large timbers, carpenters, blacksmiths, not to mention the time I need to train you in their implementation. Some other time, there will be other wars to fight\u2026. It is late, and both of us will need our strength for the assault tomorrow. Three-Leaves should be back soon, anyways.\u201d<br /> <br /> And, lo and behold, in comes Three-Leaves, tromping all over the carpet spread over the grass, carrying a bedroll and muttering darkly to himself. Masanori helps you remove your panoply (much faster than the guardsman!), then bows and retreats to his own setup by the foyer.<br /> <br /> Seemingly almost as soon as you lay your head down, you are woken up by Masanori shaking you awake.<br /> <br /> \u201cWhat time is it?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cA little before dawn.\u201d<br /> <br /> After taking a moment to process the whole \u2018waking-up\u2019 thing, you get up off the cot, not without a great deal of help from Masanori, and head out into the main section of the pavilion. Three-Leaves is snoring loudly, and the air has a not-insignificant chill to it.<br /> <br /> Autumn is coming, and you had better get a move-on.<br /> <br /> Shrugging on a jacket, you turn to Masanori, \u201cI would like to call an officer\u2019s meeting soon, before sunrise if possible, so we can go over the plan of attack. Wake him,\u201d jerking your head towards the sleeping ex-bandit, \u201cand have him bring breakfast, or hot drinks at least.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cBy your command.\u201d, and he strides off, not without delivering a swift kick to Three-Leaves ribs.<br /> <br /> It only takes a few minutes for most of your regimental officers to walk in, with some having risen long before dawn, and you are able to start your officer\u2019s meeting just as the sun starts to rise.<br /> You rise to your feet at the head of the trestle-table, \u201cGood morning everyone. I hope you had a restful night, because we are all going to need it before long.\u201d<br /> <br /> You point towards the map, staked out in the center of the table.<br /> <br /> \u201cOur general plan of attack is twofold. Firstly, the gate of Dun Nashuon will be assaulted by the Men-at-Arms of Jianghu, supported by the Militia, all under my command. Secondly, while we are assaulting the gate, the Hill-Tribes, under the command of Warchief Samoset of the Paskamansett, will climb the hillside from the east and north, in effort to outflank and destroy the opposition at the gate.\u201d<br /> <br /> This gets translated to the Hill-Tribe warleaders, who have but a few questions of their own.<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt;When do you want us to attack?&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c&lt;Preferably, when the gate gets destroyed, but we will sound a blast on our horns to signal you anyways. If you can keep the attack silent until then , so to spring a better trap, that would be nice, but I know that isn\u2019t going to happen.&gt;\u201d<br /> <br /> He harrumphs in amusement, fully aware of how hard it is to keep a Tribesman at bay, and you continue.<br /> <br /> \u201cThe general plan is relatively straightforward: The ram, led by Three-Leaves here (he shifts uncomfortably under the gaze of your retainers), will proceed up the road to the gate and try to bash it down. While that is happening, other units, equipped with ladders, will attempt to scale the palisade next to and near to the gate, in effort to flank the defending forces. All the while, the longbowmen, both of the Men-at-Arms and the Militia, will provide covering fire from behind the safety of the mantlets.\u201d<br /> <br /> Looking up from the map and around the room, you ask, \u201cAny questions?\u201d<br /> <br /> There are more than a few, most of them just quibbling over details, and you take the time to hammer out any misunderstandings. The sun is well over the horizon by the time you are able to dismiss your officers and equip your wargear. When you leave your pavilion, Masanori close by your heels, your ears and eyes are filled with the sights and sounds of men rushing to and fro, carrying equipment and materiel.<br /> <br /> In the staging-ground cleared out by your forces, you catch a glimpse of your soldiers being shepherded into formation, grabbing ahold of the mantlets of split and lashed bamboo, hefting shields and double-checking arms and armor. As you mount Xuansu and ride up, the men slowly fall silent, and you curse when you remember the words of Sergeant Oxblood (who, <i>coincidentally, </i>is looking at you expectantly).<br /> <br /> Gods damn it, you <i>hate</i> public speaking!<br /> <br /> Glumly, you ride out, to the front of the assembled regiments where you can be clearly seen and heard. Raising your voice, you cry out,<br /> <br /> \u201cMen of Jianghu! For too long have you lived in fear of the bandits of the Akibara Forest! Too long have you suffered their predations, their thievery, the murder of your family members and the destruction of your livelihoods! <i>NO LONGER</i>! For no longer will you have to cower in your homes. No longer will you have to dole out grain, the product of your sweat and blood, to people who give you nothing in return. For they are there!\u201d, pointing up at the fortress overhead, \u201cAnd you, are here, armed and armored, ready to take the fight to them, ready to pay back all the pain and anger they have heaped on you! They cannot escape our righteous retribution! Who is with me!\u201d<br /> <br /> The roar from the host filling the valley is stupendous, and you only-slightly-hyperbolically think they could have heard it back at Yagura Castle. Wheeling Xuansu about dramatically (but <i>gently</i>, you don\u2019t want to fall off!), you point your spear imperiously up the slope, at the defenders of Dun Nashuon. Brass bugles sound, resonating with each other and reverberating off the hills, and with the thunder of hundreds of feet, your army starts to march.<br /> <br /> Wearily, you ride back up to your Oathsworn bodyguards, and as you approach, Masanori, grinning broadly, calls out, \u201cWould you like some <i>ham</i> for your sandwich, my Lord?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cYou want some cheese with that <i>whine</i>, Masanori? Piss off, I <i>hate</i> speeches.\u201d<br /> <br /> Masanori keeps grinning, and Sergeant Oxblood starts laughing, trying to hide it as best he can, and eventually, you join in as well.<br /> <br /> \u201cAlright, I admit it, I pretty much just stole that one from a storybook.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cI mean \u2026 it wasn\u2019t <i>bad</i>, just\u2026.. very clich\u00e9d.\u201d Chimes Masanori, still chuckling. Shaking your head, you fall in with the rest of your retainers, and nudge Xuansu up the hill.<br /> <br /> Thanks to the helpful advice of both Sergeant Oxblood and Masanori, the actual approach to the main gate was actually uneventful, relatively speaking. Yes, as soon as your army got within range of the bowmen in Dun Nashuon, they started loosing arrows. But, since they were all firing individually, loosing in ragged lines and at extreme range, their projectiles mainly fall well short. On top of that, the militiamen, working together with the \u2018actual\u2019 longbow Men-at-Arms, run ahead of the men pushing the assault equipment, setting up the mantlets on top of the berms bracketing the road. These mantlets, in addition to providing cover from enemy arrows, allow the archers to loose arrows of their own, from relative safety. The mantlets even catch the few arrows that do manage to reach your forces, giving your archers \u2018free\u2019 ammunition that they are all-too-glad to send back posthaste.<br /> <br /> Mainly, you are \u2026.. bored? Bored, as the ram <i>slowly</i> makes its way up the path. Far from being a crude hacked-down tree, your men cannibalized one of the wagons, pulling off the sidewalls and suspending the trunk from ropes. Overhead, they even have a protective roof, made of split bamboo, that provides cover from both the sun and from any particularly-well-aimed arrows. By far, the most arduous thing about the ram is that it apparently <i>keeps wanting to roll downhill</i>. The poor bastards pushing the thing have to spend half as much energy keeping the thing from rolling backwards as they do keeping it moving uphill. Plus, the thing turns like a beached whale, especially around the switchbacks in the road.<br /> <br /> It takes at least two hours of steady pushing to move the ram to the front of the gate, and when you rise over the crest of the road, your heart drops. Evidently, one of the bandit-captains was smarter than the rest, and ordered a great volume of water dumped in front of the gate, churning the thin soil to mud. The assault halts as you have to order some of the militiamen to run back down the hill and drag up some of the extra mantlets, in order to throw them into the mud so the wheels can provide traction. All the while, arrows whistle overhead, loosed from militiaman bows, and the longbow regiments form up in blocks on the road behind the mantlets. As the ram trundles forward, the men sweating and straining, you hear their officers shout, \u201cNOCK\u2026. DRAW\u2026\u2026\u2026 LOOSE!\u201d<br /> <br /> With a whisper like the winter wind, over 200 arrows split the air, travelling too fact to see until the very apex of their arc. There, squinting against the sun almost directly overhead, you see the arrows slow, stop, and reverse, screaming back to earth.<br /> <br /> They impact just on the other side of the palisade, and you hear what sounds like nothing other than a quiet sigh. Your mouth dries out in a heartbeat as you realize you just heard several dozen men <i>die</i>, all at once.<br /> <br /> Almost immediately after the arrows impact, the screaming starts from those unlucky enough to be killed by the fusillade, and another volley is drawn and released. And another. And another. All the while, the ram slams into the pine-slab gate, cracking the boards with every stroke.<br /> <br /> The gate holds, withstanding every impact \u2026.. until it doesn\u2019t, cracking into pieces almost instantly. The men go to pull the ram back, but get stuck on <i>something</i>, the mud, a lathe of bamboo, and behind you, you can hear the brass note of a bugle telling the Hill-Tribes to commence their attack. Your heart rises into your throat, and your stomach correspondingly drops to your knees, when you start to hear the warbling call of the Hill-Tribe mountain-goat warhorns, and realize <i>things are not going according to plan</i><u>!</u><br /> <br /> You curse, then turn to your Oathsworn retinue. \u201cCome on, we are going to have to push in! Help me move the ram!\u201d<br /> <br /> Leaping forwards, you get grabbed by Three-Leaves, eyes-wide. \u201cBoss, the ram is stuck, we can\u2019t move it!\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c<i>No fucking shit</i>! If you aren\u2019t going to help push, get out of the way!\u201d, and with that, you grab ahold of the undercarriage.<br /> <br /> Your Oathsworn retainers catch your meaning almost instantly, and they rush in to grab ahold of the ram. Counting off, you lift and push the wagon-carriage in unison, heaving it up on its side and mostly out of the way. Turning to the men bunched up behind you, you call out, \u201cNEXT UNIT THROUGH, MOVE IT FURTHER!\u201d, and turn to charge through the gate.<br /> <br /> Evidently, that little bit of effort on your part was too much, and your heart pounds in your chest, pulse racing in your ears. Your head pounds with the beating of the march, and you fall back to the middle-rank of your Oathsworn retainers, charging shields-raised through the wreckage of the gate.<br /> <br /> Inside the gate is an abattoir.<br /> <br /> Easily several dozen men lie dead on the ground, pierced through by yard-long war arrows, and that many more lay wounded or dying, screaming on the blood-soaked earth. What few men remain standing quickly take to their heels when they see heavy shock infantry charging at them, only to be replaced by a motley crew of bandits and ruffians, trying to prevent your men from gaining any sort of headway into the fortress.<br /> <br /> You see the front-rank of your Oathsworn drop their spears, drawing swords and daggers for the close-in melee, and you heft <i>Moonlight Over Water</i> into an overhand grip.<br /> <br /> The two groups collide with a resounding <i>crash</i>, louder than the breaking of the gate, and the world descends into mud, blood, sweat and steel.<br /> <br /> Your Oathsworn retinue, not in a very tight formation to begin with, spreads out almost as soon as they make contact with the enemy, to better put their individualistic weapons and training to good use. The bandits\u2026. quickly learn that numbers only count for so much, and quality almost always beats quantity. The majority of their weapons, barring a lucky hit in the armpit, mostly just glance off mail and lamellar. The impromptu attack is doing <i>extremely</i> well, at least by your reckoning, and you are immensely surprised when Masanori grabs you by the shoulder, shouting in your face.<br /> <br /> \u201cCall for a retreat or regroup!\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cWhy?!\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cIf we advance too far, we will get surrounded and cut down! Plus, we can help the soldiers climbing the ladders!\u201d Following his pointing finger, you see that the siege-ladders have been leant against the palisade, and troopers armed with halberds and backswords are climbing up and over the rampart, struggling with the bandits on the wallwalk.<br /> <br /> Nodding, you call out, as loud as you can, \u201cOathsworn of Jianghu, to me! To me! Fall back to the gate!\u201d<br /> <br /> Somehow, they hear you over the clashing and slashing of blades and screams of dying men, and perform a fighting retreat. You are almost back to the gate, where your flanks are secure-ish, when one of your retainers trips and falls. To your horror, you recognize them as Shin Takeshi, the elderly retainer who has been so helpful, with the Tribes and with this campaign.<br /> <br /> As you watch, several bandits, recognizing an opportunity when they see one, break off from their group and charge the man. He either doesn\u2019t see them, or is too much pain to respond, and he doesn\u2019t raise an arm in his defense.<br /> <br /> You:<ol class=\"decimal\"><li>Leap to his defense! You can\u2019t just let the man die, after all he has done for you!</li> <li>Stay with the formation! You are in no shape to fight, especially against multiple people!</li> <li>Order a charge again! As already proven, the bandits are no match for the Oathsworn sword!</li> <li>Something else? (write in)</li> </ol>I meant to get this one out on Saturday, but that obviously didn't happen. I also worked overnight on Sunday and Monday, so that wasn't happening either.<br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-26T20:58:40-07:00","id":1121173,"title":"","content":"<b>Order the charge!</b> More men are through the gate and over the walls then there were even two minutes ago, so a push part way back (to Takes) should not over-extend your line.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Like my Avatar? Courtesy of<span style=\"color:#0000FF\"> Jen</span>! : <span style=\"font-size:12px\">Anybody want their characters to be experimented on ? </span>post 98<br /> <i>An Exalt is never unarmed.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4018-l-het-esh","name":"L'het'esh","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4018_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"521"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-26T21:20:46-07:00","id":1121178,"title":"","content":"Throw the spear (calling on Luna) to skewer one or two, we can't break formation as we are in no shape and charging the oathsworn defeats the purpose of the fighting retreat. The extra seconds will hopefully give Shin the moment he needs to defend himself. We might even get in two throws if we are close to the spears dropped by the gate.<br /> <br /> Worst case scenario we don't gain anything and pick our spear up later. Either way it's the lowest risk option. It's also the greatest opportunity for badassery.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11567,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11567-jairain","user":"Jairain","date":". 07-27-2017, 12:23 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11567-jairain","name":"Jairain","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11567_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"345"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-27T04:42:31-07:00","id":1121257,"title":"","content":"(Is Takeshi in Close or Short range at this point? If he's up to Medium, I would order a charge. Otherwise...)<br /> <br /> 4. Order your Oathsworn to change tactics from retreat to <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Withdrawal_(military)\" target=\"_blank\">withdrawal</a>. As a group, engage with the bandits so you can grab Takeshi and then resume moving towards the gate - <i>slowly, </i>this time, so nobody gets left behind or falls out of formation.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>On the frontier of the Wild South, there's only one woman with the grit to take on its most dangerous outlaws and bring them <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/984977-back-alive-or-maybe-dead-quest\" target=\"_blank\">Back Alive, or Maybe Dead.</a><br /> <br /> Avatar by <a href=\"http://puzzlinghappenstance.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">K.S. Brenowitz</a>","edited":{"userid":908,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/908-semicasual","user":"semicasual","date":". 07-27-2017, 09:56 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/908-semicasual","name":"semicasual","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar908_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2499"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-27T05:40:19-07:00","id":1121269,"title":"","content":"Hm. The smart thing to do here is not break formation. We're already injured, we shouldn't be attempting heroics.<br /> <br /> But...well...let's not do the smart thing. Because, gods dammit, we swore Oaths, and the reward for loyal service to one's liege shouldn't be dying in battle while one's liege stands by and watches! I say we offer a quick prayer to Luna (something along the lines of, &quot;I don't know what you want from me, but I'm betting it's not getting murdered by random bandits, so help me out here!&quot;) and wade in and see what our fancy new spear can do. We only need to drive the enemy back for a short time to get Shin out, and it would be unfair to order the rest of our formation to make tactically unwise choices just because we're doing so.<br /> <br /> If we can pull out our bow and drop the bandits without breaking formation to give Shin the room he needs to get up and get back in formation, fine. If we can't, though, wade in with the magic spear.<br /> <br /> Unrelated note? Kasai needs to invest in more throwing weapons. A bow is unwieldy and we keep coming into situations where we want to kill an enemy at short range without losing our ability to fight at close range if the enemy closes. I propose a couple javelins or some throwing axes.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>So I'm making <a href=\"http://godkickingboot.smackjeeves.com/\" target=\"_blank\">God-Kicking Boot</a>, an Exalted webcomic, now. Updates on Sundays. Full-color, mediocre but slowly improving art. It's a thing.<br /> <br /> The absence of a monument can, in its own way, be something of a monument also.</i> -Roger Zelazny","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/512-wise-old-guru","name":"Wise Old Guru","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar512_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1097"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-27T08:17:37-07:00","id":1121317,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>semicasual</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1121257#post1121257\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">(Is Takeshi in Close or Short range at this point? If he's up to Medium, I would order a charge. Otherwise...)<br /> <br /> 4. Order your Oathsworn to change tactics from retreat to <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Withdrawal_(military)\" target=\"_blank\">withdrawal</a>. As a group, engage with the bandits so you can grab Takeshi and then resume moving towards the gate - <i>slowly, </i>this time, so nobody gets left behind or falls out of formation.</div> </div> </div> </div>Takeshi is at Short range to both Kasai and the bandits, 'farther' Short range to the rest of the Oathsworn. The terrain is Difficult; tons of bodies and mud.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-27T08:35:36-07:00","id":1121323,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wise Old Guru</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1121269#post1121269\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Hm. The smart thing to do here is not break formation. We're already injured, we shouldn't be attempting heroics.<br /> <br /> But...well...let's not do the smart thing. Because, gods dammit, we swore Oaths, and the reward for loyal service to one's liege shouldn't be dying in battle while one's liege stands by and watches! I say we offer a quick prayer to Luna (something along the lines of, &quot;I don't know what you want from me, but I'm betting it's not getting murdered by random bandits, so help me out here!&quot;) and wade in and see what our fancy new spear can do. We only need to drive the enemy back for a short time to get Shin out, and it would be unfair to order the rest of our formation to make tactically unwise choices just because we're doing so.<br /> <br /> If we can pull out our bow and drop the bandits without breaking formation to give Shin the room he needs to get up and get back in formation, fine. If we can't, though, wade in with the magic spear.<br /> <br /> Unrelated note? Kasai needs to invest in more throwing weapons. A bow is unwieldy and we keep coming into situations where we want to kill an enemy at short range without losing our ability to fight at close range if the enemy closes. I propose a couple javelins or some throwing axes.</div> </div> </div> </div>If anything, something like a javelin would be equally unwieldy.<br /> <br /> The Vanehani do not use javelins in war, and seeing an Oathsworn warrior hefting some would turn a few heads and raise a few eyebrows. The Hill-Tribes <i>do</i> use javelins in war, but they range between 3 and 5 feet long. They are usually carried in the shield hand, and thrown as part of the charge.<br /> <br /> The Hill-Tribes, and people in areas close to them (aka Jianghu) also use axes, especially the <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tomahawk\" target=\"_blank\"><i>tamahaac</i></a>, which is used as not only a melee and throwing weapon, but as a general-purpose tool across much of the Mid-East, North-East and North (where it is known locally as the <i><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Francisca\" target=\"_blank\">francesca</a>)</i>. They are commonly used not only by the Tribes, but also by woodsmen (not a few Men-at-Arms are carrying tomahawks as tools and sidearms), and are often taken as trophies in battle.<br /> <br /> Takeshi is also not going to be able to get up, not on his own. He twisted/sprained/broke his ankle. Such is a risk in any battle, but especially when you are as old as he is. He is in his 60s, and does not have the same Luna-given blessing as Kasai's grandfather does.<br /> <br /> Oathsworn are expected to be combatants as long as they are physically capable of entering the battlefield under their own power, and the socially-accepted range for that is 20 to 60 years of age. Once one hits their 60s, it is expected you either have children to take up your Oathsworn mantle, or be given a REMF-role in things.<br /> <br /> Kasai, however, doesn't really have the manpower needed to let people sit out. And Shin Takeshi would probably not want to sit out, anyways.<br /> <br /> That above point; that feudal warlords are expected to have children follow in their footsteps, is a <u>big problem</u>, and will come up at some point, not just with Kasai. For example, Shin Takeshi has multiple children and grandchildren (including a granddaughter Kasai's age, <i>tee hee</i>)..... but none of them are <i>Oathsworn</i>. Some of them are trained combatants, but the 'whole point' of the feudal system is that fighters (and families) are granted lands <i>specifically</i> so their landlord has fighters down the line.<br /> <br /> If a retainer-clan doesn't produce fighters, a landlord is technically within their rights to strip the clan of land and title. Therefore, many clan-heirs are all-but forced to train as Oathsworn warriors, so the land and title remains in the clan. Not that this is usually a <i>problem</i>, because as nobility, the training isn't that hard, and there are social benefits to being a warrior noble....... but it does lead to those not having the necessary strength of character giving the Oaths and becoming Oathsworn. Like, for example, the Oathsworn that crippled Kasai back in Teriake.<br /> <br /> Both the Shiba clan and the Harutane clan has this problem; they lack heirs with the strength of will to become Oathsworn, or lack heirs at all. For example, Mino Shiba is her mother's heir (something she already greatly resents), and since the ruler of Xia (and every other feudal domain in the Hundred Kingdoms) is <i>socially-demanded</i> to be a warlord, Mino has received training all her life for combat; personal skills, the leading of troops, that sort of thing. She was all-but forced to accompany her mother on the campaign against Yue, and she is .... not happy.<br /> <br /> She doesn't know what she wants to be .... but a feudal warlord is <i>not it</i>. But, the clan comes first, and she does her duty, albeit grudgingly. Unknown to her, her mother <i>knows</i> she doesn't want, well, any of this, and empathizes. Mino is very much like she was at that age, and her heart breaks that she has to do this. But..... clan and realm first, forever and always, and she doesn't tell her daughter she knows.<br /> <br /> As you can expect, the two do not get along.<br /> <br /> As for the Harutane family (aka the family Kasai was a bastard in), they <i>wish</i> they had that problem. Daichi Harutane, Kasai's father and clan-head, has 6 children, Kasai (23, Male) , who as a bastard cannot inherit anything, Aki (14, Female), Kino (11, Female), Kazuo (9, Male), Uta (9, Female), and Hisoka (3, Male). As the firstborn 'trueborn' child, Aki is her father's heir..... but she has no desire to be Oathsworn, and Daichi is too nice to <i>force</i> his kids to do something.<br /> <br /> If none of the children want to be Oathsworn (Aki, Kino), or are even capable of fulfilling the duties of the position (Kazuo is a ..... sickly child), then the only way he can keep the land within his family is to marry his children to someone capable of fulfilling the position (which was one of the reasons why Mino was engaged to Haichi) . That will be .... difficult, as the Harutane family is, and was, poor to begin with, even more so after Daichi purchased Kasai's equipment, and therefore they do not have much money to offer for a dowry for <i>one</i> daughter, much less 3. Daichi is prematurely aging as a result of the stress, and his health is slowly failing. Kasai does not know this.<br /> <br /> One of the reasons Daichi wanted to legitimize Kasai, asides from him, well, actually loving his child and wanting him to succeed, was so that Kasai could take over when he came of age, thereby preventing their problems entirely. Kasai's step-mother prevented that, not out of malice, but from wanting her own children to inherit.<br /> <br /> On Kasai and his stepmother..... their relationship wasn't <i>antagonistic</i>, not like Catelyn Stark and Jon Snow, she raised him after all, and the only reason he knew she wasn't his mother was that he looks <i>nothing</i> like her, but Kasai is significantly older than the rest of his half-siblings, and he was intelligent enough to recognize both what he was, social-wise, and why she wanted his siblings to inherit. When Daichi commissioned Kasai's equipment, which as stated before was <i>very</i> expensive (and most of which is ruined, <i>D'oh!)</i>, Daichi and his wife got into a very long row about it, and if pressed, Kasai would cite that argument, and efforts to prevent more conflict in the future, as one of his reasons for leaving home to go erranting. He left rather abruptly, and Kasai's stepmother was heartbroken, since she thought she pressured him into leaving.<br /> <br /> On a related note, Kasai needs to have kids, and soon, so this problem has less a chance of arising.<br /> <br /> .....<br /> <br /> ...... Where the hell these massive tangents come from, I have no idea. I started this post talking about throwing weapons, and I end it talking about Kasai's family issues.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"https://media.giphy.com/media/njl60xjwgrkWY/giphy.gif\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/google-material/image_placeholder.svg\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://media.giphy.com/media/njl60xjwgrkWY/giphy.gif\" target=\"_blank\"> </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://media.giphy.com/media/njl60xjwgrkWY/giphy.gif\" target=\"_blank\"> https://media.giphy.com/media/njl60xjwgrkWY/giphy.gif </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> </div> </div> </div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11892,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","user":"Boston123","date":". 07-27-2017, 01:56 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-27T11:27:25-07:00","id":1121421,"title":"","content":"Can we send Masanori to go get Takashi, and cover him? Or have Masanori assist us in grabbing him?<br /> What are <i>our </i>chances of spraining an ankle, at this point? Because it sounds like leaping to Takashi's defense, spear blazing, is both something I want to do and a really, really bad idea.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3752-mushroom-king","name":"Mushroom King","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3752_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"221"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-27T22:27:50-07:00","id":1121717,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>semicasual</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1001132-oathsworn-quest?p=1121257#post1121257\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">(Is Takeshi in Close or Short range at this point? If he's up to Medium, I would order a charge. Otherwise...)<br /> <br /> 4. Order your Oathsworn to change tactics from retreat to <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Withdrawal_(military)\" target=\"_blank\">withdrawal</a>. As a group, engage with the bandits so you can grab Takeshi and then resume moving towards the gate - <i>slowly, </i>this time, so nobody gets left behind or falls out of formation.</div> </div> </div> </div>Seconded, though if we can pull off Wise Old Guru's bow shot to save Shin, I'd add that to the list.<br /> Let's not throw away our shiney new artefact spear on a bandit if we can't get it back right away. These seem like the type of people who will take pretty loot and run while they can. Not risking it lost in the confusion of battle.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Thoughts ripple out, birthing others","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1624-falleneco","name":"FallenEco","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1624_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1927"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-14T07:45:31-07:00","id":1128810,"title":"","content":"Going to go ahead and add my vote to some kind of withdraw &quot;grab Takeshi as group&quot; if that helps. Or at the very least call masanori over to help.<br /> Hope you're doing okay Boston123, or at least getting a lil bit of rest<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" />. And at any rate, thanks for the fun thread 